window.BANK={"canon":["Joining, handover and the C/O's role","Certificates, surveys and load line","Legislation: OLB, MAIB, notices, PSC","Crew, MLC and discipline","ISM and ISPS","Navigation, bridge equipment, compass and met","Ship handling and seamanship","Emergencies: grounding, collision, flooding, fire at sea","SAR, GMDSS and medical","Fire fighting","LSA, lifting gear and enclosed spaces","Cargo: containers and dangerous goods","Cargo: bulk, grain, tankers, documents","Stability, load line calculations and dry dock","Pollution and ballast water","Salvage, towage, insurance and commercial","COLREGS, lights and radar plotting","Buoyage"],"short":["Joining and handover","Certificates and surveys","Legislation","Crew and MLC","ISM and ISPS","Navigation and met","Ship handling","Emergencies","SAR, GMDSS, medical","Fire fighting","LSA and enclosed spaces","Containers and DG","Bulk, tankers, documents","Stability and dry dock","Pollution","Salvage and commercial","COLREGS and radar","Buoyage"],"rows":[{"id":"Q1","sec":0,"q":"How do you take over as Chief Officer? Before boarding, on boarding, handover.","tier":"red","rep":187,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Taking over is a company SMS procedure. ISM 6.3 requires the company to familiarise new personnel with their duties, and STCW I/14 and A-VI/1 require safety familiarisation before I&#x27;m given duties. So I follow the company&#x27;s handover checklist and sign it.<\/p><p><strong>Before boarding<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Read the handover notes and the last C/O&#x27;s report sent by the company.<\/li><li>If possible, get an office briefing from the superintendent, the DPA and the CSO: PMS status, open non-conformities, last audits, PSC history, conditions of class and the trade.<\/li><li>Check my own documents: CoC, flag endorsement, ENG1, STCW certificates, SEA.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>From the quay, before I step on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Draught and load line marks: is she over her marks, is she listing?<\/li><li>Hull condition, moorings tight with rat guards.<\/li><li>Gangway: rigged and secured, safety net, lifebuoy with line, lighting, and the angle suits the tide.<\/li><li>At the top: the ISPS level is posted, the watchman checks my ID, logs me in and calls the duty officer. If nobody stops me, that&#x27;s my first finding.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Report to the Master with my documents, and sign the articles/crew agreement.<\/li><li>Safety familiarisation tour: my muster station and emergency duties, LSA and FFE, emergency escape routes, alarms.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Handover with the outgoing C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Stability: loadicator, its test conditions and last approval, the current condition, stack weights and the lashing computer.<\/li><li>Cargo: bay plan, DG manifest and positions, reefer list, OOG units, the CSM and lashing gear inventory.<\/li><li>Certificates and survey status: what&#x27;s due, conditions of class, last PSC and flag reports.<\/li><li>PMS: overdue jobs, defects, spares, critical equipment.<\/li><li>Records: GRB, ballast water record book, cargo gear register, drills and training, rest hours.<\/li><li>Keys, SSO duties if I&#x27;m the SSO, permits in force, hot work or enclosed space work going on.<\/li><li>People: deck crew strengths and weaknesses, cadets and their record books.<\/li><\/ol><p>Then a walk round deck with him, and I read the draughts myself.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>We both sign the checklist, I tell the Master I&#x27;ve taken over, and I note it in my log. Anything I&#x27;m not happy with, a defect, missing record or an unsafe condition, I report to the Master before the outgoing C/O leaves, so it&#x27;s clear what condition I took the ship over in.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What would you refuse to sign for in the handover?","pa":"<p>Anything I haven&#x27;t checked myself. That means a stability or loadicator condition I haven&#x27;t verified, DG stowage I haven&#x27;t seen, records I know are false (rest hours, ORB/GRB), or equipment reported as working when it isn&#x27;t (LSA, fire detection). I sign only for what I&#x27;ve seen. Anything wrong goes on the handover sheet and to the Master.<\/p>","asked":["How will you take over from Chief mate?","What does a bulk carrier have?","What all documents you will handover to Master?","What are all your certificates that you will hand-over to the Master?","How will you join the vessel?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",30],["Zia Ul Haq",29],["Shahid Akhter",22],["Muhammad Iqbal",20],["Examiner not named",12]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q2","sec":0,"q":"What do you do in the first 30 minutes after arriving in port?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>My priority order is: the ship is safely secured, the access is safe and secure, then cargo can start safely. The SMS arrival checklist covers it.<\/p><p><strong>1. Moorings and access<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Lines tight and evenly tended, no lines on the same bollard fighting each other, rat guards fitted.<\/li><li>Gangway rigged and secured, safety net under it, lifebuoy with line, lighting at night. Check it against the range of tide and the change of draught during cargo.<\/li><li>Mooring watch set: who tends the lines and how often.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Security (ISPS)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Set the port&#x27;s security level and confirm it with the PFSO through the agent.<\/li><li>Gangway watch manned with ID checks and a visitors&#x27; log.<\/li><li>If the ship and the port are at different levels, or the port asks for it, complete a Declaration of Security.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Draughts and density<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Read the draughts fore, aft and midships on both sides, take the dock water density with a hydrometer.<\/li><li>Compare with the loadicator arrival condition. A difference tells me about the constant, ballast or a tank I haven&#x27;t accounted for.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Terminal meeting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Agree the load and discharge plan and sequence with the planner or foreman: restows, OOG, DG, reefers, hatch cover moves, gantry allocation, expected completion.<\/li><li>Exchange contact numbers and the emergency procedure, and complete the ship/shore safety checks the terminal uses.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Stability and stress<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Confirm the sequence keeps GM, shear force, bending moment and torsion within limits at every stage, not just at the end.<\/li><li>Agree the ballast plan, and that no ballast moves without my say.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Port requirements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Agent, immigration, customs and free pratique.<\/li><li>Local rules on hot work, ballast discharge, garbage, ECA fuel, and bunkering notifications.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Safety and pollution rounds<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Scuppers plugged if required, SOPEP gear ready, fire wires rigged if the port wants them.<\/li><li>Deck watch set, night orders written, and the crew know the work and rest plan.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Only when the ship is secure, the access is safe and controlled, and the plan is checked do I tell the Master we&#x27;re ready to start cargo. Doing these in order stops the classic problems: lines parting, a gangway falling off, a stranger walking on board, or the ship going over a stress limit halfway through the discharge.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Draughts don't agree with the loadicator by 15 cm. What do you do?","pa":"<p>Don&#x27;t ignore it. Re-read the draughts on both sides and amidships for hog or sag. Re-check the dock-water density at the right depth and position. Re-sound ballast, bunkers and fresh water, and check the constant and the loadicator inputs. If the difference is still there, assume the ship is heavier: tell the Master and the planner, suspect mis-declared box weights (VGM) and don&#x27;t exceed the marks.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q3","sec":0,"q":"What are the Chief Officer's duties and where are they defined?","tier":"red","rep":56,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Where they&#x27;re defined<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>The SMS under the ISM Code.<\/strong> ISM 3.2 requires the company to define and document the responsibility, authority and interrelation of all personnel. My C/O duties are written in the SMS manual.<\/li><li><strong>STCW Reg II/2 and Code A-II/2.<\/strong> These set the management-level competences I&#x27;m certificated for: loading, stowage, securing and discharge; stability, trim and stress; ship&#x27;s safety and security; pollution prevention; compliance with legislation; leadership and management.<\/li><li><strong>My job description and contract (SEA)<\/strong>, and the Master&#x27;s standing orders.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What that means on a container ship<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Cargo:<\/strong> check the stowage plan from the planners, stack weights and heights, visibility, DG segregation and DOC compliance, reefers and their power, OOG units, and lashing to the CSM and the lashing software.<\/li><li><strong>Stability and stress:<\/strong> run the loadicator, plan the ballast, trim and draught, check UKC and air draught, and manage ballast water under the BWM plan and record book.<\/li><li><strong>Maintenance through the PMS:<\/strong> hatch covers and seals, lashing gear, mooring and anchoring equipment, hull and tanks, and the register of lifting appliances.<\/li><li><strong>Safety:<\/strong> LSA and FFE maintenance and drills, normally delegated by the Master. Often I&#x27;m also the safety officer under COSWP and the Merchant Shipping (Health and Safety at Work) Regs, and the SSO under the SSP.<\/li><li><strong>People:<\/strong> organise deck work, check rest hours, permits to work, training and familiarisation of new joiners, cadets and their record books, discipline at first level.<\/li><li><strong>Pollution:<\/strong> Garbage Record Book, ballast water records, deck-side oil spill readiness under SOPEP.<\/li><li><strong>Emergencies:<\/strong> I lead the emergency party at the scene under the muster list; the Master runs the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Navigation:<\/strong> I keep a bridge watch on most ships and must be fit to take command.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The key point<\/strong><\/p><p>I&#x27;m the Master&#x27;s deputy and the ship&#x27;s working manager. If the Master dies or is incapacitated, I take command, so I must know the passage plan, the certificates and the company procedures as well as the cargo.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>I&#x27;d sum it up to the examiner this way: the ISM SMS says what the company has given me, STCW says what I must be competent to do, and on board I&#x27;m responsible for the cargo, stability, deck maintenance, safety equipment and the deck crew.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Where in the ISM Code does it require responsibilities to be defined?","pa":"<p><strong>ISM 3.2<\/strong>: the company defines and documents the responsibility, authority and interrelation of all personnel who manage, perform and verify work affecting safety and pollution prevention. <strong>ISM 5<\/strong> covers the Master&#x27;s responsibilities, and <strong>ISM 6.2<\/strong> requires the Master and crew to be qualified and familiar with their duties.<\/p>","asked":["Where will you find Duties as a chief officer?","What are your responsibilities as a chief mate for fresh water management?","What are your responsibilities as chief mate?","What will be the roles of Chief Officer?","Concerns as chief mate during loading operations."],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",6],["Zia Ul Haq",6],["Robert Jackson",6],["Shahid Akhter",4],["Chris Williams",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q4","sec":0,"q":"Chief Officer's duties under ISM and under STCW?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Under the ISM Code<\/strong><\/p><p>My duties are whatever the company assigns me in the SMS. ISM 3.2 makes the company define and document the responsibilities, authority and interrelation of all personnel. In practice I:<\/p><ul><li>implement the SMS on deck: procedures, checklists, permits to work and risk assessments (ISM 7, shipboard operations);<\/li><li>familiarise new joiners with their safety duties before sailing (ISM 6.3) and identify training needs (ISM 6.5);<\/li><li>run emergency drills and keep the crew ready (ISM 8);<\/li><li>report accidents, hazardous occurrences, near misses and non-conformities, and close out corrective actions (ISM 9);<\/li><li>maintain the ship and equipment through the PMS, including critical equipment (ISM 10);<\/li><li>keep the documents and records controlled and up to date (ISM 11);<\/li><li>support the Master&#x27;s review and the internal and external audits (ISM 12).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Under STCW<\/strong><\/p><p>STCW Reg II/2 and Code A-II/2, Table A-II/2, list the management-level competences:<\/p><ul><li>plan and ensure safe loading, stowage, securing, care during the voyage, and unloading;<\/li><li>assess reported defects and damage to cargo spaces, hatch covers and ballast tanks;<\/li><li>carriage of dangerous goods;<\/li><li>control trim, stability and stress;<\/li><li>monitor and control compliance with legislation and measures for safety of life, security and protection of the marine environment;<\/li><li>maintain the safety and security of the crew and passengers and the operational condition of LSA, FFE and other safety systems;<\/li><li>develop emergency and damage-control plans;<\/li><li>use leadership and managerial skills;<\/li><li>organise and manage the provision of medical care on board.<\/li><\/ul><p>Other STCW parts touching me: A-VIII/1, fitness for duty and rest hours; A-VIII/2, watchkeeping principles, including cargo watches in port; and A-I/14, company responsibilities, which I carry out through familiarisation.<\/p><p><strong>UK angle<\/strong><\/p><p>STCW is applied in the UK by the Merchant Shipping (STCW) Regulations 2022 (SI 2022/1342), with MSN 1856 for deck CoCs and MSN 1868 (M) Amd 1 for safe manning and watchkeeping. Hours of rest are in the Merchant Shipping (Hours of Work) Regulations 2018 and MSN 1877 (M) Amd 2.<\/p><p><strong>How they fit together<\/strong><\/p><p>STCW makes sure I have the competence. ISM makes the company put that competence to work through documented duties. An auditor checks the ISM side by asking me to show the procedure and the record. A flag or PSC inspector checks the STCW side by asking for my certificate and watching me do a drill.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>In short, ISM tells me what the company expects of me, and STCW sets the competence I must have.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who on board is responsible for familiarisation, and when must it be done?","pa":"<p>The <strong>Master<\/strong> is responsible, under the company&#x27;s procedure (<strong>ISM 6.3<\/strong>). In practice the C/O or another designated officer does it. Safety familiarisation must be given <strong>before the person is assigned shipboard duties<\/strong> (STCW I/14 and A-VI/1). If more than 25% of the crew haven&#x27;t taken part in drills on that ship in the past month, an abandon ship and fire drill must be held <strong>within 24 h of leaving port<\/strong> (SOLAS III/19.3.2).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q5","sec":0,"q":"How do you allocate work to the crew and set the watch arrangements?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Rest-hour rules first.<\/strong> For a UK ship it&#x27;s the Merchant Shipping (Hours of Work) Regulations 2018 and MSN 1877 (M) Amd 2, matching MLC A2.3 and STCW A-VIII/1:<\/p><ul><li>minimum 10 hours&#x27; rest in any 24 hours, and 77 hours in any 7 days;<\/li><li>rest in no more than two periods, one of them at least 6 hours;<\/li><li>no more than 14 hours between consecutive rest periods.<\/li><\/ul><p>I post the table of shipboard working arrangements in the working language and English, and everyone keeps an individual rest-hour record, signed by them and by the Master. I check the records against what people actually did, not just the planned hours.<\/p><p><strong>Watch arrangements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Master sets the bridge watch system.<\/li><li>I set the deck watches in port: gangway watch, cargo watch, mooring watch. On a container ship with short port stays the real risk is the crew arriving in port tired, working cargo all day and then sailing tired. So I plan the rotation backwards from the departure time.<\/li><li>STCW A-VIII/2 Part 4-1 requires a proper cargo watch in port, so the OOW and deck ratings must be rested and competent.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Daily work planning<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Start from the PMS and the priorities: safety-critical items first, then cargo-related work, then general maintenance.<\/li><li>Match jobs to competence and certificates, for example only trained people on enclosed space entry or working aloft, and cadets supervised.<\/li><li>Do the risk assessment, and issue a permit to work where the SMS requires it: hot work, enclosed space entry, working aloft or overside, electrical isolation.<\/li><li>Hold a toolbox talk: hazards, PPE, communications, who&#x27;s in charge, and the stop-work authority.<\/li><li>Supervise, and check the job is finished, the area is safe and the PMS is updated.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Special cases<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Young persons under 18: MSN 1838 (M) Amendment 2. No night work, more rest, no hazardous work unless it&#x27;s supervised training.<\/li><li>Emergencies and drills can break rest under the rules, but I give compensatory rest afterwards and minimise disturbance.<\/li><li>If the work can&#x27;t be done within the hours, I tell the Master. Either the work waits or the company provides more people. Faking rest-hour records is a falsification offence and a PSC detention item.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>So I plan rest first, then fit the work around it, with risk assessment and permits for the dangerous jobs. Fatigue is the commonest underlying cause in MAIB reports.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What are the limits on exceptions to the rest-hour rules?","pa":"<p>The Master can suspend the rest schedule for the <strong>immediate safety<\/strong> of the ship, people or cargo, or to help others in distress, and must then give <strong>compensatory rest<\/strong>. Planned exceptions are allowed only by an approved agreement. Even then there must be at least <strong>70 h rest in 7 days<\/strong>, for no more than 2 consecutive weeks. Daily rest may be split into <strong>no more than 3 periods<\/strong>, one at least 6 h. (STCW A-VIII/1, applied by <strong>MSN 1877 Amd 2<\/strong>.)<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q6","sec":0,"q":"What information do you give the 2nd Officer for passage planning, and what do you want back?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Passage planning is required by SOLAS V/34, in UK law through the Merchant Shipping (Safety of Navigation) Regulations 2020, with guidance in MGN 610 (M+F) Amd 1 and the IMO guidelines in A.893(21). The plan belongs to the Master. The 2/O drafts it. As C/O my job is to feed it the ship&#x27;s condition and check it against my loading plan.<\/p><p><strong>What I give the 2/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Departure and arrival draughts, trim, displacement and GM, and the expected changes from fuel and water consumption.<\/li><li>Air draught for the departure and arrival condition, which is critical on a container ship with high stacks under bridges and cranes.<\/li><li>DG positions and classes, so routes and ports with DG restrictions are known.<\/li><li>Reefer numbers and power demand, which affect the generators and any fuel changeover.<\/li><li>The ballast plan: whether we need ballast water exchange or must run the BWMS, and any port with special ballast rules.<\/li><li>Cargo limits: lashing calculations done for a certain GM and weather, maximum speed or heading limits from the lashing software, heavy-weather routeing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I want back<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Load line zones and seasonal changes on passage, so I load to the most restrictive mark, allowing for consumption.<\/li><li>UKC and air draught checked at every critical point: channels, canals, bridges, port approaches, including squat.<\/li><li>Ballast water exchange areas, or confirmation the D-2 system covers it.<\/li><li>MARPOL special areas and ECAs, with fuel changeover points and times for the engine room, and the garbage discharge restrictions.<\/li><li>High Risk Areas, with the security level, routeing, UKMTO reporting and hardening measures.<\/li><li>The weather plan: expected sea state, rolling and parametric-roll risk, and where we&#x27;d divert.<\/li><li>Ship reporting systems, VTS and pilotage points on the route.<\/li><li>Canal or strait requirements, for example Suez documents and tonnage certificate, which affect what I prepare.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Checking it<\/strong><\/p><p>I cross-check the passage plan against the cargo and stability plan. If the passage plan shows a draught restriction or a winter zone the loading plan doesn&#x27;t allow for, one of them must change before we sail.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Master approves and signs the plan. I sign that I&#x27;ve read it, and so do the other watchkeepers. The examiner wants to hear that the loading plan and the passage plan are made together, not separately.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does the load line zone matter to *your* loading at the departure port?","pa":"<p>Load Line Article 12: the appropriate mark must not be submerged on <strong>departure, during the voyage or on arrival<\/strong>. If the ship will enter a stricter zone or season on passage, I load at departure so she&#x27;s at the mark for that zone when she reaches it, after allowing for the fuel and water used on the way.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q7","sec":0,"q":"Sailing in September from Qatar to the North Sea — what does the examiner want you to mention?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>The examiner wants me to show that the route drives my cargo, stability and compliance decisions. I&#x27;d take it in order along the route.<\/p><p><strong>1. Load line zones (Load Line Convention Annex II; MS (Load Line) Regs 1998 and MSN 1752)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Gulf and Gulf of Oman are in the Summer/Tropical areas depending on dates; the Arabian Sea is a seasonal area with the SW monsoon, so September may still be Summer there (verify the exact dates on the zones chart).<\/li><li>Red Sea is Tropical, the Mediterranean is Summer in September.<\/li><li>The North Atlantic Winter Seasonal Zone starts on 1 November for most of it. Arriving in September it&#x27;s Summer, but if we&#x27;re delayed into November, the arrival draught matters.<\/li><li>I load to the most restrictive mark I&#x27;ll meet on passage, allowing for consumption between zones.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. MARPOL special areas<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Gulfs area, Gulf of Aden, Red Sea, Mediterranean and North-West European waters are special areas under Annex I and/or V. Garbage discharge restrictions apply.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. ECAs and fuel (Annex VI)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Mediterranean SOx ECA has applied since 1 May 2025, and the North Sea ECA covers SOx and NOx Tier III for new engines.<\/li><li>Plan the changeover to 0.10% fuel before entry, record the time, position and tank volumes, and agree it with the Chief Engineer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Ballast water<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Exchange areas or BWMS operation under the BWM Convention. In UK waters the Ballast Water and Sediments Regs 2022 and MSN 1908 apply, with D-2 required for all ships since 8 Sep 2024.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Security<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Strait of Hormuz, Gulf of Oman, Gulf of Aden and southern Red Sea including Bab-el-Mandeb.<\/li><li>Check the current threat assessment, UKMTO and MSCHOA registration and reports, BMP Maritime Security (March 2025, revised June 2026), and the company&#x27;s decision to transit or go via the Cape.<\/li><li>Raise the security level as the SSP requires, and harden the ship.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Suez Canal<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Suez tonnage certificate, documents, convoy timing, searchlight, mooring boats and crew, and DG declarations for the canal.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Weather and navigation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The end of the SW monsoon in the Arabian Sea, Mediterranean winds, autumn gales in Biscay and the Western Approaches, and fog in the Channel.<\/li><li>Ship reporting systems and TSS: CALDOVREP in the Dover Strait, and VTS on the approach.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>So the answer is: load to the most restrictive zone, plan the special areas and ECA changeover, ballast water, the security transit, the canal, and the weather. Then I check the passage plan and the loading plan agree.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"If the company diverts you round the Cape, what changes in your loading?","pa":"<p>More fuel, fresh water and stores mean less cargo deadweight. The <strong>load line zones<\/strong> change: around the Cape in southern winter I&#x27;d check the winter seasonal area on the zones chart. Expect <strong>heavy weather<\/strong> off South Africa, so review GM, stack weights, lashing patterns and parametric-roll risk, and agree a weather routeing. The ECA, special-area and bunkering plans change too.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q8","sec":0,"q":"Pre-departure preparations by the Chief Officer?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>I work through the company&#x27;s pre-departure checklist under the SMS, and I report to the Master only when all of it is done.<\/p><p><strong>1. Cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Final load list checked against the bay plan; no box on board that isn&#x27;t on the plan.<\/li><li>DG positions and segregation confirmed against the IMDG Code and the DG Document of Compliance; DG manifest final.<\/li><li>Reefers plugged in, running and at the set temperatures.<\/li><li>OOG and heavy units secured to the CSM.<\/li><li>Lashing complete and checked against the lashing computer; twistlocks locked; lashing bars tight.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Stability and stress<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Final loadicator condition: GM, shear forces, bending moments and torsion within limits, IMO intact stability criteria met, visibility from the bridge, stack weights within limits.<\/li><li>Draughts read fore, aft and midships on both sides, dock water density taken, FWA and DWA applied. Not over the marks for the load line zone.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Weathertight integrity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Hatch covers seated and cleated, access hatches, ventilators and watertight doors closed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Ballast<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ballast completed to plan, BWMS operated where required, and the ballast water record book written up.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Final stowage plan, DG manifest, cargo documents and the Master&#x27;s copies.<\/li><li><strong>Draught and freeboard entry<\/strong> in the Official Log Book before proceeding to sea, under the Load Line Regulations and the OLB Regs. The Master makes the entry; I usually supply the figures.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Security<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Stowaway search and security sweep to the SSP, especially in high-risk ports.<\/li><li>Crew head count: everyone on board, nobody missing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Moorings and anchors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Stations manned and briefed, anchors cleared and ready, pilot ladder rigged to SOLAS V/23 and checked.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Engine room liaison<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Bunkers, lub oil and fresh water ROB, any ECA changeover, and the expected departure time for engine readiness.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>9. Bridge checks<\/strong> (with the 2/O)<\/p><ul><li>Steering gear tested within 12 hours before departure, SOLAS V/26.<\/li><li>Navigation equipment, communications, signals, passage plan approved.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Then I tell the Master the ship is ready for sea in all respects. If anything is not ready, a box not lashed, a stress over the limit or a crew member missing, I say so and we don&#x27;t sail until it&#x27;s fixed or he decides it&#x27;s safe. That&#x27;s where the C/O earns his keep.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What draught and freeboard details go in the Official Log Book before sailing?","pa":"<p>Under the OLB Regulations the entry records:<\/p><ul><li>the draughts forward and aft (and the mean);<\/li><li>the freeboard;<\/li><li>the density of the water;<\/li><li>the load line mark that applies;<\/li><li>any fresh water or river-consumption allowance used;<\/li><li>the date, time and place.<\/li><\/ul><p>The Master makes the entry and an officer witnesses it.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q9","sec":0,"q":"Night orders for cargo operations in port (container ship)?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>My night orders follow the SMS and sit alongside the Master&#x27;s standing orders. They&#x27;re written, signed by me, and every OOW signs that they&#x27;ve read them.<\/p><p><strong>For the OOW on cargo watch<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>The plan.<\/strong> Follow the agreed load/discharge sequence and bay plan. Report at once any restow, a box not on the plan, a box that looks overweight, or a damaged container.<\/li><li><strong>Stability and draught.<\/strong> Watch the list, trim and draught. Run the loadicator at the intervals I set and check it against the draughts. No ballast changes without my approval.<\/li><li><strong>Dangerous goods.<\/strong> Check placards and condition of DG boxes. If a box leaks, smokes or smells: stop cargo in that area, keep people clear and upwind, call me, and get the EmS schedule ready.<\/li><li><strong>Reefers.<\/strong> Plug in and switch on, check set temperatures against the manifest, and report alarms.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing.<\/strong> Check lashing is applied to the CSM/lashing plan as each bay is completed: twistlocks locked, bars and turnbuckles tight, hatch covers seated before boxes go on.<\/li><li><strong>Moorings and gangway.<\/strong> Tend lines with the tide and draught. Keep the gangway safe and adjusted. Watch for passing ships causing surge.<\/li><li><strong>Security.<\/strong> Keep ISPS access control at the current level. Nobody on board without ID and a log entry.<\/li><li><strong>Safety.<\/strong> Stevedores to wear PPE and stay away from lashing in progress overhead. Record any stevedore damage on a damage report and have the foreman sign it. Gantry cranes stop in strong wind, so tell me if they stop.<\/li><li><strong>Pollution.<\/strong> Scuppers plugged where required, no spills, report any sheen.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Call me if<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>the list exceeds the figure I set, for example 2°;<\/li><li>there&#x27;s any DG problem;<\/li><li>there&#x27;s damage, an injury, a fire or a pollution incident;<\/li><li>weather affects the moorings or stops the cranes;<\/li><li>the plan or the expected completion time changes;<\/li><li>anything at all worries you. If in doubt, call me.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Specific numbers and specific triggers, not &quot;call me if there&#x27;s a problem&quot;. I&#x27;d say I always add &quot;you will not be criticised for calling me&quot;, because a junior officer must not hesitate. And I check the orders were followed when I come on in the morning.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A DG container is leaking during discharge. What are your first actions?","pa":"<p>Stop cargo work in that area and keep people clear and upwind. Raise the alarm and tell the Master and the terminal. Identify the cargo from the manifest (UN number). Then follow the <strong>EmS spillage schedule<\/strong> and <strong>MFAG<\/strong>: the right PPE and SCBA, no ignition sources if it&#x27;s flammable, and contain it so nothing reaches drains or the sea. Call the port emergency services.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q10","sec":0,"q":"An AB asks to extend his contract. Considerations?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The UK regulations are the Merchant Shipping (Maritime Labour Convention) (Minimum Requirements for Seafarers etc.) Regs 2014, which cover the SEA. The international base is MLC Standard A2.1 (SEA) and Standard A2.5.1 (repatriation).<\/p><p><strong>1. Maximum period on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Under MLC Standard A2.5.1 the maximum continuous period of service on board before repatriation must be less than 12 months.<\/li><li>I can only support an extension that keeps him within that limit, and within any shorter limit in the CBA or company policy.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Who can agree it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The SEA is a contract between the seafarer and the shipowner. Only the company can agree an amendment, signed by both, with a copy given to him and one kept on board.<\/li><li>The Master and I can recommend. We can&#x27;t authorise it ourselves.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Certificates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>His ENG1 must be valid for the whole extension. If it would run out during the extension, he needs a new ENG1 (MSN 1886) from an approved doctor before it expires, or he can&#x27;t stay.<\/li><li>His STCW certificates, endorsements and any flag endorsement must also stay valid.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Fitness and fatigue<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Look at his rest-hour record and his work. Long contracts bring fatigue and reduced alertness.<\/li><li>Ask him why. If there&#x27;s a money or family problem, the company&#x27;s welfare support may help more than an extension.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Wages and leave<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Paid annual leave under MLC Standard A2.4 is at least 2.5 calendar days per month of employment. The extension doesn&#x27;t remove that.<\/li><li>Wages for the extra period must be agreed and paid correctly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Crew planning<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The relief, visas, the next crew-change port, repatriation costs, and the Safe Manning Document: does the change affect manning at all?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><p>If it&#x27;s within the limits, he&#x27;s fit, his certificates are valid and he&#x27;s working well, I recommend it to the Master. The Master passes it to the company. When the company agrees, the SEA amendment is signed and I check the crew list and rest-hour planning are updated.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Three things: under 12 months, only the company can amend the SEA, and his certificates and fitness must last the extension.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who pays for repatriation, and when is a seafarer entitled to it?","pa":"<p>The <strong>shipowner<\/strong> pays (MLC Std A2.5.1), and can&#x27;t charge the seafarer except for serious default. A seafarer is entitled to repatriation when:<\/p><ul><li>the SEA expires;<\/li><li>it&#x27;s ended by the owner, or by him for justified reasons;<\/li><li>he&#x27;s sick or injured;<\/li><li>the ship is wrecked or sold;<\/li><li>the owner can&#x27;t meet his obligations;<\/li><li>the ship is going to a war zone without his consent;<\/li><li>he reaches the maximum period of service (less than 12 months).<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["How you will extend his contract?"],"ex":[["Sarah Lawlor",1]],"yrs":"2025–2025"},{"id":"Q11","sec":0,"q":"How do you know the ship is running properly when you take over?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>I look for evidence, not what I&#x27;m told. A ship can look clean and still be badly run. I check four things: audits, survey status, records, and the ship and people themselves.<\/p><p><strong>1. Audits and inspections<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The last internal and external ISM audit reports, ISPS audit, MLC inspection, flag State inspection and PSC reports.<\/li><li>Are the non-conformities and deficiencies closed with a real root cause and corrective action, or just ticked off? The same finding coming back is a sign the SMS isn&#x27;t working.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Class and certificates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The class survey status report: surveys due, conditions of class and their due dates, and the certificate survey windows.<\/li><li>Certificates valid and in date, and the ship matches them.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Planned maintenance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Overdue jobs, especially critical equipment under ISM 10.3: steering, emergency generator, fire pumps, LSA, FFE, hatch covers, lashing gear, moorings.<\/li><li>Spares inventory: are critical spares on board?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Official log book, deck log, rest-hour records, drill and training records, ORB and GRB, ballast water records, permits to work, near-miss reports.<\/li><li>Gaps are a warning, but so are perfect copy-and-paste entries. Rest hours that are always exactly 10 hours, or drills with identical times every month, aren&#x27;t believable.<\/li><li>Few near-miss reports usually means people aren&#x27;t reporting, not that nothing happens.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Stability<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The loadicator test conditions are run and agree with the booklet.<\/li><li>The draughts I read agree with the calculated condition. A big constant tells me something isn&#x27;t recorded.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. A walk round<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Hatch covers and seals, lashing gear condition, mooring lines and winches, LSA and FFE ready, housekeeping, leaks and rust, fire doors closed, escape routes clear.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. The people<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Watch the first drill. Do people know their duties, does equipment work?<\/li><li>Talk to the crew. Do they know the procedures? Is morale OK? Do they feel able to report problems?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I do with it<\/strong><\/p><p>I make a list, prioritise the safety items, and report to the Master. Anything that&#x27;s a non-conformity goes into the SMS reporting system, and via the Master to the company and DPA. I set the deck crew&#x27;s work priorities from it.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>To hear &quot;evidence&quot;: audits, class status, PMS, records, and seeing the crew in action.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the \"quarterly listing\" or survey status report and what does it show you?","pa":"<p>It&#x27;s the class <strong>survey status report<\/strong>. It lists:<\/p><ul><li>every class and statutory survey done and due, with its window;<\/li><li>certificate expiry dates;<\/li><li><strong>conditions of class<\/strong> and recommendations with their due dates;<\/li><li>memoranda and exemptions.<\/li><\/ul><p>I use it to plan surveys and to check nothing is overdue.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★1.1","sec":0,"q":"You join and find two conditions of class outstanding and a PSC deficiency code 17 from the last port. What do you do?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Conditions of class<\/strong><\/p><p>A condition of class is a requirement from the classification society to repair or survey something by a set date. Under SOLAS II-1/3-1 the ship must be designed, built and maintained to class rules, and the RO issues statutory certificates on the flag&#x27;s behalf. So an overdue condition can lead to class suspension and the statutory certificates becoming invalid. The ship could then be detained.<\/p><p>I would:<\/p><ol><li>Read the class survey status report: what each condition is, the due date, and whether it&#x27;s a safety item, such as a hull defect, hatch cover or fire main.<\/li><li>Confirm with the Master and the superintendent the plan to clear each one: repair, spares ordered, which port the surveyor attends.<\/li><li>If the date can&#x27;t be met, the company asks class for an extension or postponement before the due date. Once it&#x27;s overdue, it&#x27;s too late.<\/li><li>Put any interim measures in place, for example extra inspections or restrictions, and record them.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>PSC deficiency code 17<\/strong><\/p><p>Under the Paris MoU and most other MoUs, code 17 means &quot;rectify the deficiency before departure&quot;. It&#x27;s one step below detention.<\/p><p>I would check:<\/p><ol><li>What the deficiency was, and was it actually fixed before we sailed?<\/li><li>Is there evidence: a rectification report, a class or service-company certificate, or the PSC officer&#x27;s acknowledgement?<\/li><li>If it wasn&#x27;t fixed and we sailed anyway, that&#x27;s serious. The ship may have sailed in breach, the next port will see it on the inspection database, and we&#x27;re likely to be targeted for inspection.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions for both<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Inform the Master and, through him, the company and the DPA.<\/li><li>Raise or update the ISM non-conformity with the root cause and corrective action, under ISM 9.<\/li><li>Assign the work through the PMS and track it.<\/li><li>Keep the evidence on board in a file for the next PSC officer, auditor or class surveyor.<\/li><li>On a UK ship, also make sure the MCA is aware where a statutory certificate is affected.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>To hear that I don&#x27;t just accept it. I find out the due dates, make sure there&#x27;s a plan, get extensions before they expire, and keep the evidence. A ship sailing with an unrectified code 17 or an overdue condition of class is a detention waiting to happen.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the difference between PSC action codes 16, 17 and 30?","pa":"<p>Paris MoU action codes:<\/p><ul><li><strong>16<\/strong>: rectify within <strong>14 days<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>17<\/strong>: the Master must rectify <strong>before departure<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>30<\/strong>: grounds for <strong>detention<\/strong>; the ship stays until it&#x27;s fixed.<\/li><\/ul><p>Code 10 means already rectified; code 15 means rectify at the next port.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★1.2","sec":0,"q":"Outgoing C/O leaves without a handover. What's the risk, and how do you protect yourself?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The risk<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I&#x27;m accepting responsibility for cargo, stability, certificates and maintenance I know nothing about.<\/li><li>I could miss a DG stowage problem, an overweight stack, a ballast tank not where the loadicator says, an overdue condition of class, a critical defect or an open non-conformity.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s a breach of the company&#x27;s ISM handover and familiarisation procedure, ISM 6.3, and the company&#x27;s duty under STCW I/14 to make sure I&#x27;m familiar with my duties.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Report it at once<\/strong> to the Master and, through him, to the company and the DPA. It&#x27;s an ISM non-conformity.<\/li><li><strong>Try to contact the outgoing C/O<\/strong> by phone or email for the key points: open issues, defects, anything unusual about the cargo.<\/li><li><strong>Do my own handover<\/strong> using the company checklist:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>stability: run the loadicator, check its test conditions, and read the draughts myself to compare;<\/li><li>cargo: bay plan, DG manifest and positions, reefers, lashing;<\/li><li>certificates and the class survey status report;<\/li><li>PMS: overdue items and critical equipment;<\/li><li>the last PSC, flag and audit reports, and open NCs;<\/li><li>keys, LSA and FFE, permits in force.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Walk the whole deck<\/strong>: hatch covers, lashing, moorings, LSA, FFE, gangway, security.<\/li><li><strong>Talk to the bosun and the 2/O and 3/O<\/strong>, who usually know what&#x27;s been going on.<\/li><li><strong>Document what I found<\/strong>: defects, missing records, discrepancies. Give it to the Master and enter it in my log. The Master may make an OLB entry.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Before sailing<\/strong><\/p><p>If anything I&#x27;ve found is unsafe for sailing, such as cargo, stability or certificates, I tell the Master before departure. He decides, but I&#x27;ve given him the information.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>This protects the ship first. It also protects me, because it records the condition I found things in when I took over, so I&#x27;m not blamed for something that happened before I joined. The examiner wants to hear: report it, do my own checks, document it, and tell the Master before sailing.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Would you sail if you couldn't verify the stability condition?","pa":"<p>No. I&#x27;d run the loadicator myself from actual soundings and the cargo list, and read the draughts. If I still couldn&#x27;t verify the condition, I&#x27;d tell the Master in writing that stability isn&#x27;t verified. The ship shouldn&#x27;t sail until it is (SOLAS II-1/5-1, Load Line Regs).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q12","sec":1,"q":"What is HSSC? Survey types and windows?","tier":"orange","rep":24,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>HSSC is the Harmonised System of Survey and Certification. In the UK it&#x27;s the Merchant Shipping (Survey and Certification) Regulations 2015 (SI 2015/508), with the detail in MSN 1751 (M). Internationally it came from the 1988 SOLAS and Load Line Protocols, in force 3 February 2000, and MARPOL adopted the same system.<\/p><p><strong>Why it exists<\/strong><\/p><p>Before HSSC each certificate had its own survey dates, so ships were constantly stopped for surveys. HSSC lines up the main certificates (SAFCON, SEC, SRC, Load Line, IOPP, IAPP, NLS, IBC/IGC) on one anniversary date and one 5-year cycle.<\/p><p><strong>Seven survey types<\/strong><\/p><p>Initial, renewal, periodical, intermediate, annual, inspection of the outside of the bottom, and additional.<\/p><p><strong>Which certificate gets which survey<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Annual:<\/strong> SAFCON, SEC, Load Line, IOPP, IAPP.<\/li><li><strong>Intermediate:<\/strong> SAFCON, IOPP, IAPP, in place of the 2nd or 3rd annual.<\/li><li><strong>Periodical:<\/strong> SEC, in place of the 2nd or 3rd annual; SRC, at every anniversary.<\/li><li><strong>Load line<\/strong> has annual surveys only.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The windows<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Annual and periodical: within 3 months either side of the anniversary date.<\/li><li>Intermediate: within 3 months either side of the 2nd or 3rd anniversary.<\/li><li>If a survey isn&#x27;t done in its window, the certificate ceases to be valid. Survey windows can&#x27;t be extended.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Renewal<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Done within 3 months before expiry: the new certificate runs 5 years from the old expiry date, so I don&#x27;t lose time.<\/li><li>Done more than 3 months before expiry: 5 years from the date the survey was completed.<\/li><li>Done after expiry, where an extension was granted: 5 years from the old expiry date.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Bottom inspections<\/strong><\/p><p>Two in any 5-year period, one at the renewal, with no more than 36 months between any two. The intermediate one may be an in-water survey if eligible.<\/p><p><strong>Validity and extensions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Maximum 5 years. The Passenger Ship Safety Certificate is 1 year.<\/li><li>Up to 3 months&#x27; extension to let the ship reach the port where she&#x27;ll be surveyed, or 1 month for ships on short voyages.<\/li><li>If the renewal survey is done but the new certificate can&#x27;t be issued or placed on board, the old one can be endorsed for up to 5 months.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>I&#x27;d show I know my ship&#x27;s anniversary date and track the windows on the class survey status report. A common mistake, which was in my own notes: load line has <strong>annual<\/strong> surveys and the radio certificate has <strong>periodical<\/strong> surveys, not the other way round.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"If the annual survey isn't done within the window, what happens to the certificate?","pa":"<p>It <strong>ceases to be valid<\/strong>. A survey window can&#x27;t be extended. The company tells the MCA or RO, the survey is done as soon as possible, and the ship doesn&#x27;t sail without the Administration&#x27;s authority. Otherwise PSC will treat the certificate as invalid and can detain the ship.<\/p>","asked":["What do you know about HSSC?","HSSC – explained MSN and general information.","What is HSSC, Certificates under HSSC, Explain in detail what annual, intermediate and periodically mean and the window for each including renewal?","HSSC, just general overview"],"ex":[["Shahid Akhter",5],["Donald Donaldson",3],["Timothy Cummins",3],["Doug Smith",2],["Ranjit Joseph",2]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q13","sec":1,"q":"Examples of when a certificate extension is requested?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Extensions are covered by SOLAS I/14 and the equivalent Load Line and MARPOL regulations, applied in the UK by the Survey and Certification Regs 2015 and MSN 1751 (M). An extension applies to the <strong>expiry date<\/strong> of a certificate at renewal. It does not extend an annual, intermediate or periodical survey window. Miss one of those and the certificate is invalid.<\/p><p><strong>Typical cases<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Not at the survey port.<\/strong> The certificate expires while the ship is on passage. The Administration can extend it up to <strong>3 months<\/strong>, only so the ship can complete the voyage to the port where she&#x27;ll be surveyed. She can&#x27;t leave that port again without a new certificate.<\/li><li><strong>Short-voyage ships.<\/strong> A ship on short voyages that hasn&#x27;t been extended under the first rule can get up to <strong>1 month<\/strong> grace from the expiry date.<\/li><li><strong>Renewal done, new certificate not available.<\/strong> For example, the surveyor has finished but class head office hasn&#x27;t issued the new certificate, or it can&#x27;t be got to the ship. The existing certificate is endorsed as valid for up to <strong>5 months<\/strong> from the expiry date.<\/li><li><strong>Dry dock delayed.<\/strong> No yard berth or the docking is postponed. The company may ask class and the MCA to postpone the bottom survey, or to accept an in-water survey if the ship is eligible.<\/li><li><strong>Conditions of class.<\/strong> Waiting for spares or a specialist, so the company asks class to postpone the due date before it expires.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How it&#x27;s done<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The company or Master applies to the MCA, or to the RO acting for it, <strong>before<\/strong> the expiry date.<\/li><li>The application gives the reason, the ship&#x27;s position and itinerary, the condition of the ship, and the plan and date for the survey.<\/li><li>The surveyor may want to inspect the ship, or see recent survey results, before agreeing.<\/li><li>The extension is endorsed on the certificate, and the new certificate, when issued, runs from the original expiry date so the ship doesn&#x27;t gain time.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I do as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>I track the dates on the survey status report and warn the Master and company months ahead. I prepare the ship and records so a surveyor can see she&#x27;s in good condition.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Approval is never automatic. The examiner wants: 3 months to the survey port, 1 month for short voyages, 5 months&#x27; endorsement, only for renewals, and apply before it expires.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"After a 3-month extension, from what date does the new certificate run?","pa":"<p>From the <strong>expiry date of the old certificate before the extension<\/strong> was granted (SOLAS I/14(e)). The extension doesn&#x27;t give the ship extra time on the new certificate.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q14","sec":1,"q":"Statutory vs non-statutory certificates? Which are \"non-statutory\"?","tier":"red","rep":70,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Statutory certificates<\/strong><\/p><p>These are required by law: the international conventions as applied in the UK by Merchant Shipping regulations. They&#x27;re issued by the MCA or by a Recognised Organisation acting for it. Without a valid one the ship can be detained by PSC or by the flag.<\/p><p>Examples:<\/p><ul><li>SOLAS: Cargo Ship Safety Construction, Safety Equipment and Safety Radio certificates, the DG Document of Compliance.<\/li><li>Load Line certificate.<\/li><li>MARPOL: IOPP, ISPP (sewage), IAPP and IEE certificates; ballast water and anti-fouling certificates.<\/li><li>ISM: the company&#x27;s DOC (copy on board) and the ship&#x27;s SMC. ISPS: the ISSC.<\/li><li>MLC certificate and DMLC.<\/li><li>Safe Manning Document, Certificate of Registry, International Tonnage Certificate.<\/li><li>The State insurance certificates for Bunkers, CLC and Wreck Removal, which the UK issues on the strength of the P&amp;I club&#x27;s blue card.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Non-statutory certificates<\/strong><\/p><p>These are not required by convention or UK law. They come from class, insurers, charterers or canal authorities:<\/p><ul><li>the class certificate itself;<\/li><li>the P&amp;I certificate of entry and the hull and machinery policy;<\/li><li>anchor and chain certificates, issued under class rules;<\/li><li>Suez and Panama Canal tonnage certificates;<\/li><li>charterer, terminal or vetting approvals.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the class certificate is the tricky one<\/strong><\/p><p>SOLAS II-1/3-1 requires the ship to be designed, built and maintained to the rules of a recognised classification society. So keeping class is in effect required, but the class certificate itself is a contract between the owner and class.<\/p><p><strong>Why &quot;non-statutory&quot; doesn&#x27;t mean optional<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If class is suspended, the statutory certificates issued by that RO are normally suspended as well.<\/li><li>If the P&amp;I cover lapses, the State insurance certificates are invalid, and the Compulsory Insurance Regs 2012 require insurance for ships of 300 GT and above in UK waters.<\/li><li>Without Suez or Panama certificates the ship can&#x27;t transit.<\/li><li>Without charterer approvals she can&#x27;t trade.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d say on board<\/strong><\/p><p>I keep both kinds in the certificate file and track all the dates on one list. A PSC officer looks at the statutory ones first, but will ask for the insurance and class status too.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Statutory means required by law and issued by or for the flag State. Non-statutory means required by contract or commerce. The examiner wants the class certificate example and the link between insurance and the blue-card certificates.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Which statutory certificates depend on the ship having insurance?","pa":"<p>The certificates that depend on insurance:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Bunkers Convention<\/strong> certificate, over 1,000 GT;<\/li><li><strong>Wreck Removal<\/strong> certificate, 300 GT and above;<\/li><li><strong>CLC<\/strong>, for tankers with more than 2,000 t of persistent oil;<\/li><li><strong>Athens<\/strong>, for passenger ships;<\/li><li>the two <strong>MLC financial security<\/strong> certificates, for repatriation/abandonment and for death/long-term disability;<\/li><li>proof of insurance under the <strong>Compulsory Insurance Regs 2012<\/strong>, 300 GT and above.<\/li><\/ul><p>All are valid only while the insurance is in force.<\/p>","asked":["All statutory certificates?","You are Master of a vessel less than 3000 GT. What all certificates will you check if you have or no?","What will be checked?","Certificates under SOLAS Convention?","List all certificates?"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",6],["Chris Roe",6],["Nayyer Naqvi",5],["Colum Leggett",5],["Michele Moro",5]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q15","sec":1,"q":"List the certificates on your last ship and their validity.","tier":"red","rep":68,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>I&#x27;d answer this for my own last ship. Here is the full list for a UK-flag container ship, with validity.<\/p><p><strong>Registration and tonnage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Certificate of British Registry: 5 years.<\/li><li>Continuous Synopsis Record (SOLAS XI-1/5): kept for the ship&#x27;s life, all documents on board.<\/li><li>International Tonnage Certificate (1969): no expiry unless the ship is altered or changes flag.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS and Load Line (5 years, HSSC)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Cargo Ship Safety Construction certificate: annual and intermediate surveys.<\/li><li>Cargo Ship Safety Equipment certificate, with Form E: annual and periodical surveys.<\/li><li>Cargo Ship Safety Radio certificate, with Form R: periodical surveys every year.<\/li><li>International Load Line certificate: annual surveys. With it, the Record of Conditions of Assignment.<\/li><li>Document of Compliance for dangerous goods (SOLAS II-2/19): normally aligned with SAFCON.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>MARPOL<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>IOPP with its supplement, ISPP (sewage) and IAPP: 5 years.<\/li><li>International Energy Efficiency certificate: for the ship&#x27;s life, unless major conversion.<\/li><li>International Anti-Fouling System certificate: no expiry, replaced if the system changes.<\/li><li>International Ballast Water Management certificate: 5 years.<\/li><li>Hong Kong Convention Inventory of Hazardous Materials certificate: 5 years.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Management and crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Company DOC copy, SMC and ISSC: 5 years, with intermediate audit or verification between the 2nd and 3rd anniversary.<\/li><li>Maritime Labour Certificate and DMLC Parts I and II: 5 years, intermediate inspection.<\/li><li>Safe Manning Document: no fixed expiry, reissued if trade, equipment or manning changes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Insurance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Bunkers Convention, Wreck Removal and, for tankers, CLC certificates: for the insurance period, normally to 20 February.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ship Sanitation certificate: 6 months.<\/li><li>Ship radio licence from Ofcom: revalidated periodically (verify, I believe every 10 years).<\/li><li>Class certificate: 5-year special survey cycle.<\/li><li>Non-statutory: P&amp;I certificate of entry, Suez and Panama tonnage certificates.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Approved documents<\/strong> (not certificates, but PSC asks)<\/p><p>Stability booklet and loading computer approval, Cargo Securing Manual, SOPEP, BWM plan, Garbage Management Plan, SSP, register of lifting appliances.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>I&#x27;d finish by saying &quot;and the class survey status report shows me every expiry date and survey window&quot;. If your last ship was Singapore flag, say so and mention that Singapore uses ROs for nearly all statutory certificates, but the list is the same because it comes from the conventions.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Which of these certificates has no expiry date?","pa":"<p>No expiry:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>International Tonnage Certificate<\/strong>, unless the ship is altered;<\/li><li>the <strong>IEE certificate<\/strong>, for the ship&#x27;s life;<\/li><li>the <strong>IAFS certificate<\/strong>, unless the antifouling changes;<\/li><li>the <strong>CSR<\/strong>, kept for life;<\/li><li><strong>DMLC Part I<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p>The SMD has no fixed expiry, but it&#x27;s reissued if the ship&#x27;s operation changes.<\/p>","asked":["Validity of the Load Line Certificate?","LOADLINE/SAFCON surveys, how will you prepare for these and what would you expect to be checked ?","What is a load line chart?","Isps And Loadline Audit Preparation.","What is type A AND type B?"],"ex":[["Khairul Anam",13],["Examiner not named",12],["Alec Keep",8],["Chris Roe",8],["Colum Leggett",4]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q16","sec":1,"q":"Which certificates must be displayed on the bridge?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>Very few certificates legally have to be displayed. Most must be <strong>carried on board and produced on demand<\/strong>. The examiner is checking that I know the difference.<\/p><p><strong>Certificates that must be posted<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MLC:<\/strong> a copy of the Maritime Labour Certificate and the DMLC must be posted in a conspicuous place on board, available to the seafarers (MLC A5.1.3.12).<\/li><li><strong>Load line:<\/strong> since the 2018 changes explained in MGN 579, failing to display the load line certificate is no longer an offence on a UK ship. It must still be carried and produced on demand.<\/li><li><strong>Safe Manning Document:<\/strong> often posted on the bridge as company practice. The legal requirement is to carry it (SOLAS V/14 and MSN 1868). I&#x27;d check whether the UK requires display (verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Plans and notices required on or near the bridge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Muster list<\/strong> in conspicuous places throughout the ship, including the bridge, engine room and crew accommodation (SOLAS III/8 and III/37).<\/li><li><strong>Fire control plan<\/strong> permanently exhibited, with a copy in a weathertight container outside the deckhouse for shore fire fighters (SOLAS II-2/15).<\/li><li><strong>Steering gear change-over<\/strong> procedures and block diagram, on the bridge and in the steering gear room (SOLAS V/26).<\/li><li><strong>Wheelhouse poster<\/strong> of manoeuvring characteristics, with the pilot card and manoeuvring booklet (IMO A.601(15)).<\/li><li><strong>Table of shipboard working arrangements<\/strong> (MLC A2.3 and the Hours of Work Regs 2018).<\/li><li><strong>Garbage placards<\/strong> for crew and passengers (MARPOL V/10.1), in the working language and English, French or Spanish.<\/li><li><strong>Operating instructions<\/strong> for LSA at or near the equipment, and lifesaving signals illustrated (SOLAS V/29 table).<\/li><li><strong>Oil pollution<\/strong> placard near overboard discharge points, and SOPEP contacts.<\/li><li><strong>Emergency instructions<\/strong> for each person on the cabin card.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Company practice, not a rule<\/strong><\/p><p>Many companies keep the radio licence, GMDSS shore-based maintenance record and the navigational warnings on the bridge, and post the company policy statements on safety, environment and drugs and alcohol. That&#x27;s ISM good practice, not a display rule.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The answer the examiner wants is: &quot;only the MLC certificate and DMLC have to be posted. The rest are carried and produced. What&#x27;s posted on the bridge is the muster list, fire control plan, steering change-over diagram, wheelhouse poster and working arrangements.&quot; Then mention the 2018 load line change, because examiners like to test it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the difference between \"displayed\" and \"carried on board\" for a PSC officer?","pa":"<p><strong>Displayed<\/strong> means posted where the crew can see it, for example the MLC certificate and DMLC copy, or the muster list. <strong>Carried<\/strong> means kept on board and produced on request. PSC checks the originals are on board and valid. A missing posting is a separate deficiency.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q17","sec":1,"q":"You lose the Certificate of Registry in a foreign port. Action?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Registration of UK ships is under the Merchant Shipping Act 1995 Part II and the Merchant Shipping (Registration of Ships) Regulations 1993, administered by the UK Ship Register in Cardiff. The Certificate of Registry is the proof of the ship&#x27;s nationality, so losing it in a foreign port can stop the ship being cleared.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Confirm it&#x27;s really lost.<\/strong> Search the bridge, ship&#x27;s office, Master&#x27;s cabin and the agent&#x27;s papers. It&#x27;s often in the agent&#x27;s file after clearance.<\/li><li><strong>If stolen<\/strong>, report it to the local police and get a written police report.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the company at once.<\/strong> The company, or the Master, applies to the UK Ship Register for a replacement, explaining how it was lost. The Register can issue a replacement and send it, or a certified copy, quickly.<\/li><li><strong>Contact the British consul<\/strong> through the agent if the port authorities need proof of nationality before the replacement arrives. The consul can confirm the ship&#x27;s British nationality and may help with an interim document (verify the procedure).<\/li><li><strong>Tell the port State authorities and the agent.<\/strong> A ship without proof of nationality may be delayed or detained, and clearance can be refused.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Evidence to use meanwhile<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A scanned copy of the certificate from the company.<\/li><li>The Continuous Synopsis Record, which shows the flag and registration details.<\/li><li>The other statutory certificates, all of which show the flag, official number and IMO number.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s marks: name and port of registry on the stern, and the IMO number.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An Official Log Book entry describing the loss and the actions taken.<\/li><li>Raise an ISM non-conformity. The cause is document control: who holds the original, who takes it ashore, where it&#x27;s kept.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the original turns up<\/strong><\/p><p>Return it to the Register, or deal with it as they direct. Only one valid certificate may exist, so the old one must not be kept on board alongside the new one.<\/p><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><p>Keep the originals in a secure file with a register of who has them. When the agent takes documents ashore, sign them out and back in. Keep scanned copies with the company.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Three things: search and confirm, tell the company and the Registry, and get the consul involved for proof of nationality, while recording it in the OLB. Remember the ship can&#x27;t clear without it, so this is urgent.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is the Certificate of Registry essential for clearance?","pa":"<p>It&#x27;s the ship&#x27;s proof of <strong>nationality and ownership<\/strong>. It shows she&#x27;s entitled to fly the flag and to the protection of that state. Customs and the port authority need it to clear the ship, and convention certificates depend on the flag.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q18","sec":1,"q":"What is BOPIC / CLC / Wreck Removal certificate?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>These are <strong>State certificates proving compulsory insurance<\/strong>. The flag State issues them on the strength of the P&amp;I club&#x27;s certificate of entry, the &quot;blue card&quot;. In the UK the MCA issues them.<\/p><p><strong>1. Bunkers Convention 2001 certificate (BOPIC)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Required for ships over <strong>1,000 GT<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Covers liability for pollution damage caused by the ship&#x27;s own bunker oil.<\/li><li>My container ship needs it, because a container ship can carry thousands of tonnes of heavy fuel.<\/li><li>The registered owner is liable, up to the LLMC limits, with direct action against the insurer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. CLC 1992 certificate<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Required for ships carrying more than <strong>2,000 tonnes of persistent oil as cargo<\/strong>, so tankers.<\/li><li>Covers oil pollution damage from the cargo. The 1992 Fund pays above the shipowner&#x27;s limit.<\/li><li>A container ship doesn&#x27;t need it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Nairobi Wreck Removal Convention 2007 certificate<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Required for ships of <strong>300 GT and above<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Covers the cost of locating, marking and removing a wreck that&#x27;s a hazard to navigation or the environment.<\/li><li>In the UK it&#x27;s given effect by the Wreck Removal Convention Act 2011.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Also in the UK<\/strong><\/p><p>The Merchant Shipping (Compulsory Insurance of Shipowners for Maritime Claims) Regulations 2012 (SI 2012/2267) require ships of 300 GT and above entering UK ports to carry insurance for maritime claims, and to have evidence of it on board. MLC also requires financial security for repatriation and abandonment (A2.5.2) and for death and long-term disability claims (A4.2.1), with the certificates posted on board.<\/p><p><strong>Validity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Valid only for the <strong>insurance period<\/strong> shown on the certificate, normally the P&amp;I policy year ending <strong>20 February<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the insurance lapses or the ship leaves the club, the certificate is invalid.<\/li><li>Carried on board and produced to PSC. Without it the ship can be detained or refused entry.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I check as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>Before 20 February each year I make sure the new blue cards have gone to the flag and the new State certificates are on board, or a copy is, before the old ones expire.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The three thresholds: Bunkers over 1,000 GT, CLC over 2,000 t of oil cargo, Wreck Removal 300 GT and above, and that they last only as long as the insurance. My old notes said &quot;CLC valid for life&quot;; that&#x27;s wrong.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who issues the certificate for a UK ship, the P&I club or the MCA?","pa":"<p>The <strong>MCA<\/strong> issues the state certificate. The <strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong> provides the &quot;blue card&quot; that proves the insurance.<\/p>","asked":["WRECK REMOVAL CERTIFICATE AND REQUIREMENT"],"ex":[["Tarik Yassin",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"Q19","sec":1,"q":"Ship Sanitation Certificate: law, validity, after 6 months?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The Ship Sanitation Certificate comes from the WHO <strong>International Health Regulations (IHR) 2005<\/strong>, Article 39 and Annex 3. In the UK, port health authorities apply it under the Public Health (Ships) Regulations 1979 as amended (verify the current UK SI).<\/p><p><strong>Two forms<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ship Sanitation Control Exemption Certificate (SSCEC):<\/strong> issued when the inspection finds no evidence of infection or contamination, including vectors such as rats and insects.<\/li><li><strong>Ship Sanitation Control Certificate (SSCC):<\/strong> issued when control measures were needed and applied, for example disinfection, de-ratting or disinsection.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Validity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>6 months.<\/strong><\/li><li>It can be <strong>extended by 1 month<\/strong> if the inspection or control measures can&#x27;t be done at that port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the inspector looks at<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Galley and pantry hygiene, food storage temperatures and stock rotation.<\/li><li>Potable water: tanks, treatment, testing records, hoses and connections.<\/li><li>Waste and sewage systems.<\/li><li>Medical locker and the medical log.<\/li><li>Accommodation hygiene.<\/li><li>Evidence of pests: rats, cockroaches, flies, and the pest control records.<\/li><li>Standing water, for example in lifeboats or containers, which can breed mosquitoes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After 6 months<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The certificate has expired. The ship should arrange an inspection at an authorised port, from the WHO list.<\/li><li>An expired certificate can lead to an inspection on arrival, control measures, delayed free pratique, and port State problems. In some ports the ship may be held until it&#x27;s renewed.<\/li><li>So I plan the renewal with the agent, before expiry, at a port that is authorised to issue it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Supporting documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Maritime Declaration of Health<\/strong>, sent before arrival.<\/li><li>Pest control, galley cleaning and potable water records.<\/li><li>Fresh water tank cleaning and chlorination records.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>I normally support this through the galley and provision stores inspections, fresh-water tank cleaning and chlorination, and the weekly Master&#x27;s inspection under MLC Reg 3.2 and A3.2.7. That&#x27;s exactly what the port health officer checks.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>IHR 2005, two types, 6 months plus a 1-month extension, and that I plan it with the agent before it runs out.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What will the port health inspector look at on board?","pa":"<p>The port health inspector looks at:<\/p><ul><li>the galley and provision stores, including cold-room temperatures;<\/li><li>the potable water system: tanks, chlorination and test records;<\/li><li>garbage and waste handling;<\/li><li>pest control, looking for signs of rodents or insects;<\/li><li>the medical locker and accommodation hygiene;<\/li><li>the <strong>Maritime Declaration of Health<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q20","sec":1,"q":"Is class mandatory? Why do ships stay in class? How does class help the C/O? What is the quarterly listing?","tier":"orange","rep":27,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Is class mandatory?<\/strong><\/p><p>Not directly, but in effect yes. SOLAS II-1/3-1 requires the ship to be designed, built and maintained to the structural, mechanical and electrical requirements of a classification society recognised by the Administration, or to an equivalent national standard. Almost every flag, including the UK, uses class rules as that standard. The UK also delegates most statutory surveys to its Recognised Organisations, which are class societies. If class lapses, the statutory certificates issued by that RO are suspended too.<\/p><p><strong>Why ships stay in class<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Insurers:<\/strong> hull and machinery underwriters and P&amp;I clubs require class. Loss of class usually means loss of cover.<\/li><li><strong>Charterers and cargo interests<\/strong> require it in the charter party.<\/li><li><strong>Banks<\/strong> require it as a condition of the mortgage.<\/li><li><strong>PSC targeting:<\/strong> the class society&#x27;s performance is part of the ship&#x27;s risk profile.<\/li><li><strong>Certification:<\/strong> it&#x27;s the practical route to statutory certificates.<\/li><li><strong>Resale value:<\/strong> a ship out of class is worth far less.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How class helps the C/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>survey status report<\/strong>: all surveys due and done, conditions of class, certificate expiry dates.<\/li><li>Conditions of class and memoranda, and what&#x27;s needed to clear them.<\/li><li>Approval of the stability booklet, loading computer, lashing software and Cargo Securing Manual, where the flag has delegated it.<\/li><li>Technical advice after damage, and emergency response services, for example damage stability and strength calculations after a grounding.<\/li><li>An attending surveyor for repairs, with their acceptance in writing.<\/li><li>Rules for anchors and chain, cargo gear and the register of lifting appliances.<\/li><li>Guidance on hull and tank inspections, and the ESP for bulk carriers and tankers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The quarterly listing<\/strong><\/p><p>That&#x27;s the class survey status report, issued periodically and on request. It lists:<\/p><ul><li>all class and statutory surveys done and due, with their windows;<\/li><li>certificate expiry dates;<\/li><li>conditions of class, with due dates;<\/li><li>memoranda or additional notes, which are information rather than requirements.<\/li><\/ul><p>I use it at handover, before PSC or flag inspections, before audits and when planning the dry dock.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>So the answer is: SOLAS II-1/3-1 makes class standards mandatory in practice; ships stay in class for insurance, charter, finance and certification; and the survey status report is my main tool for tracking what&#x27;s due.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What happens to your SAFCON if class is suspended?","pa":"<p>The RO-issued statutory certificates, such as SAFCON and Load Line, are normally <strong>suspended or withdrawn<\/strong>, because SOLAS II-1/3-1 needs the ship kept to class standards. The MCA is informed, and the ship can&#x27;t trade until class is restored.<\/p>","asked":["What is a condition of class?","Who are classification society's and what do they do?","What is condition of class and sight one example?","List the 6 MCA approved class societies"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",13],["Alec Keep",4],["Examiner not named",3],["Timothy Cummins",2],["Greig Laird",2]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q21","sec":1,"q":"What are ACS, EAS, ESP, CAS?","tier":"red","rep":71,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":"ACSS/EASS 2025","a":"<p><strong>First, the update.<\/strong> The old names are out of date. The UK schemes are now <strong>ACSS<\/strong> and <strong>EASS<\/strong>. ESP and CAS are IMO schemes, not MCA ones.<\/p><p><strong>ACSS: Alternative Compliance Safety System (MGN 568 (M) Amd 1)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For eligible UK ships, the MCA <strong>delegates<\/strong> the <strong>statutory surveys<\/strong> to an authorised <strong>RO<\/strong> (class): SAFCON, Load Line, IOPP, IAPP and, under ACSS, the <strong>Safety Equipment<\/strong> surveys too.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA keeps<\/strong> the <strong>ISM<\/strong> company DOC and ship SMC audits, the <strong>ISSC<\/strong> and the <strong>MLC<\/strong> inspections.<\/li><li>The ship gets the <strong>Alternative Compliance Safety Certificate (ACSC, MSF 1115)<\/strong>, formerly the Certificate of Inspection, which <strong>expires with the SMC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Entry<\/strong>: not passenger ships; <strong>no detention<\/strong> in the last <strong>36 months<\/strong>; no PSC inspection with more than <strong>5 deficiencies<\/strong> in the last 12 months; classed with a <strong>UK-authorised RO<\/strong>; and <strong>1 month&#x27;s notice<\/strong> of dockings. The application uses MSF 4753, then MSF 5100.<\/li><li><strong>Removal<\/strong>: an accident or pollution incident, conditions not cleared, expired certificates, a PSC <strong>detention<\/strong>, repeated deficiencies, or surveys not arranged.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>EASS: Enhanced Authorisation Safety System (MGN 561 (M) Amd 1, from 1 April 2025)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A step further, for eligible ships of <strong>500 GT<\/strong> and over with international certificates.<\/li><li>The RO also does the <strong>SMC<\/strong> audits, the <strong>ISSC<\/strong> verifications and the <strong>MLC<\/strong> inspections.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA keeps<\/strong> the <strong>company DOC<\/strong> audits, flag inspections, and exemptions.<\/li><li>Check the eligibility figures in the MGN before quoting them.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>ESP: Enhanced Survey Programme<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS XI-1/2<\/strong> and the <strong>2011 ESP Code<\/strong> (A.1049(27)), for <strong>bulk carriers<\/strong> and <strong>oil tankers<\/strong> (and chemical tankers under class rules).<\/li><li>Enhanced <strong>close-up surveys<\/strong> and <strong>thickness measurements<\/strong>, increasing with age, and a <strong>survey programme<\/strong> agreed with class before the renewal survey.<\/li><li>The survey report file is kept on board.<\/li><li>Not applicable to my container ship.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>CAS: Condition Assessment Scheme<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Under <strong>MARPOL Annex I<\/strong> (with MEPC.94(46)), for <strong>older single-hull oil tankers<\/strong> to keep trading during the phase-out.<\/li><li>An enhanced, documented inspection of the structure, beyond ESP.<\/li><li><strong>Largely historic<\/strong> now that single-hull tankers have been phased out.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the examiner wants to hear<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The current UK names and MGNs, what&#x27;s delegated under each, what the MCA keeps, and the ACSC.<\/li><li>That ESP and CAS are IMO schemes for bulkers and tankers.<\/li><li>My notes said &quot;MGN 668&quot; for ESP. Don&#x27;t quote it; ESP is SOLAS XI-1/2 and the ESP Code.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>ACSS under MGN 568 Amd 1 delegates statutory surveys including Safety Equipment to the RO, the MCA keeps ISM, ISPS and MLC, and the ship holds the ACSC; EASS under MGN 561 Amd 1 also delegates the SMC, ISSC and MLC; ESP is SOLAS XI-1/2 close-up surveys for bulkers and tankers; CAS is the historic MARPOL scheme for old single-hull tankers.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Which certificates does the MCA still issue itself for a ship on ACS?","pa":"<p>Under the <strong>ACSS<\/strong> (MGN 568 Amd 1), the MCA keeps the <strong>ISM DOC and SMC, the ISSC and the Maritime Labour Certificate<\/strong>. It issues the <strong>Alternative Compliance Safety Certificate<\/strong> (formerly the Certificate of Inspection), which expires with the SMC. Exemptions, equivalents and short-term certificates also stay with the MCA.<\/p>","asked":["What do you know about ACS & EAS?","Difference between ACSS & EASS?","What do you understand by ACS?","Under ACS what all certificates are issued by MCA?","What certificate is still done by MCA under EAS?"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",12],["Shahid Akhter",8],["Zahid Raza",8],["Doug Smith",7],["Siddhartha Paul",6]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q22","sec":1,"q":"What is an in-water survey? Which ships are not eligible?","tier":"orange","rep":30,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In-water surveys are allowed under the HSSC bottom inspection rules (SOLAS I and the Survey and Certification Regs 2015), class rules, and the MCA&#x27;s guidance on in-water surveys (MGN 546 in my notes, verify the number).<\/p><p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>An examination of the ship&#x27;s bottom and underwater appendages while she is afloat, instead of in dry dock. It&#x27;s done by approved divers, or an ROV, with live video and recordings, witnessed by the class or MCA surveyor.<\/p><p><strong>When it&#x27;s used<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For the statutory <strong>bottom inspection<\/strong> under HSSC: two in every 5 years, no more than 36 months apart. Normally the intermediate one can be an in-water survey; the one at renewal must be in dry dock.<\/li><li>For class bottom surveys where class rules allow, normally needing the notation &quot;IWS&quot; or equivalent.<\/li><li>To assess bottom damage after a grounding or contact, before deciding on dry dock.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Conditions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Normally only in place of the <strong>intermediate<\/strong> bottom survey.<\/li><li>The hull must be clean and the water clear enough to see. The divers must be from a firm approved by class.<\/li><li>The ship usually needs underwater markings for orientation, at frames and bulkheads.<\/li><li>Rudder and propeller clearances, rudder pintle and bearing wear, stern tube seals, sea chests and gratings, and anodes are checked.<\/li><li>The MCA or class is notified in advance with the plan.<\/li><li>If any damage or significant defect is found, the ship may have to dry-dock.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Not eligible<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Oil tankers and bulk carriers under the Enhanced Survey Programme that are over <strong>15 years<\/strong> old. They must dry-dock.<\/li><li>Ships with damage, or a condition of class needing the bottom repaired.<\/li><li>Fishing vessels: the MCA requires dry docking for them.<\/li><li>Passenger ships need a bottom inspection every year, with special arrangements for alternating dry dock and in-water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>As C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>I&#x27;d prepare the ship: trim and draught to suit the divers, stop the propeller and lock it out, isolate sea suctions near the divers, stop overboard discharges, hoist diving flag A, warn traffic, and record everything.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A container ship normally qualifies for the intermediate survey in water, which saves a docking. The examiner wants: in place of the intermediate only, approved divers witnessed by a surveyor, and ESP ships over 15 years not eligible.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How many bottom surveys in five years, and what's the maximum interval?","pa":"<p><strong>Two<\/strong> inspections of the outside of the bottom in any <strong>5-year<\/strong> period, with <strong>no more than 36 months<\/strong> between them.<\/p>","asked":["What is ESP which types of vessel has to undergo – what all will be checked during ESP ?","Do you know about the enhanced survey programme?","Tell me About ESP and applicable for which ships (asked about how inspections are carried out?","ESP survey for a bulk carrier; procedure and conductor.","ESP is required for Which Vessels ?"],"ex":[["Shahid Akhter",8],["Khademul Insan",8],["Zia Ul Haq",3],["Colum Leggett",3],["Chris Bates",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q23","sec":1,"q":"Describe Type A and Type B (B-60/B-100) ships and their flooding requirements.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Load Line Convention 1966 Annex I, Regulation 27, applied in the UK by the Merchant Shipping (Load Line) Regulations 1998 and MSN 1752 (M).<\/p><p><strong>Type A ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Designed to carry <strong>only liquid cargoes in bulk<\/strong>: tankers.<\/li><li>The cargo tanks have only small access openings, closed by watertight gasketed steel covers.<\/li><li>The cargo has low permeability when the tanks are full, and the ship has high subdivision.<\/li><li>So she gets the <strong>smallest freeboard<\/strong> from the Type A table.<\/li><li>A Type A ship over 150 m with a freeboard less than Type B must survive flooding of any compartment, with a permeability of 0.95. In longer ships the machinery space is also treated as a floodable compartment with permeability 0.85 (verify the length limit).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Type B ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Every ship that isn&#x27;t Type A<\/strong>: dry cargo, container, bulk carrier.<\/li><li>Large hatch openings, so a bigger tabular freeboard.<\/li><li>She can be given a reduced freeboard if she meets extra survival requirements.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>B-60<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Over 100 m, with steel weathertight hatch covers.<\/li><li>Freeboard can be reduced by up to <strong>60%<\/strong> of the difference between the Type A and Type B tables.<\/li><li>She must survive flooding of <strong>any single compartment<\/strong> (excluding the machinery space) at 0.95 permeability.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>B-100<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Must meet the B-60 requirements and also have extra protection: safe crew access to the accommodation and working areas (gangway or underdeck passage), and freeing arrangements.<\/li><li>Freeboard reduced by up to <strong>100%<\/strong> of the difference, so it can be as low as Type A.<\/li><li>Must survive flooding of <strong>any two adjacent fore-and-aft compartments<\/strong>, excluding the machinery space.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Machinery space<\/strong><\/p><p>On longer ships, the machinery space alone must also be assumed flooded at 0.85 (verify: I believe over 225 m).<\/p><p><strong>Equilibrium condition after flooding<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The final waterline must be below any openings through which further flooding could take place.<\/li><li>The angle of heel from unsymmetrical flooding not more than 15°, or 17° if no part of the deck is immersed.<\/li><li>Metacentric height in the flooded condition at least 50 mm.<\/li><li>Sufficient residual stability.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Damage extent<\/strong><\/p><p>Vertical from the baseline upwards without limit, and transverse B/5 or 11.5 m, whichever is less (verify, my old notes said &quot;greater&quot;).<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>To know that A is tankers with the smallest freeboard, B is everything else, and that B-60 and B-100 get reduced freeboards in return for surviving one and two compartments flooded. A bulk carrier with B-60 or B-100 also links to SOLAS XII damage stability.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What does \"satisfactory condition of equilibrium\" after flooding mean?","pa":"<p>After the assumed flooding the ship must float with:<\/p><ul><li>the final waterline below any opening through which progressive flooding could happen;<\/li><li>heel no more than about <strong>15°<\/strong> (17° if the deck edge isn&#x27;t immersed);<\/li><li>residual GM of at least <strong>50 mm<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a positive GZ range of at least <strong>20°<\/strong>, with max GZ at least <strong>0.1 m<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p>This is LL Annex I reg 27. Verify the figures before quoting them.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q24","sec":1,"q":"How do you prepare for a load line survey?","tier":"red","rep":99,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The Merchant Shipping (Load Line) Regulations 1998, MSN 1752 (M) and MGN 579 (M). The annual survey confirms that the hull and superstructure haven&#x27;t been altered in a way that affects the freeboard, and that the fittings and appliances for the protection of openings, guard rails, freeing ports and access are maintained (Load Line Convention Article 14(1)(c)).<\/p><p><strong>My checklist: the Record of Conditions of Assignment<\/strong><\/p><p>The ROCA is kept on board with the certificate. It lists every opening and fitting the freeboard was assigned on. I walk the deck with it and check each item.<\/p><p><strong>1. Hatch covers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Gaskets: no permanent set, cuts or missing sections, correct compression.<\/li><li>Compression bars straight and not corroded.<\/li><li>Cleats and wedges complete and working, cross-joint seals, drainage channels and non-return drains clear.<\/li><li>Hose test or ultrasonic test any doubtful seals.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Ventilators and air pipes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Coaming heights as on the ROCA.<\/li><li>Closing devices free and working.<\/li><li>Air pipe heads: floats or balls free, not corroded, flame screens intact.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Doors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Weathertight doors in superstructures and deckhouses: gaskets, dogs, hinges, sill heights.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Side scuttles, deadlights and cargo ports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Deadlights fitted and closing, glass intact.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Scuppers and overboard discharges<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Non-return valves working, positive closing where required.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Freeing ports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Flaps free, not blocked, not welded or wired shut, grids in place.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Crew protection<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Guard rails and bulwarks at least 1 m high, intact.<\/li><li>Lifelines, walkways and gangways where required for B-60 or B-100.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Marks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Deck line, load line marks and draught marks clearly painted and in the right place, checked against the certificate.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Load line certificate and ROCA.<\/li><li>Approved stability information on board.<\/li><li>PMS records for maintenance and repairs to closing appliances.<\/li><li>The deck log record of hatch cover and closing device checks.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before the surveyor arrives<\/strong><\/p><p>I put defects right, or if they can&#x27;t be fixed, I report them to the Master and the company so they can be discussed with the surveyor first. Hiding a defect is the worst thing I could do.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A defective hatch cover, vent or freeing port is a load line non-compliance and an offence, and can make the ship unseaworthy. The examiner wants to hear &quot;ROCA&quot;, the list of openings, and &quot;marks&quot;.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the purpose of the Record of Conditions of Assignment?","pa":"<p>It records how this ship meets each <strong>condition of assignment<\/strong>: hatches, doors, ventilators, air pipes, scuppers, freeing ports and guardrails, with heights and closing arrangements. The surveyor and crew use it to check the ship still matches the basis on which her freeboard was assigned. Any change needs approval.<\/p>","asked":["How would you prepare as Chief mate for a load line survey?","How often is a loadline survey carried out?","What is the condition of assignment?","How you will prepare a vessel for Load line survey in dry dock?","What is condition of assignment?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",11],["Nayyer Naqvi",10],["Khairul Anam",9],["James Gilbert",9],["Shahid Akhter",7]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q25","sec":1,"q":"Load line items affecting seaworthiness?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>Load line items affecting seaworthiness are anything that keeps the ship <strong>watertight or weathertight above the waterline<\/strong>, keeps her <strong>reserve buoyancy<\/strong>, or <strong>protects the crew<\/strong> on deck. They&#x27;re the conditions of assignment in the Load Line Convention Annex I, applied in the UK by the Load Line Regs 1998 and MSN 1752, with guidance in MGN 579. A defect in any of them is a non-compliance and may be an offence.<\/p><p><strong>1. Hatch covers<\/strong><\/p><p>Defective gaskets, compression bars or cleats. On a container ship that means water into holds full of cargo, cargo damage, and in bad weather possible flooding. Many losses of bulk carriers started at the forward hatch covers.<\/p><p><strong>2. Doors in superstructures and deckhouses<\/strong><\/p><p>Weathertight gaskets and dogs, and the sill heights.<\/p><p><strong>3. Ventilators and air pipes<\/strong><\/p><p>Closing devices and coaming heights. A seized float in an air pipe head can let sea water into a tank, or stop air escaping so the tank is over-pressurised during filling.<\/p><p><strong>4. Side scuttles and deadlights<\/strong><\/p><p>Broken glass or missing deadlights below the freeboard deck.<\/p><p><strong>5. Scuppers and overboard discharges<\/strong><\/p><p>Non-return valves working, so water can&#x27;t flood back.<\/p><p><strong>6. Freeing ports<\/strong><\/p><p>If blocked, they trap water on deck. That adds weight high up and a large free surface, reducing stability.<\/p><p><strong>7. Guard rails, bulwarks, walkways and lifelines<\/strong><\/p><p>They protect the crew working on deck in heavy weather.<\/p><p><strong>8. Overloading<\/strong><\/p><p>Submerging the appropriate load line is an offence under the Load Line Convention Article 12 and the UK Regs, after allowing for fresh water and the fuel used on the way to sea.<\/p><p><strong>9. Stability information<\/strong><\/p><p>Missing, unapproved or not followed. The freeboard assumes the ship meets the stability criteria.<\/p><p><strong>10. Marks<\/strong><\/p><p>Load line marks wrong or not visible.<\/p><p><strong>What I do if I find one<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Fix it before sailing, if possible.<\/li><li>If not, report it to the Master, the company and the RO, and get their agreement on temporary repairs or restrictions.<\/li><li>Record it in the PMS and the log.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t sail with an opening that can&#x27;t be closed.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A defect in any condition of assignment can make the ship unseaworthy and detainable, and the Master and owner liable under MSA 1995 s.98 for a dangerously unsafe ship. The examiner wants watertight integrity, reserve buoyancy and crew protection, with examples.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"You can't close one ventilator on No. 3 hold. Can you sail?","pa":"<p>No, not as she is. A defective ventilator closing appliance means a condition of assignment isn&#x27;t met, so the ship doesn&#x27;t comply with her Load Line Certificate. Repair it, or get the RO/MCA to accept a temporary arrangement, before sailing. Tell the Master and the company.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q26","sec":1,"q":"Which certificates are affected after grounding / hull breach / collision / major modification?","tier":"orange","rep":24,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>SOLAS I/11(c) requires that after an accident, or when a defect is found that affects the safety of the ship or the efficiency or completeness of its equipment, the Master or the company reports it at the earliest opportunity to the Administration or the RO that issued the certificate. If the ship is in a foreign port, the port State is told too. For a UK ship, a grounding or collision is also an MAIB report under the Accident Reporting and Investigation Regs 2026 and MGN 564 Amd 2.<\/p><p><strong>Certificates are not automatically invalid<\/strong><\/p><p>The surveyor decides whether an additional survey is needed, and whether the certificate stays valid, gets a condition, or is withdrawn.<\/p><p><strong>After a grounding, hull breach or collision, typically affected<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Class certificate:<\/strong> most likely a condition of class, with temporary repairs and a date for permanent repairs.<\/li><li><strong>Safety Construction certificate:<\/strong> hull, structure, machinery, watertight integrity.<\/li><li><strong>Load Line certificate:<\/strong> hull damage, closing appliances, watertight integrity.<\/li><li><strong>Safety Equipment certificate:<\/strong> if LSA or FFE was damaged or used.<\/li><li><strong>IOPP:<\/strong> if fuel tanks, oily-water systems or the double bottom were damaged, or there was pollution.<\/li><li><strong>Safety Radio:<\/strong> if aerials or equipment were damaged.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The SMC<\/strong><\/p><p>It isn&#x27;t automatically invalid. But the MCA may order an <strong>additional ISM audit<\/strong> to check the SMS worked and the corrective actions are effective, especially if the accident shows a failure of the system.<\/p><p><strong>After a major modification<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>SAFCON and Load Line: possibly a new freeboard, new ROCA.<\/li><li><strong>Tonnage:<\/strong> re-measurement and a new International Tonnage Certificate.<\/li><li><strong>Stability:<\/strong> a new inclining or lightship check, re-approval of the stability booklet, and new loading computer data.<\/li><li>IOPP, IAPP and the EEXI or IEE documents if the systems or engine change.<\/li><li>Safe Manning Document, if the operation or manning changes.<\/li><li>Cargo Securing Manual if lashing arrangements change.<\/li><li>Fire control plan and safety plans updated.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Sound all tanks and compartments, read the draughts, and give the Master the facts.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19) to close the boundaries of any flooded compartment, pump, and correct any list.<\/li><li>Damage stability and strength assessment, with class emergency response if the company has it.<\/li><li>Photographs and records for the surveyor, MAIB and insurers.<\/li><li>Track the surveyor&#x27;s conditions and the permanent repair dates.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS I/11(c), the MAIB, the specific certificates, and that the SMC is not automatically invalid but may need an additional audit. My old notes said &quot;SMC invalid after grounding&quot;; that&#x27;s wrong.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who decides whether an additional survey is needed?","pa":"<p>The <strong>Administration (MCA) or the RO<\/strong> acting for it, after the report required by <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["Which certificates of vessel will be affected ?","What all certificates will get affected?","What certificates will be affected because of grounding?","If GPS fails, what certificate will be affected and what actions should be taken?","Hull breach action, what are the parties to be informed, what certificates will be affected?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",3],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",3],["Michele Moro",3],["Shahid Akhter",2],["Sarah Lawlor",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q27","sec":1,"q":"Bilge suction not working — action, who to inform, which certificates?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>A bilge suction that doesn&#x27;t work means I can&#x27;t remove water from a space. If that space floods, I can&#x27;t control it. So it&#x27;s a serious defect affecting the ship&#x27;s safety.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Find the cause<\/strong> with the engine room: a blocked strum box or rose box, a stuck valve, a leaking pipe drawing air, a failed non-return valve, or the pump itself.<\/li><li><strong>Check alternatives:<\/strong> other suctions to the same space, another bilge pump, the general service or ballast pump, the emergency bilge suction.<\/li><li><strong>Sound the space<\/strong> and the neighbouring bilges and holds regularly, and record it.<\/li><li><strong>Rig portable or submersible pumps<\/strong> if needed, especially in a cargo hold.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong>, with the Master deciding whether it&#x27;s safe to continue loading or to sail.<\/li><li><strong>Record it<\/strong> in the deck and engine logs and the PMS.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Who to inform<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Master and, through him, the company and DPA.<\/li><li>Under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong> the defect must be reported to the Administration or the RO that issued the certificate, and to the port State if we&#x27;re in a foreign port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Certificates affected<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Cargo Ship Safety Construction certificate:<\/strong> bilge pumping arrangements are a SOLAS II-1 construction and machinery requirement (II-1/35-1).<\/li><li><strong>Class certificate:<\/strong> the surveyor may impose a condition of class with a time limit, or require repair before sailing.<\/li><li><strong>Not the SMC.<\/strong> I raise an ISM non-conformity under ISM 9 and analyse why the PMS didn&#x27;t catch it. The SMC stays valid.<\/li><li>If water in a hold could affect the cargo, for example a bulk cargo liable to liquefy, the hold bilges are even more critical.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Repair<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Clear the strum box, change the valve or repair the pipe.<\/li><li>Test the suction in front of the engineer and record it.<\/li><li>Show the repair to the surveyor if he&#x27;s attending.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><p>The ship doesn&#x27;t sail with a defective bilge system unless the RO or MCA surveyor accepts temporary measures in writing. If PSC finds it first, it&#x27;s likely a detainable deficiency.<\/p><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><p>Bilge suctions and alarms on the PMS, tested regularly, and strum boxes cleaned after every cargo, especially after bulk or dirty cargo.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Find the cause and the alternatives, tell the Master, company and RO under SOLAS I/11(c), SAFCON and class are affected, raise an NC, and don&#x27;t sail without the surveyor&#x27;s agreement.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the surveyor likely to accept as a temporary measure?","pa":"<p>Typically:<\/p><ul><li>portable or submersible pumps of enough capacity;<\/li><li>an approved alternative suction or cross-connection;<\/li><li>a closer sounding regime;<\/li><li>a short voyage to a repair port, with a <strong>condition of class<\/strong> and a deadline.<\/li><\/ul><p>It must be agreed in writing.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★2.1","sec":1,"q":"Your Safety Radio certificate's periodical survey window closed yesterday. Is the certificate valid?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>No, it isn&#x27;t valid.<\/strong> Under SOLAS I/14 and the Survey and Certification Regs 2015, with MSN 1751 (M), if an annual, intermediate or periodical survey isn&#x27;t completed within its window, the certificate ceases to be valid. For the Safety Radio certificate the periodical survey is every year, within <strong>3 months either side of the anniversary date<\/strong>. Unlike a renewal, a survey window <strong>cannot be extended<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Tell the Master immediately<\/strong>, and through him the company and DPA.<\/li><li><strong>The company contacts the MCA or the RO acting for it<\/strong> to arrange the survey at the earliest opportunity, at this port if possible, and asks what the ship may do in the meantime.<\/li><li><strong>Only the Administration can authorise movement<\/strong>, for example a single voyage to a port where a radio surveyor can attend. That authority must be in writing.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t sail on the strength of an invalid certificate<\/strong> without that written authority. PSC treats an invalid certificate as a detainable deficiency, and on a UK ship it&#x27;s an offence.<\/li><li><strong>Make sure the equipment works.<\/strong> The certificate is invalid on paper, but the GMDSS equipment must still be tested and working, with the shore-based maintenance record and the battery and EPIRB dates in order, ready for the surveyor.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the charterer and the agent<\/strong> of the possible delay.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Prepare for the survey<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Form R, the Record of Equipment, checked against the actual equipment.<\/li><li>GMDSS log and daily, weekly and monthly test records.<\/li><li>EPIRB annual test and shore-based maintenance record, SART and two-way VHF batteries.<\/li><li>Radio licence, operator certificates (GOC), and the reserve source of energy test.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Root cause<\/strong><\/p><p>Raise an <strong>ISM non-conformity<\/strong>: why did our certificate tracking and the class survey status report monitoring miss it? Usually it&#x27;s because someone assumed the survey was annual on the same window as the others, or the survey was booked but cancelled. Corrective action: a single tracking list, checked monthly by the Master or C/O and by the office.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The examiner is testing two things. First, that a missed survey window makes the certificate invalid, and it can&#x27;t be extended like a renewal. Second, that I don&#x27;t just sail and hope. I tell the company, get the survey arranged, and only move with the flag&#x27;s written authority.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the difference between an extension and an endorsement?","pa":"<p>An <strong>extension<\/strong> gives the certificate extra validity past its expiry: up to 3 months to reach a survey port, or 1 month for short voyages (SOLAS I/14). An <strong>endorsement<\/strong> is the surveyor&#x27;s signature recording a survey done, or the up-to-5-month endorsement when the renewal survey is complete but the new certificate isn&#x27;t on board yet.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★2.2","sec":1,"q":"What is MIN 653 and what's the difference between an MSN, MGN and MIN?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>MIN 653 (M) Amendment 1<\/strong> is the MCA&#x27;s Deck Oral Examination Syllabus. It sets out what&#x27;s examined at each deck CoC level, including my Chief Mate Unlimited oral, which is built on STCW A-II/2. It&#x27;s read with <strong>MSN 1856 (M+F) Amd 1<\/strong>, which sets the UK requirements for deck officers&#x27; certificates, and <strong>MIN 690 Amd 6<\/strong>, which covers booking, the Notice of Eligibility and the online exam process. MIN 653 Amd 1 has been extended to <strong>1 May 2027<\/strong>, and booking moved online on <strong>9 March 2026<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The three types of M Notice<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>MSN: Merchant Shipping Notice<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Carries <strong>mandatory<\/strong> technical detail that a Statutory Instrument refers to.<\/li><li>Because the SI calls it up, it has the force of law. Breaching it can be an offence.<\/li><li>Examples: MSN 1751 for survey and certification, MSN 1877 for hours of rest, MSN 1781 for the COLREGs, MSN 1676 for LSA.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>MGN: Marine Guidance Note<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Guidance<\/strong> and good practice on how to comply with the law.<\/li><li>Not law in itself, but an inspector, the MAIB or a court will expect you to have followed it, or to have done something at least as good.<\/li><li>Examples: MGN 579 on load line, MGN 564 on accident reporting, MGN 659 on enclosed spaces, MGN 610 on SOLAS V.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>MIN: Marine Information Note<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Information<\/strong> for a specific audience: exam arrangements, training, administrative changes, new codes being adopted.<\/li><li>Time-limited, with an <strong>expiry date<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Examples: MIN 653 (syllabus), MIN 690 (exams), MIN 688 (COSWP 2026 edition).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Suffixes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>(M) merchant ships, (F) fishing vessels, (M+F) both.<\/li><li>&quot;Amd&quot; shows the amendment number, so I always quote the latest one.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters in the exam<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Examiners expect UK references first: &quot;MS (Hours of Work) Regs 2018 and MSN 1877&quot;, then the convention behind it.<\/li><li>An MSN quoted as guidance, or an MGN quoted as law, shows I don&#x27;t understand the system.<\/li><li>Superseded numbers, like MSN 1832 for PSC, now MGN 711, or MGN 458, now MGN 564, are a common slip.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The one-line answer: &quot;MSN is mandatory, MGN is guidance, MIN is time-limited information. MIN 653 is the syllabus I&#x27;m being examined on.&quot;<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Give me an MSN that's part of the law and the SI it supports.","pa":"<p><strong>MSN 1751<\/strong>, which carries the detail for the <strong>MS (Survey and Certification) Regulations 2015<\/strong>. Another is <strong>MSN 1752<\/strong> for the <strong>MS (Load Line) Regulations 1998<\/strong>. Because the SI refers to the MSN, the MSN has the force of law.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★2.3","sec":1,"q":"What's an ambulatory reference?\" (MGN 579)","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>An <strong>ambulatory reference<\/strong> is when a UK Statutory Instrument refers to an international instrument <strong>&quot;as amended from time to time&quot;<\/strong>. When IMO amends the convention or code, the amendment takes effect in UK law automatically on its international in-force date, without a new UK SI each time.<\/p><p><strong>The load line example: MGN 579 (M)<\/strong><\/p><p>MGN 579 explains the 2018 changes to the UK load line regulations. The 2018 amendment regulations introduced ambulatory referencing for the Load Line Convention. The same update:<\/p><ul><li>removed the offence of not <strong>displaying<\/strong> the load line certificate and the draught and freeboard notice, although the certificate must still be carried and produced;<\/li><li>brought the UK into line with the 2008 IS Code for ships built on or after 1 July 2010;<\/li><li>updated the regulations to reflect the 1988 Protocol and its later amendments.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters to me as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>I have to follow the <strong>current amended text<\/strong> of the convention, not the wording printed in an old SI.<\/li><li>Books and notes quoting old SI wording can be out of date. The MCA now tells us about changes by M Notices.<\/li><li>When an IMO amendment comes into force, for example a new SOLAS regulation on 1 January, it applies to my UK ship from that date, even if nobody has written a new SI.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The opposite: a static reference<\/strong><\/p><p>A static reference fixes a particular version of the instrument, for example &quot;SOLAS as it stood on 1 January 2010&quot;. A later amendment then needs a new SI before it takes effect in the UK. That was common in older UK regulations and is one reason UK law sometimes lagged behind IMO.<\/p><p><strong>Where else the UK uses it<\/strong><\/p><p>Most modern UK Merchant Shipping regulations now use ambulatory references, for example the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020 for SOLAS V, the Fire Protection Regs 2023, the LSA Regs 2020 and the ISM Regs 2026. That&#x27;s how a new SOLAS amendment, like the lost-container reporting in V/31 and V/32 from 1 January 2026, applies to UK ships straight away.<\/p><p><strong>The limit<\/strong><\/p><p>The UK can still choose not to accept an amendment, or can make a reservation, and anything outside the convention, such as UK-only penalties, still needs an SI.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>&quot;as amended from time to time&quot;, automatic effect from the IMO in-force date, and the 2018 load line changes in MGN 579, especially that display is no longer an offence.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why might the UK still choose not to apply an IMO amendment automatically?","pa":"<p>Several reasons:<\/p><ul><li>to allow parliamentary scrutiny;<\/li><li>the amendment may need UK-specific changes, exemptions or a different application date;<\/li><li>it may conflict with other UK law;<\/li><li>the UK may object to it under tacit acceptance;<\/li><li>its cost or impact needs assessing first.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q28","sec":2,"q":"Which publication guides OLB entries? Who makes page 1 entries? When is a new OLB opened, and when is it sent to the RSS?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The law is the <strong>Merchant Shipping (Official Log Books) Regulations 1981<\/strong>, as amended. They apply to UK ships, except ships under 25 GT and pleasure vessels; fishing vessels have their own regulations. The practical guidance is the MCA&#x27;s <strong>&quot;A Master&#x27;s Guide to the UK Flag&quot;<\/strong> and the instructions printed inside the front of the log book itself.<\/p><p><strong>Who makes the page 1 (front) entries<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>Master<\/strong>. They cover:<\/p><ul><li>the ship&#x27;s particulars: name, official number, port of registry, gross tonnage;<\/li><li>the name and address of the owner or manager;<\/li><li>the Master&#x27;s name and CoC number;<\/li><li>the date and place the log was opened;<\/li><li>later, each change of Master, with the date and place.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Signatures on entries generally<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Each entry is signed by the <strong>Master and one other member of the crew<\/strong>. Without both, the entry may not be valid as evidence.<\/li><li>Some entries name who the second signatory must be. For example, steering gear tests and drills are signed by the Master and an officer; a birth is witnessed by the mother; a death by a member of the crew.<\/li><li>Entries are made <strong>as soon as practicable<\/strong> after the event.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When a new OLB is opened<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An OLB normally covers <strong>6 months<\/strong> from the first entry. It&#x27;s closed at the end of that period, or earlier if the ship is sold, transferred to another flag, laid up, or the crew agreement ends and all crew are discharged.<\/li><li>A new OLB is opened straight away, so there&#x27;s no gap.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When it&#x27;s sent to the RSS<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The closed log goes to the Registry of Shipping and Seamen (the UK Ship Register) within <strong>48 hours<\/strong> of the ship&#x27;s arrival at the first port after the 6 months, or after the last crew member is discharged if that&#x27;s earlier.<\/li><li>In practice the Master sends it with the crew agreement and any returns of births and deaths.<\/li><li>A copy is kept by the company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>The OLB is a statutory document, admissible in a UK court as evidence of what it records. A missing, late or badly kept OLB is an offence and a PSC or flag inspection finding.<\/p><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>I often prepare the entries for the Master: drills, LSA and FFE inspections, draught and freeboard, accommodation and provisions inspections. I make sure the second signature is from the right person and that the entries are made at the time.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>OLB Regs 1981, the Master makes page 1 entries, Master plus one crew member sign each entry, 6 months, and to the RSS within 48 hours of arrival after the 6 months.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who signs an entry recording a death, and who signs one for a birth?","pa":"<p>Both entries are made by the <strong>Master in person<\/strong>. A <strong>birth<\/strong> is witnessed by the <strong>mother<\/strong>. A <strong>death<\/strong> is witnessed by a <strong>member of the crew<\/strong>, and the cause of death is entered by the ship&#x27;s doctor, or by the Master if there&#x27;s no doctor. (OLB Regs 1981, Schedule paras 34–35.)<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q29","sec":2,"q":"How do you correct a wrong OLB entry?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The Merchant Shipping (Official Log Books) Regulations 1981, with the instructions in the log book and the Master&#x27;s Guide to the UK Flag.<\/p><p><strong>The rule: never alter the original<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>No erasing, no correction fluid, no overwriting, no scribbling out, and never tear a page out.<\/li><li>Altering, defacing or destroying an OLB entry is an <strong>offence<\/strong> under the regulations. It also destroys the log&#x27;s value as evidence, because the court can no longer trust any entry in it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How to correct it<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Leave the wrong entry exactly as it is, readable.<\/li><li>Make a <strong>new entry<\/strong> at the next available space, with the date and time.<\/li><li>Refer clearly to the wrong entry, for example: &quot;Reference entry dated 12/10/26 at 1430: for &#x27;J. Smith, AB&#x27; read &#x27;J. Jones, AB&#x27;.&quot;<\/li><li>Say why, if useful, for example &quot;name entered in error&quot;.<\/li><li>The correcting entry is <strong>signed in the same way as the original<\/strong>: the Master and the same category of crew member. For a steering gear entry that means the Master and an officer.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it&#x27;s done this way<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The history stays visible. A court, the MAIB or an inspector can see what was written originally, when it was corrected, and by whom.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s what keeps the OLB <strong>admissible<\/strong> as evidence without further proof.<\/li><li>If the change looks like an attempt to hide something, for example a changed time of a drill or an injury, the honest correction protects the Master and the officers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Deck log is the same principle<\/strong><\/p><p>The deck log is a company document, not the OLB, but I correct it the same way: a single line through the wrong word so it can still be read, the correction alongside, and the initials of the officer and the date. Never erase. After an accident the MAIB and lawyers look first for altered logs, and a suspicious alteration can undermine the whole defence.<\/p><p><strong>What I tell junior officers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Write in ink, at the time.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t copy-paste or write up the log hours later from memory.<\/li><li>If you&#x27;ve made a mistake, tell me and correct it properly. A visible correction is normal. A hidden one is a disciplinary matter and possibly a crime.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Never alter, erase or remove; make a new dated entry referring to the old one; signed by the Master and a crew member; and why: so the record remains evidence.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is the OLB given more weight in court than the deck log?","pa":"<p>The OLB is a <strong>statutory document<\/strong>, admissible in court <strong>without further proof<\/strong>. The deck log is a company record. It has to be produced with a sworn statement (affidavit), and its maker can be cross-examined.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q30","sec":2,"q":"OLB vs deck log?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The Official Log Book (OLB)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>statutory document<\/strong> under the Merchant Shipping (Official Log Books) Regulations 1981, issued by the MCA.<\/li><li>It records the specific matters in the <strong>Schedule<\/strong> to the regulations: crew and changes of Master, drills and musters, inspections of LSA and FFE, steering gear tests, draught and freeboard on sailing, births and deaths, illness and injury, discipline, seafarers left behind, deceased seafarers&#x27; property, and so on.<\/li><li>Entries are signed by the <strong>Master and a member of the crew<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>admissible in court as evidence<\/strong> of what it records, without anyone having to come and prove it.<\/li><li>It goes to the <strong>RSS<\/strong> when it&#x27;s closed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The deck (bridge) log<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>company and SMS document<\/strong>, not prescribed by statute in its format.<\/li><li>A working, time-by-time record of navigation: courses, positions, weather, engine orders, events, cargo and ballast operations, watch handovers, pilot on and off, anchoring and mooring.<\/li><li>It supports the SOLAS V/28 requirement to keep records of navigational activities and daily reporting.<\/li><li>In court it&#x27;s <strong>not admissible on its own<\/strong>. It has to be produced through the person who made or kept it, usually exhibited to a witness statement or affidavit, and they can be cross-examined on it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How they connect<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Many OLB entries are <strong>copied from the deck log<\/strong>: drill times, steering gear tests, the draught on departure.<\/li><li>If the deck log entry is discredited in cross-examination, for example it was altered or written up later, the OLB entry copied from it becomes unsafe as well.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other records that work the same way<\/strong><\/p><p>The engine log, the GMDSS log, the Oil and Garbage Record Books (which are statutory under MARPOL), the ballast water record book, and the ECDIS and VDR data. After an accident they&#x27;re all compared with each other. Differences destroy credibility.<\/p><p><strong>Good practice<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Both logs are written <strong>at the time<\/strong>, accurately, in ink.<\/li><li>Never altered: corrections go in as new entries, or a single line through, initialled and dated.<\/li><li>Kept for the period the company and law require, and preserved after an accident. Never &quot;tidied up&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The OLB is statutory, Schedule entries, signed by Master and crew, admissible without further proof; the deck log is a company record, needs a witness to produce it, but is the source for many OLB entries. So both must be honest and contemporaneous.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you correct a mistake in the deck log?","pa":"<p>Follow the SMS. Never erase or obliterate. Strike through once so the original stays readable, initial and date it, and write the correct entry, or make a correcting entry below. The original must stay visible.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q31","sec":2,"q":"List all OLB entries.","tier":"red","rep":49,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The full list is in the <strong>Schedule to the Merchant Shipping (Official Log Books) Regulations 1981<\/strong>, with guidance in the Master&#x27;s Guide to the UK Flag and the instructions printed in the log. Each entry is signed by the Master and a member of the crew. I&#x27;d group them like this.<\/p><p><strong>1. Ship and crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ship&#x27;s particulars, the Master&#x27;s details, the date the log was opened (page 1, by the Master).<\/li><li>Changes of Master.<\/li><li>Crew agreement entries: engagement and discharge, crew list changes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Seaworthiness and safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Draught and freeboard<\/strong> before proceeding to sea, and the density of the water. The Master makes the entry and an officer witnesses it.<\/li><li><strong>Musters and drills<\/strong>: abandon ship and fire drills, boat launching, damage control drills, and the reasons if any weren&#x27;t held.<\/li><li><strong>Inspections of LSA and FFE<\/strong>, and their condition.<\/li><li><strong>Steering gear<\/strong> tests within 12 hours before departure and the emergency steering drills every 3 months, signed by the Master and an officer.<\/li><li><strong>Watertight doors<\/strong> and other openings: closing and drills, where applicable.<\/li><li><strong>Hatches and openings closed<\/strong> before sailing, where required.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Living conditions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Accommodation inspections<\/strong> by the Master.<\/li><li><strong>Provisions and water inspections<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Complaints about food and water, and what was done.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Health and safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Appointment of the <strong>safety officer<\/strong> and election of <strong>safety representatives<\/strong>, and the safety committee.<\/li><li><strong>Illness and injury<\/strong>, and the treatment given.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Births and deaths<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Both entries are made by the <strong>Master in person<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>birth<\/strong> is witnessed by the <strong>mother<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>death<\/strong> is witnessed by a <strong>member of the crew<\/strong>, and the <strong>cause of death<\/strong> is entered by the ship&#x27;s doctor, or by the Master if there&#x27;s no doctor.<\/li><li>OLB Regs 1981 Schedule paras 34–35.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Discipline and conduct<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Misconduct, offences and fines under the company&#x27;s Code of Conduct, with the seafarer&#x27;s reply read to him and recorded.<\/li><li>Seafarers <strong>left behind<\/strong>, failing to join, or deserting.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Property and wages<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Deceased seafarers&#x27; property<\/strong> and wages, inventoried and witnessed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Other<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Collisions, accidents and <strong>distress messages<\/strong> received and the action taken, where required.<\/li><li>Any other matter the regulations require, or that the Master considers should be recorded.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>I wouldn&#x27;t try to recite all of them. The examiner wants the categories and a few with their signatures: draught and freeboard, drills, steering gear, births and deaths, discipline, and the right second signatory. My old notes said &quot;the mother signs a birth entry&quot;; the correct position is the Master makes it and the mother witnesses it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What exactly goes in the draught and freeboard entry?","pa":"<p>The entry records:<\/p><ul><li>the draughts forward and aft, and the mean;<\/li><li>the freeboard;<\/li><li>the density of the dock water;<\/li><li>the load line mark that applies (zone/season);<\/li><li>any fresh water or river-consumption allowance;<\/li><li>the date, time and place.<\/li><\/ul><p>The Master enters it and an officer witnesses it.<\/p>","asked":["What Is in the Official Logbook ?","What are your responsibilities as Chief Officer?","What are the contents of the Official Log book?","Would this be recorded in the Official Log Book?","What entries would you make in the Official Logbook?"],"ex":[["Khairul Anam",15],["Examiner not named",6],["Byron Griffiths",5],["Nayyer Naqvi",4],["Colum Leggett",4]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q32","sec":2,"q":"Who must report to the MAIB, and what?","tier":"red","rep":34,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":"Incomplete MAIB list — fail, May 2022","law":"MAIB Regs 2026","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Merchant Shipping (Accident Reporting and Investigation) Regulations 2026<\/strong> (SI 2026/430), in force 15 May 2026, replacing the 2012 Regs. The guidance is <strong>MGN 564 (M+F) Amendment 2<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Who must report<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master<\/strong>, or if he can&#x27;t, the senior surviving officer.<\/li><li>The <strong>owner or company<\/strong>, which has the same duty, so we don&#x27;t rely on each other.<\/li><li>Also, for a harbour, the harbour authority.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Which ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>UK ships anywhere<\/strong> in the world.<\/li><li><strong>Any ship in UK territorial waters<\/strong>, and non-UK ships within a UK harbour authority&#x27;s area or carrying passengers to or from a UK port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What must be reported<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Marine casualties<\/strong>: an event connected with the operation of the ship resulting in death or <strong>serious injury<\/strong>, a person lost overboard, the loss or abandonment of a ship, material damage to a ship, grounding, collision, disablement, fire or explosion, pollution, or damage to the environment.<\/li><li><strong>Marine incidents<\/strong>: events which, if not corrected, would endanger the ship or people or the environment. That includes near misses the MAIB wants to know about.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Serious injury<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Regs: a person <strong>unable to undertake their full range of activities for more than 72 hours<\/strong>, starting within 7 days of the injury.<\/li><li>MGN 564 Amd 2: unable to perform their <strong>usual duties for more than 72 hours<\/strong>, or <strong>in hospital for more than 24 hours<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That replaced the old &quot;major injury&quot; list.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Not reportable<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Deliberate acts<\/strong> intended to cause harm: assault, suicide, homicide.<\/li><li>Equipment defects and <strong>PSC detentions<\/strong> on their own, not linked to a casualty.<\/li><li>Passenger injuries not connected with the ship&#x27;s operation.<\/li><li>Shore incidents not involving the ship&#x27;s equipment.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How and when<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>As soon as practicable, by the quickest means available<\/strong>: the MAIB 24-hour line, <strong>+44 (0)23 8023 2527<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>Accident Report Form<\/strong> online, by email or post.<\/li><li>MGN 564 says notification <strong>must not be delayed until the company&#x27;s internal investigation is finished<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Preserve evidence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Save the VDR<\/strong> data immediately.<\/li><li>Keep the ECDIS, logs, charts, records, damaged equipment and CCTV.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t alter or tidy anything.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The MAIB&#x27;s role<\/strong><\/p><p>It investigates to prevent recurrence. It does <strong>not apportion blame or liability<\/strong>.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 2026 Regs and MGN 564 Amd 2, the Master and the company, the 72-hour and 24-hour tests, the exclusions, the 24-hour line, and saving the VDR. My old notes used the 2012 Regs and a &quot;major injury&quot; list; both are out of date.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Is a PSC detention on its own reportable to the MAIB?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> The 2026 Regulations and MGN 564 Amd 2 exclude detentions and defects that aren&#x27;t linked to a casualty. Report the detention to the company/DPA, the flag (MCA) and class, and raise an ISM non-conformity.<\/p>","asked":["What is the role of the MAIB?","How will you report to MAIB?","You mentioned about MAIB. What is it & how will you report?","MAIB and its purpose.","What are the reports to be sent to MAIB?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",12],["Examiner not named",5],["Inamul Bhuiyan",4],["Khairul Anam",4],["Zia Ul Haq",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q33","sec":2,"q":"Detention vs improvement vs prohibition notice?","tier":"yellow","rep":9,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>All three come from the <strong>Merchant Shipping Act 1995<\/strong>. They&#x27;re powers of MCA inspectors, used on UK ships and on foreign ships in UK waters.<\/p><p><strong>Improvement notice: MSA s.261<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Issued when an inspector believes a person is breaching the regulations and is likely to continue or repeat the breach.<\/li><li>It states the breach and the reasons, and gives a period to put it right, normally <strong>not less than 21 days<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ship carries on operating while it&#x27;s fixed.<\/li><li>Example: rest-hour records not kept properly, or a risk assessment missing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prohibition notice: MSA s.262<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Issued when an activity on board involves, or will involve, a <strong>risk of serious personal injury or serious pollution<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It <strong>stops that activity<\/strong> until the matter is put right.<\/li><li>It can take effect <strong>immediately<\/strong>, or at the end of a stated period.<\/li><li>It targets an activity, not the whole ship. Example: no work on deck aloft until fall protection is provided, or no enclosed space entry until gas detectors are calibrated.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Appeal: MSA s.264<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Either notice can be referred to <strong>arbitration within 21 days<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That <strong>suspends an improvement notice<\/strong>, but <strong>not a prohibition notice<\/strong> unless the arbitrator directs.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Detention: MSA s.95<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Used when the ship is <strong>dangerously unsafe<\/strong>: unfit to go to sea without serious danger to people, because of her condition, equipment, manning, overloading or cargo.<\/li><li>The <strong>whole ship<\/strong> is detained until it&#x27;s put right.<\/li><li><strong>s.96<\/strong>: the owner can refer it to <strong>arbitration within 21 days<\/strong>, by a single arbitrator. That doesn&#x27;t lift the detention unless the arbitrator directs.<\/li><li><strong>s.97<\/strong>: compensation if the detention had no reasonable grounds.<\/li><li><strong>s.98<\/strong>: the owner and Master commit an offence if a dangerously unsafe ship goes to sea.<\/li><li><strong>s.284<\/strong>: the enforcement power used to detain the ship physically.<\/li><li>For a foreign ship, PSC detention is under the PSC Regs 2026 and the Paris MoU, with guidance in MGN 711.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The difference in one line<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Improvement notice: <strong>fix it<\/strong> within a time, keep working.<\/li><li>Prohibition notice: <strong>stop that activity<\/strong> now.<\/li><li>Detention: <strong>the ship doesn&#x27;t sail<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do as C/O on receiving one<\/strong><\/p><p>Inform the Master and company, read the notice carefully, stop the activity if it&#x27;s a prohibition, plan the corrective action, record it in the logs, raise an ISM non-conformity, and keep evidence for any appeal.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The section numbers, what each stops, and the 21-day arbitration with what it suspends. My old notes said detention was &quot;s.284&quot;; the power to detain is <strong>s.95<\/strong>.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A prohibition notice is served on your enclosed-space entry. Can you appeal and carry on?","pa":"<p>You can appeal to <strong>arbitration within 21 days<\/strong> (MSA s.264), but a prohibition notice <strong>stays in force<\/strong> unless the arbitrator directs otherwise. So there&#x27;s no entry until the matters in the notice are put right.<\/p>","asked":["What is Prohibition Notice?","You are a Chief officer on board a UK Flag vessel and OOW reports that some authorities notice work being done on deck and issue prohibition notice. What are your actions?","What is an improvement notice","DETENTION (Cannot sail until approved to by the MCA)","What is PSC (prepare for PSC inspection, what is detention, Ban, High/standard/low risk ships)"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",2],["Chris Roe",2],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",1],["Timothy Cummins",1],["Steve Meaking",1]],"yrs":"2022–2024"},{"id":"Q34","sec":2,"q":"Someone dies on board — procedure?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>First aid and resuscitation<\/strong>, continuing until a doctor advises otherwise. Get <strong>TMAS<\/strong> advice; for a UK ship that&#x27;s through HM Coastguard (MGN 623).<\/li><li>Record the <strong>time and circumstances<\/strong> of death, and the treatment given.<\/li><li><strong>Preserve the scene<\/strong> if the cause is unclear. Photograph it, close off the area, keep evidence.<\/li><li><strong>Look after the body<\/strong> with dignity: in a body bag, in cold storage away from food stores, with the advice of TMAS.<\/li><li><strong>Secure the person&#x27;s property<\/strong>: inventoried by two people, witnessed, sealed and listed.<\/li><li>Consider the effect on the crew: who was close to him, who found him.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reporting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Official Log Book<\/strong> entry, made by the <strong>Master in person<\/strong> and witnessed by a member of the crew, with the cause of death entered by the doctor or the Master.<\/li><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>, the <strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>, the <strong>agent<\/strong> at the next port, and the <strong>next of kin<\/strong> through the company. The company should tell the family, not the crew over social media.<\/li><li><strong>Return of death<\/strong> to the RSS on form MSF 4605, formerly RBD 1, under the Merchant Shipping (Returns of Births and Deaths) Regulations 1979.<\/li><li><strong>MAIB<\/strong> if the death is connected with the operation of the ship, or the cause is unknown, under the Accident Reporting Regs 2026. <strong>Deliberate acts<\/strong>, such as suicide or homicide, are excluded. If the cause is unknown, it is reportable.<\/li><li>If a <strong>crime is suspected<\/strong>: protect the evidence, keep the suspect separate if safe, and inform the police at the next port, the company and the flag State.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Inquiry<\/strong><\/p><p>Under <strong>MSA 1995 s.271<\/strong>, a superintendent or proper officer holds an inquiry into a death on a UK ship, at the next port in the UK or abroad, unless a coroner is holding an inquest.<\/p><p><strong>At the next port<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The local authorities: police, port health, the coroner or medical examiner.<\/li><li>The agent handles the death certificate and the <strong>repatriation of the remains<\/strong>, with the P&amp;I club.<\/li><li>The body isn&#x27;t buried at sea, except in very exceptional circumstances and with authority.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Wages and property<\/strong> are dealt with under the regulations, through the company and the RSS.<\/li><li><strong>Crew welfare<\/strong>: a debrief, access to welfare and counselling services, and rest.<\/li><li><strong>ISM<\/strong>: an investigation if the death could have a work-related cause, with lessons learned.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>First aid and TMAS, preserve the scene and property, OLB entry and who signs it, return of death to the RSS, MAIB if connected with ship operation, the s.271 inquiry, and care for the crew.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who signs the OLB entry for a death?","pa":"<p>The <strong>Master in person<\/strong>, witnessed by a <strong>member of the crew<\/strong>. The cause of death is entered by the ship&#x27;s doctor, or by the Master if there&#x27;s no doctor.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q35","sec":2,"q":"What is PSC? Flag State? RO?","tier":"red","rep":64,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":"PSC Regs 2026","a":"<p><strong>Flag State<\/strong><\/p><p>The State whose flag the ship flies. For a UK ship that&#x27;s the UK, acting through the <strong>MCA<\/strong>. Under <strong>UNCLOS Article 94<\/strong> and the conventions, the flag State has <strong>primary responsibility<\/strong> for its ships:<\/p><ul><li><strong>making the law<\/strong>: the Merchant Shipping Act 1995 and the regulations;<\/li><li><strong>surveys and certification<\/strong>, directly or through ROs;<\/li><li><strong>flag State inspections<\/strong> and audits;<\/li><li><strong>manning<\/strong> and certificates of competency;<\/li><li><strong>enforcement<\/strong>: improvement and prohibition notices, detentions, prosecutions;<\/li><li><strong>casualty investigation<\/strong>: the MAIB.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Recognised Organisation (RO)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>classification society authorised by the flag State<\/strong> to carry out statutory surveys and issue certificates on its behalf.<\/li><li>Authorisation is under the IMO <strong>RO Code<\/strong>, mandatory through SOLAS, MARPOL and Load Line since 2015, and a written agreement with the flag.<\/li><li>Examples: Lloyd&#x27;s Register, DNV, ABS, Bureau Veritas, ClassNK, RINA, KR.<\/li><li>The UK uses ROs under schemes such as <strong>ACSS<\/strong> (MGN 568 Amd 1) and <strong>EASS<\/strong> (MGN 561 Amd 1).<\/li><li>The <strong>flag State remains responsible<\/strong>. It monitors the RO&#x27;s work by audits and inspections.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Port State Control (PSC)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Inspection of <strong>foreign ships in a State&#x27;s ports<\/strong>, to check the ship, crew and documents meet the conventions: SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW, Load Line, MLC, Tonnage, COLREG, AFS, BWM.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s organised regionally through <strong>Memoranda of Understanding<\/strong>: Paris (Europe and the North Atlantic, including the UK), Tokyo, Indian Ocean, Black Sea, USCG on its own, and others.<\/li><li>In the UK, the PSC Regs 2026 (SI 2026/563) and <strong>MGN 711<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Inspectors can:<\/li><li>record deficiencies with <strong>action codes<\/strong>, such as 17 (rectify before departure) or 30 (detention);<\/li><li><strong>detain<\/strong> the ship if it&#x27;s substandard;<\/li><li><strong>refuse access<\/strong>, or ban, repeat offenders;<\/li><li>report the RO if the detention is RO-related.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How they fit together<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>flag State<\/strong> is the <strong>first line of defence<\/strong>: it should make sure the ship meets the standards.<\/li><li>The <strong>RO<\/strong> does much of that work for the flag.<\/li><li><strong>PSC<\/strong> is the <strong>safety net<\/strong> for when the flag State doesn&#x27;t do its job, which is why ships under poor flags are targeted.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Flag State has primary responsibility, ROs act for the flag but the flag stays responsible, and PSC inspects foreign ships under regional MoUs and can detain.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who is ultimately responsible for a certificate issued by an RO?","pa":"<p>The <strong>flag state (Administration)<\/strong>. Under SOLAS I/6 and the RO Code, it remains fully responsible for certificates issued on its behalf.<\/p>","asked":["Do you inform flag state ?","What do you know about PSC?","What is Paris MOU and Port State Control, what Port State inspection would you expect after grounding?","How is a ship targeted within the Paris MoU and what categories according to risk?","What is the difference between Port State and Flag State control?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",26],["Michele Moro",14],["Colum Leggett",13],["Chris Roe",11],["Examiner not named",7]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q36","sec":2,"q":"PSC risk profile and inspection windows?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":"PSC Regs 2026","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Paris MoU New Inspection Regime (NIR)<\/strong>, since 2011. In the UK it&#x27;s applied by the PSC Regs 2026 and MGN 711.<\/p><p><strong>Ship risk profile<\/strong><\/p><p>Every ship gets a risk profile, calculated in <strong>THETIS<\/strong> from:<\/p><p><strong>Generic factors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ship type<\/strong>: chemical, gas, oil tankers, bulk carriers and passenger ships score higher.<\/li><li><strong>Age<\/strong>: over 12 years scores higher.<\/li><li><strong>Flag performance<\/strong>: White, Grey or Black list, and whether the flag has completed the IMO audit.<\/li><li><strong>RO performance<\/strong>: high, medium, low or very low.<\/li><li><strong>Company ISM performance<\/strong>, based on the deficiencies and detentions of all its ships.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Historic factors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Number of <strong>deficiencies<\/strong> and <strong>detentions<\/strong> in the last <strong>36 months<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Risk levels and inspection windows<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>High Risk Ship (HRS):<\/strong> periodic inspection <strong>5–6 months<\/strong> after the last inspection, and always an expanded inspection.<\/li><li><strong>Standard Risk Ship (SRS):<\/strong> <strong>10–12 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Low Risk Ship (LRS):<\/strong> <strong>24–36 months<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Priority<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Priority I: must be inspected.<\/strong> Either the ship&#x27;s window has closed, or there&#x27;s an <strong>overriding factor<\/strong>: a collision, grounding or stranding on the way to port; a pollution violation; manoeuvring dangerously; suspended or withdrawn class; a report from another authority; a ship on the &quot;refusal of access&quot; path.<\/li><li><strong>Priority II: may be inspected.<\/strong> Either the ship is inside her window, or there&#x27;s an <strong>unexpected factor<\/strong>: a complaint by a pilot or the crew, cargo or stowage problems, a previous deficiency not followed up, anomalies in documents.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Types of inspection<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Initial inspection:<\/strong> certificates and documents, and an overall look at the ship&#x27;s condition.<\/li><li><strong>More detailed inspection:<\/strong> when there are <strong>clear grounds<\/strong> that the ship doesn&#x27;t meet the requirements.<\/li><li><strong>Expanded inspection:<\/strong> for high-risk ships, and older passenger ships, oil, gas and chemical tankers and bulk carriers. It covers a defined list of risk areas.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it means for me<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I can check our ship&#x27;s risk profile and window on the Paris MoU public website.<\/li><li>Before entering a Paris MoU port near our window, I prepare as for an inspection: certificates, records, LSA and FFE ready, crew briefed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Two common mistakes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>There are <strong>only Priority I and II<\/strong>, no &quot;Priority 3&quot;.<\/li><li>The <strong>ship doesn&#x27;t invite PSC<\/strong>. Inspection is the port State&#x27;s decision.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The factors, the three windows, the two priorities with examples, and the three inspection types.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What factors would make your ship Priority I regardless of time window?","pa":"<p>Paris MoU <strong>overriding factors<\/strong> make a ship Priority I:<\/p><ul><li>a collision, grounding or stranding on the way to port;<\/li><li>an alleged illegal discharge;<\/li><li>unsafe manoeuvring or not following routeing measures;<\/li><li>class suspended or withdrawn for safety reasons;<\/li><li>a report or notification from another member state;<\/li><li>not being in the inspection database.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q37","sec":2,"q":"Can the MCA carry out PSC on a UK ship?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":"PSC Regs 2026","a":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> Port State Control is the inspection of <strong>foreign-flagged ships<\/strong> in a State&#x27;s ports. So the MCA carries out PSC only on non-UK ships in UK ports, under the <strong>PSC Regs 2026<\/strong> (SI 2026/563) and <strong>MGN 711<\/strong>, as a member of the Paris MoU.<\/p><p><strong>What the MCA does on UK ships: flag State control<\/strong><\/p><p>Under the Merchant Shipping Act 1995 and the regulations, the MCA acts as the flag State. It carries out:<\/p><ul><li><strong>flag State inspections<\/strong>, risk-based, linked to the MCA&#x27;s own profile of the ship and the company;<\/li><li><strong>surveys and audits<\/strong>: statutory surveys where not delegated, ISM DOC and SMC audits, ISPS verifications and MLC inspections, depending on whether the ship is under ACSS or EASS;<\/li><li><strong>unannounced inspections<\/strong> and investigations after complaints, accidents or a PSC detention abroad;<\/li><li><strong>oversight of the ROs<\/strong> that survey UK ships.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Enforcement powers on UK ships<\/strong><\/p><p>The MCA still has full powers:<\/p><ul><li><strong>improvement notices<\/strong> (MSA s.261) and <strong>prohibition notices<\/strong> (s.262);<\/li><li><strong>detention<\/strong> of a dangerously unsafe ship (s.95);<\/li><li><strong>prosecution<\/strong> of the owner, Master or others for offences;<\/li><li>withdrawal of certificates, and removal of the ship from ACSS or EASS.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The practical difference<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A UK ship in Southampton is inspected by the MCA as a <strong>flag State<\/strong> matter. The result isn&#x27;t a Paris MoU PSC inspection and doesn&#x27;t go into THETIS as one.<\/li><li>The same UK ship in Rotterdam can get a <strong>Paris MoU PSC inspection<\/strong> from the Dutch authorities. Any detention there counts against the UK flag on the White, Grey and Black list and against the RO and the company.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s why the MCA follows up PSC detentions of UK ships abroad. It wants to protect the UK&#x27;s White List position.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><p>To check I understand the difference between flag State and port State, and that the MCA isn&#x27;t toothless on UK ships just because it doesn&#x27;t do &quot;PSC&quot; on them. It has stronger powers as the flag.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The one-line answer: &quot;No, PSC is for foreign ships. On a UK ship the MCA acts as flag State, with inspections, surveys and audits, and it can still issue notices, detain under s.95 and prosecute.&quot;<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What can a UK MCA surveyor do on a UK ship that a PSC officer can't?","pa":"<p>As <strong>flag state<\/strong>, an MCA surveyor can:<\/p><ul><li>survey the ship, and issue, endorse or withdraw UK certificates;<\/li><li>grant exemptions and dispensations;<\/li><li>do flag inspections and ISM, ISPS and MLC audits;<\/li><li>serve improvement and prohibition notices;<\/li><li>detain under s.95, and prosecute.<\/li><\/ul><p>A PSC officer can only check compliance and detain a <strong>foreign<\/strong> ship. He can&#x27;t issue certificates.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q38","sec":2,"q":"Paris MoU black/grey/white lists?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it ranks<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>White, Grey and Black (WGB) list<\/strong> ranks <strong>flag States<\/strong>, not individual ships, by the PSC record of their ships in the Paris MoU region. It&#x27;s based on the total number of inspections and detentions over a <strong>rolling three-year period<\/strong>, and the Paris MoU publishes it every year in its annual report. A flag needs a minimum number of inspections over the period to be listed.<\/p><p><strong>The three lists<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>White list:<\/strong> a consistently low detention rate, showing a quality flag. The UK has been on the White List and near the top of it (check the latest annual report).<\/li><li><strong>Grey list:<\/strong> average performance.<\/li><li><strong>Black list:<\/strong> poor performance, split into graded bands: medium risk, medium to high, high, and very high risk.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Risk profile:<\/strong> flag performance is a <strong>generic factor<\/strong> in the ship risk profile. A black-listed flag pushes the ship towards <strong>High Risk<\/strong>, so more frequent and expanded inspections. A white-listed flag that&#x27;s also completed the IMO member State audit scheme helps a ship be Low Risk.<\/li><li><strong>Refusal of access (banning):<\/strong> ships under black or grey-listed flags with repeated detentions within set periods can be <strong>banned<\/strong> from Paris MoU ports and anchorages. A ship can also be banned for jumping detention or not going to an agreed repair yard. The exact thresholds are set in the MoU (verify before quoting).<\/li><li><strong>Commercial:<\/strong> charterers, cargo owners and insurers look at it. Black-list flags make ships harder to charter and insure.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Other performance lists<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>RO performance list:<\/strong> high, medium, low and very low performance, based on RO-related detentions. It feeds into the ship risk profile.<\/li><li><strong>Company performance:<\/strong> high, medium, low or very low, based on the deficiency and detention record of all the company&#x27;s ships. That feeds in too.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other MoUs<\/strong><\/p><p>The Tokyo MoU has its own WGB list. The US Coast Guard has its own targeting matrix and Qualship 21 programme.<\/p><p><strong>What it means for me<\/strong><\/p><p>On a UK ship, a white-listed flag is an advantage. But one detention of my ship counts against the flag, the RO and the company, and changes our own risk profile for 36 months.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>It ranks flags over three years; white, grey, black; it feeds the ship risk profile and refusal of access; and there are similar RO and company performance lists.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Is the WGB list about the ship or the flag, and what list is the UK on?","pa":"<p>The <strong>flag<\/strong>, not the ship. The UK has been on the <strong>White List<\/strong> (check the latest Paris MoU annual report).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q39","sec":2,"q":"What are THETIS and CERS?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>THETIS<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The information system run by the <strong>European Maritime Safety Agency (EMSA)<\/strong> for the Paris MoU inspection regime.<\/li><li>It <strong>stores<\/strong> all PSC inspection results, deficiencies and detentions.<\/li><li>It <strong>calculates<\/strong> each ship&#x27;s risk profile and its <strong>Priority I or II<\/strong> status.<\/li><li>It tells member States which ships are <strong>due or overdue<\/strong> for inspection.<\/li><li>It <strong>shares data<\/strong> across the region, so a deficiency recorded in one port is visible in the next.<\/li><li>The UK still uses it as a Paris MoU member.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>So anything PSC writes about my ship in Rotterdam follows her to every Paris MoU port.<\/strong> A code 17 not closed out will be the first thing the next inspector looks for.<\/p><p><strong>CERS<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Consolidated European Reporting System<\/strong>. It <strong>is the MCA&#x27;s system for vessel traffic monitoring reporting, and it&#x27;s still in use<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The UK law is the <strong>Merchant Shipping (Vessel Traffic Monitoring and Reporting Requirements) Regulations 2004<\/strong> (SI 2004/2110), with <strong>MSN 1899 (M+F)<\/strong>, and guidance in MGN 438 and MGN 563 (verify the current versions).<\/li><li>Masters and agents use it to send to the MCA:<\/li><li><strong>pre-arrival notification<\/strong>, at least <strong>24 hours<\/strong> ahead, for ships of <strong>300 GT<\/strong> and above bound for a UK port;<\/li><li><strong>dangerous or polluting goods<\/strong> notifications, for <strong>all ships<\/strong>, by the time of departure from the loading port;<\/li><li>waste, security and other port-call information, depending on the port.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s normally done through the <strong>CERS Workbook<\/strong>, sent by the agent and the port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Don&#x27;t confuse with<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>CALDOVREP<\/strong>, the VHF report in the Dover Strait, which is a ship reporting system under SOLAS V/11.<\/li><li>The MARPOL <strong>waste delivery advance notification<\/strong>, which goes to the port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters to me as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>I supply the DG manifest and the cargo information that go into the CERS report. If the DG information is wrong or late, the ship can be delayed, fined or reported.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>THETIS is the Paris MoU database that sets the risk profile and shares inspection results; CERS is the MCA&#x27;s reporting system for pre-arrival and dangerous or polluting goods notifications, still in use under the VTM Regs 2004 and MSN 1899. My old notes said CERS might have gone after Brexit; that&#x27;s wrong.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What information must be given before arrival when carrying DG into a UK port?","pa":"<p>Through <strong>CERS<\/strong> (VTM Regs 2004, MSN 1899).<\/p><ul><li><strong>Pre-arrival, 24 h ahead:<\/strong> ship ID, ETA and persons on board.<\/li><li><strong>DG:<\/strong> correct technical names, UN numbers, IMO class, quantity and stowage position; confirmation that the DG manifest is on board; and a 24 h contact for cargo information. Due by departure from the loading port.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q40","sec":2,"q":"What is a CIC? What is the current one?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What a CIC is<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>Concentrated Inspection Campaign<\/strong> is a <strong>three-month<\/strong> campaign, normally run jointly by the Paris and Tokyo MoUs and often others, focusing on <strong>one area of compliance<\/strong> with a standard questionnaire. It runs <strong>alongside<\/strong> normal PSC inspections: the inspector does the usual inspection and adds the CIC questions.<\/p><p><strong>Why they do it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To measure compliance in an area where deficiencies or casualties show a problem.<\/li><li>To raise awareness among owners and crews.<\/li><li>To gather data for IMO.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rules<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A ship gets <strong>one CIC inspection per MoU<\/strong> in the campaign period.<\/li><li>Questions answered &quot;no&quot; can become deficiencies, and serious failings can lead to <strong>detention<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The results are published afterwards.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The current one<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>Paris and Tokyo MoU CIC on Cargo Securing<\/strong>, <strong>1 September to 30 November 2026<\/strong>. It checks compliance with the SOLAS requirements for the loading, stowage and securing of cargo units and cargo transport units: SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5, the CSS Code and the ship&#x27;s approved Cargo Securing Manual.<\/p><p><strong>What it means for my container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>approved Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong> is on board, current and followed. The edition matches the lashing system actually fitted.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing equipment<\/strong> is certified, inventoried and in good condition, with damaged gear quarantined.<\/li><li><strong>Stack weights and heights<\/strong> are within the CSM limits.<\/li><li><strong>VGM<\/strong> received for every container (SOLAS VI/2).<\/li><li>The <strong>lashing software<\/strong> and loadicator are approved and used, with the departure condition printed.<\/li><li><strong>Lashings actually applied<\/strong> to the plan: twistlocks locked, bars tensioned, fixed fittings sound.<\/li><li><strong>Crew knowledge<\/strong>: the officers and deck crew know the CSM, the heavy-weather guidance, and how lashings are inspected.<\/li><li><strong>Records<\/strong>: PMS for lashing gear and fixed fittings, lashing inspection checklists.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I prepare<\/strong><\/p><p>Brief the officers and bosun, inspect lashing gear and fittings, update the inventory, check the CSM is the right edition, and keep the lashing printouts for each departure.<\/p><p><strong>Recent CICs for context<\/strong><\/p><p>Earlier campaigns covered things like STCW hours of rest, fire safety, MLC crew wages and SEAs, and navigation. My old notes cited the 2024 CIC on crew wages and SEAs; that&#x27;s out of date.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The definition, three months, one per ship, can lead to detention, and the current topic, with what it means for my ship. Always check the latest CIC before the exam, because it&#x27;s a favourite question.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What will the PSC officer ask the C/O about the Cargo Securing Manual?","pa":"<p>He&#x27;ll ask:<\/p><ul><li>Is the CSM approved and on board? Which edition?<\/li><li>Where are the lashing patterns and stack limits?<\/li><li>How is lashing gear inspected and maintained, and what&#x27;s the inventory?<\/li><li>What happens to damaged gear?<\/li><li>What does the CSM say about heavy weather?<\/li><li>How is the lashing software used, and who checks the stevedores&#x27; lashing?<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["What is CIC?"],"ex":[["Siddhartha Paul",1]],"yrs":"2025–2025"},{"id":"Q41","sec":2,"q":"Types of legislation; what is an SI?","tier":"red","rep":61,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>UK maritime law is built in <strong>layers<\/strong>. I&#x27;d describe them from the top.<\/p><p><strong>1. International conventions<\/strong><\/p><p>SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW, Load Line, MLC, COLREG and so on. The UK is a <strong>dualist<\/strong> State: a convention isn&#x27;t UK law until Parliament gives it effect.<\/p><p><strong>2. Primary legislation: Acts of Parliament<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For us, mainly the <strong>Merchant Shipping Act 1995<\/strong>, which consolidated earlier shipping Acts.<\/li><li>Others: the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, the Pilotage Act 1987, the Merchant Shipping and Maritime Security Act 1997, the Wreck Removal Convention Act 2011, the Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009.<\/li><li>Acts set the framework, create offences and give the Secretary of State <strong>powers to make regulations<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Secondary legislation: Statutory Instruments<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>SI<\/strong> is law made by a minister under powers in an Act, and laid before Parliament.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>quicker to make and change<\/strong> than an Act, and it&#x27;s how most convention requirements reach us.<\/li><li>Examples: the Survey and Certification Regs 2015, the Hours of Work Regs 2018, the Accident Reporting and Investigation Regs 2026 (SI 2026/430), the ISM Regs 2026.<\/li><li>SIs are <strong>numbered by year<\/strong>, for example SI 2026/430.<\/li><li>Many now use <strong>ambulatory references<\/strong>, so IMO amendments apply automatically.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. M Notices<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MSNs<\/strong> contain mandatory detail called up by an SI, so they&#x27;re part of the law.<\/li><li><strong>MGNs<\/strong> are guidance on how to comply.<\/li><li><strong>MINs<\/strong> are time-limited information.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Codes<\/strong><\/p><p>For example the <strong>Code of Safe Working Practices<\/strong>, which has legal status through the Health and Safety Regs, and IMO codes like ISM, ISPS and IMDG, which are mandatory through SOLAS and applied by the SIs.<\/p><p><strong>6. Case law<\/strong><\/p><p>Decisions of the courts, which interpret the Acts and SIs and bind lower courts. For example, cases on &quot;seaworthiness&quot;, &quot;due diligence&quot; and the Master&#x27;s duties.<\/p><p><strong>How it works in practice<\/strong><\/p><p>SOLAS V says &quot;carry a VDR&quot;. The Safety of Navigation Regs 2020, an SI under the MSA 1995, make it UK law. MGN 610 explains how to comply. If I don&#x27;t comply, it&#x27;s an offence under the SI, prosecuted under the Act.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Conventions, Acts, SIs, M Notices, codes, case law; and a clear definition: &quot;an SI is secondary legislation made by a minister under powers in an Act, laid before Parliament, quicker to change, and it&#x27;s how most conventions become UK law&quot;. Give an example with its number.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Is an MGN legally binding?","pa":"<p><strong>No, not in itself.<\/strong> An MGN is guidance on how to comply. But a court or the MCA will expect it to have been followed, and ignoring it can be evidence that you didn&#x27;t take reasonable steps. An <strong>MSN<\/strong> is binding when an SI refers to it.<\/p>","asked":["Refer. MGN 561","Ch-2, MGN 71 and SOLAS Ch-3, Reg 19 Sub-2.","What is MGN, MSN and MIN?","MGN 560,explained dynamic n static test procedures.","What new things are added in mgn 659?"],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",13],["Chris Bates",7],["Kaiser Ali",5],["James Gilbert",4],["Zia Ul Haq",4]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"★3.1","sec":2,"q":"PSC boards in Rotterdam during the cargo-securing CIC. What will they look at on your container ship?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The campaign:<\/strong> the <strong>Paris and Tokyo MoU CIC on Cargo Securing<\/strong>, <strong>1 September to 30 November 2026<\/strong>. It checks the SOLAS requirements for the loading, stowage and securing of cargo units: SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5, the CSS Code, and the ship&#x27;s approved Cargo Securing Manual. The inspector adds a standard questionnaire to the normal PSC inspection.<\/p><p><strong>What I&#x27;d expect them to look at<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. The Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Approved by the flag or RO, on board, current.<\/li><li>The edition matches the lashing system and fittings actually on board, and any changes have been approved.<\/li><li>Officers know where to find the stack weight limits, the lashing patterns, and the heavy-weather guidance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Lashing equipment<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>inventory<\/strong> of portable gear: twistlocks, lashing bars, turnbuckles, bridge fittings, stackers.<\/li><li><strong>Certificates<\/strong> for each type, showing the MSL and the approval.<\/li><li><strong>Condition<\/strong>: how we inspect, how damaged gear is <strong>quarantined<\/strong> and replaced, and enough spares.<\/li><li>Mixed types of twistlock not used in the same tier unless the CSM allows it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Fixed fittings<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Deck sockets, D-rings, lashing bridges, cell guides: sound, not cracked or wasted, and in the PMS.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Stowage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stack weights and heights<\/strong> within CSM limits.<\/li><li><strong>VGM<\/strong> received for every box (SOLAS VI/2).<\/li><li>Heavy boxes not on top of light ones unless the software allows it.<\/li><li>DG stowed and segregated per IMDG and the DG Document of Compliance.<\/li><li>Bridge visibility (SOLAS V/22).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Lashing software and loadicator<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Approved, used for every departure, with <strong>printouts<\/strong> showing the lashing forces within limits for the assumed GM, route and weather.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. On deck<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Lashings <strong>actually applied<\/strong> to the plan, twistlocks <strong>locked<\/strong>, bars <strong>tensioned<\/strong>, no missing lashings in the outer rows.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Crew knowledge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>They&#x27;ll ask me and the deck crew: how do you check lashings, what do you do with damaged gear, what does the CSM say about heavy weather, how do you avoid parametric rolling?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>PMS for lashing gear and fittings, lashing inspection checklists at sea and in port, and the company&#x27;s heavy-weather and parametric-roll guidance (MSC.1/Circ.1228).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I&#x27;d prepare before Rotterdam<\/strong><\/p><p>Brief the officers and bosun, walk the deck with the lashing plan, update the inventory, quarantine damaged gear, and have the CSM, certificates and lashing printouts ready.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Serious failings can lead to <strong>detention<\/strong>. The examiner wants to hear the CSM, gear and certificates, fittings, stack weights and VGM, the software, lashings actually applied, and crew knowledge.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you show the PSC officer that the lashing applied matches the lashing plan?","pa":"<p>Three things:<\/p><ol><li>The approved CSM and the <strong>lashing software printout<\/strong> for the departure condition: every stack within limits, using the actual GM.<\/li><li>The <strong>signed bay-by-bay lashing checklists<\/strong>, plus our check of the stevedores&#x27; work.<\/li><li>A walk to a sample bay with the plan, to show the bars, turnbuckles and locked twistlocks match.<\/li><\/ol>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★3.2","sec":2,"q":"What changed in UK accident reporting this year?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"MAIB Regs 2026","a":"<p><strong>The big change<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>Merchant Shipping (Accident Reporting and Investigation) Regulations 2026<\/strong> (SI 2026/430), in force <strong>15 May 2026<\/strong>, replaced the 2012 Regulations. The new guidance is <strong>MGN 564 (M+F) Amendment 2<\/strong>, May 2026.<\/p><p><strong>Key points<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Discretion to investigate.<\/strong> The MAIB now has more discretion over whether to investigate accidents involving non-UK ships, cargo ships under 500 GT and fishing vessels, so it can focus resources where the safety lessons are.<\/li><li><strong>Alignment with the IMO Casualty Investigation Code<\/strong> (MSC.255(84)), mandatory through SOLAS XI-1/6, including the definitions of marine casualty, very serious marine casualty and marine incident.<\/li><li><strong>Serious injury<\/strong> replaces the old &quot;major injury&quot; list:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the Regs: unable to undertake their full range of activities for more than <strong>72 hours<\/strong>, starting within 7 days;<\/li><li>MGN 564 Amd 2: unable to perform usual duties for more than 72 hours, or <strong>in hospital for more than 24 hours<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Exclusions<\/strong> made clearer:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>deliberate acts<\/strong>: assault, suicide, homicide;<\/li><li>equipment defects and PSC detentions <strong>not linked to a casualty<\/strong>;<\/li><li>passenger injuries not connected with the ship&#x27;s operation;<\/li><li>shore incidents not involving the ship&#x27;s equipment.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What stays the same<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Master and the company both have the duty to report.<\/li><li>Report <strong>as soon as practicable by the quickest means available<\/strong>: the 24-hour line, then the online Accident Report Form.<\/li><li>Notification must <strong>not be delayed<\/strong> until the company&#x27;s internal investigation is finished.<\/li><li><strong>Preserve evidence<\/strong> and <strong>save the VDR<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The MAIB investigates for <strong>safety, not blame<\/strong>. Its reports can&#x27;t be used to apportion liability, and evidence compelled from a witness is protected.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other 2026 UK changes to mention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>ISM Code Regs 2026<\/strong>, in force 31 March 2026, with MGN 708.<\/li><li>The <strong>PSC Regs 2026<\/strong> (SI 2026/563), with MGN 711, replacing MSN 1832.<\/li><\/ul><p>These three, accidents, ISM and PSC, are the new UK regulations of 2026, and examiners are asking about them.<\/p><p><strong>What it means for me<\/strong><\/p><p>I update the company&#x27;s reporting procedure and the bridge cards with the new definitions and contact details, and brief the officers.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 2026 Regs and SI number, the date, MGN 564 Amd 2, the 72-hour and 24-hour tests, the exclusions, MAIB discretion, and what hasn&#x27;t changed. Quoting the 2012 Regs or &quot;major injury&quot; now looks out of date.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Give me an example of something that's now clearly *not* reportable.","pa":"<p>Examples:<\/p><ul><li>a <strong>suicide or assault<\/strong> on board, which are deliberate acts;<\/li><li>a <strong>PSC detention<\/strong> with no casualty;<\/li><li>an equipment defect that caused no accident;<\/li><li>a shore crane accident not involving the ship&#x27;s equipment.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★3.3","sec":2,"q":"An AB breaks his wrist slipping on a wet deck. Reportable? Who signs what?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"MAIB Regs 2026","a":"<p><strong>Is it reportable? Yes, almost certainly.<\/strong><\/p><p>Under the <strong>Accident Reporting and Investigation Regulations 2026<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 564 Amd 2<\/strong>, a serious injury is one where the person can&#x27;t perform their usual duties for <strong>more than 72 hours<\/strong>, or is <strong>in hospital more than 24 hours<\/strong>. A fractured wrist will keep him off normal duties for much longer than 72 hours. It&#x27;s connected with the operation of the ship and not a deliberate act, so it&#x27;s a <strong>marine casualty<\/strong> and must be reported.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>First aid<\/strong>: immobilise the wrist with a splint and sling, ice, pain relief per the medical guide and the medical chest.<\/li><li><strong>TMAS<\/strong> advice through HM Coastguard (MGN 623). Follow it on medication and whether to divert or land him.<\/li><li>Land him for <strong>X-ray and treatment<\/strong> at the next port, or earlier if TMAS advises.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reporting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MAIB<\/strong>: as soon as practicable by the quickest means, the 24-hour line, then the Accident Report Form. The <strong>Master<\/strong> reports, and the <strong>company<\/strong> has the same duty. Don&#x27;t wait for the company investigation.<\/li><li><strong>Company and DPA<\/strong>: accident report under the SMS (ISM 9).<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>: a crew injury claim is likely.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who signs what<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>OLB<\/strong>: the injury and treatment entry, signed by the <strong>Master and a member of the crew<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Medical log<\/strong>: treatment and medication given, by the officer in charge of medical care.<\/li><li><strong>Accident report<\/strong>: the investigating officer, with the Master&#x27;s review.<\/li><li><strong>Witness statements<\/strong>: signed by each witness.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Investigation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Photos of the deck, measurements, the weather and sea state, lighting.<\/li><li>The AB&#x27;s PPE and footwear, his hours of rest, the job he was doing, the risk assessment and toolbox talk.<\/li><li>Root cause: wet deck from washing, oil or rain, no anti-slip paint, housekeeping, rushing, poor lighting, fatigue.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Corrective action<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Review the risk assessment and the working method, anti-slip coatings and housekeeping, and talk to the crew at the safety committee meeting.<\/li><li>Share the lessons with the fleet.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Welfare<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>MLC: medical care at the shipowner&#x27;s expense, <strong>sick pay<\/strong> and <strong>repatriation<\/strong> if he&#x27;s landed, under MLC Regulation 4.2 and the UK MLC regulations.<\/li><li>Look after his duties so the rest of the crew don&#x27;t break their rest hours covering for him.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Yes, because of the 72-hour test; MAIB and company; OLB signed by the Master and a crew member; investigation and root cause; and his MLC rights.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"If he were fit for light duties after 48 hours, would it still be reportable?","pa":"<p>Yes, if he can&#x27;t do his <strong>usual duties for more than 72 h<\/strong>. The 2026 Regs say &quot;unable to undertake their full range of activities&quot;. <strong>Light duties aren&#x27;t his usual duties.<\/strong> It also counts if he&#x27;s in hospital for more than 24 h (MGN 564 Amd 2).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q42","sec":3,"q":"Safe Manning Document: who issues it, how do you apply, is a survey needed, when must you re-apply?","tier":"orange","rep":26,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Internationally SOLAS V/14 and IMO Resolution A.1047(27), the principles of minimum safe manning. In the UK the requirement is in the Merchant Shipping (STCW) Regulations 2022, with the detail in <strong>MSN 1868 (M) Amd 1<\/strong>, safe manning and watchkeeping. My old handout said MSN 1767; that&#x27;s superseded.<\/p><p><strong>Who issues it<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Only the MCA.<\/strong> Class or any other RO cannot issue a UK Safe Manning Document. It&#x27;s a flag State function.<\/p><p><strong>How to apply<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The owner or operator first <strong>assesses the minimum manning<\/strong> needed to operate the ship safely, securely and without pollution, using the IMO principles.<\/li><li>It applies to the MCA on the application form (MSF 4227, verify) with:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the trading area: unlimited, near-coastal, and so on;<\/li><li>the ship&#x27;s type, size and operations: port turnaround, cargo work, mooring arrangements;<\/li><li>equipment and automation: UMS, bridge layout, mooring winches;<\/li><li>the watch arrangements, at sea and in port, and how hours of rest will be met;<\/li><li>maintenance arrangements: who does the PMS and any shore support;<\/li><li>emergency duties: enough people for the muster list, fire parties and boat crews.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>The MCA checks the proposal against the principles and the rest-hour rules, may ask questions or require changes, and then issues the SMD. It lists the minimum number and grade of officers and ratings, with their certificates.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Is a survey needed?<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>No.<\/strong> It&#x27;s a paper assessment, not a survey. The MCA may visit or inspect the ship, but there&#x27;s no SMD survey.<\/p><p><strong>When to re-apply<\/strong><\/p><p>Whenever something changes the basis of the document:<\/p><ul><li>a change of trading area, for example near-coastal to unlimited;<\/li><li>new equipment, or loss of automation such as UMS;<\/li><li>a change in operation, for example more port calls or different cargo work;<\/li><li>a change in maintenance arrangements or the number of people needed for emergencies;<\/li><li>a change of owner or flag.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Important points<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ship must <strong>never sail with fewer crew<\/strong> than the SMD requires unless the MCA grants a <strong>dispensation<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The SMD is a <strong>minimum<\/strong>. Hours of rest under MSN 1877 still apply on top of it. If the work can&#x27;t be done within rest hours with the SMD numbers, the company must carry more people.<\/li><li>PSC checks the crew list against the SMD.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/14, MSN 1868, only the MCA issues it, a paper assessment with no survey, re-apply when the basis changes, and rest hours apply on top.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Does meeting the SMD numbers prove your ship is adequately manned?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> The SMD is the <strong>minimum<\/strong>. The company and Master must make sure the ship is adequately manned for her actual operation and for rest hours (STCW VIII/1, MLC, IMO principles of safe manning). Intensive port work may need extra crew.<\/p>","asked":["How long is a safe manning document valid for?","How long is it valid for?","What would you do now that you SMD is affected?","You mentioned dispensation for SMD, how will you obtain?","2 casualties. They are part of SMD. what will be ur action?"],"ex":[["Danny Hawkins",4],["Nayyer Naqvi",3],["Zia Ul Haq",3],["Michele Moro",2],["Khairul Anam",2]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q43","sec":3,"q":"Officer landed sick abroad, no relief before departure?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Officer landed sick abroad, no relief before departure<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. Check the Safe Manning Document.<\/strong> The SMD sets the legal minimum under MSN 1868 (M) Amd 1. If losing this officer keeps us at or above it, we can sail, provided rest hours still work.<\/p><p><strong>2. If we&#x27;d drop below it<\/strong>, the Master and the company must ask the <strong>MCA for a dispensation before sailing<\/strong>. Under <strong>STCW Article VIII<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li>a dispensation can only allow someone to serve in a post for which they don&#x27;t hold the certificate if they hold the certificate for the <strong>post immediately below<\/strong>, for example a 3/O with an OOW CoC moving up to 2/O;<\/li><li>it&#x27;s valid for <strong>no more than 6 months<\/strong> and normally much shorter, for example until the next port where a relief can join;<\/li><li>it&#x27;s <strong>never given for Master or Chief Engineer<\/strong>, except in <strong>force majeure<\/strong>, and then only for the shortest possible period.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Rest hours.<\/strong> Even with a dispensation, the remaining crew must still meet <strong>MSN 1877<\/strong> rest hours: 10 in 24 and 77 in 7 days. With one officer fewer, the watch system probably needs to change, for example the Master taking a watch. I check the plan works before we sail.<\/p><p><strong>4. Risk assessment<\/strong> of the voyage with the reduced manning, including pilotage, port calls and emergencies.<\/p><p><strong>5. Record<\/strong> everything in the OLB: the officer landed, the dispensation, and the watch arrangement.<\/p><p><strong>6. Get the relief<\/strong> to join at the next port, through the company.<\/p><p><strong>7. Look after the sick officer<\/strong>: landed with the agent, medical care and repatriation at the owner&#x27;s expense under MLC Reg 4.2.<\/p><p><strong>CoC expires mid-voyage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>expired CoC<\/strong>, or a CEC that depends on it, is <strong>not valid<\/strong>. The officer can&#x27;t serve in that capacity unless the MCA authorises it.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d contact the company and the MCA at once. The options are:<\/li><li>urgent revalidation, if he meets the sea-service or training requirements;<\/li><li>a dispensation, if he holds the certificate for the post below;<\/li><li>replacing him at the next port.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d raise an <strong>ISM non-conformity<\/strong>, because certificate expiry dates should have been caught when he joined. The company&#x27;s crewing system and my own document check both failed.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Check the SMD; a dispensation from the MCA under STCW Article VIII, only for the post immediately below and never for Master or C/E except in force majeure; rest hours still apply; and record it in the OLB.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who exactly can you give a dispensation to, and for how long?","pa":"<p>Under <strong>STCW Article VIII<\/strong> a dispensation can let an officer serve <strong>one level above<\/strong> his CoC, for example 2/O as C/O, for <strong>up to 6 months<\/strong>. It isn&#x27;t given for <strong>Master or Chief Engineer<\/strong> except in force majeure, and then for the shortest possible time. The MCA decides, and the person must be competent and properly qualified for the post below.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q44","sec":3,"q":"The Master dies on passage — you hold a Chief Mate CoC. What now?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>I take command immediately.<\/strong> That&#x27;s the line of succession in the SMS and the muster list. The safety of the ship comes first; everything else follows.<\/p><p><strong>1. The ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Make sure the ship is safe: the watch is set, someone competent is on the bridge, and the voyage continues safely while I sort out the rest.<\/li><li>Take over the Master&#x27;s keys, the safe, the codes, the ship&#x27;s documents and the SSAS details.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Notify<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong> at once.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA<\/strong>, because I don&#x27;t hold a Master&#x27;s CoC. The MCA must authorise me to continue in command. Under <strong>STCW Article VIII<\/strong> a dispensation for the Master&#x27;s post is possible only in <strong>force majeure<\/strong> and for the shortest possible time. So the company will need to send a qualified Master as soon as practicable, or arrange for the ship to wait.<\/li><li>The <strong>agent<\/strong> at the next port, and the P&amp;I club.<\/li><li>The <strong>next of kin<\/strong> through the company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Medical and the body<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If there&#x27;s any doubt, resuscitation and <strong>TMAS<\/strong> advice through HM Coastguard.<\/li><li>Preserve the scene, photograph it, and if the cause is unclear or suspicious, secure the cabin.<\/li><li>Keep the body in cold storage with dignity.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Records and returns<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A full <strong>OLB<\/strong> entry. Normally the Master makes the death entry in person; as acting Master I make it, witnessed by a member of the crew, and the cause of death entered by me as there&#x27;s no doctor.<\/li><li><strong>Return of death<\/strong> on form MSF 4605 to the RSS, under the Returns of Births and Deaths Regs 1979.<\/li><li><strong>Inventory<\/strong> the Master&#x27;s property, with a witness, sealed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Investigations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Under <strong>MSA 1995 s.271<\/strong> there will be an inquiry at the next port, unless a coroner investigates.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong> is involved only if the death is connected with the operation of the ship or the cause is unknown. A natural death from illness isn&#x27;t reportable.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. The voyage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Decide with the company whether to divert to land the body and get a new Master, or carry on to the next port.<\/li><li><strong>Reorganise the watches<\/strong> so that rest hours are still met with one officer fewer. As acting Master I shouldn&#x27;t keep a full watch if I can avoid it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. The crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brief them honestly, look after those close to him, and offer welfare support.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>I take command, inform the company and the MCA because I need authority to command without a Master&#x27;s CoC, OLB and return of death, property, the s.271 inquiry, MAIB only if connected with the ship, and rest hours.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What if the death looks suspicious?","pa":"<p>Treat it as a possible crime scene:<\/p><ul><li><strong>preserve the scene<\/strong>: lock the cabin, don&#x27;t move the body unless you must, take photos;<\/li><li>take statements;<\/li><li>inform the company, the flag (MCA) and the next port&#x27;s police or authorities;<\/li><li>get TMAS advice.<\/li><\/ul><p>The MAIB doesn&#x27;t investigate deliberate acts. There&#x27;s also an s.271 inquiry and consular contact.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q45","sec":3,"q":"Autopilot fails just before departure.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First, the misconception.<\/strong> My notes said &quot;the Safe Manning Document is no longer valid&quot;. That&#x27;s overstated. The SMD isn&#x27;t automatically invalid, but I must check that the manning and rest hours still work with hand steering.<\/p><p><strong>The key regulation: SOLAS V/16.2<\/strong><\/p><p>An equipment malfunction doesn&#x27;t make the ship unseaworthy, or justify delaying her in ports where repair facilities aren&#x27;t readily available, <strong>provided suitable arrangements are made<\/strong> by the Master to take the malfunction into account in planning and executing a safe voyage to a port where repairs can be made. The UK applies this through the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020 and MGN 610.<\/p><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Report it<\/strong> to the Master, and under the SMS to the company with a defect report and a request for a technician. If the defect affects the ship&#x27;s safety equipment or certification, consider reporting under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong> to the MCA or RO.<\/li><li><strong>Try to fix it<\/strong>: check breakers, the autopilot settings and the heading input from the gyro. Often the autopilot fails because the gyro feed has failed.<\/li><li><strong>Test the alternatives<\/strong>: hand steering, non-follow-up steering, change-over between steering modes and pumps, and the emergency steering from the steering gear room. Record the tests.<\/li><li><strong>Manning<\/strong>: put a <strong>certificated helmsman<\/strong> on every watch. Check that the extra helmsmen can be provided within <strong>MSN 1877<\/strong> rest hours: 10 in 24 and 77 in 7 days. On a long ocean passage that&#x27;s a big extra demand.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong>, and brief the OOWs: helmsman changes every one to two hours to keep them alert, course recorder checked, steering orders clear.<\/li><li><strong>Update the passage plan<\/strong>: hand steering throughout; a repair port; extra care in TSSs and pilotage waters.<\/li><li><strong>If manning or rest hours can&#x27;t be met<\/strong>, because the SMD assumed autopilot operation, <strong>consult the MCA before sailing<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Record it<\/strong> in the deck log and OLB.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Repair<\/strong><\/p><p>Get the technician at the next port and repair as soon as practicable. Test it and record the test.<\/p><p><strong>PSC<\/strong><\/p><p>Expect questions from PSC: what&#x27;s wrong, when was it reported, what arrangements are in place, and when will it be fixed? The defect report and the company&#x27;s reply are my evidence.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/16.2, report it, test the hand steering, certificated helmsmen within rest hours, risk assessment and passage plan, consult the MCA if the SMD basis is affected, and repair as soon as practicable.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"On a 10-day ocean passage, how many extra hands do you need to hand-steer?","pa":"<p>Plan at least <strong>one extra qualified helmsman per watch<\/strong>, so three on a 4-on/8-off system, changing every 1–2 h to stay within rest hours. If the crew can&#x27;t do that, don&#x27;t sail until the autopilot is fixed. Long hand-steering is tiring and risky.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q46","sec":3,"q":"UMS alarms fail. Action?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>The UMS alarm and monitoring system is what allows the engine room to be left <strong>periodically unattended<\/strong>. It&#x27;s required by <strong>SOLAS II-1 Part E<\/strong> and the class UMS notation. Without it, the machinery spaces must be <strong>manned continuously<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Manned engine-room watches.<\/strong> The Chief Engineer sets up continuous watches under the STCW engineering watchkeeping principles (A-VIII/2). Usually a three-watch system with an engineer and a rating.<\/li><li><strong>The bridge.<\/strong> I make sure the OOWs know the engine room is manned, how to contact the duty engineer, and which alarms still come to the bridge, for example fire alarms and the engineer&#x27;s call system.<\/li><li><strong>Rest hours.<\/strong> A three-watch engine room on top of day work can quickly push the engineers over <strong>MSN 1877<\/strong> limits. The Chief Engineer and I:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>check the schedule against 10 hours in 24 and 77 in 7 days;<\/li><li>use ratings where their certification allows (STCW III/4 for watch ratings);<\/li><li>stop non-essential day work;<\/li><li>do a risk assessment.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Report it.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>To the <strong>company<\/strong> under the SMS, with a request for a technician and spares.<\/li><li>To <strong>class<\/strong>, because the <strong>UMS notation<\/strong> is affected and may get a condition.<\/li><li>If our <strong>SMD<\/strong> was issued assuming UMS operation and we can&#x27;t man safely within rest hours, <strong>consult the MCA<\/strong> about a dispensation or extra crew.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Fire safety.<\/strong> An unmanned engine room relies on the fire detection system. Check the fire detection is still working independently. If it isn&#x27;t, that&#x27;s even more serious.<\/li><li><strong>Record it<\/strong> in the deck and engine logs.<\/li><li><strong>Repair<\/strong> at the earliest opportunity, test it, and get class to verify it if required.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Fault-finding<\/strong><\/p><p>Often it&#x27;s a power supply, a failed alarm panel, or the extension alarm system to the cabins and the public spaces. A partial failure might be covered by temporary measures, for example manned watches only at night.<\/p><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><p>The decision whether to sail is the <strong>Master&#x27;s<\/strong>. If we can&#x27;t man the engine room safely within rest hours, we <strong>don&#x27;t sail<\/strong> until it&#x27;s resolved: extra engineers, a repair, or MCA agreement. Tired engineers on watch in a machinery space is a serious risk.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-1 Part E and class UMS, continuous watches, rest hours under MSN 1877, report to company and class and possibly the MCA, check fire detection, and the Master&#x27;s decision.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can a deck rating be used on an engine-room watch?","pa":"<p>Only if he&#x27;s <strong>competent and certificated<\/strong> for an engine-room watch (STCW A-III/4) and the SMD allows interdepartmental flexibility. Otherwise, no.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q47","sec":3,"q":"Pilot taken ill, no other pilot available — action?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In UK waters, the <strong>Pilotage Act 1987<\/strong>. A competent harbour authority sets <strong>compulsory pilotage<\/strong> areas (s.7) and can issue a <strong>Pilotage Exemption Certificate (PEC)<\/strong> to a bona fide master or first mate of the ship (s.8, verify). Abroad, the local port regulations apply.<\/p><p><strong>1. Look after the pilot first<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>First aid, and medical help through the port or agent. If it&#x27;s serious, call the port control or VTS for an ambulance or a launch.<\/li><li>Get TMAS advice if we&#x27;re at sea or at anchor.<\/li><li>Land him ashore safely, with the agent.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. The ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If we&#x27;re under way in pilotage waters, the Master takes the con at once. The bridge team carries on with the passage plan and VTS assistance.<\/li><li>Slow down, and consider anchoring in a safe place or holding position until another pilot arrives.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Inform<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>harbour master or VTS<\/strong>, the <strong>agent<\/strong> and the <strong>company<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Ask about: another pilot, a later boarding, a pilot by helicopter or launch, or whether the harbour authority can authorise a movement with <strong>VTS assistance<\/strong> or <strong>shore-based pilotage<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Don&#x27;t sail unpiloted in compulsory pilotage waters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Unless the Master, or I, hold a <strong>valid PEC<\/strong> for that port, ship and size.<\/li><li>Navigating without a pilot in a compulsory area is an <strong>offence<\/strong>, and the insurers may not cover an accident.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. If the ship must move for safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For example bad weather at an exposed berth, or a fire on the quay.<\/li><li>Do it only with the <strong>harbour authority&#x27;s agreement<\/strong>, with <strong>tugs<\/strong>, a full bridge team, the passage plan, and VTS monitoring.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Deck log entries with times.<\/li><li>A <strong>note of protest<\/strong> if the delay may lead to a commercial claim, such as demurrage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. ISM<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If it shows a gap in our procedures, for example no plan for a pilot incapacitated during pilotage, raise it at the Master&#x27;s review.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The Master&#x27;s decision<\/strong><\/p><p>The decision rests with the <strong>Master<\/strong>. Commercial pressure from the charterer, terminal or company is not a reason to sail unpiloted. <strong>SOLAS V/34-1<\/strong> protects the Master&#x27;s professional judgement, and ISM 5.2 gives him overriding authority.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Look after the pilot, the Master takes the con, inform the harbour authority, VTS, agent and company, no sailing in compulsory pilotage waters without a pilot or PEC, move only with the harbour authority&#x27;s agreement, and the Master&#x27;s decision under SOLAS V/34-1.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who issues a PEC and what does it depend on?","pa":"<p>The <strong>Competent Harbour Authority<\/strong> under the Pilotage Act 1987 issues it, to a bona fide Master or first mate. It depends on:<\/p><ul><li>the number of trips in the area;<\/li><li>passing the authority&#x27;s local knowledge exam;<\/li><li>English;<\/li><li>limits on ship size and type.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q48","sec":3,"q":"Interdepartmental flexibility under the SMD?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What the SMD says<\/strong><\/p><p>The Safe Manning Document sets the <strong>minimum numbers and grades<\/strong> of officers and ratings. It doesn&#x27;t normally say who does which job hour by hour. Under <strong>MSN 1868<\/strong>, the company&#x27;s application describes how the ship will be operated, including any use of <strong>general-purpose (GP) ratings<\/strong> or crew shared between departments.<\/p><p><strong>Can engine-room crew help with mooring, or deck crew help in the engine room?<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Yes, if:<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>SMD and the application<\/strong> allow that flexibility, or at least it doesn&#x27;t conflict with them. Many SMDs are based on GP ratings, who are trained for both deck and engine work.<\/li><li>They are <strong>trained and familiarised<\/strong> for the job:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>for mooring: snap-back zones, winch operation, line handling, communications, the mooring plan;<\/li><li>for engine-room work: the hazards, permits, the alarms and the emergency escape routes.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>There&#x27;s a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> and a <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>engine room is still safely attended<\/strong>, especially when the ship isn&#x27;t on UMS or is manoeuvring. Taking the only engine-room rating to the mooring station during arrival may leave the engineer alone at the critical time.<\/li><li>They stay within <strong>MSN 1877<\/strong> rest hours. Sharing crew between departments often hides overwork.<\/li><li>They have the right <strong>PPE<\/strong> and are <strong>supervised<\/strong> by a competent person.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What they can&#x27;t do<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Fill a <strong>watchkeeping post<\/strong> they aren&#x27;t certificated for. A navigational watch rating needs <strong>STCW II/4<\/strong>, and an engine-room watch rating needs <strong>III/4<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Do work requiring a specific certificate they don&#x27;t hold, for example a lifeboat coxswain needs a PSC&amp;RB certificate, or an electrician&#x27;s isolation work.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Emergencies<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>muster list<\/strong> shows who does what in an emergency regardless of department. That&#x27;s another reason training across departments matters.<\/p><p><strong>How I&#x27;d manage it in practice<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Agree it with the <strong>Chief Engineer<\/strong> in advance, not on the day.<\/li><li>Put it in the <strong>daily work plan<\/strong> and the rest-hour planning.<\/li><li>Make sure it&#x27;s within the <strong>SMS<\/strong> and the company&#x27;s procedures.<\/li><li>If it becomes routine because we&#x27;re short-handed, report it, because the SMD may not reflect the real workload.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>PSC<\/strong><\/p><p>PSC may ask how the ship is run with the SMD numbers. Showing a planned, trained and recorded arrangement is the answer.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The SMD sets minimum numbers, flexibility is fine if trained, risk-assessed, within rest hours and not leaving a space unattended, but nobody fills a watchkeeping post without STCW II/4 or III/4.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can a GP rating stand a navigational watch?","pa":"<p>Only if he holds an <strong>STCW A-II/4 navigational watch rating<\/strong> certificate and the SMD allows it.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q49","sec":3,"q":"What is MLC 2006? DMLC Part I and II — who fills them?","tier":"red","rep":73,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>ILO Maritime Labour Convention 2006<\/strong> is the &quot;seafarers&#x27; bill of rights&quot;. It consolidated over 60 older ILO conventions and has been in force internationally since <strong>20 August 2013<\/strong>. It sets minimum standards for seafarers&#x27; working and living conditions, and lets port States enforce them, so ships of non-ratifying flags get &quot;no more favourable treatment&quot;.<\/p><p><strong>Structure: five Titles<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Minimum requirements to work on a ship: age, medical, training, recruitment.<\/li><li>Conditions of employment: SEA, wages, hours, leave, repatriation, manning.<\/li><li>Accommodation, recreation, food and catering.<\/li><li>Health protection, medical care, welfare and social security.<\/li><li>Compliance and enforcement: flag, port and labour-supplying State.<\/li><\/ol><p>Each Title has Regulations, a mandatory Code Part A (Standards) and a guidance Part B (Guidelines).<\/p><p><strong>UK implementation<\/strong><\/p><p>A set of UK MLC regulations, for example the Minimum Requirements Regs 2014, the Hours of Work Regs 2018, the Medical Certification Regs 2010 and the regulations on accommodation and food. The MCA&#x27;s guidance on survey and certification is <strong>MSN 1848 (M) Amd 4<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Certification<\/strong><\/p><p>Ships of <strong>500 GT and over<\/strong> on international voyages carry:<\/p><ul><li>a <strong>Maritime Labour Certificate<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>Declaration of Maritime Labour Compliance (DMLC)<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p>For UK ships these are issued by the <strong>MCA<\/strong>, or by an RO under EASS.<\/p><p><strong>DMLC Part I: drawn up by the flag State<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Lists the <strong>national requirements<\/strong> for each of the 16 inspected areas, with references to the UK regulations.<\/li><li>Notes any substantially equivalent provisions or exemptions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>DMLC Part II: drawn up by the shipowner<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Sets out the <strong>measures<\/strong> the owner has adopted to make sure the ship complies on an ongoing basis, and how continuous improvement is achieved.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>reviewed and certified<\/strong> by the flag State or RO.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Validity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The MLC certificate lasts up to <strong>5 years<\/strong>, with an <strong>intermediate inspection<\/strong> between the 2nd and 3rd anniversary.<\/li><li>An <strong>interim certificate<\/strong> lasts up to <strong>6 months<\/strong>, for example for a new ship or a change of owner.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Posting<\/strong><\/p><p>A copy of the certificate and the DMLC must be <strong>posted on board<\/strong> where seafarers can see them.<\/p><p><strong>Inspections<\/strong><\/p><p>The 16 areas in the DMLC are what flag and PSC inspectors check against.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>2006, in force 2013, five Titles, MLC certificate and DMLC for 500 GT and over, Part I by the flag, Part II by the shipowner, 5 years with intermediate, and posted on board.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What happens if PSC finds a serious MLC breach?","pa":"<p>PSC can <strong>detain<\/strong> if conditions are <strong>clearly hazardous<\/strong> to seafarers&#x27; safety, health or security, or there&#x27;s a <strong>serious or repeated breach<\/strong> of MLC requirements, including seafarers&#x27; rights (Std A5.2.1(6)). The flag state is informed.<\/p>","asked":["What do you know about MLC?","What is DMLC 1 and 2?","How will you prepare the vessel for MLC inspection ?","What's the difference between DMLC1 and DMLC2?","Validity of MLC Certificate?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",12],["Kaiser Ali",12],["Zia Ul Haq",11],["Examiner not named",8],["James Gilbert",4]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q50","sec":3,"q":"The 14 areas (DMLC) / 16 inspection points (MSN 1848)?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The DMLC originally covered <strong>14 areas<\/strong>. The <strong>2014 amendments<\/strong>, in force January 2017, added <strong>two financial-security items<\/strong>. So both the certified list (MLC <strong>Appendix A5-I<\/strong>) and the PSC inspection list (<strong>Appendix A5-III<\/strong>) now have <strong>16 areas<\/strong>. The UK detail is in <strong>MSN 1848 (M) Amd 4<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The 16 areas<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Minimum age.<\/li><li>Medical certification.<\/li><li>Qualifications of seafarers.<\/li><li>Seafarers&#x27; employment agreements.<\/li><li>Use of a licensed or certified or regulated private recruitment and placement service.<\/li><li>Hours of work or rest.<\/li><li>Manning levels for the ship.<\/li><li>Accommodation.<\/li><li>On-board recreational facilities.<\/li><li>Food and catering.<\/li><li>Health and safety and accident prevention.<\/li><li>On-board medical care.<\/li><li>On-board complaint procedures.<\/li><li>Payment of wages.<\/li><li>Financial security for repatriation, including abandonment (added 2014).<\/li><li>Financial security relating to shipowners&#x27; liability for death and long-term disability (added 2014).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>A memory aid<\/strong><\/p><p>I group them: <strong>people<\/strong> (age, medical, qualifications, recruitment), <strong>contract<\/strong> (SEA, wages, hours, manning), <strong>living<\/strong> (accommodation, recreation, food), <strong>care<\/strong> (health and safety, medical care, complaints), and <strong>money<\/strong> (the two financial securities).<\/p><p><strong>The evidence an inspector looks for on my container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SEAs<\/strong> signed by both parties, the CBA, and the <strong>wage accounts<\/strong> and allotment records.<\/li><li><strong>Rest-hour records<\/strong> and the posted <strong>table of working arrangements<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>ENG1s<\/strong> and <strong>CoCs<\/strong>, and STCW certificates.<\/li><li>The <strong>list of young persons<\/strong>, if any.<\/li><li>The <strong>recruitment agency<\/strong> licence or certification for the manning agent.<\/li><li>The <strong>Safe Manning Document<\/strong> compared with the crew list.<\/li><li><strong>Accommodation<\/strong> and recreation spaces in good condition; internet access.<\/li><li><strong>Food<\/strong>: the cook&#x27;s certificate, the stores, and the Master&#x27;s weekly <strong>food and accommodation inspection<\/strong> entries in the OLB.<\/li><li><strong>Safety committee minutes<\/strong>, risk assessments and accident records.<\/li><li>The <strong>medical chest<\/strong> and medical log, and the medical care training of the officers.<\/li><li>The <strong>complaint procedure<\/strong> on the noticeboard and in the SEA.<\/li><li>The <strong>two financial-security certificates<\/strong> posted on board.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What happens if they&#x27;re not met<\/strong><\/p><p>Deficiencies, and if serious, for example wages unpaid or the ship unhygienic, <strong>detention<\/strong> under MLC Standard A5.2.1.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 16 areas and the 2014 addition of the two financial securities, and a few examples of the evidence. He may pick one area and ask what I&#x27;d show him.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Show me what evidence you'd produce for item 15.","pa":"<p>Item 15 is <strong>financial security for repatriation<\/strong>, meaning abandonment. Show the insurer&#x27;s <strong>certificate (Appendix A2-I)<\/strong>, posted in a conspicuous place on board. It gives the ship&#x27;s name and IMO number, the insurer and contact point, and the validity dates.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q51","sec":3,"q":"Latest MLC amendments?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":"MLC amendments 2024/2027","a":"<p><strong>The 2022 amendments: in force 23 December 2024<\/strong><\/p><p>These are the most recent amendments in force. They came from the lessons of the COVID crisis.<\/p><ol><li><strong>Social connectivity.<\/strong> Seafarers should have reasonable access to ship-to-shore communication, including <strong>internet<\/strong>, with any charges kept reasonable (A3.1).<\/li><li><strong>Food and water.<\/strong> <strong>Drinking water must be free of charge<\/strong>, and meals must be nutritionally balanced, of suitable quality and variety (A3.2).<\/li><li><strong>PPE.<\/strong> Personal protective equipment must be <strong>appropriately sized<\/strong>, including for women seafarers (A4.3).<\/li><li><strong>Financial security.<\/strong> The certificates for repatriation and shipowners&#x27; liability must show the <strong>registered shipowner<\/strong>, and providers must give notice before the security is cancelled.<\/li><li><strong>Repatriation<\/strong>: seafarers must be repatriated promptly, and the shipowner can&#x27;t ask the seafarer to pay an advance towards it.<\/li><li><strong>Recruitment services<\/strong>: they must tell seafarers about their rights and the financial-security arrangements.<\/li><li><strong>Medical care<\/strong>: more emphasis on prompt medical care ashore and on handling the death of a seafarer properly, including repatriation of remains.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Earlier amendments<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>2014, in force January 2017<\/strong>: financial security for <strong>abandonment<\/strong> and for <strong>shipowners&#x27; liability<\/strong>. <strong>Two months&#x27; unpaid wages<\/strong> counts as abandonment, and the provider pays up to four months&#x27; wages.<\/li><li><strong>2016, in force January 2019<\/strong>: harassment and bullying guidance, and the extension of MLC certificates in some cases.<\/li><li><strong>2018, in force December 2020<\/strong>: the SEA continues during <strong>piracy<\/strong> captivity (A2.1.7).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The 2025 amendments<\/strong><\/p><p>Further MLC amendments were adopted by the ILO in 2025, covering:<\/p><ul><li>explicit protection against <strong>harassment and bullying<\/strong>, including sexual harassment;<\/li><li><strong>shore leave<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>seafarers as key workers<\/strong>, and better repatriation and medical care in a crisis.<\/li><\/ul><p>Check the entry-into-force date before quoting it (verify; my notes say December 2027).<\/p><p><strong>What I do as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Make sure the <strong>internet policy<\/strong> is in place and fair.<\/li><li>Drinking water is <strong>free<\/strong> and the free-water policy is clear, not charged on the slop chest.<\/li><li><strong>PPE<\/strong> in the right sizes, including small sizes.<\/li><li>The financial-security certificates on board name the registered shipowner.<\/li><\/ul><p>PSC will check these on board.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The latest amendments with their date, the four or five main points, and that I&#x27;ve done something about them on board. Mentioning the 2025 amendments shows I&#x27;m up to date, as long as I don&#x27;t overstate the dates.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is \"abandonment\" under MLC, and who pays?","pa":"<p><strong>Abandonment<\/strong> (Std A2.5.2) is when the owner fails to pay for repatriation, or leaves the seafarer without maintenance and support, or otherwise cuts ties, including <strong>2 months&#x27; unpaid wages<\/strong>. The <strong>financial security provider<\/strong> pays:<\/p><ul><li>up to <strong>4 months&#x27;<\/strong> outstanding wages;<\/li><li>repatriation;<\/li><li>essential needs: food, water, accommodation, fuel and medical care.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["Recent amendment to MLC?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",1],["Examiner not named",1]],"yrs":"2022–2024"},{"id":"Q52","sec":3,"q":"What is in an SEA and who signs it?","tier":"red","rep":58,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Internationally <strong>MLC Regulation 2.1 and Standard A2.1<\/strong>. In the UK, the Merchant Shipping (Maritime Labour Convention) (Minimum Requirements for Seafarers etc.) Regulations 2014.<\/p><p><strong>Who signs<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>seafarer<\/strong> and the <strong>shipowner<\/strong>, or the shipowner&#x27;s representative. The Master often signs as the owner&#x27;s representative.<\/li><li>Before signing, the seafarer must be given the <strong>opportunity to read it and take advice<\/strong>, and to sign freely.<\/li><li>The seafarer keeps a <strong>signed original<\/strong>. A copy is kept <strong>on board<\/strong>, available to inspectors.<\/li><li>It must be in English, as well as any other language, for ships on international voyages.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Contents: Standard A2.1.4<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The seafarer&#x27;s <strong>full name<\/strong>, date of birth or age, and birthplace.<\/li><li>The <strong>shipowner&#x27;s name and address<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>place and date<\/strong> the SEA was made.<\/li><li>The <strong>capacity<\/strong> in which he&#x27;s employed.<\/li><li>The <strong>wages<\/strong>, or the formula for calculating them.<\/li><li><strong>Paid annual leave<\/strong>, at least 2.5 calendar days per month of employment.<\/li><li><strong>Termination<\/strong> conditions, including the notice period, which must be at least <strong>7 days<\/strong>, and for a fixed period, the date of expiry.<\/li><li><strong>Health and social-security benefits<\/strong> provided by the shipowner.<\/li><li><strong>Repatriation<\/strong> entitlement.<\/li><li>Reference to any <strong>collective bargaining agreement<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Any other particulars national law requires.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Record of employment<\/strong><\/p><p>The seafarer also gets a <strong>record of employment<\/strong> on board, for example a discharge book entry. It must <strong>not comment on the quality of his work or his wages<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Related points<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The SEA incorporates the CBA and the company&#x27;s Code of Conduct and complaint procedure.<\/li><li>The seafarer must have a copy of the <strong>on-board complaint procedure<\/strong> with his SEA.<\/li><li>During <strong>piracy<\/strong> captivity the SEA continues (A2.1.7).<\/li><li>Amendments, such as an extension, must be agreed and signed by both parties.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I check as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On joining, <strong>every crew member has a signed SEA<\/strong>, and the copy on board matches.<\/li><li>The capacity and the wage scale match what he&#x27;s actually doing.<\/li><li>Expiry dates are tracked so nobody overruns the 12-month limit.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Unsigned, missing or incorrect SEAs are a common <strong>PSC deficiency<\/strong>, and they were the subject of an earlier CIC on crew wages and SEAs. Serious cases can lead to detention.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MLC A2.1, signed by the seafarer and the shipowner or representative, a chance to read and take advice, the main contents, the original to the seafarer and a copy on board.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can an SEA give less than the CBA or MLC minimum?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> An SEA can&#x27;t be less favourable than the MLC minimum or the applicable CBA. A term below that is unenforceable, and PSC or the flag will treat it as a deficiency.<\/p>","asked":["What's in your SEA (seafarer employment agreement)?","Sea duration/validity?","What's in the seafarer's employment agreement?","During ur watch u saw red flare and receive distress in RT open sea?","What is the Master obliged to report at sea?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",12],["Zia Ul Haq",11],["Chris Roe",5],["James Gilbert",5],["Shiva Balasubramanian",3]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q53","sec":3,"q":"Master's obligation for accommodation, food and water inspections?","tier":"yellow","rep":11,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Internationally <strong>MLC Standard A3.1<\/strong> (accommodation) and <strong>A3.2<\/strong> (food and catering), with A3.2.7 requiring frequent inspections. In the UK, the MLC regulations on food and catering, with guidance in <strong>MSN 1845<\/strong> (verify the current amendment), and the MLC crew accommodation regulations. My handout cited the 1989 and 1997 Regulations; those are superseded.<\/p><p><strong>Food and drinking water: at least every 7 days<\/strong><\/p><p>The Master, or an officer acting under his authority, inspects with a member of the <strong>catering staff<\/strong>. It covers:<\/p><ul><li>supplies of food and drinking water: quantity, quality, variety, nutritional value;<\/li><li>storerooms and refrigerated spaces;<\/li><li>the galley and its equipment, and the handling of food.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Accommodation: at least every 7 days<\/strong><\/p><p>The Master, or an officer, inspects with one or more crew members. It covers:<\/p><ul><li>cleanliness and good order;<\/li><li>habitability: ventilation, heating, lighting, noise;<\/li><li>sanitary spaces, laundry, hospital, and recreation spaces;<\/li><li>maintenance and repair.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><p>The results are recorded in the <strong>Official Log Book<\/strong>, with any defects and the action taken. Inspectors look at these entries.<\/p><p><strong>What I look for<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Food<\/strong>: quantity for the voyage, quality, stock rotation and expiry dates, no damaged packaging, dietary and religious requirements catered for.<\/li><li><strong>Temperatures<\/strong>: fridge and freezer logs, thermometers working. Chilled food 0 to 5°C, frozen at or below −18°C (verify company limits).<\/li><li><strong>Vermin<\/strong> and cleanliness: droppings, cockroaches, pest control records.<\/li><li><strong>Drinking water<\/strong>: chlorination, test results, tank cleaning records, hoses clean and capped. Water is free of charge since the 2022 amendments.<\/li><li><strong>Galley fire safety<\/strong>: hood and filters clean, fryer thermostat and cut-out, fire blanket, extinguishers.<\/li><li><strong>Hygiene of food handlers<\/strong>: clean clothing, fit for work, food hygiene training.<\/li><li><strong>Accommodation<\/strong>: cabins clean and in repair, ventilation and air conditioning working, sanitary spaces clean and drained, laundry machines working, recreation room and internet access.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Defects go into the PMS or to the company.<\/li><li>I check at the next inspection that they&#x27;ve been fixed.<\/li><li>Complaints about food or accommodation are handled through the on-board complaint procedure.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MLC A3.1 and A3.2, every 7 days, by the Master or delegate with a crew or catering member, what&#x27;s covered, and recorded in the OLB.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What would you do if you found the freezer at –5 °C?","pa":"<p>Check the temperature log and how long it&#x27;s been warm. Condemn food that has thawed beyond safe limits, and move the rest to other cold rooms. Get the engineers to repair the freezer (it should run at about –18 °C). Record it in the provisions inspection entry, inform the Master and company, and order replacements.<\/p>","asked":["MLC accommodation inspection?","Accommodation inspection how often and how will you do it. Where will you log it?","During cabin inspection you found white powder type material on table in one of the crew cabin, what will you do in this situation?","Weekly inspection of accommodation as per MLC.","How would you conduct weekly inspections?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",4],["Chris Roe",2],["Nayyer Naqvi",1],["Kaiser Ali",1],["Jason Pateman",1]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q54","sec":3,"q":"Complaints procedure?","tier":"yellow","rep":14,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First, a correction.<\/strong> The old <strong>MSA s.44 rule<\/strong>, which needed &quot;three or more seamen&quot; to complain about provisions, is <strong>superseded<\/strong>. Today it&#x27;s the <strong>MLC on-board complaint procedure<\/strong>, which <strong>any single seafarer<\/strong> can use.<\/p><p><strong>Reference:<\/strong> MLC <strong>Regulation 5.1.5 and Standard A5.1.5<\/strong>, implemented in the UK through the MLC regulations, with the procedure described in the DMLC Part II and the company&#x27;s SMS.<\/p><p><strong>Who can complain<\/strong><\/p><p>Any seafarer, about <strong>any alleged breach of the MLC requirements<\/strong>, including his rights as a seafarer: wages, hours, food, accommodation, harassment, medical care.<\/p><p><strong>The process<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The complaint should be resolved at the <strong>lowest level possible<\/strong>: first the <strong>head of department<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If not resolved, to the <strong>Master<\/strong>, who handles it personally.<\/li><li>If still not resolved, to the <strong>company<\/strong> ashore.<\/li><li>But the seafarer can also go <strong>directly to the Master<\/strong>, and has the right to complain to <strong>external authorities<\/strong> at any time: the <strong>MCA<\/strong> as flag State, a <strong>PSC officer<\/strong> in a foreign port, or the authority in his <strong>country of residence<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Rights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>He can be <strong>accompanied or represented<\/strong> by another seafarer of his choice.<\/li><li>Complaints must be <strong>recorded<\/strong>, with a copy of the outcome to the seafarer.<\/li><li>There must be <strong>no victimisation<\/strong> of a seafarer for making a complaint.<\/li><li>Time limits for each step should be set in the procedure.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Information<\/strong><\/p><p>Every seafarer gets a <strong>copy of the procedure<\/strong> with his SEA. It includes:<\/p><ul><li>contact details for the <strong>flag State (MCA)<\/strong> and the authority of his <strong>country of residence<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the name of a <strong>person on board<\/strong> who can give confidential, impartial advice on the complaint.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><p>The complaint and the outcome are recorded, and a copy goes to the seafarer.<\/p><p><strong>As C/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I make sure the procedure is <strong>posted<\/strong>, and that crew <strong>know it exists<\/strong>.<\/li><li>I take complaints seriously and deal with them quickly and fairly, rather than discouraging them.<\/li><li>If the complaint is about me, I pass it to the Master straight away.<\/li><li>I look at the cause: a food complaint may be a budget or a cook problem, which the company needs to fix.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>A complaint that&#x27;s ignored on board often becomes a PSC complaint and an inspection, and sometimes a detention.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MLC A5.1.5, any seafarer, lowest level first, but direct access to the Master and external authorities, accompanied, recorded, no victimisation, and a copy of the procedure with the SEA.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A rating wants to complain directly to PSC. Can you stop him?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> MLC (Reg 5.1.5 and Std A5.2.2) gives seafarers the right to complain <strong>directly<\/strong> to the Master <strong>and to external authorities<\/strong>, including PSC, without victimisation. The on-board procedure doesn&#x27;t take that right away.<\/p>","asked":["Onboard complaints procedure?","You are chief off, and crew complains you about some other crew abused him. How you will deal with this situation?","Other investigations, Complaints, Representations and recommendations.","How do you know loadicator is complaint before checking all sensors / test results etc – class approved stamp","You received the complaint against Bosun he is bulling AB, what are your actions going to be?"],"ex":[["Khairul Anam",2],["Zahid Raza",2],["David Carlisle",2],["Chris Williams",2],["Chris Roe",1]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q55","sec":3,"q":"Disciplinary procedure? Code of Conduct — gross misconduct vs lesser offences?","tier":"yellow","rep":7,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>On UK ships discipline is handled under the <strong>Code of Conduct for the Merchant Navy<\/strong>, agreed by the UK Chamber of Shipping with Nautilus International and the RMT (check the current edition). It&#x27;s <strong>incorporated into the SEA<\/strong>. Criminal matters fall under the Merchant Shipping Act 1995 and the general criminal law.<\/p><p><strong>The emergency rule<\/strong><\/p><p>When the safety of the ship, the people on board or the environment is at stake, <strong>orders must be obeyed immediately and without question<\/strong>. Disputes are dealt with afterwards.<\/p><p><strong>Lesser misconduct: a graduated response<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Informal warning<\/strong>, by the head of department or a supervisor.<\/li><li><strong>Formal warning<\/strong> by an officer, recorded.<\/li><li><strong>Written warning<\/strong> by a senior officer.<\/li><li><strong>Final written warning<\/strong> by a senior officer or the Master.<\/li><\/ol><p>Examples: lateness for watch, poor work, minor breaches of company rules, being absent without permission for a short time.<\/p><p><strong>Gross misconduct: can lead to dismissal<\/strong><\/p><p>Examples:<\/p><ul><li><strong>assault<\/strong> or violence, or threats of violence;<\/li><li><strong>drugs<\/strong>, or being unfit for duty through alcohol or drugs;<\/li><li><strong>theft<\/strong> or wilful damage to the ship or property;<\/li><li>serious <strong>harassment or bullying<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>conduct endangering<\/strong> the ship or people, including refusing a lawful order relating to safety, or sleeping on watch;<\/li><li>serious breach of security, for example letting a stranger on board.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The procedure<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Make the situation safe.<\/strong> Relieve the person from duty if needed.<\/li><li><strong>Investigate<\/strong>: interview people separately, take statements, keep evidence.<\/li><li><strong>Tell the seafarer the allegation<\/strong> in writing.<\/li><li><strong>Hold a hearing<\/strong>. He may be accompanied by a friend or representative. He&#x27;s allowed to give his side.<\/li><li><strong>Decide<\/strong>, fairly and consistently, and tell him the decision and his <strong>right of appeal<\/strong> to the company.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Formal and higher outcomes are recorded in the <strong>OLB<\/strong>, with the seafarer&#x27;s reply, and a copy given to the seafarer.<\/li><li>Reported to the company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Dismissal<\/strong><\/p><p>Only the <strong>Master<\/strong> can dismiss from the ship, for gross misconduct, after a proper hearing. The company handles repatriation; for gross misconduct the costs may be recoverable from the seafarer in some cases under the SEA.<\/p><p><strong>Why a fair procedure matters<\/strong><\/p><p>An unfair dismissal can be challenged, and a disciplinary process done badly destroys morale and trust.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Code of Conduct, immediate obedience in emergencies, the warning ladder, examples of gross misconduct, a fair hearing with a friend, right of appeal, and OLB records.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who can issue a final written warning?","pa":"<p>Under the <strong>Code of Conduct for the Merchant Navy<\/strong>, formal and <strong>final written warnings<\/strong> are given by the <strong>Master<\/strong>. Dismissal goes to the company. Check the current edition before quoting it.<\/p>","asked":["How you know it's a gross misconduct?","From where you can get procedure regarding crew misconduct ?","Crew disciplinary, how would you know how to discipline and what to follow?","ARE DRUNK & ASSAULTING EACH OTHER, WHAT WILL YOU DO?","Crew comes from shore, were drunk and had a fight on board, action as a master?"],"ex":[["Ranjit Joseph",2],["David Carlisle",2],["Robert Jackson",1],["Zia Ul Haq",1],["Doug Smith",1]],"yrs":"2021–2025"},{"id":"Q56","sec":3,"q":"C/O bullying ratings and under-recording their work hours — actions as Master?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>This is two serious problems by a senior officer: <strong>falsified rest-hour records<\/strong>, which breach the Hours of Work Regs 2018, <strong>MSN 1877 (M) Amd 2<\/strong> and <strong>MLC Standard A2.3<\/strong>, and <strong>harassment and bullying<\/strong>. Both can be gross misconduct.<\/p><p><strong>1. Protect the ratings<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Interview them <strong>privately and separately<\/strong>, so they can speak freely.<\/li><li>Make sure they <strong>actually get their rest now<\/strong>, and that the work is reorganised.<\/li><li>Assure them there will be <strong>no victimisation<\/strong>, as the MLC complaint procedure requires.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Establish the facts<\/strong><\/p><p>Compare the rest-hour sheets with <strong>independent evidence<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li>the deck log and engine log;<\/li><li>cargo and port times, the terminal&#x27;s records;<\/li><li>overtime sheets and wage accounts;<\/li><li>CCTV or access records, the gangway log;<\/li><li>PMS job records with times.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Hear the C/O&#x27;s side<\/strong><\/p><p>Formally, under the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong>, with a written allegation, the right to be accompanied, and a hearing. Deliberate falsification of statutory records and bullying can be <strong>gross misconduct<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>4. Correct the records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Correct the rest-hour records, signed by me and each seafarer, with an explanation.<\/li><li><strong>Fix the work planning<\/strong> that caused it. Often the real cause is too much work for the crew available, or pressure to show &quot;compliant&quot; records.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Report<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To the <strong>company and DPA<\/strong> as an <strong>ISM non-conformity<\/strong>.<\/li><li>An <strong>OLB<\/strong> entry for any formal disciplinary outcome.<\/li><li>The company may decide to relieve the C/O.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brief all crew on the <strong>anti-harassment and bullying policy<\/strong> and the <strong>complaint procedure<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the workload is the root cause, ask the company for <strong>extra crew<\/strong>, or consult the MCA about manning.<\/li><li>Monitor the records more closely, and talk to the crew informally.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Look at myself<\/strong><\/p><p>As Master I <strong>sign off the rest-hour records<\/strong>. So how did I miss it? Did I check them against the actual work? Did the crew feel unable to come to me? That&#x27;s a failure of my oversight and of the safety culture.<\/p><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Fatigue is a major cause of accidents in MAIB reports.<\/li><li>Falsified records are a PSC detention item and an offence.<\/li><li>Bullying drives good crew away and stops people reporting problems.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Protect the ratings, get the facts from independent evidence, a fair hearing for the C/O under the Code of Conduct, correct the records, report as an NC, fix the cause, and recognise my own responsibility.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What does PSC do if they find rest-hour records that don't match the logbook?","pa":"<p>It&#x27;s an MLC and STCW deficiency, and falsified records can count as a <strong>serious breach<\/strong>. PSC may expand the inspection into fatigue and manning, can <strong>detain<\/strong>, and will inform the flag. The company must treat it as an ISM non-conformity.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q57","sec":3,"q":"Resolving a dispute between crew?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>The aim is to stop a dispute becoming a <strong>safety or disciplinary issue<\/strong>, without taking sides too early. On a small crew, a dispute affects everyone.<\/p><p><strong>1. Make it safe now<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Separate<\/strong> the people if there&#x27;s any risk of violence.<\/li><li>If needed, change their <strong>duties or watches<\/strong> temporarily, so they don&#x27;t work together on a hazardous job.<\/li><li>Make sure the watch and the ship are safe.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Listen separately<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Interview each person <strong>in private<\/strong>, then talk to witnesses.<\/li><li>Look for the <strong>real cause<\/strong>:<\/li><li>workload or unfair allocation of work;<\/li><li>language or <strong>cultural misunderstanding<\/strong>;<\/li><li>fatigue;<\/li><li>money, debts, gambling;<\/li><li>alcohol;<\/li><li>bullying or harassment;<\/li><li>personal or family problems at home.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Mediate<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If they&#x27;re both willing, bring them together with a <strong>neutral senior officer<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Let each say what the problem is, without interruption.<\/li><li>Agree <strong>clear expectations<\/strong> of behaviour and work.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> what was agreed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Escalate if needed<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the dispute involves <strong>misconduct<\/strong>, such as threats, assault or harassment, use the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong> procedure.<\/li><li>If someone feels wronged, remind them of the <strong>MLC on-board complaint procedure<\/strong> (A5.1.5), which they can use without fear of victimisation.<\/li><li><strong>Assault is gross misconduct<\/strong> and may be a <strong>police matter<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Welfare<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check for <strong>mental-health or welfare issues<\/strong>. Long contracts, isolation and family problems can make small things big.<\/li><li>Offer support through the company welfare contacts or seafarer helplines, such as ISWAN&#x27;s SeafarerHelp.<\/li><li>Make sure they get shore leave and internet access where possible.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Record and follow up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Inform the <strong>Master<\/strong> and, where needed, the <strong>company<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Check again a few days later<\/strong>. A dispute that seems settled can come back.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Prevent the next one<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Fair work allocation, clear communication, a common working language (English), and leadership that deals with problems early.<\/li><li>Cultural awareness, especially on mixed-nationality crews.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Make it safe, listen separately, find the cause, mediate, escalate under the Code of Conduct or the complaint procedure if needed, look after welfare, record and follow up. Leadership and managerial skill is an STCW A-II/2 competence, so the examiner is testing how I&#x27;d manage people.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"One party says the other is threatening him with a knife. What changes?","pa":"<p>It becomes a threat to life and a security matter:<\/p><ul><li>separate them and make everyone safe;<\/li><li>take the weapon only if it can be done safely;<\/li><li><strong>restrain<\/strong> if necessary, under the Master&#x27;s authority;<\/li><li>inform the Master, make an OLB entry and take statements;<\/li><li>treat it as a possible crime: report to the company and flag, and to police at the next port;<\/li><li>get medical checks.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q58","sec":3,"q":"Young persons: rules and how you'd employ a 17-year-old cadet?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":"MSN 1838 Amd 2","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, <strong>MSN 1838 (M) Amd 2<\/strong>, under the MS&amp;FV (Health and Safety at Work) (Employment of Young Persons) Regulations 1998, together with <strong>MLC Regulation 1.1<\/strong> and Standard A1.1.<\/p><p><strong>Definitions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Minimum age<\/strong> to work on a ship is <strong>16<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>young person<\/strong> is anyone <strong>under 18<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Risk assessment first<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>specific risk assessment<\/strong> must be done for young persons <strong>before<\/strong> they start work, taking account of their inexperience, lack of awareness of risks and immaturity.<\/li><li>The findings are <strong>explained<\/strong> to them.<\/li><li>They mustn&#x27;t do work beyond their physical or psychological capacity, or work involving exposure to harmful agents, radiation, extreme heat or cold, noise or vibration, <strong>unless<\/strong> it&#x27;s needed for training and they&#x27;re <strong>supervised<\/strong> by a competent person, with the risk reduced.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rest<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>12 hours&#x27; rest in any 24 hours<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>2 days&#x27; rest per week<\/strong>, consecutive if possible.<\/li><li>If daily work is over <strong>4.5 hours<\/strong>, a break of at least <strong>30 minutes<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Night work<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>No night work<\/strong>. Night means at least <strong>9 consecutive hours including midnight to 05:00<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The exception is where it&#x27;s part of an <strong>approved training programme<\/strong>, for example a watch with an officer as part of the cadet&#x27;s training record book, and it doesn&#x27;t harm his health.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Medical<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>His <strong>ENG1<\/strong> is valid for <strong>1 year<\/strong> while he&#x27;s under 18.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>List of Young Persons<\/strong> is kept with the crew list.<\/li><li>His rest hours are recorded like everyone else&#x27;s and checked by me.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>For my 17-year-old cadet<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> done before he starts and briefed to him.<\/li><li><strong>Day work<\/strong> only, unless a watch with an officer is part of his training record book.<\/li><li><strong>Hazardous tasks<\/strong> such as enclosed-space entry, working aloft or overside, or heavy lifting, only under close supervision as the risk assessment allows, or not at all.<\/li><li><strong>Familiarisation<\/strong> and a <strong>mentor or buddy<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rest hours monitored<\/strong> daily.<\/li><li>No alcohol, whatever the company policy for adults.<\/li><li>Welfare: contact home, someone to talk to.<\/li><li>His <strong>training record book<\/strong> kept up to date and signed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Young persons have been killed and seriously hurt in enclosed spaces, mooring and falls. The regulations reflect that.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Under 18, risk assessment first and explained, 12 hours&#x27; rest in 24, 2 days a week, no night work between midnight and 05:00 except for approved training, ENG1 for 1 year, list of young persons, and supervision.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can the cadet keep the 0000–0400 watch with the 2/O?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> Under-18s must not work at <strong>night<\/strong>: at least 9 hours including midnight to 0500 (MSN 1838 Amd 2, MLC Std A1.1). There&#x27;s only a limited exception for an approved training programme. Check MSN 1838 before relying on it.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q59","sec":3,"q":"Hours of rest and the limits on exceptions?","tier":"red","rep":62,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":"MSN 1877 Amd 2","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Merchant Shipping (Hours of Work) Regulations 2018<\/strong> and <strong>MSN 1877 (M) Amd 2<\/strong>, which apply <strong>STCW A-VIII/1<\/strong> and <strong>MLC Standard A2.3<\/strong>. My notes cited MSN 1842, MGN 566 and MSN 1767; those are out of date.<\/p><p><strong>Minimum rest<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>10 hours<\/strong> in any 24 hours.<\/li><li><strong>77 hours<\/strong> in any 7 days.<\/li><li>Rest can be split into <strong>no more than 2 periods<\/strong>, one of them at least <strong>6 hours<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>No more than 14 hours<\/strong> between consecutive rest periods.<\/li><li>Musters, fire and lifeboat drills, and drills required by law, must be done in a way that minimises the disturbance of rest and doesn&#x27;t cause fatigue.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Exceptions: only under a collective agreement, approved by the MCA<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Weekly rest<\/strong> can drop to <strong>70 hours<\/strong> in 7 days, but not for more than <strong>2 consecutive weeks<\/strong>. The interval between two exception periods must be at least <strong>twice the length<\/strong> of the exception.<\/li><li><strong>Daily rest<\/strong> can be split into up to <strong>3 periods<\/strong>, one at least 6 hours and neither of the others less than 1 hour, still with no more than 14 hours between them.<\/li><li>Daily exceptions can&#x27;t apply on more than <strong>two 24-hour periods<\/strong> in any 7 days.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Emergencies<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Master can <strong>suspend<\/strong> the rest schedule for the immediate safety of the ship, people or cargo, or to help other ships or persons in distress.<\/li><li>Afterwards, he must give <strong>compensatory rest<\/strong> to everyone who worked during their rest period, as soon as practicable.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On-call work<\/strong><\/p><p>If a seafarer on call, for example with UMS, is called out during his rest, he gets <strong>compensatory rest<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>table of shipboard working arrangements<\/strong> is posted, in the working language and English, showing the schedule at sea and in port and the limits.<\/li><li><strong>Daily records<\/strong> of each seafarer&#x27;s hours of rest, signed by the <strong>Master<\/strong> (or a person he authorises) and the <strong>seafarer<\/strong>, who gets a <strong>copy<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The records are kept and available for inspection.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Watchkeepers<\/strong><\/p><p>STCW also requires watch arrangements so that the first watch at the start of a voyage and the following watches are by officers who are rested and fit.<\/p><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>Plan the deck work around the rest hours, check the records against the actual work, and report to the Master if the work can&#x27;t be done within the limits. Never falsify.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The numbers exactly, the exceptions with their limits, the emergency rule with compensatory rest, and the records signed by both.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your crew are working cargo all night in port. How do you stay legal?","pa":"<p>Plan cargo watches in shifts so everyone gets <strong>10 h in 24 and 77 h in 7 days<\/strong>. Use shore labour for lashing where possible, bring in extra crew, or adjust the timings. Give compensatory rest, and keep the records honest.<\/p>","asked":["What do you know about hours of rest ?","Work and rest Hour requirement?","What are the hours of work and rest?","What are crew rest hours?","Ensuring crew members complete their records of work and rest."],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",9],["Examiner not named",8],["Byron Griffiths",6],["Khairul Anam",4],["Muhammad Iqbal",4]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q60","sec":3,"q":"ENG1 validity?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":"MSN 1886 Amd 2","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>Merchant Shipping (Maritime Labour Convention) (Medical Certification) Regulations 2010<\/strong>, with guidance in <strong>MSN 1886 (M+F) Amd 2<\/strong> on the medical standards and the ENG1 system, and MSN 1887 on medical certification. Internationally it&#x27;s <strong>MLC Standard A1.2<\/strong> and <strong>STCW I/9<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Who issues it<\/strong><\/p><p>An <strong>MCA-approved doctor<\/strong>, after an examination against the medical standards in MSN 1886.<\/p><p><strong>Validity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Maximum <strong>2 years<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>1 year<\/strong> if the seafarer is <strong>under 18<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>colour-vision<\/strong> part can last up to <strong>6 years<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It can be issued for a shorter period if the doctor wants to review a condition.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Restrictions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The doctor can issue a <strong>restricted ENG1<\/strong>, for example &quot;no lookout duties&quot;, &quot;near-coastal only&quot;, &quot;not fit for watchkeeping at night&quot; or &quot;requires glasses&quot;.<\/li><li>The restriction must be respected when I allocate duties and watches.<\/li><li>A <strong>temporary unfit<\/strong> (T) result means he can&#x27;t work until he&#x27;s re-examined.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If it expires during a voyage<\/strong><\/p><p>It stays valid <strong>until the next port of call<\/strong> where an MCA-approved or recognised doctor is available, for <strong>no more than 3 months<\/strong> (MLC A1.2.9).<\/p><p><strong>If found unfit<\/strong><\/p><p>The seafarer can ask for a <strong>review<\/strong> by an independent <strong>medical referee<\/strong> appointed by the MCA.<\/p><p><strong>Foreign seafarers on UK ships<\/strong><\/p><p>They can use medical certificates from countries the MCA accepts as equivalent, listed in <strong>MSN 1815<\/strong> (check the current list).<\/p><p><strong>Urgent cases<\/strong><\/p><p>In urgent cases, the MCA may allow a seafarer to work without a valid certificate until the next port where he can get one, for up to 3 months, if his previous certificate was recently expired (verify the exact rule).<\/p><p><strong>What I check as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Every <strong>ENG1<\/strong> at joining: valid, the right person, any restrictions.<\/li><li><strong>Expiry dates<\/strong> tracked against the contract length.<\/li><li>Restrictions applied in the watch and work plan.<\/li><li>Anyone who becomes ill or injured on board may need a new assessment before going back on watch.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>An expired medical is a <strong>PSC deficiency<\/strong> and an <strong>MLC breach<\/strong>, and an unfit seafarer on watch is a safety risk. After an accident, an expired ENG1 is the first thing an investigator or insurer looks at.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Approved doctor, 2 years or 1 under 18, colour vision 6 years, restrictions, valid to the next port up to 3 months if it expires at sea, review by a medical referee, and MSN 1886.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your AB's ENG1 expires tomorrow, mid-Atlantic. Can he keep working?","pa":"<p>Yes, until the <strong>next port where a recognised doctor<\/strong> is available, for a maximum of <strong>3 months<\/strong> (MLC Std A1.2 para 9). Then he needs a new certificate.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q61","sec":3,"q":"Galley inspections (COSWP Ch 23, MLC 3.2)?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Code of Safe Working Practices<\/strong> (2026 edition, MIN 688 Amd 5), chapter on food preparation and handling in the galley; <strong>MLC Regulation 3.2<\/strong> and Standard A3.2; and in the UK the food and catering guidance in <strong>MSN 1845<\/strong>. The Master, or his delegate, inspects the galley and stores <strong>at least every 7 days<\/strong>, with a member of the catering staff, and records it in the <strong>OLB<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>What I check<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. Hygiene<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Surfaces, utensils, equipment and the deckhead clean.<\/li><li>Raw and cooked food kept separate, with <strong>colour-coded<\/strong> chopping boards and knives.<\/li><li>No pests, droppings or signs of infestation, and the pest control records.<\/li><li>Food handlers in clean clothing, hair covered, <strong>fit for work<\/strong>, with no infection, and washing hands.<\/li><li>Bins lidded and emptied.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Temperatures and stock<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Chillers and freezers within limits, with <strong>temperature logs<\/strong> kept and checked; thermometers working.<\/li><li>Stock <strong>rotated<\/strong> first in, first out, and in date. Nothing damaged or blown.<\/li><li>Food thawed safely, and cooked to the right temperature.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Fire safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Extractor hoods, grease filters and exhaust ducts<\/strong> clean. A galley duct fire is a major fire source, and the ducts are often forgotten.<\/li><li><strong>Fire blanket<\/strong> and suitable extinguishers, a wet chemical type near the deep-fat fryer.<\/li><li>Fryer <strong>thermostat and high-temperature cut-out<\/strong> working.<\/li><li>Fixed fire extinguishing for the fryer and duct, if fitted, in date.<\/li><li>The <strong>emergency stops<\/strong> for galley ventilation and power known and working.<\/li><li>No storing of flammable materials near the range.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Personal safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Non-slip<\/strong> deck, kept clean and dry.<\/li><li><strong>Sea fastenings<\/strong> for pans and equipment, rails on the range, fiddles.<\/li><li><strong>Guards<\/strong> on mixers and slicers, and the cook trained to use them.<\/li><li>Safe <strong>knife storage<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Burns<\/strong> first aid available, and <strong>PPE<\/strong>: oven gloves, cold room gloves, aprons.<\/li><li>Cold room <strong>alarm and inside release<\/strong> working, so nobody can be trapped.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Water and waste<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Drinking water clean, chlorinated and <strong>free<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Food waste and garbage segregated under the <strong>Garbage Management Plan<\/strong>, with food waste comminuted if discharged at sea.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Competence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>cook<\/strong> is properly certificated (MLC requires a qualified ship&#x27;s cook) and food-hygiene trained.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Follow-up<\/strong><\/p><p>Defects go into the <strong>PMS<\/strong> and are followed up at the next inspection. Repeated problems go to the company.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MLC 3.2, COSWP, weekly inspection with the catering staff recorded in the OLB, and specific checks on hygiene, temperatures, fire safety, personal safety and competence.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the main fire risk in a galley and how do you control it?","pa":"<p><strong>Grease<\/strong> in hoods, ducts and deep-fat fryers. Control it with:<\/p><ul><li>a cleaning schedule for the hoods and ducts;<\/li><li>primary and back-up thermostats, and cut-outs on fryers;<\/li><li>never leaving the fryer unattended;<\/li><li>a fire blanket and wet-chemical extinguisher;<\/li><li>duct dampers, a fixed system where fitted, and an emergency power cut-off.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q62","sec":3,"q":"Fresh water tank inspection & drinking water standards?","tier":"red","rep":18,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":"FW tank cleaning — fail, Andrews 2022","law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>MLC Regulation 3.2<\/strong> requires drinking water of suitable quality, and since the <strong>2022 amendments<\/strong> it must be <strong>free of charge<\/strong>. In the UK it sits under the MLC food and catering requirements (MSN 1845) and the MCA&#x27;s potable water guidance (verify the current MGN). Internationally, the <strong>WHO Guide to Ship Sanitation<\/strong>. Port health checks it for the Ship Sanitation Certificate.<\/p><p><strong>Bunkering water<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Take water only from <strong>approved sources<\/strong>, with a quality certificate or test if in doubt.<\/li><li>Use <strong>dedicated, marked hoses<\/strong> for potable water, flushed before use, <strong>capped<\/strong> and stowed off the deck, never used for anything else.<\/li><li><strong>Chlorinate<\/strong> on loading, to the company&#x27;s dose.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Storage and treatment<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Potable tanks must be <strong>separate<\/strong> from other tanks, with <strong>no common boundaries<\/strong> with tanks holding non-potable liquids, and properly <strong>coated<\/strong> with an approved coating.<\/li><li>Air pipes and sounding pipes protected from contamination.<\/li><li>Keep a <strong>free chlorine residual<\/strong>. WHO guidance is about <strong>0.2–0.5 mg/l<\/strong> at the tap (verify the company figure).<\/li><li>Water from the <strong>fresh water generator<\/strong>: only produced away from coasts and polluted water, with <strong>UV<\/strong> or chlorination and <strong>mineralisation<\/strong> units working.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tank inspection<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Tanks are emptied, inspected, cleaned, <strong>recoated<\/strong> if needed and <strong>super-chlorinated<\/strong> before refilling, at the intervals set in the SMS or water safety plan (often annually), and after any contamination or repair.<\/li><li><strong>Tank entry is an enclosed-space entry<\/strong> under the Enclosed Spaces Regs 2022 and <strong>MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong>, with a permit, atmosphere test and standby.<\/li><li>People entering must wear clean protective clothing and footwear, and be free of illness.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Testing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Routine <strong>chlorine and pH<\/strong> tests on board, recorded.<\/li><li>Periodic <strong>laboratory tests<\/strong> for bacteria: <strong>E. coli and coliforms should be zero<\/strong> in a 100 ml sample.<\/li><li><strong>Legionella<\/strong> testing of hot water systems, especially after lay-up or low use. Hot water stored at 60°C, and showers flushed regularly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Distribution<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Filters, softeners and calorifiers maintained.<\/li><li>Dead legs and little-used outlets flushed.<\/li><li>Shower heads cleaned and disinfected.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Water log: bunkering, chlorination, tests.<\/li><li>Tank cleaning records.<\/li><li>The Master&#x27;s weekly inspection entry in the <strong>OLB<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Approved source and dedicated hoses, separate coated tanks, chlorine residual, tank cleaning as an enclosed space entry, lab tests with zero E. coli, Legionella, free water under MLC 2022, and records for port health.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A lab result shows coliforms in the drinking water. Actions?","pa":"<p>Stop drinking from that supply and use bottled water. Tell the Master. <strong>Superchlorinate and flush<\/strong> the system, inspect and clean the tank, and resample until the results are clear. Check the source and the bunkering hoses, record everything, and inform the company and, if needed, port health.<\/p>","asked":["How will you clean fresh water tank?","FRESH WATER TANK INSPECTION, how will you prepare?","Tanks, Lube Oil Tanks & Fresh Water Tanks.","What will you do in freshwater tank in dry dock.","You need to do super chlorination in fresh water tank with tank capacity 320mt. You need to fill the tank with super chlorinated 50ppm, you have super chlorinated solution drums of 20 litre with 16% strength. How may drums of solution you need to fill the tank"],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",6],["Michele Moro",4],["Robert Jackson",2],["Examiner not named",2],["Kaiser Ali",1]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q63","sec":3,"q":"Safety officer, safety reps, safety committee — when required, duties, powers?","tier":"red","rep":86,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Health and Safety at Work) Regulations 1997<\/strong>, as amended to include the safety officials provisions, with guidance in <strong>MGN 636 (M+F) Amd 4<\/strong> and the <strong>COSWP<\/strong> chapter on safety officials (verify the regulation numbers).<\/p><p><strong>When required<\/strong><\/p><p>On ships with <strong>five or more<\/strong> workers (verify the exact threshold), the company must appoint a safety officer, and the crew may elect safety representatives. Where a safety rep has been elected, a safety committee must be set up.<\/p><p><strong>Safety officer<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Appointed by the company<\/strong>, not the Master, and normally not the Master himself. Often the C/O.<\/li><li>The appointment is recorded in the <strong>OLB<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Duties<\/strong>:<\/li><li>make sure <strong>COSWP<\/strong> and the company&#x27;s safety instructions are followed;<\/li><li><strong>investigate<\/strong> accidents, dangerous occurrences, near misses and crew safety complaints, and make recommendations;<\/li><li><strong>inspect<\/strong> every accessible part of the ship at least every <strong>3 months<\/strong>, or more often if needed;<\/li><li>make recommendations to the Master and the company;<\/li><li><strong>promote safety<\/strong> training and awareness;<\/li><li>keep <strong>records<\/strong>: accidents, inspections, investigations;<\/li><li>he may <strong>stop<\/strong> any work he reasonably believes could cause a serious accident, and tell the person in charge.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safety representatives<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Elected by the crew<\/strong>, with at least two years&#x27; sea service (verify).<\/li><li>They have <strong>powers, not duties<\/strong>. They can:<\/li><li>take part in inspections and investigations;<\/li><li>inspect the safety officer&#x27;s records;<\/li><li>make representations to the Master or company on health and safety;<\/li><li>consult the safety officer;<\/li><li>ask the safety officer to investigate.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safety committee<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Required wherever a safety rep has been elected.<\/li><li><strong>Chaired by the Master<\/strong>, with the safety officer and the safety reps as members, and others as needed.<\/li><li>It:<\/li><li>makes sure COSWP is followed;<\/li><li>considers health and safety matters, accident and near miss reports and the safety officer&#x27;s inspection findings;<\/li><li>makes recommendations to the Master and company;<\/li><li>keeps <strong>minutes<\/strong>, which are <strong>posted<\/strong> for the crew and sent to the company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Employer&#x27;s duty<\/strong><\/p><p>The company must give safety officials the time, information and facilities to do their job, and must not penalise them for doing it.<\/p><p><strong>How it fits with ISM<\/strong><\/p><p>These are UK law, applying whether or not the ship is under ISM. The SMS usually adds its own requirements, such as monthly meetings.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Five or more crew, the company appoints the safety officer, the crew elect reps, the Master chairs the committee, the safety officer&#x27;s duties including the 3-monthly inspection and stop-work power, the reps&#x27; powers not duties, and minutes posted.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"If the safety officer stops a job you ordered as C/O, what do you do?","pa":"<p>Respect the stop. Find out his concern and put it right. Restart only when it&#x27;s safe, and refer to the Master if we disagree. Everyone has stop-work authority under the SMS, and overruling a safety stop is wrong.<\/p>","asked":["What are the Duties of the Safety Officer?","How you will carry out Safety officer inspection as a Master?","Which vessels require a Safety Officer?","What are the requirements for a Safety Officer?","What is a safety officer and what is the requirement for having one?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",15],["Byron Griffiths",7],["Examiner not named",7],["James Gilbert",7],["Timothy Cummins",7]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q64","sec":3,"q":"How do you maintain a safety culture on board?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What safety culture is<\/strong><\/p><p>Safety culture is <strong>what people do when no one is watching<\/strong>. The SMS requires the company to establish safeguards against all identified risks and to continuously improve safety management skills (<strong>ISM 1.2.2<\/strong> and 1.2.3). In the UK, <strong>COSWP<\/strong> is the practical standard, and the Health and Safety at Work Regs 1997 make it law.<\/p><p><strong>As Chief Officer I&#x27;d focus on<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Leadership by example.<\/strong> I wear my PPE, follow permits and risk assessments, and never take shortcuts in front of the crew. If I cut corners, they will.<\/li><li><strong>Stop-work authority.<\/strong> Anyone can stop an unsafe job <strong>without blame<\/strong>. I thank the person who does it, even if the job turns out to have been safe.<\/li><li><strong>Reporting.<\/strong> I encourage <strong>near-miss reporting<\/strong> and give <strong>feedback<\/strong> on what changed, so people see their reports matter.<\/li><li><strong>Just culture.<\/strong> Honest mistakes are learning points. Recklessness and deliberate violations are dealt with. People need to know where the line is.<\/li><li><strong>Planning the work.<\/strong> <strong>Real<\/strong> risk assessments, not copied ones; <strong>toolbox talks<\/strong> before every job; realistic workloads <strong>within rest hours<\/strong>. Fatigue kills safety culture.<\/li><li><strong>Training and drills<\/strong> that are realistic and <strong>debriefed<\/strong>, not box-ticking. Different scenarios, in different places, at different times.<\/li><li><strong>Learning from others.<\/strong> <strong>MAIB Safety Digests<\/strong>, <strong>CHIRP Maritime<\/strong> reports, and company fleet alerts discussed in safety meetings.<\/li><li><strong>Involvement.<\/strong> An active <strong>safety committee<\/strong>, safety reps who are listened to, and crew involved in reviewing risk assessments and procedures.<\/li><li><strong>Follow-through.<\/strong> Fix reported defects quickly. Nothing destroys trust faster than reports that disappear.<\/li><li><strong>Communication across nationalities.<\/strong> Make sure everyone understands, using a common working language, and check understanding rather than assuming.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How I&#x27;d measure whether it&#x27;s improving<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Leading indicators<\/strong>: number and quality of near-miss reports, safety observations, toolbox talks held, drills done and debriefed, defects closed on time, training completed.<\/li><li><strong>Lagging indicators<\/strong>: accidents, lost-time injuries, first aid cases, PSC deficiencies, audit non-conformities.<\/li><li><strong>Behaviour<\/strong>: do people use the stop-work card? Do they challenge me? Do they wear PPE without being told?<\/li><li><strong>Surveys<\/strong> or informal conversations with the crew.<\/li><\/ul><p>A rising number of near-miss reports with falling accidents is a good sign. Few near-miss reports usually means people aren&#x27;t reporting.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Leadership, stop-work, reporting and just culture, planning and rest hours, real training, involvement, follow-through, and how I&#x27;d measure it. This is one of his own favourite questions, so give examples from my own ship.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["How would you create a just culture/ positive safety culture onboard?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",1]],"yrs":"2025–2025"},{"id":"★4.1","sec":3,"q":"Your Filipino AB says his wages haven't been paid for two months. What do you do?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>This is a <strong>serious MLC matter<\/strong>, and possibly abandonment.<\/p><p><strong>Reference<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MLC Regulation 2.2 and Standard A2.2<\/strong>: wages must be paid at <strong>no more than monthly intervals<\/strong>, with a <strong>monthly account<\/strong> to the seafarer showing what&#x27;s paid, deductions and the exchange rate.<\/li><li><strong>Standard A2.5.2<\/strong>, from the <strong>2014 amendments<\/strong>: failing to pay contractual wages for <strong>at least two months<\/strong> counts as <strong>abandonment<\/strong>. That brings in the <strong>financial-security provider<\/strong>, who must cover up to <strong>four months&#x27;<\/strong> outstanding wages and entitlements, plus repatriation and essential needs.<\/li><li>In the UK, the MLC regulations, with the financial security certificate posted on board.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Take it seriously<\/strong> and use the <strong>on-board complaint procedure<\/strong>. Record the complaint, and assure him there will be <strong>no victimisation<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Check the facts<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>his <strong>SEA<\/strong> and <strong>CBA<\/strong>: what&#x27;s his wage and when is it due?<\/li><li>the <strong>wage accounts<\/strong> and <strong>allotment<\/strong> records on board;<\/li><li>the <strong>manning agent&#x27;s<\/strong> payment records;<\/li><li>is it <strong>just him<\/strong>, or the <strong>whole crew<\/strong>? If it&#x27;s everyone, it&#x27;s likely a company problem.<\/li><li>could the fault be in the allotment, the bank transfer or a wrong account number?<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong>, if I&#x27;m not him.<\/li><li><strong>Contact the company and manning agent immediately, in writing<\/strong>. Ask for payment and an explanation by a set deadline.<\/li><li><strong>Support the seafarer<\/strong>: keep him informed, allow him to contact his family and his union or welfare organisation, and ask whether he needs a cash advance from the ship&#x27;s cash, if the company agrees.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If it isn&#x27;t resolved<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The seafarer can complain to the <strong>MCA<\/strong> as flag State, or to <strong>PSC<\/strong> in the next port, or to the authority of his own country, such as the Philippines&#x27; Department of Migrant Workers.<\/li><li><strong>Unpaid wages are a ground for detention<\/strong> under MLC enforcement (A5.2.1).<\/li><li>If it amounts to <strong>abandonment<\/strong>, the <strong>financial-security certificate provider<\/strong> must be contacted. The ILO and IMO keep an abandonment database.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>OLB<\/strong> entry, and copies of all correspondence.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters to the ship<\/strong><\/p><p>Unpaid crew are worried, distracted and tired. That&#x27;s a safety problem as well as a legal one, and if the whole crew is unpaid, the ship may be heading for abandonment.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MLC A2.2 monthly wages, two months is abandonment under A2.5.2, the complaint procedure, check the facts, company in writing, his right to go to the flag or PSC, detention possible, and the financial-security provider.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What document on board tells you who to contact for abandonment?","pa":"<p>The insurer&#x27;s <strong>MLC financial security certificate<\/strong> (Appendix A2-I) for repatriation and abandonment, posted on board. It gives the insurer&#x27;s contact point for claims.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★4.2","sec":3,"q":"An AB returns drunk from shore leave and is due on gangway watch. Actions?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. Safety first<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Don&#x27;t let him go on watch.<\/strong> Put a <strong>sober replacement<\/strong> on the gangway at once, because it&#x27;s an <strong>ISPS access-control<\/strong> post as well as a safety post.<\/li><li>Get him safely to his cabin, but <strong>not left alone<\/strong> if he&#x27;s heavily intoxicated. Watch for vomiting and <strong>alcohol poisoning<\/strong>: recovery position, check breathing, and get medical help if needed.<\/li><li>Keep him away from machinery, the quay edge and the gangway.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Evidence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Record what was seen: speech, balance, smell, behaviour, the time, and the <strong>witnesses<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use an <strong>alcohol test<\/strong> if the company&#x27;s drug and alcohol policy allows it, with a witness present, and record the reading.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. The law<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For professional seafarers on UK ships, the <strong>Railways and Transport Safety Act 2003, Part 4<\/strong>, sets prescribed alcohol limits on duty, or when they could be called on duty in an emergency. It&#x27;s an offence to exceed them (verify the figures before quoting).<\/li><li><strong>STCW A-VIII/1<\/strong> requires flag States to set a limit of no more than <strong>0.05% blood alcohol<\/strong> or <strong>0.25 mg/l in breath<\/strong> for watchkeepers.<\/li><li>Being <strong>unfit for duty through alcohol<\/strong> is <strong>gross misconduct<\/strong> under the <strong>Code of Conduct for the Merchant Navy<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s also a breach of the company&#x27;s <strong>D&amp;A policy<\/strong>, which is normally stricter, and can lead to dismissal.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Process, once he&#x27;s sober<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Start the <strong>disciplinary process<\/strong>: written allegation.<\/li><li>A <strong>hearing<\/strong>, where he may be accompanied by a friend.<\/li><li>A <strong>decision<\/strong>, and his <strong>right of appeal<\/strong> to the company.<\/li><li>An <strong>OLB<\/strong> entry, with his reply.<\/li><li><strong>Report to the company<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>5. Look at the cause<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Was this a one-off, or is there an alcohol problem? Offer welfare support.<\/li><li>Did the shore leave arrangements, a late return, or the watch roster make it more likely?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Remind <strong>all crew<\/strong> of the <strong>D&amp;A policy<\/strong> before the next shore leave, including limits and times before duty.<\/li><li>Make sure watch rosters for shore leave are planned, with enough time between return and duty.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d also say<\/strong><\/p><p>If I&#x27;d let him go on watch, I&#x27;d share the responsibility for anything that happened: a stranger boarding, a fall from the gangway, or a fire not reported.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Don&#x27;t let him on watch, replace him, look after him medically, gather evidence and test if policy allows, RTSA 2003 and STCW limits, gross misconduct under the Code of Conduct, a fair process, OLB, and remind the crew of the D&amp;A policy.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"He refuses the breath test. What now?","pa":"<p>Treat the refusal as <strong>misconduct<\/strong> under the company&#x27;s drug and alcohol policy and the Code of Conduct. Keep him <strong>off watch<\/strong>, because he&#x27;s assumed unfit. Record it in the OLB and report to the Master and company. In the UK the police can require a test under the <strong>Railways and Transport Safety Act 2003<\/strong>, and refusing a police test is an offence.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q65","sec":4,"q":"What is ISM? Objectives, functional requirements, company/Master/DPA responsibilities?","tier":"red","rep":169,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":"ISM Regs 2026","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Internationally the ISM Code is made mandatory by <strong>SOLAS Chapter IX<\/strong>. In the UK the law is the <strong>Merchant Shipping (International Safety Management (ISM) Code) Regulations 2026<\/strong>, in force <strong>31 March 2026<\/strong>, with guidance in <strong>MGN 708 (M)<\/strong>. They replaced the 2014 Regs. My Oral Notes said &quot;13 elements&quot;; <strong>ISM Part A has 16 sections<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Objectives: ISM 1.2<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>1.2.1: <strong>safety at sea<\/strong>, prevention of <strong>human injury or loss of life<\/strong>, and avoidance of <strong>damage to the environment<\/strong>, in particular the marine environment, and to <strong>property<\/strong>.<\/li><li>1.2.2: the company should provide safe practices in ship operation and a safe working environment; <strong>assess all identified risks<\/strong> to its ships, personnel and the environment and establish appropriate <strong>safeguards<\/strong>; and <strong>continuously improve<\/strong> the safety management skills of personnel ashore and on board, including preparing for emergencies.<\/li><li>1.2.3: the SMS should ensure <strong>compliance with mandatory rules<\/strong> and regulations, and that applicable <strong>codes, guidelines and standards<\/strong> are taken into account.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Functional requirements: ISM 1.4<\/strong><\/p><p>Every SMS must include:<\/p><ol><li>a <strong>safety and environmental-protection policy<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>instructions and procedures<\/strong> to ensure safe operation and environmental protection, in line with the law;<\/li><li>defined <strong>levels of authority<\/strong> and <strong>lines of communication<\/strong> between, and among, shore and shipboard personnel;<\/li><li>procedures for <strong>reporting accidents and non-conformities<\/strong>;<\/li><li>procedures to <strong>prepare for and respond to emergencies<\/strong>;<\/li><li>procedures for <strong>internal audits and management reviews<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The company: ISM 3<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the operator isn&#x27;t the owner, the owner must report the full name and details of the company to the Administration.<\/li><li>Define and document the <strong>responsibility, authority and interrelation<\/strong> of all personnel (3.2).<\/li><li>Provide <strong>adequate resources and shore-based support<\/strong> to enable the DPA to carry out his functions (3.3).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The DPA: ISM 4<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>link between the ship and the company<\/strong>, with <strong>direct access to the highest level of management<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Monitors<\/strong> the safety and pollution-prevention aspects of each ship&#x27;s operation.<\/li><li>Makes sure <strong>adequate resources and shore support<\/strong> are provided.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The Master: ISM 5<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>5.1: <strong>implements<\/strong> the policy; <strong>motivates<\/strong> the crew; issues <strong>clear and simple instructions<\/strong>; <strong>verifies<\/strong> that requirements are observed; <strong>reviews the SMS<\/strong> and reports its deficiencies to shore management.<\/li><li>5.2: the SMS must contain a clear statement emphasising the <strong>Master&#x27;s overriding authority<\/strong> and responsibility to make decisions on safety and pollution prevention, and to request the company&#x27;s assistance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The other sections<\/strong><\/p><p>6 resources and personnel, 7 shipboard operations, 8 emergency preparedness, 9 reports and analysis of non-conformities, accidents and hazardous occurrences, 10 maintenance, 11 documentation, 12 company verification, review and evaluation; Part B 13 to 16 certification, interim certification, verification and forms.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS IX and the 2026 UK Regs, the objectives, the six functional requirements, and the company, DPA and Master sections, with 5.2 overriding authority.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is \"critical equipment\" under ISM 10.3?","pa":"<p>Under <strong>ISM 10.3<\/strong>, the company identifies equipment and technical systems whose <strong>sudden failure could cause a hazardous situation<\/strong>, and sets specific measures to keep them reliable, including testing standby and non-continuous systems. Examples are steering gear, the emergency generator, the emergency fire pump and lifeboat release gear.<\/p>","asked":["What is the ISM Code?","What are the Masters responsibility under ISM?","Functional requirements of an SMS?","ISM what is it?","What does the SMS do?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",23],["Zia Ul Haq",21],["Examiner not named",19],["James Gilbert",16],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",13]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q66","sec":4,"q":"DOC vs SMC; interim and short-term certificates?","tier":"orange","rep":35,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":"ISM Regs 2026","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>ISM Code Part B, sections <strong>13 and 14<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the <strong>ISM Regs 2026<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 708<\/strong>. For UK ships the <strong>MCA<\/strong> issues the company&#x27;s DOC. The ship&#x27;s SMC is issued by the MCA, or by an authorised RO for ships under <strong>EASS<\/strong> (MGN 561 Amd 1).<\/p><p><strong>DOC: Document of Compliance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Issued to the <strong>company<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Shows the company&#x27;s SMS complies with the Code for the <strong>ship types<\/strong> it operates. It lists those types: a DOC for container ships doesn&#x27;t cover tankers.<\/li><li>Valid up to <strong>5 years<\/strong>, with an <strong>annual verification<\/strong> within <strong>3 months either side<\/strong> of the anniversary.<\/li><li>A <strong>copy<\/strong> is kept on board, so the Master can show it to PSC. It isn&#x27;t the ship&#x27;s own certificate. The copy doesn&#x27;t need to be authenticated.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SMC: Safety Management Certificate<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Issued to the <strong>ship<\/strong>, after an audit confirms the company and ship operate under the approved SMS.<\/li><li>Valid up to <strong>5 years<\/strong>, with <strong>one intermediate verification<\/strong> between the <strong>2nd and 3rd<\/strong> anniversary. Some flags require more.<\/li><li>The SMC is <strong>only valid while the company&#x27;s DOC is valid<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Interim certificates: ISM 14<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Interim DOC<\/strong>: for a <strong>new company<\/strong>, or when a <strong>new ship type<\/strong> is added to an existing DOC. Valid up to <strong>12 months<\/strong>. The company must show it has an SMS meeting the Code and a plan to implement it fully within that time.<\/li><li><strong>Interim SMC<\/strong>: for a <strong>new ship on delivery<\/strong>, a <strong>change of company<\/strong>, or a <strong>change of flag<\/strong>. Valid up to <strong>6 months<\/strong>. In special cases the Administration may extend it by another <strong>6 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>For an interim SMC, the auditor checks: the DOC is relevant, the SMS includes the key elements, the Master and senior officers are familiar with it, the SMS has been assessed for the ship, instructions are given before sailing, and information is in a working language.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Short-term certificate<\/strong><\/p><p>After a successful initial or renewal audit, a <strong>short-term<\/strong> certificate may be issued while the full certificate is prepared, valid up to <strong>5 months<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Withdrawal<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the <strong>DOC is withdrawn<\/strong>, every SMC under it falls with it.<\/li><li>An SMC can be withdrawn if the intermediate verification isn&#x27;t done or a major non-conformity isn&#x27;t corrected.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Renewal<\/strong><\/p><p>Renewal audits can be done within 3 months before expiry, and the new certificate then runs from the old expiry date.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>DOC to the company for ship types, annual verification, a copy on board; SMC to the ship, intermediate verification; interim DOC 12 months, interim SMC 6 months plus 6; short-term 5 months; and that the SMC falls if the DOC is withdrawn.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your company's DOC is withdrawn. What happens to your ship?","pa":"<p>The ship&#x27;s <strong>SMC becomes invalid<\/strong> too, because it depends on a valid company DOC. The ship can&#x27;t operate legally and can be detained.<\/p>","asked":["What is Doc and SMC?","Validity and survey period for DOC and SMC?","What is the validity of your SMC and DOC?","What is the window period for DOC and SMC renewal?","What u know about ISM-DOC certificate and SMC certificate and Validity?"],"ex":[["Mahmood ul Hassan",5],["James Gilbert",4],["Examiner not named",4],["Zia Ul Haq",3],["Roddy MacKay",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q67","sec":4,"q":"How do you know the ship complies with ISM / how do you implement the SMS?","tier":"orange","rep":37,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The UK <strong>ISM Regs 2026<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 708<\/strong>, and ISM Code Part A. The <strong>SMC<\/strong> shows the ship complied <strong>at the last audit<\/strong>. What proves it <strong>today<\/strong> is <strong>objective evidence<\/strong> that the SMS is actually being used.<\/p><p><strong>How I implement it as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Know it and make sure the crew know it.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Familiarisation<\/strong> for every joiner before duties (ISM 6.3), with checklists signed.<\/li><li>The SMS manuals on board are the <strong>current revision<\/strong>, with obsolete copies removed (ISM 11).<\/li><li>The crew know where to find procedures for their own jobs.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Do the work the SMS way<\/strong> (ISM 7).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Risk assessments<\/strong>, <strong>permits to work<\/strong> and <strong>toolbox talks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Checklists for cargo, mooring, enclosed space entry, hot work and pre-departure actually <strong>completed at the time<\/strong>, not pre-filled.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Maintenance<\/strong> (ISM 10).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>The <strong>PMS<\/strong> up to date, <strong>critical equipment<\/strong> tested, and defects reported with their cause.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Emergency preparedness<\/strong> (ISM 8).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Drills<\/strong> to the schedule, <strong>realistic<\/strong> and <strong>debriefed<\/strong>, with lessons recorded.<\/li><li>Emergency contact lists and checklists current.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Reporting<\/strong> (ISM 9).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Near misses, accidents and non-conformities<\/strong> raised, investigated, and corrective action <strong>followed up<\/strong> to closure.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Records<\/strong> (ISM 11).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Rest hours, logs, ORB and GRB, ballast records, training and drill records, permits.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Training<\/strong> (ISM 6.5).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Identify training needs and make sure they&#x27;re met: cadets, new equipment, refreshers.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Verification<\/strong> (ISM 12).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Internal audits<\/strong> at intervals of no more than <strong>12 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Master&#x27;s review<\/strong> of the SMS to the company (ISM 5.1.5).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I know it&#x27;s working<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Do the records match what I see on deck?<\/strong> If the permit says the space was ventilated for 2 hours but the fan was never started, the SMS isn&#x27;t working.<\/li><li>Are <strong>near misses reported<\/strong>? A ship with none isn&#x27;t safe; it&#x27;s silent.<\/li><li>Do ratings know the procedures, not just the officers?<\/li><li>Are NCs closed with <strong>real<\/strong> corrective actions, or do the same findings come back?<\/li><li>Do audits, PSC and flag inspections find few deficiencies?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When I find a gap<\/strong><\/p><p>I raise it as a non-conformity, find the root cause, fix it, and if the procedure itself is wrong, I ask the Master to send a change request to the company.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The SMC is past evidence, compliance today is shown by objective evidence; then specific ways I implement it under ISM 6 to 12, and the test that the records match reality.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the difference between the Master's review and the company management review?","pa":"<p>The <strong>Master&#x27;s review<\/strong> (ISM 5.1.5) is the Master checking the SMS on board and reporting its deficiencies to shore management. The <strong>company management review<\/strong> (ISM 12) is the office evaluating the whole SMS, using audits, the Master&#x27;s reviews and incident data, and deciding what to change.<\/p>","asked":["How will you implement ISM onboard?","How you will know that your vessel is complying with ISM?","Under ISM, C/o responsibility & how will you implement on board?","How you know ISM is implemented onboard?","How would you implement this in the SMS?"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",8],["Michele Moro",7],["Kaiser Ali",5],["Chris Williams",3],["Examiner not named",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q68","sec":4,"q":"Non-conformity vs major NC vs observation; how are they processed?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Definitions: ISM 1.1<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Observation<\/strong>: a statement of fact made during a safety management audit and substantiated by objective evidence. It often points to something that <strong>could become a problem<\/strong>, but isn&#x27;t a breach yet. Example: the drill records are complete but the debriefs are very brief.<\/li><li><strong>Non-conformity (NC)<\/strong>: an observed situation where <strong>objective evidence<\/strong> indicates the <strong>non-fulfilment of a specified requirement<\/strong>. Example: a fire drill not recorded as the SMS requires, or a permit to work not signed off.<\/li><li><strong>Major non-conformity<\/strong>: an identifiable deviation that poses a <strong>serious threat<\/strong> to the safety of personnel or the ship, or a <strong>serious risk<\/strong> to the environment, that requires <strong>immediate corrective action<\/strong>, or the <strong>lack of effective and systematic implementation<\/strong> of a requirement of the Code. Examples: the emergency fire pump unserviceable and not reported, the PMS not implemented at all, or the lifeboat release gear known defective with no action.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How they&#x27;re raised<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>By the <strong>auditor<\/strong>: internal (company), external (MCA or RO), or during a flag or PSC inspection.<\/li><li><strong>On board<\/strong> under <strong>ISM 9<\/strong>: any crew member can report a non-conformity, an accident or a hazardous occurrence.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How they&#x27;re processed<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Recorded<\/strong> with the evidence.<\/li><li><strong>Root-cause analysis<\/strong>: why did it happen? Procedure, training, resources, supervision, fatigue?<\/li><li><strong>Corrective action<\/strong>: fix the immediate problem.<\/li><li><strong>Preventive action<\/strong>: stop it happening again, on this ship and across the fleet.<\/li><li>A <strong>target date<\/strong> agreed with the auditor or company.<\/li><li>The company <strong>verifies<\/strong> the action and <strong>closes<\/strong> it. The <strong>DPA<\/strong> monitors progress.<\/li><li>The results feed into the <strong>management review<\/strong> (ISM 12).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>A major non-conformity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Must be <strong>downgraded<\/strong> before the SMC can be issued or kept: immediate action to remove the serious threat, plus an agreed plan for the rest.<\/li><li>Usually followed by an <strong>additional audit<\/strong> to verify.<\/li><li>If it isn&#x27;t downgraded, the SMC may be <strong>withdrawn<\/strong>, and the ship may be <strong>detained<\/strong> by the flag or PSC.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Observations<\/strong><\/p><p>They don&#x27;t need formal corrective action, but a good company acts on them before they become NCs.<\/p><p><strong>On board<\/strong><\/p><p>As C/O I make sure our own NCs are closed with <strong>evidence<\/strong>, not just &quot;done&quot;, and that the crew aren&#x27;t afraid to raise them.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The three definitions word for word, with examples, and the process: root cause, corrective and preventive action, target date, company verification and closure, and a major downgraded before the ship sails.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Give me a shipboard example of a major NC.","pa":"<p>Examples of a major NC:<\/p><ul><li>the emergency fire pump or emergency generator not working and not reported;<\/li><li>falsified ORB entries;<\/li><li>lifeboats not ready for use;<\/li><li>an enclosed space entered with no permit or gas test.<\/li><\/ul><p>Each poses a <strong>serious threat<\/strong> needing immediate action, or shows the ISM requirement isn&#x27;t being effectively implemented.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q69","sec":4,"q":"Master's overriding authority — where is it stated?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>It&#x27;s stated in <strong>two places<\/strong>, and they&#x27;re different.<\/p><p><strong>1. ISM Code 5.2<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The Company should ensure that the SMS operating on board the ship contains a <strong>clear statement emphasising the Master&#x27;s authority<\/strong>. The Company should establish in the SMS that the Master has the <strong>overriding authority and the responsibility<\/strong> to make decisions with respect to <strong>safety and pollution prevention<\/strong> and to <strong>request the Company&#x27;s assistance<\/strong> as may be necessary.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>It&#x27;s an obligation on the <strong>company<\/strong> to put that statement in the SMS.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. SOLAS V/34-1: Master&#x27;s discretion<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The <strong>owner, the charterer, the company<\/strong> operating the ship, or any other person <strong>shall not prevent or restrict the Master<\/strong> of the ship from taking or executing any decision which, in the Master&#x27;s <strong>professional judgement<\/strong>, is necessary for <strong>safety of life at sea<\/strong> and protection of the <strong>marine environment<\/strong>.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>It protects the Master from <strong>outside pressure<\/strong>, especially commercial pressure. In the UK it&#x27;s in the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Security equivalents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS XI-2/8<\/strong>: the Master has discretion for ship safety and security. If there&#x27;s a conflict between safety and security requirements, the Master gives effect to those necessary to maintain the safety of the ship.<\/li><li><strong>ISPS A/6.1<\/strong>: the company must ensure the SSP contains a clear statement emphasising the Master&#x27;s authority, and that the Master has overriding authority to make decisions on the safety and security of the ship.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Where to find it on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>SMS manual<\/strong>, usually in the Master&#x27;s responsibilities section, and often the company&#x27;s policy statement.<\/li><li>The <strong>SSP<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Auditors and PSC officers ask to see it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My P1 notes<\/strong> label the ISM wording as &quot;SOLAS V/34-1&quot;. That&#x27;s wrong: the ISM wording is <strong>ISM 5.2<\/strong>; SOLAS V/34-1 is the &quot;not constrained by owner or charterer&quot; rule.<\/p><p><strong>Examples of when the Master would use it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Refusing to sail into a forecast storm despite charterer pressure.<\/li><li>Refusing to load overweight or wrongly declared containers, or to exceed stack weights.<\/li><li>Waiting at anchor until the crew have had their rest.<\/li><li>Diverting to land a sick seafarer.<\/li><li>Delaying departure for a defect.<\/li><li>Deviating to help a ship in distress.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it doesn&#x27;t mean<\/strong><\/p><p>It doesn&#x27;t let the Master ignore the SMS or the law. It&#x27;s authority for <strong>safety and pollution prevention<\/strong> decisions, backed by his professional judgement, and he should record his reasons.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Both references: ISM 5.2 says the company must state the Master&#x27;s overriding authority in the SMS; SOLAS V/34-1 says nobody may restrict his professional judgement on safety. Then the security equivalents and an example.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Does overriding authority let the Master ignore the SMS?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> It lets the Master make decisions for safety and pollution prevention, and ask the company for help (ISM 5.2). He still acts within the law and records and reports any departure from the SMS. The SMS stays the framework.<\/p>","asked":["Where will you find Master's overriding authority?","Heavy weather checklist, delay pilotage, Master's overriding authority."],"ex":[["Inamul Bhuiyan",2],["James Gilbert",1],["Piers Chubb",1]],"yrs":"2023–2024"},{"id":"Q70","sec":4,"q":"ISM audit after grounding — what are they looking for?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The background<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>After a grounding, the <strong>MCA<\/strong> may order an <strong>additional ISM audit<\/strong>. The company does its own investigation under <strong>ISM 9<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In the UK the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> investigates the accident separately, under the <strong>Accident Reporting and Investigation Regs 2026<\/strong>, and it <strong>does not apportion blame<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The grounding <strong>doesn&#x27;t automatically invalidate the SMC<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the auditors are looking for<\/strong><\/p><p>They want to know: <strong>did the SMS fail, or wasn&#x27;t it followed?<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Passage planning<\/strong> (SOLAS V/34, A.893(21)): was there an appraised plan? No-go areas marked? <strong>Safety contour<\/strong> and safety depth set correctly on ECDIS? <strong>Alarms<\/strong> on and audible? UKC calculations, including squat?<\/li><li><strong>The bridge team<\/strong>: the Master&#x27;s <strong>standing and night orders<\/strong>, <strong>BRM<\/strong>, the <strong>lookout<\/strong>, the <strong>Master-pilot information exchange<\/strong>, use of <strong>all means<\/strong> of position fixing, cross-checking the GNSS with radar and visual fixes.<\/li><li><strong>Fatigue<\/strong>: the <strong>rest-hour records<\/strong> of the OOW and Master, and the manning against the <strong>SMD<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Equipment<\/strong>: the <strong>PMS<\/strong> for navigation equipment, any known defects, and whether they were reported and dealt with.<\/li><li><strong>Emergency response<\/strong> (ISM 8): was the <strong>grounding checklist<\/strong> used? Soundings of all tanks, use of the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19), <strong>damage stability<\/strong>, notification of the <strong>DPA, MCA and MAIB<\/strong>, the <strong>VDR saved<\/strong>, the <strong>SOPEP<\/strong> activated if needed?<\/li><li><strong>Training and familiarisation<\/strong>: the OOW&#x27;s <strong>ECDIS type-specific<\/strong> training, familiarisation records, drill records.<\/li><li><strong>Previous NCs and near misses<\/strong>: had a similar problem been <strong>reported before and ignored<\/strong>? Had the company acted on fleet lessons?<\/li><li><strong>Company support<\/strong>: the <strong>DPA&#x27;s response<\/strong>, the resources provided, and the emergency response team ashore.<\/li><li><strong>Document control<\/strong>: were the charts and publications up to date? Was the ECDIS updated?<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Outcome<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>root cause<\/strong> found.<\/li><li>NCs or a <strong>major NC<\/strong> raised.<\/li><li><strong>Corrective action<\/strong> across the fleet, not just this ship: procedures, training, equipment.<\/li><li>The SMC is only at risk if there was a <strong>systemic failure<\/strong>, for example no passage planning at all, or rest hours routinely ignored.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>Give the auditors honest access: the records, the people, and my own account. Preserve the evidence. Don&#x27;t &quot;tidy up&quot; the records afterwards.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SMC not automatically invalid, MCA additional audit, MAIB separately, and the specific areas: passage planning and ECDIS settings, bridge team and orders, fatigue, equipment, emergency response, training, previous reports, and company support.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who downloads the VDR, and who owns the data?","pa":"<p>The data normally belongs to the <strong>shipowner<\/strong>. The <strong>Master saves<\/strong> it at once. The MAIB, or an authorised technician using the manufacturer&#x27;s software, downloads it, and the MAIB has powers to take it in a UK investigation.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q71","sec":4,"q":"How do you prepare for an SMC / ISPS audit?","tier":"red","rep":53,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Before the audit<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. Look back<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Review the <strong>previous audit report<\/strong>, the last <strong>internal audit<\/strong>, <strong>PSC<\/strong> and flag reports, and <strong>open NCs<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Make sure they&#x27;re <strong>closed with evidence<\/strong>, not just ticked.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Certificates and documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>All in date: the <strong>DOC copy<\/strong>, <strong>SMC<\/strong>, <strong>ISSC<\/strong>, <strong>SMD<\/strong>, <strong>CSR<\/strong> and the statutory certificates.<\/li><li>The <strong>SMS manuals<\/strong> are the latest revision, with obsolete copies removed. The <strong>SSP<\/strong> is the approved version with all amendments.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Drills<\/strong> and training, <strong>familiarisation<\/strong> of new joiners.<\/li><li><strong>Rest hours<\/strong>, signed.<\/li><li><strong>PMS<\/strong>, including critical equipment tests.<\/li><li>Deck and engine <strong>logs<\/strong>, the <strong>ORB and GRB<\/strong>, ballast records.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessments<\/strong> and <strong>permits<\/strong>, <strong>near-miss<\/strong> reports, accident reports.<\/li><li><strong>Safety committee<\/strong> minutes, and the <strong>Master&#x27;s review<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Company circulars received and acknowledged.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. ISPS records (ISPS A/10)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Security drills<\/strong> every 3 months and the <strong>yearly exercise<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>DoS<\/strong>, security level changes, last 10 port calls.<\/li><li>Security <strong>incident<\/strong> reports and breaches.<\/li><li><strong>SSAS<\/strong> test records.<\/li><li>Maintenance and calibration of <strong>security equipment<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Visitor log<\/strong> and the crew&#x27;s security training certificates.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Walk the ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Fire and LSA equipment ready, <strong>signage<\/strong> and escape routes.<\/li><li><strong>Restricted areas<\/strong> locked and marked.<\/li><li><strong>Access control<\/strong> at the gangway working.<\/li><li>Housekeeping and general condition.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Brief the crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Everyone should know their <strong>emergency duties<\/strong>, how to <strong>report a near miss<\/strong>, who the <strong>DPA and CSO<\/strong> are, the company policy, and their <strong>security duties<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Auditors ask <strong>ratings<\/strong>, not just officers.<\/li><li>Plan the rest hours, so the crew aren&#x27;t exhausted on the day.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. The SSP<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Make the SSP available to the auditor. For a <strong>flag State verification<\/strong>, they can see <strong>all of it<\/strong>, including the confidential sections.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>During the audit<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>opening meeting<\/strong> with the Master.<\/li><li>Answer <strong>honestly<\/strong>. If something&#x27;s wrong, say so. Hiding things turns an NC into a major.<\/li><li>Show <strong>objective evidence<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A drill is often required: be ready.<\/li><li>Agree <strong>corrective actions<\/strong> and dates at the <strong>closing meeting<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After the audit<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Raise the NCs in the company system, carry out the corrective actions, and send evidence by the agreed date.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Look back at previous findings, certificates and manuals, records for ISM and ISPS, walk the ship, brief the crew including ratings, SSP available, honest answers and objective evidence.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What if the auditor asks an AB what the SSAS is and he doesn't know?","pa":"<p>That may be correct. <strong>SSAS activation points are confidential<\/strong>, and only the Master, SSO and named officers should know them (ISPS A/9.4). An AB should know general security duties and how to report, not the SSAS details.<\/p>","asked":["How will you prepare for the ISM audit?","ISM Certificates, validity, surveys?","What are all ISM certificates?","How do you prepare for an ISM survey as a Chief Mate?","What will you prepare on the vessel?"],"ex":[["Michele Moro",17],["Examiner not named",8],["Muhammad Iqbal",7],["Zia Ul Haq",4],["Kaiser Ali",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q72","sec":4,"q":"What is ISPS? Application, functional requirements, certification?","tier":"orange","rep":35,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Internationally, <strong>SOLAS XI-2<\/strong> and the <strong>ISPS Code<\/strong>, in force since <strong>1 July 2004<\/strong>, after 9/11. Part A is <strong>mandatory<\/strong>; Part B is <strong>guidance<\/strong>. In the UK, ISPS is applied through retained <strong>EC Regulation 725/2004<\/strong>, which makes certain Part B paragraphs mandatory for UK ships, and the <strong>Ship and Port Facility (Security) Regulations 2004<\/strong>. The <strong>MCA<\/strong> certifies ships, and the <strong>DfT<\/strong> sets security levels.<\/p><p><strong>Application: ships on international voyages<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Passenger ships<\/strong>, including high-speed passenger craft.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo ships of 500 GT and over<\/strong>, including high-speed craft.<\/li><li><strong>Mobile offshore drilling units<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>port facilities<\/strong> serving them.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Objectives and functional requirements (ISPS A/1.3)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Gather and assess<\/strong> information on security threats and exchange it with Contracting Governments.<\/li><li>Require <strong>communication protocols<\/strong> for ships and port facilities.<\/li><li><strong>Prevent unauthorised access<\/strong> to ships, port facilities and their restricted areas.<\/li><li><strong>Prevent<\/strong> the introduction of unauthorised <strong>weapons, incendiary devices or explosives<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Provide means of <strong>raising the alarm<\/strong> in reaction to threats or incidents.<\/li><li>Require <strong>ship and port facility security plans<\/strong> based on security assessments.<\/li><li>Require <strong>training, drills and exercises<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>People<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Company Security Officer (CSO)<\/strong>, ashore (A/11).<\/li><li><strong>Ship Security Officer (SSO)<\/strong>, on board (A/12).<\/li><li><strong>Port Facility Security Officer (PFSO)<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Certification<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Ship Security Assessment (SSA)<\/strong> by the CSO or a recognised security organisation, including an on-scene survey.<\/li><li><strong>Ship Security Plan (SSP)<\/strong>, written from the SSA and <strong>approved by the Administration<\/strong> (the MCA).<\/li><li>A designated <strong>CSO and SSO<\/strong>, and an <strong>SSAS<\/strong> fitted (SOLAS XI-2/6).<\/li><li><strong>Verification<\/strong> audit, then the <strong>International Ship Security Certificate (ISSC)<\/strong>, valid <strong>5 years<\/strong>, with <strong>one intermediate verification<\/strong> between the 2nd and 3rd anniversary.<\/li><li>An <strong>interim ISSC<\/strong> lasts up to <strong>6 months<\/strong>, for a new ship, a change of flag, or a change of company.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Other SOLAS XI-2 requirements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>AIS<\/strong> and <strong>LRIT<\/strong>, the <strong>CSR<\/strong>, the IMO number marked on the ship.<\/li><li><strong>Control and compliance<\/strong>: port States can check the ISSC and, with clear grounds, inspect further, delay or detain (XI-2/9).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Crew training (STCW VI/5 and VI/6)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>SSO certificate; designated security duties; security awareness for everyone.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS XI-2, 2004, Part A mandatory and B guidance with EC 725/2004 making parts of B mandatory for UK ships; who it applies to; the functional requirements; SSA, SSP, CSO, SSO, SSAS; ISSC 5 years with intermediate, interim 6 months.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the Continuous Synopsis Record and why does ISPS care about it?","pa":"<p>The <strong>CSR<\/strong> (SOLAS XI-1/5) is the ship&#x27;s life history. It records her name, flag, port, IMO number, owners, any bareboat charterer, the ISM company, class, and who issued her DOC, SMC and ISSC. ISPS cares because security depends on knowing <strong>who controls the ship<\/strong>. It stops a ship&#x27;s identity being laundered.<\/p>","asked":["What is ISPS, what you know about it, how it will affect you?","What are the requirements of the ISPS Code?","Documents required to load cargo?","Non-ISPS Compliant port (Dirty Port)","What are the sections of ISPS?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",4],["Kaiser Ali",3],["Ranjit Joseph",3],["Chris Bates",2],["Timothy Cummins",2]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q73","sec":4,"q":"Security levels: who sets them in the UK? Can the Master change them? Ship lower than port?","tier":"orange","rep":17,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Security levels (ISPS A/2)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Level 1: normal.<\/strong> The level at which ships and port facilities normally operate. <strong>Minimum<\/strong> appropriate protective security measures are maintained <strong>at all times<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Level 2: heightened.<\/strong> A heightened risk of a security incident. <strong>Additional<\/strong> protective measures are maintained for a period of time.<\/li><li><strong>Level 3: exceptional.<\/strong> A security incident is <strong>probable or imminent<\/strong>, although it may not be possible to identify the specific target. <strong>Further specific<\/strong> protective measures are maintained for a <strong>limited period<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who sets them<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For <strong>UK ships<\/strong>, the <strong>Department for Transport<\/strong> (its maritime security function) as the Administration. The <strong>CSO<\/strong> passes the level to the ship.<\/li><li>In a <strong>foreign port<\/strong>, the <strong>port State<\/strong> sets the level for the port facility, and the ship must comply with that level.<\/li><li>In a UK port, the DfT sets the port facility&#x27;s level.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Can the Master change it?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Master <strong>can&#x27;t lower<\/strong> the level set by the Administration or the port State.<\/li><li>He <strong>can<\/strong>, with the CSO, put in <strong>additional measures<\/strong>, or work to a <strong>higher level&#x27;s measures<\/strong>, if his own assessment demands it.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s backed by his <strong>overriding authority<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS XI-2/8<\/strong> and <strong>ISPS A/6<\/strong>. He must report it to the CSO and, through him, the flag.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Ship lower than the port?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>No.<\/strong> Before entering, or while in, a port where the Contracting Government has set <strong>Level 2 or 3<\/strong>, the ship must <strong>acknowledge<\/strong> the instruction and <strong>confirm<\/strong> to the PFSO that it has started implementing the appropriate measures (ISPS A/7.3 and A/7.4).<\/li><li>If the ship can&#x27;t comply, it must tell the authorities before any interaction.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Ship higher than the port?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Allowed.<\/strong> The port doesn&#x27;t have to raise its level to match.<\/li><li>But the ship should <strong>request a Declaration of Security<\/strong> (A/5.2), and the PFSO must acknowledge the request.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>At sea<\/strong><\/p><p>The ship operates at the level set by its flag, or by a coastal State if it&#x27;s in that State&#x27;s territorial sea and the State has set a level.<\/p><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><p>All level changes are recorded in the <strong>security records<\/strong> (ISPS A/10), and the ship keeps the levels for the last 10 port calls, which PSC can ask for.<\/p><p><strong>Measures at each level, in practice<\/strong><\/p><p>Access control, monitoring restricted areas and deck areas, handling cargo and stores, and the delivery of ship&#x27;s stores, all tighter as the level goes up, as set out in the SSP.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The three definitions, the DfT for UK ships and the port State in its ports, the Master can&#x27;t lower it but can add measures, the ship can&#x27;t be lower than the port, the ship can be higher with a DoS requested, and records.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"At sea, who tells your UK ship to go to Level 2?","pa":"<p>The <strong>UK government<\/strong> (DfT maritime security), passed to the ship through the <strong>CSO/company<\/strong> or the MCA. A coastal state can also set the level while she&#x27;s in its waters.<\/p>","asked":["How you will change ship security levels?","What to do when going to a port when the ship has a higher security level than the port?","You are approaching to a port and you get a call from the port you need to change your security level, how will you do it?","Tell me about Security, what are the different security levels?","There's something common in all 3 security levels. What are those 4 cornerstone of isps?"],"ex":[["Robert Jackson",3],["Ranjit Joseph",3],["Michele Moro",2],["Kaiser Ali",2],["David Carlisle",2]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q74","sec":4,"q":"SSO / CSO duties?","tier":"orange","rep":28,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>ISPS A/11<\/strong> (CSO) and <strong>A/12<\/strong> (SSO). Training and certification under <strong>STCW VI/5<\/strong> (SSO), with designated security duties and security awareness for all crew under <strong>VI/6<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>CSO: the Company Security Officer, ashore<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Advises<\/strong> ships of the <strong>threat levels<\/strong> likely to be encountered, using appropriate security assessments and other information.<\/li><li>Makes sure the <strong>Ship Security Assessment<\/strong> is carried out.<\/li><li>Makes sure the <strong>SSP is developed<\/strong>, submitted for <strong>approval<\/strong>, and thereafter <strong>implemented and maintained<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Makes sure the SSP is <strong>modified<\/strong> to correct deficiencies and meet the security requirements of the individual ship.<\/li><li>Arranges <strong>internal audits and reviews<\/strong> of security activities.<\/li><li>Arranges the <strong>initial and subsequent verifications<\/strong> by the Administration or RSO.<\/li><li>Makes sure <strong>deficiencies and non-conformities<\/strong> found in audits, reviews and verifications are dealt with promptly.<\/li><li>Enhances <strong>security awareness and vigilance<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Makes sure <strong>adequate training<\/strong> is given.<\/li><li>Makes sure there&#x27;s effective <strong>communication and cooperation<\/strong> between the SSO and the PFSOs.<\/li><li>Makes sure security and safety requirements are <strong>consistent<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If sister ships share a plan, makes sure the plan reflects each ship accurately.<\/li><li>Makes sure <strong>alternative or equivalent arrangements<\/strong> approved for a ship are implemented.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>SSO: the Ship Security Officer, on board<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Carries out <strong>regular security inspections<\/strong> of the ship to make sure appropriate measures are maintained.<\/li><li><strong>Maintains and supervises<\/strong> the implementation of the SSP, including any <strong>amendments<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Coordinates<\/strong> the security aspects of the handling of <strong>cargo and ship&#x27;s stores<\/strong> with other crew and the PFSO.<\/li><li><strong>Proposes modifications<\/strong> to the SSP.<\/li><li><strong>Reports<\/strong> to the CSO any deficiencies and non-conformities found during internal audits, reviews and inspections, and carries out corrective actions.<\/li><li>Enhances <strong>security awareness<\/strong> on board.<\/li><li>Makes sure <strong>adequate training<\/strong> has been given to the crew.<\/li><li><strong>Reports all security incidents<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Coordinates<\/strong> implementation of the SSP with the CSO and the PFSO, including the DoS.<\/li><li>Makes sure <strong>security equipment<\/strong> is properly <strong>operated, tested, calibrated and maintained<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>In practice on my container ship<\/strong><\/p><p>The C/O is often the SSO. So I:<\/p><ul><li>set the gangway watch and visitor control;<\/li><li>plan and run the <strong>security drills<\/strong> every 3 months and the stowaway searches;<\/li><li>keep the <strong>security records<\/strong>: levels, DoS, drills, incidents, last 10 ports;<\/li><li>test the <strong>SSAS<\/strong> with the CSO;<\/li><li>brief the crew on the security level and measures in each port;<\/li><li>liaise with the PFSO on arrival.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>ISPS A/11 and A/12, STCW VI/5, the main duties of each, the CSO making sure the SSA and SSP are done and approved, and the SSO implementing it on board, with inspections, drills, records and equipment.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can the Master be the SSO?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes.<\/strong> The SSO can be the Master or any officer the company designates (ISPS A/12). On many ships it&#x27;s the C/O.<\/p>","asked":["What are the duties of SSO","What are the duties of the Ship Security Officer?","Duties, SSO), SSC test in hand, LRIT test.","What certification should a SSO and other crew members hold?","SSO Certificate for SSO, STSD & PSA for crew."],"ex":[["Roddy MacKay",2],["Ross Cochrane",2],["Siddhartha Paul",2],["James Steer",2],["Timothy Cummins",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q75","sec":4,"q":"When is a Declaration of Security needed?","tier":"orange","rep":32,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it is: ISPS A/5<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>Declaration of Security (DoS)<\/strong> is an <strong>agreement<\/strong> between a ship and a port facility, or another ship, setting out <strong>who carries out which security measures<\/strong> at the interface.<\/p><p><strong>Who decides<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>Contracting Government<\/strong> (the port State) decides when a DoS is needed by assessing the risk the ship-port interface or ship-to-ship activity poses to people, property or the environment.<\/p><p><strong>A ship can request one (A/5.2) when<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The ship is operating at a <strong>higher security level<\/strong> than the port facility or the other ship.<\/li><li>There&#x27;s an <strong>agreement on a DoS between Contracting Governments<\/strong> covering certain international voyages or specific ships on those voyages.<\/li><li>There&#x27;s been a <strong>security threat or incident<\/strong> involving the ship or the port facility.<\/li><li>The ship is at a <strong>port that isn&#x27;t required to have an approved PFSP<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ship is doing <strong>ship-to-ship activities<\/strong> with another ship that isn&#x27;t required to have an approved SSP.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Rules<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When a ship or port facility requests a DoS, the <strong>PFSO or SSO must acknowledge<\/strong> the request (A/5.3).<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>completed and signed<\/strong> by the <strong>Master or SSO<\/strong> for the ship and the <strong>PFSO<\/strong> for the port facility, or the Master or SSO of the other ship.<\/li><li>It states:<\/li><li>the <strong>security level<\/strong> of each party;<\/li><li>the <strong>activities<\/strong> covered;<\/li><li>how long it&#x27;s <strong>valid<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>who does what<\/strong>: access control, monitoring of restricted areas and deck areas, monitoring the waterside, handling cargo and stores, communications.<\/li><li>A model form is in ISPS Part B, Appendix 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The DoS is kept on board for the period specified by the Administration (verify the UK period) and recorded in the security records (A/10).<\/li><li>It&#x27;s one of the records PSC can ask for.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In practice<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In most ports at Level 1 with both parties at the same level, no DoS is needed.<\/li><li>If the company or flag raises us to Level 2 while the terminal stays at Level 1, I request one.<\/li><li>At an anchorage with a bunker barge or a ship-to-ship transfer, a DoS may be needed with the other ship.<\/li><li>Some ports require one for every call, which is their right.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>ISPS A/5, an agreement on who does which measures, the port State decides when it&#x27;s needed, the five situations where the ship can request it, the PFSO or SSO must acknowledge, signed by both, and kept on board.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What are the typical items you'd agree on in a DoS at a container terminal?","pa":"<p>At a container terminal:<\/p><ul><li>each side&#x27;s security level and contact details;<\/li><li>who controls access at the gangway, and ID and visitor checks;<\/li><li>handling of stores and cargo;<\/li><li>monitoring restricted areas;<\/li><li>searches and communications;<\/li><li>the period the DoS covers.<\/li><\/ul><p>It&#x27;s signed by the Master or SSO and the PFSO.<\/p>","asked":["When will you exchange DoS?","What is DOS (Declaration of Security)?","Contents of DOS?","Explain your understanding about DOS?","Went onto ISPS. What is a DOS?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",18],["James Steer",6],["Inamul Bhuiyan",5],["Roddy MacKay",3],["Fazal Wadood",3]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q76","sec":4,"q":"Security drills and exercises — frequency?","tier":"orange","rep":21,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>ISPS B/13.6 and B/13.7<\/strong>. For UK ships these paragraphs are made <strong>mandatory<\/strong> through EC Regulation 725/2004.<\/p><p><strong>Drills<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>once every 3 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Also <strong>within one week<\/strong> of a crew change in which <strong>more than 25%<\/strong> of the ship&#x27;s personnel are people who haven&#x27;t taken part in a security drill on <strong>that ship<\/strong> in the <strong>last 3 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>They test <strong>individual elements<\/strong> of the SSP.<\/li><li>Examples:<\/li><li>searching for an <strong>unauthorised person<\/strong> or <strong>suspicious package<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>stowaway search<\/strong> before departure;<\/li><li>recognising and controlling access to <strong>restricted areas<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>raising the alarm<\/strong> and the SSAS procedure (without sending a real alert);<\/li><li>handling a <strong>bomb threat<\/strong>;<\/li><li>procedures on <strong>changing security level<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>piracy<\/strong> response and citadel muster, in a high-risk area.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Exercises<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>once each calendar year<\/strong>, with <strong>no more than 18 months<\/strong> between them.<\/li><li>They test <strong>communications, coordination, resource availability and response<\/strong>.<\/li><li>They can be:<\/li><li><strong>full-scale or live<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>tabletop<\/strong> simulation or seminar;<\/li><li><strong>combined<\/strong> with other exercises, such as a port facility exercise, a search and rescue exercise or an emergency response exercise.<\/li><li>The <strong>CSO<\/strong> should take part. PFSOs and authorities may also take part.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records (ISPS A/10)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>All drills and exercises are recorded with the <strong>date<\/strong>, the <strong>scenario<\/strong>, the <strong>participants<\/strong>, and <strong>lessons learned<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The SSO reports to the CSO, and the CSO uses the lessons to improve the SSP.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Crew training<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>New joiners get <strong>security familiarisation<\/strong> before duties (STCW VI/6).<\/li><li>Those with designated security duties need the <strong>designated security duties<\/strong> certificate; everyone else <strong>security awareness<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A good example on my container ship<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>stowaway search before departure<\/strong> using the ship-specific search checklist, with areas allocated, a time limit, and reporting back to the bridge. That counts as a security drill if it&#x27;s <strong>planned, briefed and recorded<\/strong> as one. Another: a suspicious package found in the accommodation during cargo, with cordon, reporting to the PFSO, and search.<\/p><p><strong>What auditors look for<\/strong><\/p><p>Drills done on time, after crew changes, with real scenarios and real lessons, not the same paperwork every three months.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Drills every 3 months, within one week of a more than 25% crew change, exercises once a calendar year and no more than 18 months apart, the CSO taking part, examples, and records under A/10.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"You take over a ship from a new company. When must the first drill be done?","pa":"<p>Security <strong>drills every 3 months<\/strong>. If more than <strong>25% of the crew<\/strong> change and haven&#x27;t taken part in a drill on that ship within 3 months, hold one <strong>within a week<\/strong> (ISPS B/13.6). Exercises are once each calendar year, with no more than 18 months between them.<\/p>","asked":["Drills – how would you conduct a security drill?","What's the difference between security drills and security exercise?","How often you do Security Drill?","MFP- HRA, preparing the vessel, carrying out security drills.","Q4 ISPS CODE- WHAT IS DOS AND WHEN TO EXCHANGE DOS and what is requirement security drill ?"],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",6],["Examiner not named",3],["Kaiser Ali",2],["Khairul Anam",2],["Michele Moro",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q77","sec":4,"q":"What parts of the SSP can PSC see?","tier":"orange","rep":22,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS XI-2/9<\/strong> (control and compliance measures) and <strong>ISPS A/9.8 and A/9.8.1<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>What PSC normally checks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>ISSC<\/strong>. A valid ISSC is accepted as evidence of compliance <strong>unless there are clear grounds<\/strong> to believe otherwise.<\/li><li>The <strong>Continuous Synopsis Record<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s current <strong>security level<\/strong>, and the security information for the <strong>last 10 port calls<\/strong>: the levels, any special or additional measures, and any ship-to-ship activities.<\/li><li>The <strong>security records<\/strong>: drills, exercises, DoS, incidents, SSAS tests.<\/li><li>That an <strong>approved SSP<\/strong> is on board.<\/li><li><strong>Crew knowledge<\/strong> of their security duties: they may ask a rating what he&#x27;d do if he found a stranger on deck.<\/li><li>The <strong>SSO&#x27;s<\/strong> training certificate and the crew&#x27;s security training.<\/li><li>The physical measures: access control at the gangway, restricted areas.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Clear grounds<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the PSC officer has <strong>clear grounds<\/strong> that the ship doesn&#x27;t comply, for example the gangway is unmanned, the crew don&#x27;t know the procedures, or the records are missing, they may be given <strong>limited access<\/strong> to the <strong>specific SSP sections<\/strong> relating to that non-compliance.<\/li><li>But only with the <strong>consent of the flag State or the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Confidential sections (ISPS A/9.8.1)<\/strong><\/p><p>These <strong>can&#x27;t be inspected<\/strong> unless the Contracting Governments concerned agree. They&#x27;re the sections of A/9.4 covering:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Restricted areas<\/strong> and measures to prevent unauthorised access.<\/li><li>Procedures for <strong>responding to security threats or breaches<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Procedures for responding to <strong>instructions at Level 3<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Duties of crew<\/strong> with security responsibilities.<\/li><li>Procedures for <strong>inspection, testing, calibration and maintenance<\/strong> of security equipment.<\/li><li><strong>SSAS activation point<\/strong> locations.<\/li><li><strong>SSAS operating, testing, activation, deactivation and resetting<\/strong> procedures.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Who can see the whole SSP<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>flag State (MCA)<\/strong> and its <strong>ISPS auditors<\/strong> or RSOs doing verifications.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What PSC can do if not satisfied<\/strong><\/p><p>Under SOLAS XI-2/9: inspection of the ship, delay, detention, restriction of operations, expulsion from port, or refusal of entry for a ship that hasn&#x27;t arrived.<\/p><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><p>Show the ISSC and records, and let them see the SSP is on board. If they ask for a confidential section, I&#x27;d politely explain that it&#x27;s confidential under A/9.8.1, call the Master, and the company and flag if needed. Never just hand it over.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The ISSC, CSR, last 10 ports and records; limited access to relevant sections on clear grounds with the flag&#x27;s or Master&#x27;s consent; the confidential sections listed; and that the flag can see it all.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A PSC officer insists on reading the whole SSP. What do you do?","pa":"<p>Politely explain <strong>ISPS A/9.8.1<\/strong>. With clear grounds he gets limited access to the non-confidential sections, with the consent of the flag or the Master. The confidential sections need the flag state&#x27;s agreement. Contact the CSO and the flag (MCA), offer to let the flag decide, and record it.<\/p>","asked":["What is a ship security plan?","What all part can you show to PSC in SSP","SSP what is it and what does it contain?","What problems I had faced during wartime. I said normal security precaution as per SSP and There was no GPS signal, so we increased the lookout level.","Refer to MGN 70, Publication. Stoways at sea and SSP"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",3],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",2],["Zia Ul Haq",2],["Tarik Yassin",2],["Alec Keep",2]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q78","sec":4,"q":"SSAS — what is it and how is it tested?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS XI-2/6<\/strong>, with IMO performance standards for the SSAS (<strong>MSC.147(77)<\/strong>, verify), and the ship&#x27;s SSP.<\/p><p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>covert ship-to-shore alert<\/strong> used when the ship&#x27;s security is threatened or compromised: piracy, hijack, armed intrusion, terrorism.<\/p><p><strong>What it must do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Be activatable from the <strong>bridge<\/strong> and at least <strong>one other location<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Activation points must be <strong>protected against accidental use<\/strong>, and the points not on the bridge must be easy to use but not obvious.<\/li><li>Send a <strong>ship-to-shore alert<\/strong> to the <strong>competent authority<\/strong> designated by the flag. For UK ships, as set out in the SSP, usually the company and the CSO, who pass it to the flag and the authorities.<\/li><li>Give the ship&#x27;s <strong>identity<\/strong>, <strong>position<\/strong>, <strong>date and time<\/strong>, and indicate that the ship&#x27;s security is under threat.<\/li><li><strong>Not alert other ships<\/strong>, and <strong>not raise any alarm on board<\/strong>, so the attackers don&#x27;t know.<\/li><li><strong>Keep transmitting<\/strong> until deactivated or reset.<\/li><li>Work from a power source that keeps running if the main power fails, where required.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Confidentiality<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>activation points<\/strong> and the <strong>procedures<\/strong> for operating, testing and resetting are <strong>confidential SSP sections<\/strong> (ISPS A/9.4 and 9.8.1). Only the Master, the SSO and others the SSP names should know them.<\/p><p><strong>Testing<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Follow the <strong>SSP test procedure<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Tell the recipient beforehand<\/strong>: the CSO, and the flag if the SSP requires it, so it isn&#x27;t treated as a real alert.<\/li><li><strong>Send<\/strong> the test alert from each activation point as the procedure requires.<\/li><li><strong>Confirm<\/strong> it was received and the content was correct: the ship&#x27;s name, IMO, position and time.<\/li><li><strong>Confirm the test is closed<\/strong> with the recipient.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the security records (ISPS A/10).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Do this at the <strong>interval set in the SSP<\/strong>, often every 3 months or annually, and after any <strong>repair, change<\/strong> of equipment, or change of recipient.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If it&#x27;s defective<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Inform the <strong>CSO<\/strong> and the <strong>MCA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Agree <strong>alternative arrangements<\/strong>, for example a covert phone procedure.<\/li><li><strong>Repair<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><li>Record it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If it&#x27;s activated by accident<\/strong><\/p><p>Tell the CSO immediately so the alert can be cancelled, and record it.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS XI-2/6, covert, from the bridge and one other place, ship-to-shore only, identity and position, no alarm on board, confidential locations, and the testing procedure with prior notice, confirmation and records.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The SSAS is activated by accident. What do you do?","pa":"<p>Call the <strong>CSO/company<\/strong> immediately and stand down using the <strong>SSP verification procedure<\/strong>, with code words. First confirm it wasn&#x27;t a <strong>covert activation<\/strong>. Inform the flag if they were alerted, then investigate the cause and retrain.<\/p>","asked":["How will you test SSAS?","How often is the ship Security Alert System Tested?"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",2],["Examiner not named",1]],"yrs":"2022–2024"},{"id":"Q79","sec":4,"q":"Stowaway found — actions? Port refuses to accept him?","tier":"yellow","rep":8,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>FAL Convention<\/strong> standards on stowaways, and the IMO <strong>Revised Guidelines<\/strong> on the prevention of access by stowaways and the allocation of responsibilities to seek the successful resolution of stowaway cases (FAL.13(42), verify). In the UK, <strong>MGN 70 (M) Amd 1<\/strong> on stowaways. The ship&#x27;s <strong>SSP and SMS<\/strong> stowaway procedures apply.<\/p><p><strong>Actions when a stowaway is found<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong> and inform the Master.<\/li><li><strong>Search the whole ship<\/strong> systematically for <strong>more stowaways<\/strong>, and for weapons or contraband. Stowaways rarely travel alone.<\/li><li><strong>Search the stowaway<\/strong>, with <strong>two crew present<\/strong>, and remove anything that could be used as a weapon. Find any documents.<\/li><li><strong>Treat him humanely but firmly<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>a <strong>secure, ventilated<\/strong> space with food, water, a toilet and bedding;<\/li><li><strong>medical care<\/strong> if needed, with TMAS advice;<\/li><li><strong>never put him to work<\/strong>, except in an emergency;<\/li><li>brief him on emergency procedures and give him a lifejacket.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Find out<\/strong> his identity, nationality, how he boarded, and his port of embarkation. Gather documents and <strong>photographs<\/strong>, and fill in the stowaway questionnaire.<\/li><li><strong>Notify<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the <strong>company<\/strong> and the <strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>, who handle most stowaway cases;<\/li><li>the <strong>CSO<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>flag State (MCA)<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>agents<\/strong> at the port of embarkation and the next port.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Records<\/strong>: an <strong>OLB<\/strong> entry and the security records.<\/li><li><strong>Declare<\/strong> him to the next port authorities. <strong>Never hide a stowaway<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If the port refuses to accept him<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>He <strong>stays on board<\/strong> under secure, humane conditions.<\/li><li>The Master <strong>must not put him ashore illegally<\/strong>, or <strong>abandon him at sea<\/strong> or put him on a raft. That would be a crime and a breach of human rights.<\/li><li><strong>Continue the voyage<\/strong> while the company, the P&amp;I club and the flag negotiate disembarkation at a later port, or repatriation from the port of embarkation.<\/li><li>Consider a <strong>note of protest<\/strong> for any delay.<\/li><li>If he claims <strong>asylum<\/strong>, the authorities of the next port must deal with it, and the claim is confidential.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Gangway watch and access control to the SSP.<\/li><li><strong>Stowaway search before departure<\/strong>, especially in high-risk ports, with a checklist.<\/li><li>Check containers&#x27; seals and empty containers, and the areas around the rudder trunk.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Search for more, search him, humane treatment, no work, find his identity and port of embarkation, notify company, P&amp;I, CSO, flag and agents, declare him, OLB, and if refused, keep him on board and never abandon him.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you prevent stowaways in a high-risk port?","pa":"<p>In a high-risk port:<\/p><ul><li>raise the security measures: patrols, lighting, access control, locked spaces;<\/li><li>check container seals;<\/li><li>liaise with the PFSO;<\/li><li>carry out a <strong>thorough stowaway search<\/strong> with a checklist before departure, including holds, stores, the funnel and rudder trunk, plus a last-minute search.<\/li><\/ul><p>The FAL Convention guidelines apply.<\/p>","asked":["You found stowaways on board?","You find a stowaway as you are departing the berth?","You are now back to chief officer. As the Ship's Security Officer, what are your actions upon finding a stowaway?","Again carry out stowaway search","In case of stowaway which certificate could be potentially at risk?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",6],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",2],["Chris Roe",1]],"yrs":"2023–2025"},{"id":"Q80","sec":4,"q":"2/O fails to return from shore leave — actions?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. Try to find him<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Phone him, message him.<\/li><li>Contact the <strong>agent<\/strong> and ask him to check.<\/li><li>Ask fellow crew where he was <strong>last seen<\/strong> and who he was with.<\/li><li>Ask local <strong>hospitals and police<\/strong>, and consider the <strong>immigration<\/strong> authorities.<\/li><li>He may be ill, injured, arrested, or the victim of a crime. So I don&#x27;t assume he&#x27;s deserted.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Inform<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master<\/strong>, the <strong>company<\/strong>, and the <strong>CSO<\/strong>. It may be a security issue: his ID and crew pass may need to be cancelled, and the SSP may require an action.<\/li><li>The <strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>, if there&#x27;s a welfare or legal issue.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Manning<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the <strong>SMD<\/strong>. Without the 2/O we may be below the minimum.<\/li><li>The company and Master must get a <strong>relief<\/strong> or ask the <strong>MCA for a dispensation<\/strong> before sailing.<\/li><li><strong>Reorganise the watches<\/strong>, for example the Master or I taking a watch, and check that <strong>MSN 1877<\/strong> rest hours still work.<\/li><li><strong>Reassign his duties<\/strong>: passage planning, chart corrections, GMDSS, medical officer, or SSO duties if he holds them. Make sure the passage plan for the next voyage is done and checked.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. His property<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Two witnesses<\/strong> inventory, secure and record his belongings, cash and documents.<\/li><li>Close his <strong>wage account<\/strong> to that date.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>OLB<\/strong> entries: the time he was due, the action taken, and that he <strong>failed to join<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Update the <strong>crew list<\/strong> (left behind or failed to join) and the crew documents for immigration.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Before sailing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Give the <strong>agent<\/strong> his property and documents to hold, or keep them on board as the company decides.<\/li><li>Give the agent the <strong>full facts<\/strong> and the company&#x27;s contact, so the agent can deal with immigration and find him.<\/li><li>The ship may have to pay costs for repatriation or immigration fines.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. If he turns up later<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If there&#x27;s <strong>no good reason<\/strong>, it&#x27;s a disciplinary matter under the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong>. Failing to join can be gross misconduct.<\/li><li>But he may be a <strong>welfare case<\/strong>: an accident, a robbery, or arrested. I&#x27;d be careful about assumptions and listen to him first.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Prevent it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brief the crew on <strong>shore leave<\/strong> times, the buddy system in risky ports, and the return time before sailing.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Try to find him, inform the company and CSO, check the SMD and get a relief or dispensation before sailing, reorganise the watches within rest hours, inventory his property, OLB and crew list, give the agent the facts, and don&#x27;t assume desertion.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can you sail without him, and what does PSC check?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes<\/strong>, if the rest of the crew still meet the <strong>SMD and rest-hour rules<\/strong>. Otherwise you need a replacement or a dispensation. PSC checks the SMD against the crew list, rest hours and watch arrangements. For the man left behind: inform the agent, immigration, the company and the consul, make an OLB entry and secure his property.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q81","sec":4,"q":"Passage through an HRA — advice to the 2/O?","tier":"orange","rep":30,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First, the update.<\/strong> <strong>BMP5<\/strong> has been superseded by <strong>BMP Maritime Security<\/strong> (BMP MS), issued <strong>March 2025<\/strong> and revised <strong>June 2026<\/strong>. The revision adds guidance on boarding by activists. I&#x27;d use it with the current threat overview from the military and industry bodies, and check the <strong>UK government and MCA guidance<\/strong> and the <strong>security level the DfT<\/strong> has set for UK ships in that area.<\/p><p><strong>Before entry<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Company voyage risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>CSO guidance<\/strong>: transit, or route via the Cape?<\/li><li><strong>Current threat picture<\/strong>: piracy off Somalia, armed robbery, missiles and drones in the southern Red Sea and Gulf of Aden, mines, hijack threats in the Gulf of Oman.<\/li><li><strong>Current boundaries<\/strong> of the High Risk Area and the <strong>Voluntary Reporting Area<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Register and report<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>UKMTO<\/strong>: initial report on entering the VRA, then <strong>daily<\/strong> position reports, then a final report on exit;<\/li><li><strong>MSCHOA<\/strong> registration where relevant.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Route planning<\/strong>: the <strong>IRTC<\/strong> (Internationally Recommended Transit Corridor) in the Gulf of Aden, group transit times, stand-off from the threat coastline, timing to pass the highest-risk areas in darkness or daylight as advised.<\/li><li><strong>Security level<\/strong> set as directed, and the SSP measures in place.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Ship protection measures<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Razor wire<\/strong> and physical barriers, <strong>locked access<\/strong> points, ladders removed.<\/li><li><strong>Fire hoses<\/strong> rigged and ready, or water cannon.<\/li><li><strong>Lighting<\/strong> policy as advised.<\/li><li><strong>Citadel<\/strong> prepared with food, water, communications and a way to stop the engine and steer from inside, and <strong>drilled<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Full speed<\/strong> available and the engine room ready.<\/li><li><strong>AIS policy<\/strong> as the current guidance and the Master direct.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On the bridge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Enhanced lookout<\/strong>, radar and visual, especially for <strong>small fast craft<\/strong> and anything approaching at speed.<\/li><li><strong>Emergency communications list<\/strong> at hand: UKMTO, the CSO, the naval forces, and the <strong>SSAS<\/strong> procedure (Master and SSO only).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My advice to the 2/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Know the alarm<\/strong> and <strong>muster<\/strong> procedure, including the citadel signal.<\/li><li><strong>Know when to call the Master<\/strong>: any vessel approaching, any vessel matching the suspicious profile, any small craft at speed.<\/li><li>If attacked: <strong>sound the alarm<\/strong>, increase speed, manoeuvre as briefed, report to <strong>UKMTO<\/strong>, and get the crew to the citadel.<\/li><li><strong>Record all suspicious sightings<\/strong> with position and description, and <strong>report them to UKMTO<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Keep to the <strong>passage plan<\/strong> and the timing.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>BMP Maritime Security, not BMP5; the company risk assessment and current threat; UKMTO and MSCHOA; the IRTC; protection measures and citadel; enhanced lookout; and clear advice to the 2/O on alarm, calling the Master and reporting.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A skiff is approaching fast at 2 miles. Your actions?","pa":"<p>Following current BMP guidance:<\/p><ul><li><strong>full speed<\/strong> and evasive manoeuvring;<\/li><li>raise the alarm, all crew to the safe muster point or citadel;<\/li><li>report to <strong>UKMTO<\/strong>;<\/li><li>fire hoses and other deterrents;<\/li><li>the SSAS if attacked.<\/li><\/ul><p>Never put crew at risk to stop a boarding.<\/p>","asked":["You are going to HRA how will u prepare vessel for HRA?","What are your security considerations prior to entering into a high risk area?","How does piracy relate to MLC?","What preparations you do before entering Piracy Area?","While on the voyage from Jeddah in August you rcvd heavy wx warning what it would be?"],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",10],["Khairul Anam",4],["Nayyer Naqvi",4],["Zia Ul Haq",3],["Fazal Wadood",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q82","sec":4,"q":"Steering failure alarm in the HRA — immediate action?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>Losing steering is a <strong>navigational emergency<\/strong>. In a <strong>High Risk Area<\/strong> it also makes the ship an <strong>easier target<\/strong>, and the cause might be <strong>deliberate<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions: OOW<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Change over<\/strong> to the other steering pump or motor, and the other system or mode: non-follow-up, then hand steering. Check the <strong>rudder angle indicator<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master.<\/strong> Sound the alarm or <strong>call the engine room<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Send a crew member and an engineer to the <strong>steering gear room<\/strong> for <strong>emergency steering<\/strong>, using the bridge communication and the gyro repeater there. Communication with the bridge must be tested and ready (SOLAS V/26).<\/li><li>If steering can&#x27;t be restored straight away:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>reduce speed<\/strong> and <strong>stop the engines<\/strong> if necessary;<\/li><li>exhibit <strong>NUC lights or shapes<\/strong> (Rule 27) and sound the NUC fog signal if in restricted visibility;<\/li><li>broadcast a <strong>Sécurité<\/strong> and alert nearby traffic by VHF and AIS safety message.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Use the <strong>engines and thruster<\/strong> to keep clear of danger and other ships.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The security part<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Increase the lookout<\/strong>. A stopped or slow ship is vulnerable.<\/li><li>Consider whether it was <strong>sabotage<\/strong> or an <strong>attack<\/strong>: check for unauthorised people near the steering gear, and secure the steering gear room.<\/li><li>Inform the <strong>CSO<\/strong> and <strong>UKMTO<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Prepare to <strong>muster at the citadel<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If an attack starts: <strong>alarm<\/strong>, <strong>SSAS<\/strong>, <strong>distress<\/strong> as appropriate, and the <strong>UKMTO<\/strong> procedure.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the deck log.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the company, and under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong> to the flag or RO because it affects the ship&#x27;s safety.<\/li><li>Report to the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> if it&#x27;s a marine casualty or incident under the 2026 Regulations: loss of steering leading to a dangerous situation can be reportable.<\/li><li><strong>Repair<\/strong>, test and record, and get the class surveyor if needed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Emergency steering drills: SOLAS V/26<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>every 3 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>They include <strong>direct control<\/strong> from within the steering gear compartment, the <strong>communications procedure<\/strong> with the bridge, and, where applicable, the operation of <strong>alternative power supplies<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Recorded in the <strong>OLB<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Also: steering gear <strong>tested within 12 hours before departure<\/strong>, and the <strong>change-over procedures<\/strong> with a block diagram displayed on the bridge and in the steering gear room.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Change over and call the Master and engine room, emergency steering from the steering gear room, slow or stop, NUC signals, Sécurité, the security angle in an HRA with UKMTO, SSAS and citadel, then reporting; and the 3-monthly emergency steering drill under SOLAS V/26.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★5.1","sec":4,"q":"Your SSP says 'search 25% of visitors at Level 1'. Is that an ISPS requirement?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> It&#x27;s a <strong>company figure<\/strong> in our <strong>approved SSP<\/strong>, not an ISPS Code requirement. My P1 and Oral Notes present these percentages as rules. They&#x27;re company or SSP figures.<\/p><p><strong>What the ISPS Code actually requires<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>ISPS A/7<\/strong> requires the ship to carry out activities at each security level:<\/li><li><strong>controlling access<\/strong> to the ship;<\/li><li><strong>controlling embarkation<\/strong> of people and their effects;<\/li><li><strong>monitoring restricted areas<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>monitoring deck areas<\/strong> and areas around the ship;<\/li><li><strong>supervising the handling of cargo and ship&#x27;s stores<\/strong>;<\/li><li>ensuring <strong>security communication<\/strong> is readily available.<\/li><li><strong>ISPS A/9<\/strong> requires the SSP to set out the measures for each level.<\/li><li>The Code <strong>doesn&#x27;t prescribe percentages<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Part B<\/strong> gives guidance (B/9.14 onwards), for example that at Level 1 the SSP should set the <strong>frequency<\/strong> of searches of persons, their effects and vehicles, and that at Level 2 the frequency should <strong>increase<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Where the 25% comes from<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Ship Security Assessment<\/strong> looks at the ship&#x27;s layout, trade, crew and threats.<\/li><li>The CSO writes measures into the <strong>SSP<\/strong>, including specific figures, such as <strong>25%<\/strong> of visitors searched at Level 1 or <strong>50%<\/strong> at Level 2.<\/li><li>The SSP is <strong>approved by the Administration<\/strong>, the MCA for UK ships.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it still matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Once it&#x27;s in the <strong>approved SSP<\/strong>, it&#x27;s <strong>binding<\/strong> on us.<\/li><li>Failing to follow our own SSP is a <strong>non-conformity<\/strong> at an ISPS verification, and may be a <strong>PSC finding<\/strong>, and a sign to PSC that there are clear grounds for a closer look.<\/li><li>So I&#x27;d say: &quot;<strong>It isn&#x27;t an ISPS Code figure, but we must follow it because it&#x27;s in our approved plan.<\/strong>&quot;<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the figure is unworkable<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For example, at a busy container terminal with lashers and stevedores coming and going all day.<\/li><li>The <strong>SSO reports it to the CSO<\/strong>, who reviews the assessment and proposes an <strong>amendment<\/strong> to the SSP for approval by the Administration.<\/li><li>Meanwhile, we comply as far as we can and record any difficulty. We <strong>don&#x27;t just ignore it<\/strong>.<\/li><li>An amendment affecting the approved measures needs approval before it&#x27;s used.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How we apply it in practice<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A clear method: every fourth visitor, or random selection recorded in the log.<\/li><li>The gangway watch briefed.<\/li><li>Searches done respectfully, with a witness, and the right to refuse boarding if someone won&#x27;t be searched.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No, the ISPS Code doesn&#x27;t set percentages; A/7 sets the activities and Part B gives guidance; the figure comes from the SSA and approved SSP; it&#x27;s binding because it&#x27;s approved; and if it&#x27;s unworkable, the SSO goes through the CSO for an amendment.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can the SSO change a measure in the SSP himself?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> Changes to an approved SSP need <strong>flag (or RSO) approval<\/strong> (ISPS A/9.4–9.5). The CSO can bring in temporary extra measures, but the SSO can&#x27;t amend the plan himself.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★5.2","sec":4,"q":"The terminal is at Level 1 and the company tells you to go to Level 2. Do you need a DoS?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Yes, the ship should request one.<\/strong><\/p><p>Under <strong>ISPS A/5.2<\/strong>, a ship can request a <strong>Declaration of Security<\/strong> when it&#x27;s operating at a <strong>higher security level<\/strong> than the port facility. When it does, the <strong>PFSO must acknowledge<\/strong> the request (<strong>ISPS A/5.3<\/strong>).<\/p><p><strong>First, confirm the instruction<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For a UK ship, the security level is set by the <strong>DfT<\/strong>, and passed on through the <strong>CSO<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So I&#x27;d check whether this is a <strong>flag-directed Level 2<\/strong>, or the <strong>company<\/strong> asking us to apply Level 2 measures because of its own threat assessment.<\/li><li>Either way, the <strong>ship can operate above the port&#x27;s level<\/strong>. The Master can always add measures. He just can&#x27;t go below the level set by the flag or port State.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then the actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Put the Level 2 measures<\/strong> in the SSP into effect:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>fewer access points<\/strong>, with others locked;<\/li><li><strong>escort all visitors<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>more searches<\/strong> of people, effects and vehicles, to the SSP figure;<\/li><li><strong>extra deck patrols<\/strong> and monitoring of restricted areas;<\/li><li><strong>waterside monitoring<\/strong>, lighting, and watch for boats approaching;<\/li><li>tighter control of <strong>cargo and stores<\/strong> delivery.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Tell the PFSO<\/strong>, and <strong>request and complete the DoS<\/strong>, agreeing who does what:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>access control at the gangway and the terminal gate;<\/li><li>monitoring around the ship and on the quay;<\/li><li><strong>cargo and stores<\/strong> handling and checks;<\/li><li>communications between the ship and the port facility.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Brief the crew<\/strong>, and the <strong>terminal or stevedore foreman<\/strong>, so the lashers and stevedores know about the extra checks.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the level change, the DoS and the measures taken in the <strong>security records<\/strong> (ISPS A/10). It will appear in the last 10 port calls information.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the CSO that the measures are in place.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The port doesn&#x27;t have to raise its level<\/strong><\/p><p>The DoS just <strong>coordinates the measures<\/strong> at the interface. The terminal stays at Level 1 for everyone else.<\/p><p><strong>Practical difficulty<\/strong><\/p><p>At a busy container terminal, Level 2 measures can slow cargo work. I&#x27;d plan the extra watchkeepers with rest hours in mind, and tell the terminal early so they can plan too.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Yes, request a DoS under A/5.2, the PFSO must acknowledge it, confirm the instruction through the CSO, implement the SSP Level 2 measures, agree who does what in the DoS, brief the crew and terminal, and record it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Could the port facility refuse to sign the DoS?","pa":"<p>It must <strong>acknowledge<\/strong> the request, but doesn&#x27;t have to complete a DoS (ISPS A/5.3). The ship then applies her own Level 2 measures and records the request and the reply.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q83","sec":5,"q":"Passage planning requirements?","tier":"red","rep":95,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS V/34<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the <strong>Merchant Shipping (Safety of Navigation) Regulations 2020<\/strong>, with guidance in <strong>MGN 610 (M+F) Amd 1<\/strong>. Before going to sea, the Master must make sure the intended voyage is planned <strong>berth to berth<\/strong>, using appropriate nautical charts and publications, and taking into account the IMO guidance: <strong>Resolution A.893(21)<\/strong>, Guidelines for Voyage Planning. <strong>STCW A-VIII/2<\/strong> also requires it, and the SMS sets the company&#x27;s procedure.<\/p><p><strong>APEM<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. Appraisal: collect all relevant information<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Corrected <strong>ENCs<\/strong> (or paper charts), Sailing Directions, <strong>ALRS<\/strong>, tide tables and tidal stream atlases, <strong>NtMs<\/strong>, <strong>T&amp;Ps<\/strong>, navigational warnings, routeing guides and weather routeing.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s condition: <strong>draught<\/strong>, <strong>squat<\/strong>, <strong>air draught<\/strong>, manoeuvring data, and the company <strong>UKC policy<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Regulatory areas: <strong>load line zones<\/strong>, <strong>MARPOL special areas<\/strong> and <strong>ECAs<\/strong>, <strong>ballast water<\/strong> exchange areas, <strong>ship reporting systems<\/strong> and <strong>VTS<\/strong>, <strong>TSSs<\/strong>, and <strong>High Risk Areas<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Pilotage, port and berth information.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Planning<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Tracks<\/strong> and courses, <strong>wheel-over points<\/strong> and turning radius.<\/li><li><strong>Safety contour<\/strong> and <strong>safety depth<\/strong>, <strong>no-go areas<\/strong>, margins of safety and <strong>XTD limits<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Parallel indexes<\/strong> and clearing bearings.<\/li><li><strong>Abort points<\/strong>, points of no return and <strong>contingency anchorages<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fixing method and interval<\/strong>, and the primary and secondary means.<\/li><li><strong>Pilot boarding<\/strong> points and <strong>VTS reporting<\/strong> points, with VHF channels.<\/li><li>Tidal windows and <strong>UKC<\/strong> at critical points.<\/li><li>Speed and ETA, and where the engine must be on standby.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Execution: tactics<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Timing<\/strong>: for tidal windows, daylight in difficult areas, traffic density.<\/li><li><strong>Bridge manning levels<\/strong> for each phase, and engine readiness.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s condition and the effect of weather.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Monitoring<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the position by <strong>independent means<\/strong> at the planned interval.<\/li><li><strong>XTD alarms<\/strong> and the ECDIS anti-grounding alarms set.<\/li><li><strong>Amend the plan<\/strong> if conditions change, and re-brief.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Approval and briefing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master checks and approves<\/strong> the plan.<\/li><li>All bridge officers are <strong>briefed<\/strong> and <strong>sign<\/strong> it.<\/li><li>Any later change is formally <strong>amended and re-briefed<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>Feeding the plan with the ship&#x27;s draught, air draught, stability and cargo limitations, and checking it against my loading plan.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/34, the 2020 UK Regs and MGN 610, A.893(21), berth to berth, and APEM with specific content in each stage, approved by the Master and signed by the officers.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The pilot wants to take a different track from your plan. What do you do?","pa":"<p>Discuss it in the Master–pilot exchange and understand his reasons: traffic, tide, local knowledge. Check UKC and clearances on the new track and amend the plan with the Master&#x27;s agreement. The <strong>Master remains responsible<\/strong>: if the new track is unsafe, the Master refuses it.<\/p>","asked":["Voyage planning as chief mate?","What is the chief officer's input in passage planning?","How will you do passage planning ?","Passage plan as a chief mate?","For voyage from Singapore to Antwerp what inputs you will give to master?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",11],["Colum Leggett",8],["Chris Roe",8],["Kaiser Ali",7],["Khairul Anam",7]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q84","sec":5,"q":"ECDIS carriage requirements?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS V/19.2.10<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020, with MCA guidance on ECDIS in <strong>MGN 285<\/strong> (verify the current amendment) and MGN 379 on electronic navigational aids.<\/p><p><strong>Carriage was phased in<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>New ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Passenger ships<\/strong> of <strong>500 GT<\/strong> and over built on or after <strong>1 July 2012<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Tankers<\/strong> of <strong>3,000 GT<\/strong> and over built on or after <strong>1 July 2012<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo ships other than tankers<\/strong> of <strong>10,000 GT<\/strong> and over built on or after <strong>1 July 2013<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo ships other than tankers<\/strong> of <strong>3,000 GT<\/strong> and over but under 10,000 GT built on or after <strong>1 July 2014<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Existing ships, at the first survey after<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Passenger ships of 500 GT and over: 1 July 2014.<\/li><li>Tankers of 3,000 GT and over: 1 July 2015.<\/li><li>Cargo ships other than tankers of <strong>50,000 GT<\/strong> and over: 1 July 2016.<\/li><li><strong>20,000–50,000 GT<\/strong>: 1 July 2017.<\/li><li><strong>10,000–20,000 GT<\/strong>: 1 July 2018.<\/li><li><strong>Existing dry-cargo ships under 10,000 GT<\/strong> are <strong>not required<\/strong> to carry ECDIS.<\/li><\/ul><p>My notes [P1] said &quot;all cargo ships of 3,000 GT and over&quot;. That&#x27;s wrong for existing dry cargo ships.<\/p><p><strong>For ECDIS to meet the chart-carriage requirement (SOLAS V/19.2.1.4 and V/27)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The equipment is <strong>type-approved<\/strong> to the IMO performance standard (MSC.232(82)).<\/li><li>It uses <strong>official ENCs<\/strong>, kept <strong>up to date<\/strong> with the weekly updates.<\/li><li>It runs the current <strong>IHO S-52 presentation library<\/strong> and S-63 data protection.<\/li><li>There&#x27;s an <strong>adequate back-up<\/strong>: a <strong>second independent ECDIS<\/strong> on a separate power supply, or an appropriate folio of corrected <strong>paper charts<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Where no ENC exists, <strong>RCDS mode<\/strong> with an appropriate folio of paper charts.<\/li><li>Officers hold <strong>STCW generic ECDIS training<\/strong>, and the company has given <strong>type-specific familiarisation<\/strong> under ISM.<\/li><li>The software is maintained, with the manufacturer&#x27;s updates applied.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>My last ship<\/strong><\/p><p>Two ECDIS, no paper charts. <strong>Both units had to be working<\/strong> for the ship to be paperless. If one failed, the contingency plan said what to do: order paper charts for the rest of the voyage, or follow the company&#x27;s procedure, and report the defect.<\/p><p><strong>PSC checks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Type approval and S-52 version.<\/li><li>ENC permits and update status.<\/li><li>Officers&#x27; generic and type-specific training records.<\/li><li>The back-up arrangement and the contingency procedure.<\/li><li>That the officers can actually use it: they may ask the OOW to show the safety contour setting.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/19, the phase-in by ship type and size, that existing dry cargo ships under 10,000 GT aren&#x27;t required, and the conditions for ECDIS to count as the chart: type approval, official ENCs updated, back-up, and training.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"One ECDIS fails on your paperless ship the night before departure. Can you sail?","pa":"<p>Not as she is. With one ECDIS down, there&#x27;s <strong>no approved back-up<\/strong>. Repair it, or get an approved back-up (paper charts for the voyage) and tell the company and the flag. The Master decides, but PSC could detain a ship sailing without a back-up.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q85","sec":5,"q":"Mandatory sensors and alarms on ECDIS; how do you set safety contour, safety depth, shallow & deep contours?","tier":"red","rep":80,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Mandatory sensor inputs<\/strong><\/p><p>Under the IMO ECDIS performance standards (<strong>MSC.232(82)<\/strong>), ECDIS must be connected to:<\/p><ul><li><strong>position<\/strong>: GNSS;<\/li><li><strong>heading<\/strong>: gyro;<\/li><li><strong>speed and distance<\/strong>: the log.<\/li><\/ul><p>Normally also connected: <strong>radar<\/strong> overlay, <strong>AIS<\/strong>, <strong>echo sounder<\/strong>, and on newer ships the BNWAS and the autopilot for track control.<\/p><p><strong>Mandatory alarms include<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Crossing the <strong>safety contour<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Deviation from the route<\/strong> (XTD).<\/li><li><strong>Positioning system failure<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Approach to a <strong>critical point<\/strong> (wheel-over).<\/li><li>A <strong>different geodetic datum<\/strong> from WGS 84.<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS malfunction<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Indications<\/strong> include: areas with special conditions (no-go areas, TSSs, restricted areas), <strong>overscale<\/strong> display, a route planned across the safety contour, the largest-scale chart not in use, and the loss of a sensor.<\/p><p><strong>Settings, as laid down in the Master&#x27;s standing orders<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Safety contour<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>= <strong>static draught + squat + company UKC allowance<\/strong>, plus heel and a <strong>CATZOC<\/strong> allowance where needed.<\/li><li>If that depth isn&#x27;t an ENC contour, the ECDIS uses the <strong>next deeper contour<\/strong> available. So I check <strong>which contour is actually displayed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It divides safe water from unsafe water on the display and drives the anti-grounding alarm.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safety depth<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Normally the <strong>same figure<\/strong>. Spot soundings at or shallower than it are shown in <strong>bold<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Shallow contour<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>About my <strong>draught<\/strong>. Water between the shallow and safety contours is shown in a different shade.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Deep contour<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>About <strong>twice the draught<\/strong>, used in <strong>four-colour mode<\/strong> to show where squat and UKC start to matter.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Look-ahead or safety frame<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Set to give at least several minutes&#x27; warning at full speed, for example 6 minutes, and wide enough for the ship&#x27;s swing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Worked example<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Draught <strong>14.5 m<\/strong> + squat <strong>1.0 m<\/strong> + UKC <strong>1.5 m<\/strong> = <strong>17 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the ENC has only 10 m and 20 m contours, the <strong>20 m<\/strong> contour is displayed as the safety contour.<\/li><li>The safety depth is set at <strong>17 m<\/strong>, so 17 m and shallower soundings show in bold.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other settings<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>XTD limits per leg from the passage plan.<\/li><li>Vector time, track history, and radar overlay alignment.<\/li><li>Display category: Standard, switching to All when checking.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The three mandatory sensors, the main alarms, the formula for the safety contour, that the ECDIS uses the next deeper contour, the safety depth and bold soundings, the shallow and deep contours, and a worked example. He&#x27;ll often give me numbers to calculate.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Should you allow for the height of tide in the safety contour?","pa":"<p>Generally <strong>no<\/strong>. Set it on <strong>chart datum<\/strong> depths (draught + squat + UKC margin) so it protects you at all states of tide. Consider the tide in UKC planning and window calculations, not in the alarm setting, unless company policy says otherwise.<\/p>","asked":["What is safety depth and safety contour?","How to explain the limitations of ECDIS to the cadet?","Where will you find variation in ECDIS?","2/O not sure if ECDIS is primary means of navigation, where will you ask him to check?","Some ENC symbols, Catzoc?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",13],["Inamul Bhuiyan",11],["Piers Chubb",11],["Zia Ul Haq",9],["Colum Leggett",8]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q86","sec":5,"q":"How do you know you have the latest S-52 presentation library? T&P corrections? Overscale? Limitations?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. The S-52 presentation library<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ECDIS shows the <strong>S-52 presentation library edition<\/strong> on its system or status page. <strong>Edition 4.0<\/strong> became mandatory from <strong>1 September 2017<\/strong> (verify whether a later edition now applies).<\/li><li>To prove it displays correctly, load the IHO <strong>test data set (&quot;ECDIS Chart 1&quot;)<\/strong> and compare the symbols with the IHO reference.<\/li><li>Re-check after every <strong>software update<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>manufacturer&#x27;s maintenance record<\/strong> and the software version certificate confirm it.<\/li><li>The UKHO and IHO publish checks, and PSC may ask to see the version.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. T&amp;P corrections<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>ADMIRALTY <strong>ENCs<\/strong> include some <strong>T&amp;P<\/strong> information.<\/li><li>The rest is shown through the <strong>ADMIRALTY Information Overlay (AIO)<\/strong>, updated <strong>weekly<\/strong> with the ENC updates.<\/li><li>Relevant <strong>navigational warnings<\/strong> not in the data (NAVTEX, NAVAREA, local warnings) go in as <strong>manual updates<\/strong> or <strong>mariner&#x27;s notes<\/strong>, with the source and date.<\/li><li>The <strong>update log<\/strong> is checked to make sure all updates are applied and the cell editions are current.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Overscale<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If you zoom in past the <strong>compilation scale<\/strong>, the ECDIS shows an <strong>overscale indication<\/strong>: vertical lines, or a warning.<\/li><li>The data looks more accurate than it is.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>best-scale ENC<\/strong> for the area, and don&#x27;t navigate at overscale.<\/li><li>If a larger-scale ENC exists but isn&#x27;t in use, the ECDIS indicates it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Limitations of ECDIS<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The ENC is only as good as the <strong>survey<\/strong>: check the <strong>CATZOC<\/strong> (zones of confidence). Many areas were surveyed with lead lines decades ago.<\/li><li><strong>Sensor errors<\/strong>: GNSS jamming or spoofing, gyro error, log error. ECDIS shows the wrong position confidently.<\/li><li><strong>SCAMIN<\/strong> and the <strong>base display<\/strong> can hide objects. Some dangers only appear at larger scales or in &quot;All&quot; display.<\/li><li><strong>Alarm fatigue<\/strong>, or alarms switched off.<\/li><li><strong>Over-reliance<\/strong> on the display, and loss of traditional skills.<\/li><li><strong>Radar overlay misalignment<\/strong> with the chart.<\/li><li>Data from <strong>different producers<\/strong> may not match at cell boundaries.<\/li><li>The <strong>datum<\/strong> of older surveys may not be WGS 84.<\/li><li>Software faults and the risk of a crash or cyber attack.<\/li><li>Limited screen size: losing the &quot;big picture&quot;.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How I manage the limitations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Cross-check position by radar and visual fixes.<\/li><li>Plan with &quot;All&quot; display and check the route at best scale.<\/li><li>Set safety values with CATZOC margins.<\/li><li>Keep the alarms on and audible.<\/li><li>Train and familiarise officers properly.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>How to check the S-52 version (status page and Chart 1 test), T&amp;Ps through the AIO and manual updates, overscale indication, and a good list of limitations, especially CATZOC, sensor errors and SCAMIN.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is a CATZOC and how would a \"C\" zone change your UKC?","pa":"<p><strong>CATZOC<\/strong> is the <strong>category of zone of confidence<\/strong> in the survey data: A1, A2, B, C, D or U. Zone <strong>C<\/strong> means low accuracy: positions about ±500 m, depths about ±2 m + 5%, and the seabed not fully searched. <strong>Increase the UKC and the lateral margins.<\/strong><\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q87","sec":5,"q":"Guidance to the duty officer on ECDIS set-up?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>This goes in my <strong>standing orders<\/strong>, and follows the company <strong>SMS ECDIS procedure<\/strong>. At each <strong>watch handover<\/strong>, the OOW checks:<\/p><p><strong>1. Sensors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Primary and secondary <strong>GNSS<\/strong> healthy, <strong>datum WGS 84<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Gyro<\/strong> and <strong>log<\/strong> inputs agree with the repeaters.<\/li><li>Compare the GNSS position with a <strong>radar or visual fix<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check for any sensor alarm or loss.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Safety settings<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Safety contour<\/strong>, <strong>safety depth<\/strong>, <strong>shallow and deep contours<\/strong> exactly as set by the Master for the draught.<\/li><li><strong>XTD limit<\/strong> and <strong>look-ahead<\/strong> as per the plan.<\/li><li><strong>These are not changed without the Master&#x27;s permission<\/strong>, and any change is logged.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Alarms<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>All <strong>mandatory alarms enabled and audible<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Never silence them permanently<\/strong>, and don&#x27;t acknowledge an alarm without finding out why it sounded.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Display<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Correct <strong>scale<\/strong>, with <strong>no overscale<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Standard display<\/strong>, going to &quot;All&quot; when checking for detail.<\/li><li><strong>True motion<\/strong>, sensible <strong>vector time<\/strong> (for example 6 minutes).<\/li><li><strong>AIS<\/strong> and <strong>radar overlay<\/strong> checked for alignment.<\/li><li>Day, dusk or night <strong>palette<\/strong> as appropriate.<\/li><li>Track history on.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Route<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>approved, checked route<\/strong> is loaded and <strong>monitored<\/strong>, not a draft or an old one.<\/li><li><strong>Wheel-over points<\/strong> and <strong>parallel indexes<\/strong> visible.<\/li><li>The next waypoints and the ETA checked.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Data<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Chart updates<\/strong> and <strong>AIO<\/strong> current.<\/li><li><strong>T&amp;Ps<\/strong> and <strong>navigational warnings<\/strong> plotted as mariner&#x27;s notes.<\/li><li>Permits valid for the area.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Monitoring<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Fix by independent means<\/strong> at the interval in the plan: radar ranges and bearings, visual bearings.<\/li><li>Remember that <strong>ECDIS is an aid<\/strong>. The <strong>lookout comes first<\/strong>, by sight and hearing (COLREG Rule 5).<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t spend the watch looking at the screen.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Log any ECDIS <strong>fault<\/strong> or <strong>setting change<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Log the position fixes and the method.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>9. Call me if<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>you&#x27;re <strong>in doubt<\/strong>;<\/li><li>any <strong>sensor fails<\/strong> or GNSS and radar positions disagree;<\/li><li>the ship <strong>deviates from the plan<\/strong> or can&#x27;t follow it;<\/li><li>an alarm you can&#x27;t explain;<\/li><li>the ECDIS freezes or fails.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the ECDIS fails<\/strong><\/p><p>Change to the <strong>back-up ECDIS<\/strong>, check its route and settings, fix the position, and call the Master. Follow the contingency plan.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A structured handover check: sensors, safety settings, alarms, display, route, data, independent monitoring, records, and when to call the Master. He wants to hear &quot;not changed without my permission&quot; and &quot;the lookout comes first&quot;.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The OOW has set the safety contour to 10 m \"to stop the alarms\". What do you do?","pa":"<p>Correct it immediately to the value based on <strong>draught + UKC<\/strong>, and explain why. Check his other settings. Inform the Master, record it, and deal with alarm management properly. It may also be a training issue or an ISM non-conformity.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q88","sec":5,"q":"RCDS mode — when and with what?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>SOLAS <strong>V/19<\/strong> (carriage) and <strong>V/27<\/strong> (up-to-date charts), applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020, with the MCA&#x27;s ECDIS guidance in <strong>MGN 285<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>When<\/strong><\/p><p>RCDS mode is used <strong>only where no official ENC exists<\/strong> for the area. The ECDIS then displays <strong>official raster charts<\/strong>: ARCS or other RNCs, which are scanned images of paper charts.<\/p><p><strong>With what<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In RCDS mode the ECDIS <strong>does not meet chart-carriage requirements on its own<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It must be used together with an <strong>appropriate folio of up-to-date paper charts<\/strong> for the area.<\/li><li>The <strong>paper chart is the legal chart<\/strong>, and the primary means of navigation for that part of the voyage.<\/li><li>The passage plan must say where RCDS mode will be used.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Limitations to brief the OOWs on<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A raster chart is <strong>just a picture<\/strong>, so there are <strong>no automatic anti-grounding alarms<\/strong>: no safety contour or safety depth alarm.<\/li><li>You <strong>can&#x27;t query<\/strong> objects or <strong>remove clutter<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>horizontal datum<\/strong> may not be WGS 84, so check the <strong>datum shift<\/strong> notes and set the offset.<\/li><li><strong>Depth units<\/strong> may be fathoms, and the <strong>orientation<\/strong> fixed north-up.<\/li><li><strong>Updates<\/strong> are whole-chart patches, and must be applied.<\/li><li><strong>Scale changes<\/strong> are crude; zooming in just magnifies the picture.<\/li><li>Radar overlay may not align if the datum or projection differs.<\/li><li>Different charts may have different datums and scales at the joins.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>To compensate<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Draw <strong>no-go areas<\/strong>, <strong>clearing lines<\/strong> and <strong>danger circles<\/strong> manually as <strong>mariner&#x27;s notes<\/strong> or user charts.<\/li><li>Set the route <strong>XTD alarm tightly<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fix more often<\/strong> by <strong>radar and visual<\/strong> methods.<\/li><li><strong>Plot and cross-check<\/strong> on the <strong>paper chart<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Extra <strong>lookout<\/strong> and bridge manning, depending on the area.<\/li><li>A <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> for the passage.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Log when you enter and leave RCDS mode<\/strong>, and the paper charts in use.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other options<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check whether ENCs have become available, from the UKHO or another producer, before the voyage.<\/li><li>Some areas only have paper charts. If the ship is paperless and has no paper charts for the area, she needs to get them before going there.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Only where no ENC exists, together with an appropriate folio of up-to-date paper charts which are the legal charts, no anti-grounding alarms, datum shift, and how to compensate: manual no-go areas, tight XTD, frequent fixes and paper chart plotting.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why won't the ECDIS give a grounding alarm on a raster chart?","pa":"<p>A raster chart is just a picture. It has <strong>no vector depth data<\/strong>, so the ECDIS can&#x27;t check the ship against a safety contour. The OOW has to draw <strong>no-go areas<\/strong> manually and use other means to stay safe.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q89","sec":5,"q":"2/O confused about ECDIS rules — brief him as Master.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>I&#x27;d sit down with him in a quiet moment and keep it <strong>structured<\/strong>. The aim is to fix his understanding, not to embarrass him.<\/p><p><strong>1. The law<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS V/19<\/strong>: ECDIS carriage requirements.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS V/27<\/strong>: charts and publications kept <strong>up to date<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS V/34<\/strong>: <strong>passage planning<\/strong> berth to berth.<\/li><li>Applied in the UK by the <strong>Safety of Navigation Regs 2020<\/strong>, with MCA guidance in <strong>MGN 285<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 610<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. On this ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>ECDIS is our <strong>primary means of navigation<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>second ECDIS<\/strong> is the <strong>back-up<\/strong>. There are <strong>no paper charts<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>double ECDIS failure<\/strong> means following the <strong>company contingency procedure<\/strong>: radar and visual navigation, safe passage to a port, getting paper charts.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Training<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>He needs <strong>STCW generic ECDIS training<\/strong> plus <strong>type-specific familiarisation<\/strong> for <strong>this model<\/strong>, both recorded under ISM.<\/li><li>If he <strong>isn&#x27;t confident<\/strong>, he <strong>doesn&#x27;t keep a watch alone<\/strong> until he is. That&#x27;s for his protection as well as the ship&#x27;s.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Data<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Official ENCs only<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Permits<\/strong> checked for the voyage.<\/li><li><strong>Weekly updates<\/strong> and the <strong>AIO<\/strong> applied, and the <strong>update log<\/strong> kept.<\/li><li><strong>T&amp;Ps<\/strong> and <strong>navigational warnings<\/strong> added as manual updates.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Planning<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Plan at the <strong>best scale<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>&quot;check route&quot;<\/strong> function and a <strong>visual check<\/strong> of the whole route at best scale, in &quot;All&quot; display, because the automatic check can miss things.<\/li><li><strong>Safety values<\/strong> are set from my standing orders.<\/li><li>No-go areas, parallel indexes, wheel-overs, abort points.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Watchkeeping<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Alarms stay on<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cross-check<\/strong> by <strong>radar and visual<\/strong> means.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>overscale<\/strong> and <strong>SCAMIN<\/strong> hiding objects.<\/li><li><strong>Call me<\/strong> on any <strong>sensor fault<\/strong> or doubt.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Log <strong>faults<\/strong> and <strong>setting changes<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then the practical part<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I&#x27;d <strong>watch him set up a route<\/strong> and <strong>check his settings<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Ask him to show me the safety contour, the alarms, and how he&#x27;d change to the back-up.<\/li><li>Give him a scenario: &quot;GNSS fails. What do you do?&quot;<\/li><li><strong>Record the briefing<\/strong> as part of his familiarisation.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check his watches over the next few days.<\/li><li>If there&#x27;s a real gap in training, ask the company for an ECDIS refresher or the type-specific course.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The law (V/19, V/27, V/34, MGN 285), our ship&#x27;s arrangement, training requirements, data, planning, watchkeeping, records, and that I check him practically and record it. He doesn&#x27;t keep a watch alone until he&#x27;s competent.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Where in the SMS would you find the ECDIS procedures, and who audits them?","pa":"<p>In the <strong>SMS navigation and bridge procedures<\/strong>: the ECDIS section and the company&#x27;s ECDIS manual. They&#x27;re audited by the <strong>company<\/strong> in internal ISM audits, and by the <strong>flag or RO<\/strong> at SMC audits. PSC checks them too.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q90","sec":16,"q":"Radar: sea vs ground stabilisation; why 3 equally-timed observations; scanty radar information?","tier":"red","rep":11,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>MCA guidance on radar and ARPA is in <strong>MGN 379 (M+F) Amd 1<\/strong>, and <strong>COLREG Rule 7<\/strong> applies, in the UK through MSN 1781.<\/p><p><strong>Sea stabilisation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Uses the <strong>gyro heading<\/strong> and the <strong>log speed through the water<\/strong>.<\/li><li>True vectors then show each target&#x27;s <strong>heading through the water<\/strong>, which is its <strong>real aspect<\/strong>: the way she&#x27;s pointing, and so which of her lights I&#x27;d see.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s what I need for <strong>collision avoidance<\/strong> and for applying the <strong>steering rules<\/strong>, which depend on the aspect (head-on, crossing, overtaking).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Ground stabilisation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Uses <strong>GNSS<\/strong> or a <strong>bottom-track<\/strong> log for the <strong>speed and course over the ground<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use it for <strong>navigation<\/strong>: <strong>parallel indexing<\/strong>, the <strong>land staying still<\/strong> on the screen, and seeing <strong>set and drift<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In a <strong>strong tide<\/strong> or current, ground-stabilised vectors give a <strong>false picture<\/strong> of <strong>aspect<\/strong>:<\/li><li>an <strong>anchored<\/strong> ship can look as if it&#x27;s <strong>under way<\/strong> (its vector shows the tide going past);<\/li><li>a ship stemming the tide can look stopped;<\/li><li>a crossing ship can look like it&#x27;s on a different course.<\/li><li>So for COLREG decisions, <strong>sea-stabilised<\/strong> true vectors, or relative vectors, are the safer picture.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Relative vectors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Show the target&#x27;s movement relative to my ship. The <strong>CPA<\/strong> is read directly from where the relative vector passes my ship.<\/li><li>They&#x27;re the same whatever the stabilisation, because they come from the target&#x27;s movement on the screen.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why three equally-timed observations<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Two<\/strong> observations only give you a <strong>line<\/strong>: you can&#x27;t tell whether the target is steady.<\/li><li>A <strong>third<\/strong>, at the <strong>same interval<\/strong>, shows whether the target&#x27;s <strong>course or speed<\/strong> has <strong>changed<\/strong>. If the three points aren&#x27;t in a straight line, or not equally spaced, she&#x27;s manoeuvring.<\/li><li>It lets you check the <strong>CPA<\/strong> and <strong>TCPA<\/strong> are <strong>consistent<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Equal timing<\/strong> makes the plotting geometry valid: the relative velocity triangle needs equal time intervals.<\/li><li>In practice, keep plotting until the situation is clear.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Scanty radar information<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Rule 7(c)<\/strong>: assumptions shall <strong>not<\/strong> be made on the basis of <strong>scanty information<\/strong>, especially scanty radar information.<\/li><li>An <strong>ARPA<\/strong> needs about <strong>3 minutes<\/strong> of steady tracking for reliable predictions; it shows a trend after about 1 minute (MGN 379 and the IMO performance standards).<\/li><li><strong>Single<\/strong> observations, <strong>newly acquired<\/strong> targets, <strong>short plots<\/strong>, targets lost in clutter, or after own ship has manoeuvred, are <strong>scanty<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So don&#x27;t act on a first impression of a target; plot it, or wait for the ARPA to stabilise.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Sea stabilisation with gyro and log giving true aspect for collision avoidance; ground stabilisation with GNSS for navigation and the false aspect in a tide, with the anchored ship example; why three equally-timed observations (detect manoeuvres, consistency, valid geometry); and Rule 7(c) scanty information with the ARPA&#x27;s 3 minutes.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"In a 4-knot tidal stream, which stabilisation would you use to judge a crossing situation?","pa":"<p><strong>Sea stabilisation.<\/strong> It shows the target&#x27;s heading and <strong>aspect through the water<\/strong>, which is what COLREGs is about. Ground stabilisation in a strong stream gives misleading aspects.<\/p>","asked":["What is the difference between ground stabilised and sea stabilised?","12nm scale, north up, sea stabilized.","Actions – Told him I would change over to sea stabilized as I cannot accurately determine the aspect of the target.","Showed me the same plot in sea stabilized mode – Slow Down.","How do you select sea stabilized and ground stabilized and its use"],"ex":[["Alec Keep",4],["Colum Leggett",2],["Examiner not named",1],["Trevor Hughes",1],["Khairul Anam",1]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q91","sec":5,"q":"Speed log failure — what is affected and what do you do?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS V/19<\/strong> requires a speed and distance measuring device, applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020.<\/p><p><strong>What is affected<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>ARPA in sea-stabilised mode<\/strong>: targets&#x27; <strong>true course and speed<\/strong>, their <strong>aspect<\/strong>, and <strong>trial manoeuvres<\/strong> become wrong. <strong>Relative CPA and TCPA still work<\/strong>, because they come from relative motion.<\/li><li>The <strong>gyro speed-error correction<\/strong>, if it takes the log input.<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS<\/strong> dead reckoning and estimated positions, and set and drift calculations.<\/li><li>The <strong>VDR<\/strong> speed record.<\/li><li><strong>BNWAS<\/strong> or integrated bridge inputs that use it, and the autopilot if it uses speed for its tuning.<\/li><li>Anything needing <strong>speed through the water<\/strong>: <strong>squat<\/strong> and manoeuvring predictions, speed and consumption records, and the <strong>CII<\/strong> data.<\/li><li><strong>AIS<\/strong> doesn&#x27;t transmit speed through the water, so no direct effect, but other systems may cross-check.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong> and the OOWs.<\/li><li><strong>ARPA<\/strong>: switch to <strong>manual speed input<\/strong>, estimated from RPM or pitch using the manoeuvring data, or to <strong>ground-stabilised GNSS speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Brief everyone<\/strong> on the limitations:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>with GNSS speed, the ARPA gives <strong>ground-stabilised<\/strong> vectors, so in a <strong>tidal stream<\/strong> or current the target&#x27;s <strong>aspect<\/strong> can be misleading for COLREG decisions;<\/li><li>with manual speed, the accuracy is only as good as the estimate.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Use the eyes<\/strong>: visual bearings, and look at the other ship&#x27;s aspect and lights.<\/li><li>Set the <strong>gyro speed corrector<\/strong> manually.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the logbook.<\/li><li>Raise a <strong>defect report<\/strong> under the SMS and <strong>inform the company<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Arrange repair<\/strong> at the next port. Inform the <strong>MCA or RO<\/strong> if it affects the ship&#x27;s certification or a survey (SOLAS I/11(c)), and expect <strong>PSC<\/strong> to ask about it.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS V/16.2<\/strong>: the defect doesn&#x27;t make the ship unseaworthy if suitable arrangements are made to reach a repair port.<\/li><li><strong>Assess the risk<\/strong> before entering <strong>confined or tidal waters<\/strong>: extra caution, extra lookout, and possibly a pilot or tug.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Causes to check<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Sensor fouled by marine growth, a blocked doppler or electromagnetic log, a failed transducer, or a power supply fault.<\/li><li>The ETO may be able to repair it.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>ARPA true vectors and aspect wrong but relative CPA and TCPA right, gyro correction, ECDIS DR, VDR; switch to manual or GNSS speed with the tidal limitation; report to the company; repair; and care in tidal waters.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why are your relative CPAs still correct with the log failed?","pa":"<p>Because the <strong>relative motion<\/strong> is worked from the target&#x27;s successive bearings and ranges, and <strong>doesn&#x27;t use own speed<\/strong>. Only the target&#x27;s true course and speed need own speed input.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q92","sec":5,"q":"One generator fails before a coastal passage and berthing — actions?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. Inform and assess<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Get the <strong>Chief Engineer&#x27;s assessment<\/strong>: what caused it, how long the repair will take, and the <strong>load available<\/strong> from the remaining sets.<\/li><li>Is the failure likely to spread to the other generators, for example a fuel or cooling problem?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Risk assessment<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>With <strong>no spare generator<\/strong>, the risk is a <strong>blackout in confined waters<\/strong>: loss of propulsion, steering pumps and navigation equipment at the worst moment.<\/li><li>We also need enough power for the <strong>bow thruster<\/strong>, which may not run on reduced capacity.<\/li><li>The <strong>reefer<\/strong> load on a container ship can be very large.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Load management<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Shed non-essential loads<\/strong>: galley equipment, accommodation air conditioning, workshop, deck lighting.<\/li><li>On a container ship, manage the <strong>reefer load<\/strong>, for example by staggering defrosts and temporarily switching off some reefers for short periods, while <strong>protecting critical and DG reefers<\/strong> (temperature-controlled DG or high-value cargo).<\/li><li>Check the <strong>preferential trips<\/strong> work, so non-essential loads drop first if there&#x27;s an overload.<\/li><li>Have the <strong>emergency generator<\/strong> on <strong>auto-start<\/strong> and tested.<\/li><li>Start a standby generator if there&#x27;s one that can be made available.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Steering<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Run <strong>both steering motors<\/strong> in areas where navigation demands special caution (<strong>SOLAS V/25<\/strong>).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Berthing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Order <strong>extra tugs<\/strong> if the <strong>thruster<\/strong> can&#x27;t be guaranteed.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the pilot<\/strong> and <strong>VTS<\/strong> of the reduced power.<\/li><li>Prepare <strong>anchors<\/strong> fore and aft for emergency use.<\/li><li>Keep <strong>engineers on standby<\/strong> in the engine room, and the ER manned.<\/li><li>Plan the manoeuvre to avoid high power demands at the same time: for example, not the thruster at full power while the mooring winches are working.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Consider delaying<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Delay the passage or berthing until the generator is repaired, if that&#x27;s safer, for example by waiting at a safe anchorage.<\/li><li>The Master decides, and the charterer and port are told.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Records and reporting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Logbook<\/strong> entries.<\/li><li><strong>Defect report<\/strong> under the SMS.<\/li><li>Inform the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>charterers<\/strong> about any <strong>reefer impact<\/strong>, because cargo claims follow temperature excursions.<\/li><li>Inform <strong>class<\/strong> or the <strong>MCA<\/strong> if the defect affects the ship&#x27;s certification (SOLAS I/11(c)) or the electrical requirements.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Repair<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Spares and technicians at the port.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Chief Engineer&#x27;s assessment, a blackout risk in confined waters, load shedding with reefers managed, emergency generator ready, both steering motors, extra tugs and informing the pilot and VTS, anchors ready, and considering delay.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The bow thruster can't be used. How does that change your berthing plan?","pa":"<p>Order more tugs, or bigger ones, and connect them earlier. Approach slower. Choose the timing so the wind and tide help. Have the anchor ready. Tell the pilot, agree an abort plan, and consider delaying the berthing.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q93","sec":5,"q":"AIS — when can it be switched off?","tier":"yellow","rep":7,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>AIS carriage is under <strong>SOLAS V/19.2.4<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020. Operational guidance is <strong>IMO Resolution A.1106(29)<\/strong>, and in the UK <strong>MGN 324 (M+F) Amd 2<\/strong> on VHF and AIS.<\/p><p><strong>The rule<\/strong><\/p><p>AIS must be <strong>kept in operation at all times<\/strong>, at sea and <strong>at anchor<\/strong>. The only exceptions are where international agreements, rules or standards provide for the protection of navigational information.<\/p><p><strong>The Master may switch it off<\/strong><\/p><p>If he believes that continued operation could <strong>compromise the ship&#x27;s safety or security<\/strong>. Examples:<\/p><ol><li>In a <strong>High Risk Area<\/strong>, where <strong>pirates<\/strong> could use AIS to target the ship, if the current guidance (BMP Maritime Security) and the company advise it.<\/li><li>Under a <strong>specific security threat<\/strong>, such as a credible threat of attack in a conflict area.<\/li><li>Where the military or naval authorities advise ships to switch off or limit AIS data.<\/li><\/ol><p>Many current guidelines now recommend <strong>keeping AIS on<\/strong> but limiting the data, for example not showing the destination, because naval forces use AIS to protect ships. So I&#x27;d follow the latest guidance.<\/p><p><strong>Conditions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the <strong>reason<\/strong>, <strong>time<\/strong> and <strong>position<\/strong> in the logbook.<\/li><li><strong>Switch it back on<\/strong> as soon as the threat has passed.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> the action as company or security procedures require, for example to UKMTO.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In port<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Follow <strong>port and terminal rules<\/strong>. Some ports allow <strong>low power<\/strong> or switching off alongside, for example tankers at oil terminals.<\/li><li><strong>At anchor it stays on<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What you lose when you switch it off<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Other ships and VTS<\/strong> lose you.<\/li><li><strong>SAR authorities<\/strong> lose your track, which matters if something happens.<\/li><li><strong>AIS supports collision avoidance<\/strong>, though it isn&#x27;t the main means. COLREG decisions are made with radar and visual information.<\/li><li>Your <strong>LRIT<\/strong> still transmits to the flag, and the SSAS still works.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Keeping AIS data correct<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The OOW checks the <strong>static and voyage data<\/strong> at each departure: navigational status, draught, destination, ETA, number of persons, hazardous cargo category.<\/li><li><strong>Navigational status<\/strong> changed when anchoring or berthing.<\/li><li>Wrong AIS data is a PSC deficiency and misleads other ships.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/19, A.1106(29), always on including at anchor, the Master may switch it off for safety or security reasons, record it and switch it back on, port rules alongside, and what you lose. MGN 324 also warns not to use AIS or VHF to agree collision avoidance.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Is AIS information reliable enough to use for collision avoidance?","pa":"<p><strong>No, not on its own.<\/strong> AIS can be wrong (heading, position, unverified data), some vessels don&#x27;t transmit, and there are time lags. Use it for identification and awareness, and make collision-avoidance decisions on <strong>radar and visual<\/strong> information (Rule 7: no assumptions from scanty information).<\/p>","asked":["What is AIS and how does it work?","When you check visually for V-AIS, what you will see outside.","(here he wanted to hear AIS & VDR service report)","Are the vectors given by AIS targets reliable?","Morse 'D' and AIS Radar Transponder)"],"ex":[["Alec Keep",3],["Khademul Insan",1],["Piers Chubb",1],["Robert Jackson",1],["Inamul Bhuiyan",1]],"yrs":"2021–2025"},{"id":"Q94","sec":5,"q":"Ship reporting systems, VTS, routeing (SOLAS V/10, 11, 12)?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>SOLAS V/10: Ships&#x27; routeing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>IMO-adopted schemes: <strong>traffic separation schemes<\/strong>, <strong>deep-water routes<\/strong>, <strong>areas to be avoided<\/strong>, <strong>precautionary areas<\/strong>, two-way routes, recommended tracks.<\/li><li>Ships <strong>shall use<\/strong> mandatory schemes and should use the recommended ones.<\/li><li><strong>COLREG Rule 10<\/strong> governs conduct in an IMO-adopted TSS. UK guidance on TSSs is <strong>MGN 364<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Remember:<\/li><li>proceed in the appropriate <strong>lane<\/strong> in the general direction of traffic flow;<\/li><li><strong>join or leave<\/strong> at the termination, or at a small angle;<\/li><li><strong>cross at right angles<\/strong> as nearly as practicable, on a heading at right angles;<\/li><li>use the <strong>inshore traffic zone<\/strong> only as Rule 10(d) allows;<\/li><li><strong>not impede<\/strong> vessels in the lane if you&#x27;re under 20 m, sailing or fishing;<\/li><li>avoid anchoring in a TSS or near its terminations.<\/li><li>Details are in the IMO publication <strong>Ships&#x27; Routeing<\/strong>, the charts and the Sailing Directions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS V/11: Ship reporting systems<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>IMO-adopted<\/strong> systems are <strong>mandatory<\/strong> for the ships they apply to.<\/li><li>The Master reports in the set format: <strong>entry<\/strong>, <strong>position<\/strong>, <strong>deviation<\/strong>, <strong>exit<\/strong>, and <strong>dangerous cargo<\/strong> or defects.<\/li><li>Example: <strong>CALDOVREP<\/strong> in the Dover Strait, <strong>MAREP<\/strong>, the Ushant system, and the Finisterre system.<\/li><li>Details are in the <strong>ALRS<\/strong> (verify the volume: reporting systems and VTS details are in Vol 1 and Vol 6).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS V/12: Vessel Traffic Services<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Set up by <strong>coastal States<\/strong> to improve safety and efficiency of traffic and protect the environment.<\/li><li><strong>Use is mandatory<\/strong> within territorial seas where the State requires it. Outside, ships are <strong>encouraged<\/strong> to take part.<\/li><li>VTS gives <strong>information<\/strong>, <strong>navigational assistance<\/strong> or <strong>traffic organisation<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Responsibility stays with the Master<\/strong>. VTS advice doesn&#x27;t relieve him of his duties.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>UK additional requirements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ships carrying <strong>dangerous or polluting goods<\/strong> to or from UK ports, and pre-arrival notifications for ships of 300 GT and above, under the <strong>MS (Vessel Traffic Monitoring and Reporting Requirements) Regulations 2004<\/strong> and <strong>MSN 1899<\/strong>, through <strong>CERS<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Reports to HM Coastguard and the ports.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In the passage plan<\/strong><\/p><p>These go into the plan as <strong>reporting points<\/strong>, <strong>VHF channels<\/strong>, the <strong>information required<\/strong>, and the <strong>times<\/strong>. The OOW has the reports prepared before the reporting point.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>V/10 routeing and Rule 10 with MGN 364, V/11 mandatory reporting systems such as CALDOVREP, V/12 VTS with the Master still responsible, the UK VTM Regs and CERS, and that they&#x27;re all in the passage plan.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What does a VTS operator's \"advice\" mean legally?","pa":"<p>Advice isn&#x27;t an order. <strong>The Master stays responsible<\/strong> for navigation. In a mandatory VTS area the ship must follow its rules and any <strong>instructions<\/strong> it is legally allowed to give.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q95","sec":5,"q":"Danger messages — SOLAS V/31, contents?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS V/31<\/strong> (danger messages) and <strong>V/32<\/strong> (information required), applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020.<\/p><p><strong>When the Master must report<\/strong><\/p><p>To <strong>ships in the vicinity<\/strong> and to the <strong>competent authorities<\/strong>, by <strong>any means<\/strong> at his disposal, when he meets:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Dangerous ice<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>dangerous derelict<\/strong> or any other <strong>direct danger to navigation<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>tropical storm<\/strong>, or reason to believe one is near.<\/li><li><strong>Sub-freezing air temperatures<\/strong> with <strong>gale-force winds<\/strong>, causing <strong>severe ice accretion<\/strong> on superstructures.<\/li><li><strong>Winds of force 10 or above<\/strong> on the Beaufort scale, for which <strong>no storm warning<\/strong> has been received.<\/li><li>From <strong>1 January 2026<\/strong>, the <strong>loss of containers overboard<\/strong>, and observed <strong>drifting containers<\/strong> (<strong>MSC.550(108)<\/strong>).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>With the <strong>SÉCURITÉ<\/strong> priority, on VHF and through the GMDSS (DSC or the recognised satellite service), or by NAVTEX request via the coast station.<\/li><li>In <strong>UK waters<\/strong>, the report goes to <strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Every danger message should be given in English or the International Code of Signals, and in plain language where possible.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Contents: SOLAS V/32<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Ice, derelict or other danger<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>kind<\/strong> of danger.<\/li><li>Its <strong>position<\/strong> when last observed.<\/li><li>The <strong>UTC time and date<\/strong> of the observation.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tropical storm<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>statement<\/strong> that a tropical storm has been encountered, or a suspicion of one.<\/li><li><strong>Time, date<\/strong> (UTC) and <strong>position<\/strong> of the ship.<\/li><li><strong>Barometric pressure<\/strong>, corrected, with the units, and the <strong>tendency<\/strong> over the last 3 hours.<\/li><li><strong>True wind direction<\/strong> and <strong>force<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>State of the sea<\/strong> and <strong>swell<\/strong>: direction, period and height.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s <strong>true course and speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Subsequent observations<\/strong>: should be sent <strong>hourly<\/strong> if practicable, and <strong>at least every 3 hours<\/strong> while the ship is under the storm&#x27;s influence.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Ice accretion, force 10<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Similar information: position, time, air and sea temperature, wind, and the rate of icing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Lost containers (V/31 and V/32 from 2026)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Position<\/strong> of the loss, or the containers&#x27; last known position.<\/li><li><strong>Number<\/strong> of containers lost.<\/li><li>Whether any contain <strong>dangerous goods<\/strong>, with <strong>UN numbers<\/strong> and class.<\/li><li><strong>Type, size and condition<\/strong> of the containers.<\/li><li>Also reported to the <strong>flag State<\/strong>, and the ship&#x27;s company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Record it in the <strong>logbook<\/strong>, with the time sent and to whom.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The list of dangers including the 2026 lost-container addition, SÉCURITÉ, to ships nearby and the coastal authority, and the contents for ice, derelicts, tropical storms and lost containers, with the hourly or 3-hourly follow-up for storms.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Is reporting a tropical storm a legal duty or good practice?","pa":"<p>A <strong>legal duty<\/strong>. Under <strong>SOLAS V/31<\/strong>, a Master who meets a tropical storm, dangerous ice, a wind of force 10 or more without warning, or another direct danger must report it.<\/p>","asked":["SOLAS Chapter V, Regulation 31 (Danger Messages)"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",1],["Chris Roe",1]],"yrs":"2023–2026"},{"id":"Q96","sec":5,"q":"VDR — annual performance test and what it records?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS V/20<\/strong> requires a VDR on <strong>passenger ships<\/strong>, and on <strong>cargo ships of 3,000 GT and over built on or after 1 July 2002<\/strong>. Certain older cargo ships carry a <strong>simplified VDR (S-VDR)<\/strong>. Applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020.<\/p><p><strong>Annual performance test: SOLAS V/18.8<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Carried out by an <strong>approved testing or servicing facility<\/strong>, often the manufacturer&#x27;s agent.<\/li><li>It checks the <strong>accuracy, duration and recoverability<\/strong> of the recorded data, and the <strong>protective capsules<\/strong>, their mounting, and the <strong>beacons<\/strong> (underwater locator beacon battery date).<\/li><li>A <strong>certificate of compliance<\/strong> is issued and kept on board. PSC asks for it.<\/li><li>Between tests, the crew check the VDR status daily and record the checks, and test the data saving.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it records, with a time stamp<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Date and time<\/strong>, from an external source.<\/li><li>Ship&#x27;s <strong>position<\/strong>, with the datum.<\/li><li><strong>Speed<\/strong> through the water or over the ground.<\/li><li><strong>Heading<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Bridge audio<\/strong> from the microphones on the bridge and the bridge wings.<\/li><li><strong>VHF communications<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Radar<\/strong> images, and the <strong>ECDIS<\/strong> display or data.<\/li><li><strong>Echo sounder<\/strong> depth.<\/li><li><strong>Main alarms<\/strong> on the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Rudder order and response<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Engine and thruster<\/strong> orders and responses.<\/li><li><strong>Hull openings<\/strong> status, and <strong>watertight and fire door<\/strong> status.<\/li><li><strong>Wind<\/strong> speed and direction.<\/li><li><strong>AIS<\/strong> data.<\/li><li><strong>Hull stress<\/strong> and <strong>accelerations<\/strong> where fitted, and <strong>rolling motion<\/strong> on newer units.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Storage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Newer VDRs (to the 2014 performance standards, MSC.333(90)) keep at least <strong>48 hours<\/strong> in the capsules, and <strong>30 days<\/strong> in a long-term recording medium (verify against your unit&#x27;s standard).<\/li><li>They have a <strong>fixed protective capsule<\/strong>, a <strong>float-free capsule<\/strong> with an EPIRB-type beacon, and a long-term recording medium.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After an accident<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>Accident Reporting and Investigation Regs 2026<\/strong> require evidence to be preserved.<\/li><li><strong>Save the VDR data immediately<\/strong>, before it&#x27;s <strong>overwritten<\/strong>, using the save button or the procedure in the manual.<\/li><li>Keep it for the <strong>MAIB<\/strong>. Don&#x27;t download or analyse it yourself unless the MAIB asks. Record that it was saved and when.<\/li><li>The company may want a copy, but the MAIB has priority.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Other uses<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Training and incident review, such as a near miss, with the crew&#x27;s knowledge and in line with the company policy.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/20, the annual test under V/18.8 by an approved facility with a certificate, the list of what it records, the capsules and 48 hours, and saving the data straight after an accident for the MAIB.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who on board knows how to save the VDR, and how long do you have?","pa":"<p>The Master and officers, trained as the SMS requires. Press the <strong>save<\/strong> function <strong>immediately<\/strong>: older units overwrite after about <strong>12 hours<\/strong>, and newer ones keep at least 48 hours in protected memory (verify for your type).<\/p>","asked":["VDR DATA TO BE SAVED","What bridge equipment, pyro's etc. Specifically looking for VDR.","You are departing Darwin, heading to Cairns. How do you prepare the bridge for the departure. Went through the set-up of bridge equipment. He asked about VDR and"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",1],["Examiner not named",1],["Rudy",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"Q97","sec":5,"q":"NAVTEX — range, programming?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>NAVTEX is part of the <strong>GMDSS<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS Chapter IV<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the MS (Radio Installations) Regulations (verify the current version). It broadcasts <strong>Maritime Safety Information (MSI)<\/strong> as automated text, received on a dedicated receiver.<\/p><p><strong>Frequencies<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>518 kHz<\/strong>: international, in <strong>English<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>490 kHz<\/strong>: national language or local service.<\/li><li><strong>4209.5 kHz<\/strong>: HF NAVTEX in some regions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Range<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Typically about <strong>200–400 nm<\/strong> from the transmitter, depending on conditions.<\/li><li>Station details, times and coverage are in the <strong>ALRS Vol 5<\/strong> (GMDSS).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Message identity: B1 B2 B3 B4<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>B1<\/strong>: the <strong>station<\/strong> letter (A to X), identifying the transmitter.<\/li><li><strong>B2<\/strong>: the <strong>subject<\/strong>:<\/li><li><strong>A<\/strong> navigational warnings;<\/li><li><strong>B<\/strong> meteorological warnings;<\/li><li><strong>C<\/strong> ice reports;<\/li><li><strong>D<\/strong> search and rescue information, and piracy and armed attack warnings;<\/li><li><strong>E<\/strong> meteorological forecasts;<\/li><li><strong>F<\/strong> pilot and VTS service messages;<\/li><li><strong>G<\/strong> AIS messages (formerly Decca);<\/li><li><strong>H<\/strong> LORAN messages;<\/li><li><strong>J<\/strong> GNSS messages;<\/li><li><strong>K<\/strong> other electronic navaid messages;<\/li><li><strong>L<\/strong> further navigational warnings;<\/li><li><strong>Z<\/strong> no messages on hand.<\/li><li><strong>B3 B4<\/strong>: the <strong>serial number<\/strong>, 01 to 99. <strong>00<\/strong> is always printed, because it&#x27;s reserved for urgent messages.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Programming<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Select the stations<\/strong> covering the route, and <strong>change them<\/strong> as the ship moves from one area to the next. The passage plan lists the stations.<\/li><li><strong>A, B, D and L cannot be deselected<\/strong>, because they&#x27;re vital.<\/li><li>Select <strong>C<\/strong> and <strong>E<\/strong> and others as needed.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>time<\/strong>, the paper or memory, and that the receiver is working.<\/li><li>A message received correctly is not printed again; a corrupted one will be.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Use on the bridge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Read and initial<\/strong> messages each watch.<\/li><li><strong>Plot relevant warnings<\/strong> on ECDIS as manual updates or mariner&#x27;s notes.<\/li><li>Keep a log or file of the warnings in force.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Limitations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>NAVTEX covers <strong>coastal waters only<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Beyond that range, MSI comes by <strong>EGC<\/strong> through the <strong>recognised mobile satellite service<\/strong>: Inmarsat SafetyNET or Iridium SafetyCast, for NAVAREA and METAREA warnings.<\/li><li>Some areas have no NAVTEX coverage, so the satellite service is essential.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>518 kHz in English, 490 kHz local, 4209.5 HF, about 200–400 nm, the message identity, the subject letters, that A, B, D and L can&#x27;t be deselected, 00 always printed, and EGC beyond NAVTEX range.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"You're between two NAVTEX stations' coverage areas — which do you select?","pa":"<p>Select <strong>both<\/strong>: the station you&#x27;re in and the one you&#x27;re approaching. Messages A, B and D (navigational warnings, met warnings, SAR) can&#x27;t be deselected.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q98","sec":17,"q":"IALA: special marks, emergency wreck marker, new dangers, ODAS?","tier":"red","rep":41,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>UK waters are <strong>IALA Region A<\/strong>. Marks are maintained by the <strong>General Lighthouse Authorities<\/strong> (Trinity House, the Northern Lighthouse Board and the Commissioners of Irish Lights) and <strong>harbour authorities<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Special marks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Colour<\/strong>: <strong>yellow<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Topmark<\/strong>: a <strong>yellow X<\/strong> (St Andrew&#x27;s cross).<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong>: <strong>yellow<\/strong>, with <strong>any rhythm not used<\/strong> for <strong>white lights<\/strong> on other marks, so it can&#x27;t be confused (for example Fl Y, Fl(4)Y, Fl(5)Y 20s).<\/li><li><strong>Use<\/strong>: they mark <strong>spoil grounds<\/strong>, <strong>cables<\/strong> and pipelines, <strong>recreation zones<\/strong>, military exercise areas, traffic separation where conventional marks could confuse, <strong>ODAS<\/strong>, and fish farms.<\/li><li><strong>Shape<\/strong>: <strong>optional<\/strong>, but must <strong>not conflict<\/strong> with navigational marks. If a can or cone shape is used, it indicates the side on which to pass, like a lateral mark.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Emergency wreck marker<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Introduced after the <strong>Tricolor<\/strong> wreck in the <strong>Dover Strait<\/strong> in 2002, which was hit by two other ships despite being marked.<\/li><li>A <strong>pillar or spar<\/strong> buoy with <strong>blue and yellow vertical stripes<\/strong> (at least <strong>4<\/strong>, at most <strong>8<\/strong>).<\/li><li>A <strong>yellow upright cross<\/strong> topmark.<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong>: <strong>alternating blue and yellow<\/strong> flashes (1 second blue, 1 second yellow, with 0.5 second gaps).<\/li><li>It can carry a <strong>Racon &quot;D&quot;<\/strong> (Morse — · ·) and an <strong>AIS AtoN<\/strong>, real or virtual.<\/li><li>It <strong>stays<\/strong> until the wreck is <strong>properly marked<\/strong> with permanent marks and <strong>promulgated<\/strong> in navigational warnings and NtMs, usually at least 72 hours.<\/li><li>Several may be used for a large wreck.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>New dangers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A newly discovered hazard not yet shown in nautical documents: natural (sandbanks, rocks) or man-made (wrecks).<\/li><li><strong>Marked<\/strong> like any danger, with <strong>cardinal<\/strong> or <strong>lateral<\/strong> marks, but <strong>one mark may be duplicated<\/strong> (an identical mark placed nearby) until the danger is <strong>promulgated<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>Racon &quot;D&quot;<\/strong> may be fitted.<\/li><li>A <strong>navigational warning<\/strong> is issued.<\/li><li>The emergency wreck marking buoy can be used for a wreck.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>ODAS<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ocean Data Acquisition System<\/strong> buoys: data and weather buoys.<\/li><li><strong>Yellow special marks<\/strong>, lit <strong>Fl(5)Y 20s<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Give them a wide berth; they may have moorings or instruments.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Virtual AIS AtoNs<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>They appear on <strong>AIS<\/strong> and <strong>ECDIS<\/strong> as a symbol, but there&#x27;s <strong>nothing physically there<\/strong>. They&#x27;re used for new dangers, temporary marking, or where a buoy can&#x27;t be laid.<\/li><li>Verify the symbols in your notes.<\/li><li>Synthetic AIS AtoNs are transmitted from a shore station for a real buoy; real AIS AtoNs are fitted to the buoy itself.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Region A, special marks (yellow, yellow X, yellow light of a rhythm not used for white lights, uses, optional shape), the emergency wreck marker from the Tricolor (blue and yellow stripes, yellow upright cross, alternating blue and yellow light, Racon D, AIS, stays until promulgated), new dangers with duplicate marks and a Racon D, ODAS at Fl(5)Y 20s, and virtual AIS AtoNs.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"At night you see an alternating blue and yellow light ahead. What is it and how do you pass it?","pa":"<p>An <strong>emergency wreck marking buoy<\/strong>: alternating <strong>blue and yellow<\/strong> flashes, often with a racon showing Morse &quot;D&quot;. It marks a <strong>new, uncharted wreck<\/strong>. Pass it as its type and position tell you (it may be one of several), at a <strong>safe distance<\/strong>. Check navigational warnings and report it if they don&#x27;t mention it.<\/p>","asked":["Special mark can?","Iala a preferred to port","Buoyage: cardinals, special mark – wanted ID and action","What does ODAS do?","Special Mark (Spar shape with top mark fitted)"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",5],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",4],["Examiner not named",4],["Chris Williams",3],["Colum Leggett",3]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q99","sec":17,"q":"Seismic streamers marking?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>UK guidance on seismic survey vessels (MGN 168 in my notes; verify it&#x27;s still current). The seismic vessel shows <strong>COLREG Rule 27(b)<\/strong> signals as a vessel <strong>restricted in her ability to manoeuvre<\/strong>: <strong>ball-diamond-ball<\/strong> by day, and <strong>red-white-red<\/strong> all-round lights at night, with masthead lights, sidelights and sternlight when making way. Under <strong>Rule 18<\/strong>, I <strong>keep out of her way<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The hazard<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>streamers<\/strong> (hydrophone cables) are towed <strong>astern<\/strong>, are <strong>several kilometres long<\/strong> (often 5–10 km), and sit <strong>just below the surface<\/strong>, sometimes many in parallel spread over a wide area.<\/li><li>The vessel <strong>can&#x27;t alter course<\/strong> or <strong>stop<\/strong> quickly without damaging the streamers.<\/li><li>The end of the streamer is marked by a <strong>tail buoy<\/strong> with a <strong>radar reflector<\/strong> and a <strong>light<\/strong> (often <strong>yellow flashing<\/strong>), and sometimes <strong>AIS<\/strong>.<\/li><li>There may be <strong>guard<\/strong> or <strong>chase<\/strong> vessels, which warn other ships and keep them clear.<\/li><li>The operation is <strong>promulgated<\/strong> by <strong>navigational warnings<\/strong> (NAVTEX, NAVAREA) and <strong>NtMs<\/strong>, with the area and the vessel&#x27;s name.<\/li><li>Air guns used as the sound source near the stern.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>My actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Plan ahead: note the warnings and the survey area in the passage plan.<\/li><li><strong>Call<\/strong> the seismic vessel <strong>early<\/strong> on <strong>VHF<\/strong> and <strong>agree a passing plan<\/strong>: which side, the distance, the length of the streamers and where the tail buoy is.<\/li><li><strong>Pass well clear<\/strong>, <strong>never between<\/strong> the vessel and her <strong>tail buoy<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t cross close astern<\/strong>: the streamers are there.<\/li><li><strong>Follow instructions<\/strong> from the <strong>guard vessels<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Remember the streamer can <strong>drift off line<\/strong> in <strong>cross-currents<\/strong> (feathering), so it may not be directly astern.<\/li><li>Use radar to find the tail buoy, and keep a good lookout.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the <strong>logbook<\/strong>: the communications, the passing distance, and the times.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If I&#x27;m in a TSS or channel<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>She still has to comply with the rules; she&#x27;s RAM, and Rule 10(k) exempts RAM vessels in a TSS only for safety of navigation operations or cable work. Seismic work normally isn&#x27;t done in TSSs. If there&#x27;s a conflict, communicate early and follow VTS.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In restricted visibility<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Rule 19 applies, with no stand-on vessel, but I&#x27;d still keep well clear of her and her streamers.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>She&#x27;s RAM under Rule 27(b) with ball-diamond-ball and red-white-red, I keep clear under Rule 18; the hazard of streamers kilometres long just below the surface, marked by a tail buoy with a radar reflector and light, guard vessels, and navigational warnings; and my actions: call early and agree a plan, pass well clear and never between the vessel and her tail buoy, not close astern, follow the guard vessels, allow for feathering, and log it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How far astern would you expect the tail buoy, and how do you find out?","pa":"<p>Seismic streamers are often <strong>several kilometres<\/strong> long, up to about 8–10 km. The tail buoy carries a light and radar reflector. Find out from <strong>navigational warnings<\/strong>, the seismic vessel itself (VHF, AIS) and its guard vessels.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q100","sec":16,"q":"COLREGS scenarios.","tier":"red","rep":70,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Distress Signals and Prevention of Collisions) Regs 1996<\/strong> (SI 1996/75) and <strong>MSN 1781 (M+F) Amd 2<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>1. Radar-only target in restricted visibility (Rule 19(d))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If a <strong>close-quarters<\/strong> situation is developing and/or risk of collision exists, take <strong>avoiding action in ample time<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If it consists of an alteration of course, <strong>avoid<\/strong> as far as possible:<\/li><li><strong>(i)<\/strong> an alteration to <strong>port<\/strong> for a vessel <strong>forward of the beam<\/strong>, other than for a vessel being <strong>overtaken<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>(ii)<\/strong> an alteration <strong>towards<\/strong> a vessel <strong>abeam or abaft<\/strong> the beam.<\/li><li>And Rule 19(c): there&#x27;s no stand-on vessel in fog.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Fog signal heard forward of the beam (Rule 19(e))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Except where it&#x27;s been determined that risk of collision doesn&#x27;t exist, a vessel which hears <strong>apparently forward of her beam<\/strong> the fog signal of another vessel, or which can&#x27;t avoid a close-quarters situation with a vessel forward of her beam:<\/li><li><strong>reduce speed<\/strong> to the <strong>minimum<\/strong> at which she can be kept on course;<\/li><li><strong>take all way off<\/strong> if necessary;<\/li><li>and in any event <strong>navigate with extreme caution<\/strong> until danger of collision is over.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. A CBD vessel crossing from port<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Rule 15<\/strong> would make me the stand-on vessel, <strong>but<\/strong>:<\/li><li><strong>Rule 18(d)(i)<\/strong>: any vessel other than NUC or RAM shall, if circumstances admit, <strong>avoid impeding<\/strong> the safe passage of a vessel <strong>constrained by her draught<\/strong>, exhibiting the Rule 28 signals.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 8(f)(i)<\/strong>: a vessel required not to impede shall take <strong>early action<\/strong> to allow sufficient sea room.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 8(f)(ii)<\/strong>: the duty <strong>remains<\/strong> once <strong>risk of collision<\/strong> exists.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 8(f)(iii)<\/strong>: the CBD vessel must still <strong>obey<\/strong> the steering and sailing rules once risk exists.<\/li><li>So: <strong>act early<\/strong>: slow down, or <strong>alter to starboard<\/strong> to pass <strong>astern<\/strong> of her.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. A fishing vessel crossing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If she&#x27;s <strong>engaged in fishing<\/strong>, I keep clear (<strong>Rule 18(a)(iii)<\/strong>).<\/li><li>In a <strong>narrow channel<\/strong> or <strong>TSS<\/strong>, she <strong>must not impede<\/strong> vessels navigating within the channel or following a lane (<strong>Rules 9(c)<\/strong> and <strong>10(i)<\/strong>).<\/li><li>If she&#x27;s <strong>not fishing<\/strong> (no fishing signals, just a fishing boat on passage), <strong>Rules 15 and 17<\/strong> apply as for any power-driven vessel.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Overtaking in a narrow channel (Rule 9(e))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the overtaken vessel has to take action to permit safe passing, the overtaking vessel signals:<\/li><li><strong>two prolonged + one short<\/strong>: &quot;I intend to overtake you on your <strong>starboard<\/strong> side&quot;;<\/li><li><strong>two prolonged + two short<\/strong>: &quot;on your <strong>port<\/strong> side&quot;.<\/li><li>The overtaken vessel <strong>agrees<\/strong> with <strong>prolonged, short, prolonged, short<\/strong> (Morse C), and takes steps to permit safe passing, or sounds <strong>five short<\/strong> if in <strong>doubt<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 34(d)<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>Rule 13<\/strong> still applies: the overtaking vessel keeps clear throughout.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>General approach<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Determine whether risk of collision exists (Rule 7), then the situation, then the rule, then the action: early, substantial, readily apparent (Rule 8), with the correct sound signals.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Each scenario with the rule and the action: Rule 19(d) avoidances, 19(e) reduce to minimum steerage way, Rule 18(d) and 8(f) for the CBD with early action to pass astern, fishing (18(a)(iii) with 9(c) and 10(i)), and Rule 9(e) overtaking signals with 34(c) and (d) and Rule 13 still applying.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"As stand-on vessel, when may you act and when must you act (Rule 17)?","pa":"<p><strong>May<\/strong> act: as soon as it&#x27;s apparent the give-way vessel isn&#x27;t taking appropriate action (17(a)(ii)). <strong>Must<\/strong> act: when she&#x27;s so close that collision can&#x27;t be avoided by the give-way vessel alone (17(b)). In a crossing, don&#x27;t alter to port for a vessel on your own port side (17(c)).<\/p>","asked":["ROR Situations (Actions as master)","Vessel engaged in fishing ROR card?","ROR – Day Situations","Colregs Day Scenarios","ROR: Action & taken under which rule?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",7],["Zia Ul Haq",7],["Khairul Anam",6],["Muhammad Iqbal",6],["Nayyer Naqvi",5]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q101","sec":16,"q":"Restricted visibility preparations?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>UK guidance is <strong>MGN 369 (M+F) Amd 1<\/strong>. The rules are <strong>COLREG Rules 5, 6, 7, 19 and 35<\/strong>, applied by <strong>MSN 1781<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>When visibility starts to drop, the OOW<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Calls the Master<\/strong>, as the standing orders require, and <strong>informs the engine room<\/strong>, with the <strong>engines on standby<\/strong> for immediate manoeuvre.<\/li><li>Proceeds at a <strong>safe speed<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 6<\/strong>), considering the <strong>stopping distance<\/strong>, the <strong>traffic<\/strong>, the <strong>radar performance<\/strong> and its limitations, the visibility, the sea state and the proximity of hazards. In restricted visibility, safe speed is the key decision.<\/li><li>Posts <strong>extra lookout(s)<\/strong>, including one <strong>forward<\/strong> or on the <strong>bridge wing<\/strong> to <strong>listen<\/strong>, and keeps a <strong>wheelhouse door<\/strong> open so fog signals can be heard (<strong>Rule 5<\/strong>).<\/li><li>Switches to <strong>hand steering<\/strong> with a helmsman.<\/li><li>Starts <strong>sound signals<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 35<\/strong>): one prolonged blast at intervals of not more than 2 minutes when making way; two prolonged when under way but stopped.<\/li><li>Keeps the <strong>navigation lights<\/strong> on (Rule 20(c)).<\/li><li>Uses <strong>both radars<\/strong> on suitable ranges (one long range for early warning, one short range), with <strong>systematic ARPA plotting<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 7<\/strong>), and <strong>avoids assumptions<\/strong> from <strong>scanty information<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Applies <strong>Rule 19<\/strong>, <strong>not<\/strong> the steering rules for vessels in sight of one another: there&#x27;s <strong>no stand-on vessel<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Checks the <strong>position<\/strong> more often, and monitors the <strong>echo sounder<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Closes watertight doors<\/strong> where fitted.<\/li><li><strong>Stops work on deck<\/strong>, and has the <strong>crew stand by<\/strong>, with the anchors ready if in coastal waters.<\/li><li>Reviews the <strong>passage plan<\/strong>: anchoring options, traffic density, whether to wait for better visibility, and <strong>informs VTS<\/strong> where required.<\/li><li><strong>Records<\/strong> the <strong>time<\/strong> visibility reduced, and the <strong>actions taken<\/strong>, in the <strong>logbook<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Considers AIS information but doesn&#x27;t rely on it: small craft and some ships don&#x27;t transmit.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>On a container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>With a <strong>large blind sector<\/strong> forward from the deck stacks, the <strong>extra lookout<\/strong> forward and the <strong>radar plotting<\/strong> matter even more. A small boat close ahead may not be visible at all from the bridge.<\/li><li>Radar shadow sectors from the stacks and cranes must be known.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The Master&#x27;s decisions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Speed, whether to anchor or wait, the bridge team level, and whether to continue a pilotage or berthing.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MGN 369 and the rules (5, 6, 7, 19, 35); call the Master and the engine room; safe speed; extra lookouts listening; hand steering; fog signals; both radars with systematic plotting; Rule 19 with no stand-on vessel; more frequent fixes and the echo sounder; WT doors; crew standing by; reviewing the plan and VTS; logbook entries; and the container ship blind sector.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you decide what speed is \"safe\" in fog?","pa":"<p>Use <strong>Rule 6<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li>visibility and traffic density;<\/li><li>your stopping distance and turning ability;<\/li><li>wind, sea and current, and draught against depth;<\/li><li>radar limitations and range scale, sea clutter, and small craft that may not be detected.<\/li><\/ul><p>Safe speed lets you take proper action and <strong>stop within a suitable distance<\/strong> for the visibility.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q102","sec":5,"q":"Magnetic compass: parts, Flinders bar, Kelvin spheres, coefficients A–E, heeling error, when to adjust, adjustment order?","tier":"red","rep":61,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS V/19<\/strong> requires a properly adjusted standard magnetic compass, with a <strong>deviation table or curve<\/strong>, on all ships. The UK applies it through the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020.<\/p><p><strong>Parts<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>liquid-filled bowl<\/strong> with the <strong>card<\/strong> and magnets, an expansion chamber.<\/li><li><strong>Gimbals<\/strong>, so the bowl stays level.<\/li><li>The <strong>binnacle<\/strong> with the <strong>correctors<\/strong>: Kelvin spheres, Flinders bar, fore-and-aft and athwartships magnets, and heeling magnets in the bucket.<\/li><li>An <strong>azimuth mirror<\/strong> or circle for bearings.<\/li><li>A <strong>projector<\/strong> or <strong>transmitting head<\/strong>, so the helmsman can see the heading.<\/li><li>Lighting with a dimmer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Coefficients of deviation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>A<\/strong>: <strong>constant<\/strong> on all headings, from <strong>lubber-line<\/strong> error or <strong>unsymmetrical<\/strong> horizontal soft iron.<\/li><li><strong>B<\/strong>: greatest on <strong>east and west<\/strong> headings, from <strong>permanent fore-and-aft<\/strong> magnetism and <strong>vertical soft iron<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>C<\/strong>: greatest on <strong>north and south<\/strong> headings, from <strong>permanent athwartships<\/strong> magnetism.<\/li><li><strong>D<\/strong>: <strong>quadrantal<\/strong>, greatest on the <strong>intercardinal<\/strong> headings, from <strong>symmetrical horizontal soft iron<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>E<\/strong>: quadrantal, greatest on the <strong>cardinal<\/strong> headings, usually small, from unsymmetrical horizontal soft iron.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Like cures like<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Soft iron<\/strong> correctors correct <strong>induced<\/strong> magnetism.<\/li><li><strong>Permanent magnets<\/strong> correct <strong>permanent<\/strong> magnetism.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The correctors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Kelvin spheres<\/strong> (soft iron) correct <strong>D<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Flinders bar<\/strong> (soft iron) corrects the <strong>vertical soft-iron<\/strong> part of <strong>B<\/strong>, which <strong>changes with magnetic latitude<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fore-and-aft magnets<\/strong> correct the permanent part of <strong>B<\/strong>; <strong>athwartships magnets<\/strong> correct <strong>C<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Heeling magnets<\/strong> in the bucket under the compass correct <strong>heeling error<\/strong>: the deviation caused when the ship is <strong>heeled<\/strong>, which is worst on <strong>north and south<\/strong> headings.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Adjustment order<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Spheres (D)<\/strong> first, because they&#x27;re also magnetised by the magnets.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>Flinders bar<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>heeling magnets<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>larger of B or C<\/strong>, then the other.<\/li><li>Then <strong>swing the ship<\/strong> for a new <strong>deviation card<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>When to adjust<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>new ship<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Structural alteration<\/strong>, or new <strong>electrical equipment<\/strong> or steel near the compass.<\/li><li>After a <strong>long lay-up<\/strong>, or a long time on <strong>one heading<\/strong>.<\/li><li>After a <strong>lightning strike<\/strong>, a fire, or <strong>large or erratic deviations<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A big change in <strong>magnetic latitude<\/strong>, which changes the Flinders bar effect.<\/li><li>When the deviations exceed the limits in the company procedure, or if the card is more than two years old (verify the UK requirement).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who adjusts<\/strong><\/p><p>A qualified <strong>compass adjuster<\/strong>. The officers check deviation each watch by azimuth, amplitude or transit and record it.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The parts, coefficients A to E with their causes, like cures like, which corrector does what, heeling error on N/S headings, the order of adjustment, and when to adjust.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is a Flinders bar error not fixed once and for all at the builder's yard?","pa":"<p>Because the induced magnetism in vertical soft iron <strong>changes with magnetic latitude<\/strong>, and the ship&#x27;s own magnetism changes with age, repairs and cargo. The bar is fitted at the builder&#x27;s, but it&#x27;s adjusted later from deviations observed at different magnetic latitudes.<\/p>","asked":["What is the purpose of the Kelvin sphere?","When do u take a compass error?","What are the correctors on magnetic compass?","Magnetic Compass showing more deviation on departure why?","What is the flinders bar?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",20],["Khairul Anam",9],["Chris Bates",8],["Fazal Wadood",7],["Inamul Bhuiyan",6]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q103","sec":5,"q":"Synoptic chart: identify features, explain weather at a position.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>On the synoptic chart I&#x27;d point out<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Highs and lows<\/strong>, with the <strong>isobar spacing<\/strong> showing the <strong>pressure gradient<\/strong>: close isobars mean strong winds.<\/li><li><strong>Fronts<\/strong>: <strong>warm<\/strong> (red, semicircles), <strong>cold<\/strong> (blue, triangles), <strong>occluded<\/strong> (purple, both), and stationary.<\/li><li><strong>Troughs<\/strong>, <strong>ridges<\/strong> and <strong>cols<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The direction and speed of movement of the systems, if shown.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Wind<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In the <strong>Northern Hemisphere<\/strong>, wind blows <strong>anticlockwise<\/strong> round a low and clockwise round a high.<\/li><li>Over the sea it crosses the isobars slightly <strong>inwards<\/strong> towards low pressure, by about <strong>10–15°<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Buys Ballot&#x27;s law<\/strong>: with your <strong>back to the wind<\/strong>, the <strong>low is on your left<\/strong> in the Northern Hemisphere.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>geostrophic scale<\/strong> on the chart for the wind speed, and reduce it to about two-thirds for the surface wind over the sea.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Weather at a position ahead of a typical mid-latitude depression moving east<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. Ahead of the warm front<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Pressure<\/strong> falling steadily.<\/li><li><strong>Wind<\/strong> backing and freshening.<\/li><li><strong>Cloud<\/strong> thickens and lowers: <strong>cirrus<\/strong>, then <strong>cirrostratus<\/strong> (halo round the sun or moon), <strong>altostratus<\/strong>, then <strong>nimbostratus<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Continuous rain<\/strong>, getting heavier, and <strong>visibility<\/strong> getting worse.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. At the warm front<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Wind veers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The fall in pressure eases or stops.<\/li><li>Rain eases to drizzle.<\/li><li>Temperature rises.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Warm sector<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Mild and humid<\/strong>, <strong>low stratus<\/strong>, <strong>drizzle<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Poor visibility<\/strong> or <strong>fog<\/strong>, especially over cold water.<\/li><li>Pressure steady or falling slowly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. At the cold front<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Wind veers sharply<\/strong> and <strong>gusts<\/strong>, often with a squall.<\/li><li><strong>Pressure rises<\/strong> sharply.<\/li><li><strong>Temperature drops<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Heavy showers<\/strong> and <strong>squalls<\/strong> under <strong>cumulonimbus<\/strong>, possibly thunder and hail.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Behind the cold front<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Clearing<\/strong> skies, <strong>showers<\/strong>, <strong>good visibility<\/strong>, <strong>colder<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Wind may back slightly then veer again in the showers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>For the ship<\/strong><\/p><p>I&#x27;d then say what it means: heavy weather preparation, the effect on the ETA, the risk of fog in the warm sector, and the squalls at the cold front.<\/p><p><strong>My notes were wrong<\/strong><\/p><p>My notes [ON] said warm fronts are &quot;associated with high pressure&quot; and move &quot;north to south&quot;. That&#x27;s wrong: <strong>fronts belong to the depression<\/strong>, and in mid-latitudes they move roughly <strong>west to east<\/strong> with it.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Identify the features, Buys Ballot&#x27;s law, isobar spacing for wind, and a clear sequence of weather through a depression, from the warm front to behind the cold front, then what it means for the ship.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's an occlusion and what weather does it bring?","pa":"<p>An <strong>occlusion<\/strong> is where a cold front catches up the warm front and lifts the warm air off the surface. It brings a mixture of front weather: layered cloud and prolonged rain, then showers, and the wind veers. Clearing is often slow.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q104","sec":5,"q":"Early signs of a TRS; dangerous quadrant; avoidance.","tier":"red","rep":63,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Mariner&#x27;s Handbook (NP100)<\/strong> and the Sailing Directions. Danger messages under <strong>SOLAS V/31<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Early signs<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>long, low swell<\/strong> from the direction of the storm, felt before any other sign, sometimes 1,000 nm away.<\/li><li><strong>Pressure<\/strong>, corrected for <strong>diurnal variation<\/strong>: about <strong>3 hPa below<\/strong> the seasonal mean is <strong>suspicious<\/strong>; about <strong>5 hPa below<\/strong> means <strong>assume a TRS<\/strong> is near. Also, the <strong>loss of the normal diurnal pattern<\/strong> of pressure is a warning.<\/li><li>A <strong>change in the wind<\/strong> direction or strength from the normal trade wind or monsoon.<\/li><li><strong>Cloud<\/strong>: cirrus thickening to cirrostratus, then a dark <strong>&quot;bar&quot;<\/strong> of cloud on the horizon marking the storm.<\/li><li>Unusually <strong>clear visibility<\/strong> or a brilliant <strong>sunset<\/strong>, then <strong>heavy rain squalls<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Forecasts and warnings<\/strong>: NAVTEX, EGC, and the meteorological services. These are usually the first warning today.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The dangerous semicircle<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In the <strong>Northern Hemisphere<\/strong> it&#x27;s the <strong>right-hand side<\/strong> of the track, looking along the direction of travel.<\/li><li>In the <strong>Southern Hemisphere<\/strong> it&#x27;s the <strong>left<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>front quadrant<\/strong> of the dangerous semicircle is the <strong>worst<\/strong>:<\/li><li>the winds are <strong>strongest<\/strong>, because the storm&#x27;s forward speed adds to the rotation;<\/li><li>the wind tends to <strong>blow the ship into the storm&#x27;s path<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the storm may <strong>recurve<\/strong> towards the ship.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Finding where I am (NH)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Face the wind: the centre is about <strong>10–12 points<\/strong> (around 110–135°) to the right of the wind, by Buys Ballot&#x27;s law adjusted for the storm.<\/li><li><strong>Wind veering<\/strong> (shifting clockwise): I&#x27;m in the <strong>dangerous semicircle<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Wind backing<\/strong>: I&#x27;m in the <strong>navigable semicircle<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Steady wind direction with falling pressure<\/strong>: I&#x27;m <strong>in the path<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Avoidance (NH)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Dangerous semicircle<\/strong>: put the wind <strong>fine on the starboard bow<\/strong> (10–45°), and make <strong>best speed away<\/strong>. If you can&#x27;t make headway, heave to with the wind on the starboard bow.<\/li><li><strong>Navigable semicircle<\/strong>: put the wind on the <strong>starboard quarter<\/strong>, and make best speed away.<\/li><li><strong>In the path<\/strong>: put the wind on the <strong>starboard quarter<\/strong>, and head for the <strong>navigable<\/strong> semicircle.<\/li><li>In the <strong>Southern Hemisphere<\/strong>, use the mirror image: the <strong>port<\/strong> bow and quarter.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Best of all<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Avoid it early<\/strong>, using forecasts, <strong>weather routeing<\/strong>, and keeping at least <strong>250 nm<\/strong> away from the centre where possible. Plan ahead: delay sailing, change route, or seek shelter.<\/p><p><strong>Report it<\/strong><\/p><p>A danger message under SOLAS V/31 and V/32, and observations every 3 hours or more often.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The signs (swell, pressure 3 and 5 hPa, wind, cloud), the dangerous semicircle right in the NH with the front quadrant worst and why, how to find where you are from the wind shift, the avoidance rules, and that early avoidance is best.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you find the bearing of the storm centre from the wind?","pa":"<p><strong>Buys Ballot&#x27;s law.<\/strong> In the northern hemisphere, face the wind: the centre is about <strong>8–12 points (90–135°) on your right<\/strong>. In the southern hemisphere it&#x27;s on your left. Watch how the bearing changes to find which semicircle you&#x27;re in.<\/p>","asked":["How to take off the vessel from Dangerous quadrant in Northern Hemisphere?","What are the signs of a (TRS)?","You are in NH draw TRS with your vessel in Dangerous Quadrant, your avoiding action?","If there's no warning for TRS in that area & you are about to face it. Then what are the Master's responsibility?","TRS Northern hemisphere, acYons?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",18],["Muhammad Iqbal",13],["Zia Ul Haq",13],["Shahid Akhter",9],["Nayyer Naqvi",6]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q105","sec":5,"q":"Why is the South Atlantic (largely) free of TRS?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What a TRS needs<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Warm sea surface temperature<\/strong>, about <strong>26–27°C<\/strong> or more, over a large area and to a good depth, to provide heat and moisture.<\/li><li><strong>Low vertical wind shear<\/strong>, so the convection can organise vertically into a column.<\/li><li>Enough <strong>Coriolis force<\/strong>, which means forming at least about <strong>5° from the equator<\/strong>, to start the rotation.<\/li><li>An <strong>existing disturbance<\/strong>, such as an easterly wave or a trough, to start it off.<\/li><li>High <strong>humidity<\/strong> in the middle troposphere.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why the South Atlantic lacks most of these<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Cool sea temperatures.<\/strong> The <strong>Benguela<\/strong> current off south-west Africa and the generally cooler tropical South Atlantic keep SSTs <strong>below the threshold<\/strong> over most of the area.<\/li><li><strong>Strong vertical wind shear<\/strong> in the upper troposphere through most of the year. It <strong>tears developing systems apart<\/strong> before they can organise.<\/li><li>The <strong>ITCZ rarely moves far south<\/strong> of the equator in the Atlantic, only a degree or two in the southern summer. So disturbances don&#x27;t form <strong>far enough from the equator<\/strong> for Coriolis to start rotation.<\/li><li><strong>Few pre-existing disturbances<\/strong> to act as seedlings. In the North Atlantic, African easterly waves supply them; the South Atlantic has nothing similar.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>&quot;Largely&quot; matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A storm can form <strong>exceptionally<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cyclone Catarina<\/strong> struck southern Brazil in <strong>March 2004<\/strong>: the first recorded hurricane-strength storm in the South Atlantic.<\/li><li>A few tropical storms have formed since.<\/li><li>So <strong>don&#x27;t say &quot;never&quot;<\/strong>. Say &quot;<strong>very rarely, because conditions are normally unfavourable<\/strong>&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Compare the other areas<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>North Atlantic<\/strong> has hurricanes from June to November.<\/li><li>The <strong>South-West Indian Ocean<\/strong> and <strong>South Pacific<\/strong> have cyclones from November to April.<\/li><li>The <strong>South-East Pacific<\/strong> off South America is also largely free, for similar reasons: the cold <strong>Humboldt<\/strong> current.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><p>To test whether I understand <strong>why<\/strong> TRSs form, not just where. If I understand the conditions, I can judge risk in unusual cases.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The conditions for TRS formation, then why the South Atlantic lacks them: cool SST from the Benguela current, strong wind shear, the ITCZ staying near the equator, and few seed disturbances; plus Catarina in 2004 as the exception.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why don't TRS form right on the equator?","pa":"<p>The <strong>Coriolis force is zero at the equator<\/strong>, so the air can&#x27;t start rotating. Tropical revolving storms need at least about 5° of latitude.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q106","sec":5,"q":"At anchor, storm warning received — night orders?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First, decide whether to stay at anchor at all<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A large container ship has <strong>huge windage<\/strong> from the deck stacks, and her anchors are designed for <strong>holding in moderate conditions<\/strong>, not storms. Anchor equipment is typically designed for a current of about 2.5 m/s, wind of about 25 m/s and no waves (verify the class figure).<\/li><li>If the forecast is beyond that, or the <strong>port orders it<\/strong>, I&#x27;d <strong>heave up early<\/strong> and <strong>ride it out at sea<\/strong> with <strong>sea room<\/strong>, away from other ships and lee shores.<\/li><li>Leaving late, with the wind already up, is dangerous: the anchor may not come up, and the ship can be driven ashore while heaving.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If we stay, my night orders would say<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Call me at once<\/strong> if wind, sea or pressure gets <strong>worse than forecast<\/strong>, or if the <strong>anchor drags<\/strong>, or any ship nearby drags.<\/li><li><strong>Engines on immediate notice<\/strong>, with the Chief Engineer informed. Engineers on standby.<\/li><li><strong>Second anchor ready<\/strong> to let go. Consider <strong>veering more cable<\/strong> early, to five or more shackles depending on depth, to increase holding.<\/li><li><strong>Check the position frequently<\/strong>: radar ranges and bearings, transits, and the <strong>ECDIS/GNSS anchor-watch circle<\/strong> with an alarm. Watch for drag, including changes in the <strong>heading and yaw<\/strong> pattern.<\/li><li><strong>Monitor<\/strong> barometer trends, wind, swell and <strong>weather broadcasts<\/strong>. <strong>Record weather hourly<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Keep a close watch<\/strong> on other ships <strong>dragging towards us<\/strong>, and listen to <strong>VTS and port VHF<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>The bow<\/strong>: brake hard on, compressor or chain stopper and lashings on. Crew go forward <strong>only after a risk assessment<\/strong>, in pairs, and in contact with the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Container lashings<\/strong> checked before the weather arrives, and <strong>openings closed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Restrict deck work<\/strong> and rig <strong>lifelines<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Correct <strong>lights and signals<\/strong>, and keep the <strong>anchor watch log<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Pilot ladder<\/strong> and gangway secured.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If dragging<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform me<\/strong> at once.<\/li><li><strong>Start the engines<\/strong> and use them to ease the strain.<\/li><li>Give a <strong>SÉCURITÉ<\/strong> warning to other ships and VTS.<\/li><li>Let go the second anchor if it helps, or <strong>heave up and proceed<\/strong> to sea.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Before the weather arrives<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A heavy-weather checklist: stores and equipment secured, hatch covers checked, ballast for a good draught and trim, accommodation doors closed.<\/li><li>Brief the crew.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>First, whether to stay at anchor at all, because a container ship&#x27;s windage often makes going to sea safer; then the night orders: call me, engines ready, second anchor, more cable, position monitoring, weather, other ships dragging, safe access forward, and what to do if dragging.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you know the anchor is dragging rather than the ship yawing?","pa":"<p>Signs of dragging:<\/p><ul><li>fixed bearings and radar ranges drift <strong>steadily<\/strong> outside the swinging circle;<\/li><li>the GPS anchor watch alarms;<\/li><li>the cable vibrates and jerks;<\/li><li>she lies beam-on instead of head to wind.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Yawing<\/strong> swings back and forth around a mean. Dragging is a <strong>steady<\/strong> change.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q107","sec":5,"q":"Ice navigation and ice accretion — considerations?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>References:<\/strong> the <strong>Mariner&#x27;s Handbook (NP100)<\/strong>, the Sailing Directions, <strong>ice reports<\/strong> and ice charts, and in polar waters the <strong>Polar Code<\/strong> (SOLAS XIV and MARPOL), applied in the UK by SI 2021/1401 and <strong>MGN 637<\/strong>. The Polar Code means a <strong>Polar Ship Certificate<\/strong>, a <strong>Polar Water Operational Manual<\/strong>, and officers with <strong>polar training<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Ice navigation<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Avoid ice<\/strong> if possible. Route round it using <strong>ice charts<\/strong> and <strong>ice services<\/strong>, such as the International Ice Patrol off Newfoundland.<\/li><li>If you must enter: go in <strong>at right angles<\/strong>, <strong>slowly<\/strong>, at the <strong>lighter ice<\/strong>, avoiding <strong>ridges<\/strong> and pressure.<\/li><li>Check the ship&#x27;s <strong>ice class<\/strong>. A container ship usually has <strong>little or no ice strengthening<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Radar<\/strong> on suitable ranges and gain. <strong>Growlers<\/strong> and bergy bits are <strong>poor radar targets<\/strong>, especially in a swell.<\/li><li><strong>Extra lookouts<\/strong>, searchlights at night, <strong>engines on standby<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Reduce speed in poor visibility near ice.<\/li><li>Follow <strong>icebreaker<\/strong> instructions in convoy, keeping the ordered distance.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>ballast and fresh-water tanks freezing<\/strong>, <strong>sea suctions<\/strong> blocking with slush ice, and deck machinery freezing.<\/li><li>Report <strong>dangerous ice<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS V/31<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Ice accretion<\/strong><\/p><p>It comes from <strong>freezing spray<\/strong> in <strong>sub-zero air<\/strong> with strong winds, and from freezing rain or fog.<\/p><p><strong>Effects<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It <strong>raises G<\/strong> and <strong>reduces GM<\/strong>, because the ice builds up high on the ship.<\/li><li>It adds <strong>weight<\/strong> and <strong>windage<\/strong>, reduces <strong>freeboard<\/strong>, and can cause a <strong>list<\/strong> if it builds up unevenly.<\/li><li>On a container ship, ice on <strong>high deck stacks<\/strong> is especially bad for stability.<\/li><li>It blocks <strong>vents<\/strong> and <strong>freeing ports<\/strong>, freezes <strong>LSA<\/strong>, aerials and fire equipment, and makes the decks dangerous.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Action<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong> and <strong>alter course<\/strong> to cut down spray: run with the sea, or move into <strong>warmer water<\/strong> or shelter.<\/li><li><strong>Remove ice early<\/strong>, from the <strong>top down<\/strong>, with mallets, scrapers and steam. Keep the crew safe while doing it: lifelines, PPE, in pairs, in contact with the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Recalculate stability<\/strong>. The IS Code allows for ice accretion in its guidance (about 30 kg/m² on exposed decks and 7.5 kg/m² on the projected lateral area, verify the figures before quoting).<\/li><li>Keep <strong>LSA<\/strong> and <strong>fire-fighting equipment<\/strong> free of ice.<\/li><li><strong>Report severe accretion<\/strong> under V/31.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Cold weather precautions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Drain deck lines, fire mains where allowed, and keep heating on.<\/li><li>Protect crew from cold exposure.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Avoid ice, enter at right angles slowly, ice class, growlers on radar, lookouts, the Polar Code with its certificate and manual; ice accretion raises G and reduces GM; remove it from the top down; recalculate stability; and report under V/31.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"At what point would you abandon a passage because of ice accretion?","pa":"<p>Abandon or divert when ice is building <strong>faster than the crew can safely remove it<\/strong>, or when the stability calculation shows she&#x27;s nearing the minimum criteria. The IS Code icing allowance is about <strong>30 kg/m²<\/strong> on exposed decks. Before then, alter course and speed to reduce spray, and head for warmer water or shelter.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★6.1","sec":5,"q":"GNSS is jammed in the approaches. How do you fix position and what's affected on the bridge?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Recognise it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>position jumps<\/strong> or <strong>freezes<\/strong>, or the ship appears to be on land.<\/li><li>GNSS <strong>&quot;no fix&quot;<\/strong>, <strong>HDOP<\/strong> or integrity alarms.<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS and radar positions disagree<\/strong>: the radar overlay doesn&#x27;t line up with the coastline.<\/li><li><strong>AIS targets<\/strong> in odd places, or the ship&#x27;s own AIS position wrong.<\/li><li>Both receivers affected at the same time, while other equipment is fine.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s <strong>time<\/strong> may be wrong.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What&#x27;s affected on the bridge<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>ECDIS<\/strong>: position, route monitoring and its <strong>anti-grounding alarms<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>AIS<\/strong>: own ship and others.<\/li><li><strong>ARPA<\/strong> ground stabilisation and SOG/COG.<\/li><li><strong>GMDSS/DSC<\/strong> auto-position, so a <strong>distress alert would carry a wrong position<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>VDR<\/strong> time and position.<\/li><li><strong>Gyro<\/strong> speed correction if it takes GNSS speed.<\/li><li><strong>BNWAS<\/strong> or integrated bridge integrations, and the ship&#x27;s <strong>clocks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>autopilot<\/strong> track mode.<\/li><li>The <strong>SSAS<\/strong> and <strong>LRIT<\/strong> positions.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong> and switch to <strong>traditional navigation<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>radar ranges and bearings<\/strong> from conspicuous points;<\/li><li><strong>visual bearings<\/strong> and <strong>transits<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>parallel indexing<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>echo sounder<\/strong> against charted depths;<\/li><li><strong>DR and EP<\/strong> from gyro and log.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Put <strong>ARPA<\/strong> on <strong>sea stabilisation<\/strong> using the <strong>log<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Deselect GNSS<\/strong> as the ECDIS position source. Use <strong>manual<\/strong> or <strong>radar-derived<\/strong> position input if the system allows it. The ECDIS will go into dead reckoning.<\/li><li><strong>Enter the position manually<\/strong> in the <strong>DSC<\/strong> units.<\/li><li><strong>Increase<\/strong> the fixing frequency and the <strong>bridge manning<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In the approaches, consider extra <strong>pilot<\/strong> or <strong>tug<\/strong> support, or <strong>holding off<\/strong> until it&#x27;s resolved or daylight.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> the interference to the <strong>coastal State<\/strong> (VTS or Coastguard) and the <strong>company<\/strong>, and log it. In many areas this is a known risk, and the authorities collect reports.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Prevention and preparation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Know which areas have reported jamming and spoofing: the Baltic, Eastern Mediterranean, Black Sea and Gulf.<\/li><li>Keep traditional skills practised, and include GNSS failure in drills.<\/li><li>A passage plan with radar-conspicuous features and parallel indexes for the approaches.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Spoofing<\/strong><\/p><p>Spoofing is worse than jamming: the GNSS shows a confident but <strong>wrong<\/strong> position. Cross-checking by radar and visual fixes is the only defence.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>How to recognise it, everything it affects including the DSC distress position, switching to radar and visual navigation, ARPA on sea stabilisation, manual DSC position, more manning, and reporting the interference.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How would you notice spoofing, as opposed to jamming?","pa":"<p>Signs of <strong>spoofing<\/strong>: the GNSS position looks plausible but <strong>jumps or drifts<\/strong>. It disagrees with radar and visual fixes, and the radar overlay doesn&#x27;t match the ECDIS. Other ships&#x27; AIS positions look odd or clustered, and the time or speed is wrong against the log. <strong>Jamming<\/strong> shows up as loss of fix and &quot;no position&quot; alarms.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★6.2","sec":5,"q":"What changed in GMDSS on 1 Jan 2024?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"GMDSS modernised 2024","a":"<p><strong>What changed<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>modernised SOLAS Chapter IV<\/strong> entered into force on <strong>1 January 2024<\/strong>, with related changes to other chapters and to the performance standards. In the UK it&#x27;s applied through the Radio Installations Regulations (verify how they were updated).<\/p><p><strong>The main points<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Satellite services are no longer tied to Inmarsat.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>The rules now refer to a <strong>&quot;recognized mobile satellite service&quot; (RMSS)<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Iridium<\/strong> is recognised alongside <strong>Inmarsat<\/strong>, so Inmarsat-C is no longer the only way to meet the A3 requirements.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Sea area A3<\/strong> is now defined by the coverage of the <strong>RMSS the ship carries<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Iridium covers the poles<\/strong>, so for an Iridium-equipped ship, sea area <strong>A4 effectively disappears<\/strong>.<\/li><li>For an Inmarsat ship, A3 still ends at about 70–76° latitude.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Equipment carriage was simplified.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Out-of-date requirements were removed, such as <strong>NBDP<\/strong> (radio telex) on MF/HF as a mandatory function (verify).<\/li><li>Terminology and equipment descriptions updated.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>MSI<\/strong> can be received through the satellite service&#x27;s <strong>EGC-equivalent<\/strong>, such as Iridium SafetyCast, as well as <strong>NAVTEX<\/strong> and Inmarsat <strong>SafetyNET<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Terminology<\/strong> was updated throughout, and the <strong>radio survey<\/strong> and the <strong>Safety Radio Certificate&#x27;s Record of Equipment (Form R)<\/strong> reflect the new equipment lists.<\/li><li>Related changes in <strong>SOLAS III and V<\/strong>, for example to the way survival craft radio equipment is described.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What hasn&#x27;t changed<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>sea areas<\/strong> A1 (VHF DSC coverage), A2 (MF DSC coverage), A3 and A4 still exist.<\/li><li>The <strong>functional requirements<\/strong> still apply: distress alerting ship-to-shore by two independent means, ship-to-ship alerting, SAR coordination, on-scene communications, locating (SART or AIS-SART), MSI, general and bridge-to-bridge communications.<\/li><li><strong>EPIRBs<\/strong> still use <strong>COSPAS-SARSAT<\/strong> at 406 MHz.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In practice<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the ship&#x27;s <strong>SRC Record of Equipment<\/strong> shows the right <strong>RMSS<\/strong> and equipment.<\/li><li>The <strong>GMDSS operators<\/strong> must be familiar with the actual terminals fitted, which may now be Iridium.<\/li><li>The <strong>distress procedures<\/strong> and <strong>flowcharts<\/strong> by the console must <strong>match the equipment<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>GMDSS logbook<\/strong> and tests updated for the new equipment.<\/li><li>The <strong>passage plan<\/strong> uses the right MSI service for the area.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>1 January 2024, the &quot;recognized mobile satellite service&quot; with Iridium alongside Inmarsat, A3 defined by the RMSS coverage, simplified carriage, and what it means on board: Form R, operator familiarisation, and the procedures matching the equipment.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your ship is in A3 on Iridium only — can you still receive NAVAREA warnings?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes.<\/strong> <strong>Iridium<\/strong> is now a recognised mobile satellite service, and NAVAREA warnings come through <strong>Iridium SafetyCast<\/strong>, provided the NAVAREA coordinator broadcasts on it. Near the coast, NAVTEX still applies.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★6.3","sec":5,"q":"What's S-100 and when does it affect your ECDIS?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"S-100 ECDIS","a":"<p><strong>What S-100 is<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>S-100<\/strong> is the <strong>IHO&#x27;s universal hydrographic data framework<\/strong>. It replaces the <strong>S-57<\/strong> data standard behind today&#x27;s ENCs, which is limited and hard to extend.<\/p><p><strong>The family of product specifications<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>S-101<\/strong>: the new <strong>ENC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>S-102<\/strong>: <strong>high-resolution bathymetry<\/strong>, a detailed depth surface.<\/li><li><strong>S-104<\/strong>: <strong>water levels<\/strong>, including real-time and predicted tides.<\/li><li><strong>S-111<\/strong>: <strong>surface currents<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>S-124<\/strong>: <strong>navigational warnings<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Others: S-122 marine protected areas, S-127 traffic management, S-129 under-keel clearance management, S-411 ice.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The aim<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>These can be <strong>layered together<\/strong> on one display.<\/li><li><strong>Dynamic, more accurate data<\/strong>: for example, <strong>real-time tide<\/strong> applied to the <strong>safety contour<\/strong>, so the safe water shown changes with the actual water level.<\/li><li>Better <strong>under-keel clearance management<\/strong>, especially for deep-draught ships in shallow ports.<\/li><li>Easier updates and future products.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Timing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The revised IMO ECDIS performance standards (<strong>MSC.530(106)<\/strong>, verify) <strong>allow S-100-capable ECDIS from 1 January 2026<\/strong>.<\/li><li>They make it the <strong>standard for new installations from 1 January 2029<\/strong> (verify the exact dates before quoting).<\/li><li>There will be a <strong>transition period<\/strong> where ECDIS runs both <strong>S-57 and S-101<\/strong>, so existing S-57 ECDIS stays <strong>acceptable<\/strong> for a while.<\/li><li>Hydrographic offices are producing S-101 ENCs in parallel with S-57.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it means for me<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Possible <strong>software or hardware upgrades<\/strong> to the ECDIS, planned by the company.<\/li><li><strong>New type-specific training<\/strong> and <strong>familiarisation<\/strong> under ISM for the new displays and functions.<\/li><li><strong>Updated SMS procedures<\/strong> for the new layers: when to use real-time water levels, how to set the safety contour with S-102 and S-104.<\/li><li><strong>Care over which data is official<\/strong>: only official S-100 products from hydrographic offices count for carriage.<\/li><li><strong>Cyber security<\/strong>: more data connections mean more risk.<\/li><li>The <strong>STCW generic ECDIS course<\/strong> will also need updating.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Risks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Officers relying on dynamic data without understanding its limitations.<\/li><li>Mixed fleets with different ECDIS standards.<\/li><li>Data not available everywhere at first.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>S-100 is the new IHO framework replacing S-57, the main products S-101, S-102, S-104, S-111 and S-124, the benefit of dynamic layered data such as real-time tides on the safety contour, the timing from 2026 and mandatory for new installations from 2029 with a transition period, and the training and procedure changes.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Does S-102 bathymetry let you reduce your UKC allowance?","pa":"<p><strong>No, not by itself.<\/strong> S-102 gives much more detailed bathymetry, but UKC still depends on data quality, squat, tide and ship motion. Follow company UKC policy.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★6.4","sec":5,"q":"Container ship, 18 knots, head seas, rolling increasing to 25°. What's happening and what do you do?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What&#x27;s happening<\/strong><\/p><p>This is almost certainly <strong>parametric rolling<\/strong>. The guidance is <strong>MSC.1/Circ.1228<\/strong>, the IMO revised guidance to the Master for avoiding dangerous situations in adverse weather and sea conditions.<\/p><p><strong>Why it happens<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>In <strong>head or stern seas<\/strong> with a <strong>wavelength close to the ship&#x27;s length<\/strong>, the <strong>waterplane<\/strong> changes as wave <strong>crests<\/strong> pass <strong>amidships<\/strong> and then the <strong>ends<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On a container ship with a <strong>big bow flare<\/strong> and a <strong>wide, flat transom<\/strong>, the <strong>GM changes strongly<\/strong> through each wave cycle: GM is reduced with a crest amidships and increased with a trough amidships.<\/li><li>If the <strong>encounter period<\/strong> is about <strong>half the natural roll period<\/strong>, the roll builds up very quickly. Each time the ship rolls, the GM is low as she rolls out and high as she rolls back, pumping energy in.<\/li><li>It happens <strong>even with no beam sea<\/strong>, which is why it surprises people.<\/li><li>It can reach <strong>very large angles in a few cycles<\/strong>, causing <strong>container stack collapse<\/strong>, lashing failure, <strong>losses overboard<\/strong>, and damage to the ship. Famous cases include the APL China in 1998.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Immediate action<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong> if he isn&#x27;t already on the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Change the encounter period<\/strong>: alter <strong>speed<\/strong> and <strong>course<\/strong>. Usually <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> and bring the sea onto the bow at an angle, avoiding a sudden turn beam-on, which could make it worse.<\/li><li>Use the ship&#x27;s <strong>roll period and encounter period guidance<\/strong>, the <strong>polar diagram<\/strong> in the stability information, or any <strong>decision-support system<\/strong> fitted.<\/li><li>Consider <strong>changing GM<\/strong> later, by ballast, if it&#x27;s safe and the stability allows. Ballasting during heavy rolling is risky, so it&#x27;s not the first action.<\/li><li><strong>Keep the crew off deck<\/strong>. Check <strong>lashings<\/strong> only when it&#x27;s safe.<\/li><li><strong>Secure<\/strong> loose items, and warn the engine room and the crew.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the <strong>stack and lashing<\/strong> condition and look for <strong>losses<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Lost containers<\/strong> trigger the <strong>SOLAS V/31 and V/32<\/strong> reports: position, number, DG content, to nearby ships, the coastal State and the flag.<\/li><li>Report to the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>, and to the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> if it&#x27;s a marine casualty or incident under the 2026 Regs.<\/li><li>Record it in the logbook with the times, speeds and courses.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Monitor the <strong>roll period<\/strong> against the <strong>wave period<\/strong>, and know the ship&#x27;s natural roll period.<\/li><li>Use <strong>weather routeing<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Avoid the <strong>speeds and headings<\/strong> in the danger zone from the guidance.<\/li><li>Lashing calculations done for the expected weather.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Parametric rolling, MSC.1/Circ.1228, why it happens (GM variation, encounter period about half the roll period), immediate change of speed and heading, crew off deck, and the lost-container reports.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is parametric rolling worse in a lightly-damped ship with a large GM?","pa":"<p>Low damping lets roll energy <strong>build up cycle after cycle<\/strong>. A large GM gives a <strong>short natural roll period<\/strong>, so it falls into the <strong>2:1 match<\/strong> with common wave encounter periods. The big GM swings between crest and trough on a flared hull drive the roll.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q108","sec":6,"q":"Transverse thrust; turning a twin-screw ship to port fastest.","tier":"yellow","rep":11,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What transverse thrust is<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>sideways force<\/strong> a propeller puts on the <strong>stern<\/strong>, as well as its fore-and-aft thrust. It comes mainly from:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>lower blades<\/strong> working in <strong>denser, less disturbed<\/strong> water than the upper blades, which are nearer the surface and in the hull&#x27;s wake;<\/li><li>the uneven <strong>wake<\/strong> and the <strong>discharge current<\/strong> acting on the hull and rudder.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Single screw: right-handed fixed-pitch propeller<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A right-handed propeller turns <strong>clockwise<\/strong> viewed from astern when going ahead.<\/li><li>Going <strong>astern<\/strong>, it <strong>pulls the stern to port<\/strong>, so the <strong>bow swings to starboard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The effect is <strong>strongest<\/strong> when going <strong>astern from stopped<\/strong> or with little headway, because the rudder has little effect then.<\/li><li>Going <strong>ahead<\/strong>, the effect is <strong>small<\/strong>: the stern goes slightly to <strong>starboard<\/strong>, and the rudder easily overcomes it once the ship has way on.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s why a single-screw ship with a RH propeller prefers to berth <strong>port side to<\/strong>: going astern to stop throws the stern towards the quay.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Controllable-pitch propeller<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A CPP turns the <strong>same way all the time<\/strong>; going astern is done by reversing the pitch.<\/li><li>A <strong>right-hand-turning CPP<\/strong> going astern therefore behaves like a <strong>left-handed fixed propeller<\/strong>: <strong>stern to starboard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Always <strong>check the pilot card<\/strong> for the propeller type and the direction of transverse thrust astern.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Twin screw, outward turning<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Starboard propeller <strong>right-handed<\/strong>, port propeller <strong>left-handed<\/strong>. Both turn outwards at the top when going ahead.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>To turn to port fastest<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Starboard engine ahead<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Port engine astern<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Helm hard to port<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>All the effects work together<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>offset thrusts<\/strong> make a <strong>turning couple<\/strong>: ahead on the starboard side and astern on the port side turn the bow to port.<\/li><li>The <strong>starboard<\/strong> propeller ahead throws its <strong>stern to starboard<\/strong> (transverse thrust ahead, RH).<\/li><li>The <strong>port<\/strong> (left-handed) propeller <strong>astern<\/strong> also throws the <strong>stern to starboard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>starboard propeller&#x27;s wash<\/strong> acts on the rudder, which is to port.<\/li><li>The ship can turn <strong>almost in her own length<\/strong> with little headway.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Inward-turning twin screws<\/strong> behave the opposite way for transverse thrust and are less handy at low speed.<\/p><p><strong>Where it&#x27;s recorded<\/strong><\/p><p>On the <strong>pilot card<\/strong>, the <strong>wheelhouse poster<\/strong> and the <strong>manoeuvring booklet<\/strong>, with the propeller type, rotation, and the transverse-thrust direction astern.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>What causes it, a RH FPP astern throws the stern to port and the bow to starboard, a RH CPP astern does the opposite, and for an outward-turning twin screw to port: starboard ahead, port astern, helm to port, and why all the effects combine.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your single-screw RH ship must berth starboard side to — what problem does transverse thrust give you?","pa":"<p>Going astern to take the way off, the stern is thrown <strong>to port, away from the berth<\/strong>, so she lands bow first and the stern swings off. Approach at a <strong>flatter angle<\/strong>, get a stern line or spring out early, or use a tug aft.<\/p>","asked":["Ship manoeuvre explain short round turn?","What are the parts of the turning circle diagram?","How would you turn short round?","What factors affect turning circle and where do they determine this information?","(Transverse thrust)."],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",3],["Shahid Akhter",3],["Joe Mattock",1],["Timothy Cummins",1],["Examiner not named",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q109","sec":6,"q":"Anchoring: preparation, scope, deep-water anchoring concerns, anchor watch.","tier":"orange","rep":31,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Preparation<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Choose the anchorage<\/strong>: depth, <strong>holding ground<\/strong> (mud and clay good, rock and shingle poor), <strong>swinging room<\/strong> for the scope and the ship&#x27;s length, <strong>shelter<\/strong> from the expected weather, traffic, <strong>submarine cables and pipelines<\/strong>, and the <strong>port instructions<\/strong> or designated anchorage.<\/li><li><strong>Plan the approach<\/strong>: the heading into the wind or tide, the speed, the let-go position and the final position.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong> for the anchor party: PPE, communications, keeping clear of the cable, standing behind the windlass.<\/li><li><strong>Test<\/strong> the windlass, the <strong>brake<\/strong> and the <strong>communications<\/strong> with the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Clear<\/strong> the lashings, the bow stoppers or devil&#x27;s claw, and the <strong>spurling pipe covers<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Walk the anchor<\/strong> clear of the hawse pipe, and in deep water, further.<\/li><li><strong>Signals<\/strong> under <strong>Rule 30<\/strong>: one black <strong>ball<\/strong> by day and <strong>anchor lights<\/strong> by night, once brought up.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Scope<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Depends on <strong>depth, weather, current, holding ground<\/strong> and the ship&#x27;s size and windage.<\/li><li>About <strong>4 × depth<\/strong> in good weather, or more as the weather worsens, is a common figure. Some companies use a formula, such as 39 × √depth for cable in metres.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s a <strong>rule of thumb<\/strong>, not a regulation. Follow the company or Master&#x27;s guidance.<\/li><li>The cable should lie along the bottom so the pull on the anchor is horizontal.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Deep-water anchoring concerns<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Walk the anchor back under power<\/strong> all the way to just above the bottom. <strong>Never let it go by gravity<\/strong>, or the cable can <strong>run away<\/strong>, wreck the brake or windlass, and be lost.<\/li><li>The <strong>windlass<\/strong> is designed to lift the anchor plus a limited length of cable, about <strong>82.5 m<\/strong> depth under class rules (verify). In deeper water it may not be able to heave up. Check its capacity before anchoring deep.<\/li><li>The <strong>brake<\/strong> is designed to hold, but not to stop a running cable at full speed.<\/li><li>Anchors are designed for <strong>temporary mooring<\/strong> in <strong>moderate conditions<\/strong>. A big container ship with <strong>high windage<\/strong> <strong>drags easily<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Anchor watch<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Fix and record<\/strong> the position as soon as brought up, and the heading.<\/li><li>Set a <strong>swinging circle<\/strong> or anchor watch alarm on <strong>ECDIS<\/strong> or <strong>radar<\/strong>, with the radius allowing for the scope and the ship&#x27;s length.<\/li><li>Use <strong>transits<\/strong> and <strong>radar ranges and bearings<\/strong> from fixed objects, checked regularly.<\/li><li>Watch the <strong>cable lead<\/strong> and the <strong>strain<\/strong>, and the <strong>yaw<\/strong> pattern.<\/li><li>Keep <strong>engines on notice<\/strong> as the Master orders.<\/li><li>Monitor the <strong>weather<\/strong> and <strong>VHF<\/strong>, and other ships nearby.<\/li><li>Correct <strong>lights and signals<\/strong>, and a proper <strong>lookout<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong> if dragging, the weather worsens, or another ship comes close.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Choosing the anchorage, the preparations and RA, the signals, the scope as a rule of thumb, deep water walked back all the way with the windlass limit, and a proper anchor watch with the position, swing circle and when to call the Master.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you know the anchor has brought the ship up?","pa":"<p>The cable grows short and taut, then <strong>slackens<\/strong> as the ship stops and comes back. Her head <strong>settles to wind or tide<\/strong>, and the cross-bearings stay <strong>steady<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["Deep water anchoring procedure?","How will you know that your anchor is brought up?","What is maximum anchoring depth poster?","How would you tell your anchor is holding","You are proceeding for Deepsea anchoring. Procedure?"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",7],["Examiner not named",5],["Khademul Insan",5],["Inamul Bhuiyan",4],["Fazal Wadood",3]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q110","sec":6,"q":"Running, standing, open, Mediterranean and Baltic moors.","tier":"red","rep":26,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":"Running moor given instead of open moor — fail, Andrews 2022","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Running moor<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stem the tide<\/strong>, and <strong>let go the first anchor<\/strong> while still <strong>making headway<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Continue ahead, veering cable, to about <strong>twice the final scope<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Let go the second anchor<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fall back<\/strong> with the tide, <strong>heaving the first<\/strong> and <strong>veering the second<\/strong> until the cables are <strong>equal<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>It&#x27;s <strong>quick<\/strong>, but it <strong>strains the first cable<\/strong> and the windlass, because the ship is moving ahead with the anchor down. It can be done with the tide astern only with great care.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Standing moor<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stem the tide<\/strong>, and <strong>let go the first anchor<\/strong> while <strong>stopped<\/strong> or going <strong>slowly astern<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Drop back<\/strong> with the tide or engine to <strong>double the scope<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Let go the second anchor<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Heave the first<\/strong> and <strong>veer the second<\/strong> until they&#x27;re <strong>equal<\/strong>, and the ship lies midway.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>More <strong>controlled<\/strong> and less strain on the gear, but it <strong>needs the tide<\/strong> or the engine to drop back.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Open moor<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Both anchors<\/strong> laid out <strong>ahead<\/strong> at an <strong>angle<\/strong>, about <strong>60° or more apart<\/strong>, usually by letting go the first, moving across, and letting go the second.<\/li><li>It gives <strong>better holding<\/strong> and <strong>less yaw<\/strong> in wind or current from a <strong>steady direction<\/strong>, such as an anchorage with a constant strong wind.<\/li><li>Not suitable where the wind or tide reverses, because the cables will cross.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Mediterranean moor<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stern to the quay<\/strong>, with <strong>one or two anchors<\/strong> laid out ahead and <strong>stern lines<\/strong> ashore.<\/li><li>The ship approaches parallel to the quay, lets go the offshore anchor, then the other, and goes astern to the quay.<\/li><li>It <strong>saves quay space<\/strong>, common in the Mediterranean and for ferries and yachts.<\/li><li>Transverse thrust affects the approach astern.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Baltic moor<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Coming <strong>alongside<\/strong>, let go the <strong>offshore anchor<\/strong>, with a heavy <strong>wire<\/strong> from the <strong>offshore quarter<\/strong> shackled to its cable.<\/li><li><strong>Heaving<\/strong> on the cable and wire <strong>holds the ship off<\/strong> the quay.<\/li><li>This <strong>reduces impact<\/strong> on the fenders, and helps her <strong>get off<\/strong> the berth in an <strong>onshore wind<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Mooring in tidal rivers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A ship moored with two anchors in a tidal river swings with each tide. A <strong>mooring swivel<\/strong> is put in the cables to stop them <strong>twisting<\/strong> together as she swings.<\/li><li>Use a moor that keeps the ship within a small swinging circle.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Under a bridge or in a narrow channel<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>standing or running moor<\/strong> in line with the stream keeps the swinging circle small, about the ship&#x27;s length, so she doesn&#x27;t swing into the banks or a bridge.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Each moor described in steps with its advantage and drawback: running moor quick but strains the cable; standing moor controlled but needs the tide; open moor for a steady wind; Med moor stern to the quay; Baltic moor with the offshore anchor and quarter wire to hold her off; and the mooring swivel in tidal rivers.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you clear a foul hawse after the ship has swung in a moor?","pa":"<p>Wait for slack water. Use the ship&#x27;s swing, the engine or a tug to <strong>unwind the turns<\/strong>. If that fails, <strong>unshackle one cable<\/strong> and dip its end round the other with a boat and lines, then reconnect.<\/p>","asked":["How will you do a Running Moor?","Explain Running moor and Standing moor?","What's a Mediterranean moor and how would you do one?","Talk about everything like an emergency - He stopped and reframed the question but what he was looking for was Standing Moor (Hinted)","How would you do a standing moor vs running moor and di]erences between the"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",12],["Khademul Insan",4],["Roddy MacKay",3],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",2],["Tarik Yassin",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q111","sec":6,"q":"Berthing and unberthing scenarios (wind on/off, tide ahead/astern).","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>General preparation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Master-pilot information exchange<\/strong>: the plan, the pilot card, the ship&#x27;s peculiarities.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong>, considering wind, tide, the berth, the traffic and the ship&#x27;s windage.<\/li><li>A <strong>tug plan<\/strong> suited to the windage and the weather: number, bollard pull, where they make fast.<\/li><li>The <strong>bow thruster<\/strong> loses effect above about <strong>3–4 knots<\/strong>, so it&#x27;s for the final approach only.<\/li><li><strong>Engines and steering<\/strong> tested, both steering pumps running.<\/li><li><strong>Anchors<\/strong> ready to let go.<\/li><li><strong>Abort points<\/strong> agreed.<\/li><li><strong>Mooring parties<\/strong> briefed: lines order, snap-back zones, communications.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tide ahead (stemming)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>best case<\/strong>. You keep <strong>steerage<\/strong> at <strong>low speed over the ground<\/strong>, because the water is flowing past the rudder, and you can <strong>stop easily<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Approach at a small angle, and let the tide set her alongside.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tide astern<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Dangerous<\/strong>. The ship carries <strong>ground speed<\/strong> even with the engine <strong>stopped<\/strong>, and <strong>steerage is poor<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Going astern to stop brings transverse thrust and loss of steering.<\/li><li>Better to <strong>swing and stem the tide<\/strong>, <strong>wait for slack water<\/strong>, or use <strong>tugs<\/strong> and an <strong>anchor<\/strong> to control speed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Wind off the berth<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Approach at a <strong>steeper angle<\/strong> and <strong>closer<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Get the <strong>head line<\/strong> and <strong>forward spring<\/strong> ashore quickly, then the <strong>stern lines<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use <strong>tugs<\/strong> or the <strong>thruster<\/strong> to <strong>push her alongside<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t let her blow off<\/strong> before the lines are fast.<\/li><li>Heave on the lines evenly.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Wind on the berth<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Approach <strong>parallel<\/strong> and <strong>stop<\/strong> about <strong>one to two ship&#x27;s widths off<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Let her <strong>drift down<\/strong> under control.<\/li><li>Use <strong>tugs pulling off<\/strong>, or the <strong>offshore anchor<\/strong> let go on the approach, to <strong>check the landing speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Land flat<\/strong> on the fenders, at a very low lateral speed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Unberthing<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Wind off the berth<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Single up<\/strong>, let go, and let her <strong>blow clear<\/strong>, keeping the <strong>stern clear<\/strong> of the quay.<\/li><li>Watch that the stern doesn&#x27;t hit the quay or the ship astern as she moves.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Wind on the berth<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Tugs pull her off<\/strong>, or <strong>spring off the stern<\/strong> using the <strong>forward spring<\/strong> with rudder towards the quay and the engine ahead, so the stern swings out, then <strong>back out<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>offshore anchor<\/strong> laid on arrival helps: heave on it to pull the bow off.<\/li><li>With a RH propeller, going astern kicks the stern to port: use that if she&#x27;s starboard side to.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Current along the berth<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Leave <strong>into<\/strong> the current where possible, letting it lift the bow or stern off first.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Preparations including the Master-pilot exchange and tugs, tide ahead best and tide astern dangerous, wind off the berth (steep approach, lines fast, push in) and wind on the berth (parallel, drift down, check the landing), and unberthing methods for each, including springing off and using the anchor.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How much tug bollard pull do you need for your container ship in a 30-knot beam wind?","pa":"<p>Estimate the wind force: <strong>F (tonnes) ≈ A × V² ÷ 16,000<\/strong>, with A = lateral windage area (m²) and V = wind speed (m/s). For 10,000 m² in 30 kn (15.4 m/s), F ≈ 150 t. The total tug bollard pull should beat that <strong>with a margin<\/strong> of roughly 20–25%. Work it out for your own ship&#x27;s windage.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q112","sec":6,"q":"Snubbing round; turning short round.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Snubbing round<\/strong><\/p><p>Uses the <strong>anchor<\/strong> to turn the ship in a <strong>narrow river or channel<\/strong>, usually with the <strong>tide<\/strong>.<\/p><ol><li>Approach <strong>stemming the tide<\/strong> at <strong>slow speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Let go the anchor<\/strong> on the side you want to <strong>turn to<\/strong>, with a <strong>short scope<\/strong>, about <strong>one to one-and-a-half times the depth<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>cable is checked<\/strong> so the anchor <strong>drags and bites<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The anchor <strong>holds the bow<\/strong> while the <strong>tide and helm swing the stern<\/strong> round.<\/li><li>Once round, <strong>heave up<\/strong>, or proceed with the anchor <strong>dredging<\/strong> to steady the head, then heave up.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Dredging an anchor<\/strong><\/p><p>A similar technique: going slowly ahead or astern with a short scope, letting the anchor drag along the bottom to control the bow and the speed, for example when berthing in a strong current.<\/p><p><strong>Dangers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Excessive cable strain<\/strong> if the ship has too much way on.<\/li><li><strong>Fouling submarine cables or pipelines<\/strong>: check the chart.<\/li><li><strong>Overrunning the anchor<\/strong> and damaging the hull or bulbous bow.<\/li><li>The <strong>windlass<\/strong> must be in good order and the brake tested.<\/li><li>Not possible on rock or in very deep water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Turning short round<\/strong><\/p><p>Turns a <strong>single-screw ship<\/strong> in a <strong>confined space<\/strong> using <strong>engine and rudder<\/strong> only. With a <strong>right-handed fixed-pitch propeller<\/strong>, turn to <strong>starboard<\/strong>, because the transverse thrust astern helps.<\/p><ol><li><strong>Full rudder to starboard<\/strong>, with a <strong>short burst ahead<\/strong> to <strong>kick the stern to port<\/strong> without gaining much headway.<\/li><li><strong>Stop<\/strong>, then <strong>astern<\/strong>. <strong>Transverse thrust<\/strong> pulls the <strong>stern to port<\/strong>, and the <strong>bow keeps swinging to starboard<\/strong>. The rudder position matters little going astern at low speed.<\/li><li><strong>Repeat<\/strong> the ahead and astern bursts until she&#x27;s round, watching <strong>headway and sternway<\/strong> so she doesn&#x27;t gain way in either direction.<\/li><li>Use the wind if possible: a ship tends to back into the wind with sternway.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Helpful aids<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>bow thruster<\/strong> or a <strong>tug<\/strong> makes it much easier.<\/li><li>The <strong>wind<\/strong> and <strong>tide<\/strong> can help or hinder: plan the turn so they help.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><p>To test understanding of <strong>transverse thrust<\/strong>, the <strong>pivot point<\/strong> (forward when going ahead, aft when going astern), and the use of the anchor as a manoeuvring tool.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Snubbing round with a short scope on the side you&#x27;re turning to, the anchor holding the bow while the tide swings the stern, the dangers; and turning short round to starboard with a RH propeller using bursts ahead with full rudder and astern for the transverse thrust, controlling headway and sternway.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why would turning short round to port be harder with a right-handed propeller?","pa":"<p>With a right-handed propeller, the astern kicks throw the stern <strong>to port<\/strong>, which swings the head <strong>to starboard<\/strong>. So turning short round <strong>to starboard<\/strong> is easier: the astern kicks help the turn. Turning <strong>to port<\/strong>, they work against it.<\/p>","asked":["How you will carry out Turning a short round."],"ex":[["Michael Jubb",1]],"yrs":"2022–2022"},{"id":"Q113","sec":6,"q":"Squat, bank suction/cushion, interaction — and how to minimise squat?","tier":"orange","rep":26,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>UK guidance on interaction is <strong>MGN 199<\/strong>. Squat goes in the <strong>UKC policy<\/strong> and the <strong>passage plan<\/strong> (SOLAS V/34).<\/p><p><strong>Squat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>bodily sinkage<\/strong> and <strong>change of trim<\/strong> of a ship moving through <strong>shallow water<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Water <strong>speeds up<\/strong> under and around the hull, and the <strong>pressure falls<\/strong> (Bernoulli), so the ship sinks.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s roughly proportional to <strong>speed squared<\/strong>, and increases with the <strong>block coefficient<\/strong> and the <strong>blockage factor<\/strong> (the ship&#x27;s cross-section compared with the channel&#x27;s).<\/li><li><strong>Rule of thumb<\/strong>: <strong>Cb × V² ÷ 100<\/strong> metres in <strong>open shallow water<\/strong>, and about <strong>double<\/strong> in a <strong>confined channel<\/strong> (V in knots through the water).<\/li><li>Squat increases sharply when the depth-to-draught ratio is below about 1.5.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Which end<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ships with a <strong>high Cb<\/strong>, such as tankers and bulk carriers, tend to squat <strong>by the head<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fine<\/strong> container ships tend to squat <strong>by the stern<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Trim also affects it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Signs of squat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Vibration, a reduction in speed with the same RPM, the ship becoming sluggish to steer, the wave pattern changing, mud in the wake.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Bank effect<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Close to a <strong>bank<\/strong>, water builds up at the <strong>bow<\/strong> (<strong>bank cushion<\/strong>, pushing the bow <strong>off<\/strong>) and pressure drops at the <strong>stern<\/strong> (<strong>bank suction<\/strong>, pulling the stern <strong>in<\/strong>).<\/li><li>The ship <strong>sheers away<\/strong> from the bank.<\/li><li>Counter with <strong>helm towards the bank<\/strong> and <strong>slow down<\/strong>. Keep in the middle of the channel where possible.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Interaction between ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Happens when <strong>meeting<\/strong> and above all <strong>overtaking<\/strong> in narrow channels: <strong>sheering<\/strong>, <strong>suction together<\/strong>, and <strong>loss of steering<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The pressure fields round each hull: high at the bows and sterns, low amidships.<\/li><li>When overtaking, the ships may be drawn together and the overtaken ship may sheer across the bow of the overtaking one.<\/li><li><strong>Tugs<\/strong> near the <strong>bow<\/strong> are especially at risk: they can be sucked under the bow or girted.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong> before passing, keep as far apart as possible, and agree the manoeuvre with VTS or the other ship.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Minimise squat<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong>: the most effective step. <strong>Halving the speed<\/strong> cuts squat to about <strong>a quarter<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Keep near <strong>even keel<\/strong>, or with the trim the squat table recommends.<\/li><li><strong>Transit at higher water<\/strong>, within the <strong>tidal window<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Avoid <strong>passing<\/strong> other ships in the <strong>narrows<\/strong>, where the blockage doubles.<\/li><li>Allow for <strong>squat<\/strong> in the <strong>UKC<\/strong> and the ECDIS safety contour.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>What squat is and why it happens, the formula, head or stern depending on Cb, bank cushion and suction with the counter-helm, interaction when overtaking and with tugs, MGN 199, and reducing speed as the main way to minimise squat.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you notice squat on the bridge?","pa":"<p>Signs of squat:<\/p><ul><li>more <strong>vibration<\/strong>, and less speed for the same revs;<\/li><li>a change of trim, usually by the head on a full-form ship;<\/li><li><strong>sluggish steering<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a bigger wave pattern, and the echo sounder reading less;<\/li><li>mud stirred up astern.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["How do you reduce squat?","Explain interaction ?","What is bank effect.","What is squat and how u can reduce the effect?","How will you tell you are being affected by squat?"],"ex":[["Katy McDonald",8],["Examiner not named",7],["Chris Roe",4],["James Gilbert",3],["David Carlisle",3]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q114","sec":6,"q":"Tug types; girting.","tier":"orange","rep":32,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Tug types<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Conventional tug<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Screw propellers aft<\/strong> and a <strong>towing hook<\/strong> or winch near <strong>midships<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Simple, but the tow point is forward of the propellers, so it&#x27;s <strong>prone to girting<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>ASD tug (azimuth stern drive)<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Two azimuthing propulsors aft<\/strong>, very <strong>manoeuvrable<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Usually works <strong>over the bow<\/strong> on a winch, can <strong>push or pull<\/strong>, and can <strong>escort<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The most common harbour tug today.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Tractor tug<\/strong> (Voith-Schneider or forward Z-drive)<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Propulsion forward<\/strong> and the <strong>towing point aft<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The tow line pulls the stern, so the tug swings round to face the pull. That makes it <strong>inherently resistant to girting<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Good for <strong>escort<\/strong> work.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Rotor tugs<\/strong> (three propulsors) and <strong>escort tugs<\/strong> that use <strong>indirect towing<\/strong>: they angle themselves to the flow and use their hull and propulsion to <strong>steer and brake<\/strong> a ship at speed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Girting (girding)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Happens when the <strong>towline<\/strong> comes to act <strong>at right angles<\/strong> to the tug, <strong>across its beam<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It can happen if:<\/li><li>the <strong>ship gathers speed<\/strong> or <strong>sheers<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the tug moves <strong>ahead<\/strong> of the tow point, or is pulled round;<\/li><li>the ship goes astern unexpectedly with a tug on the stern.<\/li><li>The pull <strong>heels the tug over<\/strong>, and it can <strong>capsize within seconds<\/strong>. Many tug crews have died this way.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>quick-release<\/strong> hook or winch, <strong>tested<\/strong> and usable under load.<\/li><li>A <strong>gob rope<\/strong> (on conventional tugs) to keep the tow point aft.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s <strong>speed kept low<\/strong> when tugs are connected, typically under 5–6 knots for harbour tugs, as the tug master agrees.<\/li><li><strong>Clear communication<\/strong> between the pilot, the bridge and the tug: agreed VHF channel, standard phrases.<\/li><li>Tugs <strong>not positioned close under the bow<\/strong> while the ship is making way, because of <strong>interaction<\/strong> with the bow pressure wave.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s crew <strong>letting go<\/strong> the line <strong>smartly<\/strong> when ordered, and <strong>not<\/strong> letting go before ordered.<\/li><li>No sudden engine or helm movements without telling the tug.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>On board: the tug lines<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Use the <strong>ship&#x27;s<\/strong> or the <strong>tug&#x27;s<\/strong> line as agreed; check the ship&#x27;s fairleads and bitts are rated for the bollard pull.<\/li><li>Keep the mooring party <strong>clear of snap-back zones<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use a messenger and heaving line.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The tug types with their tow point and propulsion, why tractors resist girting, what girting is and how quickly it capsizes a tug, and the prevention: quick release, gob rope, low speed, communication, not under the bow, and letting go when ordered.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"As C/O at the forecastle, what are your checks before making fast a tug?","pa":"<p>Before making fast a tug:<\/p><ul><li>agree the VHF channel and whose line is used;<\/li><li>check the bitts, fairleads and Panama chock have a <strong>SWL above the tug&#x27;s bollard pull<\/strong>;<\/li><li>clear the <strong>snap-back zones<\/strong>;<\/li><li>have heaving lines and messengers ready;<\/li><li>brief the crew and check their PPE;<\/li><li>nobody stands in a bight.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["Situation. Your vessel is typically MCA ship, fixed right handed propeller, no tug and bt.","Explain how will you carryout safe operations with tugs?","Tug & Tow, more than 200m from P/S.","What is Girting of tug?","What is Girting of Tugs and how can you prevent it?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",8],["Michele Moro",7],["Chris Roe",4],["Ahsan Khan",2],["James Gilbert",2]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q115","sec":6,"q":"Pilot transfer arrangements.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Internationally <strong>SOLAS V/23<\/strong> and IMO <strong>Resolution A.1045(27)<\/strong> on pilot transfer arrangements, with the <strong>IMPA poster<\/strong> as a quick check. In the UK, SOLAS V/23 is applied by the <strong>Merchant Shipping (Safety of Navigation) Regulations 2020<\/strong>, with guidance in MGN 610. New requirements apply from <strong>2028<\/strong> (see ★7.1).<\/p><p><strong>The pilot ladder<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Certified<\/strong> to the standard, in <strong>good condition<\/strong>, no damaged steps or worn side ropes.<\/li><li><strong>Secured to strong points<\/strong>, not to the handrails or the bulwark rail.<\/li><li>Rigged on the <strong>lee side<\/strong>, <strong>clear of discharges<\/strong> and the <strong>flare<\/strong>, within the parallel body of the ship, and away from the propeller.<\/li><li><strong>Steps horizontal<\/strong> and <strong>evenly spaced<\/strong>, resting firmly against the ship&#x27;s side, with <strong>spreaders<\/strong> in place to stop it twisting.<\/li><li>The pilot should <strong>climb not less than 1.5 m and not more than 9 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Where the <strong>freeboard is over 9 m<\/strong>, a <strong>combination arrangement<\/strong> with the <strong>accommodation ladder<\/strong> is used, with the accommodation ladder leading aft and at no more than 45°.<\/li><li>The <strong>bottom of the ladder<\/strong> at the <strong>height the pilot asks for<\/strong>.<\/li><li>No shackles, knots or splices in the side ropes; the ladder in one length.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Fittings and equipment<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Man ropes<\/strong> if the pilot requests them.<\/li><li>A <strong>lifebuoy<\/strong> with a <strong>self-igniting light<\/strong>, ready.<\/li><li>A <strong>heaving line<\/strong>, ready.<\/li><li><strong>Lighting<\/strong> at night, shining on the ladder and the water, <strong>not dazzling<\/strong> the pilot boat.<\/li><li>Safe <strong>access to deck<\/strong> through a <strong>gateway<\/strong> with <strong>handholds<\/strong> or stanchions, or a bulwark ladder.<\/li><li><strong>Mechanical hoists<\/strong> are not allowed for new ships.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Supervision<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>responsible officer<\/strong>, in <strong>radio contact<\/strong> with the bridge, <strong>supervises<\/strong> the rigging and the transfer and <strong>escorts<\/strong> the pilot to the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Speed and heading<\/strong> as the pilot boat asks, giving a <strong>lee<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A crew member at the ladder with the officer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Inspection and records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ladder is <strong>inspected before each use<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Records<\/strong> are kept of when each ladder was <strong>brought into service<\/strong> and its <strong>maintenance<\/strong> and <strong>repairs<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Replace a ladder that&#x27;s damaged or reached its service limit.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Pilot ladder failures have killed pilots, and pilot transfer arrangements are among the most common <strong>PSC deficiencies<\/strong>. IMPA surveys regularly find many non-compliant arrangements.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/23 and A.1045(27), the ladder rigged to strong points on the lee side, 1.5 to 9 m climb, combination above 9 m, the equipment (man ropes, lifebuoy with light, heaving line, lighting), a responsible officer in contact with the bridge, inspection and records, and the 2028 changes. A photo may be shown to spot the faults.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The pilot refuses your ladder as unsafe. What now?","pa":"<p>Put it right:<\/p><ul><li>correct the rigging to <strong>SOLAS V/23<\/strong> and <strong>A.1045(27)<\/strong>;<\/li><li>use the spare ladder;<\/li><li>make a good lee with heading and speed.<\/li><\/ul><p>If he still refuses, he doesn&#x27;t board. <strong>Never pressure him.<\/strong> The Master informs the pilot station, and it&#x27;s recorded.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q116","sec":6,"q":"Parametric rolling.","tier":"yellow","rep":12,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>MSC.1/Circ.1228<\/strong>, the IMO revised guidance to the Master for avoiding dangerous situations in adverse weather and sea conditions.<\/p><p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>build-up of roll<\/strong> in <strong>head or following seas<\/strong>, with <strong>no beam forcing<\/strong>. It&#x27;s caused by <strong>periodic changes in stability<\/strong> as the ship passes through the waves.<\/p><ul><li>When a <strong>crest is amidships<\/strong> and the <strong>ends are in troughs<\/strong>, the <strong>waterplane narrows<\/strong> at the ends and <strong>GM drops<\/strong>.<\/li><li>When the <strong>trough is amidships<\/strong> and <strong>crests are at the ends<\/strong>, the <strong>flared bow<\/strong> and <strong>wide stern<\/strong> are immersed, and <strong>GM rises<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the timing is right, the ship rolls out when GM is low and is pushed back hard when GM is high, so each roll is bigger than the last.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Conditions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Wavelength<\/strong> about <strong>equal to the ship&#x27;s length<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Encounter period<\/strong> about <strong>half the natural roll period<\/strong> (sometimes equal to it).<\/li><li><strong>Low roll damping<\/strong>, often at <strong>low speed<\/strong> or in a <strong>following sea<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Ship forms<\/strong> with large <strong>bow flare<\/strong> and a <strong>broad, flat stern<\/strong>: large <strong>container ships<\/strong> and <strong>car carriers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A sufficient wave height.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Consequences<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Roll angles of <strong>30–40°<\/strong> can build in <strong>a few cycles<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Container stack collapse<\/strong>, <strong>lashing failure<\/strong>, <strong>cargo loss<\/strong> overboard, <strong>structural damage<\/strong> to the hull and hatch covers, and <strong>injury<\/strong> to the crew.<\/li><li>Large accelerations at the top of the stacks.<\/li><li>The APL China (1998) and other ships lost hundreds of containers this way.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Recognising it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Rolling increasing rapidly in <strong>head or stern seas<\/strong> with little beam sea.<\/li><li>The ship <strong>pitching<\/strong> at about <strong>twice<\/strong> the rate she&#x27;s rolling: two pitch cycles per roll cycle.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Avoidance<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Change the encounter period<\/strong> by altering <strong>course and speed<\/strong>. Taking the sea <strong>off the bow<\/strong> and <strong>reducing speed<\/strong> usually works. Don&#x27;t make sudden large turns that bring her beam-on.<\/li><li>Avoid the <strong>risky combinations<\/strong> shown in the ship&#x27;s <strong>operational guidance<\/strong> or <strong>decision-support software<\/strong>: the polar diagram of speed and heading against wave period.<\/li><li>Use <strong>weather routeing<\/strong> to avoid the conditions.<\/li><li>Use <strong>anti-roll tanks<\/strong> if fitted.<\/li><li>Know the ship&#x27;s <strong>natural roll period<\/strong> for the loaded condition, from the stability information or by timing the roll.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>After an event<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the stacks, lashings and the cargo; report losses under <strong>SOLAS V/31 and V/32<\/strong>; report to the company and, if required, the MAIB.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MSC.1/Circ.1228, the GM variation mechanism, the conditions (wavelength about ship length, encounter period about half the roll period), the ship types, the consequences, recognising it, and changing course and speed to avoid it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can parametric rolling happen in following seas?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes.<\/strong> It can happen in following or stern-quartering seas whenever the <strong>encounter period is about half the roll period<\/strong>, though head seas are more common on container ships.<\/p>","asked":["What is parametric rolling?","What affects the most Synchronous or Parametric and why","Parametric, Synchronized, Stiff vessel, high Gm","What are Synchronous and Parametric rolling and other effects from a heavy sea on vessels stability?","On a large containership and vessel begins Parametric rolling – why is this and how to avoid/stop this?"],"ex":[["Karen McCartney-Clarke",2],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",2],["Khairul Anam",1],["Joe Mattock",1],["Shahid Akhter",1]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q117","sec":6,"q":"Heavy weather preparations by the C/O.","tier":"red","rep":91,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Guidance:<\/strong> <strong>MSC.1/Circ.1228<\/strong> and the company&#x27;s <strong>heavy-weather checklist<\/strong>. Cargo securing must follow the approved <strong>Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong> (SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5) and the CSS Code.<\/p><p><strong>1. Cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Run the <strong>lashing software<\/strong> or check the CSM for the <strong>forecast accelerations<\/strong> and <strong>roll angle<\/strong>, and adjust: extra lashings, or move heavy boxes if possible in port.<\/li><li><strong>Tighten turnbuckles<\/strong>, check <strong>twistlocks<\/strong> are locked and <strong>bridge fittings<\/strong> secure.<\/li><li><strong>Recheck<\/strong> stacks with <strong>heavy<\/strong> or <strong>high-cube<\/strong> boxes, <strong>DG<\/strong> containers and <strong>OOG<\/strong> units.<\/li><li>Secure <strong>reefer cables<\/strong> and check the reefer monitoring.<\/li><li>Check lashings on deck cargo and any break-bulk.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Stability<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Confirm <strong>GM<\/strong> is <strong>adequate but not excessive<\/strong>. A <strong>very high GM<\/strong> gives a short, <strong>violent roll<\/strong> that damages stacks and lashings. Too low a GM risks large angles and parametric roll.<\/li><li><strong>Minimise free surface<\/strong>: keep <strong>ballast tanks pressed or empty<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Correct any list<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>parametric-roll<\/strong> guidance for the expected waves.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>trim<\/strong> for good steering and propeller immersion, to avoid racing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Watertight integrity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Hatch covers<\/strong> and <strong>cleats<\/strong> secured.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilators<\/strong>, <strong>weathertight doors<\/strong> and <strong>portholes<\/strong> closed; deadlights down where needed.<\/li><li><strong>Spurling pipes<\/strong> covered and cemented if required, <strong>anchors double-lashed<\/strong> and the chain stoppers on.<\/li><li><strong>Sounding pipes<\/strong> and air pipe heads capped and checked.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Deck<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Lifelines<\/strong> rigged along the deck.<\/li><li><strong>Loose gear<\/strong>, stores, drums, the <strong>gangway<\/strong> and <strong>ladders<\/strong> secured.<\/li><li><strong>Access to the deck restricted<\/strong>: work only after a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>, with the <strong>bridge informed<\/strong>, in pairs, with lifejackets and communications.<\/li><li>Pilot ladder and accommodation ladder stowed and lashed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Internal<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Galley<\/strong>, <strong>provision stores<\/strong>, <strong>workshops<\/strong>, <strong>engine room spares<\/strong> and the <strong>hospital<\/strong> secured.<\/li><li>Furniture and equipment in cabins and offices secured.<\/li><li><strong>Crew warned<\/strong> of the expected weather, and to secure their own cabins.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Engine room<\/strong> (with the Chief Engineer)<\/p><ul><li>Fuel tanks for the weather, avoiding free surface and sludge being stirred up; engine ready for reduced speed and racing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Monitoring<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Bilge and hold soundings<\/strong> regularly, and the bilge alarms.<\/li><li>Regular, <strong>safe rounds<\/strong>, from inside where possible.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>water on deck<\/strong>, damage and shifting cargo.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Navigation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Weather routeing, a plan to heave to or alter course, and the speed reduction.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>9. Report and record<\/strong><\/p><p>I report to the <strong>Master<\/strong> that everything is done, and <strong>record<\/strong> it in the <strong>logbook<\/strong>.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The CSM and lashing software for the forecast, lashings tightened, GM adequate but not excessive, free surface minimised, watertight integrity, anchors and spurling pipes, lifelines and restricted deck access, internal securing, soundings and rounds, and reporting to the Master and the logbook.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is a high GM a problem on a container ship in heavy weather?","pa":"<p>High GM gives a <strong>short, stiff roll<\/strong> with large accelerations, so <strong>lashing forces increase<\/strong>. Stacks can collapse and boxes be lost, the structure is stressed and crew can be injured.<\/p>","asked":["How will you prepare the vessel (as a chief mate) for heavy weather?","Heavy weather precautions?","Heavy weather prep explain?","What to do as chief officer for heavy weather?","Vessel lost the mooring rope, after heavy weather. What is your action?"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",16],["Khademul Insan",11],["Nayyer Naqvi",11],["Khairul Anam",9],["Muhammad Iqbal",7]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q118","sec":6,"q":"Emergency towing arrangements and booklet.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-1/3-4<\/strong> has <strong>two parts<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>1. Emergency towing arrangements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Tankers of 20,000 dwt and above<\/strong> must have an emergency towing arrangement at <strong>both ends<\/strong>: a <strong>strongpoint<\/strong>, a <strong>fairlead<\/strong>, <strong>pick-up gear<\/strong> and a <strong>towing pennant<\/strong>.<\/li><li>They must be <strong>deployable quickly<\/strong>, within a set time, and with <strong>no main power<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>aft<\/strong> one should be <strong>pre-rigged<\/strong>, so it can be deployed in about 15 minutes.<\/li><li>The forward one may be stowed ready for rigging.<\/li><li>The guidelines are in IMO Resolution MSC.35(63).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Emergency towing procedures<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>All passenger ships<\/strong> and <strong>cargo ships<\/strong> must carry a <strong>ship-specific emergency towing booklet<\/strong> (ETB), for ships built on or after 1 January 2010 and existing ships by 2012.<\/li><li>The guidelines are in <strong>MSC.1/Circ.1255<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the booklet contains<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Arrangement drawings<\/strong> showing <strong>strongpoints<\/strong>, <strong>bitts<\/strong>, <strong>fairleads<\/strong> and chocks, and their <strong>SWL<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>equipment<\/strong> on board for towing and <strong>where it&#x27;s stored<\/strong>: wires, shackles, messengers, chafing gear.<\/li><li>The <strong>procedures<\/strong> for making up a tow at the <strong>bow<\/strong> and the <strong>stern<\/strong>, step by step.<\/li><li>The <strong>communications<\/strong> to use with the <strong>towing vessel<\/strong>: channel, the information to exchange, signals.<\/li><li>The <strong>manpower<\/strong> and <strong>time<\/strong> needed for each operation.<\/li><li>The effects of <strong>no main power<\/strong>: how to heave lines and pennants <strong>without windlass power<\/strong>, using manual methods or emergency power.<\/li><li><strong>Stability and trim<\/strong> considerations, and the effect on the ship&#x27;s behaviour under tow.<\/li><li>A <strong>checklist<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Where possible, <strong>photographs<\/strong> of the arrangements.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Keep the booklet on the <strong>bridge<\/strong>, in the <strong>ship&#x27;s office<\/strong> and at the <strong>forecastle<\/strong>, with a copy <strong>ashore<\/strong> with the company for the emergency response team.<\/li><li>Include it in <strong>drills<\/strong>: an emergency towing drill, at least as a tabletop exercise, so the crew have done it before they need it.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>strongpoints<\/strong> and <strong>fairleads<\/strong> are <strong>maintained<\/strong> under the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, and their SWL is marked.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>equipment<\/strong> is where the booklet says and in good condition.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>crew know<\/strong> how to <strong>pass a messenger<\/strong> and a <strong>heavy line<\/strong>, and the safety precautions: snap-back zones, chafing, communications.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>After engine failure near a lee shore, the speed of connecting a tow can decide whether the ship grounds. The booklet saves time. Salvors and tugs will ask for the information it contains.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-1/3-4 in two parts, the tanker ETA at both ends for 20,000 dwt and above with the aft one pre-rigged, the ETB for all ships under MSC.1/Circ.1255, its contents including working without main power, and the C/O&#x27;s actions: copies, drills, maintenance and crew training.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your ship has lost power and a salvage tug arrives — what do you need to agree before connecting?","pa":"<p>Before connecting, agree:<\/p><ul><li>whether it&#x27;s <strong>towage or salvage (LOF)<\/strong>;<\/li><li>whose towline, and the connection point (emergency towing arrangement);<\/li><li>communications, and tow speed and route;<\/li><li>who&#x27;s in charge of the tow;<\/li><li>the crew&#x27;s tasks, and the lights and shapes.<\/li><\/ul><p>Tell the company and insurers.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q119","sec":6,"q":"Anchor cable — when do you renew it?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Anchor cable condition is a <strong>class matter<\/strong>, under the RO&#x27;s rules. <strong>SOLAS II-1/3-1<\/strong> requires the ship to be maintained to class standards, and IACS sets common requirements. The cable is checked at <strong>dockings<\/strong> and <strong>special surveys<\/strong>, and it&#x27;s listed on the <strong>anchor and cable certificate<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Renew a link or length when<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>mean diameter<\/strong> of a common link is <strong>reduced by 12% or more<\/strong> from nominal, through <strong>wear and corrosion<\/strong>. Measured at the most worn part, usually at the ends where links bear on each other.<\/li><li>Links are <strong>cracked<\/strong> or <strong>deformed<\/strong>, or the cable is badly <strong>elongated<\/strong> so it no longer fits the gypsy properly.<\/li><li><strong>Studs<\/strong> are <strong>loose<\/strong> or <strong>missing<\/strong>. Studs can sometimes be refitted, but a link without its stud loses much of its strength and can deform.<\/li><li>A <strong>joining shackle<\/strong> (Kenter) is <strong>defective<\/strong> or fails inspection: worn, cracked, the pin or lead pellet missing.<\/li><li>The <strong>swivel<\/strong> or <strong>end shackles<\/strong> are damaged.<\/li><li>After an incident such as the cable running out or snagging, if it&#x27;s damaged.<\/li><\/ol><p>My notes [P1] said <strong>10%<\/strong>. The class standard is <strong>12%<\/strong> reduction in mean link diameter.<\/p><p><strong>How it&#x27;s checked<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>In <strong>dry dock<\/strong>, the cable is <strong>ranged<\/strong> on the dock floor.<\/li><li><strong>Measured<\/strong> with <strong>callipers<\/strong> at several links per shackle length (each 27.5 m), and recorded.<\/li><li>Links are <strong>hammer-tested<\/strong> for <strong>cracks<\/strong>: a good link rings, a cracked one sounds dull.<\/li><li><strong>Joining shackles<\/strong> are opened, inspected and re-assembled with new lead pellets.<\/li><li><strong>Shackle markings<\/strong> are renewed: painted links and wire seizing at each shackle.<\/li><li>Lengths are <strong>turned end-for-end<\/strong>, or the shackles changed in position, to <strong>even out wear<\/strong>, because the first few shackles wear most.<\/li><li>The <strong>chain locker<\/strong> is cleaned, and the <strong>bitter end<\/strong> and its <strong>release<\/strong> are checked: it must be possible to release it from outside the chain locker.<\/li><li>The <strong>anchor<\/strong> itself is checked for cracks and the shank and pin.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The results are recorded for <strong>class<\/strong>, and the surveyor credits the survey.<\/li><li>Any renewal uses <strong>certified<\/strong> cable of the right grade and size.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>At sea<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The crew check the cable and markings when anchoring, and report any damage.<\/li><li>The windlass, brake and stoppers are maintained under the PMS.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A class matter under SOLAS II-1/3-1, renew at 12% reduction in mean diameter (not 10%), or cracked, deformed, studs missing, defective Kenter shackles; checked in dry dock by ranging, measuring, hammer testing, marking and turning end-for-end, and recorded for class.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How is each shackle of cable marked so you know how much is out?","pa":"<p>At each joining shackle, the <strong>number of stud links painted white<\/strong> either side shows the shackle number: one link for 1 shackle, two for 2, and so on. There&#x27;s wire on the stud of the last marked link, so it can be felt at night. Practice varies, so check your ship.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★7.1","sec":6,"q":"What's changing for pilot ladders?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"Pilot ladders SOLAS V/23 from 2028","a":"<p><strong>What&#x27;s changing<\/strong><\/p><p>IMO has adopted amendments to <strong>SOLAS V/23<\/strong> and new <strong>performance standards<\/strong> for <strong>pilot transfer arrangements<\/strong> (<strong>MSC.572(110)<\/strong> and <strong>MSC.576(110)<\/strong>). They apply from <strong>1 January 2028<\/strong> (verify the exact date and the details before quoting).<\/p><p><strong>Why<\/strong><\/p><p>Pilot ladder accidents keep killing pilots, and surveys by IMPA show a large proportion of arrangements are non-compliant. The old rules were open to interpretation.<\/p><p><strong>Main changes<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Performance standard for ladders<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Pilot ladders and associated equipment must meet the <strong>new performance standard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Ladders must be <strong>approved<\/strong> by the flag Administration, with clearer <strong>construction<\/strong> and <strong>testing<\/strong> requirements: steps, side ropes, spreaders, and strength tests.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Securing<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Ladders secured to <strong>dedicated strong points<\/strong> (verify the detail), with clearer rules on <strong>deck rigging<\/strong>, the securing of <strong>side ropes<\/strong>, and <strong>handholds<\/strong> at the access point.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Maintenance and records<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Each ladder gets a <strong>record<\/strong> of its <strong>date in service<\/strong>, <strong>inspections<\/strong> and <strong>repairs<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Ladders are <strong>replaced<\/strong> at a set <strong>service life<\/strong> or when they become defective (verify the maximum age).<\/li><li>Regular inspections by the crew, recorded.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Combination arrangements<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Freeboard limits are clarified, along with the position of the <strong>accommodation ladder<\/strong> and the <strong>platform<\/strong>, and how the pilot ladder is secured to it.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Lighting, supervision and access<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Requirements are <strong>tightened<\/strong>: lighting of the ladder and access, the responsible officer, and safe access to the deck.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Winch reels<\/strong> and <strong>retrieval lines<\/strong>: clearer requirements for ladders stowed on reels.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Check<\/strong> our ladders and arrangements against the new standard, and tell the company early, because <strong>existing ladders may need replacing<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Update the <strong>PMS<\/strong> with the new inspection and replacement requirements.<\/li><li>Make sure the <strong>records<\/strong> are ready for <strong>PSC<\/strong>: the ladder&#x27;s certificate, date in service, inspections.<\/li><li>Train the crew on the correct rigging, using the updated IMPA poster.<\/li><li>Check the strong points and access arrangements on deck.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Pilot ladder arrangements are already a <strong>frequent PSC finding<\/strong>, and after 2028 PSC will be checking the new requirements.<\/p><p><strong>UK<\/strong><\/p><p>SOLAS V/23 is applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020 with ambulatory referencing, so the amendment will apply to UK ships from its in-force date.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/23 amendments and new performance standards from 2028, approval, securing to strong points, records of date in service and replacement, combination arrangements, and what I&#x27;d do now to prepare.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who on board is responsible for rigging the pilot ladder, and who checks it?","pa":"<p>The <strong>deck crew<\/strong> rig it under the supervision of a <strong>responsible officer<\/strong>. That officer has <strong>communication with the bridge<\/strong> and <strong>escorts the pilot<\/strong> to the bridge (SOLAS V/23). On most ships that&#x27;s the C/O or the duty officer.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★7.2","sec":6,"q":"Your container ship has 30 m air draught and a 50 knot beam wind in the approach. Concerns?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>In short<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>50-knot beam wind<\/strong> on a <strong>high-sided container ship<\/strong> is very likely <strong>beyond safe operating limits<\/strong> for the approach and berthing. Many ports and companies set <strong>wind limits<\/strong> well below that for large container ships, often around 25–30 knots for berthing (check the port and company limits).<\/p><p><strong>Concerns<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Windage<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>With <strong>30 m of stacked containers<\/strong> above the waterline, the lateral windage area is enormous.<\/li><li>Wind force rises with <strong>wind speed squared<\/strong>. Going from 25 to 50 knots means roughly <strong>four times the force<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Leeway and drift angle<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>At <strong>low approach speed<\/strong>, she&#x27;ll <strong>crab<\/strong> badly with a large drift angle.<\/li><li>She&#x27;ll take up much more <strong>channel width<\/strong>, and may go <strong>off the channel edge<\/strong> or into the path of other traffic.<\/li><li>Slowing down for the pilot or the berth makes it worse.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Tugs<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>The needed <strong>bollard pull<\/strong> to hold her may <strong>exceed<\/strong> what&#x27;s available.<\/li><li>Tugs also struggle in the <strong>sea<\/strong> that comes with that wind, and may not be able to make fast safely.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Heel<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Wind heel<\/strong> increases the draught at the bilge on the lee side and <strong>reduces UKC<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Combine that with <strong>squat<\/strong> and any turn.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Thruster<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Useless<\/strong> at speed and <strong>limited<\/strong> even at slow speed against that wind.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Mooring lines<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Loads alongside could <strong>exceed<\/strong> the lines&#x27; and <strong>bollards&#x27;<\/strong> strength.<\/li><li>The ship could break out from the berth.<\/li><li><strong>Cranes<\/strong> can&#x27;t work, so there&#x27;s no point being alongside.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Pilot transfer<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Boarding the pilot in that sea may be impossible or dangerous.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Abort options<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Must be <strong>planned<\/strong>. Once committed in a narrow approach, there may be <strong>no safe way back<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Discuss<\/strong> with the <strong>Master<\/strong>, the <strong>pilot<\/strong> and the <strong>port<\/strong>. The port may already have closed.<\/li><li><strong>Delay<\/strong> the arrival: <strong>hold at sea<\/strong> or at a safe <strong>anchorage<\/strong> with <strong>plenty of sea room<\/strong>, remembering that anchoring a container ship in 50 knots is itself risky.<\/li><li>Plan <strong>tugs<\/strong>, <strong>abort points<\/strong> and the <strong>tidal window<\/strong> for when the wind eases.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the decision and the reasons. The Master&#x27;s authority under ISM 5.2 and SOLAS V/34-1 protects it from commercial pressure.<\/li><li>Keep the charterer and agent informed.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>It&#x27;s beyond normal limits; windage rising with wind speed squared; leeway and channel width; tug bollard pull; heel reducing UKC; thruster ineffective; mooring loads; abort options; and the decision to delay, discussed with the pilot and port and recorded.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How would you estimate the wind force on your ship?","pa":"<p>Use the rule of thumb: <strong>F (t) ≈ A × V² ÷ 16,000<\/strong>, with A in m² and V in m/s, then compare it with the available tug pull and the moorings. 50 kn on a high-stack container ship gives a very large force. Expect to delay the transit or berthing.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q120","sec":7,"q":"Grounding: actions as Master; as C/O; concerns; refloating options and calculations.","tier":"red","rep":105,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>As Master: immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop engines<\/strong>. Don&#x27;t go straight <strong>astern<\/strong>: it may tear the bottom, damage the propeller and rudder, or pull her off into deep water with damage I haven&#x27;t found.<\/li><li>Sound the <strong>general alarm<\/strong>, close <strong>watertight doors<\/strong>, muster and <strong>check for injuries<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Show <strong>Rule 30(d)<\/strong> signals: anchor lights plus two all-round red lights, or three balls by day; and <strong>Rule 35(h)<\/strong> in restricted visibility: three separate strokes on the bell, rapid ringing for about 5 seconds, three strokes again, and the gong aft if 100 m or more.<\/li><li><strong>Fix the position<\/strong>, and check the <strong>tide<\/strong> (rising or falling) and the <strong>weather<\/strong> forecast.<\/li><li><strong>Save the VDR<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Consider <strong>anchoring<\/strong> or ballasting down to hold her and stop her swinging broadside or driving further on.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Inform<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>coastal State<\/strong>: HM Coastguard in the UK, VTS.<\/li><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Class<\/strong>, and its <strong>emergency response service<\/strong> if enrolled.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA<\/strong> or RO under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong> for a UK ship.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and <strong>H&amp;M<\/strong> insurers.<\/li><li><strong>SOPEP<\/strong> if there&#x27;s oil, with the MARPOL report.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>As C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Sound all tanks and bilges<\/strong>, then <strong>compare<\/strong> them with the <strong>departure soundings<\/strong>. Any change means ingress.<\/li><li><strong>Inspect<\/strong> for <strong>ingress<\/strong> and <strong>pollution<\/strong>: engine room, holds, void spaces.<\/li><li>Take <strong>soundings round the hull<\/strong> to find <strong>where<\/strong> she&#x27;s aground, the depth along each side, and the type of <strong>bottom<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19): identify the <strong>breached compartments<\/strong> and their <strong>watertight boundaries<\/strong>; <strong>close<\/strong> every door, valve, vent and opening around them using the controls the plan shows; <strong>isolate and pump<\/strong> with the bilge and ballast arrangements shown; and correct any list with the <strong>cross-flooding or transfer<\/strong> arrangements, after checking it on the loadicator.<\/li><li>Run <strong>stability<\/strong> and <strong>longitudinal strength<\/strong> on the loadicator, using the <strong>damage stability<\/strong> module or class emergency response.<\/li><li>Check for <strong>cargo damage<\/strong>, and any <strong>DG<\/strong> containers affected.<\/li><li>Prepare for refloating: what can be moved or removed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Concerns<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>falling tide<\/strong> increasing the <strong>ground reaction<\/strong> and the <strong>stress<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Loss of GM<\/strong> due to the upward force at the keel.<\/li><li><strong>Tearing<\/strong> the bottom or <strong>pounding<\/strong> in a swell.<\/li><li><strong>Swinging broadside<\/strong> onto the shore.<\/li><li><strong>Pollution<\/strong> from breached fuel tanks.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Refloating options<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>rising tide<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Discharge or transfer<\/strong> ballast, fuel or cargo, to reduce the draught at the grounded point or change the trim.<\/li><li><strong>Kedge anchors<\/strong> laid out by boat or tug.<\/li><li><strong>Tugs<\/strong> or <strong>salvors<\/strong>, under <strong>LOF 2020<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Calculations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ground reaction<\/strong>: <strong>P = TPC × reduction in mean draught<\/strong> (cm), or <strong>P = (change of trim × MCTC) ÷ distance<\/strong> of the grounding point from the <strong>LCF<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Virtual loss of GM<\/strong> = <strong>P × KM ÷ Δ<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Mass to remove<\/strong> to refloat ≈ <strong>P<\/strong>, removed from near the grounding point for the best effect.<\/li><li><strong>Ingress<\/strong>: <strong>Q ≈ 4.43 × A × √h<\/strong> m³/s, with <strong>A<\/strong> the hole area in m² and <strong>h<\/strong> the head of water in metres. Multiply by <strong>1.025<\/strong> for tonnes of sea water. My notes [P1] used &quot;4.3A√D t/s&quot;; the physical formula is the one above.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stop, don&#x27;t go astern, alarm and WT doors, signals, VDR, inform everyone including class and MAIB; the C/O&#x27;s soundings, hull soundings, the damage control plan (SOLAS II-1/19) to close boundaries, pump and correct list, and damage stability; the concerns on a falling tide; the refloating options; and the ground reaction, loss of GM and ingress formulas.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why might refloating at the first high water be the wrong decision?","pa":"<p>Because you don&#x27;t yet know the damage. The hull may be holed and only <strong>sealed by the seabed<\/strong>, so she could flood or lose stability once afloat. Assess first: soundings, tank checks, stability afloat, tides, weather and salvage help. Then refloat, maybe at a later, higher tide or with tugs, or after lightening.<\/p>","asked":["You have gone aground, actions?","Your vessel has grounded what are your actions?","How will you refloat the vessel?","Aground right Ahead?","Grounding action as a chief officer?"],"ex":[["Michele Moro",33],["Nayyer Naqvi",17],["Siddhartha Paul",7],["Zia Ul Haq",7],["Khademul Insan",6]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q121","sec":7,"q":"After collision you reach port — actions as C/O?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>Arriving with collision damage, my priorities as C/O are <strong>safety, evidence, survey, insurers and cargo<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>1. Safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stability and strength<\/strong> checked for the <strong>damaged condition<\/strong>, with the loadicator or class emergency response.<\/li><li><strong>Moorings<\/strong> suited to the damaged condition and the weather.<\/li><li><strong>Continue soundings<\/strong> and keep a <strong>pollution watch<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Keep the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19) in use: damaged compartments isolated, watertight boundaries and openings closed, and the pumping arrangements ready.<\/li><li><strong>Restrict access<\/strong> to the damaged area, with barriers and warning signs.<\/li><li>Keep <strong>cargo operations<\/strong> in the damaged area <strong>stopped<\/strong> until the surveyor clears it.<\/li><li>Fire watch if there&#x27;s damage near fuel or electrical systems.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Evidence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Preserve<\/strong> the <strong>VDR<\/strong> data, <strong>logbooks<\/strong>, <strong>ECDIS<\/strong> and radar records, the <strong>engine data logger<\/strong>, the <strong>bell book<\/strong>, the <strong>charts<\/strong> and <strong>photos<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t alter records<\/strong>. Corrections only as new dated entries.<\/li><li>The <strong>OLB entry<\/strong> is made by the Master.<\/li><li>Keep damaged parts, such as paint scrapings from the other ship.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Investigators<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong> may attend for a UK ship. Inspectors can <strong>require documents<\/strong> and <strong>interviews<\/strong>, and can seize records.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB doesn&#x27;t apportion blame<\/strong>. Its findings aren&#x27;t used to decide liability.<\/li><li>A <strong>coastal-State<\/strong> investigation is also possible, and the other ship&#x27;s flag State may investigate.<\/li><li>The MCA may investigate for offences separately.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Survey<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Class surveyor<\/strong> for the damage: likely an <strong>additional survey<\/strong>, and possibly a <strong>condition of class<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA or RO<\/strong> decides on the <strong>statutory certificates<\/strong> (SOLAS I/11(c)). Certificates <strong>aren&#x27;t automatically invalid<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Expect <strong>PSC<\/strong> interest if we&#x27;re in a foreign port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Insurers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>H&amp;M<\/strong> surveyor for our hull damage, and the <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> correspondent and <strong>lawyer<\/strong> for liability to the other ship.<\/li><li><strong>Crew statements<\/strong> are taken with <strong>legal advice<\/strong>. Crew should be truthful with investigators but not discuss the collision with the other ship&#x27;s people or the press.<\/li><li><strong>Make no admission of liability<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The Master <strong>notes protest<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Inspect damaged containers<\/strong> and <strong>DG<\/strong>, especially any <strong>leakage<\/strong>, using the EmS and with PPE.<\/li><li><strong>Joint cargo surveys<\/strong> with the cargo interests&#x27; surveyors.<\/li><li><strong>Inform shippers<\/strong> through the agent.<\/li><li>A <strong>restow plan<\/strong> if boxes have to be moved for repairs.<\/li><li>Claims will follow, so record the condition of each box.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Repairs<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Temporary repairs<\/strong> approved by <strong>class<\/strong> before sailing.<\/li><li>Permanent repairs planned with the company and class, with a date.<\/li><li><strong>Record everything<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. The crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A debrief, welfare support for those involved, and rest. The OOW may be shaken.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stability and moorings, soundings, restricted access; preserving the VDR and records without alteration; the MAIB not apportioning blame; class and the MCA deciding on certificates; insurers with no admission of liability; cargo surveys including DG; and class-approved temporary repairs before sailing.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The other ship's lawyer comes aboard and asks to see your logbook. Do you show it?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> Refer him to the <strong>company and P&amp;I<\/strong>. Give only the information required by <strong>s.92<\/strong>: the ship&#x27;s name, port of registry, and last and next ports. Make no statements without the P&amp;I lawyer.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q122","sec":7,"q":"Collision with a fishing vessel — actions as Master and as C/O, forward damage.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Master: immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop<\/strong>, sound the <strong>general alarm<\/strong>, close <strong>WT doors<\/strong> and <strong>muster<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fix the position<\/strong> and <strong>save the VDR<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t back out at once<\/strong> if the bow is <strong>embedded<\/strong> in the fishing vessel. <strong>Pulling out could sink her quickly<\/strong>. Hold her with gentle ahead power, if safe, while her crew are taken off.<\/li><li><strong>Search and rescue<\/strong>: the fishing crew may be in the water.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Launch the <strong>rescue boat<\/strong> if it&#x27;s safe.<\/li><li>Throw <strong>lifebuoys<\/strong>, rig scrambling nets and the pilot ladder.<\/li><li>Send a <strong>Mayday relay<\/strong>, or alert <strong>HMCG<\/strong> or the <strong>RCC<\/strong> by <strong>DSC<\/strong> and VHF.<\/li><li>Keep a lookout and mark the position on ECDIS.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Under <strong>MSA 1995 s.92<\/strong>: <strong>render assistance<\/strong>, <strong>stand by<\/strong> until no more help is needed, and give the <strong>ship&#x27;s name, port of registry, last port and next port<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Show the right signals: NUC if we can&#x27;t manoeuvre, and sound signals in restricted visibility.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Notify<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>coastal State<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong> as soon as practicable, by the quickest means.<\/li><li><strong>Class<\/strong> and the <strong>MCA<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and <strong>H&amp;M<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Make the <strong>OLB entries<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>C/O: forward damage<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Lead the damage control party<\/strong>, using the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19): the watertight boundaries forward, the openings and their closing controls, and the pumping arrangements for the forepeak and forward spaces.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>collision bulkhead<\/strong>, the <strong>forepeak<\/strong>, the <strong>chain locker<\/strong> and the <strong>bosun&#x27;s store<\/strong>, and <strong>sound<\/strong> the forward tanks.<\/li><li>If the <strong>forepeak is flooding<\/strong>, keep the <strong>collision bulkhead intact<\/strong>: don&#x27;t open anything in it, and check for leaks through it.<\/li><li><strong>Recalculate trim, stability and strength<\/strong> with the <strong>forepeak flooded<\/strong>. The ship will trim by the head.<\/li><li>Consider <strong>transferring ballast<\/strong> aft to <strong>correct trim<\/strong>, if the stability and strength allow.<\/li><li>Check for <strong>oil pollution<\/strong> (SOPEP).<\/li><li>Check for <strong>fire<\/strong> and damage to electrical systems forward.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>anchors<\/strong>: they may be damaged, or needed in an emergency.<\/li><li>Once she&#x27;s stable, <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> to limit the <strong>pressure on the damaged bow<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Report the findings to the Master.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Evidence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep the logs, ECDIS and radar data.<\/li><li><strong>Statements<\/strong> from the OOW and lookout.<\/li><li><strong>Drug and alcohol<\/strong> test of the OOW and lookout under the company policy.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>Note of Protest<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><li>The fishing vessel&#x27;s flag and the coastal State may investigate.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Don&#x27;t back out if embedded, rescue the fishing crew, s.92 duties, the notifications including MAIB, class and MCA; and the C/O&#x27;s damage control using the damage control plan at the collision bulkhead and forepeak, stability with the forepeak flooded, trim correction, pollution check and reduced speed.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Under s.92, when are you allowed to leave the scene?","pa":"<p>Only when the other vessel <strong>needs no more help<\/strong>: the RCC releases you, or her Master says so. You must first have given your particulars. You may leave earlier if standing by would <strong>endanger your own ship<\/strong>, crew or passengers.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q123","sec":7,"q":"Master's statutory duties after a collision?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>Merchant Shipping Act 1995 s.92<\/strong>. In every collision between ships, the <strong>Master<\/strong> of each ship must, <strong>so far as he can without danger<\/strong> to his own ship, crew and passengers:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Render assistance<\/strong> to the other ship, her master, crew and passengers, as is practicable and necessary to save them from any danger caused by the collision.<\/li><li><strong>Stay by<\/strong> the other ship until he has ascertained that she needs <strong>no further assistance<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Give<\/strong> the master of the other ship the <strong>name of his own ship<\/strong>, her <strong>port of registry<\/strong>, and the <strong>port she came from<\/strong> and the <strong>port she&#x27;s bound for<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Failing to do this<\/strong> without reasonable cause is an <strong>offence<\/strong>, and the Master can be fined and his certificate dealt with.<\/p><p><strong>Related duties<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MSA 1995 s.93<\/strong>: the duty to assist ships and persons in distress.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS V/33<\/strong> and <strong>UNCLOS Article 98<\/strong>: proceed with all speed to the help of persons in distress at sea.<\/li><li><strong>Salvage Convention 1989 Article 10<\/strong>, given effect by <strong>MSA 1995 s.224 and Schedule 11<\/strong>: render assistance to any person in danger of being lost at sea.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Reporting and recording<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Report to the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> as soon as practicable by the quickest means, under the <strong>MS (Accident Reporting and Investigation) Regs 2026<\/strong> and MGN 564 Amd 2.<\/li><li>Make <strong>OLB<\/strong> entries.<\/li><li>Report to <strong>class and the flag<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Inform the <strong>company<\/strong>, the <strong>DPA<\/strong> and the <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> club.<\/li><li>Show the correct <strong>COLREG signals<\/strong> if NUC or aground as a result.<\/li><li>Inform the <strong>coastal State<\/strong> if there&#x27;s pollution or danger to navigation.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Preserve evidence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Save the VDR<\/strong> immediately.<\/li><li>Keep the <strong>logs<\/strong>, <strong>ECDIS<\/strong>, <strong>radar<\/strong> data, <strong>charts<\/strong>, engine data, and <strong>statements<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t alter anything.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Liability<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Master <strong>mustn&#x27;t admit liability<\/strong>, but must give the information s.92 requires.<\/li><li>He makes a <strong>Note of Protest<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><li>The company&#x27;s lawyers and the P&amp;I club handle the claims. Liability is apportioned by the courts under the Collision Convention 1910 and the MSA, by degree of fault.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>COLREGs<\/strong><\/p><p>The Master&#x27;s conduct before the collision is judged by the COLREGs, applied in the UK by the Distress Signals and Prevention of Collisions Regs 1996 and MSN 1781. After the collision, s.92 governs.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MSA 1995 s.92 word for word: assist, stand by, and give name, port of registry, last and next port, so far as he can without danger to his own ship; an offence not to; and the related duties: SOLAS V/33, the Salvage Convention Art 10, MAIB, SOLAS I/11(c), OLB and evidence.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Does s.92 apply if your ship isn't damaged at all?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes.<\/strong> <strong>s.92<\/strong> applies to the Master of each ship in a collision, whether or not his own ship is damaged. He must help, stand by and exchange details, so far as he can <strong>without danger to his own ship<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q124","sec":7,"q":"Hull breach — actions, who to inform, certificates affected?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Sound the <strong>general alarm<\/strong> and close <strong>WT doors<\/strong>. Muster the <strong>damage control party<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Locate the breach<\/strong> by <strong>soundings<\/strong>, <strong>bilge alarms<\/strong>, water ingress alarms, and <strong>inspection<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the compartment: close valves, doors and ventilation.<\/li><li><strong>Pump<\/strong> with the <strong>bilge<\/strong> or <strong>ballast<\/strong> pumps and <strong>portable pumps<\/strong>. Compare the ingress rate with the pumping capacity, using Q ≈ 4.43 × A × √h.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong>, and <strong>alter course<\/strong> to put the <strong>damage on the lee side<\/strong> and reduce the water pressure on it.<\/li><li><strong>Damage stability<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>use the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> and <strong>booklet<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19) and the <strong>loadicator&#x27;s damage module<\/strong>;<\/li><li>check <strong>list, trim, freeboard<\/strong> and <strong>strength<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>counter-flood<\/strong> or <strong>transfer<\/strong> ballast if needed, with care, because counter-flooding reduces reserve buoyancy.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Temporary repairs<\/strong>: <strong>shoring<\/strong>, <strong>patches<\/strong>, a collision mat, wedges, a <strong>cement box<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Consider a <strong>place of refuge<\/strong>, <strong>salvage<\/strong> under <strong>LOF 2020<\/strong>, and the <strong>SOPEP<\/strong> if a <strong>fuel tank<\/strong> is breached.<\/li><li>Prepare <strong>LSA<\/strong> and consider an <strong>urgency or distress<\/strong> message.<\/li><li><strong>Save the VDR<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Inform<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>coastal State<\/strong>: HM Coastguard in UK waters.<\/li><li><strong>Class<\/strong>, and its <strong>emergency response service<\/strong> if enrolled.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA<\/strong> and the <strong>RO<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong> for a UK ship.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and <strong>H&amp;M<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Charterers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>port State<\/strong> of the next port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Certificates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Safety Construction certificate<\/strong>, the <strong>Load Line<\/strong> certificate (watertight integrity) and <strong>class<\/strong> are the most affected.<\/li><li>The <strong>IOPP<\/strong> is affected if there&#x27;s pollution or oil tanks are breached.<\/li><li>The <strong>SEC<\/strong> if LSA or FFE is damaged.<\/li><li>They&#x27;re <strong>not automatically invalid<\/strong>. The surveyor decides on an <strong>additional survey<\/strong> and may impose a <strong>condition of class<\/strong>, with temporary repairs and a date for permanent repairs.<\/li><li>The <strong>SMC is not invalidated<\/strong>. I raise an <strong>ISM non-conformity<\/strong> and expect an investigation, and possibly an additional audit.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On a container ship, a flooded hold means cargo damage and possibly DG reactions with water. Check the DG manifest for water-reactive cargo in that hold.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Logbook and OLB entries, times, soundings and actions.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Alarm, WT doors, find and isolate the breach, pump, reduce speed and put the damage to leeward, damage stability with the damage control booklet, temporary repairs, refuge or salvage, SOPEP; the list of who to inform; and the certificates affected but not automatically invalid, with the SMC not invalidated.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you calculate the flow rate through the breach?","pa":"<p><strong>Q ≈ 0.6 × A × √(2gh)<\/strong> m³/s, with A = hole area (m²) and h = depth of the hole below the waterline (m). For 0.1 m² at 4 m: Q ≈ 0.53 m³/s, about 1,900 m³/h. Compare that with your pump capacity.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q125","sec":7,"q":"Water ingress alarm forward in rough seas (bulk carrier).","tier":"orange","rep":16,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS XII/12<\/strong> requires <strong>water level detectors<\/strong> on bulk carriers:<\/p><ul><li>in <strong>each cargo hold<\/strong>, with a <strong>pre-alarm<\/strong> at <strong>0.5 m<\/strong> above the inner bottom, and a <strong>main alarm<\/strong> at not less than <strong>15%<\/strong> of the hold depth but <strong>not more than 2 m<\/strong>;<\/li><li>in <strong>ballast tanks forward of the collision bulkhead<\/strong>, and in <strong>dry or void spaces<\/strong> forward of the foremost hold.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS XII/13<\/strong> requires a means of <strong>draining and pumping<\/strong> those forward spaces, operable from an accessible enclosed space or the bridge.<\/p><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Acknowledge<\/strong> and <strong>inform the Master<\/strong>. Note the <strong>time<\/strong> and <strong>which space<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t send crew forward on deck blindly<\/strong> in heavy seas. First <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> and <strong>alter course<\/strong> to cut down <strong>green water<\/strong> on the foredeck. Then verify, after a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Verify<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>other <strong>sensors<\/strong> and any <strong>CCTV<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>soundings<\/strong>, the <strong>forepeak<\/strong> and <strong>ballast<\/strong> readings, and <strong>trim<\/strong> changes;<\/li><li>the <strong>draught<\/strong> forward and the ship&#x27;s behaviour: is she sluggish, slow to rise to the sea?<\/li><li><strong>False alarms<\/strong> from <strong>sloshing<\/strong> or damaged sensors are common, but <strong>treat it as real until proven otherwise<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If ingress is confirmed<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>General alarm<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19): identify No. 1 hold&#x27;s <strong>watertight boundaries<\/strong> and openings, <strong>close<\/strong> every ventilator, air pipe and access around it, and use the <strong>bilge and dewatering<\/strong> arrangements it shows.<\/li><li>Start <strong>dewatering<\/strong> with the <strong>XII/13<\/strong> system and other pumps.<\/li><li>Check for <strong>hatch-cover<\/strong> or <strong>ventilator<\/strong> damage, the commonest cause of forward hold flooding in heavy weather.<\/li><li>Run <strong>damage stability and strength<\/strong> calculations.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>SOLAS XII/4<\/strong>: bulk carriers of <strong>150 m<\/strong> and over, of <strong>single side skin<\/strong> construction, carrying cargo of <strong>1,000 kg/m³<\/strong> or more, built on or after <strong>1 July 1999<\/strong>, must survive <strong>any one hold flooded<\/strong>; older ones must survive the <strong>foremost hold<\/strong> flooded.<\/li><li>Check the ship&#x27;s own damage stability data and the loadicator.<\/li><li>A flooded forward hold with heavy cargo causes large <strong>bending moments<\/strong> and <strong>shear forces<\/strong>, and can lead to the next bulkhead failing. That&#x27;s how bulk carriers have sunk very quickly.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong> further and keep the bow sheltered.<\/li><li><strong>Prepare LSA<\/strong>, and send an <strong>urgency message<\/strong> (Pan-Pan), or <strong>Mayday<\/strong> if the situation becomes grave.<\/li><li>Consider a <strong>place of refuge<\/strong> and <strong>salvage<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Report<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To the <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>, the <strong>coastal State<\/strong>, and <strong>class<\/strong>.<\/li><li>To the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> for a UK ship, and to the <strong>MCA or RO<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> in the logbook.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>The SOLAS XII requirements came after the loss of many bulk carriers in the 1990s, including the <strong>Derbyshire<\/strong> in 1980, through forward hold flooding in heavy weather.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS XII/12 alarm levels (0.5 m and 15% or 2 m), the forward spaces, XII/13 pumping; reduce speed and alter course before sending anyone forward; verify by other means; if confirmed, dewater, damage stability under XII/4, LSA ready and urgency message; and the reports.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is flooding of the foremost hold so dangerous on a bulk carrier?","pa":"<p>With heavy cargo, the flooded foremost hold trims her by the head and <strong>overloads the bulkhead<\/strong> between holds 1 and 2. It can <strong>collapse<\/strong>, causing progressive flooding and loss within minutes. That&#x27;s why SOLAS <strong>XII/4 and XII/12<\/strong> exist.<\/p>","asked":["Water Ingress alarm system?","Water ingress alarm in cargo hold during rough weather ?","As Chief Mate on watch, at 4:10 AM the water ingress alarm goes off. What are my actions?","Back to ILLC, you mentioned vents in the condition of assignment. How will the air vents of a ballast tank be protected from water ingress?","Chief Officer calls you up in early morning at 0200 hrs, that he is getting Water Ingress Alarm on WIS panel from forward bosun store, actions ?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",5],["Colum Leggett",2],["Muhammad Iqbal",2],["Ahsan Khan",2],["Ian Blair",1]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q126","sec":7,"q":"Beaching — when and how.","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>When<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Only as a last resort<\/strong>, when the ship is likely to <strong>sink or capsize<\/strong>, and beaching gives a <strong>better chance<\/strong> of saving <strong>life<\/strong>, the <strong>ship<\/strong> and <strong>cargo<\/strong>, and of <strong>limiting pollution<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>decision belongs to the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the company<\/strong> and the <strong>coastal State<\/strong> first if time allows.<\/li><li>In UK waters, the <strong>SOSREP<\/strong> may direct or approve it, and may have a view on the best location.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Choosing the beach<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Gently sloping<\/strong> sand or mud, <strong>free of rocks<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sheltered<\/strong> from the prevailing wind and sea, so she isn&#x27;t pounded.<\/li><li><strong>Away from environmentally sensitive<\/strong> areas, and away from <strong>traffic<\/strong> and channels.<\/li><li><strong>Reachable<\/strong> with the power and steering available.<\/li><li>With <strong>good holding ground offshore<\/strong> for <strong>kedging<\/strong> later.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>tidal range<\/strong> and the <strong>current<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Plan the approach at <strong>right angles<\/strong> to the beach, so she lands evenly along the keel.<\/li><li>Ideally go in on a <strong>rising tide<\/strong>, so there&#x27;s a chance to drive further up if needed, or to <strong>refloat<\/strong> later at high water. Some prefer to beach just after high water so she settles firmly as the tide falls; it depends on the plan for refloating.<\/li><li>Approach at <strong>slow speed<\/strong>, with <strong>soundings<\/strong> continuous.<\/li><li>Have <strong>both anchors ready<\/strong>. Lay an <strong>anchor off the beach<\/strong> on the way in if possible, to stop her <strong>broaching<\/strong> broadside and to help <strong>later refloating<\/strong> by heaving on it.<\/li><li>Once aground, <strong>ballast down<\/strong>: flood the double bottoms to hold her <strong>firmly<\/strong>, so she doesn&#x27;t <strong>pound<\/strong> or <strong>swing broadside<\/strong> with the tide or swell.<\/li><li><strong>Close<\/strong> all watertight openings, using the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19) to identify every boundary, door, valve and vent, and the pumping and cross-flooding arrangements.<\/li><li>Show <strong>Rule 30(d)<\/strong> signals, issue a <strong>SÉCURITÉ<\/strong>, and activate the <strong>SOPEP<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Make <strong>sounding rounds<\/strong> and <strong>stress checks<\/strong> regularly: she&#x27;s now supported unevenly.<\/li><li>Arrange <strong>salvage<\/strong> (LOF 2020) and the <strong>MAIB report<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>After beaching<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep the crew safe, with LSA ready.<\/li><li>Assess the damage, plan temporary repairs, and refloating with salvors.<\/li><li>Inform the company, class, MCA, P&amp;I and H&amp;M.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Last resort to save life, ship and environment, the Master&#x27;s decision with the coastal State and SOSREP informed; a gently sloping sheltered sandy beach away from sensitive areas; approach at right angles on a rising tide at slow speed, with an anchor laid off; ballast down to hold her; signals, SOPEP, soundings and salvage.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why ballast the ship down after beaching her?","pa":"<p>To hold her <strong>firmly on the bottom<\/strong>, so she doesn&#x27;t pound, move or drift with the tide and sea. That stops further damage. She can be refloated later by deballasting.<\/p>","asked":["HOW YOU WILL DO AN EMERGENCY BEACHING?","Beaching- considerations, bow or stern?","The vessel has now touched the beach on the bow, how will this affect the ships stability?","I am master on a vessel that has beached, what are my actions?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",2],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",2],["Danny Hawkins",1]],"yrs":"2022–2023"},{"id":"Q127","sec":7,"q":"Place of refuge.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>place of refuge<\/strong> is a place where a ship in need of assistance can take action to <strong>stabilise her condition<\/strong>, <strong>reduce hazards<\/strong> to navigation, and <strong>protect human life and the environment<\/strong>. It may be a port, a sheltered anchorage or a stretch of coast.<\/p><p><strong>IMO guidance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Resolution A.949(23)<\/strong>: Guidelines on places of refuge for ships in need of assistance.<\/li><li><strong>Resolution A.950(23)<\/strong>: sets up <strong>Maritime Assistance Services (MAS)<\/strong> as the coastal State&#x27;s <strong>contact point<\/strong> for ships in need of assistance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In the UK<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The decision rests with the <strong>Secretary of State&#x27;s Representative (SOSREP)<\/strong>, who has <strong>intervention powers<\/strong> under the <strong>MSA 1995<\/strong> (Schedule 3A) to direct the ship, the owner, salvors and harbour authorities.<\/li><li>He decides on a <strong>case-by-case risk assessment<\/strong>.<\/li><li>There&#x27;s <strong>no fixed published list<\/strong> of refuges, so each case is assessed on its merits.<\/li><li><strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong> acts as the <strong>MAS<\/strong> contact point.<\/li><li>Harbour authorities can be directed to accept a ship.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The Master&#x27;s actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Assess<\/strong> the situation: damage, stability, cargo and DG, pollution risk, crew safety, weather forecast.<\/li><li><strong>Contact<\/strong> the coastal State&#x27;s <strong>MAS<\/strong> or <strong>MRCC<\/strong> <strong>early<\/strong>, with <strong>full information<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>ship details and <strong>position<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>nature of damage<\/strong> and what&#x27;s been done;<\/li><li><strong>cargo<\/strong>, <strong>DG<\/strong> and <strong>bunkers<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>stability<\/strong> and strength;<\/li><li><strong>crew<\/strong> numbers and condition;<\/li><li><strong>salvage<\/strong> and <strong>towage<\/strong> arrangements;<\/li><li><strong>insurance cover<\/strong>, including the <strong>Bunkers<\/strong>, <strong>CLC<\/strong> or <strong>Wreck Removal<\/strong> certificates and P&amp;I details.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Engage salvors<\/strong> (LOF 2020) and the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> early, because they&#x27;ll negotiate with the coastal State.<\/li><li><strong>Follow<\/strong> the coastal State&#x27;s instructions.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What the coastal State may do<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Accept<\/strong> the request, with or without <strong>conditions<\/strong>: a tug escort, a security or <strong>bond<\/strong> for costs, <strong>lightering<\/strong> of fuel or cargo, a survey before entry.<\/li><li><strong>Refuse<\/strong> it, if the risk is greater inside than outside.<\/li><li>Direct the ship to another location.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The balance<\/strong><\/p><p>The coastal State balances the <strong>risk of letting the ship in<\/strong> against the <strong>risk of leaving her at sea<\/strong>. The Prestige (2002), refused refuge and later sank off Spain, showed the cost of refusing.<\/p><p><strong>The Master&#x27;s duty<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep the crew safe, prevent pollution, and cooperate with the authorities.<\/li><li>Record the requests, the responses and the times.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The definition, A.949(23) and A.950(23), SOSREP&#x27;s decision in the UK with intervention powers and no fixed list, HMCG as the MAS, the information the Master gives including insurance, salvors early, and that the coastal State balances the risks and may impose conditions.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can a coastal State refuse a damaged ship entry to a place of refuge?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes.<\/strong> There&#x27;s no absolute right of refuge. IMO <strong>A.949(23)<\/strong> guides the decision, and the state weighs the risks. In the UK, <strong>SOSREP<\/strong> decides.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q128","sec":7,"q":"Blackout after an engine-room fire.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. Emergency power<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the <strong>emergency generator<\/strong> has started and taken the <strong>emergency switchboard<\/strong>. <strong>SOLAS II-1/43<\/strong> requires it to start and supply automatically within <strong>45 seconds<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Steering gear<\/strong> on its <strong>alternative power supply<\/strong> where fitted (<strong>SOLAS II-1/29<\/strong>), also within 45 seconds.<\/li><li>Emergency lighting, the fire pump on the emergency switchboard, navigation lights, GMDSS and the general alarm should all be on emergency power.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Navigation safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Show <strong>NUC lights and shapes<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 27(a)<\/strong>) and sound <strong>signals<\/strong> in restricted visibility (Rule 35(c)).<\/li><li>Broadcast a <strong>SÉCURITÉ<\/strong>, or a <strong>PAN-PAN<\/strong> if the situation needs it, and <strong>inform VTS<\/strong> or the coastal State.<\/li><li><strong>Plot the drift<\/strong>, and if drifting into danger in <strong>anchorable depth<\/strong>, prepare and use the <strong>anchors<\/strong>. Without power, the windlass may not work, so letting go by the brake is possible but heaving up isn&#x27;t.<\/li><li>Arrange <strong>tug assistance<\/strong> if needed, and consider a towage or salvage agreement.<\/li><li>Keep a good lookout, and keep other ships informed by VHF.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. The fire comes first<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Confirm the fire is <strong>out or contained<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If a <strong>fixed CO₂ system<\/strong> has been released, the space stays <strong>sealed<\/strong> for the <strong>soak time<\/strong> recommended by the maker and the company, often many hours.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cooling<\/strong> and a <strong>fire watch<\/strong> on all six sides.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t restart ventilation<\/strong> or <strong>open the space prematurely<\/strong>, or it will <strong>re-ignite<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Restoring power<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Only after the <strong>Chief Engineer<\/strong> has assessed it and <strong>re-entry is controlled<\/strong>: the space checked for temperature, the <strong>atmosphere tested<\/strong>, and a <strong>BA team<\/strong> ready.<\/li><li><strong>Step-by-step load restoration<\/strong>: <strong>essential services<\/strong> first (steering, fire pumps, lighting, navigation), then the <strong>main engine auxiliaries<\/strong>, then the <strong>reefer load<\/strong> in stages.<\/li><li>Find the cause of the fire before restarting the equipment involved.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Reefer temperatures<\/strong>: record them, and restart the reefers in groups once power allows.<\/li><li><strong>DG<\/strong> stowed near the engine room: check for heat damage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Reporting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong>: for a UK ship, a fire is a marine casualty.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>OLB<\/strong> entries, and save the <strong>VDR<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An investigation of the fire and the blackout.<\/li><li>Recharge or replace the CO₂ and other systems used, and inspect the space before sailing.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The emergency generator within 45 seconds and steering on alternative power, NUC signals, Sécurité or Pan-Pan, plot the drift and anchors, tugs; the fire first, with CO₂ soak time, boundary cooling and no early re-entry; controlled power restoration with essential services first and reefers in stages; and the reports.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How long does the emergency generator have to supply, and what does it supply?","pa":"<p>For <strong>18 hours<\/strong> on a cargo ship (SOLAS II-1/43). It supplies:<\/p><ul><li>emergency lighting at muster stations, alleyways, machinery spaces and control stations;<\/li><li>navigation lights;<\/li><li>radio;<\/li><li>fire detection and the general alarm;<\/li><li>the <strong>emergency fire pump<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the steering gear: 30 min on ships of 10,000 GT and over, 10 min on others (II-1/29.14).<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["ACTION AS MASTER IN BLACK OUT… NOW EMERGENGY GENERATOR HAS STARTED .. WHAT ELSE"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"★8.1","sec":7,"q":"Grounded on a falling tide, no ingress. Do you try to refloat at once?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>No, not without assessing first.<\/strong><\/p><p>On a <strong>falling tide<\/strong>, an immediate attempt to go astern may:<\/p><ul><li><strong>tear the bottom<\/strong> plating as she&#x27;s dragged over the ground;<\/li><li><strong>damage the propeller and rudder<\/strong>;<\/li><li>pull her off into <strong>deeper water<\/strong> with damage I <strong>haven&#x27;t found<\/strong>, so she could flood and sink in deep water;<\/li><li>and probably <strong>won&#x27;t work<\/strong>, because she&#x27;s getting <strong>harder aground<\/strong> every minute as the tide falls.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Instead<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop engines<\/strong>, show <strong>signals<\/strong> (Rule 30(d)), <strong>save the VDR<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the coastal State, the <strong>company<\/strong>, <strong>class<\/strong>, the <strong>MCA<\/strong> (SOLAS I/11(c)), the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sound all tanks and bilges<\/strong> and <strong>compare<\/strong> with the <strong>departure soundings<\/strong>. <strong>&quot;No ingress&quot; must be confirmed, not assumed<\/strong>. Repeat the soundings regularly, because damage can open as the tide falls and the stress changes.<\/li><li><strong>Sound round the hull<\/strong> to find <strong>where<\/strong> she&#x27;s aground and what the <strong>bottom<\/strong> is: sand, mud or rock.<\/li><li>Keep the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19) ready: if any soundings change, it shows the <strong>watertight boundaries<\/strong> to close, the <strong>pumping<\/strong> arrangements, and how to correct a list with <strong>cross-flooding<\/strong> or transfer.<\/li><li><strong>Calculate<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the <strong>ground reaction<\/strong> as the tide falls: <strong>P = TPC × fall in mean draught<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the resulting <strong>loss of GM<\/strong>: <strong>P × KM ÷ Δ<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>longitudinal stress<\/strong>: a falling tide could make her <strong>hog or sag<\/strong> over a rock, with the risk of the hull failing.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Hold her in position<\/strong> if needed so she doesn&#x27;t <strong>swing broadside<\/strong>: <strong>ballast down<\/strong>, or lay <strong>anchors<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Plan to refloat<\/strong> at the <strong>next rising tide<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>work out how much <strong>draught must be reduced<\/strong> at the grounding point, by <strong>discharging<\/strong> or <strong>transferring<\/strong> ballast, fuel or cargo;<\/li><li>whether <strong>tugs<\/strong> or <strong>salvors<\/strong> are needed, under <strong>LOF 2020<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the time of high water and the <strong>height<\/strong> available.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Check she&#x27;ll be <strong>stable and seaworthy<\/strong> once afloat, including in the damaged condition if damage is found.<\/li><li><strong>Class<\/strong> may want an inspection, by divers or in dry dock, before she continues.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>When might an immediate attempt be justified?<\/strong><\/p><p>Only if the ship is in danger where she is, for example a rising sea that will pound her, or on a <strong>rising<\/strong> tide where she&#x27;s only just touched, with soundings confirming no damage. Even then, with the Master&#x27;s agreement and the stability checked.<\/p><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><p>Times, soundings, tide heights, calculations, and all communications.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No, not at once on a falling tide; confirm no ingress by soundings, with the damage control plan ready; sound round the hull; calculate the ground reaction, loss of GM and stress; hold her; plan the refloat for the next rising tide; and inform everyone including class and the MAIB.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What would make you decide to stay aground rather than refloat?","pa":"<p>Stay aground if:<\/p><ul><li>refloating would cause <strong>flooding or sinking<\/strong>, or she&#x27;d lack stability afloat;<\/li><li>weather or tide would put her in a <strong>worse position<\/strong>;<\/li><li>no tugs or salvage are ready;<\/li><li>refloating would make pollution worse;<\/li><li>salvors advise against it.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★8.2","sec":7,"q":"You lose 12 containers overboard in the Bay of Biscay. Reporting?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"SOLAS V/31–32 from 1 Jan 2026","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS V/31 and V/32<\/strong>, as amended by <strong>MSC.550(108)<\/strong>, in force <strong>1 January 2026<\/strong>. The UK applies them through the <strong>Safety of Navigation Regs 2020<\/strong> with ambulatory referencing.<\/p><p><strong>Without delay, the Master reports to<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Ships in the vicinity<\/strong>, by <strong>SÉCURITÉ<\/strong> broadcast on VHF and through the GMDSS.<\/li><li>The <strong>nearest coastal State<\/strong>, which in Biscay will be <strong>France or Spain<\/strong> depending on the position, through their MRCC (CROSS Étel or MRCC Finisterre, for example).<\/li><li>The <strong>flag State<\/strong>: the <strong>MCA<\/strong> for a UK ship.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The report includes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>position<\/strong>, or <strong>last known position<\/strong>, and the <strong>time<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>number<\/strong> of containers lost.<\/li><li>Whether they carried <strong>dangerous goods<\/strong>, with <strong>UN numbers<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The container <strong>type<\/strong>, <strong>size<\/strong> and <strong>condition<\/strong>, for example 40&#x27; high cube, reefer, damaged.<\/li><li>Any other information, such as the weather and the drift.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the Master can&#x27;t report<\/strong>, the <strong>company<\/strong> must. The <strong>flag State<\/strong> uploads the information to IMO <strong>GISIS<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Drifting containers<\/strong> sighted by <strong>any<\/strong> ship must also be reported.<\/p><p><strong>Other reports<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>MARPOL Protocol I<\/strong> and <strong>Annex III<\/strong>: the loss of <strong>harmful substances in packaged form<\/strong> (marine pollutants) must be reported to the <strong>coastal State<\/strong> without delay.<\/li><li><strong>MAIB<\/strong>: a UK ship should report it as soon as practicable under the <strong>Accident Reporting and Investigation Regs 2026<\/strong>, especially if there was structural damage or injury. <strong>Save the VDR<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>, the <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> club and the <strong>charterers<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>OLB<\/strong> and <strong>logbook<\/strong> entries.<\/li><li>The <strong>shippers&#x27; and consignees&#x27;<\/strong> details through the company, for the claims and for any hazard to the public if boxes wash ashore.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Check the remaining stacks<\/strong> and <strong>lashings<\/strong>, only when it&#x27;s safe to go on deck.<\/li><li><strong>Review stability<\/strong> with the boxes lost, and any damaged boxes hanging or leaning.<\/li><li>Look at <strong>parametric rolling<\/strong> or excessive accelerations as a <strong>cause<\/strong>, and review the speed and course.<\/li><li>Check for <strong>damage<\/strong> to hatch covers, lashing bridges and the hull.<\/li><li><strong>Photographs<\/strong> and records for the investigation and the insurers.<\/li><li>Inform the next port, and plan a survey.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Lost containers are a danger to navigation, especially to small craft, and DG containers are a pollution and safety hazard. The 2026 amendments came after several large losses in the Pacific and North Sea.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS V/31–32 as amended by MSC.550(108) from 1 January 2026, report to ships nearby, the nearest coastal State and the flag, with the position, number, DG with UN numbers, and the type and condition; the company if the Master can&#x27;t; MARPOL Protocol I for marine pollutants; the MAIB; and checking the remaining stacks and the cause.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who pays for recovering the lost containers, and under which convention could the coastal State act?","pa":"<p>The <strong>owner, through P&amp;I<\/strong>. Containers lost overboard are &quot;wreck&quot; under the <strong>Nairobi Wreck Removal Convention<\/strong>. The coastal state can require the owner to locate, mark and remove them if they&#x27;re a hazard in its convention area, and claim against the WRC insurance.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q129","sec":8,"q":"Sea areas A1–A4.","tier":"red","rep":51,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>GMDSS sea areas are defined in <strong>SOLAS IV/2<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the MS (Radio Installations) Regulations (verify the current version). The <strong>modernised Chapter IV<\/strong> has been in force since <strong>1 January 2024<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Sea area A1<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Within <strong>radio-telephone coverage<\/strong> of at least one <strong>VHF coast station<\/strong> with <strong>continuous DSC alerting<\/strong> on Ch 70.<\/li><li>In practice about <strong>20–30 nm<\/strong> from the station, but it depends on the <strong>declared coverage<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Sea area A2<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Outside A1<\/strong>, within coverage of an <strong>MF coast station<\/strong> with <strong>continuous DSC alerting<\/strong> on 2187.5 kHz.<\/li><li>Often around <strong>100–150 nm<\/strong>, again only where a State has <strong>declared<\/strong> it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Sea area A3<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Outside A1 and A2<\/strong>, within the <strong>coverage<\/strong> of a <strong>recognized mobile satellite service (RMSS)<\/strong> supported by the ship&#x27;s <strong>own terminal<\/strong>, with continuous alerting.<\/li><li>For <strong>Inmarsat<\/strong> geostationary satellites, roughly <strong>70°N to 70°S<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Since 2024 the rules <strong>no longer name Inmarsat only<\/strong>: <strong>Iridium<\/strong> is also recognised.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Sea area A4<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Everything else<\/strong>, mainly the <strong>polar regions<\/strong>.<\/li><li>With an <strong>Iridium<\/strong> terminal, A3 coverage is effectively <strong>global<\/strong>, so A4 hardly applies to that ship. An Inmarsat ship in the Arctic is still in A4, and needs HF DSC.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Important points<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ranges in my notes are <strong>rough guides<\/strong> only. A1 and A2 <strong>exist only where a coastal State has declared them<\/strong>, and many coasts have none. Check the <strong>ALRS Vol 5 (GMDSS)<\/strong> and the <strong>GMDSS Master Plan<\/strong> on IMO <strong>GISIS<\/strong> for the actual limits.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s <strong>equipment<\/strong> and its <strong>Safety Radio Certificate<\/strong> (the <strong>Record of Equipment<\/strong>, Form R) state which <strong>sea areas<\/strong> she&#x27;s fitted for. <strong>The voyage must stay within them.<\/strong><\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Equipment summary<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>All areas<\/strong>: VHF DSC with a Ch 70 watch, NAVTEX, MSI receiver (EGC), EPIRB, SART or AIS-SART, two-way VHF for survival craft.<\/li><li><strong>A2<\/strong>: add <strong>MF DSC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>A3<\/strong>: add <strong>MF/HF DSC<\/strong> or an <strong>RMSS terminal<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>A4<\/strong>: <strong>MF/HF DSC<\/strong>, or an RMSS that covers the area such as Iridium.<\/li><li><strong>Maintenance<\/strong> in A3 and A4 by two of three methods: duplication, shore-based maintenance, at-sea maintenance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In the passage plan<\/strong><\/p><p>I note which sea areas the voyage passes through, the coast stations and MSI services, and check our equipment covers them.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS IV/2, A1 VHF DSC, A2 MF DSC, A3 the recognized mobile satellite service with Iridium now included, A4 the rest; that A1 and A2 exist only where declared, checked in ALRS Vol 5 and GISIS; and that the SRC shows which sea areas the ship can trade in.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your ship is certified for A1+A2+A3 — can she trade to Svalbard?","pa":"<p>Depends on the satellite system. Svalbard is near or beyond the edge of <strong>geostationary (Inmarsat)<\/strong> cover, which is effectively sea area <strong>A4<\/strong>. If her recognised satellite service is <strong>Iridium<\/strong>, A3 cover reaches the polar region. She&#x27;d also need the <strong>Polar Code<\/strong> certificate and equipment. Check the SRC record of equipment and ALRS.<\/p>","asked":["In which sea area would you have NAVTEX?","You talk about NAVTEX where do you the information about the NAVTEX Stations?","You received a distress alert on VHF DSC?","Received dsc alert on hf 6 mhz. Action?","GMDSS areas what are they?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",9],["Alec Keep",8],["Nayyer Naqvi",6],["Kaiser Ali",5],["Colum Leggett",5]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q130","sec":8,"q":"OOW action on receiving a DSC distress alert.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The procedure follows <strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong> and the <strong>IMO DSC distress flowcharts<\/strong>, which should be posted at the GMDSS console. The duty to assist is <strong>SOLAS V/33<\/strong>, applied in the UK through the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020.<\/p><p><strong>VHF or MF DSC distress alert<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the alert: who (MMSI), <strong>position<\/strong>, <strong>nature<\/strong> of distress, <strong>time<\/strong>, in the <strong>GMDSS log<\/strong>, and <strong>call the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Set a watch<\/strong> on the associated <strong>voice channel<\/strong>: <strong>VHF Ch 16<\/strong> or <strong>MF 2182 kHz<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Plot<\/strong> the position and work out the <strong>time to reach it<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Wait a short time<\/strong> for a <strong>coast station or RCC<\/strong> to acknowledge, especially in A1 or A2. Coast stations are the main acknowledgers.<\/li><li>If the casualty is <strong>nearby<\/strong> and we <strong>can help<\/strong>, <strong>acknowledge by voice<\/strong> (R/T) on Ch 16 or 2182 kHz: &quot;Mayday, [casualty&#x27;s MMSI/name] three times, this is [our ship] three times, received Mayday.&quot; Inform the <strong>RCC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t acknowledge by DSC<\/strong> unless <strong>instructed<\/strong> by an RCC or coast station. The exception is where the flowchart allows it because <strong>no coast station has acknowledged<\/strong> and the alert keeps repeating, and we&#x27;re in a position to help.<\/li><li>If no one has acknowledged, <strong>relay ashore<\/strong> by any means to an <strong>RCC or coast station<\/strong>: DSC distress relay addressed to that station, voice, or satellite. In UK waters that&#x27;s <strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Proceed to assist if able, under SOLAS V/33.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>HF DSC distress alert<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>We may be <strong>thousands of miles away<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Never acknowledge by DSC or voice<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Watch<\/strong> the associated <strong>HF frequency<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Relay<\/strong> to an <strong>RCC<\/strong> if no coast station acknowledges within about <strong>5 minutes<\/strong>, by HF DSC relay addressed to that station or by satellite.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Satellite alert received by EGC<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Follow the RCC instructions in the message.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Follow the RCC&#x27;s instructions<\/strong>: they coordinate the SAR.<\/li><li>Keep the log, with times of every action.<\/li><li>If we can&#x27;t help, or another ship is better placed, the RCC may release us. If we don&#x27;t go, the Master must record the reasons in the logbook (SOLAS V/33.1).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>False alerts<\/strong><\/p><p>If our own ship sends a false alert, cancel it immediately by DSC (where possible) and by voice on the associated channel: &quot;All stations, this is [ship], cancel my distress alert of [time].&quot;<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Record and call the Master, watch Ch 16 or 2182, plot and assess, give coast stations time to acknowledge, acknowledge by voice if nearby and able to help, not by DSC unless told, relay if no one acknowledges, HF never acknowledged, and follow the RCC; with SOLAS V/33 and IAMSAR Vol III.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"You receive a false alert from your own ship by mistake. What do you do?","pa":"<p><strong>Cancel at once.<\/strong> Reset or switch off the DSC. Send a voice cancellation on the associated frequency (Ch 16 or 2182 kHz): &quot;All stations ×3, this is [ship, call sign, MMSI], cancel my distress alert of [time]&quot;. Inform the RCC, and log it.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q131","sec":8,"q":"GMDSS tests and equipment.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Carriage under <strong>SOLAS IV<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the Radio Installations Regulations (verify). The record of what&#x27;s fitted is the <strong>SRC Record of Equipment (Form R)<\/strong>, and every test goes in the <strong>GMDSS radio log<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Equipment by sea area<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>All ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>VHF<\/strong> with <strong>DSC<\/strong> on Ch 70 and a <strong>continuous Ch 70 watch<\/strong>, plus Ch 16 and 13.<\/li><li><strong>NAVTEX<\/strong> receiver.<\/li><li><strong>EGC<\/strong> (or RMSS equivalent) receiver for MSI where NAVTEX doesn&#x27;t cover.<\/li><li><strong>EPIRB<\/strong> (406 MHz Cospas-Sarsat).<\/li><li><strong>SART<\/strong> or <strong>AIS-SART<\/strong>: one each side on ships of 500 GT and over.<\/li><li><strong>Portable VHFs<\/strong> for survival craft: three on ships of 500 GT and over.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A2<\/strong>: add <strong>MF<\/strong> with <strong>DSC<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>A3<\/strong>: add <strong>MF/HF<\/strong> with <strong>DSC<\/strong>, or a <strong>recognised mobile satellite terminal<\/strong> (Inmarsat or Iridium).<\/p><p><strong>A3/A4 maintenance<\/strong>: by <strong>two of three<\/strong> methods: <strong>duplication<\/strong> of equipment, <strong>shore-based maintenance<\/strong>, <strong>at-sea maintenance<\/strong> (a qualified electronic officer).<\/p><p><strong>Power<\/strong>: a <strong>reserve source of energy<\/strong> (batteries) to run the equipment for 1 hour (or 6 hours without an emergency generator).<\/p><p><strong>Operators<\/strong>: GMDSS <strong>GOC<\/strong> holders, and a designated person for distress communications.<\/p><p><strong>Tests<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Daily<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>DSC internal test<\/strong> without radiating.<\/li><li><strong>Battery<\/strong> voltage check.<\/li><li><strong>Printer<\/strong> and paper checks.<\/li><li><strong>Log<\/strong> entries.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Weekly<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>DSC test call<\/strong> with a coast station on <strong>MF/HF<\/strong> where available.<\/li><li><strong>Reserve battery<\/strong> tested on load, where it&#x27;s not tested daily.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Monthly<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>EPIRB self-test<\/strong>, and check the <strong>HRU<\/strong> and <strong>battery<\/strong> expiry dates, the lanyard and markings.<\/li><li><strong>SART self-test<\/strong> and battery date.<\/li><li><strong>Portable VHF<\/strong> checks, and their battery dates.<\/li><li>Battery <strong>terminals<\/strong> and <strong>electrolyte<\/strong> level if applicable.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Annually<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Radio survey<\/strong> for the <strong>SRC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>EPIRB annual test<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS IV/15.9<\/strong>), and <strong>shore-based maintenance<\/strong> at intervals <strong>not exceeding 5 years<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Documents and posting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>GMDSS log<\/strong>, the <strong>Form R<\/strong>, operator certificates, the radio licence, the <strong>distress procedure flowcharts<\/strong> at the console, and the call sign and MMSI displayed.<\/li><li>The <strong>ALRS<\/strong> volumes and the <strong>ITU<\/strong> publications.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The equipment by sea area, the reserve source of energy, the maintenance options for A3 and A4, and the daily, weekly, monthly and annual tests, with the EPIRB annual test and 5-yearly shore-based maintenance, all recorded in the GMDSS log.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you do if the DSC daily test fails before sailing?","pa":"<p>Don&#x27;t sail until it&#x27;s fixed or there&#x27;s an alternative. <strong>SOLAS IV/15.8<\/strong>: the ship must be able to carry out all distress functions. Use a duplicated set if fitted, get a technician or the shore maintenance contractor, tell the company, and agree with the flag or RO if needed. Log it.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q132","sec":8,"q":"EPIRB: how does it work, frequency, checks?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>How it works<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The float-free satellite <strong>EPIRB<\/strong> transmits on <strong>406 MHz<\/strong> to the <strong>Cospas-Sarsat<\/strong> system: <strong>low-orbit<\/strong> (LEOSAR), <strong>geostationary<\/strong> (GEOSAR) and now <strong>medium-orbit (MEOSAR)<\/strong> satellites, which give almost instant detection and location.<\/li><li>The signal carries a <strong>unique coded ID<\/strong> (MMSI or serial number) linked to the <strong>registration database<\/strong>. For UK ships, the <strong>UK Beacon Registry<\/strong> run by HM Coastguard.<\/li><li>A built-in <strong>GNSS<\/strong> gives an <strong>encoded position<\/strong> in the alert.<\/li><li>Satellite <strong>Doppler<\/strong> or MEOSAR can also fix the position without the GNSS.<\/li><li>A <strong>121.5 MHz<\/strong> homing signal guides SAR aircraft and units to it. Newer beacons also transmit <strong>AIS<\/strong>, so nearby ships see it on their AIS display.<\/li><li>The alert goes through <strong>ground stations<\/strong> (LUTs) to a <strong>Mission Control Centre (MCC)<\/strong> and then the <strong>RCC<\/strong> responsible for the area. In the UK, <strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It transmits for at least <strong>48 hours<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Activation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Manually<\/strong>, by the switch.<\/li><li><strong>Automatically<\/strong>: the <strong>hydrostatic release unit<\/strong> frees it at about <strong>2–4 m<\/strong> depth as the ship sinks, and it <strong>floats free<\/strong> and activates in the water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Checks<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Monthly<\/strong> (company SMS and the maker&#x27;s instructions)<\/p><ol><li><strong>Self-test<\/strong>, without sending a live alert.<\/li><li><strong>Battery<\/strong> expiry date, and <strong>HRU<\/strong> expiry date. A disposable HRU is typically <strong>2 years<\/strong>; follow the manufacturer.<\/li><li>The <strong>lanyard<\/strong> is <strong>not tied to the ship<\/strong>, so it can float free.<\/li><li><strong>Free to float<\/strong>: nothing obstructing it, the bracket in good order.<\/li><li>Correct <strong>markings<\/strong>: ship&#x27;s name, MMSI or call sign, and <strong>registration<\/strong> details.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Annual test<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS IV/15.9<\/strong>)<\/p><ul><li>At the radio survey, or within 3 months of it: an <strong>approved test<\/strong> of <strong>frequency<\/strong>, <strong>coding<\/strong>, <strong>power output<\/strong> and <strong>registration<\/strong>, with test equipment that doesn&#x27;t send a live alert.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Shore-based maintenance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At intervals <strong>not exceeding 5 years<\/strong>, by an approved facility: battery change, full test.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Registration<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Must be kept <strong>up to date<\/strong> with the ship and <strong>contact<\/strong> details. Change it when the ship changes name, owner or flag.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If it goes off accidentally<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Don&#x27;t switch it off and hide it<\/strong>. Leave it on and <strong>inform the RCC<\/strong> (HM Coastguard) immediately so they can cancel the alert, then switch it off. Record it.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>406 MHz to Cospas-Sarsat, the coded ID and registration, GNSS position, 121.5 MHz homing and AIS, manual and HRU activation, the monthly self-test and dates, the annual test under SOLAS IV/15.9, 5-yearly shore-based maintenance, and what to do with a false alert.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The EPIRB is activated by accident — what do you do?","pa":"<p>Tell the nearest <strong>RCC or coast station<\/strong> immediately, by any means, that it&#x27;s a false alert. Switch the EPIRB off, log it and inform the company.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q133","sec":8,"q":"Helicopter operations: preparation, wind, deck party, publications, crash on deck, helicopter ditches.","tier":"red","rep":75,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Publications<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>ICS Guide to Helicopter/Ship Operations<\/strong>, with its <strong>checklist<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>company SMS<\/strong> procedure.<\/li><li>Any MCA guidance on helicopter operations (verify the current MGN).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Preparation<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>briefing<\/strong> for the deck party and the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Operating area<\/strong> cleared, <strong>loose items<\/strong> secured, <strong>aerials<\/strong> lowered or secured, rails and obstructions marked.<\/li><li><strong>Windsock<\/strong> or flags shown, so the pilot can see the wind.<\/li><li><strong>Lights<\/strong> at night: the area floodlit, but <strong>not dazzling<\/strong> the pilot.<\/li><li><strong>VHF contact<\/strong> agreed with the aircraft, often through the RCC first.<\/li><li><strong>RAM signals<\/strong> shown (Rule 27(b)) and a <strong>SÉCURITÉ<\/strong> broadcast.<\/li><li>On a <strong>container ship<\/strong>, the winching area is often the <strong>hatch tops<\/strong> or the <strong>bridge wing<\/strong>, as the pilot decides. There&#x27;s rarely a landing area.<\/li><li>Information to the RCC and the aircraft: position, course and speed, wind relative, the area, and the patient&#x27;s condition.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Wind<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Usually the pilot wants the <strong>relative wind about 30° on the port bow<\/strong> for a <strong>midships or aft<\/strong> area, so he can see the ship from the right-hand seat and has clear air (<strong>confirm with the pilot<\/strong>).<\/li><li>Keep a <strong>steady course and speed<\/strong> as agreed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Deck party<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Officer in charge<\/strong> with a <strong>radio<\/strong> to the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Hook handler<\/strong> in <strong>rubber gloves<\/strong> and <strong>helmet<\/strong>. Let the hook <strong>earth on deck first<\/strong> to discharge <strong>static<\/strong>. <strong>Never attach the hook to the ship<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fire party<\/strong> with <strong>foam<\/strong> and <strong>dry powder<\/strong>, <strong>hoses<\/strong> run out near but clear of the area, in <strong>fire suits<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Crash kit<\/strong>: axe, crowbar, cutters, ladder.<\/li><li><strong>Rescue boat<\/strong> ready.<\/li><li><strong>Patient<\/strong> prepared, with <strong>medical notes<\/strong> and passport, in a stretcher if needed.<\/li><li>Everyone in <strong>PPE<\/strong>, with no loose clothing or caps, and no smoking.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Hi-line<\/strong>: a weighted line lowered first to guide the hook: earth it, never make it fast, flake it clear.<\/p><p><strong>Crash on deck<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Sound the <strong>alarm<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Attack the fire<\/strong> with <strong>foam<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rescue<\/strong> the aircrew, using the crash rescue points and exits.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the <strong>RCC<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Ditching<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Launch the rescue boat<\/strong>, or <strong>manoeuvre<\/strong> to recover the crew.<\/li><li><strong>Mayday relay<\/strong> to the RCC.<\/li><li>Lifebuoys with lights in the water.<\/li><li>Use <strong>IAMSAR<\/strong> recovery procedures.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The ICS guide and IAMSAR, preparation including clearing the area, lights, VHF, signals and the wind about 30° on the port bow, the deck party with hook handling and static, the fire party with foam, and what to do if it crashes on deck or ditches.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why must the winch hook touch the deck before anyone handles it?","pa":"<p>The hook and cable build up <strong>static electricity<\/strong>. Letting it touch the deck <strong>earths<\/strong> it, so nobody gets a shock.<\/p>","asked":["How will you prepare the vessel for helicopter operations?","Hi line technique, what do you know about that?","Helicopter Evacuation Procedure ?","How would you prepare for a Medevac by helicopter?","How will you arrange for his repatriation by helicopter?"],"ex":[["Michele Moro",21],["Khademul Insan",20],["Nayyer Naqvi",18],["Examiner not named",10],["Doug Smith",8]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q134","sec":8,"q":"Master seriously ill — actions as C/O; TMAS; medevac.","tier":"red","rep":49,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. Immediate care<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>First aid<\/strong> using the <strong>Ship Captain&#x27;s Medical Guide<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>medical-care officer<\/strong> (STCW medical care certificate) takes charge of treatment.<\/li><li><strong>Record observations<\/strong>: pulse, breathing, temperature, blood pressure, consciousness level, and the times.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Radio medical advice: TMAS<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For a UK ship, contact <strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong>, who connect you to the <strong>UK TMAS<\/strong> doctors (<strong>MGN 623<\/strong>).<\/li><li>Use <strong>DSC/VHF<\/strong>, <strong>satellite<\/strong> or <strong>phone<\/strong>. Inmarsat short codes <strong>32<\/strong> (medical advice) and <strong>38<\/strong> (medical assistance) are commonly used (verify on your terminal).<\/li><li>Give the <strong>IAMSAR Vol III MEDICO<\/strong> information:<\/li><li>ship&#x27;s name, call sign, <strong>position<\/strong>, course, speed, <strong>ETA<\/strong> at the nearest port;<\/li><li>the patient&#x27;s <strong>age<\/strong>, sex, <strong>symptoms<\/strong>, <strong>history<\/strong>, <strong>medication<\/strong> given and allergies;<\/li><li>the <strong>observations<\/strong>;<\/li><li>what medicines are in the ship&#x27;s medical chest.<\/li><li>Follow the doctor&#x27;s advice, and record it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Command<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>C/O takes command<\/strong> in the Master&#x27;s incapacity, as next in line under the <strong>SMS<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the company and DPA<\/strong> immediately. The company informs the <strong>MCA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If I don&#x27;t hold a Master&#x27;s CoC, the MCA&#x27;s authority is needed for me to continue in command, and the company will arrange a relief.<\/li><li>Check that the <strong>safe manning<\/strong> and <strong>rest-hour<\/strong> position can still be met with the Master off duty and me in command. Ask for a <strong>relief<\/strong> or a <strong>dispensation<\/strong> if not.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Medevac, if TMAS advises<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Divert<\/strong> towards a port, or arrange a <strong>helicopter<\/strong> through the <strong>RCC<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Prepare under the <strong>ICS helicopter guide<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Send the patient with <strong>notes<\/strong>, <strong>medication record<\/strong> and <strong>passport<\/strong>.<\/li><li>An officer may need to go with him if he can&#x27;t speak for himself.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Records and contacts<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>OLB<\/strong> entries: the illness, the treatment, and the <strong>change of command<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Medical log<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and the <strong>agent<\/strong> informed: they arrange the hospital and repatriation.<\/li><li>The next of kin through the company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Running the ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Take over the Master&#x27;s keys, the safe, the documents and the SSAS details.<\/li><li>Brief the officers, and keep the voyage going safely.<\/li><li>Consider whether the Master&#x27;s illness is serious enough to be an MAIB report: a serious illness is not, unless caused by an accident.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. If he dies<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Form <strong>MSF 4605<\/strong>, the <strong>OLB<\/strong> entries, and expect the <strong>s.271 inquiry<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>First aid with the medical guide and observations, TMAS through HMCG with the MEDICO information, the C/O taking command and informing the company and MCA, manning and rest hours, medevac by helicopter or diversion, and the OLB entries including the change of command.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can you, as Chief Mate, legally take command and sail into the next port?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes, in an emergency.<\/strong> As next in command under the SMS, you take command to get the ship safely to the next port. Inform the company and the flag. Continuing after that needs a properly qualified Master or an MCA dispensation.<\/p>","asked":["Master is ill. What will you do as Chief Officer?","In the heavy weather, master is incapacitated, you are Now the master, what are your actions?","Medical ENG1 validity?","How will you prepare for medical evacuation ?","Render first Aid as per Master Medical Guide"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",13],["Michele Moro",6],["Steve Meaking",6],["Zia Ul Haq",3],["Khairul Anam",3]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q135","sec":8,"q":"Galley fire, cook badly burned — actions, contacts?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The fire<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong> and <strong>muster<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> galley <strong>power<\/strong>, the <strong>fuel supply<\/strong> to cookers and fryers, and the <strong>ventilation fans<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Close<\/strong> the <strong>range-hood<\/strong> and <strong>duct fire dampers<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fight it<\/strong> with a <strong>fire blanket<\/strong>, or the <strong>deep-fat-fryer fixed system<\/strong>, and the right portable extinguisher: <strong>wet chemical<\/strong> or <strong>CO₂<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Never use water on a fat fire<\/strong>: it turns to steam and throws burning fat everywhere.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cooling<\/strong>, and check the <strong>exhaust duct<\/strong> for fire spreading through the accommodation. Galley duct fires can travel to the funnel.<\/li><li><strong>Re-ignition watch<\/strong> after it&#x27;s out, because hot fat can re-ignite.<\/li><li>Ventilate the smoke only when the fire is out.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The casualty<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Remove him from danger<\/strong> and check his <strong>airway<\/strong>. <strong>Facial burns<\/strong>, singed nasal hair or <strong>soot<\/strong> around the mouth may mean an <strong>inhalation injury<\/strong>, which can close the airway in hours. That needs urgent medevac advice.<\/li><li><strong>Cool the burns<\/strong> with <strong>cool running water<\/strong> for about <strong>20 minutes<\/strong>. Not ice.<\/li><li><strong>Remove jewellery<\/strong> and watches before swelling starts, but <strong>don&#x27;t remove clothing stuck<\/strong> to the burn.<\/li><li><strong>Cover<\/strong> with <strong>cling film<\/strong> or a <strong>clean non-stick dressing<\/strong>. Don&#x27;t burst blisters or apply creams.<\/li><li><strong>Estimate<\/strong> the <strong>% body surface<\/strong> burned, using the <strong>rule of nines<\/strong>: head 9%, each arm 9%, front and back of the trunk 18% each, each leg 18%, perineum 1%. Or the patient&#x27;s palm is about 1%.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>shock<\/strong>: lay him down, keep him warm, give <strong>fluids<\/strong> and <strong>pain relief<\/strong> per the <strong>Ship Captain&#x27;s Medical Guide<\/strong> and TMAS advice.<\/li><li>Record observations.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Contacts<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>TMAS<\/strong> via <strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong> (UK ship) for medical advice and a <strong>medevac<\/strong> decision. Prepare for a helicopter per the <strong>ICS guide<\/strong> and <strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong>, or divert.<\/li><li>The <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>: crew injury, medical costs, repatriation and the <strong>replacement cook<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>agent<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><li><strong>MAIB<\/strong>: a fire with a serious injury is reportable as a marine casualty.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records and follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>OLB<\/strong> entries for the injury, treatment and the fire.<\/li><li>An <strong>accident report<\/strong> under the SMS (ISM 9), and an investigation: why did the fat ignite? Thermostat, overfilling, the cook left it, duct grease?<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> review for the galley.<\/li><li>Recharge and replace the extinguishers and blankets used.<\/li><li>Feeding the crew without a cook: someone competent to take over, and food hygiene.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Isolate power, fuel and ventilation, close dampers, blanket or wet chemical, never water on fat, duct check and re-ignition watch; for the cook, airway and inhalation injury, cool for 20 minutes, cover with cling film, rule of nines, shock; and the contacts including TMAS, P&amp;I and the MAIB.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does the P&I club need to know about a crew injury?","pa":"<p>P&amp;I covers <strong>crew injury and illness<\/strong> liabilities: medical costs, repatriation, sick pay and compensation claims. Telling them early lets them appoint a correspondent and doctor and collect evidence.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★9.1","sec":8,"q":"IAMSAR Vol III — which sections would you open for a MOB in heavy weather?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong> (Mobile Facilities) is required on board under <strong>SOLAS V/21<\/strong>, applied in the UK by the Safety of Navigation Regs 2020.<\/p><p><strong>The sections I&#x27;d open for a MOB in heavy weather<\/strong> (check the section headings in your own edition; the content is what the examiner wants)<\/p><p><strong>Section 2: Rendering assistance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Actions when someone is <strong>in the water<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Recovery of survivors<\/strong>, including from a <strong>high-freeboard<\/strong> ship: making a lee, using the rescue boat, nets, cradles, or the ship&#x27;s recovery equipment.<\/li><li><strong>Care of survivors<\/strong>, including <strong>hypothermia<\/strong> and lifting horizontally.<\/li><li><strong>Communications<\/strong> with the <strong>RCC<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Section 3: On-scene coordination<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Search patterns<\/strong>:<\/li><li>a <strong>sector search<\/strong> suits a MOB with a <strong>known datum<\/strong>, such as when the person was seen going over;<\/li><li>an <strong>expanding square<\/strong> suits an <strong>uncertain<\/strong> datum.<\/li><li><strong>Datum<\/strong>, <strong>drift<\/strong> and <strong>search speeds<\/strong>, and the track spacing for a person in the water.<\/li><li>The duties of the <strong>On-Scene Coordinator<\/strong> if other ships join.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Section 4: On-board emergencies<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>immediate MOB manoeuvres<\/strong>:<\/li><li><strong>Williamson turn<\/strong>: when you <strong>don&#x27;t know when<\/strong> he went over, at night or in poor visibility. It brings the ship back onto her original track. Rudder hard over, at 60° off the original heading, rudder hard over the other way, and come round onto the reciprocal course.<\/li><li><strong>Anderson turn<\/strong> (single turn): the <strong>quickest return<\/strong> when you <strong>saw<\/strong> it happen. Rudder hard over to the side he fell, and turn through about 250° to approach.<\/li><li><strong>Scharnow turn<\/strong>: for a <strong>delayed action<\/strong>, when the person went over some time ago and you want to return along the track. It doesn&#x27;t work if the person was seen going over.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In heavy weather<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Launching a rescue boat may be too dangerous<\/strong>. The <strong>Master decides<\/strong> after a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Consider <strong>making a lee<\/strong> and <strong>recovering over the side<\/strong> with the ship&#x27;s <strong>recovery-of-persons equipment<\/strong> and <strong>plan<\/strong>. <strong>SOLAS III/17-1<\/strong> requires <strong>ship-specific plans and procedures<\/strong> for the recovery of persons from the water.<\/li><li>Immediate actions:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>throw the lifebuoy<\/strong> with <strong>light and smoke<\/strong>;<\/li><li>press the <strong>MOB button<\/strong> on the ECDIS or GNSS;<\/li><li><strong>post lookouts<\/strong> who keep pointing at the person;<\/li><li><strong>alert the RCC<\/strong> by <strong>Mayday relay<\/strong> or urgency message, and other ships;<\/li><li>sound the alarm and muster the rescue team.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Turn into the wind to reduce rolling, and approach so the person is in the lee.<\/li><li>Survival time in cold water is short, so speed matters, but not at the cost of more casualties.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>IAMSAR Vol III carried under SOLAS V/21, Section 2 for rendering assistance and recovery, Section 3 for search patterns (sector for a known datum, expanding square for uncertain), Section 4 for the Williamson, Anderson and Scharnow turns and when to use each, and in heavy weather the risk assessment for the rescue boat and the SOLAS III/17-1 recovery plan.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why choose a Williamson turn over an Anderson turn at night?","pa":"<p>At night you may <strong>lose sight<\/strong> of the person. The <strong>Williamson<\/strong> turn brings the ship back <strong>down her own track<\/strong>, so it&#x27;s the most reliable way to return to where the person went in. The <strong>Anderson<\/strong> turn is quickest when the person is still in sight and you acted at once.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q136","sec":9,"q":"Fire alarm on your watch; detector in the E/R on UMS; confirmed E/R fire — full procedure.","tier":"red","rep":100,"src":"Study books","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Musters, Training and Decision Support Systems) Regs 1999<\/strong> with <strong>MGN 71<\/strong>, the <strong>Fire Protection Regs 2023<\/strong>, and the ship&#x27;s <strong>fire control plan<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS II-2<\/strong>. The <strong>SMS emergency checklist<\/strong> drives the sequence.<\/p><p><strong>1. Alarm on the panel<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Acknowledge<\/strong> it, <strong>identify the zone<\/strong> and the detector.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the duty engineer<\/strong> and the <strong>Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On <strong>UMS<\/strong>, the duty engineer <strong>investigates<\/strong> with a <strong>second person<\/strong>, <strong>never alone<\/strong>, keeping <strong>radio contact<\/strong> with the bridge. They go in ready to retreat.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t silence or reset the alarm without finding the cause. A false alarm is still recorded.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Fire confirmed<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Sound the <strong>general alarm<\/strong>, <strong>muster<\/strong>, <strong>head count<\/strong>, and <strong>announce<\/strong> the location.<\/li><li>The <strong>Master<\/strong> takes the <strong>con<\/strong> and overall command. I go to the scene as <strong>on-scene commander<\/strong>, as the muster list sets.<\/li><li><strong>Notify<\/strong> the <strong>company<\/strong> and, if needed, the <strong>coastal State<\/strong> with an urgency message.<\/li><li>Consider the ship&#x27;s position: traffic, shallow water, the effect of losing propulsion.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. First attack<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Fire party<\/strong> in <strong>full fireman&#x27;s outfit<\/strong> with <strong>SCBA<\/strong>, after a <strong>buddy check<\/strong> of the donning, with lifelines and radios, and a <strong>back-up team<\/strong> ready.<\/li><li><strong>Small fire<\/strong>: <strong>portables<\/strong>, the <strong>local water mist<\/strong> system, <strong>hoses<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the electrics and the <strong>fuel<\/strong>, using the <strong>remote quick-closing valves<\/strong> and <strong>pump stops<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Entry control<\/strong> board: names, BA pressures, times in.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Containment<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stop the E/R fans<\/strong>, close the <strong>dampers<\/strong>, <strong>skylights<\/strong> and <strong>doors<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Start <strong>boundary cooling<\/strong>: the decks above, the bulkheads, the funnel casing.<\/li><li>Start the <strong>emergency generator<\/strong> and the <strong>emergency fire pump<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Close <strong>fire doors<\/strong> in the accommodation near the engine room casing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Escalation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the fire <strong>can&#x27;t be controlled<\/strong>, <strong>withdraw everyone<\/strong>, take a <strong>head count<\/strong> (twice), <strong>seal<\/strong> the space, and <strong>release the fixed CO₂<\/strong> on the <strong>Master&#x27;s order<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>85%<\/strong> of the required CO₂ within <strong>2 minutes<\/strong> for machinery spaces.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Navigation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When we lose propulsion: <strong>NUC<\/strong> signals, AIS status, <strong>Sécurité<\/strong> or <strong>Pan-Pan<\/strong>, plot the drift, anchors ready, tugs if needed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Keep the space sealed<\/strong> and <strong>monitor<\/strong> the boundaries.<\/li><li><strong>Re-enter only<\/strong> once it has <strong>cooled<\/strong> and the <strong>atmosphere<\/strong> has been checked, with a team in <strong>SCBA<\/strong> and <strong>hoses charged<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Log everything<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> (SI 2026/430), the <strong>flag State<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong> under SOLAS I/11(c), the company and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Recharge the CO₂ before sailing; the SEC is affected until it&#x27;s replaced.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Investigate the alarm with two people on UMS, general alarm and head count, the Master on the bridge and me on scene, first attack in BA with isolation of fuel and electrics, containment and boundary cooling, emergency generator and fire pump, CO₂ on the Master&#x27;s order after the head count, no early re-entry, and the reports.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you check on the fireman before he goes in?","pa":"<p>Check:<\/p><ul><li>BA cylinder pressure;<\/li><li>the facemask seal (leak test) and the low-pressure whistle;<\/li><li>the tally on the entry control board;<\/li><li>full PPE: suit, helmet, gloves, boots;<\/li><li>the radio or lifeline, torch and axe.<\/li><\/ul><p>Then a <strong>buddy check<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["Now on fire, actions?","As a chief mate on watch, a fire alarm goes off, what are your actions?","What class of fire is cooking oil?","Requirements of Emergency fire pump, where will you find it ?","Different type of Fire doors?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",15],["Muhammad Iqbal",12],["James Gilbert",11],["Alec Keep",11],["Examiner not named",10]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q137","sec":9,"q":"Accommodation fire Singapore–Korea.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Musters Regs 1999<\/strong>, <strong>SOLAS II-2<\/strong> and the <strong>fire control plan<\/strong>. The difference here is that I&#x27;m in <strong>dense traffic<\/strong> in the South China Sea or the Taiwan Strait, with fishing fleets around.<\/p><p><strong>1. Alarm and people<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong>, <strong>muster<\/strong>, <strong>head count<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Missing persons are the priority<\/strong>: send a <strong>search-and-rescue team<\/strong> in <strong>SCBA<\/strong> to the cabins, with lifelines and a thermal camera.<\/li><li>First aid and <strong>TMAS<\/strong> for casualties.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. The bridge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Master on the bridge<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Manoeuvre<\/strong> to put the <strong>fire downwind<\/strong>: for an <strong>aft accommodation<\/strong> fire, usually the <strong>wind on the bow<\/strong>, and <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> to cut the draught feeding the fire.<\/li><li>Keep clear of traffic and shoal water: the traffic in the Taiwan Strait is heavy. Use the TSS and VTS.<\/li><li><strong>Sécurité<\/strong> to warn other ships of our restricted manoeuvrability.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Contain the fire<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stop<\/strong> the <strong>accommodation ventilation<\/strong>, close the <strong>fire doors<\/strong> and <strong>dampers<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the <strong>electrical supply<\/strong> to the area.<\/li><li>Close doors and openings to the stairways, which spread fire vertically.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Attack<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Fire party<\/strong> attacks with <strong>hoses<\/strong> or <strong>extinguishers<\/strong>, in BA.<\/li><li><strong>Back-up team<\/strong> ready.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cooling<\/strong> on the <strong>decks above and below<\/strong> and the <strong>bulkheads<\/strong> either side.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Protect the bridge and radio room<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the <strong>bridge is threatened<\/strong>, prepare <strong>emergency steering<\/strong> from the steering gear room, and take the <strong>GMDSS handhelds<\/strong>, <strong>EPIRB<\/strong> and <strong>SART<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The emergency generator and its room, and the CO₂ room, may also be near the accommodation: protect them.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Lifeboats<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Prepare the lifeboats<\/strong>, especially if they&#x27;re beside the accommodation and could be lost.<\/li><li>Brief the crew on the abandon-ship plan.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Communications<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>PAN-PAN<\/strong> or <strong>MAYDAY<\/strong> to the <strong>MRCC<\/strong>: Singapore, Hong Kong, Taipei or Korea depending on position.<\/li><li>Notify the <strong>company<\/strong> and the <strong>DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Fishing vessels and other ships may come to help.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Stability<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Drain firefighting water<\/strong> from the upper decks: water high up raises G and creates free surface.<\/li><li>Monitor the list.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><p>If the fire is <strong>not contained<\/strong>, the <strong>Master orders abandon ship in good time<\/strong>, while the craft can still be launched and before the accommodation and embarkation stations are lost.<\/p><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><p>Reports to the MAIB, flag, class, company and P&amp;I. Preserve the VDR, which may be near the bridge.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Missing persons first with a BA search team, the Master manoeuvring to put the fire downwind and slowing down, containment by ventilation and fire doors, attack with boundary cooling, protecting the bridge and radio, lifeboats ready, Pan-Pan or Mayday to the right MRCC, water and stability, and abandoning in good time.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you stop firefighting water affecting stability?","pa":"<p>Use <strong>spray or fog, not jets<\/strong>, and as little water as you need. Drain and pump it out with scuppers and portable pumps. <strong>Monitor list, trim and free surface<\/strong>, and calculate the effect on stability as water builds up.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q138","sec":9,"q":"Fire in the cargo hold at 0200.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-2/10.7<\/strong> (fixed systems for cargo spaces) and the <strong>FSS Code<\/strong>. On my container ship, a <strong>hold fire<\/strong> is normally fought with <strong>fixed CO₂<\/strong> and <strong>boundary cooling<\/strong>, not by entry.<\/p><p><strong>1. Detection<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>By the <strong>smoke detection<\/strong> or <strong>sample extraction<\/strong> system, showing which hold.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>, sound the <strong>general alarm<\/strong>, <strong>muster<\/strong>, <strong>head count<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Identify the cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Identify the <strong>hold<\/strong> and <strong>bay<\/strong> from the <strong>stowage plan<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>DG manifest<\/strong>: what DG is in that hold and next to it?<\/li><li>Look up the <strong>EmS<\/strong> and <strong>MFAG<\/strong> for any DG in or next to the hold. Some cargoes react with water, some give off toxic gases, and some aren&#x27;t suitable for CO₂.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Seal the hold<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stop the fans<\/strong>, close the <strong>vents<\/strong>, <strong>dampers<\/strong> and <strong>access hatches<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the <strong>reefer power<\/strong> in that hold if it&#x27;s <strong>safe<\/strong> to do so, because a reefer may be the cause.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t open the hatch to look.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Boundary cooling<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Cool the <strong>hatch covers<\/strong>, the <strong>coamings<\/strong>, and the <strong>adjacent holds<\/strong> and <strong>tanks<\/strong>, especially fuel tanks and the engine room bulkhead.<\/li><li>Use a <strong>thermal camera<\/strong> to monitor temperatures and track the fire.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Release CO₂<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On the <strong>Master&#x27;s order<\/strong>, after confirming <strong>nobody is in the hold<\/strong> and it&#x27;s <strong>sealed<\/strong>, release CO₂ for <strong>that hold<\/strong> per the <strong>system instructions<\/strong> and the quantity for the hold.<\/li><li>For cargo spaces, the required quantity is normally discharged within about <strong>10 minutes<\/strong> for two-thirds of the charge (verify against the ship&#x27;s system).<\/li><li><strong>Keep the reserve<\/strong> for <strong>top-up<\/strong> over the following hours or days, as the CO₂ leaks away.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Keep it sealed<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Never open the hatch at sea<\/strong>: oxygen will <strong>re-ignite<\/strong> the fire.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the company<\/strong> and <strong>divert<\/strong> to a <strong>port with shore firefighting<\/strong> capability. A hold fire may smoulder for days.<\/li><li>Report to the coastal State, and consider a place of refuge.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Stability<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Watch <strong>stability<\/strong> if water is used for boundary cooling: it collects on hatch tops and in the bilges.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>8. Log everything<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Times, CO₂ released, temperatures, actions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Reporting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong>, the <strong>flag<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong>, the company, P&amp;I, and the cargo interests.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>At the port<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The shore fire brigade decides when to open the hatch, with water ready, and the cargo is discharged with care. Investigators look at the cause: often a misdeclared DG box.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Alarm and head count, identify the hold and the DG with the EmS, seal the hold and isolate reefers, boundary cooling with a thermal camera, CO₂ on the Master&#x27;s order with the reserve kept, never open the hatch at sea, divert to a port with shore firefighting, and stability.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How long do you keep the hold sealed, and how do you know the fire is out?","pa":"<p>Keep it sealed until the <strong>boundary temperatures<\/strong> are back to normal and stay there. That may take <strong>days<\/strong>. Monitor gas samples (CO, O₂) and temperatures. Open in port with the fire brigade standing by, not at sea unless you have to.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q139","sec":9,"q":"Generator-room fire; boiler/EGB soot fire.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Generator-room fire<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop and isolate<\/strong> the affected generator, close the <strong>fuel quick-closing valves<\/strong> and <strong>stop<\/strong> the <strong>fuel pumps<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Shut<\/strong> the <strong>ventilation<\/strong> and <strong>dampers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On ships of <strong>2,000 GT<\/strong> and above, a <strong>fixed local-application water-mist<\/strong> system covers the generator engines and other high-risk areas in machinery spaces over 500 m³ (<strong>SOLAS II-2/10.5.6<\/strong>). Use that and <strong>portable extinguishers<\/strong> first.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cooling<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the room has its own <strong>fixed system<\/strong> and the fire grows, <strong>evacuate<\/strong>, take a <strong>head count<\/strong>, and <strong>release<\/strong> it on the Master&#x27;s order.<\/li><li><strong>Start the emergency generator<\/strong> if we lose power, and the emergency fire pump.<\/li><li>Load management on the remaining generators, and reefers in stages.<\/li><li>The usual alarm, muster, fire party in BA, entry control and reporting.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Causes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Fuel or lube oil spraying onto a hot exhaust or turbocharger, from a broken pipe or a loose fitting. That&#x27;s why SOLAS requires <strong>jacketed high-pressure fuel pipes<\/strong> and <strong>insulation<\/strong> of hot surfaces.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Exhaust gas boiler (EGB) soot fire<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Cause<\/strong>: <strong>soot build-up<\/strong> in the boiler tubes, often after <strong>long periods at low load<\/strong> (slow steaming), with poor combustion. The soot ignites when the temperature rises.<\/p><p><strong>Signs<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>High exhaust temperature<\/strong> after the EGB.<\/li><li><strong>Sparks<\/strong> from the <strong>funnel<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A drop in steam pressure or a high boiler temperature alarm.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Reduce load<\/strong> and <strong>stop the main engine<\/strong>, so no more hot gas and oxygen flow through the boiler.<\/li><li><strong>Keep<\/strong> the <strong>boiler circulating pump running<\/strong>. A <strong>dry boiler overheats<\/strong> and can lead to a <strong>high-temperature (hydrogen or iron) fire<\/strong>, where the steel itself burns above about 1,100°C and water splits into hydrogen and oxygen.<\/li><li>For a <strong>small soot fire<\/strong>, use the <strong>water-washing<\/strong> system with <strong>plenty of water<\/strong>. <strong>Never<\/strong> apply <strong>small amounts<\/strong> of water or steam to a <strong>very hot<\/strong> fire, because it can feed a hydrogen fire (verify against the maker&#x27;s manual).<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cool<\/strong> the <strong>funnel casing<\/strong>, and watch for spread to the engine room casing.<\/li><li>Inform the Master, and the coastal State if we&#x27;re stopped in traffic.<\/li><li>When it&#x27;s out, inspect the boiler before using it again.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Regular <strong>soot-blowing<\/strong> and <strong>water washing<\/strong> of the EGB.<\/li><li>Avoid <strong>prolonged low-load<\/strong> running, or use the soot-blowers more often when slow steaming.<\/li><li>Good combustion: fuel injection and turbocharger maintenance.<\/li><li>Monitor the exhaust temperatures and pressure drop across the boiler.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>For the generator room, isolate fuel and stop the engine, local water mist under SOLAS II-2/10.5.6, boundary cooling, fixed system if it grows, emergency power; and for the EGB soot fire, the cause (low load), the signs (high exhaust temperature, sparks), stop the engine, keep the circulating pump running, plenty of water not small amounts, cool the funnel, and prevention by soot-blowing.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is a small amount of water dangerous on a high-temperature EGB fire?","pa":"<p>Above about <strong>1,000 °C<\/strong> water breaks down into <strong>hydrogen and oxygen<\/strong>, which can cause a hydrogen fire or explosion. Use <strong>copious<\/strong> water to cool it fast, or none (cool the boundaries and let it burn out).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q140","sec":9,"q":"When do you use the fixed system in the E/R?","tier":"orange","rep":25,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-2/10.4–10.5<\/strong> and the <strong>FSS Code Chapter 5<\/strong> (fixed gas fire-extinguishing systems). <strong>Release is always the Master&#x27;s decision<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>When<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The fire is <strong>beyond control<\/strong> by <strong>portable<\/strong> means, <strong>hoses<\/strong> or the <strong>local water mist<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>spreading<\/strong>, for example into the cable trays or to the switchboard.<\/li><li><strong>Conditions<\/strong> are <strong>too dangerous<\/strong> for the fire team: heat, smoke, explosions, a team member injured, BA running low.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t leave it too late<\/strong>. A fire that grows <strong>weakens<\/strong> the space, damages the dampers and doors so they won&#x27;t seal, and the system may then <strong>not succeed<\/strong> because the space can&#x27;t be sealed or the fire&#x27;s too big.<\/li><li>Many companies have a time guide: if the fire isn&#x27;t under control within a few minutes of the first attack, prepare for CO₂.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before release<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Everyone out<\/strong>, with a <strong>head count confirmed twice<\/strong>: muster list, entry control board, and a check of the engine control room, workshops and steering gear room.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilation stopped<\/strong>, and <strong>dampers<\/strong>, <strong>skylights<\/strong>, <strong>funnel flaps<\/strong> and <strong>doors<\/strong> closed.<\/li><li><strong>Fuel quick-closing valves<\/strong> shut, and <strong>fuel and lube pumps<\/strong> stopped <strong>remotely<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Emergency generator<\/strong> and <strong>emergency fire pump<\/strong> running. <strong>Boundary cooling<\/strong> started.<\/li><li>The <strong>ship&#x27;s position<\/strong> considered: the ship will lose propulsion, so NUC signals, anchors and tugs.<\/li><li><strong>Pre-discharge alarm<\/strong> sounded, with the time delay, so anyone still inside can escape.<\/li><li><strong>Release<\/strong> the CO₂ using the <strong>two separate controls<\/strong>: the <strong>main valve<\/strong> to the space first, then the <strong>cylinders<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Quantity and time<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For machinery spaces, <strong>85%<\/strong> of the required quantity within <strong>2 minutes<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The amount is based on the gross volume of the space, normally 40% or 35% of the volume depending on the size.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After release<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Keep the space sealed<\/strong> for long enough to <strong>cool<\/strong>, which is usually <strong>many hours<\/strong>. Follow the company and maker&#x27;s guidance.<\/li><li><strong>Monitor<\/strong> the <strong>boundary temperatures<\/strong> all round, with a thermal camera.<\/li><li><strong>Re-enter only<\/strong> after a careful check of <strong>temperature<\/strong> and <strong>atmosphere<\/strong>, with a team in <strong>SCBA<\/strong> and <strong>hoses charged<\/strong>, ready for <strong>re-ignition<\/strong>. Open the space slowly from the bottom if possible.<\/li><li>Ventilate carefully when the fire is out and the space is cool.<\/li><li>Recharge the system before sailing. The Safety Equipment certificate is affected until it&#x27;s replaced.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records and reports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Log the times and actions.<\/li><li>MAIB, flag, class, company and P&amp;I.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Master&#x27;s decision; when it&#x27;s beyond portable means, spreading or too dangerous, and not too late; before release, the head count twice, everything closed, fuel stopped, emergency generator and fire pump running, pre-discharge alarm, two controls; 85% in 2 minutes; and keep it sealed for many hours, re-entering carefully.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Within what time must 85% of the CO₂ reach a machinery space?","pa":"<p><strong>85% within 2 minutes<\/strong> for machinery spaces and cargo pump rooms (FSS Code Ch 5).<\/p>","asked":["What fixed firefighting systems do you have on your ship?","What fixed fire fighting system would you expect in the galley how/where to trigger?","Describe the use and precautions to consider before using a Fixed CO2 system?","Is there any requirement of having fixed CO2 system on board.","OOW has told you, your fixed system was activated. What was that about?"],"ex":[["Alec Keep",7],["Colum Leggett",3],["Robert Jackson",2],["Khairul Anam",2],["Byron Griffiths",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q141","sec":9,"q":"Container / LNG / ro-ro / tanker fire strategies.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>On deck<\/strong>: identify the <strong>box<\/strong> and its <strong>DG<\/strong> from the bay plan and the manifest. Use <strong>water cooling<\/strong> on the box and <strong>cool the adjacent stacks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Ships built from <strong>1 January 2016<\/strong> designed for <strong>five or more tiers<\/strong> on deck carry a <strong>container water-mist lance<\/strong> (piercing nozzle) and <strong>mobile water monitors<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS II-2/10.7.3<\/strong>). The lance pierces the container door or wall to get water inside.<\/li><li><strong>Under deck<\/strong>: <strong>seal the hold<\/strong>, release <strong>CO₂<\/strong> and <strong>boundary cool<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the EmS for each DG, and watch for toxic smoke.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>LNG carrier<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stop the leak<\/strong> with the <strong>emergency shutdown (ESD)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use <strong>dry-chemical powder<\/strong> on <strong>gas fires<\/strong>, from the fixed dry powder system.<\/li><li>Use <strong>water spray<\/strong> and <strong>water curtains<\/strong> for <strong>cooling<\/strong> and to <strong>protect the structure<\/strong> from cryogenic cracking and heat.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t extinguish a gas jet fire<\/strong> unless the <strong>leak can be isolated<\/strong>, because <strong>unburnt gas<\/strong> can form a cloud and <strong>explode<\/strong>. Let it burn while cooling around it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Ro-ro<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>fixed water-spray (drencher)<\/strong> system for the vehicle deck.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilation<\/strong> shut down or controlled.<\/li><li><strong>Drain<\/strong> the firefighting water: <strong>free surface<\/strong> on the vehicle deck can <strong>capsize<\/strong> the ship very quickly (Herald of Free Enterprise showed the effect of water on a vehicle deck).<\/li><li><strong>EVs<\/strong> need <strong>prolonged cooling<\/strong>, and may re-ignite.<\/li><li>Fire patrols and CCTV for early detection.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tanker<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Emergency stop<\/strong> of cargo operations, <strong>ESD<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Maintain inert gas<\/strong> in the cargo tanks, to keep them non-flammable.<\/li><li>The <strong>fixed deck foam system<\/strong> with <strong>monitors<\/strong> for deck fires.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cooling<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>fixed system<\/strong> in the <strong>pump room<\/strong>: often CO₂, high-expansion foam or water mist.<\/li><li>Close all tank openings, and stop any bunkering or ship-to-ship transfer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Common to all<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Alarm<\/strong>, <strong>head count<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong>: fuel, cargo, electrics, ventilation.<\/li><li><strong>Contain<\/strong>: close doors and openings.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cool<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Protect escape routes<\/strong> and the LSA.<\/li><li>Watch <strong>stability<\/strong> with firefighting water.<\/li><li>Communicate with the coastal State and company, and plan for a port with shore firefighting.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Specific strategies: for containers, the water-mist lance and monitors under SOLAS II-2/10.7.3 on deck and CO₂ under deck; for LNG, ESD, dry powder, water spray and not putting out a gas jet unless the leak is isolated; for ro-ro, drenchers and draining the water because of free surface; for tankers, ESD, inert gas, deck foam and the pump room system; and the common principles.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why not put out a gas fire on an LNG ship straight away?","pa":"<p>If gas is still escaping, putting out the flame leaves an <strong>unignited gas cloud<\/strong> that can explode. First <strong>stop the gas<\/strong> with the ESD, and cool everything with water. Use dry powder once the source is shut off.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q142","sec":9,"q":"Why boundary cooling?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Why boundary cooling<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Steel conducts heat.<\/strong> A fire in one compartment heats the bulkheads and decks enough to ignite <strong>paint<\/strong>, <strong>insulation<\/strong>, <strong>cargo<\/strong> or <strong>fuel<\/strong> on the <strong>other side<\/strong>, so the fire <strong>spreads without flame passing through<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>What boundary cooling does<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Prevents spread<\/strong> by <strong>conduction<\/strong> and <strong>radiation<\/strong> into <strong>adjacent spaces<\/strong>. An <strong>A-60<\/strong> division only holds for <strong>one hour<\/strong> in the standard fire test; a real fire can last much longer and be hotter.<\/li><li><strong>Keeps structural strength<\/strong>. Hot steel <strong>loses strength<\/strong> above about 400–500°C and <strong>distorts<\/strong>. <strong>Doors<\/strong> and <strong>hatch covers<\/strong> can <strong>jam<\/strong>, and decks can buckle.<\/li><li><strong>Protects tanks<\/strong> (fuel, DG), <strong>cables<\/strong> and <strong>escape routes<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Reduces the risk of re-ignition<\/strong> after the fixed system is used, by cooling the space boundaries.<\/li><li><strong>Lets you monitor the fire<\/strong>: watch the steam, how fast the water evaporates, and the paint blistering, or use a <strong>thermal camera<\/strong>, to see where the fire is and whether it&#x27;s growing or dying.<\/li><li><strong>Protects the people<\/strong> fighting the fire and working nearby.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Spray or fog<\/strong> on <strong>all six sides<\/strong> of the space: the deckhead above, the deck below, and the four bulkheads, as far as they can be reached.<\/li><li>Work from the <strong>top down<\/strong>, because heat rises.<\/li><li><strong>Check<\/strong> by <strong>hand<\/strong> (carefully, with the back of the hand) or <strong>thermal camera<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use <strong>enough water<\/strong> and <strong>no more<\/strong>: a fine spray is more effective than a jet for cooling, and uses less water.<\/li><li><strong>Control the water<\/strong>: <strong>drain<\/strong> it or <strong>pump<\/strong> it out, because <strong>free surface<\/strong> and <strong>added top weight<\/strong> reduce <strong>GM<\/strong>. Fire-fighting water has capsized ships.<\/li><li><strong>Log<\/strong> the quantities and times.<\/li><li>Keep it going <strong>after<\/strong> the fire appears to be out, until the boundaries are cool.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Examples<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Engine room fire<\/strong>: cool the engine casing in the accommodation, the deck above, the bulkheads to the holds, and the fuel tanks.<\/li><li><strong>Hold fire<\/strong>: cool the hatch covers, the coamings, and the bulkheads to the adjacent holds.<\/li><li><strong>Accommodation fire<\/strong>: cool the decks above and below and the cabins either side.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Steel conducts heat so fire spreads without flame passing through; boundary cooling prevents spread, keeps the steel&#x27;s strength, protects tanks and escape routes, reduces re-ignition, and lets you monitor the fire; done on all six sides from the top down, with the water controlled because of free surface and stability.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you know boundary cooling is working?","pa":"<p>Feel the <strong>boundary temperature<\/strong>, or use a thermal imager. The paint stops blistering, steam stops forming, and water running off the bulkhead <strong>doesn&#x27;t boil<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q143","sec":9,"q":"What does the emergency generator supply, and for how long?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-1/43<\/strong> for cargo ships, applied in the UK by the cargo ship construction regulations. The <strong>emergency source<\/strong> must <strong>start automatically<\/strong> and <strong>take the load<\/strong> within <strong>45 seconds<\/strong> of a main power failure.<\/p><p><strong>For 18 hours it supplies<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Emergency lighting<\/strong> in alleyways, stairways and <strong>exits<\/strong>, at the <strong>muster and embarkation stations<\/strong>, in the <strong>machinery spaces<\/strong>, the <strong>control stations<\/strong>, the <strong>steering gear room<\/strong>, and where the <strong>fireman&#x27;s outfits<\/strong> are stowed, and at the emergency fire pump.<\/li><li><strong>Navigation lights<\/strong> and other lights required by <strong>COLREGs<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>VHF<\/strong>, <strong>MF<\/strong> and <strong>MF/HF<\/strong> radio installations (<strong>GMDSS<\/strong>), unless they have their own reserve source.<\/li><li><strong>Internal communications<\/strong>, the <strong>general alarm<\/strong>, and the <strong>fire detection and alarm<\/strong> system.<\/li><li>The <strong>daylight signalling lamp<\/strong>, the <strong>whistle<\/strong>, <strong>manual call points<\/strong>, and the <strong>navigation equipment<\/strong> required by SOLAS V.<\/li><li>The <strong>emergency fire pump<\/strong>, if it&#x27;s <strong>electrically driven<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Steering gear<\/strong>, where required under <strong>SOLAS II-1/29<\/strong> (the alternative power supply).<\/li><li><strong>Watertight door<\/strong> systems and their indicators, where fitted.<\/li><li>The <strong>sprinkler pump<\/strong> and the <strong>emergency bilge pump<\/strong>, where electric.<\/li><li>The <strong>lift<\/strong> cars, on some ships.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>3 hours<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Muster and embarkation station lighting<\/strong>, including <strong>over the side<\/strong>, is required for <strong>3 hours<\/strong> (the transitional source on some ships covers 30 minutes).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Passenger ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>36 hours<\/strong> instead of 18, and a <strong>transitional source<\/strong> of emergency power for 30 minutes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The emergency switchboard<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In the same space as the emergency generator, above the uppermost continuous deck, outside the machinery spaces and accessible from the open deck.<\/li><li>Normally fed from the main switchboard, with an interconnector.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Checks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Weekly<\/strong> run test, and a regular <strong>on-load<\/strong> test with <strong>automatic start<\/strong> and <strong>changeover<\/strong>, per the PMS and company procedure (often monthly, or at least every 3 months with the blackout test).<\/li><li>Check the <strong>fuel<\/strong> level (enough for 18 hours), the <strong>starting arrangements<\/strong>: <strong>two means<\/strong> of starting, such as a battery and a hydraulic or air starter, the <strong>battery<\/strong> condition, and the <strong>ventilation<\/strong> of the room, which must stay open.<\/li><li>Check the room is not used as a store and the fuel tank&#x27;s quick-closing valve is open.<\/li><li>Record the tests in the logbook.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>In a blackout or fire, the emergency generator keeps the ship&#x27;s safety systems running. PSC often asks for a demonstration of the automatic start.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-1/43, automatic start within 45 seconds, 18 hours for cargo ships, the list (emergency lighting, navigation lights, GMDSS, alarms, fire detection, emergency fire pump, steering), 3 hours for muster and embarkation lighting, and the weekly and on-load tests with two starting means.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"If the emergency generator can't start within 45 s, what is required?","pa":"<p>A <strong>transitional source<\/strong>, an accumulator battery, must take over at once and supply the essential services for <strong>30 minutes<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/43).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q144","sec":9,"q":"Fire divisions A/B/C.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The definitions in <strong>SOLAS II-2/3<\/strong>, and the testing in the <strong>FTP Code<\/strong>. In the UK, the <strong>MS (Fire Protection) Regulations 2023<\/strong>, with technical requirements in the MSN 1900 series.<\/p><p><strong>A-class divisions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Construction<\/strong>: <strong>steel<\/strong> or equivalent material, suitably <strong>stiffened<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Integrity<\/strong>: capable of preventing the passage of <strong>smoke and flame<\/strong> to the end of the <strong>one-hour<\/strong> standard fire test.<\/li><li><strong>Insulation<\/strong>: with approved non-combustible materials so that the average temperature on the unexposed side doesn&#x27;t rise more than <strong>140°C<\/strong> above the original, and no single point more than <strong>180°C<\/strong>, within the rated time:<\/li><li><strong>A-60<\/strong>: 60 minutes;<\/li><li><strong>A-30<\/strong>: 30 minutes;<\/li><li><strong>A-15<\/strong>: 15 minutes;<\/li><li><strong>A-0<\/strong>: no insulation time requirement, but still steel and smoke- and flame-tight for 60 minutes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>B-class divisions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Construction<\/strong>: <strong>approved non-combustible materials<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Integrity<\/strong>: capable of preventing the passage of <strong>flame<\/strong> to the end of the <strong>first 30 minutes<\/strong> of the standard fire test.<\/li><li><strong>Insulation<\/strong>: average temperature rise not more than <strong>140°C<\/strong>, and no single point more than <strong>225°C<\/strong>, within the rated time:<\/li><li><strong>B-15<\/strong>: 15 minutes;<\/li><li><strong>B-0<\/strong>: no insulation time requirement.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>C-class divisions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Construction<\/strong>: <strong>approved non-combustible materials<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>No requirements<\/strong> for the passage of smoke and flame, or for temperature rise.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Where they&#x27;re used<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>A-class<\/strong>: bounds <strong>main vertical zones<\/strong> on passenger ships, <strong>machinery spaces of category A<\/strong>, the <strong>galley<\/strong>, <strong>stairway enclosures<\/strong> and <strong>control stations<\/strong> such as the bridge and the emergency generator room.<\/li><li><strong>A-60<\/strong> typically between a machinery space and the accommodation or a control station.<\/li><li><strong>B-class<\/strong>: <strong>cabin bulkheads<\/strong> and <strong>corridor linings<\/strong> in the accommodation.<\/li><li><strong>C-class<\/strong>: <strong>low-risk internal partitions<\/strong>, such as within a cabin unit.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fire doors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The same rating as the division they&#x27;re in. Self-closing in many places, and must not be hooked back unless they have an approved hold-back that releases on alarm.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The practical point for the C/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Don&#x27;t let anyone cut holes<\/strong>, run <strong>cables<\/strong> or <strong>pipes<\/strong> through, <strong>hook back fire doors<\/strong>, or <strong>remove insulation<\/strong> without approval.<\/li><li>Any <strong>penetration<\/strong> must be <strong>restored<\/strong> with <strong>approved materials<\/strong> (fire-rated sealing systems) and <strong>recorded<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check this during inspections, especially after shipyard work or when new equipment is fitted.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A-class steel stopping smoke and flame for 60 minutes with the 140°C and 180°C insulation limits and the ratings A-60 to A-0; B-class non-combustible stopping flame for 30 minutes with 140°C and 225°C; C-class non-combustible with no requirements; where each is used; and the C/O&#x27;s duty to protect them from unauthorised penetrations and hooked-back fire doors.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What does \"A-60\" mean?","pa":"<p>An <strong>&quot;A&quot; class<\/strong> steel division that stops smoke and flame for 60 minutes, <strong>insulated<\/strong> so that the unexposed side rises no more than <strong>140 °C on average<\/strong> and <strong>180 °C at any point<\/strong> within <strong>60 minutes<\/strong> of the standard fire test.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q145","sec":9,"q":"Firemen's outfits, extinguishers, hoses — numbers.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-2/10<\/strong> and the <strong>FSS Code<\/strong> (verify the exact paragraphs). In the UK, the Fire Protection Regs 2023.<\/p><p><strong>Fireman&#x27;s outfits<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>two<\/strong> on <strong>cargo ships<\/strong>, plus <strong>two more<\/strong> on <strong>tankers<\/strong>. Passenger ships carry more, depending on length.<\/li><li>Each outfit has:<\/li><li><strong>protective clothing<\/strong> protecting the skin from heat and steam, with a water-resistant outer surface;<\/li><li><strong>boots<\/strong> and <strong>gloves<\/strong> of rubber or other electrically non-conducting material;<\/li><li>a rigid <strong>helmet<\/strong>;<\/li><li>an <strong>electric safety lamp<\/strong> of an approved type, explosion-proof, with a minimum burning period of <strong>3 hours<\/strong>;<\/li><li>an <strong>axe<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>SCBA<\/strong>, with at least 1,200 litres of free air, and <strong>spare cylinders<\/strong>: two spare charges per set;<\/li><li>a fireproof <strong>lifeline<\/strong> of sufficient length and strength.<\/li><li><strong>Two-way portable radios<\/strong>: at least <strong>two<\/strong> per fire party, <strong>explosion-proof<\/strong> or <strong>intrinsically safe<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Stowed in at least two widely separated places, ready for use.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>EEBDs<\/strong> (emergency escape breathing devices)<\/p><ul><li>In the <strong>accommodation<\/strong> (at least two on cargo ships) and in <strong>machinery spaces<\/strong> as shown on the plan, with spares.<\/li><li>For escape only, not for fighting fires.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Portable extinguishers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>five<\/strong> in the <strong>accommodation, service and control spaces<\/strong> on ships of <strong>1,000 GT<\/strong> and above, with <strong>spare charges<\/strong> (100% for the first ten, 50% for the rest) or spare extinguishers.<\/li><li>In <strong>machinery spaces<\/strong>: a <strong>portable foam applicator<\/strong> unit, and <strong>foam<\/strong> and <strong>CO₂<\/strong> extinguishers so that no point is more than <strong>10 m<\/strong> walking distance from one (verify).<\/li><li><strong>Large foam extinguishers<\/strong> (45 l or <strong>135 l<\/strong>) in boiler rooms and spaces with oil-fired equipment.<\/li><li>The type matched to the risk: CO₂ near electrical equipment, wet chemical in the galley.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Hoses<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>One per hydrant<\/strong>, and on cargo ships of <strong>1,000 GT<\/strong> and above, one for each <strong>30 m<\/strong> of ship length plus <strong>one spare<\/strong>, with at least <strong>five<\/strong> in total (verify). That doesn&#x27;t include hoses for machinery spaces.<\/li><li><strong>Dual-purpose<\/strong> (jet and spray) nozzles, with a shut-off.<\/li><li><strong>Hose length<\/strong> limited: not more than <strong>15 m<\/strong> in machinery spaces, <strong>20 m<\/strong> in other spaces and open decks, and <strong>25 m<\/strong> on open decks of ships with a breadth over 30 m (verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fire pumps<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Two main fire pumps, and an emergency fire pump in a separate space on cargo ships where a fire in one space could disable both main pumps.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>International shore connection<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>One on ships of 500 GT and over, so a shore fire main can be connected to the ship&#x27;s, with the standard flange dimensions.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Two fireman&#x27;s outfits on cargo ships plus two on tankers with the contents including the 3-hour lamp and SCBA spares, two radios per party, EEBDs, at least five portable extinguishers in the accommodation, the 10 m rule in machinery spaces, the hose numbers and lengths, and the international shore connection. Quote the numbers you&#x27;re sure of and say &quot;verify&quot; for the rest.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is in the fire control plan box at the gangway?","pa":"<p>A <strong>duplicate set of fire control plans<\/strong> (or a booklet) for shore firefighters, in a prominently marked weathertight enclosure outside the deckhouse (SOLAS II-2/15). Many companies add the crew list and the cargo or DG plan.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q146","sec":9,"q":"How do you inspect the fixed CO₂ system?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>MSC.1/Circ.1318<\/strong> (guidelines for the maintenance and inspection of fixed CO₂ fire-extinguishing systems), with <strong>SOLAS II-2/14<\/strong> (maintenance and operational readiness) and the <strong>maker&#x27;s manual<\/strong>. The ship&#x27;s crew does the <strong>routine<\/strong> checks and an <strong>approved service company<\/strong> does the annual and longer-interval work. Verify the exact intervals against the circular.<\/p><p><strong>Weekly or monthly: by the crew<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Pressure gauges<\/strong> on the pilot cylinders, and the <strong>release cabinet<\/strong> is <strong>sealed<\/strong>, with its <strong>alarm<\/strong> (switch) working when the door is opened.<\/li><li><strong>Release station instructions<\/strong> posted clearly at each release point.<\/li><li>The <strong>key<\/strong> is in place, in its glass-fronted box.<\/li><li>The <strong>cylinder room<\/strong> is <strong>clean<\/strong>, <strong>ventilated<\/strong>, at a suitable temperature, <strong>not used as a store<\/strong>, and has its own access from the open deck.<\/li><li><strong>Visual check<\/strong> for <strong>corrosion<\/strong>, <strong>damage<\/strong>, and that the <strong>flexible hoses<\/strong> and <strong>distribution valves<\/strong> are in position and secure.<\/li><li>The <strong>pre-discharge alarm<\/strong> and the ventilation trips, tested as the PMS requires, without releasing CO₂.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Annual: by the service company<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the <strong>cylinder contents<\/strong>, by <strong>weighing<\/strong> or a <strong>liquid-level gauge<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Recharge or replace<\/strong> a cylinder that has lost more than the allowed amount (about <strong>10%<\/strong>, verify).<\/li><li><strong>Blow<\/strong> the <strong>distribution pipes<\/strong> through with <strong>air<\/strong> or nitrogen to check they&#x27;re clear (or at the interval the circular sets).<\/li><li><strong>Test<\/strong> the <strong>pre-discharge alarms<\/strong> and the <strong>time delay<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>release mechanisms<\/strong>, <strong>actuators<\/strong>, <strong>pilot cylinders<\/strong> and the controls.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>nozzles<\/strong> for blockage and correct position.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Longer intervals<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Hydrostatic testing<\/strong> and internal inspection of a <strong>sample<\/strong> of cylinders, at about 10 years (verify), and cylinders after a set age.<\/li><li><strong>Flexible hoses<\/strong> renewed at the maker&#x27;s or circular interval, not exceeding <strong>10 years<\/strong> (verify).<\/li><li>Internal inspection of the main valves.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, and the <strong>service report<\/strong> is kept for the <strong>SEC survey<\/strong> and PSC.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Key facts<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Two separate controls<\/strong> for release: one to open the valve to the space, one to release the cylinders.<\/li><li>CO₂ discharge: <strong>85% within 2 minutes<\/strong> for machinery spaces.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the CO₂ room must not be used as a store<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Stores can <strong>damage<\/strong> the cylinders, pipes, release wires or valves.<\/li><li>They can <strong>obstruct<\/strong> access to the release controls in an emergency.<\/li><li>Stores can be a <strong>fire load<\/strong> in a room that must stay usable during a fire.<\/li><li>Someone could <strong>accidentally release<\/strong> CO₂ while moving stores, which is lethal.<\/li><li>It must be <strong>ventilated<\/strong>: if CO₂ leaks, a crew member in there could be asphyxiated. CO₂ is heavier than air and collects low down.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MSC.1/Circ.1318, crew checks of gauges, seals, instructions and the room; the annual contents check (weigh or level gauge) with the 10% limit, blowing through the pipes, alarm and time delay tests; longer-interval hydrostatic tests and hose renewal; the two controls and 85% in 2 minutes; and why the room isn&#x27;t a store.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★10.1","sec":9,"q":"A container with UN 3480 lithium batteries is smoking under deck. Actions?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"Lithium batteries","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>IMDG Code Amendment 42-24<\/strong> and the <strong>EmS Guide<\/strong>. <strong>UN 3480<\/strong> is <strong>lithium-ion batteries<\/strong> (including lithium-ion polymer), Class 9. <strong>EmS F-A, S-I<\/strong> (verify in the current Dangerous Goods List).<\/p><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Sound the <strong>general alarm<\/strong>, <strong>muster<\/strong>, <strong>head count<\/strong>. <strong>Master on the bridge<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Identify the box<\/strong> from the <strong>bay plan<\/strong> and <strong>DG manifest<\/strong>, and read the <strong>EmS schedule<\/strong>. Check what&#x27;s <strong>stowed next to it<\/strong>: other DG, reefers, fuel tanks.<\/li><li><strong>Seal the hold<\/strong>: fans off, vents and access closed. Isolate reefer power in the hold if safe.<\/li><li><strong>Release CO₂<\/strong> on the <strong>Master&#x27;s order<\/strong>, after confirming the hold is empty of people. <strong>CO₂ will smother<\/strong> the <strong>surrounding combustibles<\/strong>, packaging and other cargo, but it <strong>won&#x27;t stop thermal runaway<\/strong>, because the <strong>cells generate heat and gases internally<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cooling<\/strong> of the <strong>hatch covers<\/strong>, <strong>coamings<\/strong> and <strong>adjacent holds<\/strong>, for a long time. <strong>Monitor temperatures<\/strong> with a <strong>thermal camera<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Hazards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Toxic and flammable gases<\/strong>: <strong>hydrogen fluoride<\/strong>, <strong>hydrogen<\/strong>, carbon monoxide and electrolyte vapours.<\/li><li><strong>Explosion<\/strong> risk when the gases build up in a closed hold.<\/li><li><strong>Re-ignition<\/strong> <strong>hours<\/strong> or <strong>days later<\/strong>, as cells continue to go into thermal runaway.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t open the hatch at sea<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Report<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>DG incident<\/strong>: <strong>SOLAS VII/6<\/strong> (reporting incidents involving dangerous goods), and <strong>MARPOL Protocol I<\/strong> if pollution is involved.<\/li><li>The <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>flag<\/strong>, the <strong>coastal State<\/strong>, the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> for a UK ship, and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Divert<\/strong> to a <strong>port of refuge<\/strong> with <strong>shore firefighting<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At a berth, the shore fire brigade may consider <strong>flooding the hold<\/strong>, with <strong>stability<\/strong> checked carefully because of free surface and weight.<\/li><li>Discharge the container with care, with the fire service in attendance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Investigation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Was the cargo correctly declared? Lithium batteries are often misdeclared or poorly packed, and damaged batteries are banned unless specially packed.<\/li><li>Check the shipper&#x27;s DG declaration and packing certificate.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the DG declarations carefully, stow lithium batteries away from heat sources, and report suspicious bookings to the company.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>UN 3480 with its EmS, alarm and head count, identify the box and neighbours, seal and release CO₂ knowing it smothers the surroundings but doesn&#x27;t stop thermal runaway, long boundary cooling with a thermal camera, the toxic gas and re-ignition hazards, don&#x27;t open at sea, report under SOLAS VII/6, and divert to a port with shore firefighting.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why might the fire flare up again after CO₂?","pa":"<p>Thermal runaway keeps making <strong>its own heat and oxygen<\/strong>, and CO₂ barely cools. When the CO₂ concentration falls, or the hold is opened, it <strong>reignites<\/strong>, and the flammable gas that has built up can explode.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★10.2","sec":9,"q":"An electric car in a container on deck catches fire. Can CO₂ put it out?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"EV fires","a":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> A <strong>lithium-ion battery<\/strong> fire is driven by <strong>thermal runaway<\/strong>. The cells produce their <strong>own heat<\/strong> and <strong>oxygen-bearing decomposition gases<\/strong>, so <strong>smothering doesn&#x27;t stop it<\/strong>, and <strong>on deck<\/strong> CO₂ simply <strong>disperses<\/strong> anyway.<\/p><p><strong>Reference:<\/strong> the <strong>IMDG Code<\/strong> lists vehicles powered by lithium-ion batteries under a dedicated entry, <strong>UN 3556<\/strong> (Vehicle, lithium ion battery powered), since <strong>Amendment 41-22<\/strong> (verify). For ro-ro ships, MGN 653 covers electric vehicles on passenger ferries.<\/p><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Alarm<\/strong>, <strong>muster<\/strong>, <strong>Master on the bridge<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Manoeuvre<\/strong> to keep <strong>smoke and gases<\/strong> <strong>off the accommodation<\/strong> and the bridge: put the fire downwind.<\/li><li><strong>Identify the box<\/strong> and its <strong>neighbours<\/strong> from the <strong>bay plan<\/strong> and <strong>DG list<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cool, cool, cool<\/strong>: <strong>large<\/strong> and <strong>prolonged<\/strong> quantities of <strong>water<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Use the <strong>container piercing lance<\/strong> (water-mist lance, SOLAS II-2/10.7.3) to get <strong>water inside the box<\/strong>, close to the battery.<\/li><li>Use <strong>mobile monitors<\/strong> to <strong>cool<\/strong> the <strong>adjacent stacks<\/strong> and the <strong>hatch cover<\/strong> below.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Fire team<\/strong> in <strong>full SCBA<\/strong>, working from upwind and at a distance where possible.<\/li><li><strong>Expect<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>toxic gases<\/strong>, including <strong>hydrogen fluoride<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>jet flames<\/strong> from venting cells;<\/li><li><strong>explosions<\/strong>, as gases build up inside the container;<\/li><li><strong>re-ignition<\/strong> <strong>hours or days<\/strong> later.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Watch <strong>stability<\/strong>, and <strong>drain<\/strong> the water from the hatch tops and decks.<\/li><li><strong>Keep cooling<\/strong> after the flames have stopped, and <strong>monitor<\/strong> with a <strong>thermal camera<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Report<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To the <strong>company<\/strong>, the <strong>flag<\/strong> and the <strong>coastal State<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong> for a UK ship.<\/li><li>DG incident report under <strong>SOLAS VII/6<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Consider a <strong>port of refuge<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After the fire<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The box stays under watch and cooling until it can be landed. Don&#x27;t open it at sea.<\/li><li>Any water run-off may be contaminated.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><p>Many recent container and car carrier fires have involved lithium batteries and EVs. He wants to see that I don&#x27;t rely on CO₂ or extinguishers that worked for conventional fires.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No, CO₂ doesn&#x27;t stop thermal runaway and disperses on deck; UN 3556; alarm and manoeuvre to keep gases off the accommodation; identify the box; huge amounts of water with the piercing lance and monitors; SCBA; expect HF gas, explosions and re-ignition; stability; keep cooling and monitor; and report.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How long do you keep cooling after the flames stop?","pa":"<p>For <strong>hours<\/strong>, until the battery temperature is stable and venting has stopped, checked with a <strong>thermal imager<\/strong>. Keep a re-ignition watch for <strong>a day or more<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q147","sec":10,"q":"Lifeboat inspections: weekly, monthly, annual, 5-yearly; 5-yearly test procedure; who is involved; dangers.","tier":"red","rep":85,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Life-Saving Appliances and Arrangements) Regs 2020<\/strong>, with technical requirements in <strong>MSN 1676 (M+F) Amd 2<\/strong>, and <strong>MGN 560 (M) Amd 2<\/strong> on maintenance, examination and testing. Internationally, <strong>SOLAS III/20<\/strong> and <strong>MSC.402(96)<\/strong>, which since <strong>1 January 2020<\/strong> requires the <strong>annual and 5-yearly<\/strong> work to be done by a <strong>certified, authorised service provider<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Weekly: by the crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Visual check<\/strong> of the boats, davits, falls and release gear: ready for immediate use.<\/li><li><strong>Run the engines<\/strong> ahead and astern for at least <strong>3 minutes<\/strong> in total, with cooling water if needed.<\/li><li><strong>Test the general alarm<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the battery charging and the lights.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Monthly: by the crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Inspection<\/strong> against the <strong>SOLAS III/36<\/strong> checklist: equipment, <strong>pyrotechnics<\/strong> and expiry dates, <strong>provisions<\/strong> and water, first aid kit, the engine and fuel.<\/li><li>Recorded in the <strong>log<\/strong> and the PMS.<\/li><li>Turn out the boats from their stowed position, without people, where weather allows.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Every 3 months<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Each lifeboat <strong>launched<\/strong> with its operating crew and <strong>manoeuvred<\/strong> in the water (SOLAS III/19).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Annual: by the service provider<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Thorough examination<\/strong> and <strong>operational test<\/strong> of the <strong>boat<\/strong>, the <strong>release gear<\/strong> (on-load and off-load), and the <strong>davit<\/strong> and winch.<\/li><li><strong>Dynamic test<\/strong> of the <strong>winch brake<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A report and certificate issued.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5-yearly: by the service provider<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Overhaul<\/strong> of the <strong>release gear<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Operational test<\/strong> of the <strong>on-load release<\/strong> with <strong>1.1 × the total mass<\/strong> of the fully loaded boat (persons and equipment).<\/li><li><strong>Winch brake dynamic test<\/strong> at <strong>1.1 × the winch&#x27;s maximum working load<\/strong>: lowered at full speed and stopped on the brake.<\/li><li>Afterwards the stressed parts are re-examined.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Falls<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Turned end-for-end<\/strong> at intervals of not more than <strong>30 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Renewed<\/strong> at <strong>5 years<\/strong>, or earlier if they deteriorate.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who&#x27;s involved<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>service engineer<\/strong>, who does the work.<\/li><li>The <strong>C/O<\/strong>, in charge for the ship, and the <strong>bosun<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>class or RO surveyor<\/strong>, if attending, who witnesses.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Dangers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Hook release under load<\/strong> that isn&#x27;t fully reset, or unintended release.<\/li><li><strong>Falls parting<\/strong>, or the winch brake failing.<\/li><li><strong>Nobody in the boat during a load test<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fall-prevention devices (FPDs)<\/strong> can be used as a risk control during maintenance and drills, but <strong>never as permanent fittings<\/strong> and never left on when the boat is needed for real.<\/li><li>Many seafarers have died in lifeboat accidents during drills and tests, which is why the rules were tightened.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The weekly, monthly, annual and 5-yearly checks and who does them, the 1.1 test loads, falls end-for-ended at 30 months and renewed at 5 years, MSC.402(96) and MGN 560, who&#x27;s present, and the dangers with nobody in the boat under test.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why did MSC.402(96) take this work away from ship's crew?","pa":"<p>Many accidents came from crews <strong>not understanding<\/strong> or wrongly maintaining release gear. The work needs type-specific training, tools and parts, so it&#x27;s now done by <strong>manufacturer-authorised service providers<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["Lifeboat drill frequency for launching?","How will you carry out simulated launch on free fall lifeboat?","What is the type of lifeboat you had onboard?","Where will you find the information about lifeboat inspection?","What is the regulation for Lifeboat launching appliances ?"],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",19],["Colum Leggett",17],["Kaiser Ali",13],["Khairul Anam",12],["Shahid Akhter",7]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q148","sec":10,"q":"Load for a 5-yearly test on a 10 t SWL boat?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>MGN 560 Amd 2<\/strong> and <strong>MSC.402(96)<\/strong>: the on-load release gear test uses <strong>1.1 × the total mass<\/strong> of the lifeboat when <strong>loaded with its full complement of persons and equipment<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>First, confirm what the 10 t figure is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Take it from the boat&#x27;s <strong>data plate<\/strong> and the <strong>davit and winch certificate<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Is it the <strong>davit&#x27;s SWL<\/strong>, or the <strong>fully loaded boat mass<\/strong>: hull + equipment + persons at <strong>75 kg<\/strong> each (or 82.5 kg on newer boats, verify)? Those are <strong>different numbers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The examiner is testing whether I know the difference.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If 10 t is the fully loaded boat mass<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The test load is <strong>1.1 × 10 = 11 t<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s made up of <strong>water bags<\/strong> or <strong>weights<\/strong>, placed <strong>evenly<\/strong> in the boat, so the load on each hook is right.<\/li><li>The weights or water bags are <strong>certified<\/strong>, and the total is checked with a <strong>calibrated load cell<\/strong> if possible.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If 10 t is the davit SWL<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I need the fully loaded boat mass from the boat&#x27;s data plate, which should be less than the davit SWL. The release gear test is based on the boat mass, not the davit SWL.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The winch brake dynamic test<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At <strong>1.1 × the winch&#x27;s maximum working load<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Lowering at full speed<\/strong>, then applying the brake <strong>abruptly<\/strong> to stop it.<\/li><li>The stopping distance and the brake performance are checked against the maker&#x27;s limits.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Procedure<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>An <strong>approved service provider<\/strong> does the test, under MSC.402(96).<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong>; the area below cleared.<\/li><li>The boat is lowered to <strong>just clear of the water<\/strong> or the ground.<\/li><li>The <strong>load<\/strong> is applied, and the <strong>release<\/strong> is tested under load as the maker specifies.<\/li><li>Afterwards, the <strong>hooks<\/strong>, the <strong>release cable<\/strong>, the <strong>davit<\/strong> and the falls are <strong>examined<\/strong> for deformation and damage.<\/li><li>The <strong>certificate<\/strong> is issued and kept for the SEC survey.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>No persons in the boat<\/strong> during the test.<\/li><li>Use <strong>FPDs<\/strong> while the weights are being loaded, and remove them before the test.<\/li><li>Keep people out from under the boat and away from the falls.<\/li><li>Communications between the winch operator, the engineer and the officer in charge.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Davit test<\/strong><\/p><p>The davit itself is also tested at installation and after modification, at <strong>1.1 × SWL<\/strong> or as the maker and class specify.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>First confirm what 10 t is, from the data plate; the release gear test is 1.1 × the fully loaded boat mass, so 11 t if that&#x27;s the figure; water bags placed evenly; the winch brake dynamic test at 1.1 × the maximum working load; done by the service provider; and nobody in the boat.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you check on the hooks after the test?","pa":"<p>Check the hooks and release gear for <strong>deformation, cracks and wear<\/strong>. Operate the release and check it <strong>resets correctly<\/strong>, with the lock indicators in the right position. Then make the report and certificate entries.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q149","sec":10,"q":"On-load / off-load release; the hook replacement deadline.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Off-load release<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The hooks <strong>open only<\/strong> when the boat is <strong>waterborne<\/strong> and the <strong>weight is off<\/strong> the falls.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>simple<\/strong> and <strong>safe<\/strong>, because it can&#x27;t release with load on.<\/li><li>But you <strong>can&#x27;t release in a seaway<\/strong> with load still on the falls: in waves, the boat is lifted and dropped, and the falls never go slack, so the boat can be dragged and smashed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On-load release<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The hooks <strong>can be released<\/strong> while the <strong>load is still on<\/strong> them.<\/li><li>Released by a <strong>handle<\/strong> with <strong>two separate actions<\/strong>, behind a <strong>guard<\/strong>, so it can&#x27;t be done accidentally.<\/li><li>This lets you <strong>get away in a seaway<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It also caused <strong>many fatal accidents<\/strong>, when hooks were <strong>wrongly reset<\/strong> or <strong>released unintentionally<\/strong> during drills and maintenance, and the boat fell.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Hydrostatic interlock<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Prevents on-load release<\/strong> until the boat is <strong>waterborne<\/strong>, unless it&#x27;s <strong>deliberately overridden<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The override is for an emergency where the boat must be released above the water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The hook replacement deadline<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS III/1.5<\/strong> and <strong>MSC.1/Circ.1392<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Every <strong>on-load release hook<\/strong> had to be <strong>assessed<\/strong> against the <strong>revised LSA Code<\/strong> requirements (<strong>MSC.320(89)<\/strong>): stable hook design, no release under the boat&#x27;s weight unless operated, and so on.<\/li><li><strong>Non-compliant hooks<\/strong> had to be <strong>replaced<\/strong> no later than the <strong>first scheduled dry-docking after 1 July 2014<\/strong>, and in any case <strong>not later than 1 July 2019<\/strong>.<\/li><li>After replacement, the <strong>5-knot test<\/strong> may be required for some boats, and the crew trained on the new gear.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Crew points (MGN 560 Amd 2)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Resetting the hook is a critical task<\/strong>. Check <strong>visually<\/strong> that it&#x27;s <strong>fully reset<\/strong>: the <strong>indicator<\/strong> shows reset, the <strong>cam<\/strong> is in position, the release handle is in the closed position with the safety pin.<\/li><li>Follow the <strong>maker&#x27;s instructions<\/strong> exactly.<\/li><li><strong>Train<\/strong> the crew on the specific type of hook fitted, because they differ.<\/li><li><strong>Never<\/strong> adjust or strip the release gear: that&#x27;s for the authorised service provider under MSC.402(96).<\/li><li>Use <strong>fall-prevention devices<\/strong> during drills and maintenance where company policy requires.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t be in the boat when it&#x27;s raised or lowered unless the drill requires it, and use the minimum crew.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Free-fall lifeboats<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Released by a hydraulic release from inside the boat, with a secondary launching arrangement for recovery and maintenance.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Off-load only releases when waterborne and can&#x27;t be used in a seaway; on-load releases under load with two actions behind a guard, which caused fatal accidents; the hydrostatic interlock; the replacement under SOLAS III/1.5 and MSC.1/Circ.1392 by the first dry-docking after 1 July 2014 and no later than 1 July 2019; and resetting the hook correctly.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you confirm the hook is properly reset after a drill?","pa":"<p>Check against the manufacturer&#x27;s manual and poster:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>visual indicator<\/strong> shows reset;<\/li><li>the cam and locking pin are aligned;<\/li><li>the hydrostatic interlock is reset;<\/li><li>the handle is in the closed or reset position with its <strong>safety pin fitted<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>both hooks<\/strong> are checked.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q150","sec":10,"q":"Operational test of a liferaft davit at a joining port during a safety equipment survey.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS III/20<\/strong>, and the IMO guidance on testing davit-launched liferaft automatic release hooks (<strong>MSC.1/Circ.1206 Rev.1<\/strong>, verify), with MGN 560 Amd 2.<\/p><p><strong>Before the surveyor arrives (joining port)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Review the <strong>handover notes<\/strong>, the <strong>last service report<\/strong> and the <strong>PMS<\/strong>: when was the davit last serviced, when were the hook and wire tested?<\/li><li>Check the <strong>instructions<\/strong> at the davit, the <strong>liferaft<\/strong> service date, and that a <strong>dummy weight<\/strong> or test raft is available.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong>, <strong>permit<\/strong>, <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Operational test of the automatic release hook<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Manual release<\/strong> with a load on the hook, about <strong>150 kg<\/strong> (verify the test load).<\/li><li><strong>Automatic release<\/strong> with a <strong>dummy weight<\/strong> of about <strong>200 kg<\/strong> (verify), lowered until it <strong>lands<\/strong> on the ground, quay or water, so the hook <strong>releases automatically<\/strong> when the <strong>load comes off<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Examine<\/strong> the hook and its fastening: <strong>fully reset<\/strong>, <strong>no damage<\/strong>, no wear or corrosion.<\/li><li>If a <strong>real raft<\/strong> is used instead of a dummy weight, the hook must <strong>release it automatically<\/strong> once <strong>waterborne<\/strong>. Then the raft must be recovered and re-packed by a service station, so a dummy weight is normally used.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Also test the davit<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Swing<\/strong> the davit <strong>out and in<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Test the <strong>winch brake<\/strong> and the <strong>lowering speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>wire fall<\/strong>: no broken wires, corrosion or kinks, and its renewal date.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>lowering and recovery<\/strong> gear, and <strong>power or manual<\/strong> recovery.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>bowsing lines<\/strong> and the <strong>embarkation<\/strong> arrangements.<\/li><li>The davit must be <strong>serviced<\/strong> within the <strong>annual<\/strong> interval by the service provider.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5-yearly<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>load test<\/strong> of the davit and hook, normally at <strong>1.1 ×<\/strong> the total mass of the fully loaded liferaft, by the service provider.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>service technician<\/strong> or <strong>competent person<\/strong>, witnessed by the <strong>surveyor<\/strong>, with the <strong>C/O<\/strong> and <strong>bosun<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Keep a <strong>clear area<\/strong> under the load.<\/li><li>Communications between the winch operator and the officer in charge.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Results in the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, and the service provider&#x27;s report for the SEC survey.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Training<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Davit-launched liferaft <strong>training<\/strong> on board every <strong>4 months<\/strong> (SOLAS III/19.4.3), including inflating and lowering a training raft if fitted, or a demonstration.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The operational test of the automatic hook (manual release under load, automatic release when the load comes off with a dummy weight), the davit swing-out, winch brake, wire and recovery checks, who does and witnesses it, a clear area, and records. Say &quot;verify&quot; for the exact test loads.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the annual load test on the davit?","pa":"<p>The <strong>annual<\/strong> thorough examination includes an operational test. The <strong>proof-load test at 1.1 × SWL<\/strong> is done at least <strong>every 5 years<\/strong> by the service provider (MSC.402(96), MGN 560).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q151","sec":10,"q":"Extra equipment in a boat that is both lifeboat and rescue boat.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS III/31<\/strong> and the <strong>LSA Code<\/strong> (Chapter IV lifeboat equipment, Chapter V rescue boat requirements). In the UK, the <strong>LSA Regs 2020<\/strong> and <strong>MSN 1676 Amd 2<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The principle<\/strong><\/p><p>A boat that&#x27;s both a <strong>lifeboat<\/strong> and a <strong>rescue boat<\/strong> must meet <strong>both<\/strong> the lifeboat and the rescue boat standards, including:<\/p><ul><li><strong>manoeuvrability<\/strong> to rescue persons in the water and marshal liferafts;<\/li><li>being able to <strong>tow the largest liferaft<\/strong> carried, when loaded, at <strong>2 knots<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>recovery<\/strong> arrangements for persons from the water;<\/li><li>launching and <strong>recovery<\/strong> quickly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Extra equipment<\/strong><\/p><p>In addition to the normal lifeboat equipment, it carries the <strong>rescue-boat items<\/strong> not already on board, for example:<\/p><ol><li>A <strong>buoyant towline<\/strong> long enough to <strong>tow a liferaft<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Buoyant rescue quoits<\/strong> with <strong>buoyant lines<\/strong> of at least <strong>30 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>searchlight<\/strong>, a <strong>waterproof torch<\/strong> and a <strong>radar reflector<\/strong> (or SART).<\/li><li><strong>Thermal protective aids<\/strong>, a <strong>first aid kit<\/strong>, <strong>boat hooks<\/strong>, a <strong>bailer<\/strong> and <strong>buckets<\/strong>, and a <strong>knife<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Means of <strong>recovering persons<\/strong> from the water, such as a <strong>scramble net<\/strong>, a recovery strop or a boarding ladder.<\/li><li>A <strong>towing post<\/strong> or arrangement strong enough to tow liferafts.<\/li><\/ol><p>(Check the exact list against the LSA Code before quoting.)<\/p><p><strong>Operational differences<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>It must <strong>launch and recover quickly<\/strong> with the ship making <strong>headway<\/strong> of up to <strong>5 knots<\/strong> in calm water.<\/li><li>It needs a <strong>trained rescue-boat crew<\/strong> (PSC&amp;RB and, for fast rescue boats, the FRB course).<\/li><li><strong>Launching and manoeuvring<\/strong>: <strong>monthly<\/strong> where reasonable, and at least every <strong>3 months<\/strong> (SOLAS III/19.3.3).<\/li><li>The <strong>recovery<\/strong> arrangements must allow recovery with the full rescue crew and a casualty, in a seaway, quickly.<\/li><li>It must be <strong>ready<\/strong> for use within <strong>5 minutes<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The release gear and davit are suitable for both purposes, with the 5-knot test where required.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Many cargo ships use one of their lifeboats as the rescue boat, especially older ships. The examiner wants to see I know it then has to meet the rescue boat requirements for equipment, crew and drills.<\/p><p><strong>On a free-fall ship<\/strong><\/p><p>The free-fall boat isn&#x27;t a rescue boat, so there&#x27;s a separate rescue boat, often on davits.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS III/31 and the LSA Code, meeting both standards (manoeuvrable, tow the largest raft at 2 knots, recovery), the extra rescue boat equipment (towline, quoits, searchlight, recovery net), and the operational differences: launch at 5 knots, trained crew, monthly launching where reasonable and at least every 3 months.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How fast must a rescue boat be launched and recovered?","pa":"<p><strong>Ready for launching within 5 minutes<\/strong>. Able to be <strong>recovered within 5 minutes<\/strong> in moderate seas with its full complement and equipment (SOLAS III/17 and III/31).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q152","sec":10,"q":"Equipment in a liferaft; launching a davit-launched and a throw-over raft.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>LSA Code Chapter IV<\/strong>. In the UK, the <strong>LSA Regs 2020<\/strong> and <strong>MSN 1676 Amd 2<\/strong>, and <strong>MGN 560 Amd 2<\/strong> for servicing.<\/p><p><strong>SOLAS A pack: the main items<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Rescue and handling<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>rescue quoit<\/strong> on at least <strong>30 m<\/strong> of buoyant line.<\/li><li>A <strong>safety knife<\/strong> with a floating handle.<\/li><li><strong>Bailers<\/strong> and <strong>sponges<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Two sea anchors<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Paddles<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>whistle<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Signalling and first aid<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>first aid kit<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>waterproof torch<\/strong> with spare batteries and bulb.<\/li><li>A <strong>radar reflector<\/strong> (unless a SART is stowed in the raft).<\/li><li>A <strong>signalling mirror<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>life-saving signals<\/strong> table.<\/li><li><strong>4 rocket parachute flares<\/strong>, <strong>6 hand flares<\/strong>, <strong>2 buoyant smoke signals<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Survival<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Water<\/strong>: <strong>1.5 litres per person<\/strong>, and a drinking vessel.<\/li><li><strong>Food rations<\/strong>: <strong>10,000 kJ per person<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Seasickness tablets<\/strong> (enough for 48 hours) and <strong>seasickness bags<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fishing tackle<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Survival<\/strong> and <strong>immediate-action instructions<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Thermal protective aids<\/strong>: for <strong>10%<\/strong> of the persons or <strong>two<\/strong>, whichever is greater.<\/li><li>A <strong>repair kit<\/strong> and a <strong>pump<\/strong> or bellows for topping up the buoyancy tubes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Throw-over raft: launching<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the <strong>painter is made fast<\/strong> to a strong point on the ship (the weak link and painter are attached), and the <strong>water below is clear<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Free the lashing<\/strong> (the slip hook or the HRU&#x27;s manual release), and <strong>throw the raft over<\/strong>, ideally from the lee side.<\/li><li><strong>Pull the painter<\/strong> until it <strong>inflates<\/strong>, about 30 seconds after the gas bottle fires.<\/li><li><strong>Bring it alongside<\/strong> the embarkation ladder.<\/li><li><strong>Board dry<\/strong> if possible, by the ladder. Don&#x27;t jump onto the canopy.<\/li><li><strong>Cut the painter<\/strong> with the safety knife, and <strong>get clear<\/strong> of the ship, using the paddles.<\/li><li>Stream the sea anchor, close the entrances, and keep the survivors warm and together.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Automatic release<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>HRU<\/strong> releases the raft automatically if the ship sinks, at about 2–4 m depth.<\/li><li>The raft floats up, the painter pulls tight and fires the inflation.<\/li><li>The <strong>weak link<\/strong> breaks once the raft is inflated, so the raft isn&#x27;t dragged down.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Davit-launched raft: launching<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Hook the raft<\/strong> to the davit&#x27;s automatic release hook, and swing it out.<\/li><li><strong>Inflate<\/strong> it at <strong>embarkation level<\/strong>, held alongside by the <strong>bowsing lines<\/strong> and the painter.<\/li><li><strong>Board<\/strong> the raft, and set the <strong>automatic hook<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Lower<\/strong> the raft, and it <strong>releases automatically<\/strong> when <strong>waterborne<\/strong> (or by manual release).<\/li><li>Get clear of the ship.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The main items of the SOLAS A pack including the pyrotechnics, water 1.5 l and food 10,000 kJ per person and TPAs for 10% or two; the throw-over launch with the painter made fast, inflate, board dry, cut and clear; the HRU and weak link; and the davit-launched sequence.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How often are liferafts and HRUs serviced?","pa":"<p>Liferafts every <strong>12 months<\/strong>, extendable to 17 months in exceptional cases, at an approved station. <strong>Disposable HRUs<\/strong> are replaced at expiry, normally 2 years. Non-disposable HRUs are serviced every 12 months.<\/p>","asked":["Differences between davit launched and Throw over the side."],"ex":[["Alec Keep",1]],"yrs":"2023–2023"},{"id":"Q153","sec":10,"q":"Drill frequencies.","tier":"red","rep":68,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Musters, Training and Decision Support Systems) Regs 1999<\/strong>, with guidance in <strong>MGN 71 (M+F) Amd 1<\/strong>. Internationally, <strong>SOLAS III/19<\/strong> and <strong>V/26<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Abandon ship and fire drills<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Monthly<\/strong> each, for <strong>every crew member<\/strong>: each takes part in at least one abandon ship drill and one fire drill a month.<\/li><li><strong>After a crew change<\/strong>: <strong>within 24 hours<\/strong> of leaving port if <strong>more than 25%<\/strong> of the crew have <strong>not<\/strong> taken part in those drills <strong>on that ship<\/strong> in the previous month.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Lifeboats<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Each lifeboat <strong>launched<\/strong> with its assigned <strong>operating crew<\/strong> and <strong>manoeuvred in the water<\/strong> at least every <strong>3 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Free-fall lifeboat<\/strong>: launched with crew every <strong>6 months<\/strong>. A <strong>simulated<\/strong> launch may be accepted, provided there&#x27;s an <strong>actual launch<\/strong> at least every <strong>12 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Lifeboats and launching appliances turned out and checked during the monthly abandon ship drill.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rescue boat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Launched and manoeuvred<\/strong>: <strong>monthly<\/strong> where reasonable, and at least every <strong>3 months<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Davit-launched liferaft training<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every <strong>4 months<\/strong>, including inflation and lowering of a training raft where carried.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Enclosed space entry and rescue<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every <strong>2 months<\/strong> (SOLAS III/19.3.6).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Emergency steering<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every <strong>3 months<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS V/26<\/strong>).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other requirements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Steering gear<\/strong> test within <strong>12 hours<\/strong> before departure.<\/li><li><strong>Fire drills<\/strong> use the equipment: a fire pump with at least <strong>two jets<\/strong>, the fireman&#x27;s outfits, communications, fire doors and dampers, and at least one extinguisher where practicable.<\/li><li><strong>Damage control<\/strong> drills on passenger ships every 3 months.<\/li><li><strong>Security drills<\/strong> every 3 months (ISPS B/13.6).<\/li><li><strong>Immersion suit<\/strong> competence comes from familiarisation and training, with each person trying on their suit as required.<\/li><li><strong>Onboard training<\/strong> in the use of LSA within <strong>2 weeks<\/strong> of joining, and instruction in the use of LSA and survival at sea every month in the abandon ship drill.<\/li><li><strong>Emergency lighting<\/strong> tested at each abandon ship drill.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Record <strong>every drill<\/strong> in the <strong>official log book<\/strong>, with the details, and in the training records.<\/li><li>If a drill isn&#x27;t held, record the reason.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Making them realistic<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Drills shall be conducted as far as practicable as if there were an actual emergency (III/19.3.1), varied and debriefed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A correction<\/strong><\/p><p>My notes said to avoid quoting MGN 71. That&#x27;s wrong: <strong>MGN 71 (M+F) Amd 1<\/strong> is the current UK guidance on musters and drills, under the 1999 Regs.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The table: abandon ship and fire monthly, within 24 hours after a 25% crew change, lifeboat launched every 3 months, free-fall every 6 months with actual launch every 12, rescue boat monthly or at least 3 months, davit-launched liferaft training every 4 months, enclosed space every 2 months, emergency steering every 3 months, and records in the OLB.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you do if a drill can't be held because of weather?","pa":"<p>Record the <strong>reason<\/strong> in the OLB and hold it at the <strong>first opportunity<\/strong>. A muster-only or modified drill may be possible. Stay within the required frequencies.<\/p>","asked":["How will u conduct enclosed space drill and frequency?","How will you prepare for drill and training?","How will you carry out the drills?","Implement corrective action plan","Training and drills as per SOLAS?"],"ex":[["Robert Jackson",8],["Michele Moro",8],["Colum Leggett",7],["Khairul Anam",7],["Inamul Bhuiyan",6]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q154","sec":10,"q":"LSA audit preparation; who certifies the davit; who may dismantle it?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, <strong>MGN 560 Amd 2<\/strong>, the <strong>LSA Regs 2020<\/strong> and <strong>MSN 1676 Amd 2<\/strong>, with <strong>MSC.402(96)<\/strong>. The <strong>Safety Equipment survey<\/strong> is done by the <strong>MCA<\/strong> or an <strong>RO<\/strong> surveyor.<\/p><p><strong>Preparation (C/O, with the 3/O who usually looks after the LSA)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>PMS<\/strong> up to date:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the <strong>weekly<\/strong> and <strong>monthly<\/strong> checklists (<strong>SOLAS III/36<\/strong>) signed;<\/li><li>defects <strong>closed<\/strong>, or reported with a plan.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Service certificates<\/strong> in date:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>annual<\/strong> and <strong>5-yearly<\/strong> lifeboat, release gear and davit reports;<\/li><li><strong>liferaft<\/strong> and <strong>HRU<\/strong> servicing;<\/li><li><strong>inflatable lifejackets<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>immersion suits<\/strong>, with air pressure tests where due;<\/li><li><strong>EPIRB<\/strong> and <strong>SART<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>pyrotechnic<\/strong> expiry dates;<\/li><li>the <strong>line-throwing appliance<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Falls<\/strong> end-for-ended and <strong>renewal dates<\/strong> checked.<\/li><li><strong>Drills<\/strong> logged in the OLB, with the lifeboat launches every 3 months.<\/li><li><strong>Training manual<\/strong>, <strong>maintenance instructions<\/strong> and the <strong>muster list<\/strong> up to date, with the new crew names.<\/li><li><strong>Signs<\/strong> and <strong>photoluminescent markings<\/strong> in good condition, the IMO symbols at the LSA, the operating instructions posted.<\/li><li><strong>Boats and rafts<\/strong> physically checked: engines start, lights work, equipment complete and in date, the boats&#x27; water and rations, the hooks fully reset.<\/li><li><strong>Lifebuoys<\/strong>: lights, smoke signals, lines, positions as on the plan.<\/li><li><strong>Lifejackets<\/strong> in the cabins, at the work stations and on the bridge, with lights and whistles.<\/li><li><strong>Form E<\/strong>: everything on it is on board and in the right place.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Who certifies the davit<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>certified service provider<\/strong>, approved by the maker and the Administration under <strong>MSC.402(96)<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 555 Amd 1<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It issues the <strong>statement<\/strong> or <strong>report<\/strong> for the <strong>annual<\/strong> and <strong>5-yearly<\/strong> examinations, which the <strong>surveyor accepts<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The surveyor witnesses tests and endorses the certificate.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who may dismantle it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Only<\/strong> that <strong>certified service provider<\/strong> may <strong>dismantle<\/strong>, <strong>overhaul<\/strong> or <strong>adjust<\/strong> the <strong>release gear<\/strong> and the <strong>davit and winch brakes<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>crew<\/strong> do the <strong>routine maintenance<\/strong> in the maker&#x27;s manual: <strong>greasing<\/strong>, <strong>cleaning<\/strong>, <strong>visual checks<\/strong>, the weekly and monthly inspections.<\/li><li>The crew must <strong>not strip the release gear<\/strong> or <strong>adjust brakes<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>During the survey<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An officer goes with the surveyor, with the records.<\/li><li>The surveyor may ask for a lifeboat to be lowered, the engine run, the emergency lighting tested, and crew to demonstrate.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A PMS and records check, service certificates with dates listed, falls, drills, the training manual and muster list, physical checks against Form E; the certified service provider under MSC.402(96) certifies and is the only one who may dismantle the release gear and brakes; the crew do routine maintenance only.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What would you do if the surveyor finds an expired hydrostatic release unit?","pa":"<p>Replace it <strong>immediately<\/strong> from spares. If no spare is on board, it&#x27;s a deficiency: the raft may not float free. It affects the SEC. Record it and raise an NC on why the PMS missed it.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q155","sec":10,"q":"LOLER/PUWER: thorough examination intervals, proof loads, rocking test, register of lifting appliances, loose gear, wire discard, what crew may check.","tier":"red","rep":89,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment) Regs 2006 (LOLER)<\/strong>, guidance <strong>MGN 332 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong>, and the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Provision and Use of Work Equipment) Regs 2006 (PUWER)<\/strong>, guidance <strong>MGN 331 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong>, with <strong>COSWP<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Thorough examination by a competent person<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every <strong>12 months<\/strong> for lifting equipment generally.<\/li><li>Every <strong>6 months<\/strong> for equipment <strong>lifting persons<\/strong> and for <strong>lifting accessories<\/strong> (loose gear).<\/li><li>Or in accordance with an <strong>examination scheme<\/strong> drawn up by a competent person.<\/li><li>Also after <strong>installation<\/strong>, after <strong>major repair<\/strong> or modification, and after <strong>exceptional circumstances<\/strong> such as an overload, a collision or a long period out of use.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Testing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every <strong>5 years<\/strong> (the ILO standard) and after <strong>repair<\/strong> affecting strength. Proof loads:<\/li><li><strong>1.25 × SWL<\/strong> up to <strong>20 t<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>SWL + 5 t<\/strong> from <strong>20 to 50 t<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>1.1 × SWL<\/strong> above <strong>50 t<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Loose gear has its own proof loads, for example 2 × SWL for chains and hooks up to 25 t (verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rocking test<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On a <strong>crane slewing bearing<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Raise the jib, <strong>rock<\/strong> it fore-and-aft by applying and removing a load or moment, and <strong>measure<\/strong> the <strong>bearing clearance<\/strong> with a dial gauge against the <strong>maker&#x27;s limit<\/strong>.<\/li><li>This detects <strong>wear<\/strong> in the <strong>slewing ring<\/strong>, which can lead to the crane collapsing.<\/li><li>Done at intervals set by the maker, often annually.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records: the register<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Reports<\/strong> of thorough examination, <strong>test certificates<\/strong>, and the <strong>register of lifting appliances<\/strong> and loose gear.<\/li><li>Each item <strong>uniquely marked<\/strong> with its <strong>SWL<\/strong> and identification.<\/li><li>Kept on board for inspection.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Wire rope discard<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>More than <strong>10% broken wires<\/strong> in a length of <strong>8 diameters<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Also discard for <strong>kinks<\/strong>, <strong>bird-caging<\/strong>, <strong>heavy corrosion<\/strong>, <strong>crushing<\/strong>, heat damage, or a <strong>marked reduction in diameter<\/strong> (often 10% of nominal, verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the crew may do<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Pre-use checks<\/strong>: visual inspection of hooks, wires, shackles, limit switches, brakes.<\/li><li>The <strong>routine inspection<\/strong>, <strong>greasing<\/strong> and <strong>maintenance<\/strong> in the <strong>PMS<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Take <strong>defective gear out of use<\/strong> and <strong>quarantine<\/strong> it, tagged.<\/li><li>Crew <strong>may not<\/strong> carry out a <strong>thorough examination<\/strong> unless they&#x27;re a <strong>designated competent person<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>PUWER<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Work equipment must be <strong>suitable<\/strong>, <strong>maintained<\/strong>, inspected, and used only by <strong>trained<\/strong> people, with guards and controls.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Lifting operations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Planned<\/strong> by a competent person, <strong>supervised<\/strong>, with a lift plan, a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>, a <strong>banksman<\/strong> and communications.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>LOLER and PUWER 2006 with MGN 332 and 331, thorough examination every 12 months or 6 months for people-lifting and accessories, the proof loads (1.25, +5 t, 1.1), the rocking test, the register, the wire discard criteria, and what the crew may and may not do.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What must a report of thorough examination contain?","pa":"<p>The report of thorough examination contains:<\/p><ul><li>the employer and the <strong>equipment identification<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the dates of this exam and the last one;<\/li><li>the <strong>SWL<\/strong>;<\/li><li>whether it&#x27;s <strong>safe to operate<\/strong>;<\/li><li>any defects, the danger they pose, the repairs needed and by when;<\/li><li>the date of the next examination or test;<\/li><li>the <strong>competent person&#x27;s<\/strong> details and signature.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["Tests carried out on Cranes ?","What is Loler and Puwer?","Inspection frequency for the lifting appliances?","What is a Competent person?","Checks on lifting equipment. What Inspection do you carry out for cranes?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",11],["Doug Smith",11],["Khairul Anam",11],["Khademul Insan",10],["Chris Roe",9]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q156","sec":10,"q":"Accommodation ladder / gangway requirements and falls renewal.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Means of Access) Regs 1988<\/strong> and <strong>COSWP<\/strong>. Internationally, <strong>SOLAS II-1/3-9<\/strong> and <strong>MSC.1/Circ.1331<\/strong> (guidelines for the construction, installation, maintenance and inspection of accommodation ladders and gangways).<\/p><p><strong>Requirements<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>safe means of access<\/strong>, properly <strong>rigged<\/strong> and <strong>secured<\/strong>, with <strong>handrails<\/strong> or <strong>ropes<\/strong> on both sides.<\/li><li>A <strong>safety net<\/strong> beneath it, where there&#x27;s a risk of falling into the water or onto the quay.<\/li><li><strong>Adequate lighting<\/strong> at night.<\/li><li>A <strong>lifebuoy<\/strong> with a <strong>self-activating light<\/strong> and a <strong>line<\/strong> at the access point.<\/li><li><strong>Marked<\/strong> with the <strong>maximum safe loading<\/strong> (number of persons or weight) and the <strong>angle limits<\/strong>.<\/li><li>An <strong>accommodation ladder<\/strong> is normally used at up to <strong>55°<\/strong>, a <strong>gangway<\/strong> at up to <strong>30°<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ladder <strong>fittings<\/strong> and the <strong>winch<\/strong> kept in good order.<\/li><li><strong>Constantly watched<\/strong>, and <strong>adjusted<\/strong> for <strong>tide<\/strong> and <strong>draught<\/strong> during cargo work. The bottom of a ladder must not rest on the quay in a way that damages it, or hang too high.<\/li><li>The bottom platform horizontal, and the ladder not obstructed by cargo or mooring lines.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Inspection and maintenance (MSC.1/Circ.1331)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Examined before use<\/strong> by the crew.<\/li><li><strong>Regular inspection<\/strong> under the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, at intervals not exceeding 1 month, of the condition of the ladder, steps, fittings, wires and winch.<\/li><li><strong>Annual thorough examination<\/strong> at the SOLAS annual survey.<\/li><li><strong>5-yearly operational test<\/strong> at the <strong>maximum operational load<\/strong> (Q267).<\/li><li><strong>Winch<\/strong> and <strong>brake<\/strong> maintained.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Falls (suspension wires)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Inspected<\/strong> regularly.<\/li><li><strong>Renewed<\/strong> when necessary due to <strong>deterioration<\/strong>, or at intervals of <strong>not more than 5 years<\/strong>, whichever is earlier.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>C/O action<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>If the <strong>ladder can&#x27;t be rigged safely<\/strong>, for example at a high quay or with a large range of tide, the <strong>ship provides an alternative<\/strong> or the <strong>shore provides a gangway<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Nobody uses unsafe access<\/strong>. Stop cargo work if needed until access is safe.<\/li><li>Any <strong>accident<\/strong> is reported, and serious injuries go to the MAIB.<\/li><li>The gangway watch checks it every round, and the night orders say to adjust it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Shore gangways<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The terminal&#x27;s gangway must also be safe, and we check it: a defective shore gangway is still our crew&#x27;s problem.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Security<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The access point is the ISPS control point: a watchman, the visitors&#x27; log and ID checks.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Means of Access Regs 1988, SOLAS II-1/3-9 and MSC.1/Circ.1331, the rigging requirements with the net, lighting and lifebuoy, the marking and angle limits (55° and 30°), the inspections and 5-yearly test, falls renewed at 5 years or earlier, and the C/O&#x27;s action if it can&#x27;t be rigged safely.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who is responsible for the shore end of the gangway?","pa":"<p>The <strong>ship<\/strong> is responsible for providing safe access. The <strong>terminal<\/strong> is responsible for the landing area, lighting and shore end. Both cooperate, but the ship must stop using it if it&#x27;s unsafe.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q157","sec":10,"q":"Mooring winch brake test.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The industry standard is <strong>OCIMF MEG4<\/strong> (Mooring Equipment Guidelines, 4th edition). In the UK, <strong>PUWER 2006<\/strong> (MGN 331) and the <strong>COSWP<\/strong> mooring guidance require that the equipment is <strong>maintained and tested<\/strong>. IMO&#x27;s SOLAS II-1/3-8 requirements for mooring arrangements and the guidance in MSC.1/Circ.1619 also apply to newer ships.<\/p><p><strong>Why<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The brake must <strong>render (slip)<\/strong> before the <strong>line parts<\/strong>. A line parting is far more dangerous than a brake slipping.<\/li><li>MEG4 sets the brake <strong>holding capacity<\/strong> at <strong>60%<\/strong> of the <strong>Ship Design MBL (SDMBL)<\/strong> of the line.<\/li><li>This protects the line, the bitts and the <strong>people<\/strong> around it, since <strong>snap-back kills<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When to test<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Normally <strong>annually<\/strong>, and after <strong>renewing the brake lining<\/strong> or <strong>adjusting<\/strong> the brake, per the <strong>PMS<\/strong> and the maker.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The test<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Use the <strong>winch-brake test kit<\/strong>: a hydraulic <strong>jack<\/strong> and calibrated <strong>gauge<\/strong>, or the maker&#x27;s built-in kit.<\/li><li><strong>Calculate<\/strong> the <strong>gauge pressure<\/strong> that equals <strong>60% SDMBL<\/strong> for that drum, using the maker&#x27;s tables for the drum diameter and the jack position.<\/li><li><strong>Single layer<\/strong> of line on the drum (or the first layer), as the brake capacity depends on the layer.<\/li><li><strong>Brake applied<\/strong> to the <strong>marked torque<\/strong>: the brake handwheel or indicator <strong>mark<\/strong>, or the specified torque on the handwheel with a torque wrench.<\/li><li>The line or the test fixture in the <strong>correct direction<\/strong>: <strong>paying out<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Apply<\/strong> the load <strong>gradually<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The brake should <strong>render<\/strong> at the <strong>design value<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>If it <strong>slips too early<\/strong>: clean, adjust or <strong>reline<\/strong> it. Oil or water on the lining reduces its holding.<\/li><li>If it <strong>holds well above<\/strong> the value: <strong>adjust it down<\/strong>, because then the line will part before the brake renders.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the result. <strong>Mark<\/strong> the <strong>brake applied<\/strong> position on the spindle so the crew know how tight to apply it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Daily practice<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brakes kept <strong>dry<\/strong> and <strong>free of oil<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Moorings <strong>tended<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Nobody<\/strong> in the <strong>snap-back zone<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A brake must <strong>never be relied on<\/strong> with the gear <strong>engaged<\/strong>. Moored, the line is held on the brake with the gear disengaged.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t over-tighten brakes beyond the mark.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Mooring safety generally<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>mooring plan<\/strong>, the right number of lines, <strong>lines of the same material<\/strong> in the same direction, and checking for wear and line renewal under the line management plan.<\/li><li>Mooring parties briefed on snap-back zones, which can&#x27;t be fully marked on deck.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>OCIMF MEG4, the brake renders at 60% SDMBL to protect the line and people, tested annually and after relining, the test procedure with the kit, single layer, marked torque and paying out direction, adjust if it slips early or holds too much, record and mark; and daily brake care and snap-back.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why must the brake render before the line breaks?","pa":"<p>So the <strong>brake slips first<\/strong>, typically at about <strong>60% of the line&#x27;s MBL<\/strong> (OCIMF MEG4). That protects the line from <strong>parting and snapping back<\/strong>, and protects the winch.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q158","sec":10,"q":"Enclosed space entry procedure; gas limits; equipment; benzene.","tier":"red","rep":72,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":"MGN 659 Amd 3","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Entry into Enclosed Spaces) Regs 2022<\/strong>, with <strong>MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong> and <strong>COSWP<\/strong>. Internationally, <strong>SOLAS XI-1/7<\/strong> (portable atmosphere testing instruments) and the IMO revised recommendations for entering enclosed spaces, <strong>MSC.581(110)<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Procedure<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Is entry necessary?<\/strong> Can the job be done from outside?<\/li><li><strong>Assess the risk<\/strong>: what was in the space, what could be released, oxygen depletion by rust, cargo or inert gas.<\/li><li>Issue a <strong>permit to work<\/strong>, signed by the C/O and <strong>approved by the Master<\/strong>, and hold a <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the space: pipelines blanked or valves locked, electrics, <strong>inert gas<\/strong> isolated.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilate<\/strong>, with mechanical ventilation, and keep it running.<\/li><li><strong>Test the atmosphere<\/strong> with a <strong>calibrated<\/strong> meter, at <strong>different levels<\/strong> (top, middle, bottom) and <strong>remotely<\/strong> before entry, using a sampling hose.<\/li><li><strong>Attendant<\/strong> at the entrance with <strong>communications<\/strong>, and nobody enters without him.<\/li><li><strong>Rescue equipment<\/strong> rigged at the entrance. <strong>Bridge<\/strong> or duty officer informed.<\/li><li><strong>Test continuously<\/strong> during the work with personal monitors. <strong>Get everyone out<\/strong> on an <strong>alarm<\/strong> or a change in conditions.<\/li><li>On completion, close the permit and account for everyone.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Limits before entry<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Oxygen<\/strong>: a steady <strong>20.8–21%<\/strong> by volume (follow MGN 659 and the SMS).<\/li><li><strong>Combustibles<\/strong>: not more than <strong>1% LFL<\/strong>, steady reading.<\/li><li><strong>Toxic gases<\/strong> (H₂S, CO, benzene and others): below their <strong>occupational exposure limits<\/strong>, and IMO recommends below 50% of the OEL.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Benzene<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Found in <strong>crude oil<\/strong> and some <strong>products<\/strong>. A <strong>carcinogen<\/strong> with a <strong>very low exposure limit<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use a <strong>specific detector tube<\/strong> or a <strong>PID<\/strong> (photo-ionisation detector), because a multi-gas meter doesn&#x27;t measure it.<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s <strong>present<\/strong>, wear <strong>breathing apparatus<\/strong> and <strong>limit exposure<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Equipment<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Multi-gas meter<\/strong> (O₂, LFL, H₂S, CO), calibrated, with a <strong>personal monitor<\/strong> for each entrant.<\/li><li><strong>SCBA<\/strong> and <strong>EEBDs<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>rescue harness<\/strong> with <strong>lifeline<\/strong>, a <strong>tripod<\/strong> and <strong>winch<\/strong> for vertical access.<\/li><li>A <strong>stretcher<\/strong> suitable for confined spaces (Neil Robertson or similar).<\/li><li><strong>Intrinsically safe<\/strong> lighting and radios.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilation fans<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Resuscitator<\/strong> and first aid.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Drill<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An enclosed space entry and rescue drill every <strong>2 months<\/strong> (SOLAS III/19.3.6).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>New<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>MSC.581(110) brings in a <strong>register of enclosed spaces<\/strong> on board, with each space identified and marked.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 2022 Regs and MGN 659 Amd 3, the procedure from risk assessment and permit through isolation, ventilation, testing at levels, attendant, rescue equipment and continuous monitoring; the limits (O₂ about 21%, LFL ≤1%, toxics below OEL); benzene needs a specific tube or PID; the equipment; and drills every 2 months.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why test at different levels in the space?","pa":"<p>Gases <strong>stratify<\/strong>. H₂S, CO₂ and hydrocarbon vapours collect low; methane and hydrogen collect high; pockets form in corners and behind frames. One reading at the top can&#x27;t show what&#x27;s at the bottom.<\/p>","asked":["Enclosed space entry how to?","Enclosed Space entry while vsl is in dry dock?","WHAT GASES PORTABLE GAS METER CHECK?","What about your atmosphere testing?","FW tank entry procedure, and maintenance."],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",8],["Examiner not named",7],["Zahid Raza",5],["Colum Leggett",4],["Byron Griffiths",4]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q159","sec":10,"q":"Elevator drill.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>There&#x27;s <strong>no single SOLAS frequency<\/strong> for a lift-rescue drill. It comes under the <strong>SMS<\/strong> and the <strong>maker&#x27;s instructions<\/strong>, with <strong>LOLER 2006<\/strong> (MGN 332), because a lift carries persons and so needs a <strong>thorough examination every 6 months<\/strong>, plus <strong>COSWP<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Drill scenario: a person trapped in the lift between decks<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Talk<\/strong> to the trapped person through the <strong>intercom<\/strong> or <strong>phone<\/strong>, and <strong>reassure<\/strong> them. Find out if anyone is injured or unwell.<\/li><li><strong>Tell<\/strong> the <strong>bridge<\/strong> and the <strong>duty engineer<\/strong>, and the Master.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the lift <strong>power<\/strong> at the <strong>main switch<\/strong> and <strong>lock it out<\/strong>, so the car <strong>can&#x27;t move<\/strong> during the rescue.<\/li><li>Use <strong>only trained, authorised crew<\/strong> following the <strong>maker&#x27;s emergency rescue procedure<\/strong>, usually the ETO or engineers.<\/li><li><strong>Find<\/strong> where the car is: which floor, how far from the landing.<\/li><li><strong>Bring<\/strong> the car to the <strong>nearest landing<\/strong> using the <strong>manual brake release<\/strong> and <strong>hand-winding<\/strong>, or the <strong>emergency lowering<\/strong> function, with communication between the machine room and the landing.<\/li><li><strong>Open<\/strong> the <strong>landing door<\/strong> with the <strong>emergency key<\/strong>, then the <strong>car door<\/strong>, and <strong>get the person out<\/strong>, checking the car floor is level with the landing.<\/li><li><strong>Never<\/strong> climb into the <strong>shaft<\/strong> without a <strong>permit<\/strong>, fall protection and the power isolated.<\/li><li><strong>First aid<\/strong> if needed.<\/li><li><strong>Log<\/strong> the drill.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> any <strong>defect<\/strong>, and keep the lift <strong>out of service<\/strong> until a <strong>competent person<\/strong> has checked it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Also check<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>emergency light<\/strong>, <strong>alarm<\/strong> and <strong>communications<\/strong> in the car.<\/li><li>The <strong>lift trunk escape hatches<\/strong> and ladders.<\/li><li>The <strong>emergency key<\/strong> is kept in a known place.<\/li><li>The notice in the car: what to do if trapped.<\/li><li>The <strong>machine room<\/strong> is locked and clear.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fire<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Lifts must not be used in a fire. Some have a fire mode returning them to a set deck.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A trapped person can panic, and in a fire or a power failure, rescue is urgent.<\/li><li>People have been injured by the car moving during a rescue, so isolation is critical.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No SOLAS frequency, it&#x27;s the SMS and maker with LOLER 6-monthly examination; reassure and inform, isolate and lock out the power, trained crew following the maker&#x27;s procedure, bring the car to a landing by manual means, emergency key, never enter the shaft without a permit, first aid, log and report.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is isolating power the first step before winding the car?","pa":"<p>To stop the car <strong>moving unexpectedly<\/strong>, and to prevent <strong>electrocution or crushing<\/strong> while it&#x27;s wound by hand (lock out, tag out).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q160","sec":10,"q":"AB on the mast without a harness — action?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Work at Height) Regs 2010<\/strong>, <strong>COSWP<\/strong> and the SMS <strong>permit-to-work<\/strong> system, under the Health and Safety at Work Regs 1997.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop the job<\/strong>. Every crew member has <strong>stop-work authority<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Tell him <strong>calmly<\/strong> to <strong>stay still<\/strong> and <strong>secure himself<\/strong>: hold on firmly, wrap an arm round a fixed part.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t shout<\/strong> or <strong>startle<\/strong> him.<\/li><li>Get a <strong>harness<\/strong> to him if possible, or get him <strong>down safely<\/strong>, by the ladder with a <strong>fall-arrest<\/strong> device if fitted, one step at a time, with someone guiding.<\/li><li>If he&#x27;s tired or frightened, consider a rescue with a second person properly harnessed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Then check the controls that should have been in place<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> and a <strong>permit to work<\/strong> for <strong>working aloft<\/strong>, approved by the <strong>C/O<\/strong> or <strong>Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Full-body harness<\/strong> with a <strong>double lanyard<\/strong> or <strong>fall arrester<\/strong>, <strong>always attached<\/strong> to a strong point, plus a <strong>helmet<\/strong> and <strong>tools on lanyards<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Bridge informed<\/strong>. <strong>Radar<\/strong>, <strong>whistle<\/strong>, <strong>radio aerials<\/strong> and the <strong>horn<\/strong> <strong>isolated<\/strong> and <strong>tagged<\/strong>, because radar radiation and the whistle are dangerous aloft.<\/li><li>The <strong>ship steady<\/strong>, not in heavy weather, and not working aloft at night unless essential.<\/li><li>A <strong>competent person<\/strong> standing by <strong>below<\/strong>, with communication.<\/li><li><strong>Area below cordoned off<\/strong>, against dropped objects.<\/li><li>The person trained and fit, and not a young person unless supervised training.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Find out why<\/strong>: was there <strong>no harness<\/strong> available, was he <strong>rushing<\/strong>, was the <strong>permit<\/strong> not issued? Did someone tell him to go up quickly?<\/li><li>Record a <strong>near miss<\/strong> and report it under <strong>ISM 9<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Retrain<\/strong> and <strong>brief<\/strong> the crew at the next <strong>safety meeting<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Review<\/strong> the permit system and the harness inventory and inspections.<\/li><li>Check the officers: why didn&#x27;t the supervisor stop it? Was the permit signed without checking?<\/li><li><strong>Not punitive<\/strong> unless it was <strong>deliberate<\/strong> and <strong>repeated<\/strong>, under a just culture; but the work <strong>restarts only<\/strong> when it&#x27;s <strong>safe<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If it was deliberate after being told, it&#x27;s a disciplinary matter under the Code of Conduct.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Falls from height are one of the main causes of death and serious injury on ships in MAIB reports. A fall from a mast will almost certainly be fatal.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stop the job calmly, get him secure and down safely, then the controls that should have been there (RA, permit, harness attached, radar and whistle isolated, standby, area cordoned), and the follow-up: find the cause, near-miss report under ISM 9, retrain, review the system, just culture.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What equipment must be isolated before working on the radar mast?","pa":"<p>Isolate and <strong>lock out/tag out<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>radar scanners<\/strong>, for radiation and rotating parts;<\/li><li><strong>satcom/VSAT<\/strong> antennas;<\/li><li><strong>MF/HF<\/strong> aerials and transmitters;<\/li><li>the whistle and navigation-light circuits.<\/li><\/ul><p>All under a permit.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q161","sec":10,"q":"Risk assessment types, ALARP, how a job is done.","tier":"red","rep":40,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Health and Safety at Work) Regs 1997<\/strong>, which require the employer to <strong>assess the risks<\/strong> to workers and others, with guidance in <strong>MGN 636 (M+F) Amd 4<\/strong> and <strong>COSWP<\/strong>, and <strong>ISM 1.2.2<\/strong>, which requires the company to assess all identified risks.<\/p><p><strong>Types of risk assessment<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Generic<\/strong>: for <strong>routine tasks<\/strong>, written in the SMS, such as mooring or painting.<\/li><li><strong>Task-specific<\/strong>: the generic one <strong>reviewed<\/strong> for <strong>today&#x27;s<\/strong> conditions, people and location.<\/li><li><strong>Dynamic<\/strong>: <strong>in the moment<\/strong>, when conditions <strong>change<\/strong> during the job: weather, a new hazard, someone unwell. Stop and reassess.<\/li><li><strong>Qualitative<\/strong>: a <strong>likelihood × severity<\/strong> matrix, giving low, medium or high risk.<\/li><li><strong>Quantitative<\/strong>: numerical, for example in formal safety assessments, rarely on board.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>ALARP: As Low As Reasonably Practicable<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Reduce the risk until the <strong>cost<\/strong>, <strong>time<\/strong> or <strong>effort<\/strong> of further reduction is <strong>grossly disproportionate<\/strong> to the benefit.<\/li><li>It doesn&#x27;t mean zero risk; it means doing everything reasonable.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How a job is done<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Identify the hazards<\/strong>: what could cause harm?<\/li><li><strong>Decide who<\/strong> could be harmed and how.<\/li><li><strong>Assess<\/strong> the <strong>likelihood<\/strong> and <strong>severity<\/strong>, giving the risk level.<\/li><li>Apply the <strong>hierarchy of control<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>eliminate<\/strong> the hazard;<\/li><li><strong>substitute<\/strong> with something less hazardous;<\/li><li><strong>engineering controls<\/strong>: guards, ventilation, isolation, interlocks;<\/li><li><strong>administrative<\/strong> controls: permits to work, procedures, training, signs, supervision, rotation;<\/li><li>and finally <strong>PPE<\/strong>, the last line of defence.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the assessment and the <strong>residual risk<\/strong>: is it acceptable?<\/li><li><strong>Toolbox talk<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong> issued.<\/li><li><strong>Supervise<\/strong> and <strong>monitor<\/strong>. <strong>Stop<\/strong> if conditions change.<\/li><li><strong>Review<\/strong> afterwards, and <strong>feed lessons<\/strong> into the SMS.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>When to review risk assessments<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>After <strong>accidents<\/strong> or <strong>near misses<\/strong>.<\/li><li>After <strong>changes<\/strong>: new equipment, new procedures, new crew, different ship conditions.<\/li><li>At regular intervals set by the SMS.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Engineering vs administrative control<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Engineering<\/strong>: a <strong>machine guard<\/strong> on a lathe, <strong>forced ventilation<\/strong> in a space, a <strong>fixed gas detector<\/strong> with an alarm, an interlock that stops a winch when a gate opens, a <strong>grating<\/strong> over an opening. It works without people having to remember.<\/li><li><strong>Administrative<\/strong>: a <strong>permit to work<\/strong>, a <strong>procedure<\/strong>, <strong>training<\/strong>, a <strong>warning sign<\/strong>, <strong>limiting time<\/strong> in a noisy space. It relies on people following it.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s why engineering controls come higher in the hierarchy.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The legal duty under the 1997 Regs and ISM 1.2.2, the types (generic, task-specific, dynamic), ALARP with &quot;grossly disproportionate&quot;, the steps and the hierarchy of control with PPE last, recording residual risk, review triggers, and a clear example of engineering versus administrative controls.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["How will you do the risk assessment?","How you carry out Risk Assessment ?","What are the duties and responsibilities of a Safety officer and What is Risk assessment?","Where can you find info about risk assessments?","Supervision and risk assessment requirements."],"ex":[["Inamul Bhuiyan",6],["Examiner not named",6],["James Gilbert",5],["Ian Blair",3],["Shahid Akhter",3]],"yrs":"2021–2025"},{"id":"★11.1","sec":10,"q":"The annual lifeboat service is due in a port with no certified service provider. Options?","tier":"new","rep":1,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, <strong>MGN 560 Amd 2<\/strong>. Internationally, <strong>MSC.402(96)<\/strong>, mandatory through <strong>SOLAS III/20.11<\/strong>. The <strong>annual thorough examination<\/strong> of the lifeboats, release gear and davits must be done by a <strong>certified, authorised service provider<\/strong>. The <strong>ship&#x27;s crew can&#x27;t substitute<\/strong> for those items.<\/p><p><strong>Options<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Plan ahead<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Check the <strong>PMS<\/strong> and the <strong>voyage schedule<\/strong> months in advance.<\/li><li>Use <strong>any port earlier in the window<\/strong> where a provider is available. The annual examination can usually be done within a window around the due date, so an early port works.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Arrange for the provider to come to us<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>At the <strong>next port<\/strong>, a provider from another country may be able to attend, or a technician can be <strong>flown in<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>owners pay<\/strong>, and that&#x27;s cheaper than a <strong>delay<\/strong> or a <strong>detention<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The manufacturer may have authorised technicians in the region.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Next port with a provider is after the due date<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Contact the <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>, and through them the <strong>MCA<\/strong> (flag) or the <strong>RO<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Administration<\/strong> may agree a <strong>short extension<\/strong>, with <strong>conditions<\/strong>, such as:<\/li><li>extra <strong>crew checks<\/strong> and inspections;<\/li><li>no use of <strong>on-load release<\/strong> except in an <strong>emergency<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>fall-prevention devices<\/strong> fitted for drills;<\/li><li>no launching with crew on board until serviced.<\/li><li>That agreement must be <strong>in writing<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Never let the item lapse silently<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Never let crew strip the release gear<\/strong> to &quot;service&quot; it themselves.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep the <strong>correspondence<\/strong>, any <strong>authorisation<\/strong> from the <strong>flag State<\/strong>, and the <strong>extra checks<\/strong> done.<\/li><li>An entry in the logbook and PMS.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Consequences if not handled<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>expired LSA service<\/strong> is a likely <strong>PSC deficiency<\/strong> and can lead to <strong>detention<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Safety Equipment certificate<\/strong> depends on the LSA being maintained, and the surveyor at the next SEC survey will see the gap.<\/li><li>In an emergency, the boats might not work.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Root cause<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Why was this missed? Better planning of the service schedule against the trading pattern, and the company&#x27;s technical department booking it in advance.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MSC.402(96) and MGN 560, only a certified provider can do it; plan ahead and use an earlier port; bring a technician in; if it must go past the due date, the company asks the flag or RO for a written extension with conditions; never let it lapse silently or let the crew do it; and records.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Which items may the crew still maintain themselves?","pa":"<p><strong>Weekly and monthly inspections<\/strong> and routine care to the manufacturer&#x27;s instructions: cleaning, permitted greasing, visual checks, and lowering and turning out. <strong>Not<\/strong> the annual thorough exam, the 5-yearly overhaul, or dismantling or adjusting release gear.<\/p>","asked":["What is an approved service provider"],"ex":[["Alec Keep",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"Q162","sec":11,"q":"How do you plan and load containers?","tier":"red","rep":80,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Carriage of Cargoes) Regs 1999<\/strong> and the ship&#x27;s approved <strong>Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong>. Internationally, <strong>SOLAS VI and VII<\/strong>, the <strong>CSS Code<\/strong> and the <strong>IMDG Code<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Who plans<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>shore planner<\/strong> sends a <strong>pre-stow<\/strong> (a <strong>BAPLIE<\/strong> file).<\/li><li>The <strong>C/O checks and approves<\/strong> it. The <strong>ship&#x27;s limits always override<\/strong> the commercial plan.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Checks on the plan<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stability<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>GM<\/strong> within limits: <strong>not too stiff<\/strong>, because high GM means violent rolling and high <strong>lashing forces<\/strong>; <strong>not too tender<\/strong>, so the IS Code criteria, including the <strong>weather criterion<\/strong> with the high windage, are met;<\/li><li>below the <strong>maximum KG<\/strong> curve.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Strength<\/strong>: <strong>shear force<\/strong>, <strong>bending moment<\/strong> and <strong>torsion<\/strong> within limits at <strong>every stage<\/strong> of loading and discharge, not just the final condition.<\/li><li><strong>Stack weights<\/strong> and <strong>heights<\/strong> per tier, on the <strong>tank top<\/strong> and on the <strong>hatch covers<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing software<\/strong>: all <strong>lashing forces<\/strong> OK at the planned GM and the expected route and weather.<\/li><li><strong>Visibility<\/strong> from the bridge (<strong>SOLAS V/22<\/strong>): the blind sector ahead not more than 2 ship lengths or 500 m, whichever is less.<\/li><li><strong>Air draught<\/strong>, <strong>draught<\/strong> limits at the ports and on passage, and <strong>trim<\/strong> for good steering and propeller immersion.<\/li><li><strong>DG<\/strong>: <strong>segregation<\/strong> under the IMDG Code and the <strong>DOC for DG<\/strong>, allowed in each hold.<\/li><li><strong>Reefers<\/strong>: the <strong>plug count<\/strong> and the <strong>generator capacity<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>OOG<\/strong> (out-of-gauge) boxes and <strong>flat racks<\/strong>: positions where they fit and can be lashed.<\/li><li><strong>Heavy boxes low<\/strong> in each stack.<\/li><li><strong>VGMs<\/strong> received for every box (<strong>SOLAS VI/2<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>Port rotation<\/strong>: boxes for the first port accessible without restows.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>During loading<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Watch<\/strong> the sequence against the plan and monitor the <strong>draught<\/strong>, <strong>list<\/strong> and the <strong>loadicator<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Check the lashing<\/strong> on <strong>deck rounds<\/strong>, bay by bay.<\/li><li><strong>DG checked<\/strong> as loaded: placards, damage, the right slot.<\/li><li><strong>Damaged boxes refused<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Changes to the plan checked by me before they&#x27;re accepted.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Final<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stability<\/strong> and <strong>lashing printouts<\/strong> for the departure condition.<\/li><li><strong>Actual draughts<\/strong> compared with the <strong>calculated<\/strong> ones. A big difference suggests wrong VGMs or wrong tank figures.<\/li><li>The <strong>DG manifest<\/strong> final.<\/li><li><strong>Departure stability<\/strong> signed off by the <strong>Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The final bay plan sent to the next port.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The planner&#x27;s pre-stow checked and approved by the C/O with the ship&#x27;s limits overriding; stability (not too stiff, not too tender), strength at every stage, stack weights, lashing forces, visibility under SOLAS V/22, air draught, DG and the DOC, reefers, heavy boxes low and VGMs; then monitoring during loading, lashing checks, and the final draught comparison and printouts.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is a very high GM a problem on a container ship?","pa":"<p>A <strong>short, violent roll<\/strong> with high accelerations at the stack tops, so <strong>lashing forces<\/strong> go up. That leads to stack collapse and lost boxes, structural stress, crew injury and cargo damage.<\/p>","asked":["Documents for loading containers?","Vessel type: container","You have loaded to your draught marks but still have 50 containers to load, what will you do?","You are on a container ship, loading in South Africa and discharging in the","You are joining a Container ship as C/O, full process?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",23],["Bill Bennett",13],["Byron Griffiths",13],["Examiner not named",10],["Khairul Anam",10]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q163","sec":11,"q":"Stack weight vs stack height; lashing; CSM contents (MSC/Circ.745).","tier":"red","rep":67,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Stack weight<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>maximum total mass<\/strong> allowed in <strong>one stack<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Set by the strength of the <strong>tank top<\/strong> or <strong>hatch cover<\/strong> and the <strong>container corner posts<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It comes from the <strong>CSM<\/strong> and the <strong>loading manual<\/strong>, for each bay, row and position, on deck and in the hold.<\/li><li>Container corner posts are designed for a stacking load: a standard box allows a large superimposed mass, which limits how many heavy boxes can go on top.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Stack height<\/strong><\/p><p>Limited by:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Visibility<\/strong> from the bridge (<strong>SOLAS V/22<\/strong>).<\/li><li>The <strong>lashing bridge<\/strong> height: boxes above the lashing bridge are harder to secure.<\/li><li><strong>Air draught<\/strong> at bridges and cranes.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing forces<\/strong>, which <strong>rise sharply<\/strong> with <strong>height<\/strong>, <strong>GM<\/strong> and a short <strong>roll period<\/strong>, because accelerations are highest at the top of the stack.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The key point<\/strong><\/p><p>You can be <strong>within the weight limit<\/strong> but <strong>fail on lashing forces<\/strong> if <strong>heavy boxes are high<\/strong> in the stack. So <strong>keep heavy boxes low<\/strong>, and light ones on top.<\/p><p><strong>Lashing<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Twistlocks<\/strong> or <strong>midlocks<\/strong> between tiers, <strong>locked<\/strong>, with the <strong>left-hand/right-hand<\/strong> types correct, and not mixed types in the same tier unless the CSM allows.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing bars<\/strong> and <strong>turnbuckles<\/strong> from the <strong>lashing bridge<\/strong> or the hatch cover, <strong>tensioned<\/strong> and locked.<\/li><li><strong>Stacking cones<\/strong>, <strong>bridge fittings<\/strong> and <strong>buttresses<\/strong>, and <strong>cell guides<\/strong> under deck.<\/li><li>The <strong>lashing pattern<\/strong> comes from the <strong>CSM<\/strong> and the <strong>lashing computer<\/strong>, for the stack&#x27;s weight, height and position.<\/li><li>Outer rows and the forward bays need the strongest patterns, because they see the highest forces and green seas.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Required by <strong>SOLAS VI/5.6<\/strong> and <strong>VII/5<\/strong>, and <strong>approved<\/strong> by the flag or RO.<\/li><li>Originally drawn up to <strong>MSC/Circ.745<\/strong>, now <strong>MSC.1/Circ.1353<\/strong> (Rev.2, verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>CSM contents<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>General<\/strong> information and definitions.<\/li><li><strong>Securing devices<\/strong>, fixed and portable: the <strong>inventory<\/strong>, the <strong>MSL<\/strong> (maximum securing load), <strong>maintenance<\/strong>, <strong>inspection<\/strong> and <strong>acceptance criteria<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Stowage and securing of <strong>non-standardised<\/strong> and <strong>semi-standardised<\/strong> cargo.<\/li><li><strong>Containers<\/strong>: securing arrangements, <strong>lashing patterns<\/strong>, <strong>permissible stack weights and heights<\/strong>, and the <strong>accelerations<\/strong> used.<\/li><li>Guidance on <strong>heavy weather<\/strong> and avoiding excessive rolling.<\/li><li><strong>Records<\/strong> of maintenance and inspection of the securing gear.<\/li><li>Information on the lashing computer, where it&#x27;s used.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stack weight as the maximum mass per stack from the tank top, hatch cover and corner posts; stack height limited by visibility, lashing bridges, air draught and lashing forces; the point that heavy boxes high can fail even within weight; the lashing components; and the CSM requirement under SOLAS VI/5.6 and VII/5 with its main contents.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is MSL and how does it relate to breaking load?","pa":"<p><strong>MSL (Maximum Securing Load)<\/strong> is the safe working capacity of a securing device. It&#x27;s a fraction of the <strong>breaking load<\/strong>, for example:<\/p><ul><li>shackles, rings, deck eyes, turnbuckles and chains: <strong>50%<\/strong>;<\/li><li>web lashings: <strong>70%<\/strong>;<\/li><li>single-use wire rope: <strong>80%<\/strong>;<\/li><li>re-usable wire rope: <strong>30%<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p>This is CSS Code Annex 13.<\/p>","asked":["What is the Cargo Securing Manual?","CSM, contents, how often the loading gears are inspected?","What is contained within your CSM?","What are the dangers of electric cars in a Ro-Ro ship?","How are you going to lash this cargo?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",10],["Khairul Anam",8],["Examiner not named",7],["Siddhartha Paul",6],["Mahmood ul Hassan",4]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q164","sec":11,"q":"How do you load IMDG cargo? Documents? DG DOC (what it tells you)? Which holds for which class?","tier":"red","rep":58,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Dangerous Goods and Marine Pollutants) Regs 1997<\/strong>. Internationally, <strong>SOLAS VII Part A<\/strong>, the <strong>IMDG Code<\/strong> (Amendment 42-24), and <strong>SOLAS II-2/19<\/strong> for the <strong>Document of Compliance<\/strong> for ships carrying DG.<\/p><p><strong>Documents<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>DG declaration<\/strong> (the multimodal DG form) from the <strong>shipper<\/strong>, with the <strong>UN number<\/strong>, <strong>proper shipping name<\/strong>, <strong>class<\/strong>, <strong>packing group<\/strong>, <strong>marine pollutant<\/strong> status, and the <strong>flashpoint<\/strong> where relevant, signed.<\/li><li><strong>Container packing certificate<\/strong>, signed by the person who packed the container.<\/li><li><strong>DG manifest<\/strong> or <strong>special list<\/strong>, and the <strong>stowage plan<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS VII/4<\/strong>), giving the <strong>position<\/strong> of each DG unit on board. A copy goes ashore before departure.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s <strong>Document of Compliance for DG<\/strong> (<strong>II-2/19.4<\/strong>).<\/li><li>The <strong>EmS<\/strong> and <strong>MFAG<\/strong>, and the IMDG Code itself.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What the DOC tells you<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For <strong>each cargo space<\/strong> (on deck, each hold), <strong>which classes<\/strong> and <strong>forms<\/strong> of DG may be carried, <strong>under deck<\/strong> and in <strong>closed or open<\/strong> containers.<\/li><li>It reflects the <strong>fire<\/strong>, <strong>ventilation<\/strong>, <strong>electrical<\/strong>, <strong>bilge<\/strong> and <strong>detection<\/strong> equipment fitted in each space.<\/li><li>Example: a hold <strong>without certified safe electrics<\/strong> or enough <strong>ventilation<\/strong> can&#x27;t take <strong>class 2.1<\/strong> (flammable gases) or flammable liquids with a <strong>low flashpoint<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Class 1 is often restricted to specific holds or on deck.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s issued by the flag or RO, normally aligned with the SAFCON, and lists any conditions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Loading<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Check every DG box<\/strong> against the <strong>declaration<\/strong> and the <strong>DOC<\/strong>: is it allowed in that hold?<\/li><li>Check <strong>segregation<\/strong> (IMDG <strong>7.2<\/strong> and <strong>7.4<\/strong> for container ships) and the <strong>stowage category<\/strong> (A to E).<\/li><li>Check <strong>placards<\/strong> and <strong>labels<\/strong> on all four sides, and that the unit <strong>isn&#x27;t leaking or damaged<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check <strong>accessibility<\/strong> for any DG that needs it, such as temperature-controlled DG in reefers.<\/li><li>Keep DG <strong>away from heat sources<\/strong> and the <strong>accommodation<\/strong> where the Code requires (&quot;away from living quarters&quot;, &quot;protected from sources of heat&quot;).<\/li><li><strong>EmS<\/strong> and <strong>MFAG<\/strong> available on the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Final DG manifest<\/strong> to the <strong>Master<\/strong> and <strong>shore<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Which holds for which class<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Determined by the <strong>DOC<\/strong> and the <strong>stowage category<\/strong>:<\/li><li>for cargo ships, categories <strong>A, B and E<\/strong> may go <strong>on or under deck<\/strong>; <strong>C and D<\/strong> are <strong>on deck only<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Plus the stowage codes in the DGL column 16a, such as &quot;protected from heat&quot; or &quot;clear of living quarters&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 1997 UK Regs and SOLAS VII, the documents (DG declaration, packing certificate, DG manifest under VII/4, the DOC under II-2/19), what the DOC tells you hold by hold with an example, the loading checks of segregation, stowage category, placards and damage, and EmS and MFAG available.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Which classes typically can't go under deck on your ship?","pa":"<p>It depends on <strong>your DG DOC table<\/strong> (SOLAS II-2/19) and each cargo&#x27;s IMDG <strong>stowage category<\/strong>. Commonly restricted under deck are <strong>Class 1<\/strong> (except 1.4S), <strong>flammable gases<\/strong>, <strong>low-flashpoint Class 3<\/strong>, <strong>5.2 organic peroxides<\/strong>, and anything with stowage category <strong>on deck only<\/strong>. Always check the DOC.<\/p>","asked":["What are the contents of DG DOC?","How do you know you can load DG?","Can you load Dangerous Goods on your ship?","What other documents required to load DG cargo?","What is the convention and code for DG cargo?"],"ex":[["Ahsan Khan",8],["Khairul Anam",7],["Examiner not named",7],["Zia Ul Haq",6],["Donald Donaldson",5]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q165","sec":11,"q":"Segregation without a loadicator; under-deck stowage restrictions; placards.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>IMDG Code Chapter 7.2<\/strong> (segregation, with the segregation table) and <strong>Chapter 7.4<\/strong> (stowage and segregation on container ships).<\/p><p><strong>Segregation without software<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Find each DG&#x27;s <strong>class<\/strong> and check the <strong>Dangerous Goods List<\/strong> column <strong>16b<\/strong> for any <strong>special segregation<\/strong> requirements, such as &quot;separated from acids&quot;.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>segregation table<\/strong> (<strong>7.2.4<\/strong>) to find the requirement between the two classes:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>1: &quot;away from&quot;<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>2: &quot;separated from&quot;<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>3: &quot;separated by a complete compartment or hold from&quot;<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>4: &quot;separated longitudinally by an intervening complete compartment or hold from&quot;<\/strong>;<\/li><li>X: no general requirement, check the DGL.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Apply the <strong>container-ship table<\/strong> (<strong>7.4.3<\/strong>) to convert that into <strong>horizontal and vertical<\/strong> positions. It&#x27;s different for <strong>closed and open<\/strong> containers, and for <strong>on deck<\/strong> and <strong>under deck<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Example: &quot;<strong>separated from<\/strong>&quot; for <strong>closed<\/strong> boxes <strong>on deck<\/strong> needs <strong>one container space<\/strong> fore-and-aft or athwartships between them.<\/li><li>&quot;Away from&quot; for closed containers may allow them to be adjacent with no restriction vertically on deck in some cases (check the table).<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Check both <strong>horizontally<\/strong> (fore-and-aft and athwartships) and <strong>vertically<\/strong> (one on top of the other).<\/li><li>Write it on the bay plan and check again before loading.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Under-deck stowage restrictions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Use the <strong>stowage category<\/strong> from DGL column <strong>16a<\/strong>:<\/li><li>for cargo ships, <strong>A, B and E<\/strong>: <strong>on or under deck<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>C and D<\/strong>: <strong>on deck only<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Also follow the <strong>stowage codes<\/strong>, such as &quot;<strong>protected from sources of heat<\/strong>&quot;, &quot;<strong>away from living quarters<\/strong>&quot;, &quot;<strong>clear of living quarters<\/strong>&quot;, and &quot;shade from radiant heat&quot;.<\/li><li>And the <strong>DOC<\/strong> limits for each hold.<\/li><li>Class 1 has its own compatibility groups and stowage rules.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Placards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Class placards<\/strong>, at least <strong>250 × 250 mm<\/strong>, on <strong>all four sides<\/strong> of the container.<\/li><li>Plus the <strong>UN number<\/strong> (on an orange panel or on the placard), the <strong>marine pollutant<\/strong> mark, the <strong>elevated temperature<\/strong> mark, and the <strong>fumigation warning<\/strong> where they apply.<\/li><li>For limited quantities only: the LQ mark instead of placards.<\/li><li><strong>Remove<\/strong> them once the box is <strong>empty and clean<\/strong>, so it isn&#x27;t treated as DG.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Checking on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On loading, every DG box is checked for placards against the manifest.<\/li><li>A box with wrong or missing placards is refused or corrected before loading.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The segregation table in 7.2.4 with the four terms, then the container-ship table in 7.4.3 for closed or open and on or under deck, an example such as one container space for &quot;separated from&quot;, the stowage categories A to E with C and D on deck only, the stowage codes, the DOC, and placards 250 × 250 mm on four sides with the UN number and marks.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the difference between \"separated from\" and \"separated by a complete compartment\"?","pa":"<p>Under deck, &quot;<strong>separated from<\/strong>&quot; means in <strong>different holds or compartments<\/strong>; on deck, at least <strong>6 m<\/strong> apart horizontally. &quot;<strong>Separated by a complete compartment or hold<\/strong>&quot; means a whole hold or compartment in between; on deck, at least <strong>12 m<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q166","sec":11,"q":"LQ / EQ — how much can you load?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>IMDG Code <strong>Chapter 3.4<\/strong> (Limited Quantities) and <strong>Chapter 3.5<\/strong> (Excepted Quantities).<\/p><p><strong>Limited Quantities (LQ)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>maximum inner packaging<\/strong> quantity is given in DGL <strong>column 7a<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Packages<\/strong> are limited to <strong>30 kg gross<\/strong>, or <strong>20 kg<\/strong> for <strong>shrink-wrapped trays<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>LQ mark<\/strong> (a black and white diamond) replaces the class labels on packages.<\/li><li><strong>No DG placard<\/strong> on the CTU. A CTU carrying <strong>only LQ<\/strong> shows the <strong>LQ mark<\/strong> (at least <strong>250 mm<\/strong>) on <strong>all four sides<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Mostly <strong>exempt from segregation<\/strong>, but they still go on the <strong>DG declaration<\/strong> (the transport document) and the DG manifest, with the words &quot;limited quantity&quot; or &quot;LTD QTY&quot;.<\/li><li>Some provisions still apply, such as the training of people handling them.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Excepted Quantities (EQ)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Very small quantities<\/strong>, coded <strong>E1 to E5<\/strong> in <strong>column 7b<\/strong>, which set the <strong>maximum per inner packaging<\/strong> and <strong>per outer packaging<\/strong>. The outer package maximum ranges up to about <strong>1 kg or 1 litre<\/strong> (verify the code values).<\/li><li><strong>EQ mark<\/strong> on packages, and &quot;<strong>dangerous goods in excepted quantities<\/strong>&quot; on the transport document.<\/li><li><strong>Almost all other IMDG provisions<\/strong> don&#x27;t apply.<\/li><li>E0 means the substance can&#x27;t be carried as EQ.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How much can you load?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Code limits the <strong>quantity per packaging<\/strong> and <strong>per package<\/strong>, <strong>not the total per ship<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So in principle the <strong>number of packages is unlimited<\/strong>, subject to:<\/li><li>the <strong>DOC<\/strong>, which may restrict classes in some holds;<\/li><li><strong>stowage<\/strong> requirements;<\/li><li>the <strong>shipper&#x27;s declaration<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>company or charterer&#x27;s<\/strong> DG acceptance rules;<\/li><li><strong>port restrictions<\/strong>, which may limit quantities in port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>LQ boxes don&#x27;t carry placards, so in a fire the crew may not know they contain DG. The manifest tells you.<\/li><li>Large numbers of LQ boxes of flammable aerosols or paints can still make a big fire.<\/li><li>Misdeclared LQ is a common cause of incidents.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>LQ in 3.4 with column 7a, package limits of 30 kg and 20 kg for shrink-wrapped trays, the LQ mark replacing labels and on the CTU, no placards, mostly exempt from segregation, still declared; EQ in 3.5 with codes E1 to E5 and almost all provisions not applying; and that the Code limits the package, not the total, so the limit on board comes from the DOC, stowage, company and port rules.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Does an LQ container need to be shown on the DG manifest?","pa":"<p>The transport document must show <strong>&quot;LTD QTY&quot;<\/strong>. Whether the ship&#x27;s DG manifest or special list must include it depends on the IMDG provisions in force, so check IMDG Chapter 3.4 and 5.4.3 and the company procedure.<\/p>","asked":["How do you know how much LQ/EQ you can load?"],"ex":[["Timothy Cummins",1]],"yrs":"2023–2023"},{"id":"Q167","sec":11,"q":"What is VGM?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS VI/2<\/strong> (paragraphs 4 to 6), in force <strong>1 July 2016<\/strong>. In the UK it&#x27;s applied through the <strong>MS (Carriage of Cargoes) Regs 1999<\/strong> as amended, with MCA guidance in <strong>MGN 534 (M)<\/strong> (verify).<\/p><p><strong>What VGM is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Verified Gross Mass<\/strong>: the <strong>total mass<\/strong> of a <strong>packed container<\/strong>: the <strong>tare<\/strong>, the <strong>cargo<\/strong>, the <strong>packing<\/strong> and the <strong>dunnage<\/strong> and securing materials.<\/li><li>The <strong>shipper<\/strong> named on the bill of lading must <strong>verify<\/strong> it and send it to the <strong>carrier<\/strong> and the <strong>terminal<\/strong>, <strong>in time<\/strong> for the <strong>stowage plan<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Two methods<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Method 1<\/strong>: <strong>weigh<\/strong> the <strong>packed container<\/strong> on <strong>calibrated, certified<\/strong> equipment, such as a weighbridge or a crane load cell.<\/li><li><strong>Method 2<\/strong>: <strong>weigh all the contents<\/strong> (packages, pallets, dunnage) and <strong>add<\/strong> the container <strong>tare<\/strong> mass, using a method <strong>approved<\/strong> by the State where it was packed. In the <strong>UK<\/strong>, the shipper needs <strong>MCA approval<\/strong> for Method 2.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Estimating is not allowed<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The ship&#x27;s position<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>packed container without a VGM must not be loaded<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Master<\/strong> and the <strong>terminal<\/strong> both have that duty.<\/li><li>The VGM must be signed by an authorised person for the shipper.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters to the C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Misdeclared weights<\/strong> cause wrong <strong>GM<\/strong>, wrong <strong>bending moments<\/strong> and <strong>shear forces<\/strong>, exceeded <strong>stack weights<\/strong>, and wrong <strong>lashing forces<\/strong>.<\/li><li>They can lead to <strong>stack collapses<\/strong> and <strong>containers lost overboard<\/strong>, and they were a factor in the MSC Napoli loss in 2007.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>draught discrepancies<\/strong>: an <strong>actual draught deeper than calculated<\/strong> is a sign of heavy boxes.<\/li><li>Watch the gantry crane&#x27;s load cell readings, where the terminal shares them.<\/li><li>If a box is clearly heavier than declared, ask the terminal to weigh it, and refuse it if the difference is unsafe.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>UK enforcement<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The MCA inspects shippers and can take enforcement action.<\/li><li>The UK enforcement tolerance is <strong>±5%<\/strong> (verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do with a discrepancy<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Inform the Master and the terminal, and the planner.<\/li><li>Re-run the loadicator with the corrected weight.<\/li><li>Restow if needed, or short-ship.<\/li><li>A <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> to the terminal and shipper, and record it.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS VI/2 from 1 July 2016, the definition including tare, cargo, packing and dunnage, the shipper&#x27;s responsibility, the two methods with Method 2 needing MCA approval in the UK, no VGM no loading, the effect on GM, stresses, stack weights and lashing, draught discrepancies as a warning, and MGN 534.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you do if the departure draught is 20 cm deeper than the loadicator predicts?","pa":"<p>The ship is heavier than calculated. Re-check the draughts, density, soundings and constant. If it&#x27;s still unexplained, suspect <strong>mis-declared weights (VGM)<\/strong>: inform the Master, the terminal and the planner, stop or limit loading, re-calculate stability, don&#x27;t exceed the marks, and issue a letter of protest.<\/p>","asked":["What do you know about VGM?","Confirm VGM Approval Certificate","What is a VGM file & how will you cross check whether the VGM datas are correct","Ask agent for DG Manifest, MSD, Packing Certificate, VGM Certificate"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",2],["Khairul Anam",1],["Fazal Wadood",1],["Zahid Raza",1],["Kris Askey",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q168","sec":11,"q":"Container safety approval plate (CSC).","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>International Convention for Safe Containers (CSC) 1972<\/strong>. In the UK, the <strong>Freight Containers (Safety Convention) Regs 1984<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The plate<\/strong><\/p><p>Every container in international transport must carry a <strong>CSC Safety Approval Plate<\/strong>, <strong>permanently fixed<\/strong> in a readily visible place, usually on the <strong>door<\/strong>. It shows:<\/p><ol><li>&quot;<strong>CSC Safety Approval<\/strong>&quot;, with the <strong>country of approval<\/strong> and the <strong>approval reference<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Date<\/strong> (month and year) of <strong>manufacture<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Manufacturer&#x27;s identification number<\/strong> for the container.<\/li><li><strong>Maximum operating gross mass<\/strong> (kg and lb).<\/li><li><strong>Allowable stacking load for 1.8 g<\/strong> (kg and lb): the maximum mass that may be stacked on top of it.<\/li><li><strong>Transverse racking test load<\/strong> (newtons).<\/li><li><strong>End-wall<\/strong> and <strong>side-wall<\/strong> strength, if different from the standard.<\/li><li><strong>One-door-off<\/strong> stacking strength, if applicable.<\/li><li><strong>Next examination date<\/strong>, or an <strong>ACEP<\/strong> number (Approved Continuous Examination Programme).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Examinations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>first periodic examination<\/strong> is within <strong>5 years<\/strong> of manufacture.<\/li><li>Then at intervals of <strong>no more than 30 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Unless the container is under an <strong>ACEP<\/strong>, where it&#x27;s examined at each major repair, refurbishment or on-hire/off-hire interchange, with an interval not exceeding 30 months, and the ACEP number is on the plate.<\/li><li>The owner is responsible for keeping it safe.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How the C/O uses it<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the <strong>maximum gross mass<\/strong> against the <strong>VGM<\/strong>: a box declared heavier than its maximum gross mass is overloaded.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>allowable stacking load<\/strong> for the position in the stack: the mass of the boxes above must not exceed it.<\/li><li>Check the plate is there and in date.<\/li><li><strong>Refuse<\/strong> <strong>damaged boxes<\/strong>, or boxes with <strong>no<\/strong> or <strong>expired<\/strong> plates. Look at the <strong>corner posts<\/strong>, <strong>rails<\/strong>, <strong>doors<\/strong> and <strong>corner castings<\/strong>: damage there affects the strength the lashing calculations rely on.<\/li><li>Report damaged boxes found on board to the terminal with a damage report.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Other markings<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>BIC code<\/strong>: owner code, serial number and check digit.<\/li><li>The <strong>size and type<\/strong> code (for example 45G1 for a 40&#x27; high-cube).<\/li><li>The <strong>customs<\/strong> approval plate (TIR) where fitted.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The CSC 1972 and the UK 1984 Regs, the plate&#x27;s contents including maximum gross mass, stacking load at 1.8 g and racking load, the examination intervals (first within 5 years, then 30 months, or ACEP), and how I use it: VGM against maximum gross mass, stacking load, and refusing damaged or unplated boxes.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is an ACEP?","pa":"<p><strong>ACEP: Approved Continuous Examination Programme<\/strong> (CSC). It&#x27;s an alternative to periodic examination, where the owner examines containers continuously during interchanges. The CSC plate shows <strong>&quot;ACEP&quot;<\/strong> and the approval reference.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q169","sec":11,"q":"Photo of collapsed container stacks — likely causes; containers lost in heavy weather — what happened and how do you defend yourself?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Likely causes of collapsed stacks<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Excessive accelerations<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>parametric<\/strong> or <strong>synchronous rolling<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>high GM<\/strong> giving a <strong>short, violent<\/strong> roll period;<\/li><li><strong>slamming<\/strong> or <strong>whipping<\/strong> in heavy head seas.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Overweight<\/strong> or <strong>misdeclared<\/strong> boxes (wrong <strong>VGM<\/strong>), or <strong>heavy boxes high<\/strong> in the stack, exceeding the <strong>stack weight<\/strong> or <strong>lashing<\/strong> limits.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing faults<\/strong>: wrong <strong>pattern<\/strong>, <strong>slack<\/strong> bars, <strong>unlocked<\/strong> or <strong>wrong<\/strong> twistlocks, <strong>worn<\/strong> or <strong>defective<\/strong> gear, mixed types.<\/li><li><strong>Structural failure<\/strong> of the <strong>corner posts<\/strong>, or a <strong>damaged box<\/strong> low in the stack.<\/li><li>A <strong>wrong lashing calculation<\/strong> for the <strong>actual GM<\/strong>, for example calculated for one GM and sailed with another after ballast changes.<\/li><li><strong>Poor routing<\/strong> or <strong>ship handling<\/strong> in heavy weather: head or following seas near a parametric resonance, too much speed.<\/li><li>Lashing bridges or fittings failed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reporting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS V/31 and V/32<\/strong>, amended by <strong>MSC.550(108)<\/strong> from <strong>1 January 2026<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Master reports<\/strong> the loss <strong>without delay<\/strong> to <strong>nearby ships<\/strong>, the <strong>nearest coastal State<\/strong> and the <strong>flag State<\/strong>, giving:<\/li><li>the <strong>position<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>number<\/strong> of boxes lost;<\/li><li>any <strong>DG<\/strong> and <strong>UN numbers<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>type<\/strong>, <strong>size<\/strong> and <strong>condition<\/strong> of the containers.<\/li><li><strong>MARPOL Protocol I<\/strong> if marine pollutants were lost.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong> if a reportable casualty is involved, the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I defend the ship: evidence<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Departure stability<\/strong> and <strong>lashing printouts<\/strong> showing compliance with the CSM for the actual GM.<\/li><li>The <strong>VGMs<\/strong>, and any <strong>draught discrepancy<\/strong> noted at departure.<\/li><li>The <strong>pre-departure lashing inspection<\/strong> record, bay by bay.<\/li><li><strong>Weather routeing<\/strong> advice and the <strong>forecasts<\/strong> received.<\/li><li>The <strong>deck log<\/strong>: weather, course and speed changes, <strong>heavy weather precautions<\/strong> taken, and <strong>lashing checks at sea<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>VDR<\/strong> saved, including the motion data, and <strong>photos<\/strong> of the damage.<\/li><li>The <strong>CSM<\/strong> and the <strong>lashing gear maintenance<\/strong> records and inventory.<\/li><li>A <strong>note of protest<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the P&amp;I club early<\/strong>, so they can appoint surveyors and lawyers.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>After the event<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the remaining stacks and lashings when safe, and the stability with the losses.<\/li><li>Look at the cause honestly for the company&#x27;s investigation, and change practice if needed.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The causes (accelerations from parametric rolling or high GM, misdeclared weights, lashing faults, structural failure, wrong calculations, ship handling), the SOLAS V/31–32 reports from 2026 with the contents, and the evidence to defend the ship: printouts, VGMs, lashing checks, weather and log entries, VDR, maintenance records, note of protest and P&amp;I.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is parametric rolling and how do you get out of it?","pa":"<p>Big rolls build up when the <strong>wave encounter period is about half the ship&#x27;s natural roll period<\/strong>, in head or following seas with wavelength about the ship&#x27;s length. Get out by <strong>changing course and/or speed<\/strong> to change the encounter period, usually slowing down in head seas.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q170","sec":11,"q":"Hatch cover won't close after loading below deck — why and what now?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Likely causes<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>container sticking up too high<\/strong>: a <strong>high-cube<\/strong> box in a cell planned for <strong>standard height<\/strong>, or a stack with <strong>too many tiers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A box <strong>not seated<\/strong> properly: <strong>jammed<\/strong> in <strong>bent cell guides<\/strong>, or a <strong>stacking cone<\/strong> or <strong>twistlock<\/strong> left on top of the stack.<\/li><li><strong>Loose lashing gear<\/strong>, <strong>debris<\/strong> or a <strong>cone<\/strong> on the <strong>coaming<\/strong> or in the hatch cover seating.<\/li><li>The <strong>cover<\/strong> or <strong>coaming<\/strong> damaged or misaligned, or the hatch cover <strong>cleats<\/strong> or <strong>locators<\/strong> fouled.<\/li><li>The <strong>wrong bay or stack<\/strong> loaded compared with the plan.<\/li><li>A large <strong>list<\/strong>, or <strong>deflection<\/strong> of the hull (hogging or sagging) affecting the fit of the cover.<\/li><li>Hydraulic or mechanical fault on a folding or lift-away cover.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop.<\/strong> Don&#x27;t let the <strong>crane driver force<\/strong> the cover down. Forcing it will <strong>damage<\/strong> the cover, the <strong>containers<\/strong> and the <strong>cargo<\/strong>, and the hold must be <strong>weathertight<\/strong> for the <strong>load line<\/strong> conditions of assignment.<\/li><li><strong>Inspect<\/strong> with the <strong>foreman<\/strong>, and compare with the <strong>bay plan<\/strong>. Look for the <strong>high box<\/strong> or the <strong>obstruction<\/strong>, using a torch or from the lashing bridge.<\/li><li>Have the <strong>terminal discharge or restow<\/strong> the offending box.<\/li><li><strong>Clear debris<\/strong> and loose gear from the coamings and seating.<\/li><li><strong>Check<\/strong> the <strong>cell guides<\/strong> and the <strong>cover<\/strong> for <strong>damage<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Once closed, check the cover is <strong>properly seated<\/strong> and <strong>cleated<\/strong>, with the compression on the seals. Do a <strong>weathertightness check<\/strong> (hose or ultrasonic) if there&#x27;s any doubt.<\/li><li>If the cover is damaged, report it to class, because it&#x27;s a closing appliance under the load line.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>damage report<\/strong> and a <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> to the <strong>stevedore<\/strong> or <strong>terminal<\/strong> if they were at fault.<\/li><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong> and the <strong>planner<\/strong>, and <strong>log<\/strong> the <strong>delay<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Photographs.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the plan for high-cubes in the cells, especially in the top tier under the covers.<\/li><li>Brief the cargo watch to check for cones and gear on the stack tops before the cover is closed.<\/li><li>Check the coaming tops before closing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A hatch cover not properly closed is a load line non-compliance and can let water into a hold full of cargo.<\/li><li>Containers on top of the cover add weight and must sit on a properly secured cover.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The causes (a high box, a box not seated, a cone left on top, debris, damage, the wrong box, deflection), stop and don&#x27;t force it, inspect with the foreman against the plan, restow or clear the obstruction, check for damage, confirm seating and weathertightness, and the damage report, letter of protest and log.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can you sail with a hatch cover not fully seated?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> It&#x27;s not weathertight, so the load line conditions of assignment aren&#x27;t met. Stack support may also be wrong. Clear the obstruction or restow, and get class or RO agreement for any temporary arrangement.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q171","sec":11,"q":"Loaded to your marks with 50 containers still to load — what now?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The UK <strong>MS (Load Line) Regs 1998<\/strong> and <strong>MSN 1752<\/strong>. <strong>Overloading is an offence<\/strong>, and the ship <strong>can be detained<\/strong>. I <strong>can&#x27;t exceed the marks<\/strong> to load the remaining boxes.<\/p><p><strong>Verify first<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Read the draughts<\/strong> accurately, <strong>fore<\/strong>, <strong>aft<\/strong> and <strong>midships<\/strong> on <strong>both sides<\/strong>, allowing for <strong>hog or sag<\/strong>. Use the mean of means.<\/li><li>Take a <strong>dock water density<\/strong> sample with the hydrometer, and apply the <strong>DWA<\/strong> (and FWA if relevant).<\/li><li>Check the <strong>zone<\/strong> and <strong>allowances<\/strong>: bunkers and fresh water used before reaching the next zone or a more restrictive one, and <strong>port draught<\/strong> limits. If the ship will pass into a more restrictive zone, she must be at that mark by then.<\/li><li>Compare the <strong>actual displacement<\/strong> with the <strong>loadicator<\/strong>. If the ship is <strong>deeper than calculated<\/strong>, the <strong>declared weights<\/strong> (<strong>VGMs<\/strong>) are probably <strong>wrong<\/strong>, or a tank figure is wrong. <strong>Report<\/strong> this to the <strong>Master<\/strong> and the <strong>terminal<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the soundings of the ballast and fresh water tanks, and the bunker figures with the Chief Engineer.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Options<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Discharge unnecessary ballast<\/strong>, if <strong>stability<\/strong>, <strong>stress<\/strong>, <strong>propeller immersion<\/strong> and <strong>visibility<\/strong> allow. Re-run the loadicator.<\/li><li><strong>Fresh water<\/strong> or other <strong>consumables<\/strong>, if appropriate: but the ship must keep enough for the voyage.<\/li><li>Review the trim: not usually a solution to the mean draught.<\/li><li>Otherwise the <strong>remaining 50 boxes<\/strong> are <strong>short-shipped<\/strong>, and the <strong>planner chooses<\/strong> which.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What I don&#x27;t do<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Load them anyway and hope the draught comes up during the passage.<\/li><li>Discharge ballast that makes the ship unstable or overstressed, or reduces the propeller immersion so the ship can&#x27;t be handled safely.<\/li><li>Accept pressure from the terminal or the charterer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong>, the <strong>company<\/strong>, the <strong>agent<\/strong> and the <strong>charterer<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Issue a <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> on the <strong>weight discrepancy<\/strong>, if the VGMs appear wrong, to the terminal and through them to the shippers.<\/li><li><strong>Log everything<\/strong>: draughts, density, displacement, the loadicator figure and the difference.<\/li><li>Make the <strong>draught and freeboard entry<\/strong> in the OLB before sailing.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Overloading reduces reserve buoyancy and freeboard, which the ship&#x27;s safety depends on.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s one of the most obvious things PSC can check, from the quay.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Overloading is an offence under the Load Line Regs; verify by reading the draughts carefully, the density and DWA, the zones and consumption; compare with the loadicator and suspect wrong VGMs; options of discharging ballast within stability and strength limits, or short-shipping; inform the Master, company, agent and charterer; and a letter of protest and log entries.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you calculate the DWA?","pa":"<p><strong>DWA = FWA × (1025 − ρ) ÷ 25<\/strong>, where ρ is the dock-water density in kg/m³. Example: FWA 200 mm, ρ 1010: DWA = 200 × 15 ÷ 25 = <strong>120 mm<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q172","sec":11,"q":"Brief the 2/O supervising DG loading; orders to the OOW on cargo watch.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Briefing the 2/O on DG loading<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>&quot;<strong>Here is the DG list and bay plan.<\/strong> Each DG box has a <strong>UN number<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong> and must go <strong>only<\/strong> in its <strong>planned slot<\/strong>.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;As each box comes, <strong>check<\/strong> the <strong>placards<\/strong> on all four sides, the <strong>UN number<\/strong> and the <strong>marine pollutant<\/strong> mark against the list, and look for <strong>leaks<\/strong>, <strong>damage<\/strong> and the <strong>seals<\/strong>.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;<strong>Refuse<\/strong> any <strong>leaking<\/strong> or <strong>damaged<\/strong> DG unit, or any with <strong>wrong<\/strong> or <strong>missing<\/strong> placards, and <strong>call me<\/strong>.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;<strong>Don&#x27;t accept<\/strong> a <strong>change of slot<\/strong> without my check of <strong>segregation<\/strong> and the <strong>DOC<\/strong>.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;<strong>Fire hoses<\/strong> rigged and ready near the DG stowage, <strong>no smoking<\/strong>, and the <strong>EmS<\/strong> available on the bridge, with the schedules for today&#x27;s DG marked.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;Any <strong>spill<\/strong>: <strong>stop work<\/strong>, <strong>clear the area<\/strong>, stay <strong>upwind<\/strong>, refer to the <strong>EmS<\/strong>, and <strong>call me<\/strong>.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;Temperature-controlled DG in reefers: check they&#x27;re plugged in and at the set temperature immediately.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;Class 1 and other special DG: check with me before they&#x27;re loaded.&quot;<\/li><li>&quot;Keep the DG list updated as boxes are loaded, so the final manifest is right.&quot;<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Standing orders for the OOW on cargo watch<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Moorings<\/strong>, the <strong>gangway<\/strong>, the <strong>safety net<\/strong> and the <strong>lifebuoy<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Draught<\/strong>, <strong>list<\/strong> and <strong>trim<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Ballast<\/strong> as per my plan, no changes without me.<\/li><li><strong>Stability<\/strong> and the <strong>loadicator<\/strong> updated against the <strong>actual loading sequence<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing<\/strong> checked on <strong>completed bays<\/strong> and the <strong>hatch covers<\/strong> before boxes go on them.<\/li><li>The <strong>ISPS level<\/strong> and the <strong>gangway watch<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Weather<\/strong> and <strong>tide<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Pollution<\/strong>: <strong>scuppers plugged<\/strong> during <strong>bunkering<\/strong>, and watch for <strong>hydraulic leaks<\/strong> from cranes and hatch covers.<\/li><li><strong>Reefers<\/strong>: plugged in and running.<\/li><li><strong>Call me<\/strong> if anything is <strong>outside the plan<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>a <strong>list<\/strong> beyond the figure I set;<\/li><li><strong>damage<\/strong> to the ship or boxes;<\/li><li>a <strong>DG problem<\/strong>;<\/li><li>an <strong>injury<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>weather change<\/strong> affecting the cranes or moorings;<\/li><li>and <strong>before the last box<\/strong>, so I can do the final checks.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Log<\/strong> the <strong>times<\/strong>, any <strong>damage<\/strong> and any <strong>protests<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why the briefing matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The 2/O is often the one at the gangway or on deck when the DG comes. If he doesn&#x27;t know what to look for, a leaking or wrongly placarded box goes on board, and that&#x27;s how ship fires start.<\/li><li>Written orders, signed, protect him and me.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A clear briefing for the DG checks (planned slot only, placards, UN number, leaks, refuse damaged ones, no slot changes without me, fire hoses, EmS, spill actions) and standing orders for the cargo watch with specific call-me triggers.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you do if a stevedore damages a DG container?","pa":"<p>Stop work in that area and keep people clear. Check for leakage using the <strong>EmS<\/strong>. Report to the Master, the terminal or port and the shipper. A damaged DG unit isn&#x27;t loaded or kept on board until it&#x27;s been inspected and repaired or repacked. Make a stevedore damage report and protest.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q173","sec":11,"q":"50 DG + 200 reefer containers — which documents do you refer to?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>For the 50 DG containers<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>DG manifest<\/strong> or <strong>special list<\/strong> and <strong>stowage plan<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS VII/4<\/strong>), with the position of each unit.<\/li><li>The <strong>DG declarations<\/strong> and <strong>container packing certificates<\/strong> from the shippers.<\/li><li>The <strong>IMDG Code<\/strong>, <strong>Amendment 42-24<\/strong>: <strong>Volume 2<\/strong>, the <strong>Dangerous Goods List<\/strong> for each <strong>UN number<\/strong>: class, packing group, special provisions, stowage and segregation codes (columns 16a and 16b).<\/li><li><strong>Volume 1<\/strong>: Chapter <strong>7.2<\/strong> and <strong>7.4<\/strong> for segregation and stowage on container ships.<\/li><li>The <strong>EmS Guide<\/strong> and <strong>MFAG<\/strong> (the IMDG Supplement).<\/li><li>The <strong>DOC for DG<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS II-2/19<\/strong>): which classes in which holds.<\/li><li>The <strong>CSM<\/strong> and the <strong>stability and lashing<\/strong> calculation.<\/li><li>Company DG procedures and any port restrictions.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>For the 200 reefers<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>reefer manifest<\/strong> or list: <strong>set temperature<\/strong>, <strong>ventilation<\/strong>, <strong>humidity<\/strong>, <strong>controlled atmosphere<\/strong> settings and any <strong>special instructions<\/strong> from the shipper (for example cold treatment).<\/li><li>The <strong>reefer monitoring log<\/strong> and <strong>data records<\/strong>, from the remote monitoring system.<\/li><li>The <strong>power and plug plan<\/strong>: the <strong>generator load<\/strong>, the <strong>number of plugs<\/strong>, and any <strong>reefer power distribution<\/strong> limits per bay. 200 reefers can be several megawatts.<\/li><li>The <strong>charter party<\/strong> or the line&#x27;s <strong>reefer clauses<\/strong>: who&#x27;s responsible for what.<\/li><li>The reefer maker&#x27;s manuals and alarm codes for the reefer electrician.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Where they overlap: temperature-controlled DG<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Organic peroxides<\/strong> (class <strong>5.2<\/strong>) and <strong>self-reactive substances<\/strong> (class <strong>4.1<\/strong>) need <strong>control<\/strong> and <strong>emergency temperatures<\/strong> (IMDG <strong>7.3.7<\/strong>). If the reefer fails and the temperature reaches the emergency temperature, the emergency procedure is activated.<\/li><li>Stowage must allow <strong>access<\/strong> for monitoring.<\/li><li><strong>Reefers using dry ice<\/strong> (UN <strong>1845<\/strong>) or a <strong>controlled atmosphere<\/strong> (CA, with nitrogen and CO₂) bring a risk of <strong>asphyxiation<\/strong> when opened or in a closed hold.<\/li><li>DG reefers have priority for power if there&#x27;s a generator problem.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Plus<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>cargo stowage plan<\/strong>, the <strong>loadicator<\/strong> printout and the <strong>bay plan<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The engine room&#x27;s power plan with the Chief Engineer.<\/li><li>The passage plan for the weather: reefers on deck exposed to heavy weather and green seas.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Monitoring at sea<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Reefer rounds at least twice a day, or as the remote system allows, with records.<\/li><li>DG rounds, and the EmS ready.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>For DG, the manifest and stowage plan under VII/4, declarations and packing certificates, IMDG 42-24 with the DGL, EmS and MFAG, the DOC, and the CSM; for reefers, the reefer manifest settings, monitoring records, the power and plug plan, and the charter party; and the overlap for temperature-controlled DG with control and emergency temperatures, and the dry ice and CA asphyxiation risk.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the \"emergency temperature\" for a temperature-controlled DG?","pa":"<p>The temperature at which <strong>emergency procedures<\/strong> must start for a temperature-controlled substance (self-reactive substances, organic peroxides). The <strong>control temperature<\/strong> is lower. Both are on the transport document (IMDG 7.3.7).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q174","sec":11,"q":"Which stresses act on a large container ship? Which is stressed more — bulk carrier or container ship?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Stresses on a large container ship<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Longitudinal bending<\/strong>: <strong>hogging<\/strong> and <strong>sagging<\/strong>, from the distribution of weight and buoyancy, and from waves. With heavy containers at the ends and the engine aft, still-water hogging is common.<\/li><li><strong>Shear forces<\/strong>, greatest around the quarter lengths.<\/li><li><strong>Torsion<\/strong> and <strong>warping<\/strong>. <strong>Wide hatch openings<\/strong> give very <strong>low torsional stiffness<\/strong>, especially in <strong>quartering<\/strong> or oblique seas, where the bow and stern are twisted in opposite directions. Hatch corners are stress concentration points.<\/li><li><strong>Whipping<\/strong> and <strong>springing<\/strong>: hull girder <strong>vibration<\/strong> from <strong>slamming<\/strong> (whipping) or wave excitation (springing). This affects <strong>ultra-large<\/strong> ships, which are long and flexible. It was a factor in the loss of the MOL Comfort in 2013.<\/li><li><strong>Racking<\/strong>: distortion of the transverse section when rolling.<\/li><li><strong>Panting<\/strong> at the bow and stern, from varying water pressure.<\/li><li><strong>Pounding and slamming<\/strong> at the bow, especially with large bow flare.<\/li><li><strong>Local loads<\/strong>: <strong>stack weights<\/strong> on the <strong>hatch covers<\/strong> and the <strong>tank top<\/strong>, and the <strong>lashing loads<\/strong> on the fittings.<\/li><li><strong>Parametric rolling<\/strong> loads on the lashings and the containers.<\/li><li>Thermal stresses from heated fuel tanks.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Which is stressed more: bulk carrier or container ship?<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>It depends on the load case.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>A bulk carrier<\/strong> is stressed more <strong>in port<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li>very high <strong>shear force<\/strong> and <strong>bending<\/strong> from <strong>alternate-hold loading<\/strong> with heavy cargo like iron ore;<\/li><li><strong>high-density<\/strong> cargoes concentrated in a few holds;<\/li><li><strong>fast loading rates<\/strong>, which can outrun the deballasting, and the risk of getting the <strong>load/deballast sequence<\/strong> wrong;<\/li><li>the <strong>BLU Code<\/strong> and <strong>SOLAS XII<\/strong> address these risks, with the loading instrument required.<\/li><li>Bulk carriers have broken in port and at sea from these stresses.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A large container ship<\/strong> is more vulnerable <strong>at sea<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li><strong>torsion<\/strong> with the wide open hatches;<\/li><li><strong>whipping<\/strong> and <strong>wave-induced bending<\/strong> in large, long ships;<\/li><li>typically loaded with a <strong>high GM<\/strong> and run at <strong>high speed<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>So my answer<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;A bulker sees greater <strong>still-water<\/strong> stresses, especially in port during loading. A large container ship is more critical for <strong>dynamic<\/strong> and <strong>torsional<\/strong> stresses in a seaway. <strong>Loadicator<\/strong> limits for still-water bending, shear and torsion must be respected on both, and in heavy weather the speed and heading must be adjusted.&quot;<\/p><p><strong>On my container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The loadicator checks shear, bending and torsion at each stage.<\/li><li>Some ships have hull stress monitoring, which I&#x27;d watch in heavy weather.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The list of stresses with torsion from wide hatches and whipping on large ships, local loads from stacks and lashings; and a balanced answer that a bulker is stressed more in port in still water and a container ship more in a seaway for torsion and dynamic loads, with loadicator limits on both.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Which loading condition causes maximum torsion?","pa":"<p><strong>Oblique or quartering seas<\/strong> combined with <strong>uneven loading<\/strong> across the ship, for example heavier to port forward and to starboard aft. Large hatch openings make container ships especially sensitive.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q175","sec":11,"q":"IMDG contents; EmS and MFAG.","tier":"red","rep":64,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>IMDG Code<\/strong> is mandatory under <strong>SOLAS VII Part A<\/strong> and <strong>MARPOL Annex III<\/strong>. In the UK, the <strong>MS (Dangerous Goods and Marine Pollutants) Regs 1997<\/strong>. The current edition is <strong>Amendment 42-24<\/strong>, mandatory from <strong>1 January 2026<\/strong>. Amendments come every <strong>two years<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Volume 1<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Part 1<\/strong>: <strong>general provisions<\/strong>, definitions, <strong>training<\/strong> (Chapter 1.3) and <strong>security<\/strong> provisions (Chapter 1.4).<\/li><li><strong>Part 2<\/strong>: <strong>classification<\/strong>: the nine classes and their divisions, marine pollutants.<\/li><li><strong>Part 4<\/strong>: <strong>packing<\/strong> and <strong>tank<\/strong> provisions.<\/li><li><strong>Part 5<\/strong>: <strong>consignment<\/strong> procedures: <strong>marking<\/strong>, <strong>labelling<\/strong>, <strong>placarding<\/strong> and <strong>documents<\/strong>, fumigated units and coolants.<\/li><li><strong>Part 6<\/strong>: <strong>construction<\/strong> and <strong>testing<\/strong> of packagings, IBCs, tanks and vehicles.<\/li><li><strong>Part 7<\/strong>: <strong>stowage<\/strong> and <strong>segregation<\/strong>, including container ships (7.4) and temperature control (7.3.7).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Volume 2<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Part 3<\/strong>: the <strong>Dangerous Goods List<\/strong>, <strong>special provisions<\/strong>, and <strong>limited and excepted quantities<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Appendix A<\/strong>: generic and N.O.S. names.<\/li><li><strong>Appendix B<\/strong>: glossary of terms.<\/li><li><strong>Index<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The nine classes<\/strong><\/p><p>1 Explosives; 2 Gases; 3 Flammable liquids; 4 Flammable solids and substances liable to spontaneous combustion or dangerous when wet; 5 Oxidizers and organic peroxides; 6 Toxic and infectious substances; 7 Radioactive; 8 Corrosives; 9 Miscellaneous.<\/p><p><strong>The Supplement<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>EmS Guide<\/strong>: <strong>fire<\/strong> schedules <strong>F-A to F-J<\/strong>, and <strong>spillage<\/strong> schedules <strong>S-A to S-Z<\/strong>, referenced in <strong>DGL column 15<\/strong> for each UN number.<\/li><li><strong>MFAG<\/strong>: the <strong>Medical First Aid Guide<\/strong> for use in accidents involving DG, with tables for <strong>treatment<\/strong> and <strong>poisoning<\/strong>, used with the <strong>Ship Captain&#x27;s Medical Guide<\/strong> and <strong>TMAS<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Reporting procedures<\/strong> for incidents involving DG.<\/li><li><strong>CTU Code<\/strong> guidance on packing cargo transport units.<\/li><li>The <strong>INF Code<\/strong> (irradiated nuclear fuel), and guidance on the safe use of pesticides.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How I use it on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For each DG box: the DGL entry, the EmS schedule, the segregation and stowage.<\/li><li>In an emergency: the EmS schedule first, then MFAG for casualties.<\/li><li>The crew handling DG need training under Chapter 1.3.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My notes<\/strong><\/p><p>My notes may show an older amendment. <strong>Quote 42-24<\/strong>.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS VII and MARPOL Annex III, the 1997 UK Regs, Amendment 42-24 from 1 January 2026, the parts in Volumes 1 and 2, the nine classes, and the Supplement with the EmS (F-A to F-J, S-A to S-Z, from column 15) and MFAG.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Where in the DGL do you find the EmS for a substance?","pa":"<p><strong>Column 15<\/strong> of the Dangerous Goods List.<\/p>","asked":["How do you use the IMDG Code?","How will you know that vessel can carry imdg cargo?","How will you segregate IMDG container?","What is in the IMDG goods list?","What are all the classes of IMDG Cargo?"],"ex":[["Ahsan Khan",12],["Bill Bennett",9],["Examiner not named",7],["Chris Bates",6],["Kris Askey",6]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q176","sec":11,"q":"Fumigation in transit.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The IMO <strong>Recommendations on the safe use of pesticides in ships<\/strong> (<strong>MSC.1/Circ.1358<\/strong>), and those applying to <strong>fumigation of cargo holds<\/strong> (<strong>MSC.1/Circ.1264<\/strong>). For containers, IMDG <strong>5.5.2<\/strong> (fumigated cargo transport units, <strong>UN 3359<\/strong>). UK MCA guidance exists, but verify the MGN/MSN number before quoting it.<\/p><p><strong>In-transit fumigation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Only done if the <strong>flag Administration allows<\/strong> it and the <strong>Master agrees<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Mainly for bulk grain cargoes in holds.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before sailing<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>competent fumigator<\/strong> gives <strong>written information<\/strong>: the <strong>fumigant<\/strong>, <strong>quantity<\/strong>, <strong>date and time<\/strong> applied, the <strong>hazards<\/strong>, <strong>antidotes<\/strong> and the <strong>SDS<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The fumigator checks that the <strong>spaces are gas-tight<\/strong>: hatch covers sealed, vents closed.<\/li><li>The ship has at least <strong>two trained crew<\/strong>, <strong>gas detectors<\/strong> for that fumigant, and <strong>SCBA<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Warning signs<\/strong> posted at the entrances to the fumigated spaces.<\/li><li>The <strong>accommodation<\/strong> and <strong>adjacent spaces<\/strong> are checked to be <strong>gas-free<\/strong> before sailing.<\/li><li>The Master receives the fumigator&#x27;s certificate and instructions.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>At sea<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Gas checks<\/strong> in the accommodation and working spaces at regular intervals (at least every 8 hours), <strong>logged<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>No entry<\/strong> into fumigated spaces.<\/li><li>If gas is detected in the accommodation, evacuate the area, ventilate, find the leak and seal it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>At discharge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ventilate<\/strong> before opening, and get a <strong>gas-free certificate<\/strong> before anyone enters the hold.<\/li><li>Inform the port in advance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fumigated containers on my ship<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>fumigation warning mark<\/strong> on the <strong>doors<\/strong>, showing the fumigant, the date and time.<\/li><li><strong>Declared<\/strong> on the DG documents as UN 3359 if still under fumigation.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilated<\/strong> ashore and certified <strong>gas-free<\/strong> before anyone opens them.<\/li><li>Stowed where they can be checked, and away from the accommodation and ventilation intakes.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Phosphine<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The typical fumigant, released from aluminium or magnesium phosphide tablets.<\/li><li><strong>Highly toxic<\/strong>, and <strong>flammable<\/strong> at high concentrations.<\/li><li>It may have a garlic or fishy smell, but <strong>smell is not a reliable warning<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Symptoms of phosphine exposure<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Mild<\/strong>: headache, dizziness, fatigue, nausea, vomiting, a feeling of chest tightness or pressure, coughing.<\/li><li><strong>Severe<\/strong>: breathing difficulty, pulmonary oedema (fluid on the lungs), low blood pressure, heart problems, convulsions, unconsciousness and death.<\/li><li>Symptoms can be <strong>delayed<\/strong> for hours.<\/li><li>Treatment: remove from exposure, rest, oxygen, and <strong>TMAS<\/strong> advice with the <strong>MFAG<\/strong>. No specific antidote.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MSC.1/Circ.1358 and 1264, IMDG 5.5.2 and UN 3359, flag and Master agreement for in-transit fumigation, written information from the fumigator, gas-tight spaces, trained crew and detectors, warning signs, regular gas checks, no entry, ventilation and a gas-free certificate at discharge; and phosphine symptoms.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★12.1","sec":11,"q":"What changed in IMDG on 1 Jan 2026?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"IMDG Amdt 42-24","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>Amendment 42-24<\/strong> became <strong>mandatory<\/strong> on <strong>1 January 2026<\/strong>. It could be used <strong>voluntarily<\/strong> from <strong>1 January 2025<\/strong>. The IMDG Code is amended every <strong>two years<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS VII<\/strong>, and in the UK it&#x27;s applied through the <strong>Dangerous Goods and Marine Pollutants Regs 1997<\/strong> and the implementing notice (<strong>MIN 715<\/strong> covers the adoption of Amdt 42-24; verify).<\/p><p><strong>Themes I&#x27;d mention<\/strong> (verify the details in the Code itself)<\/p><ol><li>Further changes to the <strong>battery<\/strong> provisions: <strong>lithium-ion<\/strong> and <strong>sodium-ion<\/strong> batteries, and <strong>battery-powered vehicles<\/strong> and <strong>equipment<\/strong>. Sodium-ion and EV entries may date from 41-22, so check what is actually new in 42-24.<\/li><li><strong>Vehicles and EVs<\/strong> carried as cargo: clearer rules for battery-powered vehicles, linked to the <strong>fire-risk<\/strong> concerns after several car carrier and ro-ro fires.<\/li><li>Updated <strong>segregation<\/strong> and <strong>stowage codes<\/strong> in the DGL, plus <strong>new entries<\/strong> and <strong>special provisions<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Corrections<\/strong> and <strong>alignment<\/strong> with the <strong>UN Model Regulations<\/strong> (the &quot;Orange Book&quot;).<\/li><li>Changes to documentation and marking requirements.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Practical effect for me<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check that the <strong>onboard copy<\/strong> and the <strong>stowage software<\/strong> are <strong>updated<\/strong> to 42-24. Old software can give the wrong segregation.<\/li><li><strong>Shore staff<\/strong> and <strong>crew<\/strong> need <strong>training<\/strong> on the changes (<strong>IMDG Ch 1.3<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>DG declarations<\/strong> must reflect the <strong>current<\/strong> entries; an out-of-date entry is a reason to query the shipper.<\/li><li>The <strong>EmS Guide<\/strong> and MFAG updated to match.<\/li><li><strong>PSC<\/strong> may check this, especially during the <strong>CIC on cargo securing<\/strong> (September to November 2026).<\/li><li>The company&#x27;s DG acceptance procedures updated.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How the Code changes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Adopted by the MSC, with amendments every two years.<\/li><li>A voluntary year, then mandatory.<\/li><li>The next amendment, 43-26, would follow the same pattern (verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How to answer honestly<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;Amendment 42-24 became mandatory on 1 January 2026. The main themes are batteries and battery-powered vehicles, updated DGL entries and segregation, and alignment with the UN Model Regulations. On board, I make sure our copy, software and EmS are updated and the crew are trained.&quot; If the examiner asks for a specific change I&#x27;m unsure of, I say I&#x27;d check the Code rather than guess.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>42-24 mandatory from 1 January 2026, voluntary from 2025, two-year cycle under SOLAS VII; the main themes; and the practical actions: updated copy and software, training under 1.3, declarations, and PSC checks.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you know your onboard IMDG Code is the current edition?","pa":"<p>Check the <strong>amendment number<\/strong> on the book or electronic version (<strong>42-24<\/strong>, mandatory from 1 Jan 2026) against the company&#x27;s document control and circulars, and IMO&#x27;s list of the current amendment.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★12.2","sec":11,"q":"A reefer with UN 1845 (dry ice) is under deck. Hazard?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>UN 1845<\/strong> is <strong>carbon dioxide, solid<\/strong> (<strong>dry ice<\/strong>), <strong>class 9<\/strong>. It&#x27;s used as a <strong>coolant<\/strong> in some boxes, for example for frozen goods or pharmaceuticals, rather than a running reefer unit, or as a back-up.<\/p><p><strong>The hazard<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Dry ice <strong>sublimates<\/strong> into <strong>CO₂ gas<\/strong>, about 500 litres of gas per kg.<\/li><li>CO₂ is <strong>heavier than air<\/strong> and <strong>collects<\/strong> in the <strong>bottom<\/strong> of <strong>holds<\/strong>, <strong>bilges<\/strong> and the <strong>container<\/strong> itself.<\/li><li>CO₂ is an <strong>asphyxiant<\/strong> and <strong>toxic<\/strong>: it displaces oxygen, and <strong>high concentrations<\/strong> cause <strong>rapid collapse<\/strong>, at around 10% within minutes.<\/li><li>It gives <strong>no warning<\/strong> by <strong>smell<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Under deck, with ventilation off, the hold atmosphere can become dangerous, especially for the reefer electrician doing rounds.<\/li><li>Cold burns if handled.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Precautions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>IMDG 5.5.3<\/strong> (coolants): the container carries a <strong>warning mark<\/strong> on the <strong>doors<\/strong> (&quot;<strong>contains dry ice — coolant<\/strong>&quot; or &quot;conditioner&quot;), and it must be <strong>ventilated<\/strong> before anyone <strong>enters<\/strong> the box.<\/li><li>The transport document states UN 1845 and the quantity.<\/li><li><strong>Stow<\/strong> it so a <strong>build-up<\/strong> of gas <strong>doesn&#x27;t endanger<\/strong> the crew. Prefer <strong>on deck<\/strong> where possible.<\/li><li><strong>Under deck<\/strong>, the <strong>hold ventilation<\/strong> must be <strong>running<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Treat the hold as an enclosed space<\/strong>: <strong>atmosphere testing<\/strong> for <strong>O₂ and CO₂<\/strong> before entry, a <strong>permit<\/strong>, and <strong>personal gas monitors<\/strong> for reefer checks.<\/li><li><strong>Brief<\/strong> the <strong>reefer electrician<\/strong> and the crew.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>DOC<\/strong> and the hold&#x27;s ventilation capacity for this cargo.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Nobody goes into that hold<\/strong> for reefer rounds without <strong>ventilation running<\/strong> and an <strong>O₂/CO₂ check<\/strong> first.<\/p><p><strong>Related hazards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Controlled atmosphere (CA)<\/strong> reefers use nitrogen and CO₂ to keep fruit. Opening one, or a leak into the hold, gives the same asphyxiation risk.<\/li><li><strong>Fumigated<\/strong> containers (UN 3359) are similar for toxic gas.<\/li><li><strong>CO₂ from the fixed fire system<\/strong>, if a leak occurs in the CO₂ room.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If someone collapses in the hold<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Don&#x27;t go in without BA. Raise the alarm, rescue team in SCBA, ventilation, and treat as an enclosed space casualty.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>UN 1845 class 9, it sublimates into CO₂ which is heavier than air, an asphyxiant with no warning; IMDG 5.5.3 marking and ventilation before entry; stowage on deck preferred; under deck, ventilation running and treat as an enclosed space with O₂ and CO₂ testing and personal monitors; and nobody in without that.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is a personal O₂ meter alone not enough here?","pa":"<p>Dry ice gives off <strong>CO₂<\/strong>, which is <strong>toxic in itself<\/strong>. CO₂ reaches harmful levels while oxygen is still near <strong>20–21%<\/strong>, so an O₂ meter alone can show &quot;safe&quot; while the CO₂ is dangerous. You need a <strong>CO₂ meter<\/strong> too.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★12.3","sec":11,"q":"How do you check that the lashing applied matches the lashing plan?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS VI/5.6<\/strong> and <strong>VII/5<\/strong>, the approved <strong>Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong> and the <strong>CSS Code<\/strong>. This is also the focus of the <strong>Paris and Tokyo MoU CIC on cargo securing<\/strong> (1 September to 30 November 2026).<\/p><p><strong>Before loading<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Run the <strong>lashing software<\/strong>, which uses the <strong>CSM-approved<\/strong> model, against the <strong>final GM<\/strong> and <strong>stow<\/strong>, and the expected route and weather.<\/li><li>It produces the <strong>lashing plan<\/strong> per bay and flags any <strong>overload<\/strong> on the lashings, twistlocks or corner posts.<\/li><li><strong>Adjust<\/strong> the <strong>stow<\/strong> or the <strong>pattern<\/strong> until everything is OK, for example by moving heavy boxes lower or adding lashings.<\/li><li>Give the lashing plan to the terminal&#x27;s lashing gang foreman.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>During and after loading: deck rounds by bay<\/strong><\/p><p>With the <strong>bosun<\/strong> and a <strong>duty officer<\/strong> checking against the <strong>printout<\/strong>:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Twistlocks locked<\/strong>: handles in the right position, the correct <strong>left-hand/right-hand<\/strong> type, and <strong>midlocks<\/strong> or <strong>fully automatic locks<\/strong> of the <strong>approved<\/strong> type.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing bars<\/strong> on the <strong>right tier<\/strong> and <strong>corner<\/strong> as the plan shows; <strong>turnbuckles tensioned<\/strong> and <strong>locked<\/strong> with nuts or pins; <strong>no bars missing<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Bridge fittings<\/strong> and <strong>buttresses<\/strong> for stacks where required.<\/li><li><strong>Hatch cover<\/strong> locks and cleats.<\/li><li><strong>Odd-sized<\/strong> or <strong>OOG<\/strong> units lashed as per the <strong>CSM<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Correct heights<\/strong> and <strong>positions<\/strong>: the boxes in the planned slots, especially high-cubes and heavy boxes.<\/li><li>The <strong>lashing gear<\/strong> itself in good condition: no damaged bars or locks used.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Sign-off<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The officer <strong>records<\/strong> that the bays are <strong>checked<\/strong>, on a lashing checklist.<\/li><li>The <strong>stevedore corrects<\/strong> any <strong>defects<\/strong> before <strong>departure<\/strong>. The ship doesn&#x27;t sail with lashing defects.<\/li><li>The departure lashing printout kept on board for PSC and for any claim.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Showing a PSC officer<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>CSM<\/strong> approval and the lashing software approval.<\/li><li>The <strong>departure printout<\/strong> for that voyage, showing the GM and the forces within limits.<\/li><li>The <strong>lashing checklist<\/strong> signed by bay.<\/li><li>A walk on deck to a bay of his choice, comparing what&#x27;s fitted with the plan.<\/li><li>The <strong>lashing gear inventory<\/strong> and certificates, and the PMS records.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>At sea<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Re-tension<\/strong> lashings after <strong>heavy rolling<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Inspections<\/strong> before and after <strong>heavy weather<\/strong>, when it&#x27;s safe to go on deck.<\/li><li><strong>Log<\/strong> them.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The approved software run for the final GM and stow, giving a plan per bay; deck rounds by bay with the printout checking twistlocks, bars, turnbuckles, bridge fittings and positions; defects corrected by the stevedore before sailing; signed records; how I&#x27;d show PSC; and re-tensioning and inspections at sea.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What would a PSC officer ask to see during the cargo securing CIC?","pa":"<p>He&#x27;d ask to see:<\/p><ul><li>the approved <strong>CSM<\/strong>;<\/li><li>lashing gear <strong>certificates and inventory<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>lashing software printout<\/strong>;<\/li><li>stack weights and <strong>VGM<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the PMS records for lashing gear;<\/li><li>the lashing actually applied on deck.<\/li><\/ul><p>He&#x27;ll also test the <strong>crew&#x27;s knowledge<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q177","sec":12,"q":"IMSBC: purpose, groups A/B/C, accepting a shipment, TML/MC certificates (6 months / 7 days), can test, information from the terminal.","tier":"red","rep":82,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Carriage of Cargoes) Regs 1999<\/strong> (check for a newer version). Internationally, <strong>SOLAS VI and VII Part A-1<\/strong>, which make the <strong>IMSBC Code<\/strong> mandatory.<\/p><p><strong>Purpose<\/strong><\/p><p>To make the carriage of <strong>solid bulk cargoes<\/strong> safe by giving the <strong>hazards<\/strong>, <strong>properties<\/strong>, and the <strong>loading<\/strong>, <strong>carriage<\/strong> and <strong>testing<\/strong> requirements for each cargo in its <strong>individual schedule<\/strong>, plus general provisions on trimming, stability, and the cargo information the shipper must give.<\/p><p><strong>The groups<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Group A<\/strong>: cargoes that <strong>may liquefy<\/strong> if shipped at a <strong>moisture content<\/strong> above their <strong>TML<\/strong> (transportable moisture limit). Examples: mineral <strong>concentrates<\/strong>, <strong>iron ore fines<\/strong>, nickel ore, some <strong>coals<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Group B<\/strong>: cargoes with a <strong>chemical hazard<\/strong>, such as self-heating, flammable gas, toxic gas or corrosion. Examples: <strong>coal<\/strong> (self-heating, methane), <strong>DRI<\/strong> (direct reduced iron, reacts with water to give hydrogen), sulphur, ammonium nitrate fertilisers.<\/li><li><strong>Group C<\/strong>: <strong>neither<\/strong>. They can still be very <strong>dense<\/strong>, or <strong>shift<\/strong> if not trimmed.<\/li><li>Some cargoes are both A and B.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Accepting a shipment<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>shipper&#x27;s declaration<\/strong> with full <strong>cargo information<\/strong> before loading (<strong>SOLAS VI/2<\/strong> and IMSBC section 4): the BCSN (bulk cargo shipping name), group, UN number if any, stowage factor, angle of repose, moisture content and TML for Group A, chemical properties.<\/li><li>For <strong>Group A<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>a <strong>TML certificate<\/strong>, from a test within <strong>6 months<\/strong> of loading;<\/li><li>a <strong>moisture content certificate<\/strong>, from sampling within <strong>7 days<\/strong> of loading.<\/li><li><strong>Re-test<\/strong> if there has been <strong>significant rain<\/strong> in between.<\/li><li>The moisture content must be <strong>below the TML<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>The cargo <strong>listed<\/strong> in the IMSBC Code, or carried under <strong>competent-authority conditions<\/strong> (a tripartite agreement if not listed).<\/li><li><strong>Holds clean<\/strong> and suitable, and the <strong>stability and strength plan<\/strong> agreed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The can test<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>ship-side check<\/strong> for Group A.<\/li><li>Half-fill a can with a sample, <strong>bang<\/strong> it on a hard surface about <strong>25 times<\/strong> from about 0.2 m.<\/li><li><strong>Free moisture<\/strong> or a <strong>fluid surface<\/strong> means <strong>stop loading<\/strong> and get a <strong>lab test<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It <strong>doesn&#x27;t replace<\/strong> the <strong>certificate<\/strong>, but it can reveal a wrong one.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>From the terminal (BLU Code)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Loading rate<\/strong>, the number of <strong>pours<\/strong> and the sequence.<\/li><li><strong>Air draught<\/strong> limits under the loaders.<\/li><li><strong>Berth depth<\/strong> and the UKC.<\/li><li><strong>Stopping time<\/strong> for the loaders, so the ship can stop them in time.<\/li><li>The ship/shore safety checklist.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS VI and VII make it mandatory, groups A, B and C with examples, the shipper&#x27;s declaration, the TML within 6 months and MC within 7 days with retests after rain, the can test as a check not a replacement, and the BLU information from the terminal.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you do if the can test fails?","pa":"<p><strong>Don&#x27;t load.<\/strong> Inform the Master, the shipper and the terminal, and ask for <strong>proper laboratory testing<\/strong>. The cargo may be above its TML. If loading has started, stop and consider discharging what&#x27;s on board (IMSBC 8.4: the can test is only a check, but a failed one means stop).<\/p>","asked":["Bulk carrier loading Iron Ore what would you do?","IMSBC Code, what is it ?","What are hazards associated with bulk cargo?","Now you are in a bulk carrier, what are group A cargos?","IMSBC contents, what are the groups of cargoes?"],"ex":[["Sarah Lawlor",10],["Alec Keep",9],["Examiner not named",9],["Ahsan Khan",9],["Shiva Balasubramanian",8]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q178","sec":12,"q":"Coal: hazards, ventilation, CO rising, coal fire.","tier":"orange","rep":20,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>IMSBC Code coal schedule<\/strong> (Group <strong>B<\/strong>, and <strong>A and B<\/strong> for fine coal). In the UK, the Carriage of Cargoes Regs 1999.<\/p><p><strong>Hazards<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Methane<\/strong>: flammable, lighter than air, explosive in mixtures between about 5% and 16% in air. Collects at the top of the hold and in adjacent spaces.<\/li><li><strong>Self-heating<\/strong>, leading to <strong>fire<\/strong> and <strong>carbon monoxide<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Oxygen depletion<\/strong> in the hold and <strong>adjacent spaces<\/strong>, such as the access trunks and store rooms.<\/li><li><strong>Corrosion<\/strong> from <strong>acidic bilge water<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Liquefaction<\/strong> of <strong>fine coal<\/strong> (Group A).<\/li><li>Toxic gases, and coal dust explosion during loading.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Ventilation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Surface ventilation only<\/strong>, <strong>not through<\/strong> the cargo, to remove <strong>methane<\/strong>, normally for the <strong>first 24 hours<\/strong> after departure.<\/li><li>The schedule and the <strong>shipper&#x27;s information<\/strong> may require <strong>closing<\/strong> it if the coal is <strong>self-heating<\/strong>, because air feeds the heating.<\/li><li>No smoking, no naked lights, and electrical equipment in the hold areas safe.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Monitoring<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>CH₄<\/strong>, <strong>O₂<\/strong> and <strong>CO<\/strong>, through sampling points and <strong>portable meters<\/strong>, without opening the hatches.<\/li><li>The <strong>temperature<\/strong> of the cargo, through sounding pipes if fitted.<\/li><li>The <strong>pH<\/strong> of the <strong>bilges<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Log<\/strong> them regularly, at least daily.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>CO rising<\/strong><\/p><p>If <strong>CO<\/strong> reaches about <strong>50 ppm<\/strong>, or is <strong>rising steadily<\/strong> over consecutive readings, <strong>self-heating<\/strong> may be developing (IMSBC; verify the exact wording).<\/p><ol><li><strong>Close the hatches<\/strong> and <strong>all ventilation<\/strong> to starve it of oxygen, and confirm with readings over time.<\/li><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong> and the <strong>company<\/strong>. Get <strong>specialist advice<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t open<\/strong> the hatches.<\/li><li>Consider <strong>CO₂ injection<\/strong> if fitted.<\/li><li><strong>Divert<\/strong> to port for <strong>discharge<\/strong> if it doesn&#x27;t stabilise.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Coal fire<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Seal<\/strong> the hold.<\/li><li><strong>Inject CO₂<\/strong> if available.<\/li><li><strong>Boundary cool<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Divert<\/strong> to port.<\/li><li><strong>Discharge<\/strong> the affected coal ashore with <strong>fire services<\/strong> standing by.<\/li><li><strong>Water only in port<\/strong>, under <strong>specialist advice<\/strong>: water on hot coal can give hydrogen and steam explosions, and adds weight and free surface.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Entry<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The hold and adjacent spaces are enclosed spaces. Test the atmosphere before entry, with a permit.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The hazards (methane, self-heating with CO, O₂ depletion, corrosion, liquefaction), surface ventilation for 24 hours unless self-heating, monitoring CH₄, O₂, CO, temperature and pH, the 50 ppm or rising CO trigger with closing ventilation and getting advice, and the coal fire actions with water only in port.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why surface ventilation and not through-ventilation?","pa":"<p>Through-ventilation pushes <strong>air (oxygen) into the cargo mass<\/strong>, which feeds <strong>self-heating<\/strong> and spontaneous combustion. Surface ventilation clears <strong>methane<\/strong> from above the cargo without forcing air through it.<\/p>","asked":["How do you test moisture content in coal cargo?","Coal loading precautions?","Then after the ship has departed, hazards of coal cargo while underway?","COAL- Prior to Loading","You are then asked to load a cargo of coal. What is special about coal?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",13],["Sarah Lawlor",3],["Zia Ul Haq",2],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",2],["Fazal Wadood",2]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q179","sec":12,"q":"Grain: hold preparation, DOA, loading without a DOA, criteria (12°, 0.075 m·rad, GM 0.30 m), stopping grain shift.","tier":"red","rep":61,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Carriage of Cargoes) Regs 1999<\/strong>. Internationally, <strong>SOLAS VI Part C<\/strong> and the <strong>International Grain Code<\/strong> (MSC.23(59)).<\/p><p><strong>Hold preparation<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Clean<\/strong>, <strong>dry<\/strong> and free from <strong>residues<\/strong>, <strong>insects<\/strong>, <strong>rust scale<\/strong> and <strong>odours<\/strong>: &quot;grain clean&quot;.<\/li><li><strong>Bilges<\/strong> clean and <strong>strum boxes<\/strong> covered with <strong>burlap<\/strong> to stop the grain blocking the suctions.<\/li><li><strong>Hatch covers weathertight<\/strong>: hose or ultrasonic test.<\/li><li>Fresh-water rinse to remove salt, and dry.<\/li><li><strong>Hold inspection<\/strong> by a <strong>surveyor<\/strong> before loading, and a certificate of readiness.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Document of Authorisation (DOA)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Issued by the <strong>flag State<\/strong> or <strong>RO<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It shows the ship can meet the <strong>grain criteria<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>grain loading information<\/strong> must be on board: the <strong>volumetric heeling moments<\/strong> for each hold, filled and partly filled, the maximum permissible heeling moments, and worked examples.<\/li><li>The Master must show the ship complies before sailing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Loading without a DOA<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Only under the <strong>Grain Code&#x27;s limited provisions<\/strong>: the <strong>total grain<\/strong> is <strong>not more than one third of deadweight<\/strong>, with <strong>additional conditions<\/strong> on <strong>trimming<\/strong>, longitudinal divisions and <strong>GM<\/strong> (Grain Code; verify).<\/li><li>In practice, the flag State&#x27;s permission is needed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The intact stability criteria<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>angle of heel<\/strong> from the <strong>assumed grain shift<\/strong> is not more than <strong>12°<\/strong>, or the angle at which the <strong>deck edge<\/strong> is immersed if that&#x27;s less.<\/li><li>The <strong>residual area<\/strong> between the <strong>heeling arm<\/strong> and the <strong>GZ<\/strong> curves, up to the angle of maximum difference, or 40°, or the flooding angle, whichever is least, is at least <strong>0.075 m·rad<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The initial <strong>GM<\/strong>, corrected for <strong>free surface<\/strong>, is at least <strong>0.30 m<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How the heeling arm is worked out<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Total volumetric heeling moment ÷ stowage factor gives the heeling moment in tonne-metres; ÷ displacement gives the heeling arm at 0°, and it&#x27;s taken as 0.8 of that at 40°.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Stopping grain shift<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Trimming<\/strong> filled compartments <strong>full<\/strong>, so there are no voids.<\/li><li>In <strong>partly filled<\/strong> compartments:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>saucers<\/strong> or <strong>bundles<\/strong> of bagged grain in the hatch square;<\/li><li><strong>overstowing<\/strong> with bagged grain or other cargo;<\/li><li><strong>strapping and lashing<\/strong> the surface.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Shifting boards<\/strong> (longitudinal divisions) to reduce the transverse shift.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS VI Part C and the Grain Code, hold preparation to grain clean with burlap and a surveyor&#x27;s inspection, the DOA and the grain loading information, the limited provisions without a DOA, the three criteria (12°, 0.075 m·rad, GM 0.30 m), and how to stop grain shifting.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the volumetric heeling moment?","pa":"<p>The heeling moment from the assumed <strong>grain shift<\/strong>, in <strong>m⁴<\/strong>, taken from the grain loading tables. <strong>Divide by the stowage factor<\/strong> (m³/t) to get the heeling moment in t·m.<\/p>","asked":["Loading grain cargo, what all documents required?","Stability criteria for grain?","What is the stability requirement for Grain?","What are the dangers of grain cargo?","How will u come to know that grain has shifted?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",21],["Alec Keep",12],["Ahsan Khan",8],["Examiner not named",6],["Nayyer Naqvi",6]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q180","sec":12,"q":"Timber deck cargo: GM, 15% water absorption, lashing, jettisoning.","tier":"yellow","rep":12,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Load Line) Regs 1998<\/strong> (timber load lines) and the Carriage of Cargoes Regs 1999. Internationally, the <strong>IS Code 2008<\/strong> and the <strong>Code of Safe Practice for Ships Carrying Timber Deck Cargoes (TDC Code 2011)<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Stability criteria for ships with timber deck cargoes (IS Code 2008)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>area under the GZ curve<\/strong> up to <strong>40°<\/strong> at least <strong>0.08 m·rad<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum GZ<\/strong> at least <strong>0.25 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>GM<\/strong> at least <strong>0.10 m<\/strong> at all times during the voyage, after allowing for all weight changes.<\/li><li>The timber deck cargo can be counted as adding buoyancy for the GZ curve, if it&#x27;s properly stowed and secured.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Allow for weight gain<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Water absorption<\/strong> by the timber, especially in rain and spray (the IS Code allowance is around 10–15% of the deck cargo weight depending on the timber; verify the figure).<\/li><li><strong>Icing<\/strong>, in cold climates.<\/li><li><strong>Consumption<\/strong> of <strong>fuel<\/strong> and <strong>water<\/strong> during the voyage, which raises G if taken from low tanks.<\/li><li>So the <strong>GM gets worse<\/strong> as the voyage goes on. The departure GM must allow for the worst condition.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Stowage and lashing<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Stowed <strong>compactly<\/strong>, with <strong>no gaps<\/strong>, and the overall height and width within the limits.<\/li><li><strong>Height<\/strong> limited by <strong>visibility<\/strong> from the bridge (SOLAS V/22) and the <strong>load line<\/strong> rules (normally at least the height of the superstructure, and not more than a set limit).<\/li><li><strong>Independent lashings<\/strong> over the cargo, of <strong>chain<\/strong> or <strong>wire<\/strong>, at the spacing the TDC Code sets.<\/li><li><strong>Hog lashings<\/strong> and <strong>wiggle wires<\/strong> where required, for packaged timber.<\/li><li><strong>Tightening devices<\/strong> (turnbuckles) and <strong>slip hooks<\/strong>, so lashings can be <strong>tightened<\/strong> at sea and <strong>released quickly<\/strong> for jettisoning.<\/li><li><strong>Walkways<\/strong> and <strong>lifelines<\/strong> for crew access over or alongside the cargo.<\/li><li>Uprights where needed.<\/li><li>Freeing ports and access to the sounding pipes and air pipes kept clear.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>At sea<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Check<\/strong> and <strong>retighten<\/strong> the <strong>lashings<\/strong> regularly, especially <strong>early<\/strong> in the voyage as the cargo <strong>settles<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Avoid heavy rolling<\/strong>: course and speed changes.<\/li><li>Watch the list: a list could mean the cargo is absorbing water unevenly or shifting.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Jettisoning<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Only to save the ship<\/strong>, on the <strong>Master&#x27;s decision<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Release<\/strong> from a <strong>safe position<\/strong>, never with people on the cargo.<\/li><li><strong>Log<\/strong> it, <strong>report<\/strong> it as a <strong>danger to navigation<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS V/31<\/strong>), and lodge a <strong>note of protest<\/strong>. It may be a General Average sacrifice.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The IS Code timber criteria (0.08 m·rad to 40°, max GZ 0.25 m, GM 0.10 m at all times), allowances for water absorption, icing and consumption, compact stowage within visibility, independent lashings with tighteners and slip hooks, walkways and lifelines, retightening at sea, and jettisoning only to save the ship with a V/31 report.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does a timber ship's GM decrease during the voyage?","pa":"<p>Because:<\/p><ul><li>the deck timber <strong>absorbs water<\/strong>, adding weight high up;<\/li><li><strong>ice<\/strong> may build up;<\/li><li>fuel and water are used from <strong>low tanks<\/strong>;<\/li><li>slack tanks add <strong>free surface<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["What would you expect with Timber deck cargo?","Now the Timber cargo has shifted to STBD what are your actions?","How will you load timber cargo?","Why can Timber have a lower initial GM?","Precautions for loading timber cargo. Consult Code of safe practice for timber deck cargo but general things like friction enhancement, air pipes + vents kept free etc."],"ex":[["David Carlisle",3],["Khairul Anam",2],["Danny Hawkins",2],["Examiner not named",2],["Timothy Cummins",2]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q181","sec":12,"q":"Heavy lift: precautions and stability.","tier":"yellow","rep":14,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, <strong>LOLER 2006<\/strong> (MGN 332) and the <strong>COSWP<\/strong> chapter on lifting operations. The CSM for securing.<\/p><p><strong>Precautions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the <strong>crane or derrick SWL<\/strong>, its <strong>thorough examination<\/strong> certificate, and the <strong>slings<\/strong>, <strong>shackles<\/strong> and <strong>spreader<\/strong> certificates, all in the register of lifting appliances.<\/li><li><strong>Weight<\/strong> and <strong>centre of gravity<\/strong> of the lift <strong>confirmed<\/strong> from the shipper, and the lifting points.<\/li><li>A <strong>lifting plan<\/strong> and <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>, <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong>, and <strong>one signaller<\/strong> with radios. Everyone knows the signals.<\/li><li><strong>Tag lines<\/strong>, the <strong>area cleared<\/strong>, <strong>nobody under the load<\/strong>, and <strong>moorings tended<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>gangway<\/strong> clear of the quay, or lifted, because the ship will list.<\/li><li><strong>Slack tanks<\/strong> minimised (<strong>free surface<\/strong>). <strong>Ballast ready<\/strong> to correct the list.<\/li><li><strong>Lift slowly<\/strong>: take the weight, stop and check, then continue. <strong>Land it gently<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Weather calm, no swell, and good visibility.<\/li><li>Check the hatch cover or deck where it will land can take the load, with dunnage to spread it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Stability<\/strong><\/p><p>The moment the weight <strong>leaves the quay<\/strong>, its <strong>centre of gravity<\/strong> acts at the <strong>crane jib head<\/strong> (or derrick head), whatever height it&#x27;s actually at.<\/p><ol><li><strong>Rise in KG<\/strong> = <strong>w × h ÷ Δ<\/strong>, where <strong>h<\/strong> is the vertical distance from the load&#x27;s original position (or the ship&#x27;s G when loading from the quay) to the <strong>derrick head<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>List<\/strong> at <strong>maximum outreach<\/strong>: <strong>tan θ = (w × d) ÷ (Δ × GM fluid)<\/strong>, where <strong>d<\/strong> is the <strong>outreach<\/strong> from the centreline.<\/li><li><strong>Calculate<\/strong> the <strong>maximum list<\/strong> in advance. Make sure it&#x27;s within the <strong>crane&#x27;s operating limit<\/strong> (often about <strong>5°<\/strong>) and the <strong>deck edge<\/strong> isn&#x27;t immersed.<\/li><li>Make sure <strong>GM stays positive<\/strong> with a margin in the worst moment, with the load at the crane head and the ship&#x27;s own GM reduced.<\/li><li><strong>Fill<\/strong> or <strong>press<\/strong> double-bottom <strong>ballast<\/strong> to lower G if needed. <strong>Prepare counter-ballast<\/strong>, but don&#x27;t chase the list during the lift.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Example<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Δ = 20,000 t, GM = 1.0 m, lifting 200 t from the quay at 15 m outreach, with the jib head 25 m above the ship&#x27;s G.<\/li><li>Rise in G = 200 × 25 ÷ 20,200 ≈ 0.25 m, so GM ≈ 0.75 m.<\/li><li>tan θ = 200 × 15 ÷ (20,200 × 0.75) ≈ 0.198, so θ ≈ 11°. Too much: ballast to counter the list or increase GM before the lift.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After the lift<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Secure the cargo to the CSM, with lashings calculated for the expected accelerations.<\/li><li>Recalculate the stability with the cargo in its final position.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The certificates and LOLER, the lift plan and RA, one signaller, tag lines, area clear, gangway, free surface and ballast ready, a slow lift; and the stability: G at the jib head, the rise in KG, the list formula with outreach, the maximum list within the crane limit, GM positive, and an example.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is GM worst at the moment the lift leaves the quay?","pa":"<p>Once the load is lifted, its weight acts <strong>at the crane head<\/strong>, high up and well out from the centreline. That&#x27;s when the virtual rise of G and the <strong>list are greatest<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["Heavy lift to load, how would you do it?","The crew members are preparing to pick the heavy lift using 3 slings of blue tag & 1 with red tag, actions?","Where in the world I am, my experience and sailing areas. I've been Offshore construction and heavy lift since I was a cadet. He was keen to know I'd left the North Sea and done some ocean passages.","You must load one heavy lift, what will be your concerns?","Cease heavy lift cargo ops / land weight"],"ex":[["Timothy Cummins",3],["Shahid Akhter",3],["Chris Roe",3],["Piers Chubb",3],["Sam Twitchin",2]],"yrs":"2022–2024"},{"id":"Q182","sec":12,"q":"High-density cargo; how much cargo can you load?","tier":"yellow","rep":12,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>High-density cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Cargo such as <strong>ore<\/strong> or <strong>steel<\/strong> has a <strong>low stowage factor<\/strong>, so the holds are only <strong>partly filled<\/strong> by volume even when the ship is at her marks.<\/li><li>The weight is concentrated low and in a small area.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Limits<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Tank-top strength<\/strong> (t/m²) and the <strong>maximum mass per hold<\/strong>, from the <strong>loading manual<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Alternate-hold<\/strong> or <strong>block loading<\/strong> conditions, which the ship must be approved for, and the <strong>shear force<\/strong> and <strong>bending<\/strong> limits.<\/li><li><strong>Stiff GM<\/strong>: heavy cargo low down gives a large GM, a short, <strong>violent roll<\/strong>, and <strong>stress<\/strong> on the <strong>structure<\/strong> and any lashings.<\/li><li>On <strong>bulk carriers<\/strong>, <strong>SOLAS XII<\/strong> limits for <strong>heavy cargo<\/strong> (density <strong>≥1,780 kg/m³<\/strong>): for example, restrictions on loading in the foremost hold for some older ships, and damage stability requirements.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo must be trimmed<\/strong> to spread the load and avoid <strong>cone-shaped piles<\/strong> that overload the tank top locally.<\/li><li>Loading rates must allow the deballasting to keep up.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How much can I load?<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Cargo = displacement at the allowable draught − lightship − (bunkers + FW + ballast + stores + constant)<\/strong><\/p><p>Or, from the deadweight scale: <strong>cargo = deadweight at the allowable draught − (bunkers + FW + ballast + stores + constant)<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The allowable draught is the least of<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>load line<\/strong> for the <strong>zone<\/strong>, adjusted for <strong>dock water density<\/strong> (<strong>DWA<\/strong>) and for <strong>consumption<\/strong> before entering a <strong>more restrictive<\/strong> zone.<\/li><li>The <strong>port<\/strong>, <strong>channel<\/strong> or <strong>berth<\/strong> draught (with the UKC policy).<\/li><li>The <strong>air draught<\/strong> limit, for the loaders or bridges.<\/li><li><strong>Stress<\/strong> or <strong>hold limits<\/strong> from the <strong>loadicator<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Then check<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The cargo against the <strong>tank-top limits<\/strong> per hold and the <strong>total<\/strong> for each hold.<\/li><li>The <strong>stability<\/strong> criteria at every stage, and the departure and arrival conditions.<\/li><li>The <strong>shear forces<\/strong> and <strong>bending moments<\/strong> during the loading sequence, not just at the end.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>smallest<\/strong> of these limits sets the cargo.<\/li><li><strong>Tell the charterer<\/strong> before loading, and put the figure in the <strong>notice of readiness<\/strong> or the <strong>cargo intention<\/strong>: &quot;the ship can load X tonnes, more or less 10% at the Master&#x27;s option&quot; as the charter allows.<\/li><li>If the charterer wants more, the limits still decide.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Example of the trap<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A ship can be well below her marks with ore but already at the tank-top limit, or the hold mass limit, or the bending moment limit. The examiner wants to hear that weight limits other than the load line often govern.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>High density means low stowage factor and partly filled holds; the limits (tank top, hold mass, alternate-hold conditions, SF and BM, stiff GM, SOLAS XII for heavy cargo, trimming); the cargo formula; the allowable draught as the least of load line with DWA and consumption, port depth, air draught and stress limits; and telling the charterer.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Calculate the DWA for a ship with FWA 200 mm in RD 1.010.","pa":"<p>DWA = 200 × (1025 − 1010) ÷ 25 = <strong>120 mm<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["What is High density Cargo?","What would a high density cargo do to a ship concerning stability?","Where can you find more information about whether the vessel can load high-density cargo?","You mentioned it's high-density cargo; how will this affect your 3000gt ship?","How you will plan to load a high density cargo in a old ship and define high density cargo?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",5],["James Gilbert",2],["Ross Cochrane",1],["Khademul Insan",1],["Tarik Yassin",1]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q183","sec":12,"q":"BLU Code; water ingress alarms (SOLAS XII/12); \"will your ship survive flooding of one hold?\" (XII/4).","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The BLU Code<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Code of Practice for the Safe Loading and Unloading of Bulk Carriers<\/strong>, IMO Resolution A.862(20).<\/li><li>Referred to in <strong>SOLAS VI/7<\/strong>, and supported by the <strong>BLU Manual<\/strong> for terminals.<\/li><li><strong>Ship and terminal<\/strong> exchange information <strong>before arrival<\/strong>: the ship&#x27;s particulars, the loading or unloading plan, the ballast plan, and the terminal&#x27;s equipment and rates.<\/li><li>They <strong>agree<\/strong> a <strong>loading/unloading plan<\/strong>, with the pour sequence, the quantities and the ballast stages, in writing.<\/li><li>They <strong>sign<\/strong> the <strong>ship/shore safety checklist<\/strong>, and keep <strong>communication<\/strong> throughout.<\/li><li>The <strong>ship can stop operations<\/strong> if the limits are exceeded, and the terminal must stop when asked.<\/li><li>The plan is kept on board and at the terminal, and any change is agreed in writing.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS XII/12: water ingress alarms on bulk carriers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Detectors<\/strong> in <strong>each cargo hold<\/strong> and in <strong>spaces forward<\/strong> of the cargo area (ballast tanks forward of the collision bulkhead and dry spaces), with <strong>alarms on the bridge<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Two levels<\/strong> in the holds:<\/li><li>a <strong>pre-alarm<\/strong> about <strong>0.5 m<\/strong> above the <strong>inner bottom<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>main alarm<\/strong> at not less than <strong>15%<\/strong> of the <strong>hold depth<\/strong>, but <strong>not more than 2 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS XII/13<\/strong>: pumping arrangements for the forward spaces.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS XII/4: damage stability<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Bulk carriers of <strong>≥150 m<\/strong> with <strong>single side skin<\/strong> carrying solid bulk cargo of density <strong>≥1,000 kg/m³<\/strong> must <strong>withstand flooding<\/strong> of <strong>any one cargo hold<\/strong> in all loading conditions, and remain <strong>afloat<\/strong> in a satisfactory condition of <strong>equilibrium<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That applies to ships built on or after <strong>1 July 1999<\/strong>; older ships had to meet it for the <strong>foremost hold<\/strong> under SOLAS XII/3 and the related rules.<\/li><li><strong>XII/5 and XII/6<\/strong> add structural strength requirements for heavy cargoes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>&quot;Will your ship survive flooding of one hold?&quot;<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On a <strong>bulker<\/strong>: check the ship&#x27;s <strong>SOLAS XII<\/strong> certification and the <strong>stability booklet<\/strong>, including any <strong>restricted conditions<\/strong>, such as a reduced cargo in the foremost hold, or &quot;hold no. X empty&quot; notations.<\/li><li>The answer depends on the loading condition; the booklet shows which conditions comply.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On my container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS XII doesn&#x27;t apply<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ship meets the <strong>SOLAS II-1 subdivision<\/strong> (<strong>probabilistic<\/strong>) standard, so survival depends on the <strong>attained subdivision index<\/strong> being at least the required index, not a guaranteed one-hold standard.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d refer to the <strong>damage stability information<\/strong> and the damage control booklet, and the class emergency response service.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The BLU Code under SOLAS VI/7 with the information exchange, agreed plan, checklist and the ship&#x27;s right to stop; SOLAS XII/12 alarm levels (0.5 m and 15% or 2 m); SOLAS XII/4 one-hold flooding for bulkers of 150 m and over, single skin, cargo of 1,000 kg/m³ or more; and that a container ship uses the probabilistic SOLAS II-1 standard instead.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Which bulk carriers must be fitted with a loading instrument?","pa":"<p>Bulk carriers of <strong>150 m in length and over<\/strong> (SOLAS XII/11). The instrument must give <strong>shear forces and bending moments<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q184","sec":12,"q":"Loading 13 h, deballasting 18 h — actions.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>BLU Code<\/strong> and <strong>SOLAS VI/7<\/strong>. The loading plan must keep <strong>stress<\/strong> and <strong>stability<\/strong> within limits <strong>at every stage<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The problem<\/strong><\/p><p>The terminal will <strong>finish loading 5 hours<\/strong> before I <strong>finish deballasting<\/strong>. That risks:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Exceeding SF/BM limits<\/strong> at the intermediate stages, because the cargo is in before the ballast is out.<\/li><li>Going <strong>over the draught<\/strong> or <strong>air draught<\/strong> limits at the berth or under the loaders.<\/li><li><strong>Poor trim<\/strong>, or completing loading with <strong>ballast still in<\/strong>, so the ship can&#x27;t load her full cargo or is over her marks.<\/li><li>Being forced into a rush at the end, with mistakes.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Before loading<\/strong>, tell the <strong>terminal<\/strong> and the <strong>Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Amend the loading plan<\/strong> with the terminal, in writing:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>reduce the loading rate<\/strong>;<\/li><li>build <strong>planned stops<\/strong> and <strong>pauses<\/strong> into the sequence, timed to the ballast;<\/li><li><strong>change the pour sequence<\/strong>, so the cargo goes where the ballast has already been removed.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Increase the deballasting rate<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>use <strong>all pumps<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>gravitate<\/strong> where possible, using the tide and the draught;<\/li><li><strong>strip early<\/strong>, with the eductors;<\/li><li>the <strong>engine room<\/strong> on <strong>standby<\/strong>, and the BWMS capacity checked, because treatment systems can limit the rate.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Run every stage<\/strong> on the <strong>loadicator<\/strong> and keep the <strong>printouts<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>During loading<\/strong>, compare <strong>actual vs planned<\/strong> draughts and quantities at each pour.<\/li><li>The <strong>C/O can stop loading<\/strong> if the ship <strong>falls behind<\/strong> the plan.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If the terminal refuses to adjust<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>ship&#x27;s safety overrides<\/strong>. The <strong>Master stops<\/strong> the operation.<\/li><li>I issue a <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> on any delay.<\/li><li>The BLU Code gives the ship this right, and the terminal representative should have agreed the plan with the ship&#x27;s capacity in mind.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the <strong>agreed plan<\/strong>, any <strong>departures<\/strong> from it, and the times.<\/li><li>The <strong>Ballast Water Record Book<\/strong> entries for each operation.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At the planning stage, before arrival, compare the terminal&#x27;s loading rate with the ship&#x27;s deballasting capacity, and agree a realistic plan then.<\/li><li>Many bulk carrier structural failures in port came from loading faster than the ballast could be discharged.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The risks (SF/BM, draught, trim, ballast left in), agreeing a revised plan with the terminal before loading (slower rate, pauses, changed sequence), maximising deballasting, every stage on the loadicator, monitoring actual against plan, the right to stop loading, a letter of protest if the terminal refuses, and records.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What signals do you agree with the terminal for an emergency stop?","pa":"<p>Agree it in the <strong>ship/shore safety checklist<\/strong> before loading (BLU Code). It covers:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>emergency stop signal<\/strong>, both radio phrase and audible signal;<\/li><li>the communication channels and a back-up;<\/li><li>who has authority to stop.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q185","sec":12,"q":"Is a loadicator mandatory? What if it fails? How do you know it's working?","tier":"orange","rep":38,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Is it mandatory?<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Class rules<\/strong> require a <strong>loading instrument<\/strong> on most large ships, including <strong>container ships<\/strong> above class size thresholds.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS XII/11<\/strong> requires <strong>bulk carriers of 150 m and over<\/strong> to have a <strong>loading instrument<\/strong> capable of giving <strong>shear forces and bending moments<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Tankers<\/strong> need a <strong>stability instrument<\/strong>, able to verify intact and damage stability (MARPOL I and the IBC/IGC Codes, as amended; verify the dates: new ships from 2016, existing by about 2021).<\/li><li>The <strong>software is approved by class<\/strong>, and the approval certificate is kept on board.<\/li><li>The loading manual and the stability booklet are always the approved basis.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How do I know it&#x27;s working?<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Test conditions<\/strong> in the <strong>approved loading manual<\/strong>: enter them and <strong>compare<\/strong> the results with the <strong>approved values<\/strong>, within the stated <strong>tolerance<\/strong>.<\/li><li>This is done at the <strong>annual class survey<\/strong>, and by the <strong>crew regularly<\/strong> per the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, for example <strong>monthly<\/strong>, and after any <strong>software change<\/strong> or hardware repair.<\/li><li>The <strong>lightship<\/strong> and <strong>constants<\/strong> are correct, as in the approved booklet. A wrong constant gives wrong results.<\/li><li>The <strong>actual draughts<\/strong> match the <strong>calculated draughts<\/strong> after loading: if they don&#x27;t, either the input or the program is wrong.<\/li><li>The tank sounding inputs match the actual soundings.<\/li><li>The software version is the approved one.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If it fails<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Use the <strong>approved stability booklet<\/strong> and <strong>loading manual<\/strong> to <strong>calculate manually<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>hydrostatics<\/strong> for draught, KM, TPC and MCTC;<\/li><li>the <strong>KN tables<\/strong> for the GZ curve;<\/li><li>the <strong>SF/BM tables<\/strong> or the manual method in the loading manual.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Keep <strong>conservative margins<\/strong> and <strong>slow down<\/strong> cargo operations if needed, so I can check each stage.<\/li><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong>, the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong>, and arrange <strong>repair<\/strong> or <strong>reinstallation<\/strong> of approved software. A back-up copy on a second computer is common.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the log.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t load<\/strong> a <strong>complex condition<\/strong> without a <strong>verified calculation<\/strong>.<\/li><li>For a container ship, the lashing software may also be affected, so check the CSM manual method.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Class rules, SOLAS XII/11 for bulkers of 150 m and over, the tanker stability instrument; checking it with the approved test conditions within tolerance, at the annual survey and regularly by the crew, lightship and constant, and draught comparison; and if it fails, manual calculation from the approved booklet, margins, informing class, and not loading complex conditions unverified.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What tolerance is acceptable on the test conditions?","pa":"<p>The results must agree with the approved <strong>test conditions<\/strong> within the tolerance stated in the class approval or instrument manual, usually a small percentage. Check the figure for your instrument.<\/p>","asked":["How will you test the loadicator?","How will you verify that your loadicator is working fine?","Tell me about a loadicator, how would you test it?","Loadicator?","You mentioned the loading computer during your handover. How will you check that the loading computer is giving correct readings?"],"ex":[["Zahid Raza",5],["Shiva Balasubramanian",5],["Siddhartha Paul",4],["Zia Ul Haq",4],["Byron Griffiths",3]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q186","sec":12,"q":"Mate's receipt vs bill of lading; types of B/L; letter of protest; cargo-figure disputes; shipper wants a clean B/L for damaged steel; three people with a B/L at discharge.","tier":"orange","rep":25,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>Carriage of Goods by Sea Act 1971<\/strong> (the <strong>Hague-Visby Rules<\/strong>) and <strong>COGSA 1992<\/strong> (rights of suit).<\/p><p><strong>Mate&#x27;s receipt<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Signed by the C/O<\/strong> as a <strong>receipt<\/strong> for the cargo loaded, with <strong>remarks<\/strong> on its <strong>condition<\/strong> and quantity.<\/li><li>The <strong>shipper exchanges<\/strong> it for the <strong>bill of lading<\/strong>, and the remarks on the mate&#x27;s receipt should be carried onto the B/L.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Bill of lading: three functions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>receipt<\/strong> for the goods, describing their condition and quantity.<\/li><li><strong>Evidence<\/strong> of the <strong>contract of carriage<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>document of title<\/strong>: whoever holds the original can claim the goods, and it can be sold or transferred by endorsement.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Types<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Shipped<\/strong> (on board) or <strong>received for shipment<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Clean<\/strong> or <strong>claused<\/strong> (foul), with remarks about the condition.<\/li><li><strong>Order<\/strong> (transferable by endorsement), <strong>straight<\/strong> (to a named consignee), or <strong>bearer<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Through<\/strong> or <strong>combined transport<\/strong>, <strong>house<\/strong> B/L (from a freight forwarder), <strong>charter-party<\/strong> B/L.<\/li><li><strong>Sea waybill<\/strong>: not a document of title, delivery to the named consignee without presenting an original.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Letter of protest<\/strong><\/p><p>To the <strong>terminal<\/strong>, <strong>stevedore<\/strong> or <strong>shipper<\/strong> over <strong>damage<\/strong>, <strong>delay<\/strong> or <strong>disputed figures<\/strong>, as a <strong>written record<\/strong> that the ship doesn&#x27;t accept responsibility.<\/p><p><strong>Cargo-figure disputes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Draught survey<\/strong> vs the <strong>shore figure<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Clause the B/L: &quot;<strong>weight/quantity as declared by shipper, unknown to carrier<\/strong>&quot;, or with the ship&#x27;s figure.<\/li><li><strong>Hague-Visby Article III r.3<\/strong>: the carrier can refuse to state particulars it has <strong>reasonable grounds to suspect<\/strong> are inaccurate, or has no reasonable means of checking.<\/li><li>Issue a letter of protest, and inform the owner and P&amp;I.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Clean B/L for damaged steel<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Refuse<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Clause<\/strong> the mate&#x27;s receipt and B/L <strong>accurately<\/strong> (for example &quot;<strong>rust stained<\/strong>&quot;, &quot;wet before shipment&quot;).<\/li><li>A clean B/L issued against a <strong>letter of indemnity (LOI)<\/strong> is <strong>misrepresentation<\/strong> to the buyer and the bank, the LOI may be unenforceable, and the <strong>P&amp;I cover is lost<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Contact<\/strong> the <strong>owner<\/strong> and the <strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>, and call a P&amp;I surveyor to record the condition.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Three people with a B/L at discharge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>B/Ls are usually issued in a set of three originals. <strong>Deliver against one original<\/strong>: the rest become <strong>void<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If there are <strong>competing claims<\/strong> from different holders, <strong>don&#x27;t deliver<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Contact<\/strong> the <strong>owner<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>, who may go to <strong>court<\/strong> for directions.<\/li><li>Never deliver without an original B/L (except against the owner&#x27;s instructions with an LOI, which the P&amp;I club warns about).<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The mate&#x27;s receipt and the B/L&#x27;s three functions, the types including the sea waybill, the letter of protest, clausing for disputed figures with Art III r.3, refusing a clean B/L for damaged steel because of the LOI and P&amp;I cover, and delivering against one original but stopping if there are competing claims.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why won't the P&I club cover a claim arising from a letter of indemnity?","pa":"<p>P&amp;I rules <strong>exclude<\/strong> liabilities from knowingly issuing a <strong>false or clean B/L<\/strong> for damaged or short cargo, or a misdated one. An LOI given to cover a misrepresentation is often unenforceable, so the owner is left uninsured.<\/p>","asked":["What is a bill of lading ?","What is the relationship between Bill of Lading and Mate's receipt?","What is a letter of protest?","What is a Mates Receipt?","Agent wants you to sign a clean bill of lading for damaged cargo, what will you do?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",10],["Nayyer Naqvi",3],["Fazal Wadood",3],["Shahid Akhter",3],["Doug Smith",2]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q187","sec":12,"q":"Charter-party clauses that affect the C/O.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Typical forms<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Time charters<\/strong>: <strong>NYPE<\/strong>, <strong>Boxtime<\/strong> (for container ships) or other <strong>BIMCO<\/strong> forms.<\/li><li><strong>Voyage charters<\/strong>: <strong>GENCON<\/strong> and others.<\/li><li>The <strong>charter party<\/strong> sets the commercial terms. The ship normally gets an extract or a copy of the relevant clauses from the company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Clauses that affect my work directly<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Laytime<\/strong>, <strong>NOR<\/strong> (Notice of Readiness), <strong>demurrage<\/strong> and <strong>despatch<\/strong> (voyage charters). Accurate times in the <strong>Statement of Facts<\/strong>, and the <strong>hold readiness<\/strong> when the NOR is tendered.<\/li><li><strong>Hold cleaning<\/strong> and <strong>cargo readiness<\/strong>. <strong>Failing a hold inspection<\/strong> can mean <strong>off-hire<\/strong> or the NOR being rejected.<\/li><li><strong>Stevedore damage<\/strong> clause. <strong>Notify<\/strong> the <strong>charterer<\/strong> and the <strong>stevedore<\/strong> in <strong>writing<\/strong>, usually within a <strong>set time<\/strong> (often <strong>24 hours<\/strong>; check the CP), or the <strong>claim is lost<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Employment and indemnity<\/strong>. The <strong>Master follows<\/strong> the <strong>charterer&#x27;s orders<\/strong> on the <strong>employment<\/strong> of the ship, but <strong>not unsafe<\/strong> ones, and the owner keeps responsibility for navigation and safety.<\/li><li><strong>Bills of lading<\/strong> clause: who <strong>signs<\/strong>, and <strong>clausing<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Speed and consumption<\/strong> / <strong>performance<\/strong>. Accurate <strong>noon reports<\/strong> and <strong>weather logs<\/strong>, which are <strong>used in performance claims<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Bunker<\/strong> clause: quantity on <strong>delivery<\/strong> and <strong>redelivery<\/strong>, <strong>surveys<\/strong> and <strong>BDNs<\/strong> (bunker delivery notes), and fuel quality.<\/li><li><strong>Safe port<\/strong> and <strong>safe berth<\/strong>, <strong>ice<\/strong> and <strong>war risks<\/strong>: the Master can refuse an unsafe port.<\/li><li><strong>Off-hire<\/strong> clause: <strong>breakdowns<\/strong> and <strong>deficiencies<\/strong>, such as a detention or a crew problem.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo exclusions<\/strong>, <strong>DG<\/strong> and <strong>deck cargo<\/strong> (at shipper&#x27;s risk).<\/li><li><strong>Reefer<\/strong> clause and <strong>lashing materials<\/strong>: who provides them, who&#x27;s responsible for temperatures.<\/li><li><strong>General Average<\/strong> clause (<strong>York-Antwerp Rules 2016<\/strong>), the <strong>Clause Paramount<\/strong> (<strong>Hague-Visby<\/strong>), and the <strong>dry-dock<\/strong> clause.<\/li><li><strong>On-hire<\/strong> and <strong>off-hire surveys<\/strong>. Record the <strong>condition<\/strong>, with <strong>photos<\/strong>, and the bunkers on board.<\/li><li>ECA, emissions and CII clauses, increasingly important.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Practical point<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I read the <strong>CP extract<\/strong> given to the ship by the company.<\/li><li>If a charterer&#x27;s instruction seems to conflict with safety or the CP, the Master asks the company.<\/li><li>Good records are what win or lose claims: the deck log, the Statement of Facts, the noon reports, the damage reports, and the letters of protest.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The common forms, and the clauses that affect the C/O: NOR and laytime, hold cleanliness and off-hire, stevedore damage notification within the time limit, employment and indemnity with no unsafe orders, B/L clausing, performance and noon reports, bunkers, safe port, off-hire, DG and deck cargo, reefers and lashings, GA and the Clause Paramount, on- and off-hire surveys, and the importance of records.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the risk if you sign a Statement of Facts without remarks?","pa":"<p>You <strong>accept the times and events<\/strong> as written, and lose the chance to dispute delays, weather or laytime and demurrage. If you disagree, sign with <strong>remarks<\/strong>, or &quot;for receipt only&quot;.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q188","sec":12,"q":"Tanker: inert gas.","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-2/4.5.5<\/strong>, which sets which tankers need an inert gas system (now tankers of <strong>8,000 dwt<\/strong> and above for new ships, verify), and the <strong>FSS Code Chapter 15<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Purpose<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep the <strong>cargo tank atmosphere non-flammable<\/strong> by holding <strong>oxygen below<\/strong> the level needed for <strong>combustion<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Keep a <strong>positive pressure<\/strong>, so <strong>air can&#x27;t get in<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So a spark from static, friction or a lightning strike can&#x27;t ignite the tank.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Limits<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>IG supply<\/strong>: <strong>O₂ ≤5%<\/strong> by volume.<\/li><li><strong>Tank atmosphere<\/strong>: <strong>O₂ ≤8%<\/strong>, at <strong>positive pressure<\/strong> at <strong>all times<\/strong>, except when the tank is gas-free for entry.<\/li><li>My Hand Out said &quot;8%&quot; for the supply: that&#x27;s the tank limit; the <strong>supply<\/strong> must be <strong>≤5%<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Sources<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Flue gas<\/strong> from the <strong>boilers<\/strong>, scrubbed and cooled.<\/li><li>An <strong>IG generator<\/strong> burning fuel.<\/li><li>A <strong>nitrogen generator<\/strong>, mainly on chemical and gas tankers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safety devices<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>scrubber<\/strong>: cools the gas and removes soot and sulphur.<\/li><li><strong>Blowers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>deck water seal<\/strong> and a <strong>non-return valve<\/strong>, so <strong>cargo vapour can&#x27;t flow back<\/strong> to the machinery spaces.<\/li><li><strong>P/V valves<\/strong> on each tank and a <strong>P/V breaker<\/strong> on the main line, protecting against over- and under-pressure.<\/li><li>An <strong>O₂ analyser<\/strong> and <strong>pressure alarms<\/strong>, with alarms for high O₂, low pressure, high temperature, scrubber water and deck seal level.<\/li><li>Isolating valves for each tank.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Operations<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inerting<\/strong> before loading: replacing air in the tanks with IG until O₂ is below 8%.<\/li><li><strong>Topping up<\/strong> at sea to keep the pressure positive.<\/li><li><strong>During discharge<\/strong>, IG <strong>replaces the cargo<\/strong> as it&#x27;s pumped out, so air is never drawn in.<\/li><li><strong>Purging<\/strong> with IG to get <strong>hydrocarbons below 2%<\/strong> by volume before <strong>gas-freeing<\/strong> with <strong>air<\/strong>. This keeps the tank from passing through the <strong>flammable range<\/strong> on the flammability diagram.<\/li><li><strong>Crude oil washing<\/strong> is only done with the tank inerted.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If the IG plant fails<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stop discharging<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use an <strong>external IG supply<\/strong> if one is available.<\/li><li>Close the tanks, maintain positive pressure, and don&#x27;t allow air in.<\/li><li>Inform the Master, the terminal and the company, and record it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Hazards for people<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>IG is an asphyxiant: tanks under IG are enclosed spaces with no oxygen. Leaks on deck can affect people.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-2/4.5.5 and FSS Ch 15, the purpose, supply O₂ ≤5% and tanks ≤8% at positive pressure, the sources, the safety devices with the deck water seal and P/V breaker, the operations including purging below 2% hydrocarbon before gas-freeing, and stopping discharge if it fails.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why purge to below 2% hydrocarbons before gas-freeing?","pa":"<p>So that when air is let in, the mixture <strong>never passes through the flammable range<\/strong>. Below about 2% hydrocarbons, the atmosphere stays under the <strong>critical dilution line<\/strong> on the flammability diagram as the oxygen rises.<\/p>","asked":["How would you know what cargo to load in a Gas Tanker?"],"ex":[["Ross Cochrane",1],["Khairul Anam",1],["Zia Ul Haq",1]],"yrs":"2024–2025"},{"id":"Q189","sec":13,"q":"Intact stability criteria; grain and timber criteria.","tier":"red","rep":51,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Ships built on or after <strong>1 July 2010<\/strong> follow the <strong>2008 IS Code<\/strong> (<strong>MSC.267(85)<\/strong>), mandatory through <strong>SOLAS II-1<\/strong> and the Load Line Protocol. In the UK it&#x27;s applied through the <strong>MS (Load Line) Regs 1998<\/strong> as amended, with the 2018 changes explained in <strong>MGN 579<\/strong>. <strong>SOLAS II-1/5-1<\/strong> requires the stability information to be on board.<\/p><p><strong>General intact criteria (IS Code Part A 2.2)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Area<\/strong> under the <strong>GZ curve<\/strong> at least <strong>0.055 m·rad<\/strong> up to <strong>30°<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At least <strong>0.09 m·rad<\/strong> up to <strong>40°<\/strong>, or up to the <strong>downflooding angle θf<\/strong> if that&#x27;s less.<\/li><li>At least <strong>0.03 m·rad<\/strong> <strong>between 30° and 40°<\/strong> (or θf).<\/li><li><strong>GZ<\/strong> at least <strong>0.20 m<\/strong> at an angle of <strong>30° or more<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum GZ<\/strong> at an angle of at least <strong>25°<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Initial GM₀<\/strong> at least <strong>0.15 m<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Plus the severe wind and rolling (weather) criterion (IS Code 2.3)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ship must withstand a steady beam wind and a gust while rolling, with the area balance between the righting and heeling levers. Important for container ships, with their large windage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Free surface<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ship must meet <strong>everything<\/strong> with the <strong>free surface correction<\/strong> applied (fluid GM and KG).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Grain (International Grain Code)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Heel<\/strong> from the assumed <strong>grain shift<\/strong> not more than <strong>12°<\/strong>, or the angle of <strong>deck edge immersion<\/strong> if earlier.<\/li><li><strong>Residual area<\/strong> between the heeling arm and GZ curves at least <strong>0.075 m·rad<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>GM<\/strong> (corrected) at least <strong>0.30 m<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Timber deck cargo (IS Code)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Area<\/strong> up to <strong>40°<\/strong> at least <strong>0.08 m·rad<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Maximum GZ<\/strong> at least <strong>0.25 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>GM<\/strong> at least <strong>0.10 m<\/strong> <strong>throughout<\/strong> the voyage, allowing for <strong>water absorption<\/strong>, <strong>icing<\/strong> and <strong>consumption<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Other special criteria<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Container ships and others with special features may have additional criteria in the IS Code Part B, such as for ships with large windage, or the second-generation criteria (parametric roll, pure loss of stability, surf-riding) as guidance.<\/li><li>Damage stability is separate, under SOLAS II-1 Part B-1.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I check on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>loadicator<\/strong> checks all the criteria for the condition.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum KG curve<\/strong> in the stability booklet gives a quick check.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A correction<\/strong><\/p><p>My Hand Out says maximum GZ at <strong>≥30°<\/strong>. The criterion is <strong>≥25°<\/strong>.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The six general criteria exactly (0.055 to 30°, 0.09 to 40°, 0.03 between 30° and 40°, GZ 0.20 m at 30° or more, max GZ at 25° or more, GM 0.15 m), the weather criterion, free surface applied, the grain criteria (12°, 0.075, 0.30 m) and the timber criteria (0.08, 0.25 m, 0.10 m throughout).<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the weather criterion trying to protect against?","pa":"<p>The ship <strong>rolling to windward<\/strong> in a severe beam wind and then being hit by a <strong>gust<\/strong>. The criterion checks she has enough righting energy to survive it (area b ≥ area a).<\/p>","asked":["What is Intact stability criteria?","What are the stability requirements for a vessel?","What is the intact stability criteria for Container vessel?","What is intact stability , under which code , criteria for tanker?","What are the stability issues with Offshore vessels?"],"ex":[["Alec Keep",9],["Timothy Cummins",6],["Doug Smith",4],["Bill Bennett",3],["Khairul Anam",3]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q190","sec":13,"q":"GZ curve; max KG curve; what to check in the stability booklet; GM from roll period.","tier":"orange","rep":23,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","ON"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The GZ curve<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>righting lever<\/strong> (GZ) plotted against the <strong>angle of heel<\/strong>. From it I read:<\/p><ol><li>The <strong>initial slope<\/strong> = <strong>GM<\/strong>: draw the tangent at the origin and erect a perpendicular at <strong>57.3°<\/strong> (1 radian); its height on the tangent is the GM.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum GZ<\/strong> and the <strong>angle<\/strong> it occurs at.<\/li><li>The <strong>deck-edge immersion<\/strong> angle, at the <strong>point of inflection<\/strong>, where the curve stops getting steeper.<\/li><li>The <strong>range<\/strong> of stability and the <strong>angle of vanishing stability<\/strong>, where GZ returns to zero.<\/li><li>The <strong>area<\/strong> under the curve: the <strong>dynamic stability<\/strong>, the work needed to heel the ship.<\/li><li>The <strong>downflooding angle<\/strong>, beyond which water enters through openings, so the curve is cut off there for the criteria.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The maximum KG curve (or minimum GM curve)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For each <strong>displacement<\/strong> (and trim), the <strong>highest KG<\/strong> that still meets <strong>all criteria<\/strong>, intact and damage where applicable.<\/li><li>If the <strong>actual corrected KG<\/strong> (fluid KG, with free surface) is <strong>below<\/strong> the curve, the ship <strong>complies<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s the quickest check of stability on board.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What to check in the stability booklet (SOLAS II-1/5-1)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>It&#x27;s <strong>approved<\/strong> by the flag or RO, and it&#x27;s the current version.<\/li><li>The <strong>lightship<\/strong> data from the <strong>inclining experiment<\/strong> or lightweight survey: displacement, KG, LCG.<\/li><li><strong>Hydrostatics<\/strong>, <strong>KN<\/strong> or cross curves, <strong>tank calibrations<\/strong> (sounding and ullage tables) and <strong>free surface moments<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum KG<\/strong> or <strong>minimum GM<\/strong> curves.<\/li><li><strong>Sample loading conditions<\/strong>, with worked examples.<\/li><li><strong>Downflooding points<\/strong> and their angles.<\/li><li>The <strong>Master&#x27;s instructions<\/strong> and any operational limitations.<\/li><li><strong>Damage stability<\/strong> information.<\/li><li>The <strong>loadicator test conditions<\/strong>, to check the loading computer.<\/li><li>For container ships, the visibility and stack weight information.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>GM from the roll period (IS Code)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>T = 2CB ÷ √GM<\/strong>, so <strong>GM = (2CB ÷ T)²<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>C<\/strong> is about <strong>0.35–0.45<\/strong>, from the IS Code formula using the beam, draught and length.<\/li><li><strong>Example<\/strong>: B = 40 m, C = 0.4, T = 25 s, so 2CB = 32, 32 ÷ 25 = 1.28, GM ≈ <strong>1.6 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li>How to time it: in calm sea, time several full rolls (port to port and back) and take the average.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>only an estimate<\/strong>, but it&#x27;s a useful <strong>check on the loadicator<\/strong>. A big difference means something is wrong.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>What to read from the GZ curve (GM at 57.3°, max GZ, deck edge immersion, range, area, downflooding), the max KG curve and how to use it, what&#x27;s in the approved stability booklet, and the roll period formula with an example, used as a check on the loadicator.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why might the roll-period GM be unreliable in a seaway?","pa":"<p><strong>T = 2CB/√GM<\/strong> depends on the factor <strong>C<\/strong>, which changes with the loading. In a seaway the roll is <strong>forced by the waves<\/strong>, and wind, free surface and cargo movement distort it. It&#x27;s only a rough guide.<\/p>","asked":["What are contents of stability booklet?","What stability info that should be provided to the Master when taking over a new ship, what information is contained in the stability booklet for a bulk carrier.","Showed me a Max KG Curve, and asked me what is was for."],"ex":[["Shiva Balasubramanian",5],["Bill Bennett",4],["Robert Jackson",4],["Zia Ul Haq",3],["Byron Griffiths",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q191","sec":13,"q":"GT/NT — why do they matter to the C/O; taxes on GT or NT?","tier":"red","rep":62,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>International Tonnage Convention 1969<\/strong>. In the UK, the <strong>MS (Tonnage) Regs 1997<\/strong>. The <strong>International Tonnage Certificate (1969)<\/strong> has <strong>no expiry date<\/strong>. It&#x27;s <strong>reissued<\/strong> after changes that alter the tonnage, or a change of flag.<\/p><p><strong>Gross tonnage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A measure of the ship&#x27;s <strong>total enclosed volume<\/strong>, without units.<\/li><li><strong>GT = K₁ × V<\/strong>, where V is the total volume of all enclosed spaces in m³, and <strong>K₁ = 0.2 + 0.02 log₁₀ V<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Net tonnage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>useful earning capacity<\/strong>, from the <strong>cargo spaces<\/strong>, adjusted for the moulded <strong>draught and depth<\/strong>, and the number of <strong>passengers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It <strong>can&#x27;t be less than 30% of GT<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Both are volumes, not weights.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters to the C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Thresholds<\/strong> in SOLAS, MARPOL, STCW, MLC and UK rules are mostly in <strong>GT<\/strong>. Examples:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the <strong>Garbage Management Plan<\/strong> and the <strong>Garbage Record Book<\/strong> at <strong>100 GT<\/strong> (the GRB from 1 May 2024);<\/li><li>the <strong>IOPP<\/strong> certificate, the <strong>SOPEP<\/strong> and the <strong>ORB<\/strong> at <strong>400 GT<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>SEEMP<\/strong> at 400 GT, and the <strong>CII<\/strong> rating and data collection at <strong>5,000 GT<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>ISM<\/strong> and <strong>ISPS<\/strong> at <strong>500 GT<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS<\/strong> carriage by GT and ship type;<\/li><li><strong>CoC limits<\/strong> (for example 3,000 GT and 500 GT certificates);<\/li><li>the MLC certificate at 500 GT;<\/li><li>the Bunkers certificate over 1,000 GT and Wreck Removal at 300 GT.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Manning<\/strong> and <strong>equipment<\/strong> depend on GT.<\/li><li><strong>Port costs<\/strong>: many <strong>port<\/strong>, <strong>pilotage<\/strong> and <strong>canal<\/strong> dues are based on <strong>GT<\/strong> or <strong>NT<\/strong>. <strong>Panama<\/strong> and <strong>Suez<\/strong> use their own <strong>tonnage<\/strong> systems and certificates.<\/li><li><strong>UK light dues<\/strong> are charged on <strong>NT<\/strong> (verify).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Taxes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>UK tonnage tax<\/strong> regime calculates a <strong>notional profit<\/strong> from the ship&#x27;s <strong>NT<\/strong>, instead of taxing actual profits (verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>C/O checks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>tonnage certificate<\/strong> is on board and valid.<\/li><li>The <strong>tonnage marks<\/strong> are correct where required, and nothing has been altered on board (such as enclosing a space) that would change the tonnage without re-measurement.<\/li><li>The Suez and Panama certificates are on board for the canal transits.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A common exam slip<\/strong><\/p><p>Saying GT is a weight. It&#x27;s a volume. Deadweight and displacement are weights.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 1969 Convention and the UK 1997 Regs, the certificate with no expiry, GT as total enclosed volume with the formula, NT as earning capacity not less than 30% of GT, the thresholds that depend on GT with examples, port and canal dues, light dues and tonnage tax on NT, and the C/O&#x27;s checks.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why do owners want a low NT?","pa":"<p>Many charges are based on <strong>NT<\/strong>: port dues, some canal dues, light dues and pilotage. So a <strong>lower NT means lower costs<\/strong>. GT is used for safety and manning rules.<\/p>","asked":["What is GT and uses?","What is Gross Tonnage?","Explain what GT and NT are?","What is GRT, NRT, DWT?","Duty as a Chief Officer ?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",11],["James Steer",9],["Kaiser Ali",9],["Ahsan Khan",8],["Chris Williams",8]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q192","sec":13,"q":"Dry-docking: preparation, plans needed, critical period and instant, P-force, declivity, laden docking, before flooding the dock, refloating.","tier":"red","rep":222,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Preparation<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Send the <strong>docking plan<\/strong> to the yard: the <strong>keel<\/strong> and <strong>side blocks<\/strong>, and the positions to <strong>avoid<\/strong>: <strong>bottom plugs<\/strong>, <strong>sea chests<\/strong>, <strong>echo sounder<\/strong> and <strong>log transducers<\/strong>, <strong>bilge keels<\/strong>, <strong>anodes<\/strong>, and any hull damage.<\/li><li>Send the work list, the tank condition, and the stability condition.<\/li><li><strong>Arrive upright<\/strong>, with a <strong>small stern trim<\/strong> as the <strong>dockmaster<\/strong> wants (often 0.5–1.0 m), so the stern touches first and she settles along the keel.<\/li><li><strong>Minimise free surface<\/strong>: tanks <strong>full or empty<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Enough GM<\/strong> for the <strong>critical instant<\/strong>, with a margin.<\/li><li><strong>Weights secured<\/strong>, cranes stowed, and nothing loose.<\/li><li>Record <strong>soundings<\/strong> of all tanks on arrival, for comparison before refloating.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Critical period<\/strong><\/p><p>From the moment the <strong>stern touches the blocks<\/strong> until the ship <strong>sits on the blocks overall<\/strong> (along the whole keel). During this time, the <strong>upthrust P<\/strong> at the stern grows as the water falls.<\/p><p><strong>Critical instant<\/strong><\/p><p>Just <strong>before<\/strong> she <strong>sits overall<\/strong>. <strong>P<\/strong> is at its <strong>maximum<\/strong>, and the <strong>GM<\/strong> must stay <strong>positive<\/strong> with a margin, because the side shores can&#x27;t be set until she&#x27;s sitting overall.<\/p><p><strong>The P-force<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>P = (change of trim × MCTC) ÷ distance<\/strong> of the <strong>LCF<\/strong> from the after perpendicular (or the aftermost block).<\/li><li><strong>Virtual loss of GM = P × KM ÷ Δ<\/strong> (the &quot;M&quot; method: treat P as reducing the effective KM).<\/li><li>Or, by the <strong>alternative<\/strong> method, <strong>P × KG ÷ (Δ − P)<\/strong> (treat P as raising G).<\/li><li>So the effective GM = GM − loss. Keep it positive.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Declivity<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>slope<\/strong> of the <strong>blocks<\/strong>, which in some docks are set at an angle.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>trim relative to the blocks<\/strong>, not the absolute trim, in the calculation.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Laden docking<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Much <strong>higher P<\/strong> and <strong>block loads<\/strong>, and stresses on the hull because it&#x27;s supported only on the blocks.<\/li><li>Needs <strong>class approval<\/strong> and the <strong>yard&#x27;s agreement<\/strong>, with extra blocks and shores under heavy weights.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before flooding the dock<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Work done<\/strong>, and inspected by the surveyor.<\/li><li><strong>Plugs refitted<\/strong> and the <strong>plug list signed<\/strong>, in the presence of a ship&#x27;s officer.<\/li><li><strong>Sea valves closed<\/strong> or back in their correct state, and the stern tube seals checked.<\/li><li><strong>Soundings<\/strong> compared with arrival, and <strong>weight changes<\/strong> recorded (removed and added: anchors and cable, equipment, ballast changes).<\/li><li><strong>Stability recalculated<\/strong> for the refloating.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Refloating<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>critical period<\/strong> again, in reverse.<\/li><li><strong>Stop flooding<\/strong> at stages to <strong>check for leaks<\/strong>: the sea chests, the <strong>stern tube<\/strong>, the plugs, the new welds.<\/li><li>Watch for a <strong>list<\/strong>: if she lists, stop and find the cause.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The docking plan with the positions to avoid, arriving upright with a small stern trim, free surface minimised and enough GM; the critical period and instant; the P formula and the loss of GM by both methods; declivity; laden docking needing class approval; the checks before flooding including the plug list; and staged refloating watching for leaks and list.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Trim 1.2 m by the stern, MCTC 400 t·m/cm, LCF 90 m from the AP. What is P at the critical instant?","pa":"<p>120 × 400 / 90 ≈ 533 t.<\/p>","asked":["How will you prepare the vessel for dry dock as a chief mate?","What is the Critical Period?","What is P force and how can you reduce it?","How you will prepare your vessel for emergency dry-dock?","Your vessel is entering Dry dock?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",53],["Nayyer Naqvi",47],["Muhammad Iqbal",31],["Colum Leggett",26],["Zia Ul Haq",22]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q193","sec":13,"q":"Anchor and cable maintenance in dry dock.","tier":"yellow","rep":7,"src":"Study books","tags":["P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>class rules<\/strong> (the <strong>special survey<\/strong> normally covers the anchors and cables) and the <strong>PMS<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The procedure<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Range<\/strong> the anchors and cables on the <strong>dock bottom<\/strong>, in shackle lengths. <strong>Wash<\/strong>, <strong>de-scale<\/strong> and <strong>inspect<\/strong> them.<\/li><li><strong>Each link<\/strong> is checked for:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>cracks<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>loose or missing studs<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>wear<\/strong> and <strong>pitting<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>hammer test<\/strong>: a <strong>clear ring<\/strong> is good, a <strong>dull sound<\/strong> suggests a <strong>crack<\/strong> or <strong>loose stud<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Measure<\/strong> <strong>link diameters<\/strong> at the <strong>most worn<\/strong> areas, with callipers, especially near the <strong>gypsy<\/strong> contact points and in the <strong>first shackles<\/strong>, which wear most. <strong>Renew<\/strong> a length when the <strong>mean diameter loss<\/strong> reaches <strong>12%<\/strong> (class).<\/li><li><strong>Joining shackles<\/strong> (Kenter): <strong>dismantle<\/strong>, <strong>clean<\/strong>, check the <strong>pins<\/strong> and <strong>lead plugs<\/strong> (pellets), <strong>grease<\/strong> and <strong>reassemble<\/strong> with new lead plugs, and check the shackle numbers.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>end shackle<\/strong>, the <strong>swivel<\/strong>, the <strong>crown shackle<\/strong>, and the <strong>anchor<\/strong> itself: the <strong>flukes<\/strong>, the <strong>shank<\/strong>, the <strong>pin<\/strong>, and for cracks.<\/li><li><strong>Turn<\/strong> the cable <strong>end for end<\/strong>, or change the positions of the shackle lengths, if required to <strong>spread the wear<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Re-mark<\/strong> the <strong>shackles<\/strong> with <strong>paint<\/strong> and <strong>wire<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Chain locker<\/strong>: <strong>clean<\/strong> out the mud, check the <strong>drainage<\/strong> and the bilge suction, check for corrosion, and <strong>test<\/strong> the <strong>bitter end<\/strong> securing and its <strong>quick release<\/strong> from outside the locker.<\/li><li><strong>Windlass<\/strong>: <strong>brake linings<\/strong>, <strong>gypsy<\/strong> wear, <strong>stoppers<\/strong>, and the <strong>hawse pipe<\/strong> and its fittings.<\/li><li><strong>Spurling pipe<\/strong> covers and the anchor washing system.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the <strong>measurements<\/strong> and <strong>renewals<\/strong> for <strong>class<\/strong>, and update the <strong>PMS<\/strong> and the anchor and cable certificate details.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I mark the shackles on my cable<\/strong><\/p><p>The usual method:<\/p><ul><li>At the <strong>first shackle<\/strong> (one shackle, 27.5 m): the <strong>first stud link on each side<\/strong> of the joining shackle painted white, and a turn of <strong>seizing wire<\/strong> on the <strong>stud<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At the <strong>second shackle<\/strong>: the <strong>second<\/strong> stud link on each side painted and wired.<\/li><li>At the <strong>third<\/strong>: the <strong>third<\/strong> link on each side, and so on, up to the fifth.<\/li><li>At the <strong>sixth<\/strong>, it starts again as at the first, or the company uses a different colour for the joining shackle itself.<\/li><li>The <strong>joining shackle<\/strong> itself is often painted a colour (red or white) for each shackle.<\/li><li>So the crew can count the shackles out by eye and by feel at night.<\/li><li>The last shackle (the &quot;danger&quot; length) is often painted red or yellow to warn that the bitter end is near.<\/li><\/ul><p>(Check the company&#x27;s own marking scheme; it&#x27;s in the SMS.)<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Ranging and cleaning, inspecting each link with the hammer test, measuring with a 12% renewal limit, Kenter shackles dismantled with new lead plugs, the anchor and swivels, turning end for end, re-marking, the chain locker and bitter end release, the windlass, records for class, and a clear description of how the shackles are marked.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What happens during anchor chain locker cleaning, how is it done?","Anchor and cable secured onboard","How long is an anchor shackle, What's the general rule of thumb for cable length to water depth.","Your anchor gets fouled on a cable, how will you remove this?","Your AB reported anchor cables have crossed, You are chief officer, how will you clear a foul hawse."],"ex":[["Inamul Bhuiyan",2],["Khairul Anam",1],["Nayyer Naqvi",1],["Khademul Insan",1],["Chris Roe",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"★14.1","sec":13,"q":"Your loadicator shows GM 0.9 m departing; at sea the roll period suggests about 0.4 m. Which do you believe and what do you do?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Which do I believe?<\/strong><\/p><p>I&#x27;d treat the <strong>roll period as a warning<\/strong>. It&#x27;s a <strong>real measurement<\/strong> of the ship <strong>as she is<\/strong>, while the loadicator is <strong>only as good as its inputs<\/strong>. I&#x27;d <strong>assume the lower GM is real<\/strong> until I&#x27;ve proved otherwise. A GM of 0.4 m on a large container ship may still meet the criteria, but the difference means something is wrong.<\/p><p><strong>Check the roll period first<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Time several full rolls in a steady sea, not when the ship is pitching or being forced by waves at a different period, and use the IS Code formula with the right C.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Likely causes of the difference<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Overweight<\/strong> or <strong>misdeclared<\/strong> containers, especially <strong>heavy boxes high<\/strong> in the stacks (wrong <strong>VGMs<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>Wrong tank soundings<\/strong>, or <strong>free surface<\/strong> not allowed for.<\/li><li>A <strong>wrong lightship<\/strong> or <strong>constant<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Icing<\/strong>, or <strong>water absorbed<\/strong> by the cargo.<\/li><li>Ballast or fuel transfers since departure not entered.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Re-sound every tank<\/strong>, re-enter the <strong>actual quantities<\/strong>, and apply the <strong>FSM<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Compare<\/strong> the <strong>departure draughts<\/strong> with the <strong>calculated<\/strong> draughts. A <strong>deeper ship<\/strong> points to <strong>heavy boxes<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Recalculate<\/strong> and check against the <strong>IS Code criteria<\/strong>, including the <strong>weather criterion<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s <strong>marginal<\/strong>, <strong>lower G<\/strong> by <strong>filling double-bottom ballast<\/strong>, pressing tanks <strong>full one at a time<\/strong> to avoid <strong>FSE<\/strong>. <strong>Avoid<\/strong> using <strong>top tanks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Consider the effect on the lashing forces and the draught.<\/li><li><strong>Heavy weather precautions<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>avoid <strong>beam seas<\/strong>;<\/li><li>avoid the speed and heading combinations that cause <strong>parametric<\/strong> or <strong>synchronous<\/strong> rolling;<\/li><li><strong>check lashings<\/strong>, since <strong>GM changes the lashing forces<\/strong>: lower GM means larger roll angles, higher GM means larger accelerations.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Inform the company<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Consider a <strong>port to restow<\/strong> if the criteria can&#x27;t be met.<\/li><li><strong>Log it<\/strong> and <strong>report<\/strong> the <strong>VGM discrepancy<\/strong> to the company and the next port, so the shippers can be traced.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Several container ship stack collapses and capsizes (for example the Golden Ray, a car carrier, in 2019) came from stability calculations that didn&#x27;t match the real ship. The roll period is the crew&#x27;s simple check.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Believe the real measurement until proved otherwise, the likely causes (VGMs, soundings, free surface, lightship and constant, icing), re-sounding and comparing draughts, recalculating with the weather criterion, ballasting low tanks one at a time if needed, heavy weather precautions and lashings, informing the company, and reporting the VGM discrepancy.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why might a lower GM actually reduce lashing loads?","pa":"<p>Lower GM gives a <strong>longer roll period<\/strong>, so <strong>lower accelerations<\/strong> at the stack tops and smaller lashing forces. But she must stay <strong>above the minimum stability criteria<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q194","sec":14,"q":"MARPOL Annex I discharge criteria; special areas; ORB entries and retention (3 years); SOPEP.","tier":"red","rep":95,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Prevention of Oil Pollution) Regs 2019<\/strong> (SI 2019/42). Internationally, <strong>MARPOL Annex I<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Machinery-space bilges (Reg 15): ships of 400 GT and over<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Discharge <strong>only<\/strong> when the ship is <strong>en route<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Through <strong>approved 15 ppm<\/strong> oil filtering equipment with an <strong>alarm<\/strong> and <strong>automatic stopping device<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Oil content ≤15 ppm<\/strong> without <strong>dilution<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Not from cargo pump room bilges on tankers, and not mixed with cargo residues.<\/li><li><strong>Inside special areas<\/strong>, the <strong>same 15 ppm<\/strong> rule applies.<\/li><li>In the <strong>Antarctic<\/strong> area, <strong>any discharge<\/strong> is <strong>prohibited<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Ships under 400 GT have similar rules as far as practicable.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Cargo-area discharges (tankers, Reg 34)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>More than <strong>50 nm<\/strong> from the nearest land, <strong>en route<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Instantaneous rate <strong>≤30 litres per nautical mile<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Total <strong>≤1/30,000<\/strong> of the cargo (for ships delivered after 1979).<\/li><li>The <strong>ODME<\/strong> (oil discharge monitoring equipment) and slop tank arrangements operating.<\/li><li><strong>Not<\/strong> in <strong>special areas<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Special areas (Annex I)<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Mediterranean<\/strong>, <strong>Baltic<\/strong>, <strong>Black Sea<\/strong>, <strong>Red Sea<\/strong>, <strong>Gulfs area<\/strong>, <strong>Gulf of Aden<\/strong>, <strong>Antarctic<\/strong>, <strong>North West European waters<\/strong>, <strong>Oman area of the Arabian Sea<\/strong>, and <strong>Southern South African waters<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Oil Record Book<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Part I<\/strong> for <strong>machinery-space<\/strong> operations: tankers of 150 GT and over, and other ships of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over.<\/li><li><strong>Part II<\/strong> for <strong>cargo and ballast<\/strong> operations on tankers of <strong>150 GT<\/strong> and over.<\/li><li>Entries cover: ballasting or cleaning of fuel tanks, discharge of dirty ballast, collection and disposal of oil residues (sludge), bilge water discharge overboard or to shore, bunkering, accidental discharges, and failure of the equipment.<\/li><li>Each <strong>entry<\/strong> is <strong>signed<\/strong> by the <strong>officer in charge<\/strong> of the operation, and <strong>each page<\/strong> by the <strong>Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Kept<\/strong> on board, readily available, for <strong>3 years<\/strong> after the <strong>last entry<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Shore reception receipts are kept with it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOPEP (Reg 37)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Required for ships of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over (oil tankers <strong>150 GT<\/strong> and over), approved by the Administration.<\/li><li>It contains:<\/li><li>the <strong>reporting procedure<\/strong> (MARPOL Protocol I);<\/li><li>the <strong>contact list<\/strong>: coastal States, ports, the company, in the appendices;<\/li><li>the <strong>actions<\/strong> to <strong>control<\/strong> a discharge: steps to reduce or stop the outflow, for each scenario (operational spills, tank overflow, hull leakage, grounding, collision);<\/li><li><strong>coordination<\/strong> with <strong>national<\/strong> and local authorities.<\/li><li><strong>Drills<\/strong> are held as the SMS requires.<\/li><li>Often combined with the SMPEP on chemical tankers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On my container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Bunkering spills, hydraulic oil leaks, and bilge water management are the risks. The C/O supports the Chief Engineer, who keeps ORB Part I.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The UK 2019 Regs, the machinery-space rules (en route, 15 ppm equipment with alarm and auto stop, no dilution, same in special areas, nothing in the Antarctic), the tanker rules (50 nm, 30 l/nm, 1/30,000, ODME, not in special areas), the special areas, the ORB Parts I and II with signatures and 3-year retention, and the SOPEP contents.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you write in the ORB after a bilge discharge through the OWS?","pa":"<p><strong>Code D, item 15<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li><strong>15.1<\/strong> quantity discharged;<\/li><li><strong>15.2<\/strong> start and stop <strong>times and positions<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>15.3<\/strong> method: through the 15 ppm equipment.<\/li><\/ul><p>The officer in charge signs, and each page is signed by the Master.<\/p>","asked":["What is MARPOL Annex 1?","Marpol Annex V special area?","Annex 1 machinery space discharge criteria","Which are the Marpol Certificate?","You have mentioned regarding IAPP Certificate under MARPOL Annex 6 are there any other documents that are needed under that Annex?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",21],["Siddhartha Paul",8],["Muhammad Iqbal",7],["Tarik Yassin",6],["Ahsan Khan",5]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q195","sec":14,"q":"Garbage: GMP, GRB, categories A–I, discharge table, polar rules.","tier":"red","rep":55,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the Merchant Shipping regulations on prevention of pollution by garbage (verify the current SI and year). Internationally, <strong>MARPOL Annex V<\/strong>, which since 2013 <strong>prohibits all discharges<\/strong> of garbage <strong>except<\/strong> where expressly allowed.<\/p><p><strong>Documents<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Placards<\/strong>: ships of <strong>12 m<\/strong> and over in length, telling crew and passengers the discharge rules, in the working language and English, French or Spanish.<\/li><li><strong>Garbage Management Plan (GMP)<\/strong>: ships of <strong>100 GT<\/strong> and over, or certified for <strong>15 persons<\/strong> or more. It names the person in charge, and covers collection, storage, processing and disposal.<\/li><li><strong>Garbage Record Book (GRB)<\/strong>: ships of <strong>100 GT<\/strong> and over (since <strong>1 May 2024<\/strong>, previously 400 GT), or certified for 15 persons or more, on international voyages.<\/li><li>The <strong>GRB<\/strong> and the reception facility <strong>receipts<\/strong> are kept for <strong>2 years<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>GRB categories<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>A<\/strong>: plastics.<\/li><li><strong>B<\/strong>: food wastes.<\/li><li><strong>C<\/strong>: domestic wastes.<\/li><li><strong>D<\/strong>: cooking oil.<\/li><li><strong>E<\/strong>: incinerator ashes.<\/li><li><strong>F<\/strong>: operational wastes.<\/li><li><strong>G<\/strong>: animal carcasses.<\/li><li><strong>H<\/strong>: fishing gear.<\/li><li><strong>I<\/strong>: e-waste.<\/li><li><strong>J<\/strong>: cargo residues (not HME).<\/li><li><strong>K<\/strong>: cargo residues (HME, harmful to the marine environment).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Discharge outside special areas, en route<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Food waste<\/strong>: at least <strong>3 nm<\/strong> from land if <strong>comminuted<\/strong> (to pass a <strong>25 mm<\/strong> screen), or <strong>12 nm<\/strong> if not.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo residues<\/strong> (not HME): at least <strong>12 nm<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cleaning agents<\/strong> in wash water: if not harmful to the marine environment.<\/li><li><strong>Animal carcasses<\/strong>: as far from land as possible.<\/li><li><strong>Plastics<\/strong>, <strong>domestic waste<\/strong>, <strong>cooking oil<\/strong>, <strong>incinerator ash<\/strong>, <strong>operational waste<\/strong>, <strong>e-waste<\/strong>, fishing gear: <strong>prohibited<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Special areas<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Mediterranean, Baltic, Black Sea, Red Sea, Gulfs area, North Sea, Antarctic and Wider Caribbean.<\/li><li><strong>Food waste only<\/strong>, at least <strong>12 nm<\/strong>, <strong>comminuted<\/strong>, en route. Cargo residues and wash water only under strict conditions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Polar Code (Arctic and Antarctic)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Food <strong>comminuted<\/strong> and at least <strong>12 nm<\/strong> from the nearest <strong>land<\/strong>, <strong>ice shelf<\/strong> or <strong>fast ice<\/strong>. <strong>Not onto ice<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Animal carcasses<\/strong> <strong>prohibited<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>GRB entries<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Date, time, position, category, estimated amount in cubic metres, for each discharge to sea, to a reception facility, or by incineration, and accidental losses.<\/li><li>The <strong>officer in charge<\/strong> signs each entry, and the <strong>Master<\/strong> signs each <strong>page<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On my ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The C/O is usually the designated person for the GMP, with segregated bins, a compactor and comminuter, and landing of dunnage, lashing material and packaging ashore.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A correction<\/strong><\/p><p>My notes said GMP at 400 GT. The threshold is <strong>100 GT<\/strong>, and the GRB is now <strong>100 GT<\/strong> too.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Annex V&#x27;s general prohibition, placards at 12 m, GMP and GRB at 100 GT, 2-year retention, the categories A to K, the discharge table outside special areas (food 3 nm comminuted or 12 nm, cargo residues 12 nm, plastics never), special areas (food only at 12 nm comminuted), the Polar rules, and the signatures.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you record garbage handed to a port reception facility?","pa":"<p>In the GRB: the <strong>date and time<\/strong>, the <strong>port and facility<\/strong>, the <strong>category<\/strong> and <strong>estimated amount<\/strong> (m³), signed by the officer in charge. Keep the <strong>receipt<\/strong> from the facility. The GRB is kept for <strong>2 years<\/strong> after the last entry.<\/p>","asked":["Garbage Discharge Criteria?","What is Garbage Management Plan?","What is garbage record book part B?","In Mediterranean Sea, Chief Cook want to throw garbage. How are you going to respond and what training are you going to give?","Phones up and asks you weather he can throw garbage overboard or not. What will you do?"],"ex":[["Michele Moro",12],["Nayyer Naqvi",6],["Khademul Insan",6],["Ahsan Khan",5],["Colum Leggett",3]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q196","sec":14,"q":"Annex VI: SOx limits, ECAs, changeover, FONAR, BDN (3 years).","tier":"orange","rep":18,"src":"Study books","tags":["ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":"Med SOx ECA from May 2025","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Prevention of Air Pollution from Ships) Regs 2008<\/strong> as amended. Internationally, <strong>MARPOL Annex VI<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Sulphur limits<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>0.50% m/m<\/strong> globally since <strong>1 January 2020<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>0.10%<\/strong> in <strong>Emission Control Areas<\/strong> (ECAs) for SOx.<\/li><li>A <strong>carriage ban<\/strong> on non-compliant fuel (above 0.50%) since <strong>1 March 2020<\/strong>, unless an <strong>approved scrubber (EGCS)<\/strong> is fitted and operating.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOx ECAs<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Baltic<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>North Sea<\/strong>, which includes the <strong>English Channel<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>North American<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>US Caribbean<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Mediterranean<\/strong>, from <strong>1 May 2025<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Canadian Arctic<\/strong> and <strong>Norwegian Sea<\/strong>: in force <strong>1 March 2026<\/strong>, with the <strong>0.10%<\/strong> limit from <strong>1 March 2027<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Further ECAs have been proposed (such as the North-East Atlantic), so check the latest before quoting.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>NOx Tier III<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Applies in the <strong>North American<\/strong> and <strong>US Caribbean<\/strong> NECAs to engines on ships built from <strong>2016<\/strong>, and in the <strong>Baltic<\/strong> and <strong>North Sea<\/strong> NECAs for ships built from <strong>2021<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Changeover<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>written procedure<\/strong> on board, showing how the changeover is done.<\/li><li><strong>Start early enough<\/strong> that only <strong>0.10%<\/strong> fuel is in use when entering the ECA. The time depends on the system volume and the consumption.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the <strong>volume<\/strong> of compliant fuel in each tank, the <strong>date<\/strong>, <strong>time<\/strong> and <strong>position<\/strong> when the changeover is <strong>completed<\/strong> on <strong>entry<\/strong> and <strong>started<\/strong> on <strong>exit<\/strong>, in the <strong>log<\/strong> the flag requires.<\/li><li>The C/E and the bridge agree the times from the passage plan.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>FONAR (Fuel Oil Non-Availability Report)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If compliant fuel truly <strong>can&#x27;t be bought<\/strong> despite <strong>best efforts<\/strong>, <strong>notify<\/strong> the <strong>flag State<\/strong> and the <strong>next port&#x27;s<\/strong> authority, and <strong>keep evidence<\/strong>: the attempts to buy, the suppliers contacted, the voyage plan.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s not a waiver; the port State decides.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>BDN and sample<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Bunker Delivery Note<\/strong> is kept for <strong>3 years<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MARPOL sample<\/strong>, sealed and signed, is kept for at least <strong>12 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The in-use and on-board sampling points allow PSC to take samples.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other Annex VI<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ozone-depleting substances, VOCs on tankers, shipboard incineration, and energy efficiency (EEXI, CII, SEEMP).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>At berth<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In UK and EU ports, ships at berth for more than two hours burn 0.10% fuel (verify the UK rule).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A correction<\/strong><\/p><p>My notes listed only the older ECAs. Add the <strong>Mediterranean<\/strong>, and the new <strong>Canadian Arctic<\/strong> and <strong>Norwegian Sea<\/strong> ECAs.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 2008 UK Regs, 0.50% globally since 2020 and 0.10% in ECAs, the carriage ban, the ECAs including the Mediterranean from May 2025 and the newer ones, NOx Tier III areas, the changeover procedure and records, the FONAR, and BDNs for 3 years with samples for 12 months.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you do if a bunker sample tests above the sulphur limit?","pa":"<p>Inform the Master, the company and the <strong>flag<\/strong>, and tell the port state. <strong>Segregate<\/strong> the fuel and don&#x27;t use it. Protest to the supplier, keep the <strong>MARPOL sample<\/strong>, arrange <strong>debunkering<\/strong>, and submit a <strong>FONAR<\/strong> if no compliant fuel is available. Record everything.<\/p>","asked":["What is Annex VI under MARPOL?","NOx Technical File*","What is SEEMP and part 1,2,3?","What Sulphur content in ECA area?","ECA Sulphur requirements and which regulation covers it. Where will you find Sulphur content in fuel?"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",8],["Ahsan Khan",4],["Mahmood ul Hassan",2],["Zia Ul Haq",2],["Alec Keep",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q197","sec":14,"q":"Certificates and documents for Annex VI.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>Air Pollution Regs 2008<\/strong> as amended. Internationally, <strong>MARPOL Annex VI<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Certificates<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>IAPP Certificate<\/strong> (International Air Pollution Prevention): ships of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over, with its <strong>supplement<\/strong> listing the equipment and compliance methods.<\/li><li><strong>IEE Certificate<\/strong> (International Energy Efficiency): ships of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over, recording the <strong>attained EEDI<\/strong> (new ships) or <strong>EEXI<\/strong> (existing ships). Valid for the ship&#x27;s life unless there&#x27;s a major conversion.<\/li><li><strong>EIAPP Certificate<\/strong> (Engine International Air Pollution Prevention): for <strong>each diesel engine<\/strong> over <strong>130 kW<\/strong>, with its <strong>NOx Technical File<\/strong> and the <strong>record book of engine parameters<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Statement of Compliance<\/strong> on fuel oil consumption reporting (<strong>DCS<\/strong>): issued <strong>annually<\/strong> for ships of <strong>5,000 GT<\/strong> and over by the flag or RO. It also carries the <strong>CII rating<\/strong> for the previous year.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Plans and records<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>SEEMP<\/strong> (Ship Energy Efficiency Management Plan):<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Part I<\/strong>: energy efficiency measures for the ship;<\/li><li><strong>Part II<\/strong>: the <strong>fuel consumption data collection<\/strong> methodology;<\/li><li><strong>Part III<\/strong>: the <strong>CII<\/strong> plan, the three-year implementation plan, and <strong>corrective actions<\/strong> if rated D for three years or E for one.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>BDNs<\/strong> (kept <strong>3 years<\/strong>) and <strong>MARPOL samples<\/strong> (12 months).<\/li><li>The <strong>fuel changeover log<\/strong> for ECAs, and any <strong>FONARs<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>ODS record book<\/strong> and <strong>equipment list<\/strong>, if there are rechargeable systems containing ozone-depleting substances (such as older refrigerants).<\/li><li><strong>Incinerator<\/strong> type-approval certificate, for incinerators installed after 2000 (verify).<\/li><li><strong>EGCS<\/strong> (scrubber) documents, if fitted: the approval certificate, the <strong>technical manual<\/strong> (ETM), the <strong>onboard monitoring manual<\/strong> and the <strong>record book<\/strong>, plus washwater data.<\/li><li><strong>VOC management plan<\/strong> on <strong>tankers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Engine technical documents for SCR or EGR if Tier III equipment is fitted.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>C/O role<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Annex VI is <strong>usually<\/strong> the <strong>Chief Engineer&#x27;s<\/strong> area.<\/li><li>On a <strong>container ship<\/strong>, I make sure the <strong>ODS records<\/strong> cover the <strong>reefer<\/strong> containers&#x27; and the ship&#x27;s <strong>AC<\/strong> and provision plant systems where relevant, and the deck side, such as refrigerant leaks reported.<\/li><li>I check the <strong>certificates<\/strong> are <strong>valid<\/strong> before <strong>PSC<\/strong>, and that the fuel changeover is planned in the passage plan.<\/li><li>Accurate <strong>noon reports<\/strong> and distance data feed the DCS and the CII.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>PSC checks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The certificates, the BDN for the fuel in use, the changeover record, the ODS book, and sometimes a fuel sample from the in-use point.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The four certificates (IAPP, IEE, EIAPP with the NOx Technical File, DCS Statement of Compliance with the CII rating), the SEEMP Parts I to III, BDNs and samples, the changeover log and FONAR, ODS book, incinerator approval, scrubber documents and the VOC plan, and the C/O&#x27;s part: ODS records, certificate validity and accurate reports.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is in the NOx Technical File and why must it stay with the engine?","pa":"<p>The NOx Technical File holds the engine&#x27;s <strong>certified emission settings and components<\/strong> (injection timing, turbocharger and so on) and the <strong>on-board verification procedure<\/strong>. It lets the surveyor confirm the engine is still as certified, so it stays with the engine.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q198","sec":14,"q":"Ballast water: D-1 vs D-2, exchange methods, BWMS problems, \"same location\".","tier":"red","rep":88,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":"BWM D-2 for all ships","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>BWM Convention<\/strong> entered into force <strong>8 September 2017<\/strong>. <strong>D-2<\/strong> has applied to <strong>all ships<\/strong> since <strong>8 September 2024<\/strong>. In the UK, the <strong>MS (Control and Management of Ships&#x27; Ballast Water and Sediments) Regs 2022<\/strong> (SI 2022/737), with <strong>MSN 1908 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 675 (M+F) Amd 2<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>D-1: ballast water exchange<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>95% volumetric<\/strong> exchange.<\/li><li>At least <strong>200 nm<\/strong> from the nearest land in at least <strong>200 m<\/strong> depth. If that isn&#x27;t possible, at least <strong>50 nm<\/strong> and <strong>200 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Methods<\/strong>:<\/li><li><strong>sequential<\/strong>: empty and refill each tank;<\/li><li><strong>flow-through<\/strong>: pump through <strong>3 times<\/strong> the tank volume;<\/li><li><strong>dilution<\/strong>: fill from the top and discharge from the bottom at the same rate.<\/li><li>Risks: stability, free surface, bending moments, propeller immersion, bridge visibility, overpressure of tanks.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>D-2: performance standard<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The treated discharge is limited to a set number of <strong>viable organisms<\/strong> by size: fewer than <strong>10 per m³<\/strong> of 50 µm or more, fewer than 10 per ml of 10–50 µm.<\/li><li>And <strong>indicator microbes<\/strong>: <strong>Vibrio cholerae<\/strong>, <strong>E. coli<\/strong>, <strong>intestinal enterococci<\/strong>, below set limits.<\/li><li>Achieved with a <strong>type-approved BWMS<\/strong>, approved under the BWMS Code.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>BWMS problems<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Filter clogging<\/strong> in muddy or sediment-laden water, so the flow rate drops and ballasting slows.<\/li><li><strong>UV<\/strong> performance falls in <strong>turbid<\/strong> water.<\/li><li><strong>Electro-chlorination<\/strong> is affected by <strong>low salinity<\/strong> or low <strong>temperature<\/strong> (fresh water ports).<\/li><li><strong>TRO<\/strong> (total residual oxidant) <strong>neutralisation<\/strong> must be checked before discharge, so the discharge isn&#x27;t toxic.<\/li><li>Power demand, alarms, and the crew&#x27;s understanding of the system.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If the system fails<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Use the <strong>contingency measures<\/strong> in the <strong>BWM Plan<\/strong>: retain, discharge to a reception facility, exchange where permitted, or other measures the port State agrees.<\/li><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the <strong>port State<\/strong> before arrival.<\/li><li>Record it, and repair the system.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>&quot;Same location&quot;<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Regulation A-3.5<\/strong> exempts ballast taken up and discharged at the <strong>same location<\/strong>, as the port State defines it, provided there&#x27;s <strong>no mixing<\/strong> with unmanaged ballast or sediments.<\/li><li>Some port States accept a whole port or anchorage as the same location; check before relying on it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Sediments<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Removed and disposed of under the BWM Plan, preferably to a reception facility, and recorded.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>International BWM Certificate<\/strong> (ships of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over).<\/li><li>The <strong>BWM Plan<\/strong>, approved.<\/li><li>The <strong>Ballast Water Record Book<\/strong>, kept on board for at least <strong>2 years<\/strong> after the last entry (then by the company for 3 years).<\/li><li>The BWMS type-approval certificate and operating records.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 2017 and 2024 dates, the UK 2022 Regs, D-1 (95%, 200 nm and 200 m or 50 nm, three methods and their risks), D-2 limits with a type-approved BWMS, BWMS problems (filters, UV turbidity, salinity, TRO), the BWMP contingency and informing the port State, the A-3.5 same location exemption, and the documents.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the safety risk of sequential exchange on a container ship?","pa":"<p>Emptying and refilling tanks creates <strong>temporary high shear forces and bending moments<\/strong>, <strong>reduced stability<\/strong> and possible slamming, poor propeller immersion and poor bridge visibility. On a container ship with heavy deck loads this is critical. Follow the BWMP sequence and check each step on the loadicator, or use <strong>flow-through<\/strong> if possible.<\/p>","asked":["What is ballast water management?","Methods of ballast water exchange ?","What is D1 and D2 standard?","Ballast water convention regulates the discharge of ballast water and what else?","When don't you need ballast water treatment plant?"],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",15],["Byron Griffiths",13],["Zia Ul Haq",12],["Robert Jackson",8],["Kris Askey",7]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q199","sec":14,"q":"How does MARPOL I / V affect you as C/O? What pollution-prevention equipment did you have?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>On a container ship there&#x27;s no cargo oil<\/strong>, so my <strong>Annex I<\/strong> role is <strong>deck-side<\/strong> and <strong>support<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Annex I: my part<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Bunkering<\/strong>: the C/O is often in charge on deck or assists the Chief Engineer.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Scuppers plugged<\/strong>, <strong>save-alls<\/strong> around the vents and manifold drained.<\/li><li>The <strong>SOPEP kit<\/strong> at the <strong>manifold<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Communications<\/strong> between the deck, the engine room and the barge.<\/li><li><strong>Soundings<\/strong> and the tank levels, to avoid overflow.<\/li><li>The bunker checklist and the barge&#x27;s procedures agreed.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Hydraulic oil leaks<\/strong> from <strong>cranes<\/strong>, <strong>hatch covers<\/strong>, the <strong>windlass<\/strong> and <strong>winches<\/strong>: maintenance under the PMS, <strong>cleaning up<\/strong> on deck, and using biodegradable oil where specified.<\/li><li><strong>SOPEP drills<\/strong> with the Master and C/E.<\/li><li><strong>Oily rags<\/strong> managed as <strong>operational waste<\/strong>.<\/li><li>I <strong>don&#x27;t sign<\/strong> the <strong>ORB<\/strong>, but I <strong>report<\/strong> any <strong>deck spill<\/strong>, which must be recorded.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Annex V: mostly my department<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>I&#x27;m usually the <strong>designated person<\/strong> for the <strong>GMP<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Segregation<\/strong> of garbage, the <strong>compactor<\/strong>, and making sure <strong>lashing material<\/strong>, <strong>dunnage<\/strong> and <strong>packaging<\/strong> are <strong>landed ashore<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Hold washing water<\/strong> and <strong>cargo residues<\/strong> follow the <strong>discharge table<\/strong> and are entered in the <strong>GRB<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Training the crew on the GMP and the categories.<\/li><li>The <strong>GRB<\/strong> entries and the reception facility receipts.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Ballast (BWM)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I run the <strong>BWMS<\/strong>, any <strong>exchange<\/strong>, and the <strong>BW record book<\/strong>, and the BWM Plan.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Annex IV and VI<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Sewage: mainly the engine room, but I check port rules for discharge.<\/li><li>Air: ODS records for the reefers and the ship&#x27;s systems.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Equipment on my last ship<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>OWS<\/strong> (oily water separator, 15 ppm) with an <strong>oil content meter<\/strong> and <strong>auto stop<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sludge<\/strong> and <strong>bilge holding tanks<\/strong>, and an <strong>incinerator<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Garbage compactor<\/strong>, <strong>food comminuter<\/strong> and <strong>segregated bins<\/strong>, colour-coded.<\/li><li><strong>Sewage treatment plant<\/strong> (Annex IV).<\/li><li><strong>SOPEP locker<\/strong>: <strong>absorbent pads<\/strong> and <strong>booms<\/strong>, <strong>sawdust<\/strong>, <strong>scoops<\/strong>, empty <strong>drums<\/strong> and a portable <strong>pump<\/strong>. <strong>Scupper plugs<\/strong> and <strong>save-alls<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>BWMS<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Scrubber (EGCS), if fitted, with its washwater monitoring.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To see that I know which parts of MARPOL I&#x27;m responsible for on my own ship, not just the theory.<\/li><li>Many pollution incidents on container ships are hydraulic oil leaks and bunkering spills on deck.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>For Annex I, my deck-side role in bunkering, hydraulic leaks, SOPEP drills and reporting spills; for Annex V, the GMP, segregation, landing dunnage and lashings, hold washing and the GRB; ballast water; and a confident list of the pollution-prevention equipment on my last ship.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who is the designated person for the GMP on your ship?","pa":"<p>The person <strong>named in the Garbage Management Plan<\/strong> as responsible for carrying it out (MARPOL V reg 10). That&#x27;s often the C/O.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q200","sec":14,"q":"Bilge pH >7 with coal — discharge?","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First, the chemistry<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>pH above 7<\/strong> is <strong>alkaline<\/strong>, not acidic.<\/li><li>The <strong>coal<\/strong> hazard is <strong>acidic<\/strong> bilge water, which <strong>corrodes<\/strong> the ship. The <strong>IMSBC coal schedule<\/strong> requires <strong>monitoring<\/strong> of hold bilge <strong>pH<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So a reading <strong>above 7<\/strong> means <strong>acid corrosion<\/strong> isn&#x27;t the problem.<\/li><li>The <strong>pH<\/strong> reading is <strong>not<\/strong> what <strong>allows or prohibits<\/strong> discharge. That&#x27;s MARPOL.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Is discharge permitted?<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Cargo hold bilge water<\/strong> is <strong>not<\/strong> <strong>machinery-space bilge<\/strong> water, so it isn&#x27;t Annex I unless it contains oil.<\/li><li>Under <strong>Annex V<\/strong>, it counts as <strong>wash water<\/strong> or <strong>cargo residues<\/strong> if it contains coal.<\/li><li><strong>Coal residues<\/strong> may be discharged <strong>only<\/strong> if they&#x27;re <strong>not<\/strong> classed as <strong>Harmful to the Marine Environment (HME)<\/strong>. Check the <strong>shipper&#x27;s declaration<\/strong>, which must state whether the cargo is HME.<\/li><li>Discharge at least <strong>12 nm<\/strong> from land, <strong>en route<\/strong>, <strong>outside special areas<\/strong>, and <strong>record<\/strong> it in the <strong>GRB<\/strong> (category J for non-HME residues).<\/li><li>If there&#x27;s <strong>any oil<\/strong> (an <strong>oily sheen<\/strong>), it falls under <strong>Annex I<\/strong> and must go to the <strong>holding tank<\/strong>, <strong>not overboard<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>In port<\/strong> or a <strong>special area<\/strong>, discharge only to a <strong>reception facility<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Local<\/strong> or <strong>port rules<\/strong> may be <strong>stricter<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Decision<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Test for oil<\/strong> and <strong>check<\/strong> the <strong>HME<\/strong> status.<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s <strong>clean<\/strong> and <strong>not HME<\/strong>, I can discharge <strong>outside 12 nm<\/strong>, <strong>en route<\/strong>, and enter it in the <strong>GRB<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Otherwise<\/strong> it goes <strong>ashore<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why acidic coal bilge water is dangerous to the ship<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Coal contains <strong>sulphur<\/strong> and iron pyrites. With <strong>water<\/strong> and <strong>air<\/strong>, they form <strong>sulphuric acid<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>acid<\/strong> <strong>corrodes<\/strong> the <strong>steel<\/strong>: the <strong>bilge wells<\/strong>, the <strong>tank top<\/strong>, the <strong>hopper plating<\/strong>, <strong>frames<\/strong> and the <strong>bilge pipes<\/strong> and <strong>strum boxes<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Over time it <strong>thins<\/strong> the structure, which can lead to <strong>holes<\/strong> into double-bottom tanks, <strong>leaks<\/strong>, and weakened frames.<\/li><li>It damages the <strong>bilge pumping<\/strong> system, so the hold can&#x27;t be pumped when needed.<\/li><li>It can damage coatings and any cargo stowed later.<\/li><li><strong>Action<\/strong>: monitor pH regularly; if acidic, <strong>pump<\/strong> the bilges <strong>frequently<\/strong> to keep them <strong>dry<\/strong>, and consider dosing with an alkali such as lime where the procedure allows, and record it.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>PH above 7 is alkaline, so acid corrosion isn&#x27;t the issue, and pH doesn&#x27;t decide discharge; hold bilge water with coal is Annex V wash water or residues, allowed only if not HME, at least 12 nm, en route, outside special areas, recorded in the GRB; any oil makes it Annex I to the holding tank; and why acidic coal bilge water corrodes the ship.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"★15.1","sec":14,"q":"What are EEXI and CII, and what does a D rating mean for your ship?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"CII ratings","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>MARPOL Annex VI<\/strong> Chapter 4 amendments (<strong>MEPC.328(76)<\/strong>), in force <strong>1 November 2022<\/strong>, with the requirements from <strong>1 January 2023<\/strong>. In the UK, applied through amendments to the Air Pollution Regs (verify).<\/p><p><strong>EEXI: Energy Efficiency Existing Ship Index<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>one-off technical design<\/strong> measure, like the <strong>EEDI<\/strong> but for <strong>existing ships<\/strong> of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over.<\/li><li>It calculates the ship&#x27;s CO₂ emission per tonne-mile from her design (engine power, speed, capacity), and it must be below a required value for her type and size.<\/li><li><strong>Verified<\/strong> at the <strong>first survey<\/strong> after <strong>1 January 2023<\/strong>, and recorded on the <strong>IEE Certificate<\/strong> with the EEXI technical file.<\/li><li>Often met by <strong>engine power limitation<\/strong> (<strong>EPL<\/strong> or <strong>ShaPoLi<\/strong>, shaft power limitation), which <strong>limits<\/strong> our <strong>maximum power<\/strong> in normal service.<\/li><li>The <strong>override<\/strong> is for <strong>emergencies only<\/strong> (for example to avoid danger in heavy weather) and must be <strong>recorded<\/strong> and reported to the flag.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>CII: Carbon Intensity Indicator<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>An <strong>operational<\/strong> measure for ships of <strong>5,000 GT<\/strong> and over.<\/li><li><strong>CO₂ emitted per tonne-mile<\/strong> (capacity times distance), calculated <strong>annually<\/strong> from the <strong>DCS<\/strong> data: fuel used and distance sailed.<\/li><li><strong>Rated A to E<\/strong> against a <strong>reference line<\/strong>, with a <strong>reduction factor<\/strong> that <strong>tightens each year<\/strong>, so the same performance gets a worse rating over time.<\/li><li>The rating is shown on the Statement of Compliance.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What a D rating means<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>One D<\/strong> on its own <strong>doesn&#x27;t trigger<\/strong> action.<\/li><li>But <strong>D for 3 consecutive years<\/strong>, or <strong>E in any one year<\/strong>, means a <strong>corrective action plan<\/strong> must be <strong>developed<\/strong> and included in <strong>SEEMP Part III<\/strong>, then <strong>verified<\/strong> by the Administration or RO.<\/li><li>Charterers, banks and ports increasingly look at the rating commercially, so a D or E can affect the ship&#x27;s employment.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Effect on board<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Slow steaming<\/strong> and just-in-time arrival, avoiding waiting at anchor.<\/li><li><strong>Trim optimisation<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Hull and propeller cleaning<\/strong>, and good coatings.<\/li><li><strong>Weather routeing<\/strong> to avoid wasted fuel.<\/li><li>Reducing <strong>auxiliary<\/strong> fuel: reefer management, generator loading.<\/li><li><strong>Accurate noon and fuel reports<\/strong>, because the CII is only as good as the data.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Review<\/strong><\/p><p>The CII framework is under review at IMO, with the Net-Zero Framework being negotiated (MEPC due to reconvene in November 2026), so the details may change.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>EEXI as a one-off design index for ships of 400 GT and over, verified from 2023 on the IEE certificate, often met by engine power limitation with an emergency override recorded; CII as the annual operational rating for 5,000 GT and over, A to E, tightening each year; D for three years or E once needing a corrective plan in SEEMP Part III; and the practical measures on board.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How does trim optimisation improve CII?","pa":"<p>The right trim <strong>reduces hull resistance<\/strong> at a given speed and draught. That means <strong>less fuel and CO₂ per tonne-mile<\/strong>, which improves the CII.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q201","sec":15,"q":"What is salvage? Towage vs salvage; when does towage become salvage? LOF.","tier":"orange","rep":36,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO","ON","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, <strong>MSA 1995 s.224<\/strong> and <strong>Schedule 11<\/strong>, which give the <strong>Salvage Convention 1989<\/strong> the force of law.<\/p><p><strong>What salvage is<\/strong><\/p><p>Any act or activity undertaken to <strong>assist<\/strong> a <strong>vessel<\/strong> or any other <strong>property<\/strong> in <strong>danger<\/strong> in <strong>navigable<\/strong> waters or in any other waters.<\/p><p><strong>For a salvage reward you need<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Recognised property<\/strong>: the <strong>ship<\/strong>, <strong>cargo<\/strong>, <strong>freight<\/strong> at risk, <strong>bunkers<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Real danger<\/strong>: not necessarily immediate, but real and reasonable fear of damage or loss.<\/li><li>A <strong>voluntary service<\/strong>: no <strong>pre-existing duty<\/strong>, such as a <strong>towage contract<\/strong> or the <strong>crew&#x27;s own duty<\/strong> to their ship.<\/li><li><strong>Success<\/strong>: the &quot;<strong>no cure, no pay<\/strong>&quot; principle (Art 12). Something must be saved.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The reward (Art 13)<\/strong><\/p><p>Reflects:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>salved value<\/strong> of the ship and other property;<\/li><li>the <strong>skill<\/strong> and <strong>efforts<\/strong> of the salvors in <strong>preventing<\/strong> or minimising <strong>environmental damage<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>measure of success<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>nature and degree of danger<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the salvors&#x27; <strong>skill<\/strong> and <strong>efforts<\/strong> in saving the ship, property and life;<\/li><li>the <strong>time<\/strong> used and <strong>expenses<\/strong> and losses;<\/li><li>the <strong>risk<\/strong> of liability and other risks;<\/li><li>the <strong>promptness<\/strong> of the services;<\/li><li>the <strong>availability<\/strong> and use of vessels and equipment;<\/li><li>the <strong>state of readiness<\/strong> and value of the salvor&#x27;s equipment.<\/li><li>The reward can&#x27;t exceed the salved value.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Art 14: special compensation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Where the casualty <strong>threatened the environment<\/strong>, the salvor gets his <strong>expenses<\/strong> plus up to <strong>30%<\/strong>, or up to <strong>100%<\/strong> if the tribunal awards it, <strong>even without success<\/strong> in saving the property.<\/li><li>In practice it&#x27;s replaced by the <strong>SCOPIC<\/strong> clause (Special Compensation P&amp;I Clause), which sets agreed tariff rates paid by the P&amp;I club, if the salvor invokes it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Towage vs salvage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Towage<\/strong> is a <strong>contracted<\/strong> service for an <strong>agreed fee<\/strong>: the tug is under a duty to perform it.<\/li><li>Towage <strong>becomes salvage<\/strong> when <strong>unforeseen danger<\/strong> arises and the tug performs services <strong>beyond the scope<\/strong> of the towage contract (Art 17).<\/li><li>Example: the tow catches fire, or the tow line parts in a storm and the tug has to take extraordinary risks to reconnect.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>LOF: Lloyd&#x27;s Open Form<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The current form is <strong>LOF 2020<\/strong>, a &quot;<strong>no cure, no pay<\/strong>&quot; contract with <strong>Lloyd&#x27;s arbitration<\/strong> in London to fix the reward later, so the salvor can start work at once without negotiating a price.<\/li><li><strong>SCOPIC<\/strong> can be incorporated.<\/li><li>Under <strong>Art 6(2)<\/strong>, the <strong>Master<\/strong> may <strong>sign<\/strong> on behalf of the <strong>owner<\/strong> (and the <strong>cargo owners<\/strong>).<\/li><li>The Master&#x27;s duty under Art 8: cooperate with the salvors, use best endeavours to prevent environmental damage, and accept redelivery when reasonably requested.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My notes<\/strong> cite LOF 1980 and 1995. <strong>Use LOF 2020<\/strong>.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MSA 1995 s.224 and Sch 11, the definition, the four elements (property, danger, voluntary, success), Art 13 criteria, Art 14 special compensation and SCOPIC, towage becoming salvage under Art 17, and LOF 2020 with the Master&#x27;s authority under Art 6(2).<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Is there a reward for saving life alone?","pa":"<p><strong>No separate reward from the people saved<\/strong> (Salvage Convention <strong>Art 16<\/strong>). But a life salvor is entitled to a <strong>fair share<\/strong> of the award made for saving the ship or property.<\/p>","asked":["What is Towage and Salvage?","Stowage and securing arrangements, loca on and procedures.","Would your company prefer Lloyd's open forum or towage ?","Where‬‭would‬‭you‬‭find‬‭the‬‭stowage‬‭factor?","What is SCOPIC clause?"],"ex":[["Shiva Balasubramanian",7],["David Carlisle",5],["Michele Moro",4],["Colum Leggett",4],["Sarah Lawlor",3]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q202","sec":15,"q":"Records for a vessel under tow / a salvage service.","tier":"grey","rep":0,"src":"Study books","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Why records matter<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Accurate contemporaneous records<\/strong> decide the <strong>salvage award<\/strong> and any <strong>dispute<\/strong>. Arbitrators look at the degree of danger, the salvor&#x27;s efforts, and what the ship could have done herself. Keep them in the <strong>deck log<\/strong> and on <strong>separate sheets<\/strong>, and <strong>save the VDR<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>What to record<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Times<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the first <strong>danger<\/strong> or casualty;<\/li><li>the <strong>call for help<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>offers<\/strong> from salvors and their <strong>acceptance<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>LOF signed<\/strong>: by whom, with what amendments (for example SCOPIC incorporated or not);<\/li><li><strong>tug arrival<\/strong>, <strong>towline connected<\/strong>, and any <strong>parting<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>arrival<\/strong> at the destination and <strong>redelivery<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Positions<\/strong>, <strong>courses<\/strong> and <strong>speeds<\/strong> throughout.<\/li><li><strong>Weather<\/strong> and <strong>sea state<\/strong>, regularly, at least hourly.<\/li><li>The <strong>ship&#x27;s own condition<\/strong> and <strong>capability<\/strong>: <strong>engines<\/strong>, <strong>steering<\/strong>, <strong>power<\/strong>, <strong>damage<\/strong>, <strong>flooding<\/strong>, and <strong>what the ship could have done herself<\/strong>. That <strong>reduces<\/strong> the <strong>degree of danger<\/strong> and so the <strong>award<\/strong>. If the engines could have been restarted in two hours, record it.<\/li><li><strong>Communications<\/strong> with the <strong>tug or salvor<\/strong>, the <strong>owners<\/strong>, the <strong>coastal State<\/strong>, the <strong>MRCC<\/strong>, <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong>: the <strong>exact words<\/strong>, preferably <strong>recorded<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Tug details<\/strong>: name, <strong>bollard pull<\/strong> and <strong>crew<\/strong> numbers.<\/li><li><strong>Towline details<\/strong>: <strong>whose line<\/strong>, <strong>length<\/strong> and <strong>type<\/strong>. Using the ship&#x27;s own line can matter.<\/li><li><strong>Equipment<\/strong> used, <strong>damaged<\/strong> or <strong>lost<\/strong>; the <strong>ship&#x27;s crew<\/strong> assisting; and any <strong>injuries<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Expenses<\/strong>, <strong>jettison<\/strong>, <strong>cargo damage<\/strong> and <strong>pollution<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Photos<\/strong> and <strong>video<\/strong>, and the <strong>names<\/strong> of <strong>witnesses<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If we are the salvor<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The same records, plus:<\/li><li>the <strong>danger<\/strong> the casualty was in;<\/li><li>the <strong>risks<\/strong> we took to our own ship and crew;<\/li><li>the <strong>deviation<\/strong> and its <strong>cost<\/strong> to our own voyage: fuel, time, the charterer&#x27;s loss;<\/li><li>the help given and the result.<\/li><li>The salvage reward is shared between the owner, the Master and the crew of the salving ship, so good records also protect the crew&#x27;s share.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>note of protest<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><li>A <strong>report<\/strong> to the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I/H&amp;M<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Copies of all records kept for the arbitration, which may be months later.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Why records decide the award; the list of times (danger, call, offers, LOF signed with amendments, connection, parting, arrival); positions, weather, the ship&#x27;s own capability, exact communications, tug and towline details, equipment, crew, injuries, expenses and pollution, photos and witnesses; the extra records if we&#x27;re the salvor; and the note of protest and reports.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why record what your own ship could have done to save herself?","pa":"<p>The <strong>reward<\/strong> (Art 13) depends on the <strong>danger<\/strong>, the salvor&#x27;s skill and effort, and the value saved. If the ship could largely have saved herself, the award should be smaller. It&#x27;s evidence for the owner&#x27;s case.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q203","sec":15,"q":"H&M vs P&I — what each covers.","tier":"orange","rep":32,"src":"Study books","tags":["F"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>Marine Insurance Act 1906<\/strong>. Hull policies usually use the <strong>Institute Time Clauses – Hulls<\/strong> or the <strong>International Hull Clauses<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>H&amp;M (Hull and Machinery)<\/strong><\/p><p>Usually a <strong>fixed-premium<\/strong> market policy, placed through brokers with underwriters (Lloyd&#x27;s or companies).<\/p><p>It covers:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Physical loss of or damage<\/strong> to the <strong>ship<\/strong>: the <strong>hull<\/strong>, <strong>machinery<\/strong> and <strong>equipment<\/strong>, from <strong>insured perils<\/strong> such as <strong>fire<\/strong>, <strong>collision<\/strong>, <strong>grounding<\/strong>, <strong>heavy weather<\/strong>, explosion, and crew negligence, up to <strong>total loss<\/strong> (actual or constructive).<\/li><li><strong>Collision liability<\/strong>: commonly <strong>3/4ths<\/strong> (the <strong>running down clause<\/strong>), depending on the policy; the other quarter goes to P&amp;I. Some policies cover 4/4ths.<\/li><li>The <strong>ship&#x27;s share<\/strong> of <strong>general average<\/strong> and <strong>salvage<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sue and labour<\/strong> costs: expenses reasonably incurred to avert or minimise a loss.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Exclusions: wear and tear, lack of due diligence by the owner, war and strikes (covered separately), and a deductible on each claim.<\/li><li><strong>Loss of hire<\/strong> is a separate insurance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>P&amp;I (Protection and Indemnity)<\/strong><\/p><p>From <strong>mutual clubs<\/strong>, most in the <strong>International Group<\/strong>, where shipowners share each other&#x27;s claims. It covers <strong>third-party liabilities<\/strong>:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Crew<\/strong> illness, <strong>injury<\/strong>, <strong>death<\/strong> and <strong>repatriation<\/strong>, and the MLC financial security.<\/li><li><strong>Passengers<\/strong> and other <strong>third parties<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo claims<\/strong>, subject to Hague-Visby and the B/L terms.<\/li><li>The <strong>remaining 1/4<\/strong> of <strong>collision<\/strong> liability, and damage to <strong>fixed and floating objects<\/strong> (piers, buoys, cranes).<\/li><li><strong>Pollution<\/strong> and <strong>wreck removal<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Stowaways<\/strong>, <strong>quarantine<\/strong>, the cost of <strong>diversion<\/strong> to land sick crew, and <strong>life salvage<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Some <strong>fines<\/strong> (at the club&#x27;s <strong>discretion<\/strong>).<\/li><li>The <strong>blue cards<\/strong> for the <strong>CLC<\/strong>, <strong>Bunkers Convention<\/strong> and <strong>Wreck Removal Convention<\/strong> certificates.<\/li><li>Legal costs, and the network of local <strong>correspondents<\/strong> who help the ship in port.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Not covered by P&amp;I<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Liabilities from a <strong>letter of indemnity<\/strong> for a <strong>clean B/L<\/strong> issued for damaged cargo, or delivery without production of the B/L.<\/li><li><strong>Deliberate misconduct<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Fines for wilful breaches (often).<\/li><li>Loss of the ship itself (that&#x27;s H&amp;M).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it means for me<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In an incident, the P&amp;I correspondent is often my first contact in port for crew, cargo, pollution and stowaway matters.<\/li><li>Records and early notification protect the cover.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The MIA 1906; H&amp;M as fixed-premium cover for the ship&#x27;s own physical damage, 3/4 collision liability, GA and salvage share, sue and labour; P&amp;I as mutual club cover for third-party liabilities (crew, cargo, the remaining 1/4 collision, fixed objects, pollution, wreck removal, stowaways, deviation, fines at discretion, blue cards); and what P&amp;I doesn&#x27;t cover.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who do you call first after a collision, H&M or P&I?","pa":"<p>The <strong>company notifies both<\/strong> at once. The <strong>P&amp;I correspondent<\/strong> is usually the first contact for legal help, evidence and third-party claims. <strong>H&amp;M<\/strong> appoints the surveyor for hull damage.<\/p>","asked":["What certificates are there under the MLC?","What Insurance should you have in relation to MLC?","What is NOP & LOP?","What is it, and why are P&I involved?","If temperature exceeds 55°C or CO concentration steadily rises, SEEK P&I"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",4],["Kaiser Ali",4],["Inamul Bhuiyan",3],["Zia Ul Haq",3],["Nayyer Naqvi",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q204","sec":15,"q":"Note of protest; general average; particular average.","tier":"yellow","rep":10,"src":"Study books","tags":["F","P1"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Note of protest<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>sworn declaration<\/strong> by the <strong>Master<\/strong> before a <strong>notary public<\/strong>, <strong>consul<\/strong> or <strong>magistrate<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It <strong>records<\/strong> an <strong>event<\/strong> that may lead to a <strong>claim<\/strong>: <strong>heavy weather<\/strong>, <strong>cargo damage<\/strong>, <strong>delay<\/strong>, a <strong>cargo shortage<\/strong>, or a collision.<\/li><li>Made as <strong>soon as possible<\/strong> after <strong>arrival<\/strong>, ideally within <strong>24 hours<\/strong> and <strong>before breaking bulk<\/strong> (opening the hatches).<\/li><li><strong>Reserve the right to extend<\/strong> it later, when the damage is known.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>backed by log extracts<\/strong>: the weather, the actions taken, the hatch and ventilation records.<\/li><li>In <strong>English courts<\/strong> it has <strong>limited evidential weight<\/strong>, but it&#x27;s <strong>essential<\/strong> in many <strong>civil-law<\/strong> jurisdictions, and it <strong>supports<\/strong> claims and defences. Some charter parties require it.<\/li><li>The P&amp;I correspondent or the agent arranges it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>General average<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>MIA 1906 s.66<\/strong>, and contractually the <strong>York-Antwerp Rules<\/strong> (now <strong>2016<\/strong>) through the <strong>B/L<\/strong> or <strong>charter party<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>GA act<\/strong> is an <strong>extraordinary sacrifice or expenditure<\/strong>, <strong>intentionally<\/strong> and <strong>reasonably<\/strong> made for the <strong>common safety<\/strong> of the ship and cargo, to preserve them from a common peril.<\/li><li><strong>Examples<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>jettison<\/strong> of cargo;<\/li><li><strong>port of refuge<\/strong> costs, and the costs of discharging, storing and reloading cargo there;<\/li><li><strong>beaching<\/strong> to prevent sinking;<\/li><li><strong>water damage<\/strong> from <strong>firefighting<\/strong> to cargo not on fire;<\/li><li><strong>towage<\/strong> or salvage in danger;<\/li><li>using the ship&#x27;s stores or materials as fuel in an emergency.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>All interests contribute<\/strong> proportionally to their saved values: the ship, the cargo owners, and freight.<\/li><li>An <strong>average adjuster<\/strong> apportions it.<\/li><li>The <strong>shipowner declares GA<\/strong>, and takes <strong>GA bonds<\/strong> and <strong>guarantees<\/strong> (from cargo insurers) before <strong>delivering<\/strong> the cargo, otherwise he may lose the contribution.<\/li><li><strong>C/O role<\/strong>: <strong>full records<\/strong>, <strong>photos<\/strong>, and <strong>soundings<\/strong> and <strong>quantities<\/strong>: what was sacrificed, why, when and by whose order.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Particular average<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MIA s.64<\/strong>: a <strong>partial loss<\/strong> to <strong>one interest<\/strong> from an <strong>insured peril<\/strong>, <strong>not<\/strong> for the <strong>common safety<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Example: <strong>seawater damage<\/strong> to <strong>one consignment<\/strong> from a leaking hatch in heavy weather, or a fire that damages one container.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>borne<\/strong> by <strong>that interest<\/strong> or its <strong>insurer<\/strong>, not shared.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The difference in one line<\/strong><\/p><p>GA is a deliberate sacrifice for everyone, shared by all; particular average is an accidental loss to one, borne by that one.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The note of protest as a sworn declaration within 24 hours before breaking bulk, backed by logs, with limited weight in English law but important elsewhere; GA under MIA s.66 and the York-Antwerp Rules 2016, its definition, examples, contribution by all interests, the adjuster and GA bonds before delivery, and the C/O&#x27;s records; and particular average under s.64 borne by the interest that suffered it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Is a port of refuge call after an engine breakdown always GA?","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> General average needs a <strong>real common peril<\/strong> to ship and cargo, and an <strong>intentional, reasonable<\/strong> extraordinary sacrifice or expense for the common safety (York-Antwerp Rules). Putting in for convenience, when the ship could have continued safely, isn&#x27;t GA.<\/p>","asked":["What is a note of protest?","What is General Average?","What is Particular average.?","Issue le er of protest and a ach reports from cargo and P&I surveyor. Inform Charters and Owners via DPA. Master can issue a Note of protest if damages are extensive."],"ex":[["Ahsan Khan",3],["Muhammad Iqbal",2],["Examiner not named",2],["Inamul Bhuiyan",1],["David Carlisle",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q205","sec":15,"q":"Master's obligations with a pilot on board.","tier":"orange","rep":23,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, under the <strong>Pilotage Act 1987<\/strong>, compulsory pilotage <strong>doesn&#x27;t remove<\/strong> the <strong>owner&#x27;s<\/strong> or <strong>Master&#x27;s<\/strong> <strong>responsibility<\/strong> for the ship (s.16, verify; my bank cites s.18). Internationally: <strong>STCW Code A-VIII/2 Part 3-1 paragraphs 49–50<\/strong>, <strong>SOLAS V/23<\/strong> (pilot transfer arrangements) and <strong>IMO Resolution A.960(23)<\/strong> (pilot training and operational procedures, including the Master-pilot exchange).<\/p><p><strong>The principle (STCW para 49)<\/strong><\/p><p>Despite the duties and obligations of a pilot, his <strong>presence on board<\/strong> does <strong>not relieve<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong> or the <strong>OOW<\/strong> of their <strong>duties and obligations<\/strong> for the <strong>safety of the ship<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The Master&#x27;s obligations<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. Master-pilot information exchange<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>pilot card<\/strong>: draught, air draught, manoeuvring data, the engine and propeller type, thrusters, and any <strong>defects<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>pilot&#x27;s plan<\/strong>: the route, speeds, <strong>local conditions<\/strong>, <strong>tugs<\/strong> and where they make fast, the berthing plan.<\/li><li>Agreement on how they&#x27;ll <strong>communicate<\/strong>: the <strong>language<\/strong>, the helm orders, and with the tugs.<\/li><li>The wheelhouse poster and the passage plan for the pilotage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Safe boarding<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A safe <strong>pilot ladder<\/strong> or <strong>combination arrangement<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS V/23<\/strong>), <strong>rigged<\/strong> and <strong>supervised<\/strong> by a responsible <strong>officer<\/strong>, with <strong>lighting<\/strong>, a <strong>lifebuoy<\/strong> and a <strong>heaving line<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. The bridge team stays fully engaged<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>OOW<\/strong> <strong>monitors<\/strong> the <strong>position<\/strong> and the <strong>plan<\/strong>: <strong>ECDIS<\/strong>, <strong>parallel indexing<\/strong>, radar.<\/li><li>He <strong>cross-checks<\/strong> the <strong>pilot&#x27;s orders<\/strong> and <strong>confirms<\/strong> they&#x27;re <strong>executed<\/strong> correctly: the helm, the engine, the rudder angle indicator.<\/li><li>The lookout continues.<\/li><li>The Master or the OOW is on the bridge throughout.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Challenge if in doubt<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the pilot&#x27;s intentions or actions are <strong>unclear<\/strong>, ask.<\/li><li>If the pilot&#x27;s <strong>actions endanger<\/strong> the ship, the <strong>Master<\/strong> or <strong>OOW<\/strong> <strong>takes over<\/strong> and <strong>tells<\/strong> the <strong>pilot<\/strong>, then explains and records it (STCW para 50).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Records and reports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Log<\/strong> the <strong>boarding<\/strong> and <strong>landing<\/strong> times and positions.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> the pilot&#x27;s <strong>conduct<\/strong> or <strong>defects<\/strong> in the pilotage service to the harbour authority if needed.<\/li><li>Record any incidents.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Master&#x27;s overriding authority<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>ISM 5.2<\/strong> and <strong>SOLAS V/34-1<\/strong>: the Master&#x27;s decisions on safety can&#x27;t be overridden by the pilot, the owner or the charterer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Liability<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In the UK, the ship&#x27;s owner is liable for damage caused by the ship while under compulsory pilotage, and the pilot&#x27;s own liability is limited.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Pilotage Act 1987 and the principle that the pilot doesn&#x27;t relieve the Master or OOW (STCW A-VIII/2 para 49), the Master-pilot exchange with the pilot card and the plan, safe boarding under SOLAS V/23, the bridge team monitoring and cross-checking, challenging and taking over if the ship is endangered, the log entries, and the Master&#x27;s overriding authority.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"When would you relieve a pilot of the con?","pa":"<p>When he&#x27;s <strong>incapacitated<\/strong> (ill, drunk) or is clearly <strong>endangering the ship<\/strong>: unsafe actions, breaking COLREGs, ignoring the Master&#x27;s concerns. The Master takes the con, tells the pilot authority and records it.<\/p>","asked":["Pilot Vessel at Anchor.","PILOT VSL AT ANCHOR SEEING FROM PORT","What is a Pilot card?","Duties of the OOW, Guidance on passage planning, Maintenance of bridge equipment, Pilotage,","Vessel is in channel,Pilot on board captain is having lunch you hear loud sound and when you see GPS speed its showing Zero .Vessel is grounded?"],"ex":[["Zahid Raza",3],["Chris Roe",2],["Ahsan Khan",2],["James Gilbert",2],["Shahid Akhter",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q206","sec":15,"q":"Seaworthiness.","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Study books","tags":["HO"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Definition: insurance<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Marine Insurance Act 1906 s.39(4)<\/strong>: a ship is <strong>seaworthy<\/strong> when she is <strong>reasonably fit in all respects<\/strong> to encounter the <strong>ordinary perils of the seas<\/strong> of the adventure insured.<\/p><p><strong>In insurance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Voyage policy<\/strong>: an <strong>implied warranty<\/strong> that the ship is seaworthy at the <strong>start of the voyage<\/strong> (s.39(1)). If she isn&#x27;t, the insurer may not pay.<\/li><li><strong>Time policy<\/strong> (s.39(5)): <strong>no implied warranty<\/strong>, but the insurer <strong>isn&#x27;t liable<\/strong> for loss <strong>attributable to unseaworthiness<\/strong> if the ship was <strong>sent to sea unseaworthy<\/strong> with the <strong>privity<\/strong> (knowledge) of the assured.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In carriage of goods<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Hague-Visby Rules Article III r.1<\/strong> (COGSA 1971): the carrier must exercise <strong>due diligence<\/strong>, <strong>before and at the beginning<\/strong> of the voyage, to:<\/p><ol><li><strong>make the ship seaworthy<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>properly man, equip and supply<\/strong> her;<\/li><li>make the <strong>holds<\/strong>, refrigerating and cool chambers, and all other parts where goods are carried, <strong>fit and safe<\/strong> for their reception, carriage and preservation (<strong>cargoworthiness<\/strong>).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>If the carrier fails, he can&#x27;t rely on the Hague-Visby defences for the loss caused.<\/li><li>&quot;Due diligence&quot; means taking reasonable care, not an absolute guarantee.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Statutory<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MSA 1995 s.94 to s.98<\/strong>:<\/li><li>s.94: the meaning of &quot;dangerously unsafe ship&quot;;<\/li><li>s.95: a dangerously unsafe ship can be <strong>detained<\/strong>;<\/li><li>s.98: the <strong>owner<\/strong> and <strong>Master<\/strong> commit an <strong>offence<\/strong> if a dangerously unsafe ship goes to sea;<\/li><li>s.100: the owner&#x27;s duty to take reasonable steps to make sure the ship is operated safely.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it means for the C/O in practice<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stability<\/strong> OK, and <strong>cargo secured<\/strong> to the <strong>CSM<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Watertight and weathertight integrity<\/strong>: hatch covers, vents, doors, closed and maintained.<\/li><li><strong>Certificates valid<\/strong>, <strong>safe manning<\/strong>, and the <strong>crew rested<\/strong> and competent.<\/li><li><strong>Charts<\/strong>, <strong>publications<\/strong> and <strong>equipment<\/strong> working; <strong>bunkers<\/strong>, water and <strong>stores<\/strong> sufficient for the voyage with a margin.<\/li><li><strong>LSA<\/strong> and <strong>FFE<\/strong> operational.<\/li><li><strong>Holds<\/strong> clean and fit for the cargo (cargoworthiness), and reefers working.<\/li><li>The ship within her load line, and the draught and air draught suitable.<\/li><li>A passage plan in place.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;Seaworthiness&quot; connects the C/O&#x27;s daily work to the law: if I sail with unsecured cargo, a leaking hatch or tired crew, the ship may be unseaworthy, the insurance and cargo defences may fail, and the Master and owner may commit an offence.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MIA s.39(4) definition, the voyage policy implied warranty and the time policy privity rule, Hague-Visby Art III r.1 due diligence before and at the beginning of the voyage including cargoworthiness, MSA s.94 to s.98 with detention and the offence, and the practical list for the C/O.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can a ship be seaworthy but not cargoworthy?","pa":"<p><strong>Yes.<\/strong> Seaworthy means fit to face the voyage: hull, machinery, crew and equipment. <strong>Cargoworthy<\/strong> means fit for <strong>this cargo<\/strong>. The holds may be dirty or unsuitable for grain, or the reefer plant may have failed.<\/p>","asked":["As a Master how you know vessel is SEAWORTHY","How will you impart knowledge to your cadet about seaworthiness?","He nudged me towards tell me what all will you check as a chief officer to ensure your vessel is seaworthy?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",2],["Roddy MacKay",1]],"yrs":"2025–2025"},{"id":"Q207","sec":0,"q":"You join as C/O on a newbuild still at the yard, awaiting delivery. What do you do before and after delivery? How does the ship get its ISM certificates?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["GMA"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Before I travel<\/strong><\/p><p>I get an office briefing from the superintendent, the DPA and the CSO: the delivery schedule, the yard&#x27;s delivery and takeover checklist, the company&#x27;s SMS for this ship type, which class and flag surveyors are attending, and the crew joining plan.<\/p><p><strong>At the yard, before delivery<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Meet the site team and the yard&#x27;s department heads.<\/li><li>Collect the plans and manuals: as-built drawings, GA and capacity plan, approved stability booklet, loading computer and its approval, Cargo Securing Manual, fire control and safety plans, docking plan, BWMP, GMP, SOPEP.<\/li><li>Check equipment certificates and type approvals, the spares and stores inventory against the contract, and the LSA and FFE inventory.<\/li><li>Attend the inclining experiment results and the lightship check.<\/li><li>Attend the sea trials as C/O: speed and endurance, turning circles and crash stop for the manoeuvring booklet and the wheelhouse poster, steering gear test, anchor and windlass trials, and LSA and FFE tests.<\/li><li>Check the loading computer test conditions against the approved stability booklet.<\/li><li>Snag list: every defect I find goes on the yard&#x27;s list, and I don&#x27;t accept the ship with safety items open.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How she gets her ISM certificates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The company must already hold a DOC for this ship type. If the type is new to the company, it gets an interim DOC, valid up to 12 months.<\/li><li>For a UK ship the MCA audits the ship (ISM Regs 2026, MGN 708) and issues an interim SMC, valid 6 months. It can be extended once by 6 months in special cases.<\/li><li>We then run the SMS and collect objective evidence for at least 3 months.<\/li><li>The initial SMC audit follows within the interim period and gives a full SMC valid 5 years, with an intermediate audit between the second and third anniversary.<\/li><li>Security runs in parallel: ship security assessment, an approved SSP, SSAS tested, and an interim ISSC valid 6 months.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After delivery<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Familiarisation for every crew member, including the Master, because nobody has served on her before.<\/li><li>Hold all the drills before sailing: fire, abandon ship, enclosed space entry, steering gear.<\/li><li>Bunkers, stores, water, and the first cargo plan tested on the new loadicator.<\/li><li>Report to the company that we&#x27;re ready.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The paperwork, the trials, and the interim certificates with their validity.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's in the delivery documents you sign for?","pa":"<p>Protocol of delivery, certificates (class, statutory, tonnage, registry), equipment certificates, inventory, outstanding items list.<\/p>","asked":["As this is a new build, what surveys do you expect to be carried out?"],"ex":[["Shahid Akhter",1],["Colum Leggett",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"Q208","sec":0,"q":"The ship has been laid up for 8 months with no crew. You join with an all-new crew, Master included. What do you do?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>A laid-up ship with no crew is a hazard until proven otherwise. The company and class reactivation procedure applies.<\/p><p><strong>1. Safety first, before anyone goes inside<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Treat every tank, void, chain locker, cargo hold and store as an enclosed space. Ventilate, test the atmosphere for O₂, flammables and toxic gases, and use the enclosed space entry procedure under the Merchant Shipping and Fishing Vessels (Entry into Enclosed Spaces) Regs 2022, MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3 and the SMS.<\/li><li>Check gas-free and safe for hot work before any repairs.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Certificates and class<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the lay-up survey status. Did any annual, intermediate or renewal surveys fall due during the lay-up?<\/li><li>Class normally requires a reactivation survey. All statutory certificates must be valid before sailing.<\/li><li>Check the ISM position: after a long lay-up the SMC may need an additional audit, and the ISSC an additional verification.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Systematic reactivation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>LSA: lifeboats, release gear and engines serviced by an authorised service provider, liferafts and HRUs in date, lifejackets and immersion suits.<\/li><li>FFE: fixed systems pressures and cylinders, fire pumps, emergency fire pump, detection system, extinguishers.<\/li><li>Steering gear, anchors and windlass, mooring winches and lines, which may have deteriorated.<\/li><li>Navigation: ECDIS charts and permits updated, gyro started and settled, magnetic compass deviation check, radar, AIS, VDR.<\/li><li>GMDSS: batteries, EPIRB and SART dates, licences.<\/li><li>Hatch covers, seals and drainage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Fresh water and hygiene<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Drain, clean and chlorinate the tanks and flush the system. Legionella is a real risk after months of stagnant water.<\/li><li>Provisions: discard old stock.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Fuel<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Fuel quality, water and sludge in the tanks after months; fuel samples tested.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. People<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The whole crew is new, including the Master. Every person gets familiarisation under ISM 6.3 and STCW A-VI/1 before being given duties.<\/li><li>Hold drills before sailing, because nobody knows the ship.<\/li><li>Manning to the Safe Manning Document, with rested crew. Rest hours are at risk during reactivation.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Trials<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Engine and steering trials alongside, then sea trials if the company or class requires.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>I record everything, report readiness to the Master and the company, and we only sail when certificates, equipment and crew are ready. The examiner wants enclosed spaces, survey status, Legionella and full familiarisation.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What would you test first?","pa":"<p>Anything that keeps people alive or the ship safe: fire detection, fire pumps, emergency generator, steering, LSA.<\/p>","asked":["New company put you on laid up vessel which is in layoff from last 8 month, how will you join?","How will the lay up affect certification?","A lot of cruise ships have been laid up in port for a while, what requirements might they not be fulfilling?"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",3],["James Gilbert",1]],"yrs":"2021–2024"},{"id":"Q209","sec":0,"q":"What documents and records do you take over from the outgoing C/O?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["SA"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>I work through the company&#x27;s handover checklist. I cover five groups, and I don&#x27;t sign until I&#x27;ve seen each one.<\/p><p><strong>1. Certificates and survey status<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The certificates file and expiry dates.<\/li><li>The class survey status report: surveys due and conditions of class.<\/li><li>The last PSC, flag and audit reports, and open non-conformities and deficiencies.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Plans and manuals<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>GA and capacity plan.<\/li><li>Approved stability booklet, damage stability information, and the loading computer manual with the last test record.<\/li><li>Cargo Securing Manual and the lashing software.<\/li><li>Fire control plan and safety plan.<\/li><li>Docking plan.<\/li><li>Ballast Water Management Plan, Garbage Management Plan, and the SOPEP if it&#x27;s delegated to me.<\/li><li>Training manual and the fire safety operational booklet.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Record books<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Garbage Record Book.<\/li><li>Ballast Water Record Book.<\/li><li>Deck logbook and my own C/O&#x27;s log.<\/li><li>Register of lifting appliances and cargo gear, with test certificates.<\/li><li>Drill and training records.<\/li><li>Rest-hour records for the deck department.<\/li><li>ISPS records if I&#x27;m the SSO: security drills, DoS, security incident log.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The current stowage plan and loadicator condition.<\/li><li>The DG manifest and the DG Document of Compliance.<\/li><li>Mate&#x27;s receipts and any cargo claims or damage reports.<\/li><li>What&#x27;s loading next and any special cargo.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Maintenance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>PMS status: overdue jobs, especially critical equipment.<\/li><li>The defect list and outstanding repairs.<\/li><li>Spares and stores, including lashing gear inventory.<\/li><li>Examiners have failed candidates who forgot the maintenance list, so I always mention it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Also<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keys and access.<\/li><li>Permits to work in force.<\/li><li>Crew matters: who&#x27;s good, who needs watching, cadets&#x27; record books.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Close<\/strong><\/p><p>Then the outgoing C/O and I sign the handover checklist and I report to the Master. Anything missing I list and report, so it&#x27;s clear what I took over. The examiner wants to hear &quot;certificates, plans, record books, cargo, maintenance&quot; and that both of us sign.<\/p>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What would you want to know from an outgoing chief mate"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",1]],"yrs":"2022–2022"},{"id":"Q210","sec":0,"q":"What conventions and codes apply to your ship? What are GT, NT, DWT, lightship and displacement, and which certificate shows GT?","tier":"orange","rep":39,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Conventions on my container ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>SOLAS 1974 as amended: construction, fire, LSA, radio, navigation, cargo, ISM and ISPS.<\/li><li>MARPOL 73/78 with all six annexes: oil, noxious liquids, packaged harmful substances, sewage, garbage and air.<\/li><li>STCW 1978 as amended: training, certification and watchkeeping.<\/li><li>MLC 2006: seafarers&#x27; working and living conditions.<\/li><li>Load Line Convention 1966 and its 1988 Protocol.<\/li><li>Tonnage Convention 1969.<\/li><li>COLREG 1972.<\/li><li>Anti-Fouling Systems Convention 2001.<\/li><li>Ballast Water Management Convention 2004.<\/li><li>Also Civil Liability and Bunkers conventions for insurance, and the Nairobi Wreck Removal Convention.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Main codes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>ISM and ISPS, mandatory through SOLAS IX and XI-2.<\/li><li>LSA Code and FSS Code, under SOLAS III and II-2.<\/li><li>IMDG Code, under SOLAS VII.<\/li><li>CSS Code, Code of Safe Practice for Cargo Stowage and Securing, under SOLAS VI and VII.<\/li><li>IS Code 2008, intact stability.<\/li><li>Also the FTP Code, NOx Technical Code, and the Polar Code where relevant.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tonnages<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Gross tonnage<\/strong> is a measure of the ship&#x27;s total enclosed volume, calculated by a formula from the Tonnage Convention. It has no units. It&#x27;s used for manning, certification thresholds like 500 GT, registration, and dues.<\/li><li><strong>Net tonnage<\/strong> measures the volume of the cargo spaces, adjusted for passengers and draught. It&#x27;s used for port and canal dues. It can&#x27;t be less than 30% of GT.<\/li><li>Both are shown on the <strong>International Tonnage Certificate (1969)<\/strong>. Suez and Panama have their own tonnage certificates.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Weights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Displacement<\/strong> is the total mass of the ship as she floats, equal to the mass of water displaced.<\/li><li><strong>Lightship<\/strong> is the mass of the ship complete and ready for service, with machinery and water in the boilers, but no cargo, fuel, ballast, fresh water, stores, crew or effects.<\/li><li><strong>Deadweight<\/strong> is everything she can carry: cargo, fuel, ballast, fresh water, stores, crew and effects, and the constant.<\/li><li>So <strong>displacement = lightship + deadweight<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The examiner often checks one point: GT and NT are volumes, not weights. Deadweight and displacement are weights in tonnes.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What is Deadweight ?","What is Dwt and its uses?","What is Displacement ?","Draught surveys, checking density and confirming displacement.","What happens to displacement?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",8],["Examiner not named",6],["Ahsan Khan",5],["Zia Ul Haq",4],["James Steer",4]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q211","sec":0,"q":"Before arrival (3 hours / 3 days out), what does the C/O prepare?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["GMA"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Three days out<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Arrival condition:<\/strong> agree it with the Master. Arrival draughts and trim, air draught, GM, shear forces and bending moments, and any port draught or air draught limits.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo plan:<\/strong> exchange it with the planners and the terminal. Check the discharge and load sequence for stability and stress at each stage, not just the final condition. Note restows, DG, reefers, OOG and special cargo.<\/li><li><strong>Ballast plan:<\/strong> plan ballast operations for arrival and cargo, and check the port&#x27;s ballast water rules and any BWMS requirement or reporting.<\/li><li><strong>Work and rest:<\/strong> plan the deck work so the crew arrive rested. Port is the busiest time, so I avoid heavy work the day before arrival.<\/li><li><strong>Notifications through the agent:<\/strong> MARPOL waste delivery advance notification, ISPS pre-arrival information, DG notification, crew list and health declaration.<\/li><li><strong>Lashing:<\/strong> prepare to unlash for the first discharge bays, and make sure lashing gear is available and serviceable for the loading.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Three hours out<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Mooring and anchors:<\/strong> winches tested, lines and messengers ready, anchors cleared and ready to let go, mooring stations briefed.<\/li><li><strong>Pilot ladder:<\/strong> rigged to SOLAS V/23 and the IMPA guidance, lit at night, lifebuoy with light, heaving line, and an officer to meet the pilot.<\/li><li><strong>Gangway and safety net<\/strong> ready to rig.<\/li><li><strong>Security:<\/strong> confirm the port security level, DoS if needed, gangway watch ready.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo gear:<\/strong> test our own cranes if we use them; hatch covers ready.<\/li><li><strong>Ballast:<\/strong> trim and draught for the berth.<\/li><li><strong>Engine room:<\/strong> fuel changeover if entering an ECA or the port requires it, and manoeuvring readiness.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>On arrival<\/strong><\/p><p>Read the draughts, check the dock water density, compare with the loadicator, and set up the deck and security watch.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Two things: the plan checked at every stage, and a rested crew arriving. If I can&#x27;t make the plan work, the Master and the company know three days out, not three hours out.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What are preparations you will do before arrival (Pre arrival checks & Tests)?"],"ex":[["Gaurav Minocha",1]],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q212","sec":1,"q":"What is the difference between a survey, an audit and an inspection? Which is the most stringent?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Survey<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>technical examination<\/strong> of the ship&#x27;s structure, machinery and equipment against the rules.<\/li><li>Statutory surveys under SOLAS, MARPOL and the Load Line Convention, done by the MCA or an RO, and class surveys.<\/li><li>The surveyor looks at the physical ship: plating thickness, hatch covers, LSA, FFE, machinery.<\/li><li>The result: the certificate is issued, endorsed, withheld or withdrawn, or a condition of class is set.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Audit<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>systematic check of a management system<\/strong>: ISM audits of the company and the ship, ISPS verifications, and the company&#x27;s own internal audits under ISM 12.<\/li><li>The auditor looks for <strong>objective evidence<\/strong> that we do what the SMS or SSP says: records, interviews, watching drills.<\/li><li>The result: a <strong>non-conformity<\/strong>, a <strong>major non-conformity<\/strong>, or an <strong>observation<\/strong>. Not a technical defect as such, although an auditor will report one if he sees it.<\/li><li>ISM 1.1.10 defines a major non-conformity: an identifiable deviation that poses a serious threat to the safety of personnel or the ship, or a serious risk to the environment, requiring immediate corrective action, including lack of effective and systematic implementation of a requirement of the Code.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Inspection<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>check of condition and compliance at a point in time<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Port State Control under the PSC Regs 2026 and MGN 711, flag State inspections, MLC inspections, vetting inspections, and our own internal inspections such as the Master&#x27;s weekly accommodation inspection.<\/li><li>The result: deficiencies with action codes, up to detention.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Which is most stringent?<\/strong><\/p><p>I&#x27;d say the <strong>survey<\/strong> is the most stringent for the ship&#x27;s physical condition, because it decides whether the certificate is valid and the ship can trade at all.<\/p><p>But I&#x27;d add:<\/p><ul><li>a <strong>major non-conformity<\/strong> at an ISM audit stops the SMC being issued or endorsed until it&#x27;s downgraded, so it can stop the ship too;<\/li><li>a <strong>PSC detention<\/strong> is the most immediate commercial consequence, and it&#x27;s public.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How they link<\/strong><\/p><p>A survey finding a defect the PMS should have caught becomes an ISM non-conformity. A PSC deficiency is often followed by an additional audit. They all feed into the ship&#x27;s risk profile.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Survey is technical against the rules, audit checks the system with objective evidence, inspection is a snapshot of condition. Then my opinion, with a reason.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What is the difference between survey, audit and inspection?"],"ex":[["Shiva Balasubramanian",2],["Ranjit Joseph",1],["Fazal Wadood",1]],"yrs":"2022–2024"},{"id":"Q213","sec":1,"q":"EAS — what is it, which ships qualify, what does the MCA still do?","tier":"yellow","rep":6,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["GMA"],"fail":null,"law":"EASS 2025","a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>Enhanced Authorisation Safety System (EASS)<\/strong>, guidance <strong>MGN 561 (M) Amd 1<\/strong>, applies from 1 April 2025. It replaced the older Enhanced Authorisation Scheme (EAS). It goes further than ACSS (MGN 568 Amd 1).<\/p><ul><li>Under <strong>ACSS<\/strong>, the RO does the statutory surveys, but the MCA keeps the company DOC, the ship&#x27;s SMC, the ISSC and the MLC certificate.<\/li><li>Under <strong>EASS<\/strong>, the RO also does the <strong>ISM SMC audits, ISSC verifications and MLC inspections<\/strong> on the MCA&#x27;s behalf. So almost all survey and certification work for that ship is delegated.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Which ships qualify<\/strong><\/p><p>Entry depends on risk and performance (verify the exact criteria in MGN 561):<\/p><ul><li>a good PSC record, with no recent detentions;<\/li><li>good flag inspection and class records;<\/li><li>the company&#x27;s ISM performance;<\/li><li>ship factors such as age and type;<\/li><li>a UK-authorised RO that has the delegation for EASS.<\/li><\/ul><p>Passenger ships are not normally eligible, and the MCA decides who joins.<\/p><p><strong>What the MCA still does<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Issues and audits the <strong>company&#x27;s ISM DOC<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Carries out <strong>flag State inspections<\/strong> at an interval based on the ship&#x27;s risk.<\/li><li>Grants <strong>exemptions, equivalents and dispensations<\/strong>, and approves things like the Safe Manning Document.<\/li><li>Investigates and enforces: detentions, prohibition notices, prosecutions.<\/li><li>Monitors the RO&#x27;s performance through audits.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Leaving the scheme<\/strong><\/p><p>A ship can be removed after a <strong>detention<\/strong>, a serious casualty or pollution incident, an <strong>expired certificate<\/strong>, repeated or unrectified deficiencies, or surveys not arranged on time. Class still reports deficiencies and conditions of class to the MCA.<\/p><p><strong>What it means for me as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I deal with the class surveyor and auditor for nearly everything, so the survey planning and the class survey status report become even more important.<\/li><li>A detention could move the ship out of the scheme and back to MCA surveys and audits, costing the company time and money.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MGN 561, EASS goes further than ACSS by delegating the SMC, ISSC and MLC to the RO; the MCA keeps the DOC, inspections and exemptions; and a detention or casualty can remove the ship. My old notes said &quot;EAS&quot; and &quot;ACS&quot;; the current names are EASS and ACSS.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q214","sec":1,"q":"What does a Certificate of Registry contain, how long is it valid, and how can you tell a ship's flag?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["GMA"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Merchant Shipping Act 1995 Part II and the Merchant Shipping (Registration of Ships) Regulations 1993, administered by the UK Ship Register in Cardiff.<\/p><p><strong>Parts of the UK Register<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Part I:<\/strong> merchant ships and pleasure vessels owned by qualified persons. My ship would be here.<\/li><li><strong>Part II:<\/strong> fishing vessels.<\/li><li><strong>Part III:<\/strong> small ships, the Small Ships Register.<\/li><li><strong>Part IV:<\/strong> bareboat-chartered ships, registered in the UK while bareboat-chartered to a UK charterer.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Validity<\/strong><\/p><p>For a Part I ship, the Certificate of Registry is valid for <strong>five years<\/strong>. Registration has to be renewed before expiry, or the ship is removed from the register.<\/p><p><strong>What it shows<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ship&#x27;s name and port of choice, the port of registry marked on the stern.<\/li><li>The official number and IMO number.<\/li><li>Type of ship, method of propulsion, year and place of build.<\/li><li>Main dimensions: length, breadth, depth.<\/li><li>Gross and net tonnage.<\/li><li>The registered owners and their shares; a ship is divided into 64 shares.<\/li><li>The date of registration and expiry.<\/li><\/ul><p>Mortgages are recorded on the register itself, not on the certificate. A buyer or bank checks the register.<\/p><p><strong>How to tell a ship&#x27;s flag<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>ensign<\/strong> she&#x27;s wearing. A UK merchant ship wears the Red Ensign.<\/li><li>Her <strong>port of registry<\/strong> on the stern.<\/li><li>The <strong>Certificate of Registry<\/strong> itself.<\/li><li>The <strong>Continuous Synopsis Record<\/strong>, which records the flag and its history.<\/li><li>The other certificates, all of which show the flag.<\/li><li>AIS and the IMO GISIS database will confirm it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Certificate of Registry is the proof of nationality. Under UNCLOS a ship has the nationality of the State whose flag she&#x27;s entitled to fly.<\/li><li>Without it the ship can be refused clearance or detained.<\/li><li>Flying a flag the ship isn&#x27;t entitled to, or two flags, makes her liable to be treated as a ship without nationality.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Change of registration<\/strong><\/p><p>On a change of owner, name or flag, the registration is closed or amended and the CSR is updated. The old certificate is returned to the Registry.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Five years, the main contents including official and IMO number and tonnages, and the ways to tell the flag. Mentioning the 64 shares and Part I shows real knowledge.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"You lose the CoR in a foreign port?","pa":"<p>Inform company and the RSS; consul; provisional/replacement certificate.<\/p>","asked":["How is the Certificate of Registry issued?"],"ex":[["Timothy Cummins",1],["Shahid Akhter",1],["Chris Roe",1],["Danny Hawkins",1],["Khairul Anam",1]],"yrs":"2023–2025"},{"id":"Q215","sec":1,"q":"What is the CSR? What's in it, who updates it, what happens on a change of flag?","tier":"orange","rep":23,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["GMA","MU"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Continuous Synopsis Record<\/strong> is required by <strong>SOLAS XI-1/5<\/strong>, introduced after 9/11 with the ISPS Code, applying to passenger ships and cargo ships of 500 GT and above on international voyages.<\/p><p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>An on-board record of the ship&#x27;s history, issued by the <strong>flag State<\/strong>. It lets a PSC or security officer see at a glance who has owned, operated and certified the ship.<\/p><p><strong>What&#x27;s in it<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The name of the flag State.<\/li><li>The date the ship was registered with that State.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s IMO number.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s name.<\/li><li>Port of registry.<\/li><li>Name and registered address of the registered owner.<\/li><li>Registered owner identification number.<\/li><li>Name and address of any bareboat charterer.<\/li><li>Name and address of the ISM company, and its identification number.<\/li><li>The classification society or societies.<\/li><li>Who issued the DOC, the SMC and the ISSC, and the name of the auditing body.<\/li><li>The date the ship stopped being registered with that flag, when it happens.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Who updates it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When any of these details changes, the <strong>company or Master<\/strong> fills in the amendment form (Form 2) straight away, and attaches it to the file.<\/li><li>The company tells the flag, which then issues a <strong>new numbered CSR document<\/strong> (Form 1) reflecting the change, normally within 3 months.<\/li><li>An index of amendments (Form 3) is kept.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rules<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Every document stays on board.<\/strong> The file is never broken up, and entries are never deleted or altered.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s in English, French or Spanish, and the official language of the flag.<\/li><li>It must be available for inspection at all times.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Change of flag<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The old flag closes its record by entering the date the ship left its register.<\/li><li>The <strong>whole file goes with the ship<\/strong> to the new flag. The old flag sends a copy of the CSR to the new flag.<\/li><li>The new flag issues the next CSR document, continuing the history.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>It stops a ship hiding her history by changing name, owner or flag. PSC and security officers check it against the certificates.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS XI-1/5, issued by the flag, what&#x27;s in it, the company or Master adds the amendment form, nothing removed, and on a change of flag the whole file stays with the ship.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What is a Continuous Synopsis Record?","What is a Continuous Synopsis Record and what are the forms?","What is CSR, what info can get from CSR, what happens when ship changes flag?","1 CSR, Part 2 Amendments, Part 3 List of Amendments)","Form 3 Index of Amendments to the CSR."],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",13],["James Steer",4],["Muhammad Iqbal",2],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",2],["Shahid Akhter",1]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q216","sec":1,"q":"Who may dismantle or repair critical LSA such as a davit or release gear? Who certifies it?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Who may dismantle or repair it<\/strong><\/p><p>Only the <strong>manufacturer<\/strong>, or a <strong>service provider authorised by the manufacturer and approved by the Administration<\/strong>, may carry out the thorough examination, operational testing, overhaul and repair of:<\/p><ul><li>lifeboats and rescue boats;<\/li><li>launching appliances: davits, winches, falls;<\/li><li>on-load and off-load release gear;<\/li><li>free-fall lifeboat release systems.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>References<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>IMO <strong>MSC.402(96)<\/strong>, mandatory under SOLAS III/20 since 1 January 2020: requirements for maintenance, thorough examination, operational testing, overhaul and repair.<\/li><li>In the UK, the LSA Regs 2020 and <strong>MGN 560 (M) Amd 2<\/strong> for maintenance, examination and testing, and <strong>MGN 555 (M) Amd 1<\/strong> for the authorisation of service providers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The intervals<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Weekly and monthly<\/strong> inspections: by the ship&#x27;s crew, recorded.<\/li><li><strong>Annual thorough examination and operational test:<\/strong> by the authorised service provider. This includes the release gear and the winch brake.<\/li><li><strong>5-yearly:<\/strong> overhaul of the release gear, a dynamic winch brake test, and the load test of the davit at <strong>1.1 times<\/strong> the safe working load.<\/li><li>Falls renewed at least every 5 years, or sooner if worn, and turned end for end at intervals.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who certifies it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>service provider<\/strong> issues a report and a certificate of the examination and test.<\/li><li>The class or MCA surveyor <strong>witnesses<\/strong> tests at the Safety Equipment survey and endorses the certificate. The surveyor doesn&#x27;t take the gear apart.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the crew may do<\/strong><\/p><p>The weekly and monthly checks, general maintenance and greasing as in the maker&#x27;s manual, and the drills. Not overhauling the hook or adjusting the release mechanism.<\/p><p><strong>The scenario: a yard worker starts stripping the davit<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Stop the job.<\/li><li>Check he&#x27;s an authorised service engineer: his company&#x27;s authorisation certificate from the manufacturer and the flag approval, and the work order from the company.<\/li><li>If he isn&#x27;t, he doesn&#x27;t touch it. Report it to the Master and the superintendent.<\/li><li>If he is, make sure there&#x27;s a permit to work, the boat is secured with the maintenance pendants, and the test is witnessed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it&#x27;s strict<\/strong><\/p><p>Accidents with lifeboat release gear and falls during drills killed many seafarers. That&#x27;s why MSC.402(96) and the authorisation system were brought in.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Manufacturer or authorised service provider only, MSC.402(96) and MGN 560, the annual and 5-yearly tests, the 1.1 load test, and that the surveyor witnesses, he doesn&#x27;t dismantle.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q217","sec":1,"q":"The owner and the ship's name change. Which certificates must be reissued, and what happens to validity and surveys?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["GMA"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>A change of owner and name affects nearly every document on board. I&#x27;d go through them in groups.<\/p><p><strong>1. Registration<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>New <strong>Certificate of Registry<\/strong> in the new owner&#x27;s and new name, or a new registration if the flag changes.<\/li><li>The <strong>CSR<\/strong> updated: the company or Master adds the amendment form, and the flag issues a new CSR document.<\/li><li>Name and port of registry changed on the stern and bow, and the name on the lifeboats, liferafts&#x27; identification, and lifebuoys.<\/li><li>AIS data, EPIRB registration and MMSI/radio licence updated.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. ISM and ISPS<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the <strong>managing company<\/strong> changes, ISM starts again. The new company needs a DOC for this ship type, and the ship gets an <strong>interim SMC<\/strong> (6 months), followed by an initial audit.<\/li><li>The same for the ISSC: <strong>interim ISSC<\/strong>, then the initial verification. A new ship security assessment and SSP may be needed.<\/li><li>If only the name changes and the manager stays the same, the SMC and ISSC are amended.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. MLC<\/strong><\/p><p>A new shipowner means a new <strong>DMLC Part II<\/strong> and an <strong>interim MLC certificate<\/strong>, valid up to 6 months, then the inspection. New SEAs or SEA amendments, and new financial security certificates for repatriation and abandonment.<\/p><p><strong>4. Statutory certificates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>SAFCON, SEC, SRC, Load Line, IOPP, IAPP, ISPP, IEE, BWM, AFS and IHM are <strong>reissued in the new name<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the flag doesn&#x27;t change, the <strong>survey dates carry on<\/strong> and the certificates&#x27; validity is the same.<\/li><li>If the flag changes, the new flag may require surveys before issuing its certificates, depending on the transfer arrangements.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Insurance certificates<\/strong><\/p><p>P&amp;I blue cards, and the State certificates for Bunkers, CLC if a tanker, and Wreck Removal, are reissued for the new owner.<\/p><p><strong>6. Other documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>International Tonnage Certificate reissued.<\/li><li>Safe Manning Document reissued.<\/li><li>Class certificate reissued and class notified.<\/li><li>Plans and manuals updated with the new name: SOPEP contacts, SSP contacts, the company emergency contacts, the BWM plan.<\/li><li>Suez and Panama certificates and the ship&#x27;s stamp.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Records<\/strong><\/p><p>OLB and logbook entries showing the change, and closing of the old log books if required.<\/p><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>Make a list with the superintendent, check every document as it arrives, and update the plans, notices and markings on deck.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The key point: a new company means interim ISM, ISPS and MLC certificates, while the survey dates of the statutory certificates carry on if the flag stays the same.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q218","sec":1,"q":"Where does a Chief Officer's authority to enforce safe working come from — e.g. a 2/E refuses to wear PPE, or the Bosun won't wear a harness?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The legal basis<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Merchant Shipping and Fishing Vessels (Health and Safety at Work) Regulations 1997<\/strong>, with guidance in <strong>MGN 636 (M+F) Amd 4<\/strong>. They put a duty on the employer to ensure, so far as reasonably practicable, the health and safety of workers. They also put a duty on <strong>every worker<\/strong> to take reasonable care of himself and others, to co-operate with the employer, and to use equipment and PPE as instructed.<\/li><li>Specific regulations add detail:<\/li><li>the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Personal Protective Equipment) Regulations 1999<\/strong>: the employer provides PPE, and the worker must use it;<\/li><li>the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Work at Height) Regulations 2010<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the LOLER and PUWER Regulations 2006 for lifting and work equipment;<\/li><li>the <strong>Entry into Enclosed Spaces Regulations 2022<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Code of Safe Working Practices for Merchant Seafarers (COSWP)<\/strong>, 2026 edition, MIN 688 Amd 5, shows how to comply. Following it is good evidence of compliance.<\/li><li>The <strong>SMS<\/strong> under ISM gives me the authority and the stop-work procedure, and the risk assessment says what PPE is required.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>These apply on a UK ship whether or not she&#x27;s big enough for ISM.<\/strong> They&#x27;re UK law, not company rules.<\/p><p><strong>What I do: 2/E refuses to wear PPE, or the Bosun won&#x27;t wear a harness aloft<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop the job.<\/strong> Nobody works aloft without fall protection. That&#x27;s my stop-work authority under the SMS and my duty under the regulations.<\/li><li><strong>Find out why.<\/strong> The harness may be uncomfortable or damaged, or the anchor point unsuitable. Sometimes the refusal is a real problem I can fix.<\/li><li><strong>Explain the risk and the law<\/strong> calmly: he&#x27;s breaking the law and putting himself at risk, and I can&#x27;t let the job continue.<\/li><li>If he still refuses, <strong>don&#x27;t let him do the job<\/strong>. Give it to someone else or postpone it.<\/li><li><strong>Report to the Master.<\/strong> For the 2/E, involve the Chief Engineer, his head of department.<\/li><li><strong>Discipline:<\/strong> deal with it under the company&#x27;s Code of Conduct, with a written warning if needed. A repeated or serious refusal can lead to dismissal.<\/li><li><strong>Record it<\/strong> in the safety records, and raise a near miss or non-conformity.<\/li><li>Discuss it at the next <strong>safety committee<\/strong> meeting, without naming and shaming, to see if others have the same problem.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The H&amp;S at Work Regs 1997 and the PPE and Work at Height Regs, COSWP, the duty on the worker as well as the employer, and that I stop the job. Saying &quot;the company rule&quot; alone isn&#x27;t enough; say the law.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q219","sec":1,"q":"How did ISM become international and UK law?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"ISM Regs 2026","a":"<p><strong>International history<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The ISM Code came out of a series of disasters in the late 1980s where poor management, not just poor seamanship, was the cause:<\/li><li><strong>Herald of Free Enterprise<\/strong>, 1987, capsized leaving Zeebrugge with her bow doors open. The Sheen inquiry found &quot;the disease of sloppiness&quot; ran from the shore management down.<\/li><li><strong>Scandinavian Star<\/strong>, 1990, a passenger ferry fire with 159 deaths, where the crew was unfamiliar with the ship and couldn&#x27;t communicate.<\/li><li>IMO adopted guidelines in 1989, then the <strong>ISM Code by Resolution A.741(18)<\/strong> in <strong>1993<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It became <strong>mandatory through SOLAS Chapter IX<\/strong>, adopted in 1994:<\/li><li><strong>1 July 1998<\/strong> for passenger ships, oil and chemical tankers, gas carriers, bulk carriers and high-speed cargo craft of 500 GT and above;<\/li><li><strong>1 July 2002<\/strong> for other cargo ships and mobile offshore drilling units of 500 GT and above.<\/li><li>The Code has been amended several times since, for example in 2010 and 2015.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>UK law<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Today it&#x27;s given effect by the <strong>Merchant Shipping (International Safety Management (ISM) Code) Regulations 2026<\/strong>, in force <strong>31 March 2026<\/strong>, with guidance in <strong>MGN 708 (M)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>They replaced the <strong>2014 Regulations<\/strong> and the retained <strong>EC Regulation 336/2006<\/strong>, which had extended ISM to some domestic ships.<\/li><li>The MCA audits UK companies for the DOC and ships for the SMC, or authorises ROs to do the ship audits under EASS.<\/li><li>Offences: operating a ship without a valid DOC and SMC, or failing to comply with the SMS, are offences for the company and, in some cases, the Master.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Key sections the examiner likes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>1.2: objectives.<\/li><li>1.4: functional requirements.<\/li><li>4: the DPA.<\/li><li>5: Master&#x27;s responsibility and authority, and 5.2, the Master&#x27;s <strong>overriding authority<\/strong>.<\/li><li>6.3: familiarisation.<\/li><li>9: reporting non-conformities, accidents and hazardous occurrences.<\/li><li>10: maintenance and critical equipment.<\/li><li>12: internal audits and reviews.<\/li><li>13 and 14: certification and interim certificates.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Herald and Scandinavian Star, A.741(18), SOLAS IX, 1998 and 2002, and the new UK ISM Regs 2026 with MGN 708. Quoting the 2014 Regs is now out of date.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q220","sec":1,"q":"A critical piece of equipment fails and there's no spare / several PMS jobs are overdue and you can't do them all. What do you do?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>ISM 10.3 requires the company to identify <strong>critical equipment and technical systems<\/strong> whose sudden failure could cause a hazardous situation, and to have specific measures to make them reliable, including regular testing of standby arrangements. Examples: steering gear, main engine and its controls, emergency generator and switchboard, fire detection and fire pumps, fixed fire-fighting systems, navigation equipment, mooring winches.<\/p><p><strong>A critical item fails and there&#x27;s no spare<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Make the ship safe first.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Change over to the standby or redundant system, for example the second steering pump or the other radar.<\/li><li>Put in temporary measures: extra watches, manual operation, a portable replacement.<\/li><li>Do a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> with the Master and the Chief Engineer: can we operate safely, and with what limits?<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Report it.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>To the Master and the company through the defect report, with a request for the spare and a technician.<\/li><li>Raise a <strong>non-conformity<\/strong> under ISM 9.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Report outside the ship if needed.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>If it affects a certificate or safety equipment, the MCA and class under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>We may need a condition of class, a short-term dispensation, or permission to sail to a repair port.<\/li><li>If we&#x27;re in a foreign port, the port State may need to know.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Record it<\/strong> in the logs and the PMS, including the interim measures.<\/li><li><strong>Repair<\/strong> at the next port, test it and record the test.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Several PMS jobs overdue and I can&#x27;t do them all<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Prioritise<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>safety-critical and statutory items first: LSA, FFE, steering, emergency power, closing appliances;<\/li><li>then items affecting cargo, such as hatch covers and lashing gear;<\/li><li>then general maintenance.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Re-plan the rest<\/strong> with the company and agree new dates.<\/li><li><strong>Record each postponement<\/strong> formally in the PMS with the reason and the approval. That&#x27;s an honest record.<\/li><li><strong>Ask for help<\/strong>: extra crew, riding squads or shore contractors, or time in port.<\/li><li><strong>Never sign off a job that wasn&#x27;t done.<\/strong> Falsifying PMS records is a serious non-conformity and can be a criminal offence after an accident.<\/li><li>Look at the <strong>root cause<\/strong>: too few crew, too much port time, poor planning, or an unrealistic PMS. Report it in the Master&#x27;s review.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>ISM 10.3 critical equipment, make safe and risk-assess, report to the company and under SOLAS I/11(c) if it affects a certificate, prioritise safety-critical jobs, record postponements honestly, and never falsify.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What if your critical equipment fails?"],"ex":[["Timothy Cummins",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"Q221","sec":4,"q":"Explain how a ship obtains its ISSC from scratch. What surveys follow, and what security certificates must the crew hold?","tier":"orange","rep":25,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["GMA"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Step by step from scratch<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Ship Security Assessment (SSA)<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Carried out by the <strong>company<\/strong>, through the CSO, or by a <strong>recognised security organisation<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Includes an <strong>on-scene security survey<\/strong> of the ship.<\/li><li>Identifies the ship&#x27;s security measures, key operations, possible threats, and weaknesses, including the human factors.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Ship Security Plan (SSP)<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Written from the SSA, covering the measures at each security level, restricted areas, access control, cargo and stores, communication, drills, records, the SSAS, and the duties of the CSO and SSO.<\/li><li><strong>Submitted to the MCA<\/strong> for <strong>approval<\/strong>. The approved plan is kept on board.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>People and equipment<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>CSO<\/strong> designated by the company.<\/li><li><strong>SSO<\/strong> appointed on board, with his <strong>STCW VI/5<\/strong> certificate.<\/li><li><strong>SSAS<\/strong> fitted and tested (SOLAS XI-2/6).<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Interim ISSC<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>When the plan is on board, the SSO appointed, the crew familiar with it and the SSAS working, the MCA can issue an <strong>interim ISSC<\/strong>, valid up to <strong>6 months<\/strong>, for a new ship, change of flag or change of company.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Initial verification<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>After the plan has been operating long enough to produce evidence, the <strong>initial verification<\/strong> is carried out, and a full <strong>ISSC<\/strong>, valid for <strong>5 years<\/strong>, is issued.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Later verifications<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>An <strong>intermediate verification<\/strong> between the <strong>2nd and 3rd<\/strong> anniversaries.<\/li><li>A <strong>renewal verification<\/strong> before expiry.<\/li><li><strong>Additional<\/strong> verifications if the flag requires, for example after a security incident.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the auditor checks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The SSP is approved, on board and implemented.<\/li><li><strong>SSAS<\/strong> test records.<\/li><li><strong>LRIT<\/strong> conformance (SOLAS V/19-1) and <strong>AIS<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Drills every 3 months<\/strong> and the <strong>annual exercise<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Records of security levels, DoS and the last 10 port calls.<\/li><li>Crew knowledge of their duties.<\/li><li>Access control and restricted areas.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Crew security certificates under STCW<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SSO certificate<\/strong> (STCW VI/5) for the SSO.<\/li><li><strong>Designated security duties<\/strong> (VI/6.2) for those with security roles, such as the gangway watch and the search parties.<\/li><li><strong>Security awareness<\/strong> (VI/6.1) for <strong>everyone else<\/strong>.<\/li><li>And <strong>security familiarisation<\/strong> for every new joiner before duties.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other documents<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>CSR<\/strong>, the IMO number marked on the hull and superstructure, and the ship&#x27;s contact details for the CSO.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SSA, SSP approved by the MCA, CSO, SSO and SSAS, interim ISSC 6 months, initial verification and a 5-year ISSC, intermediate between the 2nd and 3rd anniversaries, and the three STCW security certificates.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Prep for ISPS Audit, what documents would you have?","Procedure to get SMC, type of survey, Validity?","What is the validity of the ISSC?","Who conducts the annual survey of ISSC?","Administration / Government issued DOC, SMC & ISSC"],"ex":[["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",9],["Zia Ul Haq",6],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",3],["Examiner not named",2],["Bill Bennett",2]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q222","sec":4,"q":"During Master's rounds a suspicious package is found in an AB's cabin. Actions?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Treat it as a possible explosive device<\/strong> until proven otherwise.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Don&#x27;t touch or move it<\/strong>, don&#x27;t open it, and don&#x27;t cover it.<\/li><li><strong>Clear and cordon<\/strong> the area. Evacuate the nearby cabins and the deck above and below if possible.<\/li><li><strong>No radios or mobile phones<\/strong> near it, because a radio transmission can set off some devices. Use the ship&#x27;s phones from a safe distance.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong> at once. Note the package&#x27;s appearance, size, location and any markings, wires, smell or sound, without going closer.<\/li><li><strong>Close fire doors<\/strong> and stop ventilation to the area if it can be done safely, and get the <strong>fire party<\/strong> ready.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Follow the SSP procedure for a suspected device<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Raise the security level<\/strong> if directed by the flag or the port State, or apply higher-level measures.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the CSO<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>In port<\/strong>: the <strong>PFSO<\/strong> and the <strong>police<\/strong> or the port&#x27;s bomb disposal team, through the PFSO. Stop cargo work near the area. Consider evacuating the ship except for essential crew.<\/li><li><strong>At sea<\/strong>: the <strong>coastguard<\/strong> of the nearest coastal State and the <strong>flag<\/strong>, through the CSO. Get expert advice by phone. Consider proceeding to a place where help can reach the ship.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t let the crew gather on deck near the area.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Search<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>controlled search<\/strong> of the whole ship using the <strong>SSP search plan<\/strong>: there may be more than one device.<\/li><li>Search teams told not to touch anything suspicious, just report it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The cabin occupant<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Interview<\/strong> the AB <strong>with a witness<\/strong>: does he know what it is? Where did it come from? Did someone give it to him?<\/li><li>Treat him fairly. He may know nothing about it, or it may be an innocent package.<\/li><li>If it turns out to be drugs or contraband, keep the chain of custody and inform the authorities.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Everything recorded in the <strong>security records<\/strong> and the <strong>OLB<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A security incident report to the CSO and the flag.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A debrief, and review of how it got on board: access control, stores and baggage checks.<\/li><li>Consider a security drill on suspicious items.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Treat as a device, don&#x27;t touch, cordon and clear, no radios, inform the Master, follow the SSP: security level, CSO, PFSO and police in port or coastguard and flag at sea, a controlled search for more, interview the occupant with a witness, and records.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Suspicious package found in AB cabin during master's rounds, actions?"],"ex":[["Sam Twitchin",1]],"yrs":"2022–2022"},{"id":"Q223","sec":4,"q":"You're not happy with a company SMS procedure (e.g. fire-fighting). How do you get it changed?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>I wouldn&#x27;t just ignore it.<\/strong> Ignoring a procedure, even a bad one, is a non-conformity and can be dangerous if others are still following it.<\/p><p><strong>The steps<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Be clear what&#x27;s wrong.<\/strong> Is it unsafe, impractical, out of date, or just different from how I&#x27;d do it? Gather evidence: what happened in the last drill, what the equipment maker says, what COSWP or the regulations say.<\/li><li><strong>Raise it with the Master.<\/strong> Discuss it, and let the other officers and the Chief Engineer comment, because a fire-fighting procedure involves everyone.<\/li><li><strong>Discuss it at the safety committee.<\/strong> The crew may have useful views, and it shows the system working.<\/li><li>If we agree it&#x27;s wrong, the <strong>Master sends it to the company<\/strong> as part of his duty to <strong>review the SMS and report its deficiencies<\/strong> to shore management (<strong>ISM 5.1.5<\/strong>). It goes through the <strong>SMS change request<\/strong> or feedback form to the <strong>DPA<\/strong>, with a proposed new wording and the reasons.<\/li><li><strong>Meanwhile<\/strong>, if there&#x27;s a <strong>risk<\/strong>, we put an <strong>interim measure<\/strong> in place under a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>, approved by the Master and recorded. For example, an extra step in the fire-fighting procedure or a different muster point. The company should be told about it.<\/li><li><strong>The company<\/strong> reviews it, consults its technical people and other ships if needed, approves or rejects it, and tells us why.<\/li><li>When the <strong>revised procedure is issued<\/strong>, we replace the pages, <strong>remove the old ones<\/strong> (document control under ISM 11), and <strong>brief the crew<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Test it<\/strong> in the next drill, and feed back whether it works.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If the company refuses and I still think it&#x27;s unsafe<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I&#x27;d ask for the reasons in writing.<\/li><li>The Master can still use his <strong>overriding authority<\/strong> (ISM 5.2) for safety at the time, and record why.<\/li><li>The issue can be raised at the <strong>internal audit<\/strong> or the <strong>management review<\/strong> (ISM 12).<\/li><li>The DPA has direct access to the highest level of management, so it can go to the top.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>An example<\/strong><\/p><p>A fire-fighting procedure that says to release CO₂ into the engine room after 5 minutes without a full head count. I&#x27;d propose adding a mandatory head count and confirmation that the engine room is evacuated before release, and the closing of ventilation and fuel quick-closing valves.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Not ignoring it, raising it with the Master and the safety committee, the Master&#x27;s SMS review under ISM 5.1.5 through the DPA, interim measures with a risk assessment, document control when it&#x27;s changed, and briefing and testing the new procedure.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q224","sec":4,"q":"How would an ISM auditor (or you) judge whether there's a real safety culture on board?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>He&#x27;d <strong>look past the paperwork<\/strong> for evidence that people actually work safely. A ship can have perfect records and a poor safety culture.<\/p><p><strong>What he&#x27;d look for<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Near-miss reports<\/strong>: their <strong>number and quality<\/strong>. Few reports usually means people <strong>aren&#x27;t reporting<\/strong>, not that nothing happens. Good reports describe what happened and why, not just &quot;slipped, no injury&quot;.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessments and permits<\/strong> that <strong>match the actual job<\/strong>: the right hazards, the right people, signed at the time. Generic, copied or pre-signed ones are a bad sign.<\/li><li><strong>Drills<\/strong> that are <strong>realistic<\/strong>, with <strong>debriefs<\/strong> and <strong>lessons learned<\/strong>, not the same scenario in the same place every month.<\/li><li><strong>Crew who can explain the procedures<\/strong> in their own words: a rating who knows what to do if he finds a fire, or how to enter an enclosed space, not just the officers.<\/li><li><strong>Stop-work authority<\/strong> actually being used, and people <strong>not punished<\/strong> for using it.<\/li><li><strong>Non-conformities closed<\/strong> with <strong>real corrective actions<\/strong> and root causes, not just &quot;crew reminded&quot;.<\/li><li><strong>The Master&#x27;s review<\/strong> and <strong>safety committee minutes<\/strong> acted on: issues raised, actions taken, feedback given.<\/li><li><strong>Rest hours<\/strong> that look <strong>real<\/strong>, with variations matching the work, not perfect 10s every day.<\/li><li><strong>Housekeeping and PPE<\/strong> on deck when nobody is expecting a visitor.<\/li><li><strong>Officers&#x27; behaviour<\/strong>: do they follow the rules themselves? Do they listen to the crew?<\/li><li><strong>How defects are handled<\/strong>: reported promptly and fixed, or hidden until an inspection.<\/li><li><strong>Openness<\/strong>: do people talk honestly to him, or does everyone give the same rehearsed answer?<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How I&#x27;d judge it myself as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>The same things, plus:<\/p><ul><li>Watch a <strong>job in progress<\/strong> without warning: are the permit conditions really being followed?<\/li><li>Ask the crew in private what they think the <strong>biggest risk<\/strong> on board is, and whether they&#x27;d feel able to stop a job.<\/li><li>See whether <strong>lessons<\/strong> from fleet alerts and MAIB reports actually change anything.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Indicators<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Leading<\/strong>: near misses, safety observations, drills and training done, defects closed on time.<\/li><li><strong>Lagging<\/strong>: injuries, accidents, PSC deficiencies, audit NCs.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Concrete evidence beyond paperwork: near-miss reporting, real risk assessments, realistic drills, crew understanding, stop-work used, NCs closed properly, the Master&#x27;s review and safety committee acted on, and honest rest hours.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q225","sec":3,"q":"How does MLC affect you as C/O — as a seafarer and as a manager? How do you make sure the ship complies?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>As a seafarer, MLC gives me rights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A signed <strong>SEA<\/strong>, with wages paid at least monthly and a monthly account.<\/li><li><strong>Hours of rest<\/strong>: 10 in 24 and 77 in 7 days.<\/li><li><strong>Paid annual leave<\/strong> and <strong>shore leave<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Repatriation<\/strong> at the owner&#x27;s cost, and no more than 12 months on board.<\/li><li>Decent <strong>accommodation<\/strong>, <strong>food and water<\/strong> free of charge, recreation and internet access.<\/li><li><strong>Medical care<\/strong> on board and ashore, sick pay, and <strong>social security<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Health and safety<\/strong> protection.<\/li><li>Access to the <strong>on-board complaint procedure<\/strong> and to the flag State or PSC.<\/li><li>Protection against <strong>harassment and bullying<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Financial security<\/strong> for abandonment and for death or long-term disability.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>As a manager, I&#x27;m one of the people who make the ship comply<\/strong><\/p><p>In practice that means:<\/p><ol><li><strong>Hours of rest<\/strong>: keeping and checking the <strong>rest-hour records<\/strong> for the deck department, against the real work.<\/li><li><strong>Work planning<\/strong>: planning the work so no one breaches the limits, especially around port calls.<\/li><li><strong>Inspections<\/strong>: the <strong>weekly accommodation and galley inspections<\/strong> with the Master, recorded in the OLB.<\/li><li><strong>Complaints<\/strong>: handling them <strong>fairly<\/strong> and quickly through the on-board procedure, without victimisation.<\/li><li><strong>Young persons<\/strong>: following the <strong>MSN 1838<\/strong> rules for cadets under 18.<\/li><li><strong>Safety<\/strong>: leading on safety as <strong>safety officer<\/strong> under the Health and Safety at Work Regs 1997, with risk assessments and the safety committee.<\/li><li><strong>Welfare<\/strong>: shore leave where possible, internet, recreation, and spotting fatigue, stress and bullying.<\/li><li><strong>Documents<\/strong>: checking SEAs, ENG1s and certificates of the deck crew when they join.<\/li><li><strong>Training and familiarisation<\/strong>: new joiners familiarised before duties.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How the ship shows it complies<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Maritime Labour Certificate<\/strong> and <strong>DMLC Parts I and II<\/strong>, posted on board.<\/li><li>The records: SEAs, wage accounts, rest hours, inspections, complaints, safety committee minutes.<\/li><li>Inspected against the <strong>16 areas<\/strong> in MSN 1848 (M) Amd 4, by the MCA or RO, and by PSC abroad.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I make sure we comply<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I use the DMLC Part II as a checklist of what the company has said we do.<\/li><li>I check the records monthly with the Master before an inspection finds a problem.<\/li><li>I talk to the crew, because they&#x27;ll tell a PSC officer what they won&#x27;t tell me.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Both sides: my rights as a seafarer, and my management role in making the ship comply, with specific examples such as rest hours, inspections, complaints and young persons, and how compliance is shown by the certificate and DMLC.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["How does MLC affect you as a seafarer on board?"],"ex":[["Michele Moro",1],["Jason Pateman",1]],"yrs":"2022–2024"},{"id":"Q226","sec":3,"q":"A seafarer is landed sick and can't rejoin. What does the company owe him, and does it end when he gets home?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>MLC Regulation 4.2 and Standard A4.2.1<\/strong>, shipowners&#x27; liability, and in the UK the MLC regulations on shipowners&#x27; liability for sick and injured seafarers (check the UK detail).<\/p><p><strong>What the company owes him<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Medical care<\/strong>: the cost of medical care, including treatment, medicines and therapeutic appliances, from the <strong>start of the engagement<\/strong> until he&#x27;s <strong>repatriated<\/strong>, or until the date of repatriation if the sickness or injury arose during employment.<\/li><li><strong>Board and lodging<\/strong> away from home until he has <strong>recovered<\/strong>, or until the sickness or incapacity has been declared <strong>permanent<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Wages<\/strong>: full wages while he remains <strong>on board<\/strong> or until he&#x27;s repatriated.<\/li><li><strong>Sick pay after he&#x27;s left the ship<\/strong>: wages in whole or in part, from the time he&#x27;s landed, for a period national law can limit to <strong>not less than 16 weeks<\/strong> from the day of the injury or the start of the sickness (check the UK detail).<\/li><li><strong>Repatriation<\/strong>: at the shipowner&#x27;s cost, under MLC Standard A2.5.1, when he&#x27;s fit to travel. That includes travel, accommodation and food during the journey, and medical care if needed.<\/li><li><strong>Death or long-term disability<\/strong>: compensation under the contract or national law, backed by the <strong>financial security certificate<\/strong> for shipowners&#x27; liability (A4.2.1), which must be on board. The 2014 amendments made this mandatory.<\/li><li><strong>Funeral expenses<\/strong> if he dies on board or ashore during the engagement.<\/li><li><strong>Safeguarding his property<\/strong> left on board, and returning it to him.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Does it end when he gets home? No.<\/strong><\/p><p>The company&#x27;s responsibility continues:<\/p><ul><li>sick pay for the period set by law and the SEA or CBA;<\/li><li>medical care until he recovers or the condition is declared permanent, within the national limits;<\/li><li>compensation for permanent disability.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Exceptions<\/strong><\/p><p>The shipowner isn&#x27;t liable for:<\/p><ul><li>injury not incurred in the service of the ship;<\/li><li>injury or sickness due to the <strong>wilful misconduct<\/strong> of the seafarer;<\/li><li>sickness or infirmity <strong>deliberately concealed<\/strong> when he signed on.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I do on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Get him medical care quickly, with TMAS advice.<\/li><li>Record the illness or injury in the <strong>OLB<\/strong> and the medical log.<\/li><li>Arrange landing through the <strong>agent<\/strong> and the <strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>, who usually handle the medical costs and claims.<\/li><li>Pack and inventory his property.<\/li><li>Tell the company so his sick pay and repatriation are arranged.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MLC 4.2, medical care, board and lodging, wages to repatriation, sick pay for at least 16 weeks, repatriation, financial security for death and disability, and that it doesn&#x27;t end when he gets home.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q227","sec":3,"q":"A 17-year-old cadet is joining — at the yard / in dry dock / with you as C/O. What do you do?","tier":"red","rep":122,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"MSN 1838 Amd 2","a":"<p><strong>First thought:<\/strong> ideally I&#x27;d ask the company whether he could join <strong>after the dock<\/strong>, because a dry dock is one of the most dangerous places for an inexperienced person: hot work, open holes, scaffolding, heavy lifts, enclosed spaces, shore workers everywhere. If he&#x27;s already joining, I plan it carefully.<\/p><p><strong>Reference:<\/strong> <strong>MSN 1838 (M) Amd 2<\/strong> and the MS&amp;FV (Health and Safety at Work) (Employment of Young Persons) Regulations 1998, with MLC Regulation 1.1.<\/p><p><strong>Before he starts work<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> specific to him: his <strong>inexperience<\/strong>, lack of awareness of risks, and immaturity, in the dock environment.<\/li><li>I <strong>tell him the findings<\/strong>.<\/li><li>He can&#x27;t do <strong>hazardous work<\/strong> unless it&#x27;s essential for his training and he&#x27;s <strong>supervised by a competent person<\/strong>: enclosed spaces, heavy lifting, dangerous machinery, working aloft or overside, hot work, or anything alone in the dock.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>His rest<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>12 hours in every 24<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Two days&#x27; rest a week<\/strong>, consecutive if possible (some examiners accept &quot;may be reduced, but not below 36 consecutive hours&quot;; verify in MSN 1838 before quoting).<\/li><li>A <strong>30-minute break<\/strong> if he works more than <strong>4½ hours<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>No night work<\/strong>, meaning a 9-hour period including midnight to 0500, except under an <strong>approved training programme<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>ENG1<\/strong>, valid for 1 year while he&#x27;s under 18.<\/li><li>His <strong>training record book<\/strong> and his <strong>training agreement<\/strong> with the company or college.<\/li><li>STCW basic safety training certificates.<\/li><li>He goes on the <strong>list of young persons<\/strong> with the crew list.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Joining<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Full <strong>safety and security familiarisation<\/strong> before any work, including the dock&#x27;s own safety rules, alarms and escape routes, which are different from the ship&#x27;s at sea.<\/li><li>A <strong>mentor or buddy<\/strong>: an experienced officer or AB.<\/li><li>He <strong>never works on deck alone<\/strong> in the dock.<\/li><li>Proper <strong>PPE<\/strong> in his size.<\/li><li>His <strong>daily work plan<\/strong> set by me, with jobs from his training record book.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Welfare<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A safe cabin. For a <strong>female cadet<\/strong>: her own cabin and sanitary facilities, and a clear briefing on the anti-harassment policy.<\/li><li>Contact with home, and someone to talk to.<\/li><li>No alcohol.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>As C/O on board generally<\/strong><\/p><p>I&#x27;m normally the <strong>shipboard training officer<\/strong>. I check his progress, sign his book, and make sure his rest hours are recorded and kept.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Ask whether he could join later; risk assessment first and explained; no hazardous work unless supervised training; 12 hours&#x27; rest, 2 days a week, no night work; ENG1, list of young persons; familiarisation and a buddy.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Young person Rest hour?","You have a 17-year-old cadet joining the vessel, what are your considerations?","Young cadet of 17 years is going to join in yard, how you will prepare him?","Young person is joining the vessel, how would you sign them on ?","Hours of rest over 18/ under 18?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",14],["Michele Moro",13],["Khademul Insan",10],["Muhammad Iqbal",9],["Doug Smith",9]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q228","sec":3,"q":"Crew have exceeded their rest hours; the charterer and port want you alongside now and are sending tugs. Or the dock is flooding and two crew are over their hours. What do you do?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The Hours of Work Regs 2018, <strong>MSN 1877 (M) Amd 2<\/strong>, and <strong>STCW A-VIII/1<\/strong>. The minimum rest hours can only be broken for the <strong>immediate safety<\/strong> of the ship, people or cargo, or to help others in distress. The MCA is clear that a <strong>planned pilotage<\/strong> or the <strong>normal workload of a scheduled port call<\/strong> is <strong>not<\/strong> an emergency.<\/p><p><strong>The tugs and the berth<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Tell the Master<\/strong> clearly that the crew aren&#x27;t fit to safely handle the ship in. Give him the facts: who is over their hours, by how much, and how long they need.<\/li><li><strong>The Master&#x27;s decision<\/strong>: as Master, I&#x27;d use my <strong>overriding authority<\/strong> under ISM 5.2 and SOLAS V/34-1 to <strong>wait at anchor<\/strong> or drift safely until the crew have had their rest.<\/li><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the charterer, agent, port and company, in writing. The tugs can wait or be re-booked.<\/li><li><strong>Commercial pressure doesn&#x27;t override safety.<\/strong> If the charterer complains, the company and the P&amp;I club back the Master. Berthing with exhausted mooring crews is how people get killed by snapping lines.<\/li><li><strong>Alternatives<\/strong> that might help: use other crew who are rested, if they&#x27;re competent and within their own hours; reduce the number of people needed with the terminal&#x27;s line handlers; delay only the parts of the job that need the tired people.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Give <strong>compensatory rest<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Record the hours honestly<\/strong>. Never adjust the records to make them look compliant.<\/li><li>Look at <strong>why<\/strong> it happened: the watch arrangement, the port rotation, cargo work planning, short port stays. If the schedule can&#x27;t be met with the current crew, the company needs to know, and maybe more crew or a new SMD.<\/li><li>Raise an ISM non-conformity if the planning failed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The dry dock flooding<\/strong><\/p><p>Flooding the dock is a <strong>planned operation<\/strong>, not an emergency. So:<\/p><ul><li>I&#x27;d <strong>stop the two crew working<\/strong> and use others.<\/li><li>If there aren&#x27;t enough rested people, the flooding waits, or the yard provides more help.<\/li><li>A tired person during refloating, when tank soundings and stability matter and the ship could list, is a real risk.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When it would be an emergency<\/strong><\/p><p>If the ship were dragging her anchor towards danger, or there was a fire, I could suspend the rest schedule. Afterwards everyone gets compensatory rest, and it&#x27;s recorded.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Planned operations are not emergencies, the Master&#x27;s overriding authority to wait, inform the charterer and company, compensatory rest, honest records, and fix the cause.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q229","sec":3,"q":"A crew member's on-board complaint (no ventilation, no lights) is refused by the Master and the company. What next?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>MLC on-board complaint procedure<\/strong>, Regulation 5.1.5 and <strong>Standard A5.1.5<\/strong>, as set out in the company&#x27;s procedure and the DMLC Part II. And <strong>MLC Standard A3.1<\/strong>, which sets the accommodation standards, including <strong>ventilation<\/strong> and <strong>lighting<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The steps the seafarer has already taken<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Raised it with his <strong>head of department<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>company<\/strong> ashore.<\/li><\/ol><p>All of them have refused.<\/p><p><strong>What next: his rights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>He&#x27;s entitled to complain <strong>directly to the flag State<\/strong>, the <strong>MCA<\/strong>, using the contact details given to him with his SEA.<\/li><li>Or to a <strong>port State inspector<\/strong> in the next port. The PSC officer can inspect the accommodation and, if it&#x27;s serious, detain the ship (MLC A5.2.1).<\/li><li>Or to the authority in his <strong>country of residence<\/strong>.<\/li><li>He can also contact a <strong>seafarers&#x27; union<\/strong> such as Nautilus or the ITF, or a <strong>welfare organisation<\/strong> ashore, who will often take it to the authorities.<\/li><li>He <strong>must not be victimised<\/strong> for complaining, at any stage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My role as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the complaint and each response, with dates, and give him a copy.<\/li><li><strong>Advise him<\/strong> on his rights if he asks. The procedure should name a person on board who can give him confidential advice.<\/li><li><strong>Not discourage<\/strong> him or punish him.<\/li><li><strong>Look at the problem myself<\/strong>: a cabin without ventilation or lighting is a <strong>breach of the accommodation standards<\/strong> and a health and safety issue. In hot climates it can cause heat stress; without lighting, he can&#x27;t move safely at night.<\/li><li><strong>Try to fix it<\/strong>: a temporary light, a portable fan, or moving him to another cabin until it&#x27;s repaired. Often the refusal is about cost or time, and a practical fix is possible.<\/li><li>If I think the Master and company are wrong, I&#x27;d say so to the Master, and record my view.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An unresolved complaint about basic living conditions is exactly what PSC looks for.<\/li><li>It damages trust: if crew see complaints ignored, they stop reporting safety problems too.<\/li><li>It could become an MLC detention, which costs far more than a fan and a light fitting.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The MLC complaint procedure steps, his right to go directly to the MCA, PSC or his own country&#x27;s authority, unions and welfare organisations, no victimisation, records, and that I recognise it&#x27;s a breach of A3.1 and try to fix it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q230","sec":3,"q":"Who appoints the safety officer, who elects safety reps, who chairs the committee? What qualifies a safety rep? Prepare next Monday's safety committee agenda.","tier":"orange","rep":22,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>MS&amp;FV (Health and Safety at Work) Regulations 1997<\/strong>, as amended to include the safety officials provisions, with <strong>MGN 636 (M+F) Amd 4<\/strong> and the <strong>COSWP<\/strong> chapter on safety officials. It applies on ships with <strong>five or more<\/strong> seafarers (verify the regulation numbers).<\/p><p><strong>Who does what<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>company appoints the safety officer<\/strong>. It shouldn&#x27;t be the Master. Often the C/O.<\/li><li>The <strong>seafarers elect safety representatives<\/strong>: one rep for a small crew, more for a larger crew, and they can represent departments.<\/li><li>A safety rep needs at least <strong>two years&#x27; consecutive sea service<\/strong> since age 18, and for a tanker some of that on tankers (verify the detail).<\/li><li>The <strong>Master chairs<\/strong> the safety committee, which includes the safety officer and the reps, and others as needed.<\/li><li>All appointments are recorded in the <strong>OLB<\/strong> or the committee minutes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Next Monday&#x27;s agenda<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Opening<\/strong>: attendance, apologies.<\/li><li><strong>Minutes and actions<\/strong> from the last meeting: what&#x27;s been done, what&#x27;s still open, and why.<\/li><li><strong>Accidents, near misses and investigations<\/strong> since the last meeting: what happened, the root cause, the corrective action.<\/li><li><strong>Safety officer&#x27;s inspection<\/strong> findings: hazards found, defects reported, PMS items.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessments and permits<\/strong>: new or reviewed risk assessments, permits issued, any job where the procedure didn&#x27;t work.<\/li><li><strong>Drills and training<\/strong>: results and lessons from recent drills; training due; new joiners&#x27; familiarisation.<\/li><li><strong>Company circulars and fleet alerts<\/strong>, new equipment or procedures, and relevant <strong>MAIB<\/strong> or <strong>CHIRP<\/strong> reports.<\/li><li><strong>Health and welfare<\/strong>: rest hours, fatigue, food and water, recreation, internet, any welfare concerns.<\/li><li><strong>PPE<\/strong>: condition, sizes, replacement.<\/li><li><strong>Crew suggestions<\/strong>: ideas from the crew and the reps.<\/li><li><strong>Upcoming work<\/strong>: dry dock, port calls with special risks, heavy weather season.<\/li><li><strong>Any other business<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Date of next meeting<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>After the meeting<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Minutes<\/strong> written, signed by the Master, <strong>posted<\/strong> for the crew and <strong>sent to the company<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Actions given to named people with dates.<\/li><li>Anything the ship can&#x27;t fix goes to the company, with a reply expected.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I&#x27;d run it to make it useful<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Let the reps and ratings speak first, not just the officers.<\/li><li>Use a real example from our own ship.<\/li><li>Report back on the company&#x27;s response at the next meeting, so people see results.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Five or more crew, company appoints the safety officer, crew elect reps with two years&#x27; service, Master chairs, OLB records, and a practical agenda with minutes posted and sent to the company.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Who all are in the safety committee ?","Who appoints the Safety Officer?","What is safety representative?","Safety committee form and select representative"],"ex":[["Mahmood ul Hassan",7],["Shiva Balasubramanian",3],["Zahid Raza",3],["Timothy Cummins",3],["Nowshad Jobair",2]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q231","sec":3,"q":"As safety officer, how do you investigate an accident (e.g. AB broke a leg falling in a ballast tank)?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. The casualty and the scene<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Casualty first<\/strong>: first aid, stretcher recovery from the tank as an enclosed-space rescue, with BA if the atmosphere is in doubt. Don&#x27;t create a second casualty.<\/li><li><strong>TMAS<\/strong> advice, and arrange landing or evacuation.<\/li><li><strong>Make the area safe<\/strong>, then <strong>preserve the scene<\/strong>: don&#x27;t move equipment, ladders, lights or tools unless needed for safety. Photograph first.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Gather evidence, quickly<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Photographs<\/strong> and measurements: the ladder, the lighting, the tank condition, mud, rust scale, water.<\/li><li>The <strong>equipment<\/strong> involved: harness, lifeline, torch, footwear.<\/li><li>The <strong>permit to work<\/strong> and <strong>enclosed space entry permit<\/strong>, the atmosphere test results, the <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> and the toolbox talk record.<\/li><li><strong>Witness statements<\/strong>, taken <strong>separately<\/strong> and as soon as possible, in their own words.<\/li><li><strong>Hours of rest<\/strong> for the casualty and the team.<\/li><li>A <strong>drug and alcohol test<\/strong>, if the SMS requires it after an accident.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s motion, weather and time of day.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Find out what happened and why<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>sequence<\/strong> of events.<\/li><li>The <strong>root cause<\/strong>, not just &quot;he slipped&quot;. For example:<\/li><li>was the procedure followed?<\/li><li>was the procedure adequate? Was three points of contact possible?<\/li><li>was the ladder damaged or slippery? Was there enough lighting?<\/li><li>supervision: who was in charge?<\/li><li>training and familiarisation?<\/li><li>fatigue or time pressure?<\/li><li>Use a structured method, such as the company&#x27;s root-cause tool or the &quot;5 whys&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Report<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I write the <strong>company accident report<\/strong> (ISM 9).<\/li><li>The <strong>Master reports to the MAIB<\/strong> if it&#x27;s reportable. A fractured leg will usually be a <strong>serious injury<\/strong> under the 2026 Regulations and MGN 564 Amd 2, because he&#x27;ll be unable to do his usual duties for more than 72 hours.<\/li><li><strong>OLB<\/strong> entry of the injury and treatment.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> club informed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Recommendations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To the Master and the <strong>safety committee<\/strong>: for example, ladder repairs, better lighting, a rest platform, fall arrest on long vertical ladders, changes to the procedure.<\/li><li>Share the lessons with the fleet.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Restart<\/strong><\/p><p>The job restarts only after a <strong>new risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong>, and a briefing to everyone involved.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Casualty first, scene preserved, evidence including permits and statements, root cause not blame, company report, MAIB because it&#x27;s a serious injury, OLB, recommendations to the safety committee, and a new risk assessment before restarting.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q232","sec":3,"q":"How would you explain risk assessment to a cadet? What do you do for a job that isn't done regularly?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["SA"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>How I&#x27;d explain it to a cadet<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;Risk assessment is just <strong>organised common sense<\/strong>. Before any job, we ask five questions and write down the answers.&quot;<\/p><ol><li><strong>What could hurt someone?<\/strong> The <strong>hazards<\/strong>: height, moving machinery, electricity, chemicals, heat, ship motion, heavy loads, enclosed spaces.<\/li><li><strong>Who could be hurt, and how?<\/strong> The people doing the job, people nearby, people who come along later.<\/li><li><strong>How likely, and how bad?<\/strong> That gives the <strong>risk<\/strong>, often on a matrix of likelihood times severity: low, medium, high.<\/li><li><strong>What will we do about it?<\/strong> The <strong>control measures<\/strong>, in order:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>remove<\/strong> the hazard if we can;<\/li><li><strong>use something safer<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>guard<\/strong> it or isolate it;<\/li><li>use a <strong>procedure<\/strong> or permit;<\/li><li><strong>PPE<\/strong> as the last line of defence, not the first.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Write it down, brief everyone, and review it<\/strong> if anything changes. The residual risk must be acceptable before we start.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>An example: working aloft on a container lashing bridge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Hazards: a fall, dropped tools, ship motion, weather, a container being moved by the crane.<\/li><li>Controls: the area <strong>secured<\/strong> and the crane stopped; <strong>harness and fall arrester<\/strong> attached to a strong point; tools on <strong>lanyards<\/strong>; a <strong>standby man<\/strong>; good weather only; a <strong>permit<\/strong> for working aloft; communications.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>For a job we don&#x27;t do regularly<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>task-specific risk assessment<\/strong>, not a generic one copied from the folder.<\/li><li>A <strong>permit to work<\/strong> if it&#x27;s hazardous: hot work, enclosed spaces, working aloft or overside, electrical isolation.<\/li><li>Read the <strong>maker&#x27;s instructions<\/strong> and any company procedure.<\/li><li>A <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong> with everyone involved, including the &quot;what if?&quot; questions.<\/li><li>A <strong>competent person supervising<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Stop and reassess if anything isn&#x27;t as expected.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>References<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The MS&amp;FV (Health and Safety at Work) Regulations 1997, which make risk assessment a legal duty for the employer.<\/li><li><strong>COSWP<\/strong> (2026 edition), the chapter on managing occupational health and safety and risk assessment.<\/li><li>The company&#x27;s <strong>SMS<\/strong> under ISM 1.2.2.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d add for the cadet<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Everyone has <strong>stop-work<\/strong> authority. If something changes or doesn&#x27;t feel right, stop and ask.<\/li><li>The risk assessment isn&#x27;t paperwork. It&#x27;s the plan that keeps you alive.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A simple explanation, the hierarchy of control with PPE last, a real example, and for an unusual job: a specific risk assessment, permit, toolbox talk and supervision.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q233","sec":3,"q":"The OOW has set up the radar badly (12 nm range in coastal waters, no targets acquired, PI off, ship about to ground) — as C/O taking over, and as Master if it's a repeat. What has he breached?","tier":"yellow","rep":9,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>My first job is the ship, not the 2nd Mate.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Taking over as C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Take the con.<\/strong> Check the position on ECDIS and visually: where are we, and how close to danger?<\/li><li>If we&#x27;re <strong>standing into danger<\/strong>: <strong>alter away<\/strong> and <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> at once, and if needed sound the general alarm.<\/li><li><strong>Set the radar up properly<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the right <strong>range<\/strong> for coastal waters, for example 6 nm with frequent changes to 3 and 12;<\/li><li>gain, sea and rain <strong>clutter<\/strong> adjusted;<\/li><li><strong>ARPA<\/strong> on, targets <strong>acquired<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>parallel indexes<\/strong> set for the passage plan&#x27;s clearing lines;<\/li><li><strong>vectors and trails<\/strong> set, true and relative as needed.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Call the Master.<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Then find out why<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Is he <strong>fit for duty<\/strong>? Is he ill?<\/li><li>Is he <strong>tired<\/strong>? Check his <strong>rest hours<\/strong> for the last week.<\/li><li>Has he been <strong>drinking<\/strong>? If I suspect it, a breath test with a witness, under the company&#x27;s D&amp;A policy.<\/li><li>Does he know <strong>how to use the equipment<\/strong>? Has he had type-specific familiarisation?<\/li><li>Was he distracted, for example on his phone?<\/li><li>Make sure he&#x27;s <strong>relieved and rested<\/strong>, and doesn&#x27;t go back on watch until it&#x27;s sorted out.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What he&#x27;s breached<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>STCW Code A-VIII/2<\/strong>: the principles of keeping a navigational watch, including proper use of radar and a proper lookout.<\/li><li><strong>COLREG Rule 5<\/strong> (lookout) and <strong>Rule 7<\/strong> (risk of collision, proper use of radar).<\/li><li>The <strong>Master&#x27;s standing orders<\/strong> and the <strong>passage plan<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>SMS<\/strong> bridge procedures.<\/li><li>His <strong>SEA<\/strong> and the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>As Master, if it&#x27;s a repeat<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Investigate<\/strong> properly: facts, statements, ECDIS and VDR data.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s a breach of his SEA, the SMS and STCW watchkeeping duties. Under the <strong>Merchant Navy Code of Conduct<\/strong>, putting the ship and lives at risk is <strong>gross misconduct<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Disciplinary hearing<\/strong>, where he can have a friend present.<\/li><li><strong>Record it<\/strong> in the OLB and give him a copy.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the company<\/strong>. Dismissal is possible.<\/li><li><strong>Retraining<\/strong> is needed either way: radar and ARPA, ECDIS, bridge resource management.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reporting<\/strong><\/p><p>If we actually grounded or came close, it may be an MAIB marine incident report.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Ship first, take the con, alter and slow down, set up the radar properly, call the Master, then find out why (fatigue, alcohol, competence), what he&#x27;s breached, and the disciplinary process for a repeat, with retraining.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q234","sec":3,"q":"Two watchkeepers fight over a new watch schedule / a cadet says the Bosun is bullying him / AB asleep on watch. Actions?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. Two watchkeepers fighting over the watch schedule<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Go up and stop it.<\/strong> The bridge must be safe first, so I <strong>put someone competent on watch<\/strong> or take the watch myself.<\/li><li><strong>Separate<\/strong> them, check for <strong>injuries<\/strong> and give first aid.<\/li><li><strong>Take statements<\/strong> from each and from any witnesses.<\/li><li>A <strong>fight is gross misconduct<\/strong> under the <strong>Code of Conduct for the Merchant Navy<\/strong>. The <strong>Master<\/strong> holds a hearing, with each allowed a friend, makes a decision, and the <strong>OLB<\/strong> entry is completed. The <strong>company<\/strong> is informed. If there&#x27;s a serious injury, it may also be a police matter at the next port.<\/li><li><strong>Deal with the cause<\/strong>: the watch arrangement. Was it unfair? Did it break rest hours? I&#x27;d review it with the Master so it&#x27;s fair and compliant.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. A cadet says the Bosun is bullying him<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Take it seriously and confidentially.<\/strong> Thank him for telling me.<\/li><li><strong>Protect the cadet<\/strong>: change his work so he isn&#x27;t working alone with the Bosun while it&#x27;s sorted out, and assure him there will be <strong>no victimisation<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Gather facts<\/strong>: what happened, when, who saw it.<\/li><li><strong>Speak to the Bosun<\/strong>, hear his side.<\/li><li>Follow the <strong>company&#x27;s anti-harassment and bullying policy<\/strong> and the <strong>MLC complaint procedure<\/strong>. Harassment and bullying can be gross misconduct.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it and <strong>report<\/strong> it to the Master and company.<\/li><li>The <strong>2025 MLC amendments<\/strong> make the protection against harassment and bullying explicit, and the ILO/ICS guidance already covers it.<\/li><li><strong>Follow up<\/strong> with the cadet over the next weeks, because bullying often continues quietly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. AB asleep on watch<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Relieve him immediately<\/strong> and make sure the watch is safe: lookout, steering.<\/li><li><strong>Check fatigue and rest hours<\/strong>: was he overworked? Was he ill? Was he on medication?<\/li><li>Check whether the <strong>BNWAS<\/strong> was working and set correctly, because it should have alarmed.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>disciplinary process<\/strong>: sleeping on watch endangers the ship and is normally gross misconduct, but if the cause is fatigue from overwork, the ship&#x27;s management shares the blame.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the OLB and report to the company.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The common thread<\/strong><\/p><p>In all three: make it <strong>safe first<\/strong>, get the <strong>facts<\/strong>, use the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong> fairly, <strong>record and report<\/strong>, and <strong>fix the cause<\/strong>, whether it&#x27;s a watch schedule, a bully, or fatigue.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>To see that I protect the ship and the people first, use the proper procedures without overreacting or ignoring it, and look for the underlying cause.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q235","sec":3,"q":"What can a C/O do for minor misconduct, and who can dismiss?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>Code of Conduct for the Merchant Navy<\/strong>, which is part of every seafarer&#x27;s SEA on a UK ship.<\/p><p><strong>What a C/O can do for minor misconduct<\/strong><\/p><p>Minor breaches are dealt with at the <strong>lowest level<\/strong> possible, by the head of department. As C/O, I can:<\/p><ol><li>Give an <strong>informal warning<\/strong>: a quiet word, explaining what&#x27;s wrong and what I expect.<\/li><li>Give a <strong>formal warning<\/strong>, recorded, explaining that further misconduct will lead to more serious action.<\/li><\/ol><p>Examples of minor misconduct:<\/p><ul><li>lateness for work or watch;<\/li><li>poor standard of work or not following instructions;<\/li><li>minor breaches of company rules, such as smoking in the wrong place once;<\/li><li>being absent from the ship without permission for a short time;<\/li><li>untidiness or poor hygiene in the cabin.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What goes to the Master<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Repeated<\/strong> minor misconduct, or <strong>more serious<\/strong> misconduct.<\/li><li>The Master gives a <strong>formal written warning<\/strong>, then a <strong>final written warning<\/strong>.<\/li><li>These are recorded in the <strong>OLB<\/strong>, with the seafarer&#x27;s reply, and a copy given to him.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who can dismiss<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Only the Master<\/strong> can dismiss a seafarer from the ship.<\/li><li>Only for <strong>gross misconduct<\/strong>, or after the final warning stage for continued misconduct.<\/li><li>After a <strong>proper hearing<\/strong>, where the seafarer can be <strong>accompanied<\/strong> by a friend or representative and give his side.<\/li><li>He has a <strong>right of appeal<\/strong> to the company.<\/li><li>The company arranges repatriation.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Gross misconduct examples<\/strong><\/p><p>Assault, drugs, being unfit through alcohol, theft, serious harassment or bullying, sleeping on watch, refusing a lawful safety order, endangering the ship.<\/p><p><strong>Principles<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Fair and consistent<\/strong>: the same standard for everyone, regardless of rank or nationality.<\/li><li><strong>Investigate first<\/strong>, then decide.<\/li><li>The seafarer knows the allegation and can reply.<\/li><li>Proportionate response.<\/li><li><strong>Recorded<\/strong>: formal warnings and above go in the OLB.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My approach in practice<\/strong><\/p><p>Most problems are solved by an early informal word. I find out why someone is late or performing poorly, because there&#x27;s often a reason: fatigue, a family problem, a misunderstanding. The formal steps are there when that doesn&#x27;t work.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Lowest level first, the C/O can give informal and formal warnings, the Master gives written and final written warnings, only the Master can dismiss, only after a fair hearing with a friend, right of appeal, and OLB records for formal warnings.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q236","sec":3,"q":"New officers join: a 2/O from Indonesia, a 2/E from the EU, a British C/E. What certificates do you check? What if the 2/O has no CEC?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>For a UK ship, the STCW Regs 2022, <strong>MSN 1856<\/strong> for deck officers and <strong>MSN 1868<\/strong> for manning, and <strong>STCW Regulation I/10<\/strong> for the recognition of foreign certificates.<\/p><p><strong>The 2/O from Indonesia<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>His <strong>national CoC<\/strong> for the capacity, plus a <strong>UK Certificate of Equivalent Competency (CEC)<\/strong>, because a foreign CoC isn&#x27;t valid on a UK ship on its own. The UK only issues CECs to holders of CoCs from countries it has an agreement with.<\/li><li>If his CEC application is in process, he can serve on <strong>documentary proof that he&#x27;s applied<\/strong>, the MCA&#x27;s Certificate of Receipt of Application, for up to <strong>3 months<\/strong> (STCW I/10).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The 2/E from the EU<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The same: his EU member State CoC plus a <strong>UK CEC<\/strong>, since the UK left the EU. The recognition agreements continue for many EU States.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The British C/E<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>His <strong>UK CoC<\/strong>: the right <strong>capacity<\/strong> and its <strong>limitations<\/strong>, such as <strong>power<\/strong> (kW) and <strong>tonnage<\/strong> or area limits, and its <strong>revalidation<\/strong> date.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>For all of them<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>STCW certificates<\/strong>: Basic Safety Training (or refreshers within 5 years), <strong>Advanced Fire Fighting<\/strong>, <strong>Medical First Aid or Medical Care<\/strong>, PSC&amp;RB, <strong>security<\/strong> training for designated duties, and any <strong>ship-type endorsements<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>For the 2/O<\/strong>: <strong>GMDSS<\/strong> operator certificate and its endorsement, <strong>ECDIS<\/strong> generic training, and type-specific familiarisation on board.<\/li><li><strong>ENG1<\/strong>, or an <strong>accepted foreign medical certificate<\/strong> from a country listed in MSN 1815, in date for the contract.<\/li><li><strong>Passport, visas<\/strong> and <strong>seaman&#x27;s discharge book<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>SEA<\/strong> signed by both parties.<\/li><li><strong>Flag endorsement<\/strong> where the flag requires it (for a UK ship, the CEC itself).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What if the 2/O has no CEC and hasn&#x27;t applied?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>He <strong>can&#x27;t serve<\/strong> as 2/O on a UK ship.<\/li><li>The Master contacts the <strong>company and the MCA<\/strong> at once.<\/li><li>Options: the company applies urgently and gets the receipt of application; a <strong>dispensation<\/strong> from the MCA under STCW Article VIII if someone else on board holds a suitable certificate; or he&#x27;s replaced.<\/li><li>The ship mustn&#x27;t sail below the <strong>Safe Manning Document<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Expired certificates<\/strong><\/p><p>If someone&#x27;s <strong>ENG1<\/strong> or STCW certificates have expired, he <strong>can&#x27;t be signed on or work<\/strong> in that capacity. Only the <strong>MCA<\/strong> can grant a dispensation.<\/p><p><strong>After signing on<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Familiarisation<\/strong> before any duties (ISM 6.3, STCW I/14), and the crew list, rest-hour records and muster list updated.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Foreign officers need a UK CEC, or proof of application for up to 3 months under STCW I/10; the C/E&#x27;s capacity and limitations; the STCW certificates, ENG1 and SEA; and without a CEC he can&#x27;t serve unless the MCA allows.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q237","sec":5,"q":"GPS (both receivers) fails at sea. What happens to the rest of the bridge, and how do you navigate?","tier":"orange","rep":22,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First, keep the ship safe<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Note the <strong>last good position<\/strong> and time, and start a <strong>DR<\/strong> plot.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In <strong>confined or coastal waters<\/strong>: <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> and use every means: <strong>radar ranges and bearings<\/strong>, <strong>visual bearings<\/strong>, <strong>parallel indexing<\/strong>, the <strong>echo sounder<\/strong> against the chart.<\/li><li>Increase the <strong>bridge manning<\/strong> and the <strong>lookout<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What&#x27;s affected on the bridge<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>ECDIS<\/strong> goes into <strong>dead reckoning<\/strong> with an alarm. Route monitoring and the anti-grounding alarms depend on the position, so I <strong>fix the position manually<\/strong> and enter it, or use a radar-derived position if the system allows.<\/li><li><strong>ARPA<\/strong> loses <strong>ground stabilisation<\/strong> and <strong>SOG/COG<\/strong>, so true vectors may be wrong. I switch it to <strong>sea-stabilised<\/strong> with <strong>log speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>AIS<\/strong> transmits <strong>no position<\/strong>, so other ships and VTS don&#x27;t see where we are. I tell VTS if we&#x27;re in a VTS area.<\/li><li><strong>GMDSS DSC<\/strong> loses its <strong>automatic position<\/strong>, so I <strong>put it in manually<\/strong> and update it regularly. A distress alert must carry the right position.<\/li><li><strong>VDR<\/strong> records no position.<\/li><li><strong>Gyro<\/strong> speed and latitude correction may need <strong>manual input<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Any <strong>autopilot track mode<\/strong> stops, so I change to heading mode.<\/li><li><strong>BNWAS<\/strong>, <strong>LRIT<\/strong>, <strong>SSAS<\/strong> and the ship&#x27;s <strong>clocks<\/strong> may be affected.<\/li><li><strong>Weather routeing<\/strong> and fuel-monitoring systems lose their input.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How I navigate<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Coastal<\/strong>: radar and visual fixes, parallel indexes, transits and clearing bearings, the echo sounder.<\/p><p><strong>Offshore<\/strong>: <strong>celestial navigation<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li><strong>star sights<\/strong> at <strong>nautical twilight<\/strong>, when you have both the stars and the horizon;<\/li><li><strong>sun sights<\/strong> by day, with a <strong>meridian altitude<\/strong> at noon for latitude;<\/li><li><strong>moon and planets<\/strong> when available;<\/li><li><strong>DR and EP<\/strong> between fixes, allowing for set and drift.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Also<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check whether it&#x27;s a <strong>receiver fault<\/strong> (one unit) or <strong>interference<\/strong> (both, and other ships reporting it). If it&#x27;s jamming or spoofing, report it to the coastal State.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> the failure or interference to the <strong>company<\/strong>, and get the <strong>ETO<\/strong> or a technician to fix it.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS V/16.2<\/strong>: suitable arrangements are made to reach a repair port.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the logbook, with the position fixes and methods.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Keep the ship safe with the last position and DR, the effect on ECDIS, ARPA, AIS, DSC, VDR, gyro and autopilot, switch ARPA to sea-stabilised, manual DSC position, radar and visual navigation in coastal waters, celestial navigation offshore, and reporting.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["GPS failure at sea, actions?","Your at sea, GPS failed what would be your actions?","In mid of ocean GPS failed. (Celestial fix/Intercept/PL)","You are on a watch at sea, your both GPS have failed, showing zeroes, what would you do?","So, what other equipment would be affected?"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",5],["Examiner not named",4],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",3],["Robert Jackson",3],["Chris Roe",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q238","sec":5,"q":"Gyro fails / magnetic and gyro differ by 15°. Actions and causes?","tier":"yellow","rep":14,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Gyro failure<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Change to <strong>hand steering<\/strong> by the <strong>magnetic compass<\/strong> (or the transmitting magnetic compass on the steering console).<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong> and the <strong>ETO<\/strong> or engineer.<\/li><li><strong>Tell the OOWs<\/strong> and the helmsman.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What&#x27;s affected<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>autopilot<\/strong>: heading or track control.<\/li><li><strong>Radar heading stabilisation<\/strong>: the radar goes <strong>head-up and unstabilised<\/strong>, so the <strong>ARPA is degraded<\/strong>. True vectors and target tracking become unreliable.<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS<\/strong> heading and the radar overlay.<\/li><li><strong>AIS<\/strong> heading.<\/li><li><strong>VDR<\/strong> heading record.<\/li><li>Possibly the <strong>DSC<\/strong> and the <strong>BNWAS<\/strong>, and the <strong>gyro repeaters<\/strong> on the bridge wings and in the steering gear room.<\/li><li>The <strong>rate-of-turn indicator<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Manning<\/strong><\/p><p>With an extra <strong>helmsman<\/strong> needed on every watch, I must make sure his <strong>rest hours<\/strong> aren&#x27;t broken (MSN 1877), and that the SMD manning still works.<\/p><p><strong>Getting it back<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the power supply and alarms.<\/li><li>After a power failure, a gyro needs <strong>several hours to settle<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the speed and latitude inputs.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The magnetic and gyro differ by 15°<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Find out which is wrong<\/strong> by getting the compass error:<\/p><ul><li>an <strong>azimuth<\/strong> of the sun or a star;<\/li><li>an <strong>amplitude<\/strong> at sunrise or sunset;<\/li><li>a <strong>transit<\/strong> bearing of two charted objects;<\/li><li>or comparing with the <strong>GNSS COG<\/strong> in calm conditions with no current, as a rough check.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the gyro is wrong<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Wander<\/strong> or still <strong>settling<\/strong> after a power failure.<\/li><li><strong>Wrong speed or latitude settings<\/strong>, causing a large steaming error.<\/li><li>A <strong>fault<\/strong> in the gyro or follow-up system.<\/li><li><strong>Ballistic error<\/strong> after a rapid change of course or speed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the magnetic compass is wrong<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>change in deviation<\/strong>:<\/li><li><strong>magnetic cargo<\/strong> such as steel, or containers loaded near the bridge;<\/li><li><strong>steel or electrical equipment<\/strong> brought near the compass;<\/li><li>a <strong>lightning strike<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>big change in magnetic latitude<\/strong>, affecting the Flinders bar;<\/li><li>a <strong>disturbed corrector<\/strong> after maintenance;<\/li><li>the ship on one heading for a long time.<\/li><li>A <strong>bubble<\/strong> in the bowl or a damaged card.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the <strong>deviation card<\/strong> and the <strong>compass error history<\/strong> in the compass log.<\/li><li>Record the error and the cause in the logbook.<\/li><li>If the magnetic compass deviation has changed significantly, arrange for a <strong>compass adjuster<\/strong> at the next port.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Hand steering on the magnetic compass, call the Master and ETO, what&#x27;s affected (autopilot, radar stabilisation and ARPA, ECDIS, AIS, VDR), rest hours for the extra helmsman, and for a 15° difference, find which is wrong by azimuth, amplitude or transit, and the causes of each.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Gyro failure, actions?","Explain the principals of how a gyro compass works?","What other methods of checking for gyro errors are there?","TELL ME ABOUT YOUR ACTION FOR GYRO FAILURE, VSL IS IN LONG OCEAN PASSAGE, VSL","How does the gyro know it is pointing in the right direction?"],"ex":[["Robert Jackson",5],["Examiner not named",3],["Alec Keep",3],["Chris Bates",2],["Shiva Balasubramanian",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q239","sec":5,"q":"Calculate a safety depth and safety contour for given draught, squat, UKC and tide. The route check crosses the safety contour — what do you expect the 2/O to do? ENCs not available for the next voyage — options?","tier":"yellow","rep":6,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Safety depth and safety contour: the calculation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Safety depth<\/strong> = what the ship needs not to touch = <strong>draught + squat + the company&#x27;s UKC requirement<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Example<\/strong>:<\/li><li>draught <strong>12.0 m<\/strong>;<\/li><li>squat <strong>0.8 m<\/strong>;<\/li><li>UKC <strong>10%<\/strong> of draught = <strong>1.2 m<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>safety depth = 14.0 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Height of tide<\/strong> is added <strong>only<\/strong> if the plan is tied to that <strong>tide window<\/strong>. Otherwise I use chart datum depths, because tide can&#x27;t be relied on at all times.<\/li><li>The <strong>safety contour<\/strong> is set at that value. The <strong>ECDIS<\/strong> uses the <strong>next deeper contour<\/strong> available in the ENC. If the ENC has 10 m and 20 m contours, the 20 m contour is displayed.<\/li><li>Allow for <strong>CATZOC<\/strong>: if the survey is poor, add a margin.<\/li><li><strong>Squat<\/strong> depends on speed, block coefficient and the depth-to-draught ratio, so I use the ship&#x27;s squat table or the formula, for example Cb × V² ÷ 100 in open water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The route check crosses the safety contour<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I expect the 2/O to <strong>re-plan the leg<\/strong>, so the route stays in safe water with a margin.<\/li><li>If it <strong>truly can&#x27;t be avoided<\/strong>, for example in a port approach or a dredged channel:<\/li><li>it&#x27;s planned with a <strong>tide window<\/strong>: the times when the height of tide gives the required UKC;<\/li><li><strong>speed restrictions<\/strong> to reduce squat;<\/li><li><strong>documented<\/strong>, with the UKC calculation and the tidal window in the passage plan;<\/li><li><strong>approved by the Master<\/strong>, with a briefing to the bridge team;<\/li><li>the <strong>safety contour<\/strong> may be adjusted for that leg with the Master&#x27;s approval, and changed back afterwards.<\/li><li>What I don&#x27;t accept is <strong>ignoring the alarm<\/strong> or switching it off.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>ENCs not available for the next voyage: options<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Paper charts<\/strong>, an appropriate folio, fully corrected, as the primary means.<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS in RCDS mode<\/strong>, using official raster charts, <strong>with an appropriate folio of up-to-date paper charts<\/strong>, as <strong>MGN 285<\/strong> describes, because RCDS doesn&#x27;t meet carriage requirements on its own.<\/li><li>Check whether <strong>ENCs<\/strong> from another hydrographic office are available for the area, and order the <strong>permits<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>, and the passage plan updated for the different means.<\/li><li><strong>Delay<\/strong> until the ENCs or charts arrive, if neither of the above can be done.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Never use <strong>unofficial<\/strong> charts as the primary means.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A correct calculation (draught + squat + UKC, tide only for a tide window), the ECDIS using the next deeper contour, re-planning a leg that crosses the safety contour or a documented tide window approved by the Master, and the options for missing ENCs: paper charts, or RCDS with paper charts, or delay.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q240","sec":5,"q":"Rules of ship routeing: how do you know a TSS is IMO-adopted? Who may use the inshore traffic zone? Who may enter the separation zone?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>How do you know a TSS is IMO-adopted?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An IMO-adopted scheme is shown in the IMO publication <strong>Ships&#x27; Routeing<\/strong>, and in the <strong>Sailing Directions<\/strong> and <strong>ALRS<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Chart notes<\/strong> say whether it&#x27;s IMO-adopted, and the scheme is shown in magenta.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 10<\/strong> applies to <strong>IMO-adopted<\/strong> schemes. It doesn&#x27;t relieve any vessel of her obligations under other rules.<\/li><li>National or port schemes that aren&#x27;t IMO-adopted may have their own local rules.<\/li><li>UK guidance on TSSs is <strong>MGN 364<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rule 10: the main points<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>10(b)<\/strong>: a vessel using a TSS shall proceed in the appropriate lane in the general direction of traffic flow; keep clear of the separation line or zone as far as practicable; join or leave at the termination, or from the side at as small an angle as practicable.<\/li><li><strong>10(c)<\/strong>: avoid crossing lanes; if obliged to, cross on a <strong>heading at right angles<\/strong> to the general direction of traffic flow.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who may use the inshore traffic zone: Rule 10(d)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A vessel shall not use an inshore traffic zone when she can safely use the appropriate lane in the adjacent TSS.<\/li><li>However, <strong>vessels of less than 20 m<\/strong>, <strong>sailing vessels<\/strong> and <strong>vessels engaged in fishing<\/strong> may use it.<\/li><li><strong>Other vessels<\/strong> may use it when <strong>en route to or from a port, offshore installation or structure, pilot station or any other place situated within the inshore traffic zone<\/strong>, or <strong>to avoid immediate danger<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who may enter the separation zone: Rule 10(e)<\/strong><\/p><p>A vessel other than a crossing vessel, or one joining or leaving a lane, shall not normally enter a separation zone or cross a separation line except:<\/p><ul><li>in cases of <strong>emergency<\/strong> to avoid immediate danger;<\/li><li>to engage in <strong>fishing<\/strong> within a separation zone.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other parts of Rule 10<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>10(f)<\/strong>: navigate with particular caution in areas near the <strong>terminations<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>10(g)<\/strong>: <strong>avoid anchoring<\/strong> in a TSS or near its terminations.<\/li><li><strong>10(h)<\/strong>: a vessel not using a TSS shall avoid it by as wide a margin as practicable.<\/li><li><strong>10(i)<\/strong>: a vessel engaged in fishing shall <strong>not impede<\/strong> the passage of any vessel following a lane.<\/li><li><strong>10(j)<\/strong>: a vessel of less than 20 m or a sailing vessel shall not impede the safe passage of a power-driven vessel following a lane.<\/li><li><strong>10(k) and (l)<\/strong>: vessels restricted in their ability to manoeuvre doing safety operations, or cable work, in a TSS are exempt to the extent necessary.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>IMO-adopted shown in Ships&#x27; Routeing and on the chart, the ITZ users under 10(d) including the &quot;to or from a place within the zone&quot; and &quot;immediate danger&quot; exceptions, and the separation zone exceptions under 10(e): crossing, joining or leaving, emergency, and fishing within it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q241","sec":5,"q":"Speed log fails at night (no one ashore). Effect and actions?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The effect<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Nothing goes completely dark, but <strong>collision avoidance is affected<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>ARPA<\/strong> needs <strong>speed through the water<\/strong> to calculate the other ship&#x27;s <strong>true course and speed<\/strong> and <strong>aspect<\/strong> in sea-stabilised mode.<\/li><li>Without it, the <strong>true vectors<\/strong> and <strong>aspect<\/strong> are <strong>wrong<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>relative motion<\/strong>, <strong>CPA<\/strong> and <strong>TCPA<\/strong> are <strong>still right<\/strong>, because they come from the target&#x27;s relative movement on the screen.<\/li><li>Also affected: the gyro speed correction, ECDIS DR, and the VDR speed record.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Actions at night, with no one ashore to help<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong>, and put it in the <strong>night orders<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Switch the <strong>ARPA speed input<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>to <strong>manual<\/strong>, using the speed from the <strong>engine telegraph<\/strong> and the <strong>manoeuvring table<\/strong> (RPM and pitch against speed);<\/li><li>or to <strong>GNSS speed over the ground<\/strong>, knowing that gives <strong>ground-stabilised<\/strong> vectors. In a <strong>current or tidal stream<\/strong>, the target&#x27;s aspect then shows its course over the ground, not its heading. That can <strong>mislead<\/strong> COLREG decisions, for example making a crossing ship look like it&#x27;s head-on.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Tell all the OOWs<\/strong> about the limitations.<\/li><li>Use <strong>visual bearings<\/strong> and look at the other ship&#x27;s <strong>aspect and lights<\/strong> with my own eyes. Use the compass bearing trend to judge risk of collision (Rule 7).<\/li><li>Set the <strong>gyro speed corrector<\/strong> manually.<\/li><li>Keep larger <strong>CPAs<\/strong>, and call the Master earlier.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the logbook.<\/li><li>The ETO or engineer may be able to check the log sensor or the power supply in the morning.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>In the morning<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform the company<\/strong> with a defect report.<\/li><li>If it affects certification, report to the <strong>flag or RO<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>. SOLAS V/16.2 allows the ship to continue to a repair port with suitable arrangements.<\/li><li><strong>Repair<\/strong> at the next port, and record it.<\/li><li>Expect <strong>PSC<\/strong> to ask about it if it&#x27;s still defective in port.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>ARPA true vectors and aspect wrong, relative CPA and TCPA still right; switch to manual speed or GNSS speed with the tidal stream limitation; brief the OOWs; use visual bearings and aspect; record it; and report and repair in the morning.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What all equipment's will affect after speed log failure?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"Q242","sec":5,"q":"Steering fails mid-ocean (ship swinging to starboard). Actions? Emergency steering procedure, FU vs NFU, rotary vane gear, SOLAS tests.","tier":"orange","rep":15,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Immediate actions: steering fails mid-ocean, ship swinging to starboard<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Change to the other steering mode<\/strong>: <strong>NFU<\/strong>, then the <strong>other pump or system<\/strong>. Check the <strong>rudder angle indicator<\/strong>: is the rudder stuck hard over?<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong> and the <strong>engine room<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the ship keeps swinging, <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> or <strong>stop the engine<\/strong> to limit the swing, as traffic and sea room allow. Remember a stopped ship may lie beam-on to the sea and roll heavily.<\/li><li><strong>Show NUC<\/strong> lights or shapes (Rule 27) and sound the NUC signal in restricted visibility.<\/li><li><strong>Warn ships nearby<\/strong> and broadcast a <strong>Sécurité<\/strong>, or a <strong>Pan-Pan<\/strong> if there&#x27;s a risk to the ship.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the time and position.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Emergency steering<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>An <strong>officer and helmsman<\/strong> go to the <strong>steering gear room<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Establish <strong>communications<\/strong> with the bridge: sound-powered phone or VHF.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>compass repeater<\/strong> there, and <strong>steer on orders<\/strong> from the bridge, using local control of the pumps.<\/li><li>The <strong>engineers<\/strong> look for the fault: control system, pump, hydraulic leak, electrical supply.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Follow-up and non-follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>FU (follow-up)<\/strong>: the <strong>rudder goes to the angle set<\/strong> on the wheel or tiller and stays there. A feedback unit compares the rudder angle with the order.<\/li><li><strong>NFU (non-follow-up)<\/strong>: the <strong>rudder moves while the tiller is held<\/strong> to one side, and <strong>stops when it&#x27;s released<\/strong>. There&#x27;s no feedback loop, so the helmsman watches the rudder angle indicator.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rotary vane steering gear<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Vanes<\/strong> fixed to the <strong>rudder stock<\/strong> rotate inside a <strong>housing<\/strong> with fixed vanes (stoppers).<\/li><li><strong>Hydraulic oil<\/strong> pumped into alternate chambers turns the stock.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s compact and gives a constant torque, but needs good seals.<\/li><li>The other type is the <strong>ram<\/strong> type: two or four rams acting on a tiller.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS requirements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS II-1/29<\/strong>: the main steering gear can put the rudder from <strong>35° on one side to 30° on the other in 28 seconds<\/strong> at the deepest draught and full speed; the <strong>auxiliary<\/strong> from <strong>15° to 15° in 60 seconds<\/strong> at half speed or 7 knots.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS V/26<\/strong>:<\/li><li>steering gear <strong>checked and tested within 12 hours before departure<\/strong>: full movement, the power units, the remote control systems, the bridge steering positions, the emergency power, rudder angle indicators, the alarms, and the change-over;<\/li><li><strong>emergency steering drills<\/strong> at least <strong>every 3 months<\/strong>, with direct control from the steering gear compartment, communications, and alternative power supplies;<\/li><li><strong>change-over procedures<\/strong> with a <strong>block diagram<\/strong> displayed on the bridge and in the steering gear room;<\/li><li>recorded in the <strong>OLB<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS V/25<\/strong>: more than one power unit running in areas where navigation demands special caution.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Change mode and pump, call the Master and engine room, slow or stop, NUC and Sécurité, emergency steering procedure, FU vs NFU explained, how a rotary vane gear works, and the SOLAS II-1/29 and V/26 requirements.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Your steering failure action?","What is emergency steering?","During navigation, steering failure. As a Ch. Mate, what's your action?","Vessel in HRA, Steering Failure Alarm. Immediate action?","You are on a passage, in the middle of Atlantic Ocean, late afternoon, your lookout does some maintenance outside wheelhouse, your steering fails and ship starts to veer to starboard, what are your actions?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",7],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",4],["Nayyer Naqvi",2],["Khairul Anam",2],["Fazal Wadood",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q243","sec":5,"q":"Teach a cadet to take a sun sight / star sights — which twilight? Sextant errors and IE \"on the arc\". Azimuth vs amplitude.","tier":"yellow","rep":14,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["MU"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Teaching a sun sight<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the <strong>sextant errors<\/strong> first, especially <strong>index error<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Set the shades, and bring the <strong>sun down<\/strong> to the horizon.<\/li><li><strong>Swing<\/strong> the sextant gently from side to side (&quot;rocking&quot;) to find the <strong>lowest point of the arc<\/strong>, so the sextant is vertical.<\/li><li>Bring the <strong>lower limb<\/strong> to just touch the horizon.<\/li><li>Call &quot;<strong>stop<\/strong>&quot; and note the exact <strong>time<\/strong> (UTC) to the second.<\/li><li>Read the sextant.<\/li><li><strong>Apply the corrections<\/strong>: <strong>index error<\/strong>, <strong>dip<\/strong> (from the height of eye), <strong>refraction<\/strong>, <strong>semi-diameter<\/strong> and parallax, to get the true altitude.<\/li><li>Work an <strong>intercept<\/strong> (Marcq St Hilaire) for a position line, or a <strong>meridian altitude<\/strong> at noon for latitude.<\/li><li>Plot the <strong>position line<\/strong>, and cross it with another sight later (a running fix).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Star sights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Taken at <strong>nautical twilight<\/strong>, when the <strong>stars are visible<\/strong> and the <strong>horizon is still clear<\/strong>: the sun between 6° and 12° below the horizon.<\/li><li>Pre-compute the stars, choose three or more with good spread in azimuth.<\/li><li>Morning twilight: the stars disappear first in the east; evening twilight: the horizon goes dark first in the east.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Sextant errors, in the order they&#x27;re corrected<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Perpendicularity<\/strong>: the index mirror isn&#x27;t <strong>perpendicular<\/strong> to the plane of the instrument.<\/li><li><strong>Side error<\/strong>: the horizon mirror isn&#x27;t <strong>perpendicular<\/strong> to the plane of the instrument.<\/li><li><strong>Index error<\/strong>: the index and horizon mirrors aren&#x27;t <strong>parallel<\/strong> when the index is at zero.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>The non-adjustable errors are centring, graduation and shade errors, shown on the certificate.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Index error &quot;on&quot; and &quot;off&quot; the arc<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the reading at zero is <strong>on the arc<\/strong> (a positive reading), the error is <strong>subtracted<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s <strong>off the arc<\/strong>, it&#x27;s <strong>added<\/strong>.<\/li><li>&quot;<strong>If it&#x27;s on, take it off; if it&#x27;s off, put it on.<\/strong>&quot;<\/li><li>Find it by looking at the horizon, or a star, and bringing the direct and reflected images together.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Azimuth vs amplitude<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>amplitude<\/strong> is the true bearing of a body when it <strong>rises or sets<\/strong>: its <strong>true altitude is zero<\/strong>, when the centre of the sun is about half a diameter above the visible horizon. It&#x27;s measured from east or west, and it&#x27;s <strong>quick<\/strong> to calculate from the latitude and declination.<\/li><li>An <strong>azimuth<\/strong> is the true bearing of a body at <strong>any altitude<\/strong>. It needs the time, the position and the tables or a calculation (the ABC tables or a formula).<\/li><li><strong>Both are used to get the compass error<\/strong>: compare the true bearing with the compass bearing. The error is then used to find the gyro error and the magnetic compass deviation.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>How to take the sight, the corrections, nautical twilight for stars, the three adjustable errors in order, &quot;on the arc subtract, off the arc add&quot;, and the difference between amplitude and azimuth with both used for compass error.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Sextant and errors?","How will you take sight?","What do we use a sextant for?","Picture of Sextant and how will you teach cadet to use Sextant?","Then came the picture of sextant on my screen and i was expecting some questions on it but he just asked my opinion what i think of celestial navigation should it be continued or we should go all electronic?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",10],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",4],["Muhammad Iqbal",2],["Kris Askey",1]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q244","sec":5,"q":"Chart extract: tidal diamonds (work a tidal stream), reporting point, lighthouse and leading-light characteristics, sector light.","tier":"orange","rep":24,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Lighthouse characteristics<\/strong><\/p><p>For example, <strong>&quot;Fl(2) 10s 25m 18M&quot;<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Fl(2)<\/strong>: group flashing, <strong>two flashes<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>10s<\/strong>: the <strong>period<\/strong>: the whole sequence repeats every <strong>10 seconds<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>25m<\/strong>: the <strong>elevation<\/strong> of the light above <strong>MHWS<\/strong> (or the chart&#x27;s height datum);<\/li><li><strong>18M<\/strong>: the <strong>nominal range<\/strong> in nautical miles, the range in a meteorological visibility of 10 nm.<\/li><li>A colour may be given: W, R, G. No colour means white.<\/li><li>The <strong>geographical range<\/strong> depends on the height of the light and my height of eye, and the <strong>luminous range<\/strong> on the visibility. The light is seen at the lesser of the two.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A sector light<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Shows <strong>different colours<\/strong> over <strong>different arcs<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>white sector<\/strong> usually marks the <strong>safe channel<\/strong>; the <strong>red<\/strong> and <strong>green<\/strong> sectors show the sides, or the <strong>dangers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The bearings of the sector limits are given <strong>from seaward<\/strong>, towards the light.<\/li><li>An intensified sector or an obscured sector may also be shown.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Leading lights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Two lights, the <strong>rear one higher<\/strong>, which give a <strong>line to follow<\/strong> when they are <strong>in line<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The bearing of the leading line is printed on the chart.<\/li><li>If the rear light opens to the right, you&#x27;re to the left of the line, and vice versa.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A reporting point<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Shown as a <strong>magenta circle<\/strong> with a number or letter and the VHF channel.<\/li><li>You <strong>report to VTS<\/strong> when you pass it.<\/li><li>The details are in the <strong>ALRS<\/strong> and the Sailing Directions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Working a tidal stream from a tidal diamond<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Find the <strong>time of high water<\/strong> at the <strong>standard port<\/strong> given on the chart, from the <strong>tide tables<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Work out the <strong>hours before or after HW<\/strong> for the time I need.<\/li><li>Read the <strong>direction<\/strong> and <strong>rate<\/strong> from the <strong>diamond&#x27;s table<\/strong> for that hour.<\/li><li>Choose <strong>springs or neaps<\/strong>, and <strong>interpolate<\/strong> using the <strong>range of the day<\/strong> compared with the mean spring and neap ranges.<\/li><li>Use that <strong>set and rate<\/strong> in the <strong>course-to-steer<\/strong> or <strong>EP<\/strong> construction: draw the tidal vector from the start position for the time interval.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Course to steer<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Lay off the ground track, draw the tidal vector from the start, then swing the distance run through the water to cut the track: that gives the course through the water. Then apply leeway and compass error.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Read a light characteristic correctly including period, elevation and nominal range, explain sector lights (bearings from seaward) and leading lights, identify a reporting point, and work a tidal diamond with the HW time, springs or neaps, interpolation and the vector construction.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Tide 1 Knot Anchorage Spot","Strong Onshore wind, Tide from ahead, how do you make your vessel?","Contents of Tidal Stream","IN DEATIL HOW TO DO TIDE CALCULATION USING TIDAL DIAMONDS?","Single screw right hand propeller, onshore wind and tide from head, no BT, no tug?"],"ex":[["Danny Hawkins",9],["Chris Roe",5],["Ahsan Khan",3],["Examiner not named",2],["Timothy Cummins",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q245","sec":5,"q":"Met instruments: hygrometer (why wet and dry bulbs differ), precision aneroid barometer, hydrometer (read the bottom of the meniscus? — see answer), Stevenson screen; how do you predict fog?","tier":"orange","rep":38,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The hygrometer (psychrometer)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Two thermometers in a <strong>Stevenson screen<\/strong>: a <strong>dry bulb<\/strong> and a <strong>wet bulb<\/strong>, the wet bulb&#x27;s bulb covered in <strong>muslin<\/strong> kept wet with <strong>distilled water<\/strong> from a reservoir.<\/li><li>The <strong>wet bulb reads lower<\/strong> because <strong>evaporation<\/strong> from the muslin <strong>cools<\/strong> it.<\/li><li>The <strong>drier the air<\/strong>, the <strong>faster<\/strong> the evaporation and the <strong>bigger the difference<\/strong>. In saturated air, both read the same.<\/li><li>From the two readings, <strong>tables<\/strong> give the <strong>dew point<\/strong> and <strong>relative humidity<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Keep the muslin clean and the water topped up, and read the wet bulb first.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The Stevenson screen<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>louvred white box<\/strong>, mounted on the bridge wing on the <strong>windward side<\/strong>, away from heat sources such as funnels and engine room vents.<\/li><li>It protects the thermometers from <strong>direct sun<\/strong> and <strong>rain<\/strong> while letting air circulate freely.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The precision aneroid barometer<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Measures <strong>atmospheric pressure<\/strong> with an evacuated capsule.<\/li><li>Read it and correct for <strong>height above sea level<\/strong> and <strong>index error<\/strong> from its certificate, to get the pressure at sea level.<\/li><li>The <strong>tendency<\/strong>, the change over <strong>three hours<\/strong>, matters most: a fall of 3 hPa or more in 3 hours warns of deterioration.<\/li><li>In the tropics, correct for the <strong>diurnal variation<\/strong>, because a small departure from normal can mean a TRS.<\/li><li>Check it against a shore barometer in port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The hydrometer<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Measures the <strong>density<\/strong> of the <strong>dock water<\/strong>, so I can work out the <strong>dock water allowance (DWA)<\/strong> and the true draught.<\/li><li>Take the sample at <strong>mid-depth<\/strong> and away from <strong>discharges<\/strong> and outfalls, in a clean bucket.<\/li><li>Let the hydrometer <strong>float freely<\/strong>, without touching the sides, and let it settle.<\/li><li><strong>Read<\/strong> at the level of the water surface, with your <strong>eye level<\/strong> with it: the <strong>bottom of the meniscus<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then DWA = FWA × (1025 − dock density) ÷ 25.<\/li><li>The hydrometer should be a <strong>load line hydrometer<\/strong>, checked and certified.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Predicting fog<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Sea fog (advection fog)<\/strong>: when <strong>warm, moist air<\/strong> moves over <strong>colder water<\/strong>, and the air is cooled to its <strong>dew point<\/strong>. So when the <strong>dew point is close to the sea temperature<\/strong>, fog is likely. Examples: Newfoundland, the English Channel in spring.<\/li><li>Watch the <strong>dew point<\/strong> and <strong>sea temperature<\/strong> trend each hour.<\/li><li><strong>Radiation fog<\/strong> forms over land on clear, calm nights and can drift out over harbours and estuaries.<\/li><li><strong>Frontal fog<\/strong> in the warm sector and along fronts.<\/li><li><strong>Sea smoke (arctic sea smoke)<\/strong>: very cold air over warmer water.<\/li><li>Use the forecasts, NAVTEX and visibility reports.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Why the wet bulb reads lower and how it gives dew point and humidity, the Stevenson screen, the precision aneroid with height and index corrections and the 3-hour tendency, the hydrometer method and reading, and predicting fog from the dew point approaching the sea temperature.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What rule applies to fog?","How would you predict Fog?","He then showed me a picture of a Stevenson's Screen, and asked if I knew what it was, and could I explain what it is used for?","Showed a picture of an aneroid barometer. What is it?","Picture of Stevenson Screen & told me to explain the use?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",27],["Zahid Raza",7],["Siddhartha Paul",3],["Byron Griffiths",3],["Alec Keep",3]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q246","sec":5,"q":"Synoptic chart shows a deepening depression near the port; pilot due in 1–2 hours; charterer pushing. Master's decision and authority?","tier":"yellow","rep":8,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>From the chart, I&#x27;d describe what I see<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>deepening low<\/strong> with <strong>tightly packed isobars<\/strong> near the port.<\/li><li>A <strong>cold front<\/strong> coming through.<\/li><li>The wind <strong>backing then veering<\/strong> and <strong>increasing<\/strong>, say <strong>gale force 8 to 9<\/strong>, with a <strong>heavy swell<\/strong> in the approaches.<\/li><li>Heavy rain and squalls at the front, and poor visibility.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then the question<\/strong><\/p><p>Can the <strong>passage out<\/strong>, the <strong>pilot disembarkation<\/strong> and the <strong>first hours at sea<\/strong> be done safely?<\/p><ul><li>Is the <strong>pilot boarding area<\/strong> exposed? Will the pilot boat be able to work?<\/li><li>Can the <strong>tugs<\/strong> work safely?<\/li><li>Is the ship&#x27;s <strong>condition<\/strong> suitable: stability, lashing calculations for that weather, draught and trim?<\/li><li>Will we be in <strong>confined waters<\/strong> when the front passes?<\/li><li>What does the <strong>port authority<\/strong> say: is the port closing?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If it&#x27;s not safe<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>As <strong>Master<\/strong>, I&#x27;d <strong>delay sailing<\/strong> until the front has passed.<\/li><li>My authority is the <strong>Master&#x27;s overriding authority<\/strong> in <strong>ISM 5.2<\/strong>, and <strong>SOLAS V/34-1<\/strong>: no one, the owner, charterer or anyone else, can <strong>prevent or restrict<\/strong> me taking a decision that&#x27;s necessary, in my <strong>professional judgement<\/strong>, for the safety of life at sea and protection of the environment.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d <strong>inform<\/strong> the company, the <strong>charterer<\/strong> and the agent, and <strong>record my reasons<\/strong> in the logbook, with the forecast and the synoptic chart.<\/li><li>The charterer&#x27;s pressure is commercial. The safety of the ship and crew comes first, and the P&amp;I club and the company should support a reasonable decision.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d consider a <strong>note of protest<\/strong> if the delay may lead to a claim.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If I decide it&#x27;s safe to sail<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Carry out the full <strong>heavy-weather preparation<\/strong> before leaving the berth:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>lashings<\/strong> checked and extra lashings where the software requires them for the expected weather;<\/li><li><strong>hatch covers<\/strong>, vents and doors closed;<\/li><li>loose gear secured on deck and below;<\/li><li><strong>ballast<\/strong> for a good stability, draught and trim, within the lashing software limits;<\/li><li>the crew briefed, and nobody on deck in exposed areas once we&#x27;re out.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Agree a <strong>boarding or disembarkation plan<\/strong> with the <strong>pilot<\/strong>: making a lee, the pilot ladder position, possibly disembarking in a sheltered spot or taking the pilot to the next port if it&#x27;s too dangerous to disembark.<\/li><li><strong>Passage plan<\/strong> reviewed for the weather, with an <strong>abort point<\/strong> and a safe anchorage.<\/li><li><strong>Engine<\/strong> and <strong>steering<\/strong> ready, and both steering pumps running.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Read the chart correctly, assess whether each phase is safe, the Master&#x27;s authority under ISM 5.2 and SOLAS V/34-1, inform and record the reasons, and if sailing, the heavy-weather preparations and a pilot plan.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q247","sec":5,"q":"Synoptic theory: occluded front, ridge, geostrophic scale, pressure gradient force; synoptic vs analysis chart; why Newfoundland has fog; semi-permanent Azores high.","tier":"red","rep":68,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>An occluded front<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Forms when the <strong>faster cold front catches up with the warm front<\/strong>, and <strong>lifts the warm sector air<\/strong> off the ground.<\/li><li>It brings <strong>cloud and rain<\/strong> like a warm front, sometimes with showers like a cold front.<\/li><li>The depression is usually <strong>mature<\/strong> or <strong>filling<\/strong> at this stage, and the occlusion becomes <strong>weaker<\/strong> as it ages.<\/li><li>Shown in <strong>purple<\/strong> on the chart, with alternate triangles and semicircles.<\/li><li>It can be a warm occlusion or a cold occlusion, depending on which air mass is colder.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A ridge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>tongue of high pressure<\/strong> extending between two lows.<\/li><li>Usually <strong>fair weather<\/strong>, light winds, and sometimes a short spell of good weather between depressions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The pressure gradient force<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Acts from <strong>high to low pressure<\/strong>, at right angles to the isobars.<\/li><li>The <strong>closer the isobars<\/strong>, the <strong>stronger<\/strong> the force and the <strong>stronger the wind<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The geostrophic wind and the scale<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At height, the pressure gradient force is balanced by the <strong>Coriolis force<\/strong>, so the wind blows <strong>parallel to the isobars<\/strong>: the geostrophic wind.<\/li><li>The <strong>geostrophic scale<\/strong> on the chart converts <strong>isobar spacing<\/strong> into <strong>geostrophic wind speed<\/strong>, for the latitude.<\/li><li>At the <strong>surface<\/strong> over the sea, <strong>friction<\/strong> reduces the wind to about <strong>two-thirds<\/strong> of the geostrophic value, and <strong>backs<\/strong> it by about <strong>10–15°<\/strong> across the isobars <strong>towards low pressure<\/strong> in the Northern Hemisphere.<\/li><li>Over land the reduction and backing are greater.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Synoptic, analysis and prognosis charts<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>analysis chart<\/strong> shows the <strong>actual<\/strong> situation at a given time, from observations.<\/li><li>A <strong>forecast (prognosis) chart<\/strong> shows the <strong>expected<\/strong> situation at a future time, for example 24 or 48 hours ahead.<\/li><li>&quot;Synoptic&quot; means a view of the weather over a wide area at the same time.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why Newfoundland has fog<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Advection fog<\/strong>: <strong>warm, moist air<\/strong> from over the <strong>Gulf Stream<\/strong> blows over the <strong>cold Labrador Current<\/strong> on the Grand Banks.<\/li><li>The air is cooled to its <strong>dew point<\/strong>, and fog forms.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s most common in <strong>spring and summer<\/strong>, and can persist for days. Icebergs add to the danger.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The semi-permanent Azores high<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>subtropical high<\/strong> of the North Atlantic, centred near the Azores, also called the Azores-Bermuda high.<\/li><li>Formed by <strong>descending air<\/strong> in the <strong>Hadley cell<\/strong>: air rising at the ITCZ moves poleward aloft and sinks around 30°N.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s stronger and further north in <strong>summer<\/strong>, which brings settled weather to Western Europe; weaker and further south in winter.<\/li><li>It drives the <strong>NE trade winds<\/strong> on its southern side and the <strong>westerlies<\/strong> on its northern side, and steers depressions and hurricanes.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Clear definitions of each, the surface wind being about two-thirds of geostrophic and backed 10–15°, analysis vs forecast charts, advection fog over the Labrador Current, and the Azores high from the Hadley cell.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Coloured Synoptic Chart – what's the weather?","What's the difference between an analysis and synoptic chart?","Warm front and Warm sector?","How u will find the wind speed?","Explain what weather you would expect along the voyage?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",16],["Chris Bates",15],["Alec Keep",6],["Bill Bennett",6],["James Gilbert",6]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q248","sec":5,"q":"Polar Code: which conventions; certificate; ice information sources; ice accretion effects.","tier":"orange","rep":26,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Which conventions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Polar Code<\/strong> (International Code for Ships Operating in Polar Waters) is mandatory through:<\/li><li><strong>SOLAS Chapter XIV<\/strong> for <strong>safety<\/strong>, in force <strong>1 January 2017<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>MARPOL<\/strong> Annexes I, II, IV and V for <strong>pollution prevention<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>STCW<\/strong> Chapter V has the <strong>polar training<\/strong> requirements.<\/li><li>It applies to ships operating in <strong>Arctic and Antarctic<\/strong> waters, as defined by the Code.<\/li><li>In the UK, <strong>SI 2021/1401<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 637<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Certificate and documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>Polar Ship Certificate<\/strong>, issued after a survey. It shows the ship&#x27;s <strong>category<\/strong> (A, B or C), ice class, and the operational limitations, including the polar service temperature.<\/li><li>A <strong>Polar Water Operational Manual (PWOM)<\/strong>, on board, with the ship&#x27;s capabilities and limitations, procedures and contingency plans.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Polar training<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Basic<\/strong> training for officers in charge of a navigational watch on ships in polar waters.<\/li><li><strong>Advanced<\/strong> training for the <strong>Master and Chief Mate<\/strong> in polar waters other than open water, depending on the ice conditions and the ship&#x27;s category.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Sources of the latest ice information<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ice charts<\/strong> from the national <strong>ice services<\/strong> (Canada, US, Norway, Denmark, Russia).<\/li><li><strong>NAVTEX<\/strong> and <strong>SafetyNET/SafetyCast<\/strong> ice warnings, and NAVAREA warnings for the Arctic.<\/li><li>The <strong>International Ice Patrol<\/strong> for <strong>icebergs<\/strong> off <strong>Newfoundland<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sailing Directions<\/strong> and the <strong>Mariner&#x27;s Handbook (NP100)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Satellite imagery and weather routeing services.<\/li><li>The <strong>Masters of other ships<\/strong>, and icebreakers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Ice accretion effects<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Adds <strong>weight high up<\/strong>, so it <strong>raises G<\/strong>, <strong>reduces GM<\/strong> and <strong>freeboard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Can cause a <strong>list<\/strong> if it builds up unevenly.<\/li><li>Increases <strong>windage<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Blocks vents<\/strong>, freeing ports and air pipes.<\/li><li><strong>Freezes LSA<\/strong>, fire-fighting equipment, aerials and deck machinery.<\/li><li>Makes the <strong>decks dangerous<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Avoiding and removing it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Change course or speed<\/strong> to reduce spray: run with the sea, or move into <strong>warmer water<\/strong> or shelter.<\/li><li><strong>Remove the ice<\/strong> with <strong>mallets<\/strong>, scrapers and <strong>steam<\/strong>, from the top down.<\/li><li>Keep the <strong>crew safe<\/strong> while doing it: lifelines, PPE, cold-weather clothing, in pairs, with communications.<\/li><li>Recalculate <strong>stability<\/strong> with the ice allowance.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Reporting<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>SOLAS V/31<\/strong> requires the Master to report <strong>severe ice accretion<\/strong>, and <strong>dangerous ice<\/strong>.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS XIV and MARPOL, the Polar Ship Certificate and PWOM, polar training, the ice information sources including the International Ice Patrol, the effects of ice accretion on stability and equipment, how to avoid and remove it, and the V/31 report.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Dangers of ice accretion on ship?","Ques: What navigational equipment is affected in high latitude areas?","Principal requirement for ice navigation in terms of navigation equipment","Monitor ice reports, air temperature, sea temperature","Will you remove ice from Lower part or Upper part ?"],"ex":[["Joe Mattock",9],["Khairul Anam",6],["Chris Roe",6],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",6],["Khademul Insan",3]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q249","sec":8,"q":"As OOW/C/O you receive an MF/HF DSC distress alert far from the distress position (A2/A3). Actions, frequency, who to relay to and where you find the station?","tier":"yellow","rep":8,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>On receiving an MF or HF DSC distress alert<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Note<\/strong> the <strong>position<\/strong>, <strong>time<\/strong> and <strong>nature<\/strong> of the distress, and the MMSI.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Set a watch<\/strong> on the associated <strong>radiotelephony frequency<\/strong> and listen:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>2182 kHz<\/strong> for <strong>MF<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the associated <strong>HF<\/strong> voice frequency for the band the alert came in on, for example 4125, 6215, 8291, 12290 or 16420 kHz.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Acknowledging<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Don&#x27;t acknowledge by DSC<\/strong> unless <strong>instructed by the RCC<\/strong>, because <strong>coast stations<\/strong> are the <strong>main acknowledgers<\/strong>, and a ship&#x27;s acknowledgement can stop the DSC alert repeating before a coast station has received it.<\/li><li>On <strong>HF<\/strong>, a ship far away <strong>never acknowledges<\/strong> by DSC or voice.<\/li><li>On <strong>MF<\/strong>, if we&#x27;re close enough to help and no coast station has acknowledged, we may acknowledge by <strong>voice on 2182 kHz<\/strong>, and inform the RCC.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Relaying<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If <strong>no coast station acknowledges<\/strong> within about <strong>5 minutes<\/strong>, and we <strong>can&#x27;t help<\/strong>, the <strong>Master relays<\/strong> the alert to the <strong>nearest RCC or coast station<\/strong>:<\/li><li>by <strong>DSC distress relay<\/strong> addressed to <strong>that station<\/strong> (an individual call, not an all-ships relay);<\/li><li>by <strong>radiotelephony<\/strong>;<\/li><li>or by <strong>satellite<\/strong> (Inmarsat or Iridium).<\/li><li>The relay contains the casualty&#x27;s identity, position, nature of distress and time.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Where to find the station<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The coast station and RCC details come from the <strong>Admiralty List of Radio Signals<\/strong>, the <strong>GMDSS volume (Vol 5)<\/strong>, and the <strong>GMDSS handbook<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The IMO <strong>GISIS<\/strong> GMDSS Master Plan has the coast stations and their coverage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Plot<\/strong> the position, and work out whether we can <strong>assist<\/strong>: distance, time, our condition and the weather.<\/li><li>If we can, inform the RCC and <strong>proceed<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS V/33<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If we don&#x27;t go, the Master <strong>records the reason<\/strong> in the logbook.<\/li><li><strong>Record it all<\/strong> in the <strong>GMDSS log<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reference<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>IMO DSC flowcharts<\/strong> are in <strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong> and should be posted at the GMDSS console.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the rule<\/strong><\/p><p>Unnecessary acknowledgements and relays by distant ships clutter the distress frequencies and confuse the RCC. Coast stations have better equipment and are linked to the RCCs.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Note and inform the Master, watch 2182 or the HF voice frequency, don&#x27;t acknowledge by DSC unless the RCC says so, never acknowledge HF, relay after about 5 minutes if no coast station has, addressed to that station, using the ALRS Vol 5 for the station details, and record it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q250","sec":8,"q":"VHF Mayday from a sinking fishing vessel 25 nm away; they abandon to a liferaft and lose contact. How do you work out where to search, which pattern and why? 4 casualties in a raft — how do you recover them?","tier":"orange","rep":29,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Working out where to search: the datum<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The search starts from a <strong>datum<\/strong>: the <strong>last known position<\/strong>, moved by the <strong>drift<\/strong> since then.<\/li><li><strong>Drift = total water current + leeway<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Total water current<\/strong>: tidal stream, ocean current and wind-driven current.<\/li><li><strong>Leeway<\/strong>: the movement of the target through the water caused by the wind. A <strong>liferaft&#x27;s leeway<\/strong> depends on the <strong>wind<\/strong> and whether its <strong>drogue<\/strong> (sea anchor) is out, its size and whether it&#x27;s loaded. A raft without a drogue drifts much faster.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong> has the method and the leeway tables.<\/li><li>The datum is updated as time passes. The probable error grows with time, so the search area grows.<\/li><li>Tell the RCC what I&#x27;m doing: they may give a datum and a search area from their own calculations.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Which pattern and why<\/strong><\/p><p>On my own, as the <strong>first ship<\/strong> on scene:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Expanding square<\/strong> if the <strong>datum is fairly accurate<\/strong> and the area small. It starts at the datum and spirals outwards in increasing legs, always turning 90° the same way.<\/li><li><strong>Sector search<\/strong> for a <strong>small area<\/strong> close to a <strong>known point<\/strong>, such as a person or a raft seen close to the position. It passes over the datum several times from different directions.<\/li><li>With more ships, the OSC may use a <strong>parallel track search<\/strong> over a larger area.<\/li><li><strong>Track spacing<\/strong> is based on the <strong>target<\/strong> (a raft is easier to see than a person), the <strong>visibility<\/strong>, the <strong>sea state<\/strong>, and the height of eye, using the IAMSAR tables.<\/li><li>Use <strong>radar<\/strong> (rafts with radar reflectors or a SART), lookouts high up, and listen on VHF.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Recovering four people from a liferaft<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Make a lee<\/strong>: approach upwind and drift down, or put the raft on the lee side.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>rescue boat<\/strong> if conditions allow, or the ship&#x27;s <strong>means of rescue<\/strong>: <strong>scrambling nets<\/strong>, <strong>ladders<\/strong>, a <strong>rescue basket<\/strong> or cradle, and the <strong>SOLAS III/17-1<\/strong> recovery plan.<\/li><li>Secure the raft alongside with a line, away from the propeller.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>hypothermia<\/strong>: lift people <strong>horizontally<\/strong> if possible, because lifting them vertically can cause a fatal drop in blood pressure (post-rescue collapse).<\/li><li>Treat for hypothermia, shock and injuries; get <strong>TMAS<\/strong> advice.<\/li><li>Count the survivors, check against the crew number the fishing vessel reported.<\/li><li>Recover the raft if possible, or mark it so it isn&#x27;t reported as a new casualty.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Throughout<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep the <strong>RCC informed<\/strong>: position, progress, survivors recovered.<\/li><li>Act as <strong>On-Scene Coordinator<\/strong> until the RCC appoints one.<\/li><li>Record everything in the logbook and GMDSS log.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The datum from the last known position plus drift (current plus leeway, with the drogue effect), IAMSAR Vol III, expanding square for an accurate datum or sector search for a small area, track spacing factors, recovery with a lee and the ship&#x27;s recovery equipment, hypothermia and horizontal lifting, and keeping the RCC informed.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What types of Search Patterns are there?","SAR its different types of search patterns .","Prepare vessel for SAR ops?","Search and rescue patterns, track spacing for expanded square search.","Which IAMSAR volume and section would you consult?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",7],["Examiner not named",5],["Timothy Cummins",2],["Tarik Yassin",2],["Alec Keep",2]],"yrs":"2021–2025"},{"id":"Q251","sec":8,"q":"On night watch you see a red flare / a SART appears on radar. Actions?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>A red flare<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>red flare<\/strong> or rocket is a <strong>distress signal<\/strong> (COLREGs Annex IV).<\/li><li><strong>Note the bearing<\/strong> and <strong>time<\/strong>, and mark the position on ECDIS.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Alert<\/strong> the <strong>MRCC<\/strong> or <strong>coast station<\/strong>: give our position, the bearing, the estimated distance and the time.<\/li><li>Send a <strong>Mayday relay<\/strong> if no one else is responding.<\/li><li>Post <strong>extra lookouts<\/strong> and <strong>proceed towards it<\/strong>, at a safe speed, keeping a watch on <strong>VHF 16<\/strong> and <strong>DSC 70<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use <strong>radar<\/strong>: look for a target near the bearing, and for a <strong>SART<\/strong> signal.<\/li><li>Prepare the <strong>rescue boat<\/strong>, nets and the hospital.<\/li><li>Record it in the logbook. If it&#x27;s a false alarm, still report it so the RCC can stand down.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Duty to assist<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>SOLAS V/33<\/strong>: the Master must proceed with all speed to assist persons in distress, if he can. If he doesn&#x27;t, he records the reason.<\/p><p><strong>A SART appears on radar<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>How it shows<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>radar SART<\/strong> responds to <strong>X-band (9 GHz)<\/strong> radar only. It shows on the screen as a <strong>line of 12 dots<\/strong> extending <strong>outward<\/strong> from its position along its bearing, about 8 nm long.<\/li><li>As you close within about <strong>1 nm<\/strong>, the <strong>dots become arcs<\/strong>, and then <strong>concentric circles<\/strong> very close.<\/li><li>The <strong>innermost dot<\/strong> marks the SART&#x27;s <strong>position<\/strong> (roughly).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>AIS-SART<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Shows on the AIS display and ECDIS as a SART symbol with &quot;SART ACTIVE&quot; and its position, from its own GNSS.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> it to the <strong>RCC<\/strong> with the bearing and range, or the AIS-SART position.<\/li><li><strong>Proceed<\/strong> towards it, with lookouts.<\/li><li>Reduce the radar <strong>gain<\/strong> or <strong>sea clutter<\/strong> carefully: too much clutter suppression can hide the SART dots. Detuning the radar slightly can make the SART stand out.<\/li><li>Prepare to recover survivors.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>A SART in a liferaft is how survival craft are found at night and in poor visibility. The OOW must recognise the display, because it can look like interference.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A red flare is a distress signal, note the bearing and time, call the Master, alert the MRCC, Mayday relay if no one else responds, proceed with lookouts under SOLAS V/33; and the SART as 12 dots on X-band radar becoming arcs and circles within about 1 nm, with the innermost dot as its position.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q252","sec":8,"q":"Helicopter crashes/ditches near your ship during an evacuation. What do you do?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>A helicopter that ditches near us becomes the casualty.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>If it ditches in the water<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong> and muster the rescue team.<\/li><li><strong>Broadcast<\/strong> a <strong>Mayday relay<\/strong> and <strong>inform the RCC<\/strong> at once. They&#x27;ll know the aircraft and its crew.<\/li><li><strong>Launch the rescue boat<\/strong> to recover the crew, if conditions allow. The crew will have <strong>immersion suits<\/strong>, lifejackets, lights and possibly a liferaft.<\/li><li><strong>Lifebuoys<\/strong> with <strong>lights<\/strong> go in the water.<\/li><li><strong>Manoeuvre<\/strong> the ship to make a lee, without running over the wreckage or survivors.<\/li><li>Mark the position on ECDIS, and keep a <strong>lookout<\/strong> on the survivors.<\/li><li>Prepare to <strong>recover survivors<\/strong> over the side using the ship&#x27;s recovery equipment (SOLAS III/17-1), and treat them for hypothermia and injuries.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>fuel<\/strong> on the water and the risk of fire.<\/li><li><strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong> has the procedures for aircraft ditching, and helicopters sink or invert quickly.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If it crashes on deck<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Sound the alarm<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>fire party<\/strong>, which should already be standing by in <strong>fire suits<\/strong> with <strong>foam<\/strong> ready, attacks the fire immediately: <strong>foam<\/strong> for the fuel, <strong>dry powder<\/strong> as back-up.<\/li><li><strong>Rescue the crew<\/strong>: the deck crew should know where the <strong>emergency exits<\/strong> and <strong>crash rescue points<\/strong> are, from the ICS guide. Use the <strong>crash kit<\/strong>: axe, crowbar, cutters.<\/li><li>Keep everyone else clear of the rotor area and the tail rotor.<\/li><li><strong>First aid<\/strong> and TMAS advice.<\/li><li>Boundary cooling, and check for fuel running into the hatches or scuppers.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the RCC.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The original <strong>patient<\/strong> still needs evacuation: the RCC will send another aircraft or the ship diverts.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the <strong>company<\/strong>, the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> (a marine casualty involving the ship), the <strong>flag<\/strong> and the aviation authorities, who will investigate.<\/li><li><strong>Preserve evidence<\/strong>: VDR, photos, logs.<\/li><li>Debrief and look after the crew.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Proper preparation: a cleared area, the right wind, communications, and the fire party ready before the helicopter arrives.<\/li><li>Brief the deck party on what to do if it goes wrong.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>If it ditches, alarm, Mayday relay and RCC, rescue boat and lifebuoys, make a lee and recover survivors; if it crashes on deck, the fire party already in fire suits with foam attacks the fire and rescues the crew through the known exits; and IAMSAR Vol III and the ICS guide.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q253","sec":8,"q":"Hi-line transfer — what is it, and what must the deck crew never do?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What the hi-line is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>weighted line<\/strong> (a hi-line or trail line) that the helicopter lowers to the deck <strong>before the winch hook<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The deck party uses it to <strong>guide the hook and stretcher<\/strong> to the deck, so the helicopter can <strong>stand off<\/strong> to one side and <strong>doesn&#x27;t have to hover directly overhead<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s used when the winching area is small or surrounded by obstructions, as on many container ships, or in a rolling ship.<\/li><li>It has a weak link, so if it snags it breaks rather than pulling the aircraft down.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The procedure<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The helicopter lowers the <strong>hi-line<\/strong> first.<\/li><li><strong>Let it touch the deck<\/strong> or the water first, to <strong>discharge static electricity<\/strong>. The static charge from the rotor can give a severe shock.<\/li><li>A crew member, wearing <strong>gloves<\/strong>, takes the line and <strong>flakes it clear<\/strong> on deck as it&#x27;s taken in, so it can run freely.<\/li><li>The helicopter then lowers the <strong>hook<\/strong> and the <strong>winchman<\/strong> or stretcher, while the deck party <strong>pulls gently<\/strong> on the hi-line to bring them in.<\/li><li><strong>Keep tension gentle<\/strong> to <strong>steady the load<\/strong>, not to pull it down.<\/li><li><strong>Disconnect only when the winchman says<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On recovery, the deck party pays out the hi-line under gentle tension to steady the load as it goes up.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What the deck crew must never do<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Never make the hi-line fast<\/strong> to the ship, or wrap it round a hand or any part of the body. If the ship rolls or the helicopter moves, it could pull the aircraft down or injure the crew member.<\/li><li><strong>Never touch the line or the hook<\/strong> before it has <strong>earthed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Never attach the winch hook<\/strong> to the ship.<\/li><li><strong>Never stand under<\/strong> the load, or let the line foul on anything.<\/li><li><strong>Never approach the helicopter<\/strong> or go under the rotor without a signal from the crew.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The deck party<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An officer in charge with a radio to the bridge.<\/li><li>Crew in PPE: helmets with chin straps, gloves, no loose clothing.<\/li><li>The fire party ready.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>References<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>IAMSAR Vol III<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>ICS Guide to Helicopter/Ship Operations<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A weighted line lowered first so the helicopter can stand off, earth it first to discharge static, gloves, flake it clear, gentle tension, disconnect when the winchman says, and the key never: never make it fast to the ship.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q254","sec":8,"q":"The Master brings morphine and tells you to give it to an injured AB. Will you? How are controlled drugs kept and disposed of?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Will I give it on the Master&#x27;s say-so? Not on my own judgement or his alone.<\/strong><\/p><p>Morphine is a <strong>controlled drug<\/strong> and <strong>dangerous<\/strong> if wrongly given:<\/p><ul><li>with a <strong>head injury<\/strong>, it can mask deterioration and depress breathing;<\/li><li>with <strong>breathing problems<\/strong>, it depresses respiration further;<\/li><li>with <strong>low blood pressure<\/strong> or shock, it can make it worse;<\/li><li>with <strong>abdominal pain<\/strong> before diagnosis, or alcohol or other drugs in the patient.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Call <strong>TMAS<\/strong> through the <strong>coastguard<\/strong> (<strong>MGN 623<\/strong>).<\/li><li>Give the doctor the casualty&#x27;s <strong>details<\/strong>: age, the injury, observations (pulse, breathing, BP, consciousness), allergies, any medication already given.<\/li><li><strong>Follow the doctor&#x27;s advice<\/strong> on whether to give it, the <strong>dose<\/strong> and the <strong>route<\/strong>, using the <strong>Ship Captain&#x27;s Medical Guide<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Monitor<\/strong> the patient after giving it: breathing rate, consciousness, and record the observations.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> the dose, time, route and who authorised it.<\/li><li>If the Master insists without medical advice, I&#x27;d explain my concern respectfully and ask him to speak to TMAS first. It protects the patient and the Master.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How controlled drugs are kept<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Locked<\/strong> in a separate locked cabinet or compartment within the medicine chest, <strong>under the Master&#x27;s control<\/strong>. The Master or a designated officer keeps the key.<\/li><li>A <strong>controlled drugs register<\/strong> records <strong>every receipt<\/strong> and <strong>every dose<\/strong> given: date, time, patient, dose, route, the balance remaining, <strong>signed<\/strong> by the person giving it and <strong>witnessed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The stock is <strong>checked<\/strong> regularly against the register.<\/li><li>The <strong>scale<\/strong> of medical stores for the ship&#x27;s category is set by the UK medical stores regulations and guidance (MSN 1768, verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How they&#x27;re disposed of<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Out-of-date<\/strong> or unwanted controlled drugs are <strong>handed to a pharmacist<\/strong> or an <strong>authorised person<\/strong> ashore, such as a doctor or the supplier.<\/li><li>A <strong>receipt<\/strong> is obtained and <strong>recorded<\/strong> in the register.<\/li><li><strong>Never<\/strong> thrown overboard or into the garbage.<\/li><li>Some ports require the controlled drugs to be declared on arrival, and they&#x27;re checked by customs.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The medicine chest<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Inspected<\/strong> regularly, at least annually by a pharmacist or the supplier, against the <strong>scale<\/strong> for the ship&#x27;s category.<\/li><li>Expired items replaced.<\/li><li>The inspection certificate kept.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No, not without medical advice, because of the dangers (head injury, breathing, BP); call TMAS under MGN 623 and follow the doctor; record the dose; controlled drugs locked under the Master&#x27;s control with a register signed and witnessed; disposal to a pharmacist with a receipt; and the chest inspected against the scale.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q255","sec":7,"q":"After an accident, who must be informed — and who do candidates forget?","tier":"red","rep":9,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Reporting chain cut short — fail, Andrews 2022","law":"MAIB Regs 2026","a":"<p><strong>First the Master<\/strong>, who informs the <strong>company<\/strong> and the <strong>DPA<\/strong> at once. The DPA is the <strong>link to top management<\/strong> (ISM 4) and starts the company&#x27;s <strong>emergency response<\/strong> team.<\/p><p><strong>Then the statutory and other reports<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>MAIB<\/strong>: under the <strong>MS (Accident Reporting and Investigation) Regulations 2026<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 564 Amd 2<\/strong>, <strong>as soon as practicable by the quickest means<\/strong>: the <strong>24-hour line (+44 (0)23 8023 2527)<\/strong>, then the <strong>Accident Report Form<\/strong>. Don&#x27;t wait for the company&#x27;s internal investigation. <strong>Save the VDR<\/strong> straight away.<\/li><li><strong>Flag (MCA) and class<\/strong>: <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>: any accident or defect affecting the ship&#x27;s safety or the efficiency or completeness of her equipment. They decide whether an <strong>additional survey<\/strong> is needed. Certificates <strong>aren&#x27;t automatically invalid<\/strong>, but they&#x27;re <strong>at risk<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Port State<\/strong>: required if we&#x27;re in a <strong>foreign port<\/strong>, and in practice the <strong>next port State<\/strong> before we arrive. <strong>Candidates have been marked down for missing this.<\/strong><\/li><li><strong>Coastguard, coastal State or VTS<\/strong>: for <strong>distress<\/strong>, a <strong>danger to navigation<\/strong> (SOLAS V/31), or <strong>pollution<\/strong> (MARPOL Protocol I and the SOPEP contacts).<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I and H&amp;M insurers<\/strong>: for <strong>liability<\/strong>, <strong>crew injury<\/strong> and <strong>hull damage<\/strong>. They appoint <strong>surveyors<\/strong> and <strong>lawyers<\/strong>, and the P&amp;I correspondent at the next port.<\/li><li><strong>Charterers<\/strong>, <strong>cargo interests<\/strong>, the <strong>agent<\/strong>, and the <strong>harbour authority<\/strong> if we&#x27;re in port.<\/li><li><strong>Next of kin<\/strong> through the <strong>company<\/strong>, <strong>never first from the ship<\/strong> or through the crew&#x27;s social media.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Who candidates forget<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>port State<\/strong> or the <strong>next port State<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>class emergency response service<\/strong>, which can calculate damage stability and strength.<\/li><li>The <strong>harbour authority<\/strong> for an accident in port.<\/li><li>The <strong>charterers<\/strong> and <strong>cargo interests<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>CSO<\/strong> if it&#x27;s a security-related incident.<\/li><li>The <strong>flag<\/strong> directly, rather than assuming the company will do it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On board<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>OLB entries<\/strong>, made by the Master with the right signatures.<\/li><li><strong>Statements<\/strong> from witnesses, taken separately.<\/li><li><strong>Evidence kept<\/strong>: VDR, ECDIS, logs, charts, damaged equipment, photos.<\/li><li><strong>Drug and alcohol testing<\/strong> if the company policy requires.<\/li><li>The <strong>internal ISM investigation<\/strong> (ISM 9).<\/li><li>A <strong>Note of Protest<\/strong> at the next port if a claim may follow, for example for cargo damage.<\/li><li>Care for the <strong>crew<\/strong>: injuries, shock, rest.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Order<\/strong><\/p><p>The order depends on the emergency. If life is at risk, the <strong>distress or urgency<\/strong> message comes first, then the company. The MAIB report doesn&#x27;t come before saving life.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Master, company and DPA; MAIB with the 2026 Regs and the 24-hour line; MCA and class under SOLAS I/11(c); the port State, which candidates forget; coastguard; insurers; charterers and agent; next of kin through the company; and the on-board records including a Note of Protest.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What counts as a serious injury for the MAIB?","pa":"<p>Unfit for more than 72 hours, or in hospital for more than 24 hours (MGN 564 Amd 2).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q256","sec":9,"q":"E/R fire on your watch in the Dover Strait / sea area A1 — full actions; when CO₂; then the ship is a dead ship at anchor, tugs 18–24 h away and the weather is worsening — what now? Which certificates are affected?","tier":"yellow","rep":10,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Immediately<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>General alarm<\/strong>, <strong>call the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Keep the con<\/strong>: <strong>hand steering<\/strong>, and use the <strong>remaining way<\/strong> to get <strong>clear of the traffic lane<\/strong> and <strong>shoal water<\/strong>. In the Dover Strait, the TSS and the sandbanks are close.<\/li><li>When we <strong>lose propulsion<\/strong>: <strong>NUC<\/strong> lights and shapes, <strong>AIS<\/strong> status changed, <strong>anchors cleared<\/strong> and ready.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>sea area A1<\/strong>: a <strong>VHF DSC distress alert<\/strong> on <strong>Ch 70<\/strong> and <strong>Mayday<\/strong> on <strong>Ch 16<\/strong> if we&#x27;re in <strong>grave and imminent danger<\/strong>; otherwise an <strong>urgency<\/strong> call (Pan-Pan). Inform <strong>Dover Coastguard<\/strong> (CNIS) either way.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Fire<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Muster<\/strong> and <strong>head count<\/strong> first.<\/li><li><strong>Stop E/R ventilation<\/strong>, close the <strong>fire dampers<\/strong>, <strong>skylights<\/strong> and <strong>funnel flaps<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Trip<\/strong> the <strong>fuel quick-closing valves<\/strong> and the fuel pumps.<\/li><li>Start the <strong>emergency generator<\/strong> and <strong>emergency fire pump<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the fire is <strong>small<\/strong>, a <strong>BA team<\/strong> attacks it, with <strong>boundary cooling<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s <strong>big<\/strong>, the <strong>Master<\/strong> orders the <strong>CO₂<\/strong> release, but only after the <strong>head count<\/strong> confirms the <strong>E/R is empty<\/strong> and every opening is <strong>shut<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then <strong>keep it closed<\/strong>, <strong>cool<\/strong> the <strong>boundaries<\/strong>, and <strong>don&#x27;t re-enter early<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Dead ship at anchor, tugs 18–24 hours away, weather worsening<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>I won&#x27;t wait 24 hours<\/strong> while the company negotiates.<\/li><li>Under the <strong>Master&#x27;s overriding authority<\/strong> (<strong>ISM 5.2<\/strong>, <strong>SOLAS V/34-1<\/strong>), engage a <strong>salvor<\/strong> on <strong>LOF 2020<\/strong>. The <strong>Master can sign for the owner<\/strong> and the cargo (<strong>Salvage Convention Art 6(2)<\/strong>).<\/li><li>Meanwhile, <strong>veer more cable<\/strong>, and have the <strong>second anchor<\/strong> ready.<\/li><li>Keep the <strong>coastguard<\/strong> informed. In UK waters, the coastguard may bring in <strong>SOSREP<\/strong>, who can direct the salvage and has the Emergency Towing Vessel arrangements.<\/li><li>Prepare the <strong>emergency towing<\/strong> arrangement from the <strong>ETB<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Prepare the <strong>LSA<\/strong>, and consider evacuating non-essential crew if it gets worse.<\/li><li>Record the times and the decisions.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Certificates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SEC<\/strong>: the CO₂ has been used and must be <strong>recharged<\/strong> before sailing.<\/li><li><strong>Safety Construction<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong> for the <strong>fire damage<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Load Line<\/strong>, if the structure or closing appliances are damaged.<\/li><li>The <strong>surveyor decides<\/strong>: they&#x27;re not automatically invalid.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Reports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MAIB<\/strong>, the <strong>MCA<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong> (SOLAS I/11(c)), <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and <strong>H&amp;M<\/strong>, the company, and the <strong>next port<\/strong>.<\/li><li>OLB entries, and save the VDR.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The navigation first in the Dover Strait (clear the lane, NUC, anchors, DSC distress or urgency in A1), then the fire with CO₂ only after the head count, then for the dead ship with worsening weather, not waiting but engaging a salvor on LOF 2020 with the Master&#x27;s authority under Art 6(2), and SOSREP; and the certificates affected.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What must be done before you release CO₂?","pa":"<p>Head count complete, E/R evacuated, ventilation stopped and openings closed, fuel shut off, Master&#x27;s order.<\/p>","asked":["You have an Engine Room fire?","Are dry powder fire extinguishers available and ready for use?","What equipment would be checked?","17 Fire in engine room in boiler","E/R fire leading to M/E failure action ?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",3],["Colum Leggett",1],["Roddy MacKay",1],["Chris Roe",1],["Joe Mattock",1]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q257","sec":9,"q":"Small fires: deep-fat fryer, galley dustbin, laundry, AB's cabin, mess room, paint store. Media, hazards, follow-up.","tier":"orange","rep":24,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>General approach for any small fire<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong>, and only tackle it myself if it&#x27;s <strong>small and safe<\/strong>, with the <strong>right extinguisher<\/strong> and an escape route behind me.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> power, fuel and ventilation, and <strong>close the door<\/strong> to contain it.<\/li><li>Bring the <strong>fire party<\/strong> with <strong>BA<\/strong> and <strong>boundary cooling<\/strong> if it isn&#x27;t out in <strong>seconds<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Deep-fat fryer<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Never water<\/strong>: it turns to steam instantly and throws burning fat everywhere.<\/li><li><strong>Kill the power<\/strong> at the <strong>emergency stop<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use the fryer&#x27;s <strong>fixed wet-chemical<\/strong> system if fitted, a <strong>wet-chemical (Class F)<\/strong> extinguisher, or a <strong>fire blanket<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Close the <strong>hood<\/strong> and <strong>duct dampers<\/strong>, and <strong>check the exhaust duct<\/strong>, because <strong>duct fires spread<\/strong> through the ship.<\/li><li><strong>CO₂<\/strong> only knocks the flames down: the oil stays above its <strong>auto-ignition temperature<\/strong> and <strong>re-ignites<\/strong>. Wet chemical cools and forms a soap layer (saponification).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Galley dustbin, cabin, mess room<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Class A<\/strong> fires (solid combustibles), so <strong>water<\/strong> or <strong>foam<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Put a <strong>lid<\/strong> or a <strong>blanket<\/strong> on a <strong>bin<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In a <strong>cabin<\/strong>: <strong>search for people<\/strong>, check the <strong>adjacent cabins<\/strong> and <strong>deckheads<\/strong>, and <strong>isolate the power<\/strong> in case it&#x27;s <strong>electrical<\/strong> (a charger, a heater).<\/li><li>Close the cabin door to contain it, and check the corridor.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Laundry<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Usually a <strong>dryer<\/strong>: lint in the drum and the duct.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate the power<\/strong>, use <strong>CO₂<\/strong> on the electrics, then <strong>water<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Clean the lint ducts<\/strong> later, and check the dryer&#x27;s thermostat.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Paint store<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Class B<\/strong> with <strong>flammable vapour<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t open the door<\/strong>: fresh air could cause a flash fire or explosion.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>fixed system<\/strong> that <strong>SOLAS requires for paint lockers<\/strong> (CO₂, dry powder, water spray or equivalent).<\/li><li><strong>Cool the boundaries<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>CO₂ extinguisher hazards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Asphyxiation<\/strong> in small spaces.<\/li><li><strong>Cold burns<\/strong> from the <strong>horn<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Static<\/strong> discharge.<\/li><li><strong>Very little cooling<\/strong>, so <strong>re-ignition<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>re-ignition watch<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilate<\/strong> the space once it&#x27;s safe.<\/li><li><strong>Investigate<\/strong> the <strong>cause<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Recharge or replace<\/strong> the extinguishers used.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the <strong>company<\/strong>. Report to the <strong>MAIB<\/strong> if it&#x27;s a marine casualty under the 2026 Regs and MGN 564 Amd 2: a fire is normally a marine casualty, so check the definition and report if it applies.<\/li><li>Make an <strong>OLB<\/strong> entry, and <strong>review<\/strong> the <strong>drills<\/strong> and the <strong>galley inspections<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Share the lessons at the safety committee.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Raise the alarm, isolate and contain; never water on fat, wet chemical or blanket, check the duct, CO₂ re-ignition risk; water or foam on Class A with a search in a cabin; laundry dryer power then CO₂; don&#x27;t open the paint store, use its fixed system; CO₂ hazards; and the follow-up.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the main fire risk in a galley and how do you control it?","pa":"<p>Grease in hoods and ducts; a cleaning schedule, primary and back-up thermostats on fryers, lids on, and power cut-off linked to the fixed system.<\/p>","asked":["Fire in galley, action ?","You're on the bridge as Master, there's a fire in the Galley and the cook is missing actions?","Galley fire what do you do try to contain or extinguish?","Fire in Deep fat fryer. Your action as an on-scene commander?","During watch, the fire alarm panel indicates a fire in the galley – what actions will you take?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",5],["Steve Meaking",3],["Ross Cochrane",2],["Bill Bennett",2],["Kris Askey",2]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q258","sec":9,"q":"A DG trailer catches fire on an enclosed vehicle deck; then on an open deck. Water on the vehicle deck is affecting stability — actions?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>\"The <strong>DG manifest and stowage plan<\/strong> give the UN number and class. The <strong>EmS Guide<\/strong> gives the fire schedule: the media, whether water is suitable, and the special hazards. I'd sound the alarm, muster, take a head count and inform the Master, with a distress or urgency call if needed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Enclosed vehicle deck:<\/strong> get drivers and passengers off the deck. Stop ventilation unless the EmS says otherwise. Release the <strong>drencher<\/strong> for that section and the adjacent sections. BA teams go in with hoses, and the decks above and below get boundary cooling. Keep everyone clear of toxic smoke.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Open deck:<\/strong> attack from upwind with hoses and monitors. Cool the burning trailer and the units next to it. Alter course to keep the smoke away from the accommodation air intakes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Water on the vehicle deck<\/strong> is the big danger. Firefighting water on a large undivided deck sits high up with a huge <strong>free surface<\/strong>, so the effective GM collapses and she lists. The list then runs more water to the low side. I'd make sure the <strong>scuppers and drains are clear and working<\/strong>, use only as much water as the fire needs, and stop drenching once it's under control. I'd calculate stability. Counter-ballasting is dangerous while the water can still shift, and makes things worse if she's lolling, so I'd remove the water and lower G first.<\/p>\n<p>The lessons are the <strong><em>Herald of Free Enterprise<\/em><\/strong> (vehicle deck flooded) and the <strong><em>Al-Salam Boccaccio 98<\/em><\/strong> (2006), which capsized after firefighting water built up on her car deck.\"<\/p>\n<p>⚠️ Your bank cites <em>Hoegh Osaka<\/em> here. Her 2015 list came from loading and ballast errors that left too little stability, not from firefighting water. Use <em>Al-Salam Boccaccio 98<\/em> for this point.<\/p>","pq":"Where do you find the EmS number?","pa":"<p>The DG declaration and manifest, and the IMDG Dangerous Goods List (column 15).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q259","sec":9,"q":"How do you make a fire drill realistic?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS III/19.3<\/strong> says drills shall, <strong>as far as practicable<\/strong>, be conducted <strong>as if there were an actual emergency<\/strong>. The <strong>fire drill content<\/strong> is in <strong>III/19.3.4<\/strong>. In the UK the <strong>Musters, Training and Decision Support Systems Regulations 1999<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 71<\/strong> apply.<\/p><p><strong>What the fire drill must include (III/19.3.4)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Reporting to stations and preparing for the duties in the muster list.<\/li><li><strong>Starting a fire pump<\/strong>, using at least <strong>two jets<\/strong> of water to show the system is working.<\/li><li>Checking the <strong>fireman&#x27;s outfits<\/strong> and other personal rescue equipment.<\/li><li>Checking the relevant <strong>communication<\/strong> equipment.<\/li><li>Checking the operation of <strong>watertight doors<\/strong>, <strong>fire doors<\/strong>, <strong>fire dampers<\/strong> and the main inlets and outlets of the <strong>ventilation<\/strong> systems in the drill area.<\/li><li>Checking the arrangements for <strong>subsequent abandonment<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>To make it real<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Plan it<\/strong> with the Master, but <strong>don&#x27;t tell the crew<\/strong> the <strong>time<\/strong> or the <strong>scenario<\/strong>. Warn only those who <strong>must know<\/strong> for safety: the bridge, the engine room and the port.<\/li><li><strong>Vary<\/strong> the <strong>location<\/strong> and <strong>type<\/strong>: the <strong>E/R<\/strong>, the <strong>galley<\/strong>, a <strong>reefer container<\/strong>, a <strong>DG container<\/strong> in a hold, the <strong>accommodation at night<\/strong>, the paint store, a lithium battery box.<\/li><li>Use a <strong>smoke machine<\/strong>, a <strong>casualty<\/strong> or <strong>missing person<\/strong> (a <strong>dummy<\/strong>) to find and recover, and complications: a <strong>blocked escape route<\/strong>, a <strong>radio failure<\/strong>, a BA set &quot;failing&quot;.<\/li><li>The teams <strong>actually don BA<\/strong> and use the <strong>entry-control board<\/strong>, with real BA pressures and time limits.<\/li><li><strong>Charge the hoses<\/strong> with at least <strong>two jets<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Close<\/strong> the <strong>fire doors<\/strong>, <strong>dampers<\/strong> and <strong>ventilation<\/strong> for real.<\/li><li><strong>Prepare for abandonment<\/strong>: muster with lifejackets, and swing out the boats if it&#x27;s part of the scenario.<\/li><li><strong>In port<\/strong>, involve the <strong>shore fire brigade<\/strong>, who need to know the ship&#x27;s layout and the fire control plan.<\/li><li>Combine with <strong>first aid<\/strong>: the casualty is treated and stretchered.<\/li><li>Use <strong>tabletop<\/strong> exercises for decisions the crew can&#x27;t practise for real, such as the CO₂ release decision.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Debrief<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Time it<\/strong>, then <strong>debrief<\/strong>: focus on <strong>what<\/strong> went wrong, not <strong>who<\/strong>.<\/li><li>What did we learn? What would we do differently?<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it, and feed the <strong>lessons<\/strong> into the <strong>SMS<\/strong> and the next drill.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What makes drills fail<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The same scenario in the same place every month.<\/li><li>Announced in advance.<\/li><li>No real equipment used.<\/li><li>No debrief.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Audits<\/strong><\/p><p>Auditors and PSC officers often ask to witness a drill, and judge whether it&#x27;s realistic (the MCA guidance on ISM audits is in MGN 708).<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS III/19.3 &quot;as if an actual emergency&quot; and the III/19.3.4 contents, unannounced and varied scenarios, smoke and dummies, real BA and hoses, closing doors and dampers, the shore fire brigade in port, and a debrief that feeds back into the SMS.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What must every fire drill include?","pa":"<p>Reporting to stations, starting a fire pump with at least two jets, checking fireman&#x27;s outfits and rescue equipment, communications, fire doors, dampers and ventilation, and arrangements for abandonment.<\/p>","asked":["Again how will you make it realistic?","How will you make the dill realistic?"],"ex":[["Chris Bates",2]],"yrs":"2023–2023"},{"id":"Q260","sec":7,"q":"Collision: the 2/O called you because he was unsure, you hear the crash before you reach the bridge and he is petrified. Actions, statutory duties, certificates affected — and the points examiners say candidates miss.","tier":"red","rep":19,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Collision follow-up (VDR, protest, D&A test) — fail, Rahman 2024","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First I relieve the 2/O.<\/strong> Someone stays with him: he&#x27;s a <strong>casualty<\/strong> and also a <strong>witness<\/strong>. I <strong>take the con<\/strong> and <strong>call the Master<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>General alarm<\/strong>, <strong>muster<\/strong> and <strong>head count<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Engines<\/strong> as needed. If we&#x27;re <strong>embedded<\/strong> in the other ship, staying there may keep the <strong>hole plugged<\/strong>: don&#x27;t go astern without thinking.<\/li><li><strong>NUC signals<\/strong> if we can&#x27;t manoeuvre, or <strong>anchor<\/strong> if the depth allows. Update the <strong>AIS<\/strong> navigational status.<\/li><li><strong>Start the fire pump<\/strong>, because <strong>collisions start fires<\/strong>: fuel and electrical damage.<\/li><li><strong>Close watertight doors<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>emergency team<\/strong> sounds round <strong>all tanks, bilges and holds<\/strong> and checks for <strong>pollution<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Check the engine room<\/strong> too, even if the damage is forward: the <strong>shock<\/strong> can damage machinery, <strong>shafting<\/strong> and <strong>fuel lines<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19): identify the damaged compartments and their <strong>watertight boundaries<\/strong>, <strong>close<\/strong> all openings and valves around them, <strong>pump<\/strong> with the arrangements shown, and correct any list with <strong>cross-flooding<\/strong> or ballast transfer.<\/li><li><strong>Damage stability<\/strong>, using the <strong>loading computer<\/strong> and <strong>class&#x27;s emergency response service<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>other ship<\/strong>: is she sinking, on fire, are people in the water?<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Statutory duty: MSA 1995 s.92<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Assist<\/strong> the other ship and her people.<\/li><li><strong>Stand by<\/strong> until that&#x27;s no longer needed.<\/li><li><strong>Give<\/strong> our <strong>name<\/strong>, <strong>port of registry<\/strong>, and the <strong>ports we&#x27;re from and bound to<\/strong>.<\/li><li>All so far as we can without danger to our own ship and people.<\/li><li><strong>Mayday<\/strong> or <strong>Pan-Pan<\/strong>, and inform the <strong>coastguard<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Evidence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Save the VDR immediately<\/strong>, before it overwrites.<\/li><li>Keep the <strong>ECDIS<\/strong> and <strong>radar<\/strong> data, the <strong>logs<\/strong> and <strong>charts<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Drug and alcohol test<\/strong> the OOW and lookout, under the company policy.<\/li><li><strong>Statements<\/strong> from everyone involved, separately.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Reports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>MAIB<\/strong> (2026 Regs), the <strong>MCA and class<\/strong> (SOLAS I/11(c)), the <strong>company and DPA<\/strong>, <strong>P&amp;I and H&amp;M<\/strong>, and the <strong>next port State<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>Note of Protest<\/strong> at the first port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Certificates<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Not automatically invalid<\/strong>: the <strong>surveyor decides<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At risk: <strong>class<\/strong>, <strong>Safety Construction<\/strong> and <strong>Load Line<\/strong> if the structure is damaged, the <strong>SEC<\/strong> if LSA is damaged, and the <strong>IOPP<\/strong> if oil tanks are breached.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo<\/strong> may have to be discharged before repairs.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What examiners say candidates miss<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Looking after the <strong>2/O<\/strong> as a casualty and witness.<\/li><li><strong>Fire<\/strong> risk and starting the fire pump.<\/li><li>Checking the <strong>engine room<\/strong> and shafting, not just the bow.<\/li><li>The <strong>damage control plan<\/strong>, and <strong>class emergency response<\/strong> for damage stability.<\/li><li><strong>Drug and alcohol testing<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>next port State<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Not backing out if <strong>embedded<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>AIS status<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The 2/O afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Why was he unsure? Why didn&#x27;t he act? Fatigue, training, standing orders? He may need support and retraining, and the investigation will look at the call-the-Master culture.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Relieve the 2/O and take the con, alarm and head count, engines with care if embedded, fire pump, soundings everywhere including the engine room, damage stability, s.92 duties, VDR and D&amp;A testing, the reports including the port State, and certificates at risk but not automatically invalid.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why the fire pump and E/R check if the damage is forward?","pa":"<p>Shock damage and fire risk anywhere in the ship; water must be ready for boundary cooling. These are the points the Rahman 2024 examiner failed the candidate for missing.<\/p>","asked":["Collision with FV, Actions?","Collision with other vessel action as a CO ?","Free fall Life boat test","Vessel had a collision action?","Surveyor listened to my answer and later he added that OOW called you on radio to come on bridge. Next he asked once you reached on bridge what first thing you do before taking collision avoidance action?"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",3],["Alec Keep",3],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",3],["Danny Hawkins",2],["James Steer",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q261","sec":7,"q":"Allision while alongside (your ship hits the quay or is hit). What do you consider?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>An <strong>allision<\/strong> is contact with a <strong>fixed object<\/strong>, such as the quay. Whether we hit the quay or another ship hits us alongside, I deal with <strong>people first, then the ship, then liability<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>1. Safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stop cargo operations<\/strong> and <strong>clear people off the gangway<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>moorings<\/strong> for <strong>parted or overloaded<\/strong> lines, and <strong>re-tend<\/strong> them. Watch for snap-back.<\/li><li>On a container terminal, check we <strong>haven&#x27;t struck the gantry cranes<\/strong> or <strong>lashing platforms<\/strong>, and that <strong>nobody ashore is hurt<\/strong>. Crane booms over the ship are a big risk if the ship moves.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>gangway<\/strong> and shore access.<\/li><li><strong>Fire<\/strong>: check for damage to fuel tanks or electrical systems near the contact point.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. The ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Sound<\/strong> all tanks and spaces near the contact point.<\/li><li>Check for <strong>pollution<\/strong> from <strong>bunker tanks<\/strong>, and activate the SOPEP if needed.<\/li><li><strong>Inspect the hull<\/strong> by boat or <strong>diver<\/strong>, and from inside.<\/li><li>If anything is flooding, use the <strong>damage control plan<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/19) to close the watertight boundaries and pump, and check <strong>stability<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the propeller and rudder if the contact was aft.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. The quay<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Photograph<\/strong> the <strong>fenders<\/strong> and the <strong>damage<\/strong> with <strong>times<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Arrange a <strong>joint survey<\/strong> with the terminal or port, the P&amp;I surveyor and the ship.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t sign anything admitting liability.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Reports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master<\/strong>, the <strong>company<\/strong> and the <strong>DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>harbour master<\/strong>, who may <strong>restrict our movements<\/strong> until we&#x27;re surveyed.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong>: contact causing damage is a <strong>marine casualty<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Class<\/strong> and the <strong>MCA<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>: class decides whether we can sail or need an <strong>emergency dry dock<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> for the <strong>quay damage<\/strong> (third-party liability), <strong>H&amp;M<\/strong> for our own damage.<\/li><li>The <strong>charterers<\/strong> and the <strong>agent<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. If another ship hit us<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It&#x27;s a <strong>collision<\/strong>, so <strong>MSA 1995 s.92<\/strong> applies to her Master: he must stand by and give his details.<\/li><li><strong>Get her details<\/strong>: name, port of registry, last and next port.<\/li><li><strong>Save our VDR<\/strong>, CCTV and the deck log entries.<\/li><li>Send her a <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> holding her responsible, and make our own <strong>Note of Protest<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Photographs of her and of the damage, and witness statements, including the terminal&#x27;s.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Everything goes in the <strong>logs<\/strong>, with <strong>statements<\/strong> from the OOW, the mooring parties and the gangway watch.<\/li><li>An ISM investigation: why did it happen? Moorings, passing ship surge, the pilot or tug, the berthing speed?<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The definition, people first (gangway, cranes, moorings), then the ship (soundings, pollution, hull inspection), the quay (photos and joint survey), the reports including the harbour master, MAIB, class and MCA, P&amp;I for the quay, and if another ship hit us, s.92 applies to her, with a letter of protest and our Note of Protest.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who pays for the quay?","pa":"<p>The owner&#x27;s P&amp;I club covers liability for fixed and floating objects; the port will claim against the ship.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q262","sec":7,"q":"Engines stopped 24 nm from land, repair needs 36 hours (or the tow line parts and the tug now claims salvage). Actions as Master.","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First I ask how long I&#x27;ve got.<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I work out the <strong>drift<\/strong> from the wind, the current, the forecast and the observed set, and compare it with the <strong>24 nm<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At <strong>2 knots<\/strong> we&#x27;d be aground in <strong>12 hours<\/strong>, long before a <strong>36-hour<\/strong> repair. So <strong>waiting isn&#x27;t an option<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the drift is away from land, or the weather is improving, I have more time, but I still plan for the worst case.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>NUC<\/strong> lights and shapes, the <strong>AIS<\/strong> status changed, and a <strong>Sécurité<\/strong> or <strong>Pan-Pan<\/strong> broadcast.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the coastguard<\/strong>, with our position, the drift, the time to danger and the repair estimate. In UK waters they can involve <strong>SOSREP<\/strong>, who can direct a tow, including the emergency towing vessel arrangements.<\/li><li><strong>Anchors ready<\/strong>. Check the <strong>depth<\/strong> against the <strong>windlass capacity<\/strong> from the maker&#x27;s and class data (about 82.5 m for lifting the anchor and three shackles, verify). In <strong>deep water<\/strong> an anchor may only slow the drift, so I plan to <strong>anchor<\/strong> once we reach <strong>suitable depth<\/strong>, well before the danger.<\/li><li>Prepare the <strong>emergency towing<\/strong> arrangement from the <strong>Emergency Towing Booklet<\/strong> (SOLAS II-1/3-4).<\/li><li><strong>Inform the company<\/strong> at once, and the engineers give honest repair times.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Towage or salvage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Arrange <strong>towage early<\/strong> on a <strong>commercial towage contract<\/strong> (such as a BIMCO TOWCON or a daily rate) while we&#x27;re <strong>not in danger<\/strong>. It&#x27;s <strong>cheaper<\/strong> and stays under our control.<\/li><li>If <strong>danger<\/strong> develops, with the drift closing the coast or the weather worsening, I&#x27;d go to <strong>salvage<\/strong> on <strong>LOF 2020<\/strong>. I can sign it under <strong>Salvage Convention Art 6(2)<\/strong> on behalf of the owner and cargo.<\/li><li>Salvage needs <strong>danger<\/strong>, a <strong>voluntary<\/strong> service and a <strong>useful result<\/strong>: <strong>no cure, no pay<\/strong> (Art 12).<\/li><li>The Master&#x27;s overriding authority (ISM 5.2) means I don&#x27;t wait for the company to finish negotiating if the ship is in danger.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Reporting and records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the <strong>flag<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS I/11(c)<\/strong>), and to <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and <strong>H&amp;M<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Record everything<\/strong>: times, positions, drift, the weather, communications, offers and agreements.<\/li><li>MAIB if it becomes a marine casualty or incident.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the towline parts and the tug claims salvage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Under <strong>Art 17<\/strong>, a tug <strong>can&#x27;t claim salvage<\/strong> for performing its <strong>towage contract<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It can <strong>only<\/strong> claim if it provides services <strong>beyond<\/strong> that contract because of a <strong>new danger<\/strong> it didn&#x27;t cause, and those services are exceptional.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d <strong>record the facts<\/strong>: why the line parted, the weather, whose line, what the tug did.<\/li><li><strong>Sign nothing<\/strong> on the spot unless the ship&#x27;s <strong>safety<\/strong> needs it, and refer it to the <strong>owners<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Work out the time to danger from the drift; NUC signals, Pan-Pan and the coastguard (SOSREP in the UK); anchors ready with the windlass limit; towage early on a commercial contract, salvage on LOF 2020 under Art 6(2) if danger develops; reports and records; and for the parted tow, Art 17: no salvage for the towage contract unless beyond it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Towage or salvage — what's the difference?","pa":"<p>Towage is a contract, the ship isn&#x27;t in danger and the price is agreed; salvage needs danger, a voluntary service and success, and the reward is set under Art 13.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q263","sec":7,"q":"Heavy weather: you suspect the anchor has come loose; a mooring rope is missing overboard; the Master is injured; containers are lost. Actions? How do you turn round in heavy weather?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"SOLAS V/31–32 from 1 Jan 2026","a":"<p><strong>Start with speed and heading<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong>, and put the <strong>sea where she rides easiest<\/strong>, following IMO&#x27;s guidance to Masters on adverse weather (<strong>MSC.1/Circ.1228<\/strong>).<\/li><li>On a container ship, break <strong>parametric rolling<\/strong> by changing <strong>course and speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Call the Master, if he isn&#x27;t injured, and the engine room.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>1. Anchor working loose<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>No one goes forward into green water<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Watch it on <strong>CCTV<\/strong>, <strong>sound<\/strong> the <strong>forepeak<\/strong> and the <strong>chain locker<\/strong>, because a loose anchor can hole the hull.<\/li><li>Then pick a <strong>smooth<\/strong> and <strong>turn to run before the sea<\/strong> at <strong>slow speed<\/strong> so the <strong>forecastle stays dry<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>team goes forward<\/strong> under a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong>, with <strong>harnesses<\/strong>, <strong>lifelines<\/strong>, <strong>radios<\/strong> and an <strong>officer in charge<\/strong>, in contact with the bridge.<\/li><li><strong>Secure<\/strong> it with the <strong>brake<\/strong>, the <strong>stopper<\/strong>, the <strong>devil&#x27;s claw<\/strong> and <strong>lashings<\/strong>, and check the spurling pipe covers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Mooring rope missing overboard<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If it&#x27;s <strong>trailing<\/strong>, it can <strong>foul the propeller<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Tell the <strong>engine room<\/strong>, <strong>slow or stop the shaft<\/strong>, and <strong>recover<\/strong> it if safe.<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s fouled the propeller, the ship may be <strong>NUC<\/strong>: show the signals and broadcast a Sécurité.<\/li><li>Find out how it went overboard: poor lashing of the mooring deck.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Master injured<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>First aid<\/strong>, <strong>TMAS<\/strong> advice.<\/li><li>I <strong>take command<\/strong> under the SMS, and <strong>inform the company<\/strong>, who inform the MCA.<\/li><li>Consider <strong>diverting<\/strong> to land him or a <strong>medevac<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Report a <strong>serious injury<\/strong> to the <strong>MAIB<\/strong>.<\/li><li>OLB entries.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Containers lost<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Under <strong>SOLAS V/31–32<\/strong> as amended (<strong>MSC.550(108)<\/strong>, in force <strong>1 January 2026<\/strong>), report <strong>without delay<\/strong> to <strong>ships nearby<\/strong>, the <strong>nearest coastal State<\/strong> and the <strong>flag<\/strong>: the <strong>position<\/strong>, the <strong>number<\/strong> lost, any <strong>DG<\/strong> with <strong>UN numbers<\/strong>, and the containers&#x27; <strong>type and condition<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Check the remaining stacks<\/strong> and <strong>stability<\/strong>, only when it&#x27;s safe.<\/li><li>Inform the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Turning round in heavy weather<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Warn all departments<\/strong>: the engine room, the galley, the crew.<\/li><li><strong>Crew off deck<\/strong>, everything <strong>secured<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Watch the wave groups<\/strong> for a <strong>smooth<\/strong>: waves come in groups, with a lull after a few large ones.<\/li><li><strong>Start the turn<\/strong> at the <strong>end of a big group<\/strong>, so the ship is beam-on during the smoothest water.<\/li><li><strong>Full helm<\/strong> and <strong>increased power<\/strong> through <strong>beam-on<\/strong>, to turn as quickly as possible.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong> once round, to avoid <strong>broaching<\/strong> or being <strong>pooped<\/strong> when running before the sea, and to avoid surf-riding.<\/li><li>Watch the stern and the roll, and be ready to adjust.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Running before the sea<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep the speed lower than the wave speed, and the sea a little on the quarter, not dead astern, to reduce the risk of broaching. Check the parametric-roll guidance for following seas too.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Speed and heading first with MSC.1/Circ.1228; for the anchor, no one into green water and turn to run before the sea before sending a team with a permit; the rope fouling the propeller; taking command and MAIB for the Master; the 2026 lost-container reports; and the technique for turning: wait for a smooth, full helm and power through beam-on, then reduce speed.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is beam-on the critical moment?","pa":"<p>Maximum rolling and the risk of synchronous rolling, with seas boarding over the side.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q264","sec":10,"q":"Lifeboat 5-yearly: who carries it out, who must be present, what is a competent person, what's examined (davit structure — remove paint), proof load & NDT; free-fall version (1.1 × with water bags — how do you verify the weight?).","tier":"red","rep":95,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Lifeboat release tests — fail, Andrews 2022","law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS III/20.11<\/strong> and <strong>MSC.402(96)<\/strong>, and in the UK <strong>MGN 560 (M) Amd 2<\/strong>, with <strong>MGN 555 Amd 1<\/strong> on the authorisation of service providers.<\/p><p><strong>Who carries it out<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Only a certified service provider<\/strong>: the <strong>manufacturer<\/strong>, or a provider it <strong>authorises<\/strong> and the <strong>Administration approves<\/strong>, with <strong>technicians certified<\/strong> for that <strong>make and type<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Not the crew<\/strong>. The crew can&#x27;t overhaul release gear or adjust brakes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Who must be present<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>service engineer<\/strong>, who does the work.<\/li><li>A <strong>ship&#x27;s officer in charge<\/strong>, normally me as <strong>C/O<\/strong>, who controls the job on board, issues the permit, and <strong>briefs everyone<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MCA or RO surveyor<\/strong>, who <strong>witnesses<\/strong> but takes no part.<\/li><li>The bosun and crew to assist with rigging and the area.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What a competent person is<\/strong><\/p><p>Someone with the <strong>training, knowledge and experience<\/strong> to do the job safely and to <strong>recognise defects<\/strong> and their implications. Here, the <strong>certified technician<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Before the test<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong>, records up to date, the area below cleared.<\/li><li>The boat <strong>weighed<\/strong> to prove she hasn&#x27;t <strong>gained weight<\/strong> from water absorbed in the <strong>laminate<\/strong> or buoyancy. A heavy boat means the test load and the davit margin are wrong.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What&#x27;s examined<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>davit structure<\/strong> for <strong>corrosion<\/strong> and <strong>cracks<\/strong>, with <strong>paint removed<\/strong> at <strong>critical welds<\/strong> and <strong>stressed points<\/strong> so they can be seen.<\/li><li><strong>NDT<\/strong> (non-destructive testing, such as dye penetrant or magnetic particle) where required.<\/li><li><strong>Sheaves<\/strong>, <strong>pins<\/strong>, bearings, the <strong>winch<\/strong>, the <strong>brake<\/strong>, the <strong>limit switches<\/strong> and the <strong>falls<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Falls<\/strong>: end-for-end at <strong>30 months<\/strong>, <strong>renewed<\/strong> at <strong>5 years<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The boat: hull, hooks, release cable, hydrostatic interlock, engine.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Tests<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>on-load release gear<\/strong> is <strong>overhauled<\/strong> and <strong>tested<\/strong> at <strong>1.1 × the fully loaded boat<\/strong> mass.<\/li><li>The <strong>winch<\/strong> has a <strong>dynamic brake test<\/strong> at <strong>1.1 ×<\/strong> its maximum working load: <strong>lowered at full speed<\/strong>, then the <strong>brake applied abruptly<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Nobody is in the boat<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Afterwards the <strong>stressed parts<\/strong> are <strong>re-examined<\/strong> for deformation and cracks.<\/li><li>The certificate and report issued.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Free-fall lifeboat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>secondary launching appliance<\/strong> (the davit used for recovery and maintenance) is tested at <strong>1.1 ×<\/strong> with <strong>certified water bags<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Verifying the weight<\/strong>: a <strong>calibrated load cell<\/strong> in line, with its <strong>calibration certificate<\/strong> checked, against the <strong>target load<\/strong> worked out from the boat&#x27;s <strong>data plate<\/strong> (the fully loaded mass × 1.1). Water bags alone can be inaccurate because they&#x27;re filled to a mark.<\/li><li>The free-fall release itself is tested as the maker specifies, often by a simulated launch or a launch into the water with no one on board.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS III/20.11 and MSC.402(96), only a certified provider, the C/O in charge and the surveyor witnessing, the competent person definition, weighing the boat, paint removed at welds and NDT, the 1.1 load tests and dynamic brake test, nobody in the boat, and verifying the free-fall test load with a calibrated load cell.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A dock worker starts stripping the davit — do you let him?","pa":"<p>Only if he&#x27;s the authorised service provider&#x27;s certified technician on a job the company has ordered; otherwise stop the job.<\/p>","asked":["Explain 5 yearly Load test?","Lifeboat 5 yearly, who needs to be present?","Lifeboat 5 yearly tests – MGN 560","Lifeboat Davit weekly, monthly, annual and 5 yearly inspection","Life Boat 5 Yearly test?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",10],["Kaiser Ali",9],["Inamul Bhuiyan",9],["Khairul Anam",8],["Examiner not named",8]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q265","sec":10,"q":"What is the \"5-knot test\" for lifeboats / rescue boats?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>installation test<\/strong> for <strong>lifeboats<\/strong> on <strong>cargo ships of 20,000 GT<\/strong> and above, and for <strong>lifeboats that also serve as rescue boats<\/strong>, whenever <strong>on-load release gear<\/strong> is <strong>fitted or replaced<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It proves the boat can be <strong>launched and released<\/strong> with the ship making <strong>headway<\/strong> at <strong>5 knots<\/strong> in calm water, which is what <strong>SOLAS<\/strong> and the <strong>LSA Code<\/strong> require of those boats.<\/li><li><strong>Rescue boats<\/strong> must also be capable of launching with the ship making headway of up to 5 knots.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>References<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The test method is in <strong>MSC.81(70) Part 2<\/strong> (testing of LSA, the production and installation tests).<\/li><li>IMO&#x27;s <strong>hook-replacement guidelines (MSC.1/Circ.1392)<\/strong> call for it after new hooks are fitted.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How it&#x27;s done<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The boat is lowered and released while held on its <strong>painter<\/strong>, with the ship making about <strong>5 knots through the water<\/strong>.<\/li><li>She is <strong>released<\/strong> and must <strong>get away from the ship&#x27;s side safely<\/strong>, under her own power, without being swamped or striking the hull.<\/li><li>The painter release is tested.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>In port<\/strong><\/p><p>The 5 knots can be <strong>simulated<\/strong>:<\/p><ul><li><strong>alongside in a 5-knot current<\/strong>;<\/li><li>using a <strong>tug&#x27;s wash<\/strong> to create the flow;<\/li><li><strong>towing<\/strong> the boat to give the <strong>equivalent drag<\/strong> on the falls and painter.<\/li><\/ul><p>The <strong>MCA hasn&#x27;t prescribed a single method<\/strong>; any <strong>safe method<\/strong> the <strong>surveyor accepts<\/strong> will do.<\/p><p><strong>Who and how safely<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Done by the <strong>service provider<\/strong> and <strong>witnessed<\/strong> by the <strong>surveyor<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A full <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>, the <strong>rescue boat<\/strong> ready, good <strong>communications<\/strong> between the bridge, the boat and the deck.<\/li><li>The minimum crew in the boat, with lifejackets, helmets and seat belts.<\/li><li>Calm water and good visibility.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>In an abandonment, the ship may not be able to stop. A boat that can&#x27;t release at speed may be dragged and capsized against the ship&#x27;s side.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>An installation test for lifeboats on cargo ships of 20,000 GT and above and lifeboat/rescue boats when on-load release gear is fitted or replaced, proving launch and release at 5 knots, MSC.81(70) and MSC.1/Circ.1392, how it&#x27;s done at sea or simulated in port, and done by the service provider, witnessed by the surveyor, with full safety precautions.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does it matter on a big ship?","pa":"<p>A large ship can&#x27;t stop quickly, so her boats may have to be launched with way still on.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q266","sec":10,"q":"3-monthly lowering & manoeuvring of a davit-launched lifeboat — how do you make it safe? Lifeboat seems sluggish in the water — why?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS III/19<\/strong> requires each lifeboat to be <strong>launched<\/strong> with its <strong>assigned operating crew<\/strong> and <strong>manoeuvred in the water<\/strong> at least every <strong>3 months<\/strong>. We follow <strong>MGN 560 Amd 2<\/strong> and the <strong>SMS<\/strong>. People have died in these drills when boats fell.<\/p><p><strong>To make it safe<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Weekly and monthly checks<\/strong> done, and the <strong>falls<\/strong>, <strong>hooks<\/strong> and <strong>brake<\/strong> in date, with the annual service current.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong>, <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong> and the <strong>Master&#x27;s permission<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Lee side<\/strong>, <strong>calm sea<\/strong>, <strong>propeller stopped<\/strong> if near the stern, the <strong>rescue boat ready<\/strong>, and the area below clear.<\/li><li><strong>Lower the empty boat first<\/strong>, to the water or just above it, and <strong>recover<\/strong> it. That proves the <strong>winch brake<\/strong> and the <strong>release gear<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then <strong>check the hooks are fully reset<\/strong>: the <strong>indicator<\/strong>, the <strong>cam<\/strong> position, the <strong>hydrostatic interlock<\/strong>, the handle and safety pin. Examiner Insan wanted this <strong>pre-test before anyone embarks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>minimum crew embarks<\/strong>, at the embarkation deck, or by <strong>ladder at water level<\/strong> if the SMS allows. They wear <strong>lifejackets<\/strong> and <strong>helmets<\/strong> and use the <strong>seatbelts<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fall-prevention devices<\/strong> only if company policy requires them, and removed before release.<\/li><li><strong>Radio contact<\/strong> between the officer in charge, the <strong>bridge<\/strong> and the <strong>boat<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Engine started<\/strong> before she&#x27;s <strong>waterborne<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Manoeuvre<\/strong> in the water, then recover, with the crew disembarking before the boat is hoisted to the stowed position if the procedure says so.<\/li><li><strong>Debrief<\/strong> and record in the OLB.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Sluggish boat in the water: possible causes<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Water inside<\/strong>: drain it or use the bilge pump.<\/li><li><strong>Water absorbed<\/strong> into the <strong>GRP laminate<\/strong> or buoyancy foam. The MCA has warned that boats become <strong>heavy<\/strong> this way over the years.<\/li><li><strong>Marine growth<\/strong> on the hull or <strong>propeller<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>damaged propeller<\/strong>, or the <strong>gearbox<\/strong> not fully engaged.<\/li><li><strong>Poor engine power<\/strong>: fuel, air filter, injectors.<\/li><li>A <strong>painter<\/strong> or <strong>sea anchor<\/strong> still attached, or a line around the propeller.<\/li><li><strong>Overloading<\/strong>, or too much equipment.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Bring her back<\/strong>, investigate, and <strong>weigh<\/strong> her with a <strong>calibrated load cell<\/strong> against the <strong>certified weight<\/strong> on the data plate.<\/li><li>If she&#x27;s <strong>heavy<\/strong>, she&#x27;s <strong>out of service<\/strong>: the davit and release gear were designed for the certified mass.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the company<\/strong> and the <strong>surveyor<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Record it and get the service provider to assess.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS III/19 every 3 months, MGN 560 Amd 2, RA and Master&#x27;s permission, lower the empty boat first and check the hooks fully reset before anyone embarks, minimum crew with lifejackets, helmets and seatbelts, communications, engine started first; and for a sluggish boat, water inside or absorbed, growth, propeller, gearbox, painter or overloading, and weighing her with a load cell.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The boat is overweight — what now?","pa":"<p>Out of service, service provider to investigate, company informed; flag/RO informed because it affects the SEC; dispensation or a replacement boat.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q267","sec":10,"q":"Accommodation ladder: what's on the marking plate, how is it load-tested, and how often?","tier":"red","rep":44,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-1/3-9<\/strong>, with the IMO guidelines <strong>MSC.1/Circ.1331<\/strong>. Maintenance and inspection follow <strong>SOLAS III/20.7.2<\/strong>. It&#x27;s covered by <strong>SOLAS<\/strong>, not LOLER, and in the UK the Means of Access Regs 1988 apply to its use.<\/p><p><strong>The marking plate, at each end<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>maximum and minimum design angles<\/strong> of inclination.<\/li><li>The <strong>design load<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum load on the bottom platform<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum operational load<\/strong>, if it&#x27;s <strong>less<\/strong> than the design load.<\/li><li>The maker and the year of build.<\/li><li>The <strong>maximum angle<\/strong> is typically <strong>55°<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Testing<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. On installation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>static load test<\/strong> at the <strong>maximum working load<\/strong> (the design load).<\/li><li>The <strong>winch<\/strong> tested through at least <strong>two hoisting and lowering cycles<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Annually, at the SOLAS annual survey<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>thorough examination<\/strong> of:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>steps<\/strong>, <strong>platforms<\/strong>, <strong>pivots<\/strong> and <strong>rollers<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>suspension points<\/strong>, <strong>stanchions<\/strong> and <strong>handrails<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>davit<\/strong>, <strong>wire<\/strong> and <strong>sheaves<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p>For the <strong>winch<\/strong>: the <strong>brake<\/strong>, the <strong>remote control<\/strong> and the <strong>power supply<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>3. Every 5 years<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>operational test<\/strong> of the <strong>ladder<\/strong> and the <strong>winch<\/strong> with the <strong>maximum operational load<\/strong>. That&#x27;s the <strong>design load<\/strong>, or the <strong>marked maximum operational load<\/strong> if lower.<\/li><li>For ladders <strong>without a known design load<\/strong>, the <strong>owner nominates<\/strong> a load, which then becomes the <strong>maximum operational load<\/strong> and is marked.<\/li><li><strong>Water bags<\/strong> or weights are <strong>spread evenly<\/strong> along the length, at the <strong>angle<\/strong> that gives the <strong>maximum bending moment<\/strong> (usually horizontal or the lowest angle).<\/li><li>A <strong>preventer<\/strong> is rigged as <strong>back-up<\/strong>, so if anything fails the ladder doesn&#x27;t fall.<\/li><li>After the test, the ladder is <strong>checked<\/strong> for <strong>permanent deformation<\/strong>, cracks and damage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Wires<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Renewed<\/strong> when they deteriorate, or at least every <strong>5 years<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Results recorded in the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, with the test certificates.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Monthly<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A crew inspection under the PMS: steps, rails, fittings, wire, winch.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-1/3-9 and MSC.1/Circ.1331, not LOLER; the marking plate contents (angles, design load, bottom platform load, operational load); the installation static test, annual thorough examination, and 5-yearly operational test at the maximum operational load with evenly spread weights and a preventer; and wires renewed at 5 years.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Rigging it alongside?","pa":"<p>Lowest platform no more than 600 mm above the waterline at the lightest seagoing draught, resting firmly against the hull, safety net, lifebuoy with line, lighting, and tended as the tide and draught change.<\/p>","asked":["Testing of accommodation ladder?","What is the max angle for Gangway / Accommodation ladder and who has the overall responsibility for its adjustment?","Gangway Requirements?","Gangway to be always manned.","Access gangway from the ship to shore most important."],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",9],["Siddhartha Paul",7],["Tarik Yassin",4],["Nayyer Naqvi",3],["Alec Keep",3]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q268","sec":10,"q":"Enclosed space: before planning the entry, what's your first question? Gases tested, units, limits, the O₂ reading of 22–24% (why?), the COSWP caveat, flammability diagram, which instrument measures hydrocarbons?","tier":"yellow","rep":10,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"MGN 659 Amd 3","a":"<p><strong>My first question<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Does anyone need to go in at all?<\/strong> Could a <strong>camera<\/strong>, remote sounding or another method do the job? If entry is needed, the rules are the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Entry into Enclosed Spaces) Regulations 2022<\/strong>, <strong>MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong>, <strong>COSWP<\/strong>, and <strong>SOLAS XI-1/7<\/strong>, which requires a portable atmosphere tester on board, with the IMO recommendations <strong>MSC.581(110)<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Gases and units<\/strong> (instruments to MSC.1/Circ.1477)<\/p><ol><li><strong>Oxygen<\/strong> in <strong>% by volume<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Flammable gas<\/strong> in <strong>% of LFL<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>CO<\/strong> and <strong>H₂S<\/strong> in <strong>ppm<\/strong>, compared with the <strong>exposure limits<\/strong> in the HSE&#x27;s <strong>EH40<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Add <strong>detector tubes<\/strong> for anything the cargo or the space suggests: benzene, ammonia, CO₂, phosphine.<\/li><li><strong>Test remotely<\/strong>, at <strong>several levels<\/strong> (top, middle, bottom), with <strong>ventilation stopped<\/strong> briefly so the reading represents the space, and <strong>readings steady<\/strong>. Then re-ventilate and keep testing during entry.<\/li><li>The instrument <strong>calibrated<\/strong> and <strong>bump-tested<\/strong> before use.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Limits<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Normal <strong>oxygen<\/strong> is <strong>20.8%<\/strong> (about 21%).<\/li><li><strong>Flammable<\/strong>: nil is desirable, and up to <strong>1% LFL<\/strong> is acceptable if steady.<\/li><li><strong>Toxics<\/strong> below the <strong>OEL<\/strong> (IMO guidance: no more than half).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The COSWP caveat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An oxygen reading of <strong>20%<\/strong> means something has <strong>displaced<\/strong> the oxygen (about 5% of the air has been replaced by another gas), so I&#x27;d <strong>test further<\/strong> for <strong>toxic<\/strong> gases.<\/li><li>A steady 20% is acceptable only once the other risks are ruled out.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>22–24% oxygen<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>That&#x27;s <strong>enrichment<\/strong>, and it&#x27;s <strong>dangerous<\/strong>: clothing, grease and oil <strong>ignite easily<\/strong> and <strong>burn fiercely<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check the meter&#x27;s <strong>calibration<\/strong> first.<\/li><li>Then look for a <strong>leaking oxygen cylinder<\/strong> or a <strong>hot-work hose<\/strong> (oxy-acetylene) in or near the space.<\/li><li><strong>Never ventilate with oxygen<\/strong>. No entry until it&#x27;s normal.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The flammability diagram<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Oxygen<\/strong> (horizontal) against <strong>hydrocarbon<\/strong> content (vertical), with the <strong>flammable zone<\/strong> between the <strong>LFL<\/strong> (about 1%) and the <strong>UFL<\/strong> (about 10%) at 21% O₂, narrowing as O₂ falls to about 11%.<\/li><li>The <strong>critical dilution line<\/strong> shows which mixtures can be <strong>diluted with air<\/strong> without passing through the flammable zone. That&#x27;s why tanks are purged with inert gas below about 2% hydrocarbon before gas-freeing with air.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Hydrocarbon instruments<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>catalytic explosimeter<\/strong> (combustible gas indicator) reads <strong>% LFL<\/strong> but <strong>needs oxygen<\/strong> to work, so it reads wrongly in inert atmospheres.<\/li><li>In <strong>inert<\/strong> or <strong>low-oxygen<\/strong> atmospheres, I&#x27;d use an <strong>infrared<\/strong> meter or a <strong>tankscope<\/strong> (non-catalytic) reading <strong>% by volume<\/strong> hydrocarbon.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Is entry necessary; the Regs, MGN 659 Amd 3, COSWP and SOLAS XI-1/7; the gases and units; testing remotely at levels with steady readings; the limits; the 20% caveat; the danger of 22–24% enrichment and its causes; the flammability diagram with the critical dilution line; and the explosimeter needing oxygen versus the infrared or tankscope for inert atmospheres.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can the entrant's personal gas monitor be used to clear the space?","pa":"<p>No, unless it&#x27;s certified for remote sampling with a pump and hose; personal monitors are for warning during the entry.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q269","sec":10,"q":"Two people collapse inside the forepeak in dry dock (Bosun shouts that the AB is down). Actions and reporting.","tier":"yellow","rep":8,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First I tell the Bosun to get out now.<\/strong> Two casualties mustn&#x27;t become three, and <strong>nobody goes in without breathing apparatus<\/strong>. Most enclosed-space deaths are rescuers.<\/p><p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong>, and <strong>call the yard&#x27;s emergency services<\/strong> and an <strong>ambulance<\/strong>. In dry dock they&#x27;re <strong>closer<\/strong> and better equipped, and our own systems may be shut down.<\/li><li><strong>Rescue team<\/strong> in <strong>SCBA<\/strong> with <strong>lifelines<\/strong>, under the entry control.<\/li><li>Each casualty gets an <strong>escape set<\/strong> or <strong>air supply<\/strong> as soon as they&#x27;re reached.<\/li><li><strong>Increase the ventilation<\/strong>, but don&#x27;t rely on it.<\/li><li>Rig the <strong>tripod<\/strong> and the <strong>Neil Robertson stretcher<\/strong>, and have the <strong>oxygen resuscitator<\/strong> and <strong>CPR<\/strong> ready at the entrance.<\/li><li>The <strong>casualties go to hospital<\/strong>, even if they recover on deck.<\/li><li><strong>Head count<\/strong>, including <strong>yard workers<\/strong>. My <strong>17-year-old cadet<\/strong> must <strong>not<\/strong> take part in the rescue.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Afterwards<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop all enclosed-space work<\/strong>, <strong>withdraw the permit<\/strong> and <strong>secure the scene<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Keep the <strong>gas meter<\/strong> and its <strong>data log<\/strong>, the <strong>permit<\/strong> and the <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Re-test<\/strong> the atmosphere to find the cause. <strong>Rust<\/strong> in a closed forepeak uses up oxygen; paint, cargo residues or inert gas from the yard&#x27;s work can also be the cause.<\/li><li><strong>Statements<\/strong> and <strong>photos<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reports<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master<\/strong> and the <strong>company/DPA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>MAIB<\/strong>: a <strong>death or serious injury<\/strong> on a UK ship is a <strong>marine casualty<\/strong>, <strong>even in dry dock<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>flag (MCA)<\/strong>, the <strong>local authorities or police<\/strong>, and the <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> club.<\/li><li>In a <strong>UK yard<\/strong>, the <strong>HSE<\/strong> may also be involved for yard staff.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If one dies<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>OLB<\/strong> entries: made by the <strong>Master<\/strong> and witnessed by a crew member.<\/li><li>Form <strong>MSF 4605<\/strong> to the <strong>RSS<\/strong>.<\/li><li>An <strong>inquiry<\/strong> under <strong>MSA 1995 s.271<\/strong>, unless a <strong>coroner<\/strong> is investigating.<\/li><li><strong>Next of kin<\/strong> told through the <strong>company<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before sailing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check we still meet the <strong>Safe Manning Document<\/strong>, and get <strong>replacements<\/strong> or an <strong>MCA dispensation<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Give the <strong>crew support<\/strong>: the shock of losing a shipmate, and the guilt of the rescuers.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The lessons<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Why was the space entered? Was there a permit? Was it tested? Was the attendant there?<\/li><li>The Enclosed Spaces Regs 2022 and MGN 659 Amd 3 require all of that, and MSC.581(110) a register of enclosed spaces.<\/li><li>In dry dock, coordinate permits with the yard, so their work doesn&#x27;t affect our spaces.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stop the Bosun going in, alarm and the yard&#x27;s emergency services, a BA rescue team with air for the casualties, tripod and stretcher, oxygen and CPR, head count, keep the cadet out, secure the evidence and find the cause, report to the MAIB even in dry dock, the s.271 inquiry if there&#x27;s a death, and manning before sailing.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why did they collapse when the Bosun was fine at first?","pa":"<p>The atmosphere wasn&#x27;t tested properly or wasn&#x27;t re-tested; oxygen is depleted low down or in pockets, ventilation stopped, or conditions changed.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q270","sec":10,"q":"FW tank: inspection, pressure test (how), coatings, and dirty water from the galley tap. Which guidance?","tier":"yellow","rep":6,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Guidance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The UK guidance is <strong>MSN 1845<\/strong> (food and catering, including drinking water; check the current amendment), which has the <strong>table<\/strong> of <strong>inspection, cleaning and disinfection<\/strong> intervals.<\/li><li>There&#x27;s also the MCA&#x27;s <strong>potable-water<\/strong> guidance (MGN 397 in my notes; verify the current number) and the <strong>WHO Guide to Ship Sanitation<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>MLC Regulation 3.2<\/strong>: suitable drinking water, <strong>free of charge<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Inspection and cleaning<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>It&#x27;s an <strong>enclosed-space entry<\/strong>: risk assessment, permit, ventilation, atmosphere testing, standby, and the entrants in <strong>clean<\/strong> protective clothing and footwear, free of illness.<\/li><li><strong>Drain<\/strong>, <strong>ventilate<\/strong>, then <strong>inspect<\/strong> the <strong>coating<\/strong>, <strong>sediment<\/strong> and <strong>corrosion<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Clean<\/strong> and <strong>wash<\/strong> with fresh water; <strong>no unapproved chemicals<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Repair<\/strong> the coating only with a product <strong>certified for potable water<\/strong>, and let it <strong>cure<\/strong> for the maker&#x27;s <strong>full time<\/strong>, then ventilate.<\/li><li>Then <strong>super-chlorinate<\/strong> (a high chlorine dose for a set contact time), <strong>flush<\/strong>, <strong>refill<\/strong>, and <strong>sample<\/strong> for <strong>bacteria<\/strong> before the tank goes back into use.<\/li><li>Do it at the <strong>MSN intervals<\/strong>, and after <strong>repairs<\/strong> or <strong>suspected contamination<\/strong>; <strong>record<\/strong> it.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Pressure test, by one of two methods<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Hydrostatic<\/strong>: fill to the <strong>top of the air pipe<\/strong> and inspect the <strong>outside<\/strong> of the tank for <strong>leaks<\/strong>. Simple, but uses a lot of water and adds weight.<\/li><li><strong>Low-pressure air<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>blank<\/strong> the <strong>vents<\/strong>, and connect <strong>service air<\/strong> through a <strong>reducing valve<\/strong>, with a <strong>relief valve<\/strong> and a <strong>calibrated gauge<\/strong> or <strong>water manometer<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>press up<\/strong> to the <strong>low pressure<\/strong> the yard or class specifies (verify; my notes say about 0.2 bar);<\/li><li>apply <strong>soapy water<\/strong> to the <strong>welds<\/strong>, <strong>manhole<\/strong> and <strong>pipe penetrations<\/strong>, and look for bubbles;<\/li><li><strong>never over-pressurise<\/strong>: a tank isn&#x27;t designed for much pressure, and can be distorted or burst.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Dirty water from the galley tap<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop using it<\/strong>, put up <strong>notices<\/strong>, and provide <strong>bottled water<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Check other taps<\/strong> to see whether it&#x27;s <strong>local<\/strong> (one pipe) or <strong>system-wide<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the tank, <strong>sample<\/strong>, <strong>flush<\/strong> and <strong>chlorinate<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Inspect<\/strong> the tank and the <strong>hydrophore<\/strong> and any filters.<\/li><li><strong>Find the cause<\/strong>: bunkering from a dirty source, a <strong>tank changeover<\/strong> stirring up sediment, a <strong>rusty pipe<\/strong>, a failed filter or UV unit.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it and <strong>report<\/strong> to the <strong>company<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If anyone is ill, TMAS advice, and consider whether it&#x27;s a notifiable health matter for the next port.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MSN 1845 with its intervals, the potable water guidance and WHO guide, MLC 3.2; inspection as an enclosed space with certified coatings, curing, super-chlorination and bacteria sampling; the two pressure test methods with the low-pressure air precautions; and for dirty tap water, stop use, check other taps, isolate, flush, chlorinate, find the cause and record.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Where would you take fresh water from?","pa":"<p>Approved sources only, through dedicated, marked, capped hoses that are kept off the deck; chlorinate on loading.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q271","sec":10,"q":"Inspect a dry-powder extinguisher and an SCBA set.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-2/14<\/strong>, IMO&#x27;s maintenance guidelines for fire protection systems (<strong>MSC.1/Circ.1432<\/strong>), and the maker&#x27;s instructions.<\/p><p><strong>Dry-powder extinguisher<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>In place<\/strong>, the <strong>right type<\/strong> for the location, <strong>instructions legible<\/strong>, and the <strong>service label<\/strong> and <strong>hydrostatic test date<\/strong> in date.<\/li><li><strong>Stored-pressure type<\/strong>: the <strong>gauge<\/strong> in the <strong>green<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cartridge type<\/strong>: the <strong>cartridge weighed<\/strong> against its <strong>marked weight<\/strong>, and replaced if it&#x27;s lost more than the maker allows.<\/li><li><strong>Safety pin<\/strong> and <strong>tamper seal<\/strong> intact.<\/li><li><strong>Hose<\/strong> and <strong>nozzle<\/strong> clear, not cracked or blocked.<\/li><li><strong>Body<\/strong> free of dents and corrosion, especially at the base.<\/li><li><strong>Bracket<\/strong> secure.<\/li><li><strong>Caking<\/strong>: <strong>invert<\/strong> it and <strong>feel the powder move<\/strong>. Caked powder won&#x27;t discharge.<\/li><li><strong>Annual service<\/strong> by a <strong>competent person<\/strong>, and a discharge and hydrostatic test at the intervals in MSC.1/Circ.1432 (often 10 years).<\/li><li>Record the check on the label and in the PMS.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>SCBA set<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Harness<\/strong>, <strong>straps<\/strong> and <strong>backplate<\/strong> sound, with no cuts or worn buckles.<\/li><li><strong>Cylinder<\/strong> <strong>secured<\/strong>, undamaged, and within its <strong>test date<\/strong> (composite cylinders have a life limit).<\/li><li><strong>Cylinder pressure<\/strong>: a <strong>full charge<\/strong>. If it&#x27;s <strong>below<\/strong> the <strong>maker&#x27;s minimum<\/strong> (often 90%), <strong>change<\/strong> the cylinder.<\/li><li><strong>High-pressure leak test<\/strong>: <strong>open<\/strong> the valve, <strong>close<\/strong> it, and <strong>watch<\/strong> the <strong>gauge<\/strong>. The <strong>drop over a minute<\/strong> must be within the <strong>maker&#x27;s limit<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Low-pressure warning whistle<\/strong>: <strong>bleed slowly<\/strong> and confirm it <strong>sounds<\/strong> at the <strong>maker&#x27;s set pressure<\/strong> (verify; my notes say about 55 bar).<\/li><li><strong>Face mask<\/strong>: <strong>visor<\/strong>, <strong>seals<\/strong> and <strong>straps<\/strong>; a <strong>negative-pressure<\/strong> seal test; the <strong>demand valve<\/strong> gives <strong>positive pressure<\/strong> and <strong>shuts off<\/strong> when you take the mask off.<\/li><li><strong>Spare cylinders<\/strong> charged, two per set on board.<\/li><li>The <strong>compressor<\/strong> air quality tested at the interval in the guidelines (often every 6 months).<\/li><li><strong>Record everything<\/strong> in the <strong>PMS<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>FSS Code<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Each SCBA set must hold at least <strong>1,200 litres<\/strong> of free air, or give at least 30 minutes&#x27; use.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why these checks matter<\/strong><\/p><p>In a fire, an extinguisher that won&#x27;t discharge or a BA set with a leaking cylinder can cost a life. The weekly and monthly checks catch most problems.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>For the extinguisher, location, type, labels and dates, the gauge or cartridge weight, pin and seal, hose, body, and caking, with an annual service; for the SCBA, harness, cylinder date and pressure, the high-pressure leak test, the warning whistle, the mask seal and demand valve, spares, and records; plus 1,200 litres from the FSS Code.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The whistle doesn't sound — what do you do?","pa":"<p>The set is out of service and labelled, a spare is issued, and it goes ashore for service by an approved station.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q272","sec":10,"q":"Painting the bow at anchor / overhauling a crane on passage — plan the job.","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":["STC"],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Both jobs come down to plan, organise and supervise.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Painting the bow at anchor<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Master&#x27;s permission<\/strong>. Check <strong>port rules<\/strong>: many ports <strong>forbid over-side work<\/strong> at anchor, and some forbid painting because of pollution.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>weather<\/strong>, <strong>swell<\/strong>, <strong>traffic<\/strong> and <strong>current<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong>, a <strong>permit to work<\/strong> for <strong>over-side work at height<\/strong>, and a <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong>. The <strong>Work at Height Regulations 2010<\/strong> and <strong>COSWP<\/strong> apply.<\/li><li><strong>Stage<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>inspect<\/strong> the stage and its <strong>ropes<\/strong>, which must be in good condition and <strong>kept only for staging<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>test<\/strong> it with a <strong>load close to the deck<\/strong> before use;<\/li><li><strong>secure<\/strong> the ropes to <strong>strong points<\/strong>, not to the <strong>rails<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Each man wears a <strong>harness<\/strong> on an <strong>independent lifeline<\/strong> made fast on deck, and a <strong>lifejacket<\/strong>. <strong>Tools on lanyards<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>lifebuoy<\/strong> with a <strong>line<\/strong> ready, the <strong>rescue boat<\/strong> ready, and a <strong>competent person on deck<\/strong> at all times with a <strong>radio<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>windlass brake<\/strong> and <strong>stopper<\/strong> on, <strong>nobody near the hawse<\/strong>, and no anchor work while the stage is rigged.<\/li><li>The <strong>bridge<\/strong> keeps <strong>anchor watch<\/strong> and <strong>warns<\/strong> of passing <strong>wash<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>No paint into the sea<\/strong>: drip trays, careful handling, the spill kit ready; <strong>no smoking<\/strong>.<\/li><li>No work over the side near the overboard discharges, and the engine room told not to discharge.<\/li><li>Stop if the weather changes.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Overhauling a crane on passage<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Follow the <strong>PMS<\/strong> and the <strong>maker&#x27;s manual<\/strong>, with <strong>competent people<\/strong>, normally the ETO and engineers with the deck crew.<\/li><li><strong>Calm weather<\/strong>, the <strong>crane stowed<\/strong> in its <strong>rest<\/strong> and secured.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> it with <strong>lock-out/tag-out<\/strong>: electrical and hydraulic.<\/li><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong> for <strong>work at height<\/strong> on the crane, harnesses and fall arrest.<\/li><li><strong>Lifting the parts<\/strong>: plan how heavy components will be handled, with the right gear.<\/li><li>If a <strong>new wire<\/strong> is fitted, check its <strong>certificate<\/strong> matches the <strong>size<\/strong> and <strong>construction<\/strong> the maker specifies, and record it in the <strong>register of lifting appliances<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>After the repair<\/strong>, under <strong>LOLER 2006<\/strong> (MGN 332), a <strong>thorough examination<\/strong> by a <strong>competent person<\/strong> before the crane is used, plus a <strong>proof load test<\/strong> if the repair affects its <strong>strength<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Function test the limit switches, brakes and emergency stops.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the PMS and the register.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Common to both<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brief the bridge and the engine room.<\/li><li>Supervise throughout, and stop the job if conditions change.<\/li><li>Close the permit and restore the isolations when finished.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>For painting at anchor, the Master&#x27;s permission and port rules, RA and permit, the stage tested and secured to strong points, harness on an independent lifeline and lifejacket, lifebuoy and rescue boat, windlass secured, the bridge warning of wash, no pollution; for the crane, maker&#x27;s manual, isolation, permit, certified wire, and a LOLER thorough examination and possibly a proof test before use.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who can be the competent person under LOLER?","pa":"<p>Someone with the practical and theoretical knowledge and experience to detect defects and judge their importance, independent enough to be impartial; often class or a specialist firm.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q273","sec":12,"q":"How do you work out the maximum cargo you can load? Which single document tells you what weight the ship can carry?","tier":"red","rep":45,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First I find the governing draught<\/strong><\/p><p>It&#x27;s the <strong>most restrictive<\/strong> of these limits:<\/p><ol><li>The <strong>load line mark<\/strong> for the <strong>zone and season<\/strong> I&#x27;m in, and for <strong>every zone on the route<\/strong>, because she must be at the correct mark when she <strong>enters a stricter zone<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>port, berth and canal<\/strong> draughts, with the UKC policy.<\/li><li>The <strong>air draught<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Strength and stability limits from the loadicator.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Back-calculate from the most restrictive point<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Take that draught and <strong>add<\/strong> the <strong>fuel and water burned<\/strong> before we reach it.<\/li><li>That gives my <strong>sailing draught<\/strong> from this port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Correct for dock water<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the dock water is <strong>less dense<\/strong> than 1.025, I can <strong>submerge the mark<\/strong> by the <strong>dock water allowance<\/strong>:<\/li><li><strong>DWA = FWA × (1025 − ρ) ÷ 25<\/strong>, with ρ the dock water density in kg/m³.<\/li><li>She&#x27;ll <strong>rise to the mark<\/strong> when she reaches sea water.<\/li><li>The FWA is on the Load Line certificate, or <strong>FWA = Δ ÷ (4 × TPC)<\/strong> mm.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then the cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>From the <strong>deadweight scale<\/strong> I read the <strong>deadweight<\/strong> at that draught.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo = deadweight − fuel − fresh water − stores − ballast I must keep − the constant<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>constant<\/strong> is the unknown weight on board (sediment, stores not accounted for), found from previous draught surveys.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then I check that the cargo fits and is safe<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>slots<\/strong> or the <strong>hold volume<\/strong> and the <strong>stowage factor<\/strong>: will it physically fit?<\/li><li><strong>Tank-top<\/strong> and <strong>hatch-cover<\/strong> loads, and on my ship <strong>stack weights<\/strong>, <strong>lashing limits<\/strong> and the <strong>bridge visibility<\/strong> line (SOLAS V/22).<\/li><li><strong>Shear forces<\/strong> and <strong>bending moments<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>stability criteria<\/strong> at <strong>every stage<\/strong>, on the <strong>loadicator<\/strong>, including the arrival condition.<\/li><li>Trim and propeller immersion.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The single document<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>capacity plan<\/strong> with its <strong>deadweight scale<\/strong>. It gives the <strong>deadweight<\/strong> against <strong>draught<\/strong> in <strong>salt and fresh water<\/strong>, the <strong>TPC<\/strong>, the displacement, and the <strong>load line marks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Load Line Certificate<\/strong> gives the <strong>assigned freeboards<\/strong> and the <strong>FWA<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>stability booklet<\/strong> and <strong>loading manual<\/strong> give the <strong>limits<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Worked example<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Summer draught 12.0 m, but the ship will enter a winter zone after burning 300 t of fuel. Winter draught 11.75 m, TPC 60 t/cm.<\/li><li>300 t ÷ 60 = 5 cm, so sailing draught = 11.75 + 0.05 = 11.80 m (less than summer, so this governs).<\/li><li>Dock density 1.010: DWA = FWA (say 250 mm) × 15 ÷ 25 = 150 mm, so load to 11.95 m in the dock.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The governing draught as the most restrictive of load line zones on the route, port depths and air draught; back-calculating with consumption; the DWA formula; deadweight minus consumables, ballast and constant; checking that it fits and is safe; and the capacity plan with the deadweight scale as the single document.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"You're down to your marks and the terminal still has ten boxes for you?","pa":"<p>They stay ashore. I won&#x27;t exceed the marks or the limits. I tell the Master, the planner and the terminal, check the VGMs if she&#x27;s deeper than calculated, and record the short shipment.<\/p>","asked":["How would you know maximum cargo to load?","How would you know how much cargo to load?","How will you calculate how much cargo you can load?","You are on ballast passage, Company found Charter, they want to know how much cargo you can load?","How do you know how much cargo you can load?"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",10],["Colum Leggett",6],["Shahid Akhter",5],["Ahsan Khan",5],["Muhammad Iqbal",3]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q274","sec":12,"q":"You're told to load an unfamiliar bulk cargo (pet coke, fertiliser, iron ore stored in the open, grain after sulphur). Process?","tier":"yellow","rep":6,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Carriage of Cargoes) Regulations<\/strong> (check the current version), and internationally <strong>SOLAS VI and VII<\/strong>, which make the <strong>IMSBC Code<\/strong> mandatory.<\/p><p><strong>First, the cargo information<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>shipper<\/strong> must give me the <strong>cargo information<\/strong> before loading (<strong>IMSBC section 4<\/strong>, SOLAS VI/2).<\/li><li>I read the cargo&#x27;s <strong>individual schedule<\/strong> in the IMSBC Code:<\/li><li><strong>Group A<\/strong> may <strong>liquefy<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>Group B<\/strong> has a <strong>chemical hazard<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>Group C<\/strong> has neither.<\/li><li>I check the hazards, the stowage and segregation, the hold cleanliness, ventilation, weather precautions, and the loading and carriage requirements.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If the cargo isn&#x27;t listed<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It <strong>can&#x27;t be loaded<\/strong> until the <strong>competent authorities<\/strong> of the <strong>loading port<\/strong>, the <strong>flag<\/strong> and the <strong>discharge port<\/strong> agree the <strong>conditions<\/strong>. That&#x27;s the <strong>tripartite agreement<\/strong>, under IMSBC 1.3.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>For Group A: two certificates<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>TML certificate<\/strong>, from a test within <strong>6 months<\/strong> of loading.<\/li><li>The <strong>moisture content certificate<\/strong>, from sampling and testing <strong>no more than 7 days<\/strong> before loading.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>The moisture content must be <strong>below the TML<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>can test<\/strong> is my check; if it shows <strong>free water<\/strong>, I <strong>stop<\/strong> and ask for a <strong>retest<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The examples<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Iron ore fines<\/strong>: <strong>Group A<\/strong>, they can <strong>liquefy<\/strong>. Ore <strong>stored in the open<\/strong> and <strong>rained on<\/strong> may be <strong>wetter<\/strong> than its certificate says, so I&#x27;d want a <strong>retest<\/strong> and <strong>stop loading in rain<\/strong>. Several ships have capsized from liquefied nickel ore and iron ore fines.<\/li><li><strong>Pet coke<\/strong>: Group B in some forms; check the schedule for <strong>temperature limits<\/strong> (hot pet coke can be loaded only below a set temperature, and not over fuel tanks that are heated), and the dust.<\/li><li><strong>Fertiliser<\/strong>: ammonium nitrate-based fertilisers are Group B oxidisers, which can decompose and support fire; segregation from fuel, heat and combustibles, and the hold clean of organic residues.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then apply the schedule<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Hold cleanliness<\/strong>, <strong>ventilation<\/strong>, <strong>segregation<\/strong> from other cargoes and heat sources, and <strong>temperature limits<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>loading plan<\/strong> agreed with the terminal under the <strong>BLU Code<\/strong>, checking <strong>stresses<\/strong> and <strong>stability<\/strong> for each <strong>pour<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Gas monitoring where needed, and enclosed space precautions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Grain after sulphur<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Grain<\/strong> comes under the <strong>Grain Code<\/strong>, not IMSBC.<\/li><li><strong>Sulphur<\/strong> residue and water make <strong>acid<\/strong>, and sulphur dust contaminates food cargo.<\/li><li>So I&#x27;d <strong>fresh-water wash<\/strong> thoroughly, check for acid corrosion, <strong>dry<\/strong> the holds, and pass the <strong>hold inspection<\/strong> to <strong>grain standard<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The shipper&#x27;s information and the individual schedule, the groups, the tripartite agreement for unlisted cargo, the TML (6 months) and MC (7 days) certificates with the can test, the rain risk for ore stored in the open, the specific hazards for pet coke and fertiliser, the BLU plan, and grain after sulphur needing washing and inspection.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the TML?","pa":"<p>The maximum moisture content considered safe for carriage. For the flow-table method it&#x27;s 90% of the flow moisture point.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q275","sec":12,"q":"Loading faster than you can deballast / ballast pump fails / you need a ballast hold to reduce air draught. Actions?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Under <strong>SOLAS VI/7<\/strong> and the <strong>BLU Code<\/strong>, the <strong>loading plan<\/strong> agreed with the terminal <strong>includes<\/strong> the <strong>ballast sequence<\/strong>. The <strong>Master can stop<\/strong> loading at <strong>any time<\/strong>, and the <strong>terminal must stop<\/strong> when asked.<\/p><p><strong>Loading ahead of deballasting<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Ask the terminal to stop or slow down<\/strong> at once.<\/li><li><strong>Read<\/strong> the <strong>actual draughts<\/strong> and <strong>trim<\/strong>, and check <strong>shear forces<\/strong> and <strong>bending moments<\/strong> on the loadicator against that stage of the plan.<\/li><li><strong>Agree<\/strong> a <strong>revised plan in writing<\/strong> before restarting: <strong>smaller pours<\/strong>, <strong>longer pauses<\/strong>, or a <strong>changed hold sequence<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Increase the deballasting: all pumps, gravity, the eductors.<\/li><li>Record it, and issue a letter of protest if the terminal didn&#x27;t follow the agreed plan.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Ballast pump fails<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop loading<\/strong> and <strong>tell<\/strong> the <strong>engine room<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>other pump<\/strong>, the <strong>eductor<\/strong>, or <strong>gravity<\/strong> if the tide and draught allow.<\/li><li>If I <strong>can&#x27;t deballast<\/strong>, I <strong>can&#x27;t follow the plan<\/strong>. I <strong>re-plan<\/strong> that stage with <strong>less cargo<\/strong>, and keep the <strong>stresses<\/strong> within the <strong>harbour limits<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Inform the Master, the terminal and the company. Get the pump repaired.<\/li><li>Consider the BWMS: if the treatment system has failed, the BWM plan&#x27;s contingency measures apply, and the port State must be informed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Ballasting a hold to reduce air draught<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For example, to get under the shiploader, or a bridge.<\/li><li>I may <strong>only<\/strong> use the hold <strong>designated<\/strong> as a <strong>ballast hold<\/strong> in the <strong>loading manual<\/strong> and <strong>stability booklet<\/strong>. Other holds aren&#x27;t built for the pressure of water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before filling<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The hold is <strong>clean<\/strong>: no cargo residues to pollute the ballast.<\/li><li>The <strong>bilge suctions<\/strong> are <strong>blanked<\/strong> and the <strong>ballast line<\/strong> is <strong>connected<\/strong>: the <strong>spool piece<\/strong> or <strong>blank<\/strong> changed over.<\/li><li>The <strong>hatch covers<\/strong> are <strong>closed<\/strong> and <strong>secured<\/strong>.<\/li><li>I&#x27;ve checked the <strong>stresses<\/strong> and <strong>free surface<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>At sea<\/strong>, the hold must be <strong>full or empty<\/strong>, <strong>never slack<\/strong>, unless the loading manual allows it, because sloshing can damage the structure and the free surface is huge.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Every movement<\/strong> goes in the <strong>Ballast Water Record Book<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>BWM plan<\/strong> and <strong>local rules<\/strong> apply when it&#x27;s <strong>discharged<\/strong>, including the D-2 standard.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS VI/7 and the BLU Code with the ship&#x27;s right to stop; for loading ahead, stop, check the actual stresses, and agree a revised plan in writing; for a pump failure, alternatives and re-planning with less cargo within the harbour limits; for a ballast hold, only the designated one, clean, bilge suctions blanked and ballast line connected, covers secured, full or empty at sea; and the BWRB.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why not part-fill the ballast hold at sea?","pa":"<p>Sloshing can damage the hatch covers and structure, and there&#x27;s a large free surface effect.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q276","sec":12,"q":"Steel coils / wind turbines / very heavy deck cargo: hazards, securing and stability.","tier":"orange","rep":21,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>CSS Code<\/strong> and the ship&#x27;s approved <strong>Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS VI/5 and VII/5<\/strong>). For anything the CSM doesn&#x27;t cover, I use the <strong>CSS Code&#x27;s assessment method<\/strong> (Annex 13). Its rule of thumb: the <strong>total MSL<\/strong> of the <strong>lashings<\/strong> on <strong>each side<\/strong> must at least <strong>equal<\/strong> the <strong>weight<\/strong> of the unit (for a unit in tonnes, the lashings&#x27; MSL in kN at least about 9.81 × weight).<\/p><p><strong>Steel coils<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The main hazard is <strong>point loading<\/strong> of the <strong>tank top<\/strong>. I check the <strong>permissible load<\/strong> per square metre in the <strong>loading manual<\/strong>, and spread it with <strong>dunnage<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Stowed with the <strong>eye fore-and-aft<\/strong>, on <strong>athwartships dunnage<\/strong>, in rows across the hold.<\/li><li><strong>Wedged<\/strong>, with a <strong>locking coil<\/strong> in each <strong>tier<\/strong>, and <strong>lashed<\/strong> into one <strong>solid block<\/strong>, so no coil can move.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilate<\/strong> against <strong>sweat<\/strong>, because condensation causes rust claims.<\/li><li><strong>Clause<\/strong> the <strong>mate&#x27;s receipt<\/strong> for <strong>wet<\/strong> or <strong>rusty<\/strong> coils.<\/li><li>Heavy weather is the danger: a loose coil can break through the ship&#x27;s side.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Wind turbine blades and towers<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Long<\/strong>, <strong>light<\/strong> and <strong>high<\/strong>, so big <strong>windage<\/strong> and <strong>overhang<\/strong> beyond the ship&#x27;s side.<\/li><li><strong>Sea-fastening<\/strong> to the <strong>shipper&#x27;s approved plan<\/strong>, with engineered saddles and frames.<\/li><li><strong>No welding<\/strong> to the ship&#x27;s structure without <strong>class approval<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The stow must <strong>not breach<\/strong> the <strong>bridge visibility<\/strong> rules of <strong>SOLAS V/22<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Lashing forces from wind and green seas, and accelerations at the ends.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s stability with the high, wind-exposed cargo: the weather criterion matters.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Very heavy cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Low down<\/strong>, it gives a <strong>large GM<\/strong>. The ship is <strong>stiff<\/strong>, <strong>rolls violently<\/strong>, and the <strong>lashing forces<\/strong> go up.<\/li><li>High on deck, it raises G and reduces GM.<\/li><li>Either way, the stability and the lashing calculation must be done for the actual condition.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Lifting with the ship&#x27;s own crane<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The moment the load <strong>leaves the quay<\/strong>, its <strong>weight acts at the crane head<\/strong>, so <strong>G rises<\/strong> immediately.<\/li><li>As the load is <strong>swung out<\/strong>, the ship <strong>lists<\/strong>: <strong>tan θ = w × outreach ÷ (Δ × GM)<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Before the lift<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>GM<\/strong> checked at the <strong>worst moment<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>slack tanks<\/strong> pressed up or emptied;<\/li><li><strong>moorings tended<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>gangway clear<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>crane SWL<\/strong> and the <strong>LOLER<\/strong> examination checked;<\/li><li>a <strong>lifting plan<\/strong> and <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The CSS Code and CSM, with the rule of thumb for lashing MSL against weight; for steel coils, point loading, eye fore-and-aft on dunnage, locking coils and a solid block, sweat and clausing; for turbine parts, windage, overhang, approved sea-fastening, no welding without class, and visibility; for heavy cargo, stiff GM and lashing forces; and the crane lift stability.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does G rise as soon as the weight is lifted, not when it's landed?","pa":"<p>The load hangs from the head, so it acts there. New KG = (Δ·KG + w·Kh)/(Δ + w), where Kh is the height of the crane head above the keel.<\/p>","asked":["How will you load Steel coil?","What will be your concerns as chief officer while loading steel coils as chief officer?","Q – loading procedure of steel coil?","What is a 6x36 wire ?","Now you are shipping a cargo of steel coiled cargoes on a smaller vessel. What specific considerations would you make with this cargo?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",8],["Chris Roe",5],["Bill Bennett",3],["Nayyer Naqvi",2],["Doug Smith",2]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q277","sec":12,"q":"A junior officer loads a DG container with no paperwork, judging by its packaging. A DG container leaks on the jetty / in the hold. Where can you stow a flammable paint container?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Loading DG with no paperwork: it&#x27;s illegal<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>SOLAS VII/4<\/strong> and <strong>IMDG Code 5.4<\/strong> require the <strong>shipper&#x27;s DG transport document<\/strong> (the declaration) and the <strong>container packing certificate<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Dangerous Goods and Marine Pollutants) Regulations 1997<\/strong> require the declaration <strong>before<\/strong> the goods are <strong>taken on board<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Packaging can be mislabelled<\/strong>, and many incidents come from misdeclared DG. So a junior officer&#x27;s judgement isn&#x27;t evidence of what&#x27;s inside.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Isolate<\/strong> the box, and check its condition.<\/li><li>Ask the <strong>terminal<\/strong> and the <strong>agent<\/strong> for the <strong>documents<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If they <strong>can&#x27;t produce them<\/strong>, the <strong>container comes off<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Debrief<\/strong> the officer: credit for initiative, but explain why it was wrong and the legal position. Review the DG loading procedure and the briefing.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>DG container leaking on the jetty<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Refuse<\/strong> it. It&#x27;s the <strong>terminal&#x27;s emergency<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Crew clear<\/strong> and <strong>upwind<\/strong>, <strong>accommodation vents<\/strong> closed.<\/li><li>Inform the terminal and the port, and record it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>DG container leaking in the hold<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong>, and get the <strong>UN number<\/strong> from the <strong>DG manifest<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Follow the <strong>EmS Guide<\/strong> <strong>spillage schedule<\/strong> for <strong>PPE<\/strong> and <strong>ventilation<\/strong>, and <strong>MFAG<\/strong> for any <strong>casualties<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>No entry<\/strong> without <strong>SCBA<\/strong> and <strong>chemical suits<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Call the <strong>shipper&#x27;s 24-hour emergency<\/strong> number on the DG declaration.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the <strong>port<\/strong>, or at sea to the <strong>coastal State<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS VII/6<\/strong> and <strong>MARPOL Annex III<\/strong> and Protocol I), and to the <strong>company<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Stop cargo work in that hold, and consider ventilation, fire precautions and the effect on adjacent cargo.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Where can I stow a flammable paint container?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Flammable paint is <strong>Class 3<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>IMDG Dangerous Goods List<\/strong> gives its <strong>stowage category<\/strong> and any &quot;<strong>away from heat<\/strong>&quot; requirement, and the packing group and flashpoint.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s <strong>DG Document of Compliance<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS II-2/19<\/strong>) shows which <strong>holds<\/strong> are approved for <strong>Class 3<\/strong> with a <strong>low flashpoint<\/strong> (below 23°C). Holds without certified safe electrical equipment or sufficient ventilation can&#x27;t take it.<\/li><li><strong>On deck<\/strong> is usually acceptable.<\/li><li>Then apply <strong>segregation<\/strong>, for example from <strong>Class 5.1<\/strong> oxidisers, and keep it <strong>clear<\/strong> of the <strong>accommodation<\/strong> and <strong>heat sources<\/strong> (heated fuel tanks, the engine room bulkhead).<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No DG without the declaration and packing certificate under SOLAS VII/4, IMDG 5.4 and the 1997 Regs, the box comes off if the documents can&#x27;t be produced, and debrief the officer; a leak on the jetty is refused; a leak in the hold uses the EmS, MFAG, SCBA, the shipper&#x27;s emergency number and reporting under VII/6; and Class 3 paint stowage by the DGL, the DOC and segregation.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Where's the emergency contact for the cargo?","pa":"<p>On the DG transport document. It&#x27;s the shipper&#x27;s 24-hour emergency response number.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q278","sec":13,"q":"Non-standard docking: forepart damaged so you need 7 m trim; docking laden; floating dock vs graving dock. How do you do it safely?","tier":"orange","rep":21,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Any non-standard docking is agreed in advance with the dock master.<\/strong><\/p><p>I send:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>docking plan<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>damage details<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>draughts<\/strong> and <strong>trim<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>stability condition<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>cargo plan<\/strong> if she&#x27;s laden.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A large trim: 7 m by the stern because the forepart is damaged<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>critical period<\/strong> runs from the <strong>stern touching<\/strong> the blocks until she <strong>sits on the blocks overall<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>upthrust<\/strong> at the stern: <strong>P = (change of trim in cm × MCTC) ÷ l<\/strong>, where <strong>l<\/strong> is the distance from the <strong>LCF<\/strong> to the <strong>after block<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>virtual loss of GM = P × KM ÷ Δ<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>big trim<\/strong> means a <strong>big P<\/strong> and a <strong>big loss of GM<\/strong>, and a large load on the aftermost blocks.<\/li><li>So I&#x27;d <strong>first try to reduce the trim<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>discharging<\/strong> cargo or fuel from aft;<\/li><li><strong>shifting weights<\/strong> forward if the damage allows;<\/li><li><strong>ballasting<\/strong> forward tanks that are intact.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>If the <strong>damage makes that impossible<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the <strong>dock builds the blocks<\/strong> to suit the <strong>trim<\/strong> (a declivity matching the ship); or<\/li><li>we use a <strong>floating dock<\/strong>, which can be <strong>trimmed<\/strong> to <strong>match<\/strong> the ship.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Either way I <strong>calculate GM<\/strong> at the <strong>critical instant<\/strong> and keep it <strong>positive<\/strong> with a <strong>margin<\/strong>: tanks <strong>full or empty<\/strong>, <strong>weights lowered<\/strong>, <strong>no list<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The damaged compartments forward may be flooded: account for them as added weight or lost buoyancy, and the damage stability.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Docking laden<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Avoid it<\/strong> if possible by <strong>discharging<\/strong> first.<\/li><li>In dock the hull rests <strong>only on the blocks<\/strong>, so the <strong>cargo weight<\/strong> causes <strong>local stresses<\/strong>, and <strong>hogging or sagging<\/strong> as the water drains.<\/li><li>The dock places <strong>extra blocks<\/strong> and <strong>shores<\/strong> under <strong>heavy weights<\/strong> and <strong>bulkheads<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It needs <strong>class approval<\/strong> and the dock master&#x27;s calculations.<\/li><li>Higher P at the critical instant, so the GM calculation is even more important.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Floating dock vs graving dock<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>floating dock<\/strong> has a <strong>lifting capacity<\/strong> and its own <strong>stability<\/strong>: the dock master checks <strong>both<\/strong>, with the ship&#x27;s weight and centre of gravity. It <strong>can trim and list<\/strong> to match the ship, which helps with a trimmed or damaged ship. The combined ship-and-dock stability matters during lifting.<\/li><li>A <strong>graving dock<\/strong> is <strong>fixed<\/strong>, and limited by the <strong>sill depth<\/strong> and the <strong>tide<\/strong>. The blocks can be built to a declivity, but the dock can&#x27;t trim.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Communication<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>During docking, the dock master, the Master and I agree the stages, and the ship&#x27;s soundings and draughts are watched.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Agreement in advance with the dock master and the information sent; for a large trim, P and the loss of GM, reduce the trim first, then blocks built to the trim or a floating dock, and GM positive at the critical instant; for laden docking, avoid it, the local stresses and extra blocks and shores, and class approval; and the floating versus graving dock difference.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does the loss of GM happen?","pa":"<p>As the water falls, part of the weight is taken by the block at the stern. The upthrust acts like removing a weight from the keel, so G rises virtually and GM falls. It&#x27;s greatest just before she settles on the blocks overall.<\/p>","asked":["Emergency dry dock. Procedure?","Drydocking – Why do we need small trim?","The master informed you the vessel is going for dry dock to repair damages during grounding. Howwill you prepare the vessel for dry dock as a chief mate?","Due to the weather your vessel suffers damage and requires an emergency dry dock. Actions and how would you prepare for this?","After that tug arrived and You are going to proceed to dry dock in fully laden condition as you can't discharge the cargo due to the damage, what precautions are you going to have with regards to going into the dock in this condition ?"],"ex":[["Inamul Bhuiyan",3],["David Carlisle",2],["Michele Moro",2],["Khademul Insan",2],["Nayyer Naqvi",1]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q279","sec":13,"q":"Dry dock H&S: controlling contractors and dangerous areas; enclosed spaces in dock; bottom plugs.","tier":"orange","rep":21,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>In dock the ship is part of a shipyard<\/strong>, and most accidents happen where crew and contractors meet.<\/p><p><strong>Control<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At the <strong>start meeting<\/strong>, I agree with the <strong>yard<\/strong> <strong>who controls what<\/strong>: which permit system applies, who does fire watches, who has the gas-free certificates.<\/li><li>Then I hold a <strong>daily coordination meeting<\/strong> with the <strong>yard safety officer<\/strong> to cover the day&#x27;s <strong>jobs<\/strong>, <strong>hot work<\/strong>, <strong>open tanks<\/strong>, <strong>overside work<\/strong> and <strong>isolations<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>1. Permits<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Yard permits<\/strong> and the ship&#x27;s <strong>SMS permits<\/strong> are <strong>matched<\/strong>. Where they differ, we adopt the stricter.<\/li><li><strong>No hot work<\/strong> or <strong>tank entry<\/strong> without a <strong>permit<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>yard&#x27;s gas-free certificate<\/strong> covers their workers.<\/li><li>My <strong>crew still test<\/strong> the atmosphere themselves and use our own <strong>enclosed-space permit<\/strong> (Enclosed Spaces Regs 2022, <strong>MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong>).<\/li><li>A <strong>single permit controller<\/strong> for hot work and enclosed spaces, so permits don&#x27;t conflict.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Fire<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Fire main pressurised<\/strong> from <strong>shore<\/strong>, with the international shore connection if needed.<\/li><li><strong>Detection<\/strong> kept <strong>live<\/strong> where possible.<\/li><li><strong>Fire watch<\/strong> during and after <strong>hot work<\/strong>, including the <strong>far side<\/strong> of bulkheads and decks, for at least 30 minutes after (company figure).<\/li><li><strong>Night fire patrols<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>CO₂ system locked off<\/strong> while people work in the engine room, with the lock-off <strong>recorded<\/strong>, and restored before sailing.<\/li><li>Extinguishers at every hot work site.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Access and dangerous areas<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>safe gangway<\/strong> with a <strong>net<\/strong> and <strong>lighting<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>dock floor<\/strong>, <strong>open hatches<\/strong>, <strong>open manholes<\/strong> and <strong>staging<\/strong> barriered off.<\/li><li>A <strong>head count<\/strong> of crew and contractors, with a boarding log.<\/li><li>A <strong>muster point ashore<\/strong>, because the ship&#x27;s own emergency arrangements may not work in dock.<\/li><li>Electrical isolations with lock-out and tag-out.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Enclosed spaces in dock<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Tanks opened for inspection are enclosed spaces until tested.<\/li><li>Ventilation through both manholes, plus forced ventilation.<\/li><li>Yard workers and crew follow the same rules; the yard&#x27;s emergency services know which spaces are open.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Bottom plugs<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>ship&#x27;s officer<\/strong> sees <strong>every plug removed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Each is <strong>labelled<\/strong> with its <strong>tank<\/strong>, <strong>recorded<\/strong> on the <strong>plug plan<\/strong>, and kept <strong>locked<\/strong> in <strong>my custody<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Before flooding<\/strong>, each one is <strong>refitted<\/strong> and <strong>sealed<\/strong> in <strong>my presence<\/strong>, witnessed by the <strong>class surveyor<\/strong> where required.<\/li><li>I <strong>check<\/strong> the plugs against the <strong>plan<\/strong>, and <strong>sound<\/strong> the tanks against the <strong>docking soundings<\/strong>.<\/li><li>As the dock <strong>floods<\/strong>, people check the <strong>engine room<\/strong> and <strong>tanks<\/strong> for <strong>leaks<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>6. The crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Briefed on the yard&#x27;s rules, alarms and emergency numbers.<\/li><li>Young persons and new joiners closely supervised.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Agreement with the yard at the start, daily coordination, matched permits with our own atmosphere testing, fire main pressurised, fire watches, CO₂ locked off and recorded, access and barriers, head counts and a muster point ashore, enclosed spaces, and bottom plugs controlled by an officer from removal to refitting, with soundings checked.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The yard says a tank is gas-free — can your AB go in?","pa":"<p>Not on their certificate alone. The ship&#x27;s permit is issued, the atmosphere is re-tested by us, and a standby and rescue plan is in place.<\/p>","asked":["Checks before the flooding the dock?","Before flooding what all check u will carry out ?","How is the preparation before flooding the Drydock?","What would you check prior to flooding the dock?","What considerations to make before undocking?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",10],["Fazal Wadood",3],["Ahsan Khan",2],["Khurram Arbab",2],["Chris Roe",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q280","sec":13,"q":"Hydrostatics: TPC, MCTC, TMD formulas; FWA formula; LCF/LCB/LCG; KN and GZ; how to find GM from the GZ curve; going from salt to fresh water — what happens to draught and trim and about which point?","tier":"red","rep":69,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The formulae<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>TPC = A × ρ ÷ 100<\/strong>: tonnes per 1 cm sinkage, where <strong>A<\/strong> is the <strong>waterplane area<\/strong> (m²) and <strong>ρ<\/strong> the density in t/m³. <strong>Sinkage (cm) = w ÷ TPC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>MCTC = Δ × GML ÷ (100 × L)<\/strong>: the <strong>moment to change trim<\/strong> 1 cm. <strong>Change of trim (cm) = trimming moment ÷ MCTC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>TMD (true mean draught)<\/strong> is the draught at the <strong>LCF<\/strong>: <strong>draught aft ± trim × (LCF from AP) ÷ LBP<\/strong>. I enter the hydrostatic tables with the <strong>TMD<\/strong>, not the simple mean of the forward and aft draughts, because the ship trims about the LCF.<\/li><li><strong>FWA (mm) = Δ ÷ (4 × TPC)<\/strong> at <strong>summer displacement<\/strong> in salt water.<\/li><li><strong>DWA = FWA × (1025 − ρ) ÷ 25<\/strong>, with ρ the dock water density in kg/m³.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The longitudinal centres<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>LCF<\/strong>: the <strong>centroid<\/strong> of the <strong>waterplane<\/strong> area. The ship <strong>trims about it<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>LCB<\/strong>: the centre of the <strong>underwater volume<\/strong>, where buoyancy acts.<\/li><li><strong>LCG<\/strong>: the centre of <strong>gravity<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At rest, <strong>LCB<\/strong> and <strong>LCG<\/strong> are in <strong>one vertical line<\/strong>. If they&#x27;re not, the ship trims until they are.<\/li><li>Trim = Δ × (LCG − LCB) ÷ (100 × MCTC), in cm.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>KN and GZ<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>KN<\/strong>, from the <strong>cross curves<\/strong>, is the <strong>righting lever<\/strong> measured from the <strong>keel<\/strong> (assuming G at K).<\/li><li><strong>GZ = KN − KG sin θ<\/strong>, using the fluid KG with free surface.<\/li><li>Plot GZ against θ for the GZ curve.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>GM from the GZ curve<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Draw the <strong>tangent<\/strong> at the <strong>origin<\/strong> and a <strong>vertical<\/strong> at <strong>57.3°<\/strong> (one radian).<\/li><li>The <strong>height<\/strong> where they meet is the <strong>GM<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Going from salt to fresh water<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Same displacement<\/strong>, <strong>lower density<\/strong>, so <strong>more volume<\/strong> is needed to support the same weight.<\/li><li>She <strong>sinks bodily<\/strong>: by the <strong>FWA<\/strong> at summer displacement (or in proportion at other displacements).<\/li><li>The <strong>extra layer<\/strong> of displaced water is centred at the <strong>LCF<\/strong>, so <strong>B<\/strong> moves towards the <strong>LCF<\/strong> and is <strong>no longer under G<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So she also <strong>changes trim<\/strong>, about the <strong>LCF<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On most loaded ships the <strong>LCF is aft<\/strong> of the <strong>LCB<\/strong>, so <strong>B moves aft<\/strong>, and she <strong>trims slightly by the head<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The change is usually small, but it matters when loading to the marks in a river port.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Worked example<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Δ = 40,000 t, TPC = 50: FWA = 40,000 ÷ 200 = <strong>200 mm<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In dock water of 1.010: DWA = 200 × 15 ÷ 25 = <strong>120 mm<\/strong>, so she can submerge the summer mark by 120 mm.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The formulae for TPC, MCTC, TMD, FWA and DWA; the definitions of LCF, LCB and LCG and that she trims about the LCF; KN and GZ = KN − KG sin θ; GM from the tangent at 57.3°; and salt to fresh water: bodily sinkage by the FWA and a change of trim about the LCF, usually by the head.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Where do you find the FWA if you can't calculate it?","pa":"<p>On the Load Line Certificate, and it&#x27;s the distance between the S and F marks on the hull.<\/p>","asked":["DWA formula, what is it?","What is MCTC and how will it affect you?","Where can you find FWA for your vessel?","What is FWA & DWA how to calculate?","Dock Water Allowance?"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",19],["Robert Jackson",18],["Examiner not named",9],["Chris Williams",6],["Bill Bennett",5]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q281","sec":13,"q":"Draw your load line marks. What does 1/48 mean, how thick is the line, whose initials are on the disc, what are timber and subdivision marks, and when may you submerge the marks?","tier":"red","rep":59,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Load Line) Regulations 1998<\/strong> as amended, <strong>MSN 1752<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 579<\/strong>; internationally the <strong>Load Line Convention 1966<\/strong> and the <strong>1988 Protocol<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Amidships, each side<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Deck line<\/strong>: <strong>300 × 25 mm<\/strong>, marking the upper surface of the freeboard deck at the side.<\/li><li><strong>Ring (disc)<\/strong>: <strong>300 mm<\/strong> outside diameter, <strong>25 mm<\/strong> wide, placed <strong>below<\/strong> the deck line by the <strong>summer freeboard<\/strong>, with a <strong>horizontal line 450 × 25 mm<\/strong> whose <strong>upper edge<\/strong> passes through the <strong>centre<\/strong> of the ring.<\/li><li>The <strong>assigning authority&#x27;s letters<\/strong> either side of the ring, for example <strong>LR<\/strong> (Lloyd&#x27;s Register) or <strong>NV<\/strong> (DNV): the body that <strong>assigned<\/strong> the freeboard and <strong>surveys<\/strong> it. Each letter about 115 mm high and 75 mm wide.<\/li><li>A <strong>vertical line 540 mm forward<\/strong> of the ring&#x27;s centre, with the <strong>load lines<\/strong> branching from it, each <strong>230 × 25 mm<\/strong>, pointing forward.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The marks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>upper edge<\/strong> of <strong>every line<\/strong> is the <strong>mark<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>S<\/strong> (summer) is <strong>level<\/strong> with the ring&#x27;s <strong>centre<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>W<\/strong> (winter) is <strong>1/48<\/strong> of the <strong>summer draught<\/strong> <strong>below<\/strong> S.<\/li><li><strong>T<\/strong> (tropical) is <strong>1/48 above<\/strong> S.<\/li><li>&quot;1/48&quot; is the old &quot;<strong>quarter-inch per foot<\/strong> of draught&quot;: a fixed proportion of the draught for the seasonal changes.<\/li><li><strong>F<\/strong> (fresh) is the <strong>FWA above S<\/strong>, and <strong>TF<\/strong> (tropical fresh) is the <strong>FWA above T<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>WNA<\/strong> (winter North Atlantic), <strong>50 mm below W<\/strong>, only on ships of <strong>100 m or less<\/strong> in length.<\/li><li>The lines are painted in a contrasting colour (white or yellow on a dark hull, black on a light hull) and permanently marked by centre-punching or welding.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Timber load lines<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>LS, LW, LT, LF, LTF<\/strong> (and LWNA) are marked <strong>abaft<\/strong> the ring, for ships with a timber deck cargo assignment.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Subdivision load lines<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On <strong>passenger ships<\/strong>, <strong>C.1<\/strong>, <strong>C.2<\/strong> and so on, under <strong>SOLAS II-1/18<\/strong>, for the different subdivision conditions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When I may submerge the mark<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>In <strong>fresh<\/strong> or <strong>dock<\/strong> water, by the <strong>FWA<\/strong> or <strong>DWA<\/strong> (LL Art 12(2)).<\/li><li>Leaving a <strong>river<\/strong> port, by the <strong>fuel<\/strong> and <strong>stores<\/strong> used before reaching the <strong>sea<\/strong> (Art 12(3)).<\/li><li>When bound for a <strong>stricter zone<\/strong>, if she&#x27;s at the <strong>correct mark<\/strong> on <strong>entering<\/strong> it.<\/li><li>In an <strong>emergency<\/strong> to save <strong>life<\/strong> or the <strong>ship<\/strong>, returning to the marks as soon as possible.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Otherwise, submerging the appropriate mark is an <strong>offence<\/strong>, and the ship can be detained.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Me to draw it: the deck line, the ring and its line, the letters of the assigning authority, the vertical line 540 mm forward with lines 230 mm long, upper edges as the marks, S at the centre, W and T at 1/48 of the summer draught, F and TF by the FWA, WNA 50 mm below W for ships of 100 m or less, timber marks abaft the ring, C marks for passenger ships, and when the marks may be submerged.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's the offence if she's overloaded?","pa":"<p>Submerging the mark is an offence for the owner and Master. Under Article 21 the PSC officer will detain her until she&#x27;s at her marks.<\/p>","asked":["Draw loadline explain it?","Draw the loadline diagram. What is a loadline zone, and where can you find onboard loadline zones?","Why do we have different load line zones?","What is load line marking and its use?","Draw Load Line mark and explain, how to calculate FWA."],"ex":[["Examiner not named",12],["Muhammad Iqbal",9],["Alec Keep",6],["Ahsan Khan",4],["Bill Bennett",4]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q282","sec":13,"q":"Stiff vs tender vs neutral GZ curves; effect of increased beam; a ship with positive GM capsizes — when?; check loadicator GM at sea; max KG curve.","tier":"orange","rep":29,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Stiff, tender and neutral<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stiff<\/strong>: <strong>large GM<\/strong>, a <strong>steep<\/strong> GZ curve near the origin, <strong>quick, violent rolls<\/strong> that <strong>overload container lashings<\/strong> and stress the structure, and are uncomfortable for the crew.<\/li><li><strong>Tender<\/strong>: <strong>small GM<\/strong>, a <strong>gentle<\/strong> slope, <strong>slow rolls<\/strong>, and <strong>little reserve<\/strong> against <strong>wind<\/strong> or a <strong>cargo shift<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Neutral<\/strong>: <strong>GM zero<\/strong>, no righting lever at small angles, though the hull form may give some at larger angles. The ship will lie at any small angle she&#x27;s put to.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Effect of increased beam<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Increasing the beam increases <strong>BM<\/strong> (for a box, <strong>BM = B² ÷ 12d<\/strong>), so <strong>KM<\/strong> and <strong>GM<\/strong> increase: a <strong>steeper<\/strong> curve and a <strong>bigger maximum GZ<\/strong>.<\/li><li>But the <strong>deck edge immerses<\/strong> at a <strong>smaller angle<\/strong>, so the <strong>maximum<\/strong> comes <strong>earlier<\/strong> and the <strong>range<\/strong> may be <strong>smaller<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Freeboard<\/strong> is what <strong>extends the range<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A ship with positive GM capsizes: when?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When she&#x27;s <strong>heeled beyond<\/strong> her <strong>angle of vanishing stability<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That happens when a <strong>heeling moment<\/strong> exceeds her <strong>maximum righting moment<\/strong>, or a <strong>gust<\/strong> and <strong>roll<\/strong> put in <strong>more energy<\/strong> than the <strong>area under the curve<\/strong> up to the downflooding or vanishing angle.<\/li><li>Causes:<\/li><li><strong>cargo shift<\/strong>, especially grain or a liquefied bulk cargo;<\/li><li><strong>beam seas<\/strong> and resonant rolling;<\/li><li><strong>wind<\/strong> on a <strong>high deck stow<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>water on deck<\/strong> or in a flooded compartment (free surface);<\/li><li>downflooding through an open door or vent at a moderate angle;<\/li><li>a turn at high speed with a low GM.<\/li><li>Positive GM only guarantees stability at small angles. The whole curve matters.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Checking the loadicator GM at sea<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I <strong>time<\/strong> several <strong>rolls<\/strong> and use the IS Code formula <strong>T = 2CB ÷ √GM<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>2C<\/strong> is about <strong>0.7–0.8<\/strong>, so roughly <strong>GM ≈ (0.8B ÷ T)²<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If that&#x27;s <strong>well below<\/strong> the computed GM, I suspect <strong>heavy or misdeclared boxes high in the stacks<\/strong>, <strong>wrong soundings<\/strong> or <strong>free surface<\/strong>, and I <strong>recalculate<\/strong> (see ★14.1).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The maximum KG curve<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In the <strong>stability booklet<\/strong>, it gives, for each <strong>displacement<\/strong> or draught, the <strong>highest KG<\/strong> at which <strong>all<\/strong> the <strong>intact criteria<\/strong>, and the <strong>damage criteria<\/strong> where they apply, are met.<\/li><li>My <strong>KG corrected for free surface<\/strong> must be <strong>below<\/strong> it.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s the quickest check after loading.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stiff, tender and neutral with their GZ curves and rolling behaviour; increased beam increases BM and GM but the deck edge immerses earlier so the range may reduce, and freeboard extends the range; capsizing with positive GM beyond the angle of vanishing stability with the causes; the roll period check; and the max KG curve with the fluid KG below it.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is a stiff container ship a problem?","pa":"<p>Short, violent rolls raise the accelerations and lashing forces. Stacks collapse and boxes are lost — the CSM limits assume a GM range.<\/p>","asked":["Then he showed me GZ curve of LOL vessel , and ask me what is it ?","Draw GZ curve per intact stability criteria; locate vanishing stability. You spoke about cross curves what are these curves?","Draw GZ curve and explain grain heeling moment?","Stiff ship, tender ship","Next Showed another slide GZ Curve of Unstable Vessel ?"],"ex":[["Bill Bennett",3],["Delowar Hossain",2],["Examiner not named",2],["Kris Askey",2],["Zia Ul Haq",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q283","sec":13,"q":"When is an inclining experiment / lightship survey required?","tier":"yellow","rep":7,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-1/5<\/strong> requires <strong>every passenger ship<\/strong>, and <strong>every cargo ship of 24 m and over<\/strong>, to be <strong>inclined<\/strong> on <strong>completion<\/strong>. The test finds the <strong>lightship displacement<\/strong> and its <strong>centre of gravity<\/strong>: <strong>vertical<\/strong>, <strong>longitudinal<\/strong> and <strong>transverse<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Sister ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Administration may accept a <strong>sister ship&#x27;s<\/strong> inclining instead.<\/li><li>A <strong>lightweight survey<\/strong> is still done, to check the displacement and LCG.<\/li><li>If the lightship <strong>displacement<\/strong> differs by more than <strong>1–2%<\/strong> (depending on length), or the <strong>LCG<\/strong> by more than <strong>0.5% of L<\/strong>, she must be <strong>inclined<\/strong> herself.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>After alterations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If <strong>alterations<\/strong> materially affect the <strong>stability information<\/strong>, it must be <strong>revised<\/strong>, and she&#x27;s <strong>re-inclined<\/strong> if necessary.<\/li><li>On a container ship, a <strong>scrubber retrofit<\/strong>, <strong>raised lashing bridges<\/strong>, <strong>new cranes<\/strong>, a BWMS installation, or a bulbous bow change would mean a <strong>lightweight check<\/strong> and a <strong>revised stability booklet<\/strong>, <strong>approved<\/strong> by the Administration or the RO.<\/li><li>Weight added over the years (paint, repairs, stores not recorded) also changes the lightship, which shows as a growing constant.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Passenger ships<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>lightweight survey<\/strong> at least every <strong>5 years<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Re-inclined<\/strong> if the <strong>displacement<\/strong> has changed by more than <strong>2%<\/strong> or the <strong>LCG<\/strong> by more than <strong>1% of L<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The test, witnessed by the surveyor<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Conditions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Calm water<\/strong>, <strong>slack moorings<\/strong>, little wind, and the ship <strong>upright<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Tanks empty<\/strong> or <strong>pressed full<\/strong>, to avoid free surface, with any slack tanks known and accounted for.<\/li><li><strong>Everything on board listed<\/strong>: weights to be added or removed to reach the lightship condition. <strong>Loose gear<\/strong> secured.<\/li><li><strong>People ashore<\/strong>, except those needed for the test.<\/li><li>Draughts read accurately, and the water density taken.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The procedure<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Known weights<\/strong> are <strong>moved across<\/strong> the deck, in several stages, to one side and then the other.<\/li><li>The <strong>deflection<\/strong> is read on <strong>pendulums<\/strong> (long plumb lines with a batten) or <strong>U-tubes<\/strong>, at each stage.<\/li><li><strong>GM = w × d ÷ (Δ × tan θ)<\/strong>, where w is the weight moved, d the distance moved, and <strong>tan θ = deflection ÷ pendulum length<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The readings are plotted to check they&#x27;re consistent.<\/li><li><strong>KG = KM − GM<\/strong>, using KM from the hydrostatics at that draught.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s <strong>corrected<\/strong> to the <strong>lightship condition<\/strong>, removing or adding the weights listed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Results<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The lightship displacement, KG and LCG go into the approved stability booklet and the loading computer.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-1/5, passenger ships and cargo ships of 24 m and over inclined on completion; sister ships with a lightweight survey and the 1–2% and 0.5% L limits; re-inclining after alterations with examples; passenger ships&#x27; 5-yearly lightweight survey with the 2% and 1% L limits; and the test conditions, procedure and formula.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does lightship KG creep up over the years?","pa":"<p>Paint, spares, mud and sediment, and added equipment. That&#x27;s why the constant grows and the booklet KG can become optimistic.<\/p>","asked":["Briefly explain how does one carry out an inclining experiment ?","Have you ever heard of an inclining test?"],"ex":[["Bill Bennett",3],["Shahid Akhter",1],["Byron Griffiths",1],["Chris Bates",1],["Zia Ul Haq",1]],"yrs":"2023–2025"},{"id":"Q284","sec":13,"q":"Damage stability: criteria for your ship, damage stability booklet vs damage control plan contents, cross-flooding, can you survive one compartment flooded?","tier":"red","rep":24,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Damage stability — fail, Jul 2023","law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-1 Part B-1<\/strong>. For a cargo ship like mine, of <strong>80 m and over<\/strong>, it&#x27;s the <strong>probabilistic method<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The probabilistic method<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>attained subdivision index A<\/strong> must be at least the <strong>required index R<\/strong>, which depends on the ship&#x27;s length (and for passenger ships, the number of persons).<\/li><li>The <strong>partial indices<\/strong> at the <strong>three draughts<\/strong> (deepest subdivision, partial and light service draught) must each reach at least <strong>0.5R<\/strong> for cargo ships (verify).<\/li><li><strong>A<\/strong> is the <strong>sum<\/strong> over <strong>all damage cases<\/strong> of (the <strong>probability<\/strong> of that <strong>damage<\/strong>, the &quot;p&quot; factor, × the <strong>probability<\/strong> of <strong>surviving<\/strong> it, the &quot;<strong>s<\/strong>&quot; factor, from the final <strong>heel<\/strong>, the residual <strong>GZ<\/strong> and <strong>range<\/strong>).<\/li><li>So <strong>survival<\/strong> with <strong>any particular compartment<\/strong> flooded is <strong>not guaranteed<\/strong>. Some damage cases, including some single-compartment ones, may not be survivable.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d check a specific case with the <strong>damage stability information<\/strong>, the <strong>loading computer&#x27;s damage module<\/strong>, or the <strong>class emergency response service<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Deterministic rules apply to other types<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Bulk carriers<\/strong> of <strong>150 m<\/strong> and over, single side skin, carrying cargo of <strong>1,000 kg/m³<\/strong> or more must survive <strong>any one hold flooded<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS XII/4<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>Tankers<\/strong>, <strong>MARPOL I/28<\/strong>: after the assumed damage:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>final heel<\/strong> no more than <strong>25°<\/strong> (<strong>30°<\/strong> if the deck edge isn&#x27;t immersed);<\/li><li><strong>residual range<\/strong> at least <strong>20°<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>maximum residual GZ<\/strong> at least <strong>0.1 m<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>area<\/strong> at least <strong>0.0175 m·rad<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Chemical tankers<\/strong>: <strong>IBC Code Chapter 2<\/strong>, by ship type 1, 2 or 3.<\/li><li><strong>Gas carriers<\/strong>: IGC Code.<\/li><li><strong>Type B-60 and B-100<\/strong> ships under the Load Line Convention (reg 27).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Damage control information (SOLAS II-1/19)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Plans<\/strong> <strong>permanently exhibited<\/strong> on the <strong>bridge<\/strong>, plus <strong>booklets<\/strong> for the officers.<\/li><li>They show, for each <strong>deck<\/strong> and <strong>hold<\/strong>:<\/li><li>the <strong>watertight boundaries<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>openings<\/strong> with their <strong>closures<\/strong> and <strong>controls<\/strong>, and their positions;<\/li><li>the <strong>means of correcting list<\/strong>: <strong>cross-flooding fittings<\/strong> (which equalise asymmetric flooding by connecting tanks on each side), <strong>pumps<\/strong> and <strong>valves<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Instructions on what to do after damage.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The damage stability booklet or information<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Carried in the <strong>stability booklet<\/strong>: the <strong>limiting KG or GM curves<\/strong> allowing for damage, so if the ship meets the max KG curve she meets the damage criteria too.<\/li><li><strong>Cautions<\/strong>, such as <strong>doors<\/strong> to be kept <strong>closed<\/strong> at sea, and openings that must be closed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Can my ship survive one compartment flooded?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>&quot;Not guaranteed. My ship meets the probabilistic standard, so overall survivability meets the required index, but a specific case needs checking with the damage stability information or the class emergency response service. A bulk carrier under SOLAS XII/4 is different: one hold flooded is required.&quot;<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-1 Part B-1 probabilistic for cargo ships of 80 m and over, A ≥ R and the partial indices, so one compartment isn&#x27;t guaranteed; the deterministic rules for bulkers (XII/4), tankers (MARPOL I/28 with its criteria), chemical and gas carriers; the damage control plan under II-1/19 with cross-flooding; and the damage stability information in the booklet.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What's permeability?","pa":"<p>The fraction of a space that can fill with water. SOLAS uses 0.95 for accommodation and voids, 0.85 for machinery and 0.60 for stores.<\/p>","asked":["What do you know about the damage stability of your vessel?","What are your Stability considerations?","What do you check for in Stability report?","What is Damage stability criteria, and from where you will get information about it.","Damage stability info available to master"],"ex":[["Khairul Anam",3],["Examiner not named",3],["Zia Ul Haq",2],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",2],["Timothy Cummins",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q285","sec":13,"q":"List vs loll: a vessel \"flops\" to one side / is found listed 8° at anchor after dry dock / lists 10° while the dock floods. How do you tell which it is and what do you do?","tier":"red","rep":111,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>List vs loll<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>List<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>G is off the centreline<\/strong>: an off-centre weight, unequal tanks, or cargo shifted, with <strong>GM positive<\/strong>.<\/li><li>She lies <strong>steadily<\/strong> to <strong>one side<\/strong> and <strong>rolls about that angle<\/strong>.<\/li><li>tan θ = GG₁ (transverse shift of G) ÷ GM.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Loll<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Negative GM<\/strong> when upright: G is above M.<\/li><li>She lies over until <strong>G and the new M<\/strong> line up, at the <strong>angle of loll<\/strong>, where GZ becomes zero.<\/li><li>She <strong>rolls slowly<\/strong> and <strong>sluggishly<\/strong> about that angle, and may <strong>flop over<\/strong> to the <strong>other side<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>If she flops port and starboard, it&#x27;s loll.<\/strong><\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How to tell<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Sound every tank<\/strong> and <strong>compare port with starboard<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Look for <strong>off-centre weights<\/strong> and <strong>free surface<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Calculate GM<\/strong> from the current condition, with free surface.<\/li><li>Watch her behaviour: steady to one side (list), or slow and flopping (loll).<\/li><li>Push her gently with the rudder or a small weight shift: a listed ship returns to the same angle; a lolling ship may go to the other side.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Listed 8° at anchor after dry dock<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>First suspect flooding<\/strong>: a <strong>bottom plug not refitted<\/strong>, or a <strong>valve left open<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sound everything<\/strong> against the <strong>docking soundings<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then look for <strong>slack tanks<\/strong>, or <strong>G raised<\/strong> by tanks <strong>emptied<\/strong> in dock, or weights added high up during the dock period (new equipment, stores).<\/li><li>Check the engine room bilges and the pump room.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Listing 10° while the dock floods<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stop flooding at once<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If needed, the dock <strong>lowers the water<\/strong> so she <strong>sits back<\/strong> on the blocks.<\/li><li><strong>Find the cause<\/strong> before she refloats: plugs, valves, slack tanks, GM.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Correcting loll: lower G<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Press up slack tanks<\/strong>, and <strong>lower<\/strong> or <strong>remove high weights<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fill<\/strong> a <strong>divided double-bottom tank<\/strong>, the <strong>smallest first<\/strong>, on the <strong>low side first<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>The list <strong>may increase slightly<\/strong> at first, because of the added weight on the low side and the free surface while filling, but she <strong>can&#x27;t flop over violently<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then fill the <strong>matching tank<\/strong> on the <strong>high side<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Never treat it as a list.<\/strong> <strong>Never<\/strong> transfer ballast from the <strong>low side<\/strong> to the <strong>high side<\/strong>, and <strong>never<\/strong> fill the <strong>high side first<\/strong>. She will <strong>flop over<\/strong> to the <strong>other side<\/strong>, to a <strong>bigger angle<\/strong>, with <strong>momentum<\/strong>, and may capsize.<\/li><li><strong>Sound all tanks<\/strong> and <strong>recalculate<\/strong> as you go.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Correcting a list<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Find and remove the cause: transfer weight or ballast to the high side, or shift cargo.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>List from G off the centreline with positive GM, steady to one side; loll from negative GM, slow rolling and flopping side to side; how to tell; after dry dock suspect plugs or valves first; while flooding, stop and put her back on the blocks; and correcting loll by lowering G, filling the smallest low-side double bottom first, never transferring to the high side.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What is the angle of loll for a wall-sided ship?","pa":"<p>Tan θ = √(2 × GM / BM), using the size of the negative GM. At that angle the effective GM is twice the original negative value.<\/p>","asked":["What is the angle of loll?","Draw gz curve for list and angle of Loll?","What is difference between list and Loll?","What is angle of list?","What is list & loll?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",19],["Zia Ul Haq",16],["Shahid Akhter",14],["Bill Bennett",10],["James Steer",9]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q286","sec":14,"q":"The 3/E/C/E asks to pump bilges overboard — in NW European waters / the Red Sea / the Med. Your answer?","tier":"yellow","rep":6,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Prevention of Oil Pollution) Regulations 2019<\/strong>, and internationally <strong>MARPOL Annex I<\/strong>. <strong>NW European waters<\/strong>, the <strong>Red Sea<\/strong> and the <strong>Med<\/strong> are all <strong>Annex I special areas<\/strong>. For <strong>machinery-space bilges<\/strong>, the special-area rule, <strong>I/15.3<\/strong>, is the <strong>same 15 ppm<\/strong> standard as outside them.<\/p><p><strong>So yes, the engineer can pump bilges overboard, but only if all of these are met<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The ship is <strong>en route<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The bilge water goes through <strong>approved 15 ppm oil filtering equipment<\/strong>, with an <strong>alarm<\/strong> and <strong>automatic stopping device<\/strong>, with the <strong>oil content meter<\/strong> working and in <strong>calibration<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>oil content<\/strong> without <strong>dilution<\/strong> doesn&#x27;t exceed <strong>15 ppm<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On a <strong>tanker<\/strong>, it doesn&#x27;t come from the <strong>cargo pump room<\/strong> and isn&#x27;t <strong>mixed<\/strong> with <strong>cargo residues<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Where it&#x27;s not allowed at all<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In the <strong>Antarctic<\/strong> area, <strong>no discharge<\/strong> is allowed at all.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo-area<\/strong> discharges from <strong>tankers<\/strong> are <strong>prohibited<\/strong> in <strong>every special area<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before agreeing I&#x27;d check<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Company<\/strong> and <strong>charterer<\/strong> policy: <strong>many require zero discharge<\/strong> in special areas, or anywhere near land.<\/li><li>Any <strong>port<\/strong> or <strong>national<\/strong> rules: some coastal States have stricter rules in their waters.<\/li><li>The <strong>equipment<\/strong>: the OWS and oil content meter working correctly, calibrated, and sealed where the company seals it.<\/li><li>The <strong>position<\/strong>: en route, not in a port, anchorage, or near an environmentally sensitive area.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Authorisation and records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master authorises<\/strong> it.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>recorded<\/strong> in the <strong>ORB Part I<\/strong>: the <strong>start and stop positions<\/strong> and times, the <strong>quantity<\/strong>, and the <strong>equipment used<\/strong>, signed by the engineer and countersigned by the Master on each page.<\/li><li>The <strong>separator is never bypassed<\/strong>. <strong>&quot;Magic pipe&quot;<\/strong> cases end in <strong>heavy fines<\/strong> and <strong>officers jailed<\/strong>, especially in the US, and whistleblower rewards mean crew report it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If there&#x27;s any doubt<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The bilge water <strong>stays<\/strong> in the <strong>bilge holding tank<\/strong> and goes <strong>ashore<\/strong> to a <strong>reception facility<\/strong>, recorded in the ORB with the receipt.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What the examiner is testing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>That I know the special area rule for machinery-space bilges is the same 15 ppm, not &quot;nothing allowed&quot;.<\/li><li>That I know the Antarctic is different.<\/li><li>That I&#x27;d still check the company policy and the equipment.<\/li><li>And the integrity point about magic pipes.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The UK 2019 Regs, these are special areas but I/15.3 applies the same 15 ppm rule for machinery bilges, so yes if en route, through approved 15 ppm equipment with alarm and auto stop, undiluted, not cargo residues; nothing in the Antarctic; check company and local rules; the Master authorises; ORB Part I entries; never bypass; and if in doubt, keep it for shore reception.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"And outside special areas?","pa":"<p>For machinery spaces it&#x27;s the same: en route, 15 ppm, approved equipment with alarm and auto-stop. Only the Antarctic bans it outright.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q287","sec":14,"q":"In the Med the cook asks to throw garbage overboard. What do you tell him?","tier":"green","rep":4,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the Merchant Shipping regulations on garbage pollution (verify the current SI), and internationally <strong>MARPOL Annex V<\/strong>. The <strong>Mediterranean<\/strong> is an <strong>Annex V special area<\/strong> (Reg 6).<\/p><p><strong>What I&#x27;d tell the cook<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Nothing goes overboard except food waste<\/strong>, and only if:<\/p><ol><li>It&#x27;s <strong>comminuted<\/strong> or <strong>ground<\/strong> to pass a <strong>25 mm<\/strong> screen.<\/li><li>The ship is <strong>en route<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ship is at least <strong>12 nm<\/strong> from the <strong>nearest land<\/strong> (or ice shelf).<\/li><li>It isn&#x27;t <strong>mixed<\/strong> with any other garbage, such as plastic wrapping, which turns the whole lot into prohibited garbage.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Everything else stays on board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Plastics never go overboard<\/strong>, anywhere, including food packaging, plastic bags and ropes.<\/li><li><strong>Cooking oil<\/strong>, <strong>domestic waste<\/strong>, <strong>incinerator ash<\/strong> and <strong>operational waste<\/strong> are all <strong>kept<\/strong> for a <strong>port reception facility<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo residues<\/strong> and <strong>harmless cleaning agents<\/strong> in <strong>wash water<\/strong> are allowed only under <strong>strict conditions<\/strong> in a special area, and that&#x27;s the <strong>C/O&#x27;s decision<\/strong>, not the cook&#x27;s.<\/li><li><strong>Animal carcasses<\/strong>: not in special areas.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>As C/O I&#x27;d make sure the galley staff know the Garbage Management Plan<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>segregation bins<\/strong> and <strong>categories<\/strong> (food, plastics, domestic, cooking oil and so on), colour-coded and labelled.<\/li><li><strong>Who is responsible<\/strong>: the designated person and the galley&#x27;s own role.<\/li><li><strong>How to use<\/strong> the <strong>comminuter<\/strong>, and keeping it working.<\/li><li>The <strong>placards<\/strong> (required on ships of <strong>12 m<\/strong> and over), posted in the galley.<\/li><li>Storage of garbage until port: lidded, away from food, and not attracting pests.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Every discharge<\/strong> goes in the <strong>Garbage Record Book<\/strong>: <strong>date<\/strong>, <strong>time<\/strong>, <strong>position<\/strong>, <strong>category<\/strong> and <strong>estimated amount<\/strong> in <strong>cubic metres<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>officer in charge<\/strong> signs each entry, and the <strong>Master<\/strong> signs each <strong>page<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>GRB<\/strong> is kept for <strong>2 years<\/strong>, with the reception facility receipts.<\/li><li>Food waste discharged at sea is recorded too.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If he isn&#x27;t sure, he keeps it on board.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Garbage violations are a common PSC and coastal State finding, and fines in the Med can be large.<\/li><li>A crew member throwing garbage overboard puts the Master and the company at risk.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Med is an Annex V special area, only comminuted food waste at 12 nm en route and not mixed, plastics never, everything else to a reception facility, cargo residues and wash water are the C/O&#x27;s decision, the GMP training, placards, the GRB entries and signatures, 2-year retention, and &quot;if in doubt, keep it on board&quot;.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What if the food waste isn't comminuted?","pa":"<p>In a special area it can&#x27;t go overboard at all. Outside special areas it may go at 12 nm or more, en route.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q288","sec":14,"q":"Bunker barge spill alongside; how do you know SOPEP is up to date and where's the contact list?","tier":"orange","rep":32,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Immediate actions, per the SOPEP<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Signal<\/strong> the barge to <strong>stop pumping<\/strong> and <strong>close<\/strong> the <strong>manifold valves<\/strong>: the agreed stop signal and the radio.<\/li><li><strong>Raise the alarm<\/strong> and <strong>muster<\/strong> the <strong>SOPEP team<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On deck, the <strong>scuppers<\/strong> are already <strong>plugged<\/strong>. <strong>Contain<\/strong> the oil with <strong>absorbents<\/strong>, and <strong>pump<\/strong> it to a tank with the portable pump.<\/li><li>Find the <strong>source<\/strong>: the hose, the manifold flange, a tank overflowing from a vent.<\/li><li>No smoking, no hot work, and no ignition sources near the spill.<\/li><li>If <strong>oil<\/strong> is <strong>in the water<\/strong>, from the barge or from us, <strong>report it at once<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reporting<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>In a <strong>UK port<\/strong>, to the <strong>harbour master<\/strong> and <strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong>, <strong>without delay<\/strong>, under the Merchant Shipping (Oil Pollution Preparedness, Response and Co-operation Convention) Regulations 1998 (verify).<\/li><li><strong>Abroad<\/strong>, to the <strong>port authority<\/strong>, using the <strong>SOPEP contact list<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then the <strong>Master<\/strong>, the <strong>company<\/strong>, the <strong>P&amp;I correspondent<\/strong> and the <strong>charterers<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Under MARPOL Protocol I, the report goes without delay with the details: ship, position, time, type and quantity of oil, the cause, and the action taken.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records and evidence<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the <strong>ORB Part I<\/strong> as an <strong>accidental discharge<\/strong>, and in the <strong>deck log<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Keep samples<\/strong> of the oil (from the water, the deck, the barge and the ship&#x27;s tanks), and <strong>photos<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Issue a <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> if the barge caused it.<\/li><li>Statements from the people involved.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The SOPEP itself<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Required by <strong>MARPOL I/37<\/strong> for ships of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over, and its <strong>main text<\/strong> is <strong>approved<\/strong> by the <strong>Administration<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>contact lists<\/strong> are in the <strong>appendices<\/strong>: <strong>coastal State<\/strong> contacts, <strong>port<\/strong> contacts and <strong>ship-interest<\/strong> contacts (the company, P&amp;I, the qualified individual for the US).<\/li><li>The appendices <strong>aren&#x27;t part of the approval<\/strong>, so they can be updated without re-approval.<\/li><li>The company keeps them updated from the <strong>IMO list<\/strong> of <strong>national operational contact points<\/strong> on the IMO website.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I know it&#x27;s up to date<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Check the <strong>record of revisions<\/strong> and the <strong>date<\/strong> of the <strong>last appendix update<\/strong>, against the <strong>current IMO list<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That it&#x27;s been <strong>reviewed<\/strong> as the <strong>SMS requires<\/strong>, for example annually.<\/li><li>That the <strong>SOPEP drills<\/strong> have been done and recorded.<\/li><li>That the <strong>SOPEP equipment<\/strong> on board matches the plan and is complete.<\/li><li>That the copy on the bridge is the same as the one in the ship&#x27;s office.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stop the barge and close valves, alarm and SOPEP team, contain on deck with plugged scuppers and absorbents, report at once to the harbour master and HMCG in the UK or the port authority abroad, then the company, P&amp;I and charterers; the ORB entry, samples, photos and a letter of protest; and the SOPEP under MARPOL I/37 with the contact lists in the appendices updated from the IMO list, and how I check it&#x27;s current.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who pays for the clean-up if the barge caused it?","pa":"<p>The polluter, but we report and respond first. P&amp;I and the letter of protest protect the owner&#x27;s position.<\/p>","asked":["What is SOPEP?","Entry/Log of chain register.","Bunker barge coming for bunkering operations, Oil spill, actions?","During the bunkering Oil spill out of a bunker vent, action?","While loading your BWTS is stopped due to filter choking?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",12],["Bill Bennett",4],["Doug Smith",3],["Examiner not named",3],["Tarik Yassin",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q289","sec":14,"q":"As Master, how do you prove Annex VI compliance to a PSC officer in port?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the <strong>MS (Prevention of Air Pollution from Ships) Regulations 2008<\/strong> as amended, and internationally <strong>MARPOL Annex VI<\/strong>. I&#x27;d show the <strong>certificates first<\/strong>, then the <strong>evidence that we comply now<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Certificates and plans<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>IAPP<\/strong> certificate and supplement.<\/li><li>The <strong>EIAPP<\/strong> certificates, the <strong>NOx Technical Files<\/strong> and the <strong>record book of engine parameters<\/strong> for each engine over 130 kW.<\/li><li>The <strong>IEE<\/strong> certificate, the <strong>EEXI technical file<\/strong>, the <strong>SEEMP Parts I–III<\/strong>, the <strong>DCS Statement of Compliance<\/strong> and our <strong>CII rating<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>incinerator<\/strong> type-approval certificate.<\/li><li>The <strong>ODS record book<\/strong> and the <strong>list of equipment<\/strong> containing ozone-depleting substances.<\/li><li><strong>Scrubber<\/strong> approval and records, if fitted: the ETM, the onboard monitoring manual, the EGCS record book and washwater data.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What proves compliance in port today<\/strong> (the part examiner Hassan pressed for)<\/p><ol><li>The <strong>BDN<\/strong> for the <strong>fuel actually in use<\/strong>: no more than <strong>0.10%<\/strong> sulphur inside an <strong>ECA<\/strong> such as the <strong>North Sea and Channel<\/strong>, <strong>0.50%<\/strong> elsewhere. <strong>BDNs<\/strong> are kept for <strong>3 years<\/strong>, with the sealed <strong>MARPOL sample<\/strong> for at least <strong>12 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>fuel changeover record<\/strong>: <strong>date<\/strong>, <strong>time<\/strong>, <strong>position<\/strong> and <strong>low-sulphur fuel quantities<\/strong> on <strong>entering<\/strong> the ECA.<\/li><li>The <strong>in-use fuel sampling point<\/strong>, so the PSC officer can <strong>take a sample<\/strong> from the system <strong>now<\/strong>, and the on-board sampling points for the tanks.<\/li><li>The <strong>engine-room log<\/strong> showing <strong>which tank<\/strong> is feeding the engines, and the tank soundings.<\/li><li>A <strong>FONAR<\/strong>, if compliant fuel wasn&#x27;t available.<\/li><li>The <strong>at-berth<\/strong> requirement: in UK and EU ports, ships alongside for more than two hours must burn <strong>0.10%<\/strong> fuel at berth (verify the UK rule).<\/li><li>If a scrubber is used, the continuous emission monitoring data showing compliance.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Other checks the PSC officer may make<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>ODS<\/strong> equipment and any leaks.<\/li><li><strong>Incinerator<\/strong> use in port (often prohibited).<\/li><li><strong>Visible smoke<\/strong>, and the engine settings against the NOx Technical File.<\/li><li>The crew&#x27;s knowledge of the changeover procedure.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I&#x27;d present it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Have the documents ready in a file before arrival in an ECA port.<\/li><li>The C/E explains the engine and fuel side; I make sure the documents and the bridge records (position of changeover) agree.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The certificates (IAPP, EIAPP with NOx files, IEE with EEXI, SEEMP, DCS and CII, incinerator, ODS book, scrubber), and the evidence of compliance today: the BDN for the fuel in use, the changeover record, the sampling point, the engine log of the tank in use, a FONAR if needed, and the at-berth rule.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What if the PSC sample is over the limit?","pa":"<p>It&#x27;s a deficiency and possibly a detention. We change over to compliant fuel and debunker if required, notify the flag and supplier, and investigate the BDN against the sample.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q290","sec":14,"q":"Sewage discharge rules.","tier":"yellow","rep":7,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>In the UK, the Merchant Shipping regulations on sewage pollution (verify the current SI), and internationally <strong>MARPOL Annex IV<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Application<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ships of <strong>400 GT<\/strong> and over, or <strong>smaller<\/strong> ships certified to carry <strong>more than 15 persons<\/strong>, on <strong>international voyages<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>They need<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>An <strong>ISPPC<\/strong> (International Sewage Pollution Prevention Certificate), valid up to 5 years.<\/li><li>One of:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>an <strong>approved sewage treatment plant<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>comminuting and disinfecting<\/strong> system;<\/li><li>or a <strong>holding tank<\/strong> of adequate capacity.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>The <strong>standard shore discharge connection<\/strong>, with the standard flange.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Discharge rules (Reg 11)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Approved treatment plant<\/strong> in operation, certified on the ISPPC: <strong>anywhere<\/strong>, provided the effluent shows <strong>no visible floating solids<\/strong> and <strong>doesn&#x27;t discolour<\/strong> the water.<\/li><li><strong>Comminuted and disinfected<\/strong> by an approved system: more than <strong>3 nm<\/strong> from the <strong>nearest land<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Untreated<\/strong> sewage from a <strong>holding tank<\/strong>: more than <strong>12 nm<\/strong>, <strong>en route<\/strong> at <strong>4 knots<\/strong> or more, at a <strong>moderate rate<\/strong> approved by the Administration, <strong>never all at once<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Sewage mixed with other wastes must meet the rules for each.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Special area: the Baltic<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Passenger ships<\/strong> may <strong>only<\/strong> discharge if their plant meets the <strong>stricter nitrogen and phosphorus<\/strong> standard. Otherwise the sewage goes <strong>ashore<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Other ships<\/strong> follow the normal rules.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In port<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Holding tank<\/strong> or <strong>treatment plant<\/strong> only, plus <strong>local rules<\/strong>: <strong>some ports ban<\/strong> any discharge.<\/li><li>Use port reception facilities where available, with a receipt.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Maintenance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>plant<\/strong> is maintained under the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, including <strong>chlorine dosing<\/strong> and <strong>sludge removal<\/strong>, and the effluent tested where required.<\/li><li>Holding tank vents and level alarms working.<\/li><li>H₂S risk in sewage tanks and spaces: enclosed space precautions for entry.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Grey water<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Grey water<\/strong> (from showers, sinks, laundry) <strong>isn&#x27;t sewage<\/strong> under MARPOL, but <strong>some ports<\/strong> and coastal States <strong>regulate<\/strong> it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>MARPOL Annex IV doesn&#x27;t require a sewage record book, but many companies and some flags require a log of discharges, and the plant&#x27;s operating records.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Annex IV applies to ships of 400 GT and over or carrying more than 15 persons, the ISPPC, treatment plant or comminuter or holding tank, the shore connection; the discharge rules (approved plant anywhere with no floating solids, comminuted and disinfected beyond 3 nm, untreated beyond 12 nm en route at 4 knots at a moderate rate); the Baltic special area for passenger ships; port rules; and maintenance.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What counts as sewage?","pa":"<p>Drainage from toilets, urinals, medical spaces and spaces with live animals, and any water mixed with them.<\/p>","asked":["International Air Pollution Prevention Certificate","What is the regs for sewage discharge?"],"ex":[["Ahsan Khan",2],["Joe Mattock",1],["Shahid Akhter",1],["Nayyer Naqvi",1],["Examiner not named",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q291","sec":14,"q":"Hong Kong Convention.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"Hong Kong Convention in force 2025","a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Hong Kong International Convention for the Safe and Environmentally Sound Recycling of Ships, 2009<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It came into force on <strong>26 June 2025<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It covers ships of <strong>500 GT<\/strong> and over on international voyages, and the <strong>yards<\/strong> that <strong>recycle<\/strong> them.<\/li><li>Its purpose: to stop ship recycling harming workers and the environment, as happened on beaching yards in South Asia.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>For the ship<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Inventory of Hazardous Materials (IHM)<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Part I<\/strong> lists the <strong>hazardous materials<\/strong> in the <strong>structure<\/strong> and <strong>equipment<\/strong>, with their <strong>location<\/strong> and <strong>quantity<\/strong>: asbestos, PCBs, ozone-depleting substances, heavy metals (lead, mercury, cadmium, chromium), and so on.<\/li><li><strong>New ships<\/strong> have one from <strong>delivery<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Existing ships<\/strong> need one within <strong>5 years<\/strong> of entry into force, or <strong>before<\/strong> going for <strong>recycling<\/strong> if that&#x27;s sooner.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>International Certificate on Inventory of Hazardous Materials<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Issued after an <strong>initial survey<\/strong>, <strong>renewed<\/strong> at least every <strong>5 years<\/strong>, with <strong>additional surveys<\/strong> after <strong>major changes<\/strong> to the structure or equipment.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Prohibited materials<\/strong> can&#x27;t be installed:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>asbestos<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>ozone-depleting substances<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>PCBs<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>TBT<\/strong>-type anti-fouling (also under the AFS Convention);<\/li><li>and others listed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>At end of life<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>IHM Parts II and III<\/strong> (operational <strong>waste<\/strong> and <strong>stores<\/strong>) are added before recycling.<\/li><li>The <strong>authorised yard<\/strong> writes a <strong>Ship Recycling Plan<\/strong> for that ship.<\/li><li>A <strong>final survey<\/strong> gives the <strong>International Ready for Recycling Certificate<\/strong>, valid for up to <strong>3 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ship goes only to an authorised facility.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>As C/O<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>I keep <strong>IHM Part I<\/strong> updated, with the company&#x27;s IHM manager.<\/li><li><strong>Every new spare<\/strong> or item of <strong>equipment<\/strong> comes with the <strong>supplier&#x27;s material declaration<\/strong> (MD) and supplier&#x27;s declaration of conformity (SDoC).<\/li><li>Anything containing a <strong>listed material<\/strong> is <strong>recorded<\/strong> in the IHM.<\/li><li><strong>Asbestos-containing spares<\/strong> are <strong>rejected<\/strong>: <strong>SOLAS II-1/3-5<\/strong> already <strong>bans<\/strong> new asbestos on all ships, and PSC has found asbestos in gaskets and insulation supplied as new spares.<\/li><li>Show the IHM certificate to PSC.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>UK position<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>UK ships are also covered by <strong>UK ship recycling law<\/strong> retained from the <strong>EU Ship Recycling Regulation<\/strong>, which already requires an IHM and an approved yard from the EU list.<\/li><li>Check the UK&#x27;s HKC position before quoting it (verify).<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 2009 Convention in force 26 June 2025, ships of 500 GT and over, the IHM Part I and its certificate renewed at 5 years, prohibited materials, end-of-life Parts II and III, the Ship Recycling Plan and the Ready for Recycling Certificate for 3 months, and the C/O&#x27;s job of keeping the IHM updated from material declarations and rejecting asbestos spares under SOLAS II-1/3-5.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who prepares the IHM on an existing ship?","pa":"<p>The company, using a specialist hazardous-materials expert who samples and surveys the ship. The flag or RO verifies it and issues the certificate.<\/p>","asked":["Requirements for compliance with Hong Kong convention"],"ex":[["Khademul Insan",1]],"yrs":"2024–2024"},{"id":"Q292","sec":15,"q":"After discharging grain you find stevedore damage. Report to whom and how?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Act straight away<\/strong>, before the stevedores leave the berth. The <strong>charter party<\/strong> usually requires stevedore damage to be <strong>notified in writing<\/strong> within a <strong>short time<\/strong>, often within <strong>24 or 48 hours<\/strong>, or <strong>before sailing<\/strong>. Late notice can lose the claim.<\/p><p><strong>1. Inspect and record<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Location<\/strong>: by <strong>hold<\/strong> and <strong>frame number<\/strong>, port or starboard.<\/li><li><strong>What&#x27;s damaged<\/strong>: <strong>frames<\/strong>, <strong>ladders<\/strong>, <strong>pipe guards<\/strong>, <strong>tank top<\/strong>, <strong>hopper plating<\/strong>, <strong>coamings<\/strong>, hatch covers.<\/li><li><strong>Dimensions<\/strong>: length and depth of the dent or crack.<\/li><li><strong>Photos<\/strong> with a <strong>scale<\/strong> and <strong>date<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Witnesses<\/strong>: names of the duty officer, the bosun and the foreman.<\/li><li><strong>Sound the tanks<\/strong> below or next to it, in case the <strong>tank top<\/strong> or a <strong>hopper tank<\/strong> is <strong>holed<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Stevedore Damage Report<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Give it to the <strong>stevedore foreman<\/strong> or <strong>superintendent<\/strong>, and ask them to <strong>sign<\/strong> and <strong>acknowledge liability<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If they <strong>refuse<\/strong>, note &quot;<strong>signature refused<\/strong>&quot;, with a <strong>witness<\/strong>, and <strong>hand it over anyway<\/strong>, keeping a copy.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Letter of protest<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To the <strong>stevedores<\/strong> and the <strong>terminal<\/strong>, holding them responsible.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Copies<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>To the <strong>charterers<\/strong>, who usually <strong>appoint<\/strong> the stevedores and are <strong>responsible<\/strong> for their damage under the C/P.<\/li><li>To the <strong>owners<\/strong> through the <strong>agent<\/strong>.<\/li><li>To <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> if it&#x27;s serious, and H&amp;M if repairs are needed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Class<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the damage is <strong>structural<\/strong>, or affects <strong>watertight integrity<\/strong> or <strong>access ladders<\/strong>, inform <strong>class<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>surveyor inspects<\/strong> and may impose a <strong>condition of class<\/strong> or require <strong>repair before we load<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If it affects the <strong>certificates<\/strong>, the <strong>flag<\/strong> is told too (<strong>SOLAS I/11<\/strong>).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. On board<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Record it in the <strong>deck log<\/strong> and the <strong>defect list<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Make <strong>temporary repairs<\/strong>, or <strong>barrier off<\/strong> damaged ladders for safety.<\/li><li>Plan permanent repairs with the company, and the cost recovery.<\/li><li>Check the next cargo isn&#x27;t affected: a damaged tank top under heavy cargo, or a holed hopper tank with ballast.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>duty officer watches<\/strong> the <strong>grabs<\/strong> and <strong>bulldozers<\/strong> during discharge, and <strong>stops careless operators<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Report damage as it happens, not at the end.<\/li><li>Brief the stevedores at the start on the ship&#x27;s vulnerable areas.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Act before the stevedores leave and within the C/P time limit; inspect and record with location, dimensions, photos and witnesses, and sound the tanks; the Stevedore Damage Report signed or &quot;signature refused&quot;; a letter of protest; copies to charterers, owners and P&amp;I; class if structural or affecting watertight integrity, and the flag under SOLAS I/11; log entries and temporary repairs; and prevention by watching the grabs.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The damage is found after sailing — hidden damage?","pa":"<p>Report it to the charterers and owners at once, with photos and a damage report, and lodge the claim. A late notice weakens it but doesn&#x27;t necessarily lose it.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q293","sec":16,"q":"In a TSS, a fishing vessel (or sailing vessel, or <20 m vessel) is crossing and risk of collision exists. Who is the give-way vessel? What does \"not impede\" mean?","tier":"red","rep":123,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Stood on too long in a TSS — fail, Rahman 2024","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The TSS rules<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Rule 10(i)<\/strong>: a vessel <strong>engaged in fishing<\/strong> shall <strong>not impede<\/strong> the passage of any vessel <strong>following a traffic lane<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 10(j)<\/strong>: a vessel <strong>under 20 m<\/strong> or a <strong>sailing vessel<\/strong> shall <strong>not impede<\/strong> the safe passage of a <strong>power-driven vessel<\/strong> following a traffic lane.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What &quot;not impede&quot; means: Rule 8(f)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>8(f)(i)<\/strong>: a vessel which is required not to impede the passage of another vessel shall, when required by the circumstances, take <strong>early action<\/strong> to allow <strong>sufficient sea room<\/strong> for the safe passage of the other vessel.<\/li><li><strong>8(f)(ii)<\/strong>: a vessel required not to impede is <strong>not relieved<\/strong> of that obligation if approaching the other so as to involve <strong>risk of collision<\/strong>, and shall, when taking action, have full regard to the action which may be required by the rules of Part B.<\/li><li><strong>8(f)(iii)<\/strong>: a vessel whose passage is <strong>not to be impeded<\/strong> (me) <strong>remains fully obliged<\/strong> to comply with the rules of <strong>Part B<\/strong> when the two vessels are approaching so as to involve risk of collision.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>So once there&#x27;s risk of collision, the normal rules apply<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Fishing vessel crossing<\/strong>: under <strong>Rule 18(a)(iii)<\/strong>, I&#x27;m a power-driven vessel and must <strong>keep out of the way<\/strong> of a vessel <strong>engaged in fishing<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sailing vessel<\/strong>: the same, under <strong>18(a)(iv)<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Power-driven vessel under 20 m<\/strong> crossing from my <strong>port<\/strong> side: <strong>Rule 15<\/strong> makes her the <strong>give-way<\/strong> vessel, so I&#x27;m <strong>stand-on<\/strong> under <strong>Rule 17<\/strong>, but I <strong>watch her closely<\/strong>, because she has already failed to &quot;not impede&quot;. If she crosses from my <strong>starboard<\/strong> side, I&#x27;m the give-way vessel under Rule 15.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>My action for the fishing or sailing vessel<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Hand steering<\/strong>, <strong>call the Master<\/strong>, engine ready.<\/li><li>Take <strong>early, substantial<\/strong> action, as Rule 8 and Rule 16 require.<\/li><li><strong>Usually a large reduction of speed<\/strong>, to let her <strong>cross ahead<\/strong>, because in a <strong>lane<\/strong> my room to alter is <strong>limited<\/strong> by the separation zone and other traffic, and I must allow for her <strong>gear<\/strong> if she&#x27;s fishing (Rule 26 signals show the direction of gear over 150 m).<\/li><li>An alteration to <strong>starboard<\/strong> may be possible if there&#x27;s room and it doesn&#x27;t create a new risk with other traffic in the lane.<\/li><li>Sound the appropriate <strong>manoeuvring signal<\/strong> (Rule 34(a)) for any alteration, or three short blasts for astern propulsion.<\/li><li><strong>Check the bearing<\/strong> until she&#x27;s <strong>past and clear<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>I don&#x27;t stand on<\/strong> because she &quot;should not impede&quot;.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Many candidates say &quot;she must not impede, so I&#x27;m stand-on&quot;. That&#x27;s wrong once risk of collision exists: 8(f)(iii) puts me back under Part B.<\/li><li>The &quot;not impede&quot; duty is about her taking early action before risk of collision develops.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Rules 10(i) and (j), Rule 8(f)(i), (ii) and (iii) word for word in meaning, that once risk exists I&#x27;m fully under Part B, so I give way to a fishing vessel (18(a)(iii)) and a sailing vessel (18(a)(iv)), I&#x27;m stand-on for a power-driven vessel under 20 m from port (Rule 15), and my action: early and substantial, usually a large speed reduction, with signals and checking the bearing.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Who is the give-way vessel?","pa":"<p>Before risk of collision exists, she must not impede. Once risk of collision exists, I keep out of the way of a fishing or sailing vessel (Rule 18), and she still must not impede (8(f)(ii)). Examiner Rahman 2022: &quot;if risk of collision exists you&#x27;re already past the point of not impeding.&quot;<\/p>","asked":["TSS – PDV on Stbd Side – actions?","FV in crossing TSS from port to stbd?","Fishing vessel fishing inside the lane. Not engaged in fishing","RAM vessel crossing from port side in TSS, what are your actions?","Fishing Vessel in TSS"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",20],["Michele Moro",13],["Chris Roe",8],["Zia Ul Haq",8],["Colum Leggett",7]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q294","sec":16,"q":"CBD on your port side crossing (or you are CBD with a RAM/NUC on your port). Word-for-word Rule 8(f)(i) and Rule 18(d).","tier":"red","rep":149,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>\"<strong>Rule 18(d)(i):<\/strong> 'Any vessel other than a vessel not under command or a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre shall, if the circumstances of the case admit, avoid impeding the safe passage of a vessel constrained by her draught, exhibiting the signals in Rule 28.'\n<strong>Rule 18(d)(ii):<\/strong> 'A vessel constrained by her draught shall navigate with particular caution having full regard to her special condition.'<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rule 8(f)(i):<\/strong> 'A vessel which, by any of these Rules, is required not to impede the passage or safe passage of another vessel shall, when required by the circumstances of the case, take early action to allow sufficient sea-room for the safe passage of the other vessel.'<\/p>\n<p>A CBD vessel is <strong>not<\/strong> in the Rule 18(a)–(c) hierarchy, so she isn't 'privileged' in the usual sense. My duty is to <strong>avoid impeding<\/strong> her, and to act <strong>early<\/strong> — before risk of collision develops. So with a CBD vessel crossing from my port side, I don't wait as stand-on vessel. I'd take early action: <strong>reduce speed substantially<\/strong> so she passes well ahead of me in her deep water, hand steering, call the Master. I would <strong>not alter to port<\/strong> across her bow. If risk of collision still develops, 8(f)(ii) says I'm still not relieved of my duty not to impede, and I must also have regard to the steering rules.<\/p>\n<p>If <strong>I<\/strong> am the CBD vessel and a <strong>NUC or RAM<\/strong> vessel is on my port side, 18(d)(i) <strong>exempts them<\/strong> from the duty not to impede me, because they are even less able to manoeuvre. So under <strong>18(d)(ii)<\/strong> I navigate with particular caution, slow down early, sound the signals and keep clear as far as my channel allows.\"<\/p>","pq":"What signals does a CBD vessel show?","pa":"<p>Rule 28: three all-round red lights in a vertical line, or a cylinder by day. Fog signal one prolonged and two short (Rule 35(c)).<\/p>","asked":["CBD crossing on port side, actions?","CBD port side actions?","Own vsl PDV, CBD from port side","You are CBD and RAM crossing port side ?","CBD VESSEL SEEN FROM PORT SIDE?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",24],["Nayyer Naqvi",20],["Muhammad Iqbal",19],["Examiner not named",15],["Zia Ul Haq",15]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q295","sec":16,"q":"A PDV on your port side (ROC) is not giving way. What do you do and say, step by step? When MAY you act and when MUST you? Can you alter to port?","tier":"red","rep":11,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The situation<\/strong><\/p><p>She&#x27;s a power-driven vessel crossing from my <strong>port<\/strong> side, so under <strong>Rule 15<\/strong> she <strong>must keep out of the way<\/strong>, and I&#x27;m the <strong>stand-on<\/strong> vessel. Under <strong>Rule 17(a)(i)<\/strong> I <strong>keep my course and speed<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Step by step<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Confirm risk of collision<\/strong>: <strong>compass bearings<\/strong> and <strong>radar<\/strong> plotting. A <strong>steady bearing<\/strong> (or not appreciably changing) means <strong>risk exists<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 7(d)<\/strong>), and remember that risk may exist even with an appreciable bearing change, especially with a large ship or at close range.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>, put the <strong>steering in hand<\/strong>, and get the <strong>engine ready<\/strong> for immediate manoeuvre.<\/li><li>Try VHF only to confirm her identity and intentions, not to agree a manoeuvre that breaks the rules (MGN 324 warns about VHF-assisted collisions).<\/li><li><strong>As soon as it&#x27;s apparent<\/strong> she&#x27;s <strong>not taking appropriate action<\/strong>, I sound <strong>at least five short and rapid blasts<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 34(d)<\/strong>), and may back it up with the <strong>light signal<\/strong> of at least five short and rapid flashes.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 17(a)(ii)<\/strong>: from that point I <strong>MAY<\/strong> take action <strong>by my manoeuvre alone<\/strong> to avoid collision.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 17(b)<\/strong>: when, from any cause, she&#x27;s <strong>so close<\/strong> that collision <strong>can&#x27;t be avoided<\/strong> by the action of the give-way vessel <strong>alone<\/strong>, I <strong>MUST<\/strong> take such action as will <strong>best aid to avoid collision<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 17(c)<\/strong>: a power-driven vessel which takes action under (a)(ii) to avoid collision with another power-driven vessel shall, <strong>if the circumstances of the case admit<\/strong>, <strong>not alter course to port<\/strong> for a vessel on her <strong>own port<\/strong> side.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>So my action<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>bold alteration to starboard<\/strong>, a <strong>round turn to starboard<\/strong> if necessary, which takes me away from her and behind her, and/or <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> or <strong>stop<\/strong>.<\/li><li>With the <strong>one short blast<\/strong> signal for the alteration to starboard (Rule 34(a)), or three short blasts for astern propulsion.<\/li><li><strong>Keep watching<\/strong> until she&#x27;s <strong>past and clear<\/strong>: she might alter to starboard herself at the last moment.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Rule 17(d)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>She is <strong>still not relieved<\/strong> of her obligation to <strong>keep out of the way<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Can I alter to port?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Under Rule 17(c), <strong>not<\/strong> for a vessel on my port side, if the circumstances admit. An alteration to port towards her is exactly what causes collisions when she finally turns to starboard.<\/li><li>Only if the circumstances don&#x27;t admit anything else, for example a shoal to starboard, and even then with great care.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The mistake to avoid<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Sounding five short<\/strong> and then <strong>continuing to stand on<\/strong>. Once doubt exists and she isn&#x27;t acting, I act.<\/li><li>And acting too early, before it&#x27;s apparent she isn&#x27;t giving way, which can confuse her.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Rule 15 makes me stand-on and Rule 17(a)(i) to keep course and speed; confirm risk by bearings and radar; call the Master and hand steering; five short blasts under Rule 34(d); MAY act under 17(a)(ii) when it&#x27;s apparent; MUST act under 17(b) when collision can&#x27;t be avoided by her alone; 17(c) don&#x27;t alter to port for a vessel on my port side; bold starboard alteration or slow down with the signal; and 17(d) she&#x27;s still obliged.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Can you call her on VHF?","pa":"<p>Only to make contact; don&#x27;t rely on VHF to agree manoeuvres — it causes confusion over identity and wastes time. The Rules decide the action.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q296","sec":16,"q":"Rule 19: key points; target forward of the beam vs abaft the beam; fog signal heard forward of the beam (no radar target); what to avoid; rules that apply in RV.","tier":"red","rep":189,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>\"<strong>Rule 19<\/strong> applies to vessels <strong>not in sight of one another<\/strong> when navigating in or near an area of restricted visibility.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>(b)<\/strong> Every vessel proceeds at a <strong>safe speed<\/strong> for the conditions; a power-driven vessel has her <strong>engines ready<\/strong> for immediate manoeuvre.<\/li>\n<li><strong>(c)<\/strong> Due regard to the conditions when complying with the Section I rules.<\/li>\n<li><strong>(d)<\/strong> A vessel which detects another <strong>by radar alone<\/strong> shall determine whether a close-quarters situation is developing or risk of collision exists. If so, she takes avoiding action <strong>in ample time<\/strong>. If that action is an alteration of course, so far as possible she avoids:<ul>\n<li><strong>(i)<\/strong> an alteration to <strong>port<\/strong> for a vessel <strong>forward of the beam<\/strong>, other than for a vessel being overtaken;<\/li>\n<li><strong>(ii)<\/strong> an alteration <strong>towards<\/strong> a vessel <strong>abeam or abaft the beam<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>(e)<\/strong> Except where it has been determined that risk of collision does not exist, every vessel which hears <strong>apparently forward of her beam<\/strong> the fog signal of another vessel, or which cannot avoid a close-quarters situation with a vessel forward of her beam, shall <strong>reduce her speed to the minimum at which she can be kept on her course<\/strong>. She shall, if necessary, <strong>take all her way off<\/strong>, and in any event <strong>navigate with extreme caution<\/strong> until danger of collision is over.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Rules that apply in restricted visibility:<\/strong> Section I (Rules 4–10) and Section III (Rule 19), plus the lights and shapes (Part C) and the sound signals in restricted visibility (<strong>Rule 35<\/strong>). The Section II rules (13–18) <strong>don't<\/strong> apply, because the vessels aren't in sight of one another. There is no stand-on vessel.<\/p>\n<p>My preparation: call the Master, safe speed, engines on standby, extra lookout, fog signals, hand steering, radar on suitable ranges with <strong>systematic plotting<\/strong>, and close watertight doors.\"<\/p>","pq":"Fog signal forward of the beam but no radar target?","pa":"<p>19(e): reduce to minimum steerage speed, take all way off if necessary, extreme caution — a small vessel may not show on radar (Rule 6(b)(iv)).<\/p>","asked":["What you will do in Restricted Visibility?","How can you describe Rule 19?","Which rules apply in restricted visibility?","Explain the situation?","What will you do when you hear fog signal forward of the beam?"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",32],["Zia Ul Haq",20],["Chris Roe",19],["Khademul Insan",18],["Colum Leggett",17]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q297","sec":16,"q":"Radar plot: construct OAW, CPA, TCPA, course, speed, aspect; three dots on the same spot; relative vs true trails/vectors; how much alteration (trial manoeuvre).","tier":"red","rep":172,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Rushed action before plot finished — fail 2021","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Construction: the OAW triangle<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>O<\/strong> = the target&#x27;s <strong>first<\/strong> plotted position.<\/li><li><strong>A<\/strong> = its position after the <strong>plot interval<\/strong> (for example 6 or 12 minutes).<\/li><li><strong>OA<\/strong> is the <strong>relative motion<\/strong>. Extending it past the centre (own ship) gives the <strong>CPA<\/strong> (the nearest distance from the centre to the line) and the <strong>TCPA<\/strong> (from the time at A, the distance to CPA divided by the relative speed).<\/li><li><strong>W<\/strong> is found by laying off <strong>own ship&#x27;s course reversed<\/strong> from <strong>O<\/strong>, for the <strong>distance<\/strong> own ship runs in the plot interval.<\/li><li><strong>WA<\/strong> is the target&#x27;s <strong>true course and speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>aspect<\/strong> is the <strong>relative bearing<\/strong> of own ship <strong>from the target<\/strong>, measured from the <strong>target&#x27;s head<\/strong>, red or green.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Examiner Leggett&#x27;s plot<\/strong> (own ship 035° × 10 kn; target bearing 340° at 8, 7 and 6 nm at 00, 06 and 12 minutes)<\/p><ul><li>The <strong>bearing is steady<\/strong> and the range closes <strong>1 nm every 6 minutes<\/strong>, so <strong>CPA = 0<\/strong> and the <strong>relative speed<\/strong> is <strong>10 kn<\/strong>.<\/li><li>From the 12-minute plot at 6 nm, <strong>TCPA = 36 minutes<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Target <strong>course ≈ 097°<\/strong>, <strong>speed ≈ 9 kn<\/strong>, <strong>aspect ≈ Green 63°<\/strong> (I computed 097.5°, 9.2 kn, G62.5°).<\/li><li>If own ship <strong>stopped<\/strong>, the CPA would open to about <strong>5.3 nm<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My action<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In <strong>restricted visibility<\/strong>, the target is <strong>forward of my beam<\/strong> on the <strong>port<\/strong> side. <strong>Rule 19(d)(i)<\/strong> says avoid altering to <strong>port<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So I&#x27;d make a <strong>bold alteration to starboard<\/strong>, large enough to be <strong>readily apparent<\/strong> on her radar (Rule 8(b)), typically 60° or more for a target on the port bow, and/or <strong>reduce speed<\/strong>, which is very effective here because she&#x27;s crossing ahead.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d check the result with the <strong>trial manoeuvre<\/strong> on the <strong>ARPA<\/strong> first, then confirm by <strong>continued plotting<\/strong> that the CPA is <strong>opening<\/strong> and she passes clear (Rule 8(d)).<\/li><li>In clear weather with her in sight, she&#x27;d be crossing from my port side, so I&#x27;d be the stand-on vessel under Rule 15 and 17, watching her and ready to act.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How much alteration<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Use the trial manoeuvre: enter the new course or speed and the time delay, and see the predicted CPA. Choose one that gives the safe CPA from the standing orders (for example 2 nm).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Three plots on the same spot<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The target has the <strong>same course and speed<\/strong> as own ship: <strong>no relative motion<\/strong> and <strong>no risk<\/strong> of collision at present (examiner Insan). Keep watching: any change by either ship creates relative motion.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Relative vs true trails and vectors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Relative<\/strong> trails and vectors show the target&#x27;s movement relative to me: the CPA is seen directly. Use them to assess risk.<\/li><li><strong>True<\/strong> (sea-stabilised) show her course and speed through the water: her aspect. Use them to identify the situation under the rules.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The OAW triangle with O, A and W defined and how to get CPA, TCPA, course, speed and aspect; a worked plot; the action under Rule 19(d) with a bold starboard alteration and/or speed reduction checked by trial manoeuvre and continued plotting; three plots on one spot means same course and speed; and relative versus true vectors.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Relative or true trails — which show risk of collision?","pa":"<p>Relative trails/vectors show the CPA directly; true vectors show what the target is actually doing. Know which mode the screen is in before answering (Hossain).<\/p>","asked":["What is continuos radar plotting ?","Radar Plot – Restricted Visibility (Situation and Action?","RADAR PLOT : Vessel on starboard quarter (NO ROC), vessel on Port quarter ( ROC) . ACTIONS ?","RADAR PLOT: RV situation- one vessel on Stbd Bow and other on Stbd quarter, Action?","Radar Plot, showed me a radar screen with 3 targets,plotting sheet?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",20],["James Gilbert",17],["Examiner not named",16],["Nayyer Naqvi",12],["Zia Ul Haq",12]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q298","sec":16,"q":"Lights ID set (with day shapes and fog signals): single white light — all possibilities; mine clearance; pair trawling; RAM towing with tow >200 m and partly submerged tow; fishing gear >150 m; pilot vessel at anchor; vessel aground ≥100 m; NUC making/not making way; vertical separation and arcs.","tier":"red","rep":146,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Towing lights misread — fail, Aug 2026","law":null,"a":"<p>\"I'd always give: what it is, its length category, what it's doing, the day shape, the fog signal, and my action.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What you see<\/th>\n<th>It is<\/th>\n<th>Day shape<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Single white light<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>An anchored vessel under 50 m; a power-driven vessel under 12 m (all-round white with or without sidelights); a power-driven vessel under 7 m with max speed ≤7 kn; a sailing vessel under 7 m or a vessel under oars showing a white torch; or the <strong>sternlight<\/strong> of any vessel<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Three all-round green<\/strong> (one at the foremast head, one at each end of the fore yard) + power-driven lights<\/td>\n<td><strong>Mine clearance<\/strong> — dangerous to approach within <strong>1000 m<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Three <strong>balls<\/strong> in the same pattern<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pair trawling<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Each trawler shows trawling lights (green over white) and, by night, may direct a <strong>searchlight forward towards the other vessel of the pair<\/strong> (Annex II)<\/td>\n<td>Two cones apex together<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RAM towing, tow &gt;200 m<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Three<\/strong> masthead lights in a vertical line, sidelights, sternlight, <strong>yellow towing light<\/strong> above the sternlight, <strong>plus red-white-red<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Diamond<\/strong> + <strong>ball-diamond-ball<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Partly submerged tow<\/strong> (Rule 24(g))<\/td>\n<td>Under 25 m breadth: an all-round white at or near each end (a dracone needn't show the forward one); 25 m breadth or more: two more at the extremities of the breadth; over 100 m long: extra whites so they're no more than 100 m apart<\/td>\n<td>A <strong>diamond<\/strong> at the after end of the last object towed; if the tow is over 200 m, another diamond as far forward as practicable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fishing (not trawling), gear &gt;150 m<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Red over white<\/strong>, plus an all-round <strong>white towards the gear<\/strong>; sidelights and sternlight when making way<\/td>\n<td>Two cones apex together + a <strong>cone apex up<\/strong> towards the gear<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pilot vessel at anchor<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>White over red<\/strong> + anchor light(s)<\/td>\n<td>Anchor ball<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aground, ≥100 m<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Anchor lights for her length + <strong>two all-round red<\/strong> in a vertical line<\/td>\n<td><strong>Three balls<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NUC<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Two all-round red<\/strong>; add sidelights and sternlight when <strong>making way<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Two balls<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Arcs (Rule 21):<\/strong> masthead 225° (to 22.5° abaft the beam each side), sidelights 112.5°, sternlight 135°, all-round 360°.\n<strong>Spacing (Annex I):<\/strong> on a vessel of 20 m or more, the forward masthead light is at least 6 m above the hull (or the breadth if greater, need not exceed 12 m); the after masthead light is at least <strong>4.5 m higher<\/strong> than the forward one; the <strong>horizontal distance<\/strong> between them is at least <strong>half the length<\/strong>, but need not be more than <strong>100 m<\/strong>.\"<\/p>","pq":"A vessel shows red over white with no sidelights — what is she doing?","pa":"<p>Fishing (not trawling) and not making way through the water.<\/p>","asked":["2 white lights in a vertical line an 2 side lights what is it ?","Side- Arc of visibility of all lights?","NUC stopped what lights switch off ?","Identify Lights and action.","Green light actions?"],"ex":[["Alec Keep",15],["Examiner not named",12],["Khademul Insan",11],["Nayyer Naqvi",11],["Kaiser Ali",11]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q299","sec":16,"q":"Fog (sound) signals — full set, especially pilot vessel, aground, at anchor ≥100 m, CBD, pushing/towing; and ALL distress signals of Annex IV.","tier":"red","rep":119,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Pilot vessel fog signal / distress signals — fails 2022–23","law":null,"a":"<p>\"<strong>Rule 35<\/strong> — intervals of not more than <strong>2 minutes<\/strong> unless stated:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vessel<\/th>\n<th>Signal<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Power-driven, making way<\/td>\n<td>One <strong>prolonged<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power-driven, under way but stopped<\/td>\n<td><strong>Two prolonged<\/strong> about 2 seconds apart<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NUC, RAM, CBD, sailing, fishing, towing or pushing<\/strong> (and fishing or RAM working <strong>at anchor<\/strong>)<\/td>\n<td><strong>One prolonged + two short<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Towed vessel (last manned vessel of the tow)<\/td>\n<td><strong>One prolonged + three short<\/strong>, immediately after the towing vessel's signal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>At anchor<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Rapid ringing of the bell<\/strong> for about 5 seconds at intervals of not more than <strong>1 minute<\/strong>; ≥100 m: bell forward, then <strong>gong aft<\/strong>. May also sound <strong>one short, one prolonged, one short<\/strong> to warn an approaching vessel<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aground<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>The anchor bell (and gong) signal, plus <strong>three separate and distinct strokes on the bell<\/strong> immediately before and after the rapid ringing. May also sound an appropriate whistle signal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pilot vessel<\/strong> on pilotage duty<\/td>\n<td>The signal for making way, stopped or at anchor, <strong>and may in addition sound an identity signal of four short blasts<\/strong> (Rule 35(k))<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Under 12 m<\/td>\n<td>Need not give the above, but must make some other efficient sound signal at intervals of not more than 2 minutes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Annex IV — distress signals:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>A gun or other explosive signal fired at intervals of about a minute.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>continuous sounding with any fog-signalling apparatus<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Rockets or shells throwing <strong>red stars<\/strong>, fired one at a time at short intervals.<\/li>\n<li><strong>SOS<\/strong> by radiotelegraphy or any other signalling method.<\/li>\n<li><strong>MAYDAY<\/strong> by radiotelephony.<\/li>\n<li>The flags <strong>NC<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>square flag with a ball<\/strong>, or anything resembling a ball, above or below it.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Flames<\/strong> on the vessel (a burning tar or oil barrel).<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>rocket parachute flare<\/strong> or <strong>hand flare<\/strong> showing a red light.<\/li>\n<li>A smoke signal giving off <strong>orange smoke<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Slowly and repeatedly raising and lowering arms outstretched to each side.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li>A <strong>DSC distress alert<\/strong> (VHF Ch 70, or MF/HF on the DSC distress frequencies).<\/li>\n<li>A ship-to-shore distress alert through <strong>Inmarsat or another mobile satellite service<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Signals from an <strong>EPIRB<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Approved signals from radiocommunication systems, including <strong>survival craft radar transponders (SART)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Also, for identification from the air: a piece of <strong>orange canvas with a black square and circle<\/strong>, and a <strong>dye marker<\/strong>.\"<\/p>","pq":"You hear one prolonged and two short ahead — what could it be?","pa":"<p>Any of NUC, RAM, CBD, sailing, fishing, towing/pushing, or fishing/RAM at anchor — so treat it as a vessel with limited manoeuvrability and apply Rule 19(e).<\/p>","asked":["Identify, day shape and fog signal?","What is the fog signal?","What is the sound signal if they are in agreement?","Light and shape. Identify the vessel, fog signal and action?","Identify lights shape and fog signals?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",14],["Nayyer Naqvi",10],["Shahid Akhter",10],["Chris Roe",10],["Kaiser Ali",9]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q300","sec":16,"q":"Word-for-word set: Rule 6 (safe speed — all factors), Rule 7, Rule 8 (a)–(f), Rule 10, Rule 13 (22.5°), Rule 2 (departure).","tier":"red","rep":32,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Rule 6 not word-perfect — fail, Pateman 2021","law":null,"a":"<p>\"<strong>Rule 2 — Responsibility.<\/strong> (a) Nothing in these Rules shall exonerate any vessel, or the owner, master or crew thereof, from the consequences of any neglect to comply with these Rules or of the neglect of any precaution which may be required by the ordinary practice of seamen, or by the special circumstances of the case. (b) In construing and complying with these Rules due regard shall be had to all dangers of navigation and collision and to any special circumstances, including the limitations of the vessels involved, which may make a departure from these Rules necessary to avoid immediate danger.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rule 6 — Safe speed.<\/strong> Every vessel shall at all times proceed at a safe speed so that she can take proper and effective action to avoid collision and be stopped within a distance appropriate to the prevailing circumstances and conditions. Factors: <strong>(a) by all vessels<\/strong> — the state of visibility; traffic density, including concentrations of fishing vessels or any other vessels; manoeuvrability, with special reference to stopping distance and turning ability in the prevailing conditions; at night, background light from shore lights or back scatter of her own lights; the state of wind, sea and current and the proximity of navigational hazards; the draught in relation to the available depth of water. <strong>(b) additionally, by vessels with operational radar<\/strong> — the characteristics, efficiency and limitations of the radar; constraints imposed by the range scale in use; the effect on detection of sea state, weather and other interference; the possibility that small vessels, ice and other floating objects may not be detected at an adequate range; the number, location and movement of vessels detected; the more exact assessment of visibility possible when radar is used to measure ranges.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rule 7 — Risk of collision.<\/strong> (a) Every vessel shall use all available means appropriate to the prevailing circumstances and conditions to determine if risk of collision exists. If there is any doubt such risk shall be deemed to exist. (b) Proper use of radar, including long-range scanning and radar plotting or equivalent systematic observation. (c) Assumptions shall not be made on the basis of scanty information, especially scanty radar information. (d) Risk shall be deemed to exist if the compass bearing of an approaching vessel does not appreciably change; and may sometimes exist even with an appreciable bearing change, particularly with a very large vessel, a tow, or at close range.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rule 8 — Action to avoid collision.<\/strong> (a) Positive, in ample time, with due regard to good seamanship. (b) Large enough to be readily apparent visually or by radar; avoid a succession of small alterations. (c) With sufficient sea room, alteration of course alone may be most effective, if made in good time, substantial, and not resulting in another close-quarters situation. (d) Pass at a safe distance; check the effectiveness until the other vessel is finally past and clear. (e) If necessary, slacken speed or take all way off. (f) The 'not impede' paragraphs (i)–(iii) — see Q293 and Q294.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rule 10<\/strong> — the key paragraphs: proceed in the appropriate lane in the general direction of traffic flow; keep clear of the separation line or zone; join or leave at the termini, or from the side at as small an angle as practicable; <strong>cross on a heading as nearly as practicable at right angles<\/strong> to the general direction of traffic flow; inshore traffic zones (10(d)); not entering the separation zone except to cross, join, leave, in an emergency, or to fish in it (10(e)); fishing vessels, sailing vessels and vessels under 20 m shall not impede (10(i), (j)).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rule 13 — Overtaking.<\/strong> (a) Notwithstanding anything in Part B Sections I and II, any vessel overtaking any other shall keep out of the way of the vessel being overtaken. (b) A vessel shall be deemed to be overtaking when coming up with another vessel from a direction <strong>more than 22.5 degrees abaft her beam<\/strong>, that is, in such a position that at night she would be able to see only the sternlight of that vessel but neither of her sidelights. (c) If in any doubt, she shall assume she is overtaking. (d) A subsequent alteration of bearing does not make her a crossing vessel or relieve her of the duty to keep clear until finally past and clear.\"<\/p>","pq":"Recite Rule 17(c).","pa":"<p>A power-driven vessel which takes action in a crossing situation under (a)(ii) to avoid collision with another power-driven vessel shall, if the circumstances of the case admit, not alter course to port for a vessel on her own port side.<\/p>","asked":["How do you determine Safe Speed?","Rule 6 full ?","Explain Rule 6 and the factors determining safe speed. Vessel is on HDG 000 (T) and the target vessel on BRG 135 (T).","Explain rule 6 safe speed (I did quote the entire rule and he was happy)","Full recitation of Rule 6"],"ex":[["Kaiser Ali",7],["Alec Keep",7],["Examiner not named",4],["Muhammad Iqbal",3],["Timothy Cummins",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q301","sec":17,"q":"New danger and the emergency wreck marking buoy: IALA definition of a new danger, how it's marked (duplication, racon \"D\", AIS), light, topmark, how much room you give, when the mark is removed, what you do if you meet one with no warning.","tier":"red","rep":84,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"New danger marking — fail, Jul 2023","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>IALA definition<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>new danger<\/strong> is a <strong>newly discovered hazard<\/strong> <strong>not yet shown<\/strong> in <strong>nautical documents<\/strong>: a <strong>naturally occurring<\/strong> obstruction such as a <strong>sandbank<\/strong> or <strong>rock<\/strong>, or a <strong>man-made<\/strong> danger such as a <strong>wreck<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>How it&#x27;s marked<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>With the <strong>normal marks<\/strong>: <strong>cardinal<\/strong>, <strong>lateral<\/strong> or <strong>isolated danger<\/strong> marks, as appropriate.<\/li><li>Or with an <strong>emergency wreck marking buoy<\/strong>, for a new wreck.<\/li><li>If the danger is <strong>especially grave<\/strong>, at least <strong>one<\/strong> of the marks is <strong>duplicated<\/strong>: an <strong>identical<\/strong> mark placed next to it.<\/li><li>The mark may carry a <strong>racon<\/strong> coded <strong>Morse &quot;D&quot;<\/strong> (— · ·), showing a signal length of 1 nm on the radar.<\/li><li>And/or an <strong>AIS<\/strong> signal, including a <strong>virtual<\/strong> or <strong>synthetic<\/strong> AIS aid to navigation.<\/li><li>A navigational warning is issued: NAVTEX, NAVAREA, local warnings.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The emergency wreck marking buoy<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Blue and yellow vertical stripes<\/strong> (4 to 8 stripes).<\/li><li>A <strong>yellow upright cross<\/strong> topmark.<\/li><li>An <strong>alternating blue and yellow<\/strong> light (blue 1 second, yellow 1 second).<\/li><li>Placed as <strong>close as possible<\/strong> to the wreck, or in a <strong>pattern<\/strong> around it.<\/li><li>Verify the current IALA guidance: IALA has revised how emergency wreck marks fit into the buoyage system.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When it&#x27;s removed<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When the danger has been <strong>surveyed<\/strong> and <strong>promulgated<\/strong> in <strong>nautical publications<\/strong> (charts, Notices to Mariners), and <strong>permanent marking<\/strong> is in place, or the wreck has been <strong>removed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The emergency wreck marker normally stays at least until the information has been fully promulgated.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How much room to give<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>There&#x27;s <strong>no single IALA distance<\/strong>.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d check the <strong>navigational warning<\/strong> (NAVTEX, Notices to Mariners) for any <strong>exclusion zone<\/strong>, and quote that.<\/li><li><strong>Stay well clear<\/strong> on the safe side shown by the mark, and <strong>never pass between<\/strong> the mark and the wreck.<\/li><li>Allow for the uncertainty of the wreck&#x27;s exact position and extent, the tide, and the visibility.<\/li><li>The failed candidate in July 2023 was <strong>pressed for a number<\/strong>, so quote the warning&#x27;s zone if one is given, and otherwise say what margin I&#x27;d use and why (for example at least 1 nm in open water, more at night or in poor visibility).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If I meet one without warning<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Treat it as a <strong>new danger<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong>, <strong>call the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fix the position<\/strong> accurately.<\/li><li>Identify the mark by colour, topmark and light, and <strong>keep clear<\/strong> on the safe side.<\/li><li>Check the latest <strong>warnings<\/strong> in case I&#x27;ve missed one.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> it to the <strong>coastguard<\/strong> and the <strong>UKHO<\/strong> (hydrographic note), with the position, description and time.<\/li><li>Warn other ships if appropriate with a Sécurité.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The IALA definition; marking with normal marks or the emergency wreck marker, duplication for grave dangers, racon D and AIS; the EWM&#x27;s stripes, cross topmark and alternating light; removal after survey, promulgation and permanent marking; room to give from the navigational warning, never between the mark and the wreck; and the actions if I meet one unannounced.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What does Morse 'D' on a racon mean?","pa":"<p>A new danger — the racon shows a one-mile line on the radar display.<\/p>","asked":["Emergency Wreck Mark – what is it?","New danger buoy – light characteristics","Emergency Wreck Mark – Actions if ahead?","Definition of vessel?","How will you get more information about the emergency wreck?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",9],["Nayyer Naqvi",9],["Colum Leggett",6],["Timothy Cummins",6],["Mahmood ul Hassan",5]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q302","sec":17,"q":"Preferred-channel marks in both regions, with and against the direction of buoyage; direction-of-buoyage chart symbol; Region B areas; special mark uses (can vs cone; Fl(5)Y 20s = ODAS).","tier":"red","rep":114,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Preferred-channel marks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Modified lateral marks<\/strong>, used at a point where a channel divides, to show the <strong>preferred<\/strong> (main) channel.<\/li><li>They have a <strong>broad horizontal band<\/strong> of the <strong>other colour<\/strong>, and a <strong>Fl(2+1)<\/strong> light (composite group flashing, two flashes followed by one).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Region A<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Preferred channel to starboard<\/strong> (a modified <strong>port-hand<\/strong> mark): <strong>red can<\/strong> with a <strong>broad green band<\/strong>, <strong>red can<\/strong> topmark, <strong>Fl(2+1)R<\/strong>. Treat it as a port-hand mark: leave it to port, and the preferred channel is to starboard.<\/li><li><strong>Preferred channel to port<\/strong> (a modified <strong>starboard-hand<\/strong> mark): <strong>green cone<\/strong> with a <strong>broad red band<\/strong>, <strong>green cone<\/strong> topmark, <strong>Fl(2+1)G<\/strong>. Treat it as a starboard-hand mark.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Region B<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Preferred channel to starboard<\/strong>: <strong>green can<\/strong> with a <strong>broad red band<\/strong>, <strong>green can<\/strong> topmark, <strong>Fl(2+1)G<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Preferred channel to port<\/strong>: <strong>red cone<\/strong> with a <strong>broad green band<\/strong>, <strong>red cone<\/strong> topmark, <strong>Fl(2+1)R<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The shapes are the same as Region A; only the colours are reversed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How to work it<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Decide <strong>first<\/strong> whether you&#x27;re going <strong>with<\/strong> or <strong>against<\/strong> the <strong>direction of buoyage<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Then treat the mark as the <strong>lateral mark<\/strong> its <strong>shape<\/strong> shows: a can is a port-hand mark, a cone a starboard-hand mark, in the direction of buoyage.<\/li><li><strong>Against<\/strong> the direction of buoyage, <strong>everything reverses<\/strong>.<\/li><li>You can take either channel, but the preferred one is the main channel; the other may be shallower.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Direction of buoyage<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The direction when <strong>approaching a harbour<\/strong> from <strong>seaward<\/strong>, or as set by the <strong>authority<\/strong>, generally <strong>clockwise<\/strong> around continents (in Region A around the UK, northwards up the west coast and southwards down the east coast, and eastwards in the English Channel).<\/li><li>On the chart, it&#x27;s shown by a <strong>direction-of-buoyage arrow<\/strong> symbol (a magenta arrow with two dots).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Region B areas<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>North, Central and South America<\/strong>, <strong>Japan<\/strong>, <strong>Korea<\/strong> and the <strong>Philippines<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Region A<\/strong> is the <strong>rest<\/strong> of the world.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Special marks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Yellow<\/strong>, with a <strong>yellow X<\/strong> topmark, and a <strong>yellow light<\/strong> of any rhythm <strong>not used<\/strong> for white lights on other marks, for example <strong>Fl(5)Y 20s<\/strong>, often used for an <strong>ODAS<\/strong> data buoy.<\/li><li>If a special mark has a <strong>lateral shape<\/strong>, can or cone, it should be <strong>passed<\/strong> on the <strong>side<\/strong> that shape indicates.<\/li><li>Uses: spoil grounds, cables, recreation areas, military areas, ODAS.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Preferred-channel marks as modified lateral marks with a broad band of the other colour and Fl(2+1), described for both regions; work out with or against the direction of buoyage first and then treat it by its shape; the direction of buoyage and its chart symbol; the Region B areas; and special marks with Fl(5)Y 20s for ODAS and the can or cone shape meaning.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Heading inbound in Region B, you see a red cone with a green band — which side?","pa":"<p>Inbound (with the direction of buoyage) it&#x27;s a modified starboard-hand mark: the preferred channel is to port, so leave it to starboard.<\/p>","asked":["Preferred Channel Mark?","Pointed to a starboard lateral mark, preferred channel to port IALA A. What buoy's this?","Prefer channel to port Aila region A?","Alter to STBD for preferred channel,","Region-B Preferred channel to port (out bound)"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",16],["Examiner not named",14],["Zia Ul Haq",11],["Ahsan Khan",10],["Chris Roe",6]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q303","sec":17,"q":"Heading 180°, east cardinal on your port side and west cardinal on your starboard side, 500 m apart — which way? Cardinal with no topmark; isolated danger dead ahead not on the chart.","tier":"yellow","rep":8,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The principle<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>cardinal mark<\/strong> is named for the <strong>side<\/strong> it&#x27;s on from the <strong>danger<\/strong>, so you <strong>pass on the named side<\/strong>: an <strong>east cardinal<\/strong> has <strong>safe water to its east<\/strong>, a <strong>west cardinal<\/strong> to its <strong>west<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Heading 180°<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>My <strong>port<\/strong> side is <strong>east<\/strong>, and my <strong>starboard<\/strong> side is <strong>west<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Case 1: east cardinal on my port (east) side, west cardinal on my starboard (west) side, 500 m apart<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The east cardinal&#x27;s safe water is <strong>further east<\/strong>, and the west cardinal&#x27;s is <strong>further west<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So the <strong>danger is between them<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>I don&#x27;t pass through the middle<\/strong>.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d <strong>alter to starboard<\/strong> and pass <strong>west<\/strong> of the <strong>west cardinal<\/strong>, keeping it on my <strong>port<\/strong> side. Examiner Hassan wanted that good-seamanship starboard alteration. Passing east of the east cardinal is also safe but means an alteration to port.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Case 2: the mirror case, west cardinal to port, east cardinal to starboard<\/strong> (examiners Ali, Iqbal, Jackson, Roe and Insan have asked it)<\/p><ul><li>Now the <strong>safe water<\/strong> of <strong>both marks<\/strong> is the <strong>gap between them<\/strong>: the west cardinal&#x27;s safe side (west) and the east cardinal&#x27;s safe side (east) face each other.<\/li><li>So I <strong>pass between them<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d always say<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>&quot;I&#x27;d <strong>check the chart<\/strong>, confirm the marks&#x27; <strong>identity<\/strong> by <strong>topmark<\/strong> and <strong>light<\/strong>, and <strong>draw it<\/strong> if I need to.&quot;<\/li><li>Drawing it in the exam avoids a wrong answer under pressure.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A cardinal with no topmark<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Identify<\/strong> it by its <strong>colour<\/strong> and <strong>light<\/strong> (the flash rhythm).<\/li><li>Treat it with <strong>caution<\/strong>: it may be damaged, or not what it seems.<\/li><li><strong>Check<\/strong> the <strong>chart<\/strong> and <strong>Notices to Mariners<\/strong>, and recent navigational warnings.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> the <strong>defect<\/strong> to the <strong>coastguard<\/strong> (or the GLA through the coastguard) so it can be repaired and a warning issued.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>An uncharted isolated danger mark dead ahead<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>It may be a <strong>new danger<\/strong>, or a <strong>mark off station<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong>, <strong>call the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fix the position<\/strong> accurately.<\/li><li><strong>Check<\/strong> the <strong>latest warnings<\/strong>: NAVTEX, NtMs, local warnings.<\/li><li><strong>Keep well clear<\/strong>: an isolated danger mark means a danger of limited extent with navigable water all round, but I don&#x27;t know how big this one is.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> it to the <strong>coastguard<\/strong> and <strong>UKHO<\/strong> (the hydrographic note), with the position and description.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Cardinals are named for the side on which safe water lies; in case 1 the danger is between them so don&#x27;t pass through, alter to starboard and pass west of the west cardinal; in the mirror case pass between them; check the chart and draw it; a mark with no topmark identified by colour and light and reported; and an uncharted isolated danger mark treated with caution, slow down, fix, check warnings, keep clear and report.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Give me the light of a west cardinal.","pa":"<p>Quick flashing Q(9) 15s or very quick VQ(9) 10s — 9 flashes, like 9 o&#x27;clock.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q304","sec":6,"q":"Unberthing / berthing scenarios (single RH FPP, no tugs/thrusters): port side alongside with wind onto the berth; tide from ahead; between two ships; stbd side to with 3 kn current and cross wind; channel exit astern.","tier":"orange","rep":37,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>\"First the principles. With a <strong>right-handed fixed-pitch propeller<\/strong>, going <strong>astern<\/strong> the stern <strong>cants to port<\/strong>, and the bow swings to starboard. Going ahead from stopped there's a smaller kick the other way: the stern goes to starboard and the bow to port. So <strong>port side to<\/strong> is the natural side for <strong>berthing<\/strong> — going astern to stop pulls the stern in towards the berth.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Berthing port side to, onshore wind:<\/strong> approach at a shallower angle and slower than normal, because the wind will set me down. Get a <strong>head line and forward spring<\/strong> ashore early, then let the wind and a short kick astern bring her alongside gently. Have the <strong>starboard anchor<\/strong> ready to dredge to control the bow.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Unberthing port side to, wind onto the berth:<\/strong> the hardest case for a single RH screw, because going astern brings the stern <strong>back into the berth<\/strong>. Use the <strong>forward spring<\/strong>: rudder to port, slow ahead on the spring, so the bow is held against the fenders and the stern swings out. When the stern is well off — 20° to 30° — let go and come astern firmly, then ahead. If the wind is too strong, <strong>ask for a tug<\/strong>. That's the answer the examiner wants to hear when it's unsafe.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Starboard side to:<\/strong> the astern transverse thrust pulls the stern <strong>away<\/strong> from the berth, which helps unberthing. For berthing, approach at a shallower angle and use the spring, because going astern will throw the stern off.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Tide from ahead:<\/strong> stem it. It gives steerage at low speed over the ground. To unberth, slack the head line and hold the stern on the <strong>after spring<\/strong>, so the current sets the bow off. Then let go and go ahead.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Between two ships:<\/strong> spring off with good fendering, and keep the swing small. Check the clearance ahead and astern before using any engine movement.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Cross current of 3 knots with a cross wind:<\/strong> I'd plan the approach to land upstream and let the current set me on, with the anchor ready, and use a tug if the forces are too large.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Always: a pilot card brief, mooring party ready with heaving lines, the anchor cleared away, and a clear plan agreed before we start.\"<\/p>","pq":"Why is port side to easier for berthing?","pa":"<p>Going astern to take the way off, the transverse thrust kicks the stern to port — towards the berth.<\/p>","asked":["The berth and take a turn of 180 deg and head south to resume the passage?","Port Side Alongside?","Joining as chief mate from berth, walk me through it?","Ship Handling, you are the master here, (You were going to port but berthing got cancelled, now agent tell you to anchor in this posiLon, acLons?","How would you unberth and head south?"],"ex":[["Michele Moro",9],["Muhammad Iqbal",4],["Ahsan Khan",4],["James Gilbert",3],["Examiner not named",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q305","sec":6,"q":"MOB from the bridge: which manoeuvre (Williamson / Anderson / Scharnow) and why; what the 3/O does before you arrive; recovery; sweeping a lee for the rescue boat. Pivot point; turning-circle diagram; running vs standing vs open moor (and which under a bridge).","tier":"orange","rep":15,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>\"<strong>Man overboard — which turn:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anderson (single turn):<\/strong> for an <strong>immediate action<\/strong> with the person in sight. Hard over towards the side he fell from, and after about 250° of turn, approach with reduced speed. Quickest, but needs a handy ship.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Williamson:<\/strong> for a <strong>delayed action<\/strong> or at <strong>night / restricted visibility<\/strong>. Hard over towards the side, at about 60° from the original course hard over the other way, and come round to the <strong>reciprocal<\/strong>, back on the original track. Slow, but it brings you back along the track.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Scharnow:<\/strong> for a <strong>delayed action<\/strong> when the ship has already run on. It brings you back into the wake in less distance than the Williamson, but it's no good for an immediate action.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>What the 3/O does before I reach the bridge:<\/strong> release the <strong>lifebuoy with light and smoke<\/strong> on the side he fell, sound the <strong>MOB alarm<\/strong> as per the muster list, press <strong>MOB on the ECDIS/GPS<\/strong>, start the turn, post a <strong>lookout who keeps pointing<\/strong>, call the Master and engine room, hoist flag <strong>O<\/strong>, and alert the coastguard and nearby ships. <strong>Recovery:<\/strong> make a <strong>lee<\/strong>, rescue boat or the ship's recovery equipment under <strong>SOLAS III/17-1<\/strong>, and treat for hypothermia — lift horizontally if possible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Pivot point:<\/strong> going ahead, about a <strong>quarter to a third of the length from the bow<\/strong>, moving forward as headway increases. Going astern it moves to about a quarter of the length from the stern.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Turning circle:<\/strong> advance, transfer, tactical diameter and final diameter, and the <strong>drift angle<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Moors:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Running moor:<\/strong> used where the stream reverses, to keep the swinging circle small. Approach <strong>stemming the stream with headway<\/strong>, drop the first anchor, carry on ahead paying out about <strong>double<\/strong> the scope, drop the second, then fall back with the stream, heaving in the first and paying out the second until both have equal scope. The <strong>engine<\/strong> lays out the cable.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Standing moor:<\/strong> stemming the stream, drop the first anchor, drop back with the stream paying out double scope, drop the second, then heave in to equal scope. Easier because the stream does the work.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Open moor:<\/strong> both anchors laid ahead, <strong>spread at an angle<\/strong> towards the wind or current. It gives <strong>better holding and less yawing<\/strong> when the force comes from one main direction.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Under a bridge<\/strong>, the examiner who failed a candidate wanted the <strong>open moor<\/strong>: with a strong flow from one direction and an obstruction close by, two spread anchors hold her firmly and limit her yaw. Running and standing moors are for <strong>reversing<\/strong> tidal streams in a narrow river.\"<\/p>","pq":"How do you clear a foul hawse?","pa":"<p>Wait for the turns to be shown at slack water, then use the ship&#x27;s swing, the engine or a tug to unwind; if needed, unshackle and dip the end of one cable round the other with a lighter or the mooring boat.<\/p>","asked":["U r at stbd bridge wings some one deck Inform u that mob on stbd side what is your first action and series of action?","Is a man overboard rescues boat ready for lowering?","Working ongoing, you're on bridge watch and AB calls and tells you MOB, Actions?","You are on watch and the vessel is coming up to the beginning of the Dover straits, you have a man overboard what are you actions going to be ?","We now have a man overboard situation, witnessed by crew, with Williamson turn already completed, however we lost sight of him. What actions would I take?"],"ex":[["Inamul Bhuiyan",3],["Jason Pateman",2],["Zia Ul Haq",2],["Colum Leggett",2],["Byron Griffiths",2]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q341","sec":17,"q":"Lateral marks: Region A and B, inbound and outbound. \"Region A port-hand mark, outbound — what do you do?","tier":"red","rep":209,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":"Direction of buoyage — fails 2025–26","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What lateral marks are<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Lateral marks<\/strong> show the <strong>port<\/strong> and <strong>starboard<\/strong> sides of a channel in the <strong>direction of buoyage<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s the direction when you <strong>approach<\/strong> a <strong>harbour<\/strong> or <strong>estuary<\/strong> from <strong>seaward<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Elsewhere<\/strong>, the <strong>authorities<\/strong> set it, usually <strong>clockwise<\/strong> round the land masses, and it&#x27;s shown on the chart by an <strong>arrow<\/strong> symbol.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Region A<\/strong> (Europe, Africa, most of Asia, Australia and New Zealand)<\/p><ul><li><strong>Port-hand mark<\/strong>: <strong>red<\/strong>, <strong>can<\/strong>-shaped (or pillar or spar), <strong>red can<\/strong> topmark, <strong>red<\/strong> light of <strong>any rhythm<\/strong> except Fl(2+1).<\/li><li><strong>Starboard-hand mark<\/strong>: <strong>green<\/strong>, <strong>conical<\/strong> (or pillar or spar), <strong>green cone<\/strong> topmark (point up), <strong>green<\/strong> light of any rhythm except Fl(2+1).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Region B<\/strong> (the Americas, Japan, Korea and the Philippines)<\/p><ul><li>The <strong>colours<\/strong> are <strong>reversed<\/strong>: <strong>red<\/strong> is to <strong>starboard<\/strong>, so it&#x27;s &quot;<strong>red right returning<\/strong>&quot;.<\/li><li>Port-hand: green can; starboard-hand: red cone.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What doesn&#x27;t change<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Shapes<\/strong> and <strong>topmarks<\/strong> don&#x27;t change between regions. A <strong>can<\/strong> is <strong>always port-hand<\/strong> and a <strong>cone<\/strong> is <strong>always starboard-hand<\/strong>, in the direction of buoyage.<\/li><li><strong>Numbering<\/strong>: usually odd numbers on starboard-hand marks and even on port-hand marks in Region A from seaward, but it varies.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Region A port-hand mark, outbound: what do I do?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Outbound<\/strong>, I&#x27;m going <strong>against<\/strong> the direction of buoyage, so <strong>everything is reversed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>I leave the <strong>red can<\/strong> on my <strong>starboard<\/strong> side, and the <strong>green cones<\/strong> on my <strong>port<\/strong> side.<\/li><li>In other words, keep the red can to starboard going out, as I&#x27;d keep it to port coming in.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Lights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Fl(2+1)<\/strong> is <strong>reserved<\/strong> for <strong>preferred-channel<\/strong> marks.<\/li><li>Lateral marks can be any other rhythm: Fl, Fl(2), Q, Oc, Iso, and so on.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before answering<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I&#x27;d <strong>confirm<\/strong> the <strong>region<\/strong> and the <strong>direction of buoyage<\/strong> on the <strong>chart<\/strong>. In some areas, the direction of buoyage runs differently from what you&#x27;d expect, for example along a coast.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Common traps<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Mixing up the colours between regions.<\/li><li>Forgetting to reverse when outbound.<\/li><li>Confusing a preferred-channel mark with a lateral mark.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Lateral marks in the direction of buoyage (from seaward, or as the authority sets, shown by the arrow), Region A red can to port and green cone to starboard with any light except Fl(2+1), Region B colours reversed with &quot;red right returning&quot;, shapes never change, and outbound in Region A, leave the red can to starboard; and confirm the region and direction on the chart.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"You're entering New York at night and see a flashing red light ahead. Which side do you leave it?","pa":"<p>Region <strong>B<\/strong> (USA): red is the <strong>starboard-hand<\/strong> mark when entering, &quot;<strong>red, right, returning<\/strong>&quot;. Leave it to <strong>starboard<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["Region A- Port hand lateral mark- outbound?","Buoys (Identify,Light and Action?","Colregs/ Lights / Buoyage","Buoyage Light & Action (Inbound)","ROR, buoyage same as previous reports."],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",25],["Zia Ul Haq",18],["Kaiser Ali",15],["Examiner not named",15],["Alec Keep",14]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q342","sec":17,"q":"Cardinal marks: colours, topmarks and lights. \"Heading 090, you see a north cardinal right ahead — action?","tier":"red","rep":203,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":"Cardinal on the wrong heading — fail, Steer 2026","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The principle<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>cardinal mark<\/strong> is placed in the <strong>quadrant<\/strong> named after it, and you <strong>pass on that side<\/strong> of the mark.<\/li><li>A <strong>north cardinal<\/strong> means <strong>safe water<\/strong> is to the <strong>north<\/strong>, and the <strong>danger<\/strong> is to the <strong>south<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The four cardinals<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>North<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Topmark<\/strong>: two black cones, <strong>both point up<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Colours<\/strong>: <strong>black over yellow<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong> (white): <strong>VQ<\/strong> or <strong>Q<\/strong>, continuous.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>East<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Topmark<\/strong>: <strong>base to base<\/strong> (one up, one down, bases together).<\/li><li><strong>Colours<\/strong>: <strong>black, yellow, black<\/strong> (black bands above and below yellow).<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong>: <strong>VQ(3) every 5 s<\/strong> or <strong>Q(3) every 10 s<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>South<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Topmark<\/strong>: <strong>both point down<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Colours<\/strong>: <strong>yellow over black<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong>: <strong>VQ(6)+LFl every 10 s<\/strong> or <strong>Q(6)+LFl every 15 s<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>West<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Topmark<\/strong>: <strong>point to point<\/strong> (points together).<\/li><li><strong>Colours<\/strong>: <strong>yellow, black, yellow<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong>: <strong>VQ(9) every 10 s<\/strong> or <strong>Q(9) every 15 s<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Memory aids<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>flashes<\/strong> follow a <strong>clock face<\/strong>: <strong>3<\/strong> for east (3 o&#x27;clock), <strong>6<\/strong> for south, <strong>9<\/strong> for west, and continuous for north (12).<\/li><li>The <strong>long flash<\/strong> after south&#x27;s six stops it being <strong>mistaken<\/strong> for east or west in poor conditions.<\/li><li>The <strong>cones point<\/strong> to where the <strong>black bands<\/strong> are: north cones point up and the black is on top; south point down and black is at the bottom; east base to base with black at top and bottom; west point to point with black in the middle.<\/li><li>West&#x27;s topmark looks like a &quot;W&quot; on its side, or &quot;a wineglass waist&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Heading 090°, a north cardinal right ahead<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I must pass <strong>north<\/strong> of it, which is to my <strong>left<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So I <strong>alter to port<\/strong> and <strong>leave the buoy on my starboard<\/strong> side.<\/li><li>And check the chart for the danger to the south, which I&#x27;m now heading away from.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Heading 180°, a west cardinal ahead<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I pass <strong>west<\/strong> of it, which is to my <strong>right<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So I <strong>alter to starboard<\/strong> and leave it to <strong>port<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Heading 270°, a south cardinal ahead<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Pass south of it, to my left: alter to port and leave it to starboard.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>I always draw it before answering<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Draw the mark, label its safe side, then my heading. <strong>The danger is on the side opposite the mark&#x27;s name.<\/strong><\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Pass on the named side, the four topmarks, colours and lights exactly (N continuous, E 3, S 6 + long flash, W 9, with the VQ and Q periods), the memory aids, and the worked answer: heading 090 with a north cardinal ahead, alter to port and leave it to starboard; and draw it first.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Two cardinals 500 m apart, an east on your port bow and a west on your starboard bow. Can you go between them? (See Q303.)","pa":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong> The east cardinal has safe water to its <strong>east<\/strong> and the west cardinal to its <strong>west<\/strong>, so the <strong>danger lies between them<\/strong>. Pass east of the east mark or west of the west mark.<\/p>","asked":["What are the lights on a west cardinal mark?","North cardinal mark- Heading 090?","East cardinal mark. What does it indicate?","Pointed at a south cardinal buoy. What's this?","You heading 180 West Cardinal buoy?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",32],["Examiner not named",27],["Colum Leggett",17],["Chris Roe",15],["Zia Ul Haq",12]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q343","sec":17,"q":"Isolated danger and safe water marks; light rhythms: what are occulting, isophase and long-flash?","tier":"red","rep":166,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Isolated danger mark<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Erected on<\/strong>, or <strong>moored over<\/strong>, a <strong>danger of limited extent<\/strong> with <strong>navigable water all round<\/strong> it, such as a rock or a wreck.<\/li><li><strong>Colours<\/strong>: <strong>black<\/strong> with one or more broad horizontal <strong>red bands<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Topmark<\/strong>: <strong>two black spheres<\/strong>, one above the other.<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong>: <strong>white<\/strong>, <strong>Fl(2)<\/strong> (group flashing two). Remember &quot;two spheres, two flashes&quot;.<\/li><li><strong>Shape<\/strong>: optional, but pillar or spar usually.<\/li><li>I give it a <strong>wide berth<\/strong>. I <strong>don&#x27;t pass close<\/strong>, because I don&#x27;t know the exact extent of the danger.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safe water mark<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Shows <strong>navigable water all round<\/strong> the mark. Used as a <strong>landfall<\/strong> mark, a <strong>mid-channel<\/strong> mark or a <strong>centreline<\/strong> mark.<\/li><li><strong>Colours<\/strong>: <strong>red and white vertical stripes<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Shape<\/strong>: <strong>spherical<\/strong>, <strong>pillar<\/strong> or <strong>spar<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Topmark<\/strong>: <strong>one red sphere<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Light<\/strong>: <strong>white<\/strong>: <strong>isophase<\/strong>, <strong>occulting<\/strong>, <strong>one long flash every 10 s<\/strong>, or <strong>Morse &quot;A&quot;<\/strong> (· —).<\/li><li>I can pass <strong>either side<\/strong>, usually leaving it <strong>close to port<\/strong> when approaching a channel, as I&#x27;d do when keeping to the starboard side of the channel.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Light rhythms<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Fixed (F)<\/strong>: a continuous steady light.<\/li><li><strong>Flashing (Fl)<\/strong>: the <strong>light<\/strong> is <strong>shorter<\/strong> than the <strong>dark<\/strong>; flashes at a rate of less than 50 a minute.<\/li><li><strong>Long flash (LFl)<\/strong>: a flash of <strong>2 seconds<\/strong> or more.<\/li><li><strong>Occulting (Oc)<\/strong>: the <strong>light<\/strong> is <strong>longer<\/strong> than the <strong>dark<\/strong>. The light is mostly on and &quot;<strong>eclipsed<\/strong>&quot; briefly.<\/li><li><strong>Isophase (Iso)<\/strong>: the <strong>light<\/strong> and <strong>dark<\/strong> are <strong>equal<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Quick (Q)<\/strong>: about <strong>60 flashes<\/strong> a minute (50 to 79).<\/li><li><strong>Very quick (VQ)<\/strong>: about <strong>100–120<\/strong> flashes a minute (80 to 159).<\/li><li><strong>Ultra quick (UQ)<\/strong>: 160 or more a minute.<\/li><li><strong>Group<\/strong> rhythms: Fl(2), Oc(3), and <strong>composite<\/strong> groups like Fl(2+1).<\/li><li><strong>Morse code<\/strong> (Mo(A)), and <strong>alternating<\/strong> colours (Al.WR).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>colour<\/strong> and <strong>rhythm<\/strong> let me <strong>identify<\/strong> a mark at <strong>night<\/strong>, before I see its shape. Each type of mark has its own rhythm, so I can tell a safe water mark (Iso, Oc, LFl or Mo(A)) from an isolated danger (Fl(2)) or a cardinal (Q or VQ groups) by its light alone.<\/li><li>A stopwatch and the chart or the List of Lights confirm it.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The isolated danger mark (on a danger of limited extent with navigable water all round, black with red bands, two black spheres, Fl(2), give it a wide berth), the safe water mark (red and white vertical stripes, one red sphere, Iso, Oc, LFl 10 s or Mo(A), pass either side usually close to port), and the light rhythms with occulting, isophase and long flash defined correctly, and why rhythm identifies a mark at night.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does a safe water mark never show a red or green light?","pa":"<p>It shows there&#x27;s <strong>safe water all round<\/strong>: mid-channel or landfall. A red or green light would suggest a <strong>lateral side<\/strong>. So it shows <strong>white<\/strong> only: isophase, occulting, LFl 10s or Morse &quot;A&quot;.<\/p>","asked":["Safe water Mark – What is Occulting?","Isolated Danger Mark – Light Characteristics and where to pass?","Buoys: Mention all light characteristics and action","Safe water mark (To identify buoy, light characteristics and purpose.)","Isolated Danger mark shape & lights?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",28],["Muhammad Iqbal",20],["Examiner not named",15],["Colum Leggett",14],["Byron Griffiths",10]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q344","sec":16,"q":"Basic encounters: how do you know risk of collision exists? Head-on, PDV crossing from starboard, PDV crossing from port.","tier":"red","rep":220,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Risk of collision: Rule 7<\/strong><\/p><p>I use <strong>all available means<\/strong>:<\/p><ol><li>A <strong>series of compass bearings<\/strong>: if the bearing <strong>doesn&#x27;t change appreciably<\/strong>, risk <strong>exists<\/strong> (Rule 7(d)(i)).<\/li><li><strong>Radar plotting<\/strong> and <strong>ARPA<\/strong>, starting with <strong>long-range scanning<\/strong> to obtain early warning (Rule 7(b)).<\/li><li><strong>No assumptions<\/strong> from <strong>scanty<\/strong> radar information (7(c)).<\/li><li>Risk can exist <strong>even with a changing bearing<\/strong> when the other vessel is <strong>very large<\/strong> or <strong>towing<\/strong>, or at <strong>close range<\/strong> (7(d)(ii)).<\/li><li><strong>If in doubt<\/strong>, risk is <strong>deemed to exist<\/strong> (7(a)).<\/li><li>AIS helps identify the target but isn&#x27;t a substitute for bearings and plotting.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Head-on: Rule 14<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>We&#x27;re on <strong>reciprocal<\/strong> or <strong>nearly reciprocal<\/strong> courses, so as to involve risk of collision.<\/li><li>At <strong>night<\/strong> I see her <strong>masthead lights in line<\/strong> (or nearly) and/or <strong>both sidelights<\/strong>; by day the corresponding aspect.<\/li><li><strong>Both<\/strong> of us <strong>alter to starboard<\/strong> and pass <strong>port to port<\/strong>.<\/li><li>I sound <strong>one short blast<\/strong> (Rule 34(a)).<\/li><li><strong>If in doubt<\/strong>, I <strong>assume<\/strong> it&#x27;s head-on and act accordingly (14(c)).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>PDV on my starboard side: Rule 15<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When two power-driven vessels are crossing so as to involve risk of collision, the vessel which has the other on her own <strong>starboard<\/strong> side keeps out of the way. So I&#x27;m the <strong>give-way<\/strong> vessel.<\/li><li>Under <strong>Rule 16<\/strong>, I take <strong>early and substantial<\/strong> action to keep well clear.<\/li><li>Usually a <strong>bold alteration to starboard<\/strong> to pass <strong>astern<\/strong> of her, or <strong>reducing speed<\/strong> (or both).<\/li><li>I <strong>avoid crossing ahead<\/strong> of her, if the circumstances admit.<\/li><li>I sound <strong>one short blast<\/strong> for the starboard alteration, or three short for astern propulsion, and <strong>watch<\/strong> until she&#x27;s <strong>past and clear<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Rule 8: the action must be large enough to be readily apparent, and its effectiveness checked.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>PDV on my port side: Rule 17<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I&#x27;m the <strong>stand-on<\/strong> vessel. I <strong>keep course and speed<\/strong> while <strong>watching<\/strong> closely (17(a)(i)).<\/li><li>If she <strong>isn&#x27;t acting<\/strong>, I:<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>sound <strong>five short blasts<\/strong>, adding a <strong>light signal<\/strong> (Rule 34(d));<\/li><li><strong>call the Master<\/strong>, put a <strong>man on the wheel<\/strong>, and have the <strong>engines ready<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>may<\/strong> act under <strong>17(a)(ii)<\/strong> as soon as it&#x27;s apparent she isn&#x27;t taking appropriate action;<\/li><li><strong>must<\/strong> act under <strong>17(b)<\/strong> if collision <strong>can&#x27;t be avoided<\/strong> by her alone.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>I <strong>don&#x27;t alter to port<\/strong> for a vessel on my <strong>own port<\/strong> side (<strong>17(c)<\/strong>).<\/li><li>A <strong>bold turn to starboard<\/strong>, or a <strong>round turn<\/strong>, or <strong>stopping<\/strong>, is the usual action.<\/li><li>She is still not relieved of her duty (17(d)).<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Rule 7 with bearings, radar and &quot;if in doubt&quot;; head-on under Rule 14 with both altering to starboard and one short blast; crossing from starboard under Rules 15 and 16 with a bold starboard alteration or speed reduction, not crossing ahead; crossing from port under Rule 17 with keep course and speed, five short blasts, may act, must act, and not to port.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"At what point *may* the stand-on vessel act, and at what point *must* she?","pa":"<p><strong>May<\/strong> act as soon as it&#x27;s apparent the give-way vessel isn&#x27;t taking appropriate action (17(a)(ii)). <strong>Must<\/strong> act when collision can&#x27;t be avoided by the give-way vessel alone (17(b)).<\/p>","asked":["PDV crossing STBD side, actions?","Power driven vessel on port side?","PDV for port bow. Actions?","Open sea PDV crossing from port side, rule numbers and actions?","One vessel on STBD Quarter ROC exist, action ?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",39],["Colum Leggett",38],["Muhammad Iqbal",35],["Examiner not named",24],["Chris Roe",15]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q345","sec":16,"q":"A NUC, RAM, fishing vessel, sailing vessel, tug and tow or mine clearance vessel crossing on your port side. Actions?","tier":"red","rep":233,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First, identify her properly<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Her <strong>lights<\/strong> or <strong>shapes<\/strong>, her <strong>aspect<\/strong>, and whether she&#x27;s <strong>making way<\/strong> through the water. Use binoculars, radar and AIS, and the chart (a fishing area, a dredged channel, an exercise area).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then apply Rule 18<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>power-driven vessel<\/strong> under way keeps out of the way of:<\/li><li>a vessel <strong>not under command<\/strong> (NUC);<\/li><li>a vessel <strong>restricted in her ability to manoeuvre<\/strong> (RAM);<\/li><li>a vessel <strong>engaged in fishing<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>sailing vessel<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 18 overrides Rule 15<\/strong>, so I&#x27;m <strong>not<\/strong> the stand-on vessel just because she&#x27;s on my <strong>port<\/strong> side.<\/li><li>The <strong>common mistake<\/strong> is saying &quot;she&#x27;s on my port side, so I stand on&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The exceptions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Rule 18 <strong>doesn&#x27;t override Rule 13<\/strong>: an <strong>overtaking<\/strong> vessel always keeps clear, whatever she is.<\/li><li>It <strong>doesn&#x27;t override Rules 9 and 10<\/strong>: in channels and TSSs, fishing vessels and sailing vessels must not impede vessels that can only navigate within the channel or are following a lane.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My action<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Early and substantial<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 16<\/strong>): usually <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> or <strong>stop<\/strong> and let her pass <strong>ahead<\/strong>, or <strong>alter boldly to starboard<\/strong> if that <strong>clearly opens<\/strong> the passing distance (it takes me behind her).<\/li><li>A turn to <strong>port<\/strong> only if it <strong>clearly<\/strong> takes me <strong>astern<\/strong> of her with <strong>plenty of sea room<\/strong>. <strong>Never<\/strong> a <strong>small<\/strong> alteration to port, which could bring me across her bow.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>, <strong>hand steering<\/strong>, <strong>engines ready<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The right sound signals for my manoeuvre.<\/li><li>Keep watching until she&#x27;s past and clear.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Specific cases<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Tug and tow<\/strong> over <strong>200 m<\/strong> (diamond shapes, or three masthead lights): <strong>never<\/strong> cross <strong>between<\/strong> them. Pass well <strong>astern<\/strong> of the <strong>tow<\/strong>, allowing for a <strong>long<\/strong>, possibly <strong>submerged<\/strong> line. A towing vessel is not automatically RAM, but if she shows RAM signals she is.<\/li><li><strong>Mine clearance<\/strong> (three all-round green lights or three balls): keep <strong>1,000 m<\/strong> clear (<strong>Rule 27(f)<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>Fishing<\/strong> with <strong>gear<\/strong> out: pass on the side <strong>away<\/strong> from the gear. A <strong>white light<\/strong> or <strong>cone<\/strong> pointing up towards the gear means gear over <strong>150 m<\/strong> horizontally.<\/li><li><strong>NUC stopped<\/strong> (two reds only, no sidelights): she&#x27;s drifting; keep well clear and watch her drift.<\/li><li><strong>Sailing vessel<\/strong>: watch for her tacking or gybing unexpectedly.<\/li><li><strong>CBD<\/strong> on my port side: <strong>different<\/strong>. She&#x27;s a <strong>power-driven<\/strong> vessel, so see Q294 and <strong>Rule 8(f)<\/strong>: I act early so as <strong>not to impede<\/strong> her.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If risk of collision gets close<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Rule 17(b)<\/strong> logic still applies to <strong>both<\/strong> of us: whoever is in a position to avoid collision must act.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Identify her first, then Rule 18 puts me as a power-driven vessel keeping out of the way of NUC, RAM, fishing and sailing vessels, overriding Rule 15 but not Rules 13, 9 and 10; early, substantial action, usually slowing or a bold starboard turn, never a small port turn; and the specific cases (tow, mine clearance 1,000 m, gear over 150 m, CBD being different).<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A sailing vessel on your starboard bow is overtaking you. Who gives way?","pa":"<p><strong>The sailing vessel.<\/strong> <strong>Rule 13<\/strong> (overtaking) applies &quot;notwithstanding&quot; Rule 18. Any vessel overtaking keeps out of the way, so you stand on.<\/p>","asked":["NUC on the port side?","RAM towing and tow more than 200 m on port side, action?","NUC, not making way sighted dead ahead, actions?","Open Sea-Mine clearance on port bow,action?","Fishing vessel with gear extending on Stbd side?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",44],["Zia Ul Haq",34],["Muhammad Iqbal",29],["Examiner not named",26],["Khademul Insan",18]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q346","sec":16,"q":"Overtaking: in a narrow channel (sound signals), overtaking a RAM, a sailing vessel overtaking a PDV, and being overtaken.","tier":"red","rep":142,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Rule 13: overtaking<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A vessel is <strong>overtaking<\/strong> when coming up with another from a direction more than <strong>22.5° abaft her beam<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At <strong>night<\/strong> she&#x27;d see <strong>only the sternlight<\/strong> of the vessel ahead, and neither of her sidelights.<\/li><li><strong>Notwithstanding<\/strong> anything in <strong>Rules 4–18<\/strong>, the <strong>overtaking<\/strong> vessel <strong>keeps out of the way<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>If in doubt<\/strong>, assume you&#x27;re <strong>overtaking<\/strong> and act accordingly.<\/li><li>Any <strong>later alteration<\/strong> of the bearing <strong>doesn&#x27;t<\/strong> turn it into a <strong>crossing<\/strong> situation, or relieve the overtaking vessel of her duty until she&#x27;s finally past and clear.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>So<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Overtaking a RAM, NUC or fishing vessel<\/strong>: I keep clear under <strong>Rule 13<\/strong> as well as <strong>Rule 18<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>A sailing vessel overtaking a power-driven vessel<\/strong>: the <strong>sailing vessel gives way<\/strong>, because Rule 13 overrides Rule 18.<\/li><li><strong>A vessel being overtaken<\/strong>: keeps her course and speed under Rule 17.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Narrow channel: Rule 9(e) with Rule 34(c)<\/strong><\/p><p>If I can <strong>only overtake<\/strong> if the other vessel <strong>takes action<\/strong> to permit safe passing:<\/p><ol><li>I signal <strong>two prolonged and one short<\/strong> blast: &quot;I intend to overtake you on your <strong>starboard<\/strong> side&quot;, or <strong>two prolonged and two short<\/strong> for &quot;on your <strong>port<\/strong> side&quot;.<\/li><li>If she <strong>agrees<\/strong>, she sounds <strong>prolonged, short, prolonged, short<\/strong> (Morse C), and <strong>moves over<\/strong> to make room.<\/li><li>If she&#x27;s <strong>in doubt<\/strong>, she sounds <strong>five short<\/strong> (Rule 34(d)).<\/li><li>I <strong>remain<\/strong> the <strong>overtaking<\/strong> vessel under <strong>Rule 13<\/strong> throughout. Her agreement doesn&#x27;t make her responsible for keeping clear.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Also in a narrow channel (Rule 9)<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Keep to the starboard side<\/strong> of the channel or fairway, as near the outer limit as is safe and practicable (<strong>9(a)<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>Small craft<\/strong> (under 20 m) and <strong>sailing<\/strong> vessels must <strong>not impede<\/strong> a vessel that can only navigate safely within the channel (<strong>9(b)<\/strong>). Fishing vessels likewise (9(c)).<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t cross<\/strong> a channel if that <strong>impedes<\/strong> a vessel that can only navigate within it (<strong>9(d)<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>One prolonged blast<\/strong> when approaching a <strong>bend<\/strong> or an area where other vessels may be obscured (<strong>9(f)<\/strong>), answered by any vessel around the bend.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t anchor<\/strong> in the channel if the circumstances admit (<strong>9(g)<\/strong>).<\/li><li>Interaction and squat when overtaking in a narrow channel: reduce speed, pass as far apart as possible, and agree by VHF where it helps.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Being overtaken<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I <strong>keep course and speed<\/strong> (Rule 17), but I <strong>watch her<\/strong>, <strong>call the Master<\/strong>, and stay <strong>ready to act<\/strong> under 17(a)(ii) and (b) if she doesn&#x27;t keep clear.<\/li><li>I don&#x27;t just say &quot;I&#x27;m stand-on&quot;. I might also make room in a narrow channel when she signals.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Rule 13 (22.5° abaft the beam, sternlight only, notwithstanding Rules 4–18, if in doubt assume overtaking, no later change); overtaking RAM, NUC, fishing and the sailing vessel overtaking; Rule 9(e) with the signals in 34(c), agreement and doubt; the other narrow channel rules in Rule 9; and being overtaken: stand on but watch and be ready to act.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What do you do if the overtaking vessel sounds two prolonged and one short, and you're not happy?","pa":"<p>If in doubt, sound <strong>at least five short and rapid blasts<\/strong> (Rule 34(d)). Only agree with <strong>prolonged, short, prolonged, short<\/strong> when it&#x27;s safe.<\/p>","asked":["Vessel overtaking in narrow channel?","Sailing vessel overtaking a PDV action?","Overtaking a RAM vessel?","1 Vessel at STBD Bow","Own vsl overtaking vsl"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",18],["Chris Roe",15],["Khademul Insan",15],["Chris Williams",14],["Muhammad Iqbal",11]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q347","sec":16,"q":"Vessel aground ahead (lights, shapes, fog signal, action); NUC stopped or making way; a trawler shooting or hauling nets.","tier":"red","rep":134,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>A vessel aground (Rule 30(d))<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Lights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Anchor lights<\/strong>: one all-round white if under 50 m; forward and a lower one aft if 50 m or more.<\/li><li>Plus <strong>two all-round red<\/strong> lights in a <strong>vertical line<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Day shapes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Three balls<\/strong> in a vertical line. <strong>Not<\/strong> a black <strong>cylinder<\/strong>, which is <strong>CBD<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fog signal (Rule 35(h))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Three separate, distinct strokes<\/strong> on the <strong>bell<\/strong>, then <strong>rapid ringing<\/strong> for about <strong>5 seconds<\/strong>, then <strong>three strokes<\/strong> again, at intervals of not more than 1 minute.<\/li><li>If <strong>100 m<\/strong> or more, a <strong>gong<\/strong> aft as well, after the bell.<\/li><li>She may also sound a whistle signal, such as the letter U.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My action<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>She isn&#x27;t a vessel I give way to under the steering rules. She&#x27;s a <strong>navigational danger<\/strong>, and she&#x27;s probably <strong>sitting on a shoal<\/strong>, so the real danger is the shoal.<\/li><li><strong>Check the chart<\/strong> and pass on the <strong>deep-water<\/strong> side, <strong>wide<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong> and watch the <strong>echo sounder<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>, and give her a <strong>wide berth<\/strong> for any <strong>salvage<\/strong> operation, tugs and lines.<\/li><li>Report her to VTS or the coastguard if she isn&#x27;t already known.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>NUC (Rule 27(a))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Two all-round red<\/strong> lights in a vertical line.<\/li><li><strong>Sidelights<\/strong> and <strong>sternlight<\/strong> <strong>only<\/strong> when <strong>making way<\/strong> through the water. If she shows <strong>just the two reds<\/strong>, she&#x27;s <strong>stopped<\/strong> (not making way).<\/li><li><strong>By day<\/strong>: <strong>two balls<\/strong> in a vertical line.<\/li><li><strong>Fog signal<\/strong>: <strong>one prolonged and two short<\/strong> blasts, at intervals of not more than 2 minutes, whether making way or not.<\/li><li>I <strong>keep out of her way<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 18<\/strong>).<\/li><li>If she&#x27;s stopped and drifting, plot her drift; if she&#x27;s making way, she may still be unable to manoeuvre properly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A trawler (Rule 26(b))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Green over white<\/strong> all-round lights.<\/li><li>A <strong>masthead light<\/strong> abaft and higher than the green, if <strong>50 m<\/strong> or more (optional under 50 m).<\/li><li><strong>Sidelights<\/strong> and <strong>sternlight<\/strong> when making way.<\/li><li><strong>By day<\/strong>: <strong>two cones<\/strong>, <strong>points together<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Fog signal<\/strong>: one prolonged and two short.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Annex II: additional signals for vessels fishing close together<\/strong> (lights at least 0.9 m apart, lower than the main fishing lights)<\/p><ul><li><strong>Two white<\/strong> in a vertical line: <strong>shooting<\/strong> nets.<\/li><li><strong>White over red<\/strong>: <strong>hauling<\/strong> nets.<\/li><li><strong>Two red<\/strong>: nets <strong>fast<\/strong> on an <strong>obstruction<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Pair trawlers: each may show a searchlight in the direction of the other.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>By flag (International Code of Signals)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Z<\/strong>: shooting nets. <strong>G<\/strong>: hauling nets. <strong>P<\/strong>: nets fast on an obstruction.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My action<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Keep clear<\/strong> of her <strong>and her gear<\/strong>, under Rule 18(a)(iii).<\/li><li>Pass astern of her at a good distance, because the trawl is astern of her.<\/li><li>A trawler hauling or with nets fast can&#x27;t manoeuvre at all.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The aground signals (anchor lights plus two reds, three balls, the bell with three strokes before and after and a gong if 100 m or more) and that she&#x27;s a danger marking a shoal, so pass wide on the deep side; NUC signals with sidelights only when making way and the fog signal; and the trawler&#x27;s lights and shapes with the Annex II shooting, hauling and fast signals and the flags, keeping clear of her and her gear.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"How do you tell whether a NUC is making way or just underway at night?","pa":"<p><strong>Making way:<\/strong> two all-round reds <strong>plus sidelights and sternlight<\/strong>. <strong>Not making way:<\/strong> two all-round reds <strong>only<\/strong> (Rule 27(a)).<\/p>","asked":["Vessel aground, Actions?","Vessel aground right ahead.","Vessel aground: more than 50m.","What are the day shapes for a vessel aground ?","Vessel Aground more than 50 meters."],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",18],["Zia Ul Haq",17],["Examiner not named",14],["Khademul Insan",13],["Sarah Lawlor",7]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q348","sec":0,"q":"Tell me about your last ship.","tier":"red","rep":120,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p>Prepare this word-perfect with your own figures. Examiners build the next 20 minutes from what you say, so mention only what you can explain. Speak for about two minutes and stop.<\/p><p>&quot;My last ship was the [name], a [TEU] TEU container ship, [flag] flag, classed with [class], built [year] at [yard].<\/p><p><strong>Particulars<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>[LOA] m long, [breadth] m beam, summer draught [ ] m, air draught [ ] m.<\/li><li>About [ ] GT, [ ] NT and [ ] t deadweight.<\/li><li>A single fixed-pitch right-handed propeller, a [ ] kW slow-speed two-stroke engine, service speed [ ] knots, and a bow thruster of [ ] kW.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Cargo<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>[ ] bays, [ ] rows on deck, [ ] tiers on deck and [ ] in the holds, with [ ] reefer plugs.<\/li><li>We carried DG under our DG Document of Compliance, with Class 1 restricted to [ ].<\/li><li>Lashing was to the Cargo Securing Manual and the [name] lashing software, linked to the loadicator.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safety equipment<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>LSA: [a free-fall lifeboat with a rescue boat / two davit lifeboats] and liferafts each side.<\/li><li>Fire fighting: fixed CO₂ for the engine room and holds, a local water mist system in the engine room, and a fire main with [ ] pumps.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Bridge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Dual ECDIS as the primary means of navigation, X- and S-band radars, BNWAS, VDR, GMDSS for sea areas A1 to A3.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Trade<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>[Trade route, for example Asia–North Europe via Suez], with [ ] crew.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My job<\/strong><\/p><p>As Chief Officer I was responsible for:<\/p><ul><li>stability and cargo planning with the loadicator, and checking the planners&#x27; stowage;<\/li><li>dangerous goods and lashing;<\/li><li>ballast water management and the BWMS;<\/li><li>the deck PMS: hatch covers, lashing gear, moorings, LSA and FFE;<\/li><li>safety officer duties, permits to work, and the deck crew&#x27;s work and rest;<\/li><li>I kept the 4–8 watch.&quot;<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>Every fact you give is a hook. If you say &quot;CO₂&quot;, expect &quot;how many cylinders, how do you release it?&quot; If you say &quot;free-fall&quot;, expect &quot;how do you test it?&quot; If you say &quot;BWMS&quot;, expect &quot;what type, D-2?&quot; So prepare a one-line answer behind every item, and leave out anything you can&#x27;t defend. If your last ship was Singapore flag, know the differences from UK requirements, because the examiner will ask how it would be different on a UK ship.<\/p>","pq":"What was your summer GM and the limiting stack weight on deck?","pa":"<p>Know <strong>your own figures<\/strong>: summer GM and the typical sailing GM range, and the <strong>maximum stack weight<\/strong> for 20&#x27; and 40&#x27; stacks on deck from your CSM. Say them confidently, but don&#x27;t invent them.<\/p>","asked":["Tell me about your last vessel?","Joining as Chief mate on your last ship?","What is your experience?","What was your last ship?","Q2 ISM – How implemented on last vessel , how is it going to help you as chiefmate?"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",29],["Kaiser Ali",27],["Examiner not named",17],["James Gilbert",17],["Jason Pateman",9]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q350","sec":0,"q":"What is the Planned Maintenance System, and why does it matter?","tier":"red","rep":49,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>ISM Code section 10, Maintenance of the ship and equipment. The company must establish procedures to make sure the ship is maintained in line with the rules and the company&#x27;s own requirements.<\/p><p><strong>What ISM 10 requires<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>10.1: the ship is maintained to the relevant rules and regulations.<\/li><li>10.2: inspections at appropriate intervals; any non-conformity reported with its possible cause; corrective action taken; records kept.<\/li><li>10.3: the company identifies equipment and technical systems whose sudden failure could cause a hazardous situation. These need specific measures to improve reliability, including regular testing of standby arrangements and systems not in continuous use.<\/li><li>10.4: the inspections and measures are integrated into the ship&#x27;s operational maintenance routine.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Critical equipment examples<\/strong><\/p><p>Steering gear, main engine controls, emergency generator, fire pumps and the emergency fire pump, fixed fire-fighting systems, lifeboat release gear, navigation equipment, mooring winches, hatch cover hydraulics.<\/p><p><strong>How the PMS works<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A computer-based schedule of jobs by calendar date or running hours.<\/li><li>Job cards with the procedure, safety precautions and spares needed.<\/li><li>Spares linked to jobs, with minimum stock levels for critical spares.<\/li><li>Records signed off by the person who did it and checked by the responsible officer.<\/li><li>Class can accept an approved PMS for its continuous machinery survey, so surveyors credit jobs done.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My part as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>I run the deck side: LSA, FFE, mooring and anchoring gear, hatch covers, cranes and lifting gear, lashing gear, ballast tanks and the hull. I plan the weekly work from it, check the records, and report overdue items to the Master.<\/p><p><strong>When a job is overdue or there&#x27;s no spare<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Risk-assess the effect of the overdue job.<\/li><li>Inform the Master and the company/DPA.<\/li><li>Get the job formally deferred through the PMS, with interim measures, for example extra inspections or a standby.<\/li><li>If it affects class or a statutory certificate, inform class.<\/li><li>Order spares and close it as soon as possible.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Overdue critical jobs are a classic PSC and ISM audit finding, and can lead to a non-conformity or detention.<\/li><li>A PMS that&#x27;s &quot;green&quot; on paper but not done on deck is worse than none, because it&#x27;s falsified records.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>So the PMS is the ISM 10 system that keeps the ship maintained, tests the critical equipment, and gives the evidence. The examiner wants &quot;ISM 10&quot;, &quot;critical equipment&quot; and what I do when a job can&#x27;t be done.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Give me three critical items on your ship and the test you do on each.","pa":"<p>Three examples:<\/p><ul><li><strong>Emergency generator:<\/strong> weekly start and run, and an on-load test.<\/li><li><strong>Steering gear:<\/strong> test within 12 h before departure, and an <strong>emergency steering drill every 3 months<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Emergency fire pump:<\/strong> weekly run with water on deck.<\/li><\/ul><p>Others include lifeboat engines (weekly) and fire detection tests.<\/p>","asked":["What is pms, importance and why ?","Planned Maintenance System – what is it?","How would you carry out planned maintenance on your windlass?","How will you carry out the PMS of windlass?","How often should safety inspections be carried out."],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",16],["Khairul Anam",7],["Jason Pateman",4],["Examiner not named",4],["Khademul Insan",3]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q349","sec":5,"q":"Master's standing orders and night orders. What do they contain, and how do you know the OOW is keeping a good watch?","tier":"orange","rep":32,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Standing orders<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master&#x27;s permanent instructions<\/strong> for the watch.<\/li><li>Required through the <strong>SMS (ISM 7)<\/strong>, following <strong>STCW Code A-VIII/2<\/strong> on watchkeeping principles, and the company&#x27;s bridge procedures.<\/li><li><strong>Every officer reads and signs them<\/strong> on joining, and when they change.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What they contain<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>When to call the Master<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>restricted visibility, or visibility expected to deteriorate;<\/li><li>traffic causing concern, or a <strong>CPA<\/strong> under the set minimum;<\/li><li>if the ship <strong>can&#x27;t keep to the plan<\/strong> or the ETA;<\/li><li>if the position <strong>can&#x27;t be fixed<\/strong> or the fix is doubtful;<\/li><li><strong>unexpected land<\/strong> or a nav mark not sighted when expected, or an unexpected change of depth;<\/li><li><strong>equipment failure<\/strong>: engine, steering, navigation equipment, alarms;<\/li><li><strong>weather deteriorating<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a <strong>distress<\/strong> or urgency message;<\/li><li>any security concern;<\/li><li><strong>any doubt<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Watch handover<\/strong> procedure.<\/li><li><strong>Lookout<\/strong>, and <strong>never leaving the bridge unmanned<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>BNWAS<\/strong> always on.<\/li><li><strong>Hand or auto steering<\/strong>: when to change, and testing manual steering.<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS and radar<\/strong> settings, and the safety values.<\/li><li><strong>Position-fixing<\/strong> interval by area.<\/li><li><strong>Minimum CPA<\/strong> and <strong>UKC<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>COLREGs<\/strong> compliance, and early, substantial action.<\/li><li><strong>Fatigue<\/strong> and <strong>alcohol<\/strong>: fit for duty.<\/li><li>&quot;<strong>Use the engines without hesitation<\/strong>&quot; if needed.<\/li><li><strong>Reporting<\/strong>: VTS, ship reporting systems, logbook entries.<\/li><li>Use of VHF and mobile phones on the bridge.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Night orders<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Specific to that night<\/strong>: courses and <strong>ETA points<\/strong>, expected <strong>traffic<\/strong> and <strong>weather<\/strong>, events such as reporting points or pilot boarding, and anything <strong>extra<\/strong> about <strong>calling the Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Written by the Master each night at sea, and <strong>each OOW signs<\/strong> them.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How I know the OOW is keeping a good watch<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Unannounced bridge visits<\/strong>, at different times.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>radar set-up<\/strong>: range, ARPA targets acquired, parallel indexes, vectors.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>logs<\/strong>, <strong>fixes<\/strong> and the <strong>ECDIS track<\/strong> and alarms.<\/li><li><strong>VDR<\/strong> or <strong>ECDIS playback<\/strong> after an event or as a check.<\/li><li><strong>Ask the OOW<\/strong> about the situation: &quot;What&#x27;s that target doing? What&#x27;s your plan?&quot;<\/li><li>Check the <strong>BNWAS<\/strong> settings and the <strong>rest-hour<\/strong> records.<\/li><li>Watch how he does the <strong>handover<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Follow up<\/strong> with <strong>coaching<\/strong>, then the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong> if needed. See Q233 on a radar badly set up.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Standing orders are permanent, required by the SMS and STCW, signed by every officer, with the call-the-Master list and the watch rules; night orders are specific and signed each night; and practical ways to check the watch, including unannounced visits and playback.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What would you put in your C/O standing orders for the cargo watch?","pa":"<p>My standing orders for the cargo watch:<\/p><ul><li>follow the agreed plan;<\/li><li><strong>list and stability<\/strong> limits;<\/li><li>DG checks and leaks;<\/li><li>reefer checks;<\/li><li>lashing and twistlock checks;<\/li><li>tending moorings and gangway;<\/li><li>security level;<\/li><li>weather limits for cranes;<\/li><li>stevedore damage reports;<\/li><li>no hot work without a permit;<\/li><li>clear <strong>&quot;call me&quot;<\/strong> criteria.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["What is in standing orders?","What would you be writing in your night orders in port for junior officers?","You are a master now, what u will write in Night order for a coastal passage","What instructions would you give to an OOW during cargo loading?","As Master of a vessel < 3000 GT, how would you prepare Standing Orders for your officers?"],"ex":[["James Steer",3],["Chris Bates",3],["Katy McDonald",3],["Robert Jackson",3],["Examiner not named",3]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q351","sec":10,"q":"What is a permit-to-work system? Which jobs need a permit?","tier":"orange","rep":38,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><p>A <strong>permit to work<\/strong> is a <strong>written, time-limited authorisation<\/strong> for a <strong>hazardous job<\/strong>. It sits on top of the <strong>risk assessment<\/strong>. It lists:<\/p><ul><li>the <strong>hazards<\/strong> and <strong>precautions<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>who<\/strong> does the job and <strong>who supervises<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>isolations<\/strong> (electrical, mechanical, pipelines);<\/li><li>the <strong>gas tests<\/strong> where needed;<\/li><li>the <strong>emergency arrangements<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>time<\/strong> it&#x27;s valid.<\/li><\/ul><p>It&#x27;s required by the <strong>SMS<\/strong> under the <strong>MS&amp;FV (Health and Safety at Work) Regs 1997<\/strong> and <strong>COSWP<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>Jobs that need one<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Enclosed space entry<\/strong> (the Enclosed Spaces Regs 2022 and <strong>MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>Hot work<\/strong> outside the workshop.<\/li><li>Work <strong>aloft<\/strong> or <strong>over the side<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Electrical work<\/strong> and machinery needing <strong>isolation<\/strong> (lock-out, tag-out).<\/li><li>Work on <strong>pressure systems<\/strong> or <strong>pipelines<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo tank<\/strong> entry, and work on cargo systems on tankers.<\/li><li><strong>Diving<\/strong> operations around the ship.<\/li><li>Work on deck in <strong>heavy weather<\/strong>, depending on the SMS.<\/li><li>Work on <strong>fire-fighting systems<\/strong> that takes them out of service, such as the CO₂ system.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How it runs<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>responsible officer<\/strong> issues it. The <strong>Master authorises<\/strong> the <strong>high-risk<\/strong> ones, such as enclosed space entry and hot work.<\/li><li><strong>Checks<\/strong> are done and <strong>signed<\/strong>: isolations, gas tests, equipment, fire watch.<\/li><li>A <strong>toolbox talk<\/strong> at the job, with everyone involved.<\/li><li>A <strong>copy<\/strong> is <strong>displayed<\/strong> at the site.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>valid only<\/strong> for the <strong>stated time<\/strong> and <strong>job<\/strong>. If <strong>conditions change<\/strong>, the job <strong>stops<\/strong> and is <strong>reassessed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At a <strong>shift change<\/strong>, the permit is handed over or re-issued.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>signed off<\/strong> and <strong>closed<\/strong> when the job is <strong>finished<\/strong> and the area is <strong>safe<\/strong>: isolations removed, tools cleared, people accounted for.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>kept<\/strong> as a <strong>record<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Never two permits that conflict<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For example, hot work on deck above a tank being entered, or electrical work on a pump while someone else is starting it.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s why a <strong>single permit controller<\/strong> is used, for example in <strong>dry dock<\/strong>, where the yard and the ship both issue permits. A permit board or log shows all live permits.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What makes permits fail<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Signed without checking, issued for the whole day without retesting, people starting before it&#x27;s issued, or not closed.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A written, time-limited authorisation on top of the risk assessment, the list of jobs, who issues and who authorises, the checks and toolbox talk, displayed at the site, valid only for the job and time, stop if conditions change, signed off and closed, and no conflicting permits with a single controller.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The bosun is up the mast with no harness and no permit. What do you do? (See Q160.)","pa":"<p><strong>Stop the job<\/strong>: bring him down safely and check he&#x27;s all right. Then investigate. A permit and harness are required (<strong>Work at Height Regs 2010<\/strong>, COSWP). Retrain, raise an NC, and take disciplinary action if it was wilful.<\/p>","asked":["Can you explain a permit to work?","Who would you report the accident to?","Who chairs the safety meetings?","PERMIT,TOOLBOX TALK,SAFETY MEETING, GASFREE,GAS","If time permits pack Master's personal belongings with inventory & witnesses"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",5],["Kaiser Ali",4],["James Gilbert",4],["David Carlisle",4],["Inamul Bhuiyan",3]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q352","sec":12,"q":"What is the angle of repose, and why does it matter when loading?","tier":"orange","rep":19,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What it is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>angle of repose<\/strong> is the <strong>maximum slope angle<\/strong>, measured from the <strong>horizontal<\/strong>, at which a <strong>dry, non-cohesive<\/strong> granular cargo <strong>stays stable<\/strong> without <strong>sliding<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>shipper&#x27;s declaration<\/strong> gives it under the <strong>IMSBC Code<\/strong>, and it can be checked with a <strong>tilting-box test<\/strong> (or the ship-side method in the Code).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Low angle<\/strong> (about <strong>30° or less<\/strong>): the cargo <strong>flows like grain<\/strong> and can <strong>shift<\/strong> when the ship <strong>rolls<\/strong>. It must be <strong>trimmed reasonably level<\/strong> to the boundaries of the hold, as for grain.<\/li><li><strong>Higher angles<\/strong>: the IMSBC Code allows some <strong>unevenness<\/strong>, but <strong>limits<\/strong> the difference between <strong>peaks and troughs<\/strong>. Check the cargo&#x27;s <strong>schedule<\/strong> and the Code&#x27;s <strong>trimming section<\/strong> (section 5) for the figures. <strong>Don&#x27;t guess<\/strong> them.<\/li><li>A cargo with a low angle of repose left in a peak can collapse in heavy weather and shift to one side.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Reasons for trimming<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Reduce the risk<\/strong> of the cargo <strong>shifting<\/strong>, which causes a <strong>list<\/strong> or <strong>capsize<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Spread the load<\/strong> on the <strong>tank top<\/strong>, avoiding overload under the peak.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce air pockets<\/strong> in <strong>self-heating<\/strong> cargoes such as <strong>coal<\/strong>, and allow surface ventilation.<\/li><li><strong>Keep the stability<\/strong> as calculated, with the cargo&#x27;s centre of gravity where the calculation assumed.<\/li><li>Reduce the free surface of the cargo surface.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Where it doesn&#x27;t apply<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It <strong>doesn&#x27;t apply<\/strong> to cargoes that <strong>may liquefy<\/strong> (<strong>Group A<\/strong>). Their danger is the <strong>moisture content<\/strong> against the <strong>TML<\/strong>, not the angle of repose. A liquefied cargo behaves like a liquid with a huge free surface, whatever its dry angle of repose.<\/li><li>Cohesive cargoes, which stick together, don&#x27;t have a meaningful angle of repose.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Practical<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When loading, I check that the trimming is done as the Code and the cargo&#x27;s schedule require, with the terminal&#x27;s trimming machines or the pour positions.<\/li><li>I watch for the cargo &quot;peaking&quot; under a fixed loader, and ask for the pours to be moved.<\/li><li>Record the trimming in the log.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The definition (maximum slope from the horizontal for a dry non-cohesive cargo), from the shipper&#x27;s declaration or a tilting-box test, cargoes of 30° or less trimmed level like grain, higher angles with limited peaks per the IMSBC Code, the reasons for trimming, and that it doesn&#x27;t apply to Group A cargoes, whose danger is moisture content against the TML.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why is a Group A cargo's angle of repose not the main concern?","pa":"<p>A Group A cargo&#x27;s danger is <strong>liquefaction from moisture<\/strong>, which can happen whatever the angle of repose. The key check is <strong>moisture content against TML<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["What is an angle of repose ?","What is the angle of repose?","Would you prefer a cargo with a high or low angle of repose and why ?","WHAT IS TRIMMING MOMENT?","Mentioned about Trimming, Bundling, over stowing, using tarpaulins"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",9],["Chris Roe",7],["Nayyer Naqvi",2],["Nozrati",1],["Doug Smith",1]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q353","sec":12,"q":"How do you test hatch covers for weathertightness?","tier":"yellow","rep":11,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Hatch covers are a <strong>condition of assignment<\/strong> under the <strong>Load Line Convention<\/strong>, and are <strong>surveyed by class<\/strong> at the annual load line survey.<\/li><li><strong>Leaking covers<\/strong> <strong>wet the cargo<\/strong>, which is the biggest cause of cargo claims for bulk and general cargo.<\/li><li>On <strong>bulk carriers<\/strong>, they can let in water that <strong>endangers the ship<\/strong>, especially the forward holds in heavy weather.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tests<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Chalk test<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>Chalk<\/strong> the <strong>compression bars<\/strong>, <strong>close<\/strong> and <strong>cleat<\/strong> the covers, then <strong>open<\/strong> them again.<\/li><li>The <strong>chalk line<\/strong> must be <strong>continuous<\/strong> on the <strong>rubber packing<\/strong>.<\/li><li>This checks <strong>contact<\/strong>, <strong>not tightness<\/strong>: the bar may touch but not compress the rubber enough.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Hose test<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>A <strong>water jet<\/strong> at the <strong>joints<\/strong> from <strong>outside<\/strong>, at the <strong>pressure<\/strong> and <strong>distance<\/strong> set by <strong>class<\/strong> (for example 2 bar at the nozzle, 1–1.5 m away; verify).<\/li><li><strong>Someone inside<\/strong> the hold looks for <strong>leaks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>You <strong>can&#x27;t<\/strong> do it with <strong>water-sensitive cargo<\/strong> in the hold, and it&#x27;s hard in cold weather.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Ultrasonic test<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>A <strong>transmitter<\/strong> in the hold and a <strong>detector<\/strong> run round <strong>every joint<\/strong> outside.<\/li><li>Readings above the threshold show a leak path, and it gives a numerical result.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s the <strong>most reliable<\/strong>, can be done <strong>with cargo loaded<\/strong>, and is the method <strong>preferred<\/strong> by <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The operator should be trained and the equipment calibrated.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Light test<\/strong><\/li><\/ol><ul><li>A <strong>dark hold<\/strong>, looking for <strong>daylight<\/strong> round the joints.<\/li><li><strong>Crude<\/strong>, a <strong>last resort<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Maintenance behind it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Rubber packing<\/strong>: look for <strong>permanent set<\/strong> (the compression groove too deep), <strong>cracks<\/strong>, <strong>hardening<\/strong>, missing sections, and paint on the rubber.<\/li><li><strong>Compression bars<\/strong> straight and undamaged, and clean.<\/li><li><strong>Cleats<\/strong> and <strong>quick-acting cleats<\/strong> adjusted, with their <strong>rubber washers<\/strong> in good condition.<\/li><li><strong>Cross-joint wedges<\/strong> and seals between panels.<\/li><li><strong>Drain channels<\/strong> and <strong>non-return valves<\/strong> clear, so water that passes the outer seal drains away.<\/li><li><strong>Hydraulics<\/strong> leak-free, and the panels seating evenly.<\/li><li>Steel-to-steel contact points (bearing pads) not worn, so the packing isn&#x27;t over-compressed.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Record everything<\/strong> in the <strong>PMS<\/strong>, including the test method, date and results, and the repairs.<\/li><li>Keep the test reports for class, the charterer&#x27;s surveyors and P&amp;I.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The reason (load line condition of assignment, cargo damage, ship safety), the four tests with what each checks and their limits (chalk for contact, hose not with cargo in, ultrasonic most reliable and possible with cargo, light as last resort), and the maintenance items: packing, compression bars, cleats, cross-joints, drains, hydraulics, recorded in the PMS.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your hatch cover won't close fully after loading. Why, and what now? (See Q170.)","pa":"<p>See Q170: it may be an obstruction, a misaligned cover, poor seating or damaged gaskets. It&#x27;s not weathertight, so <strong>don&#x27;t sail<\/strong> until it&#x27;s corrected or class agrees a temporary measure.<\/p>","asked":["Testing of hatch cover?","How you carry out test with ultrasonic system?","WX/T testing of hatches. I.e. Hose test, Chalk etc.","How will u load Grain cargo(Here I mentioned everything about Grain, started from Grain Doc,hold prepration,hatch cover water tightness testing,bilge eductor testing ,water ingress alarm test,DWT calculation considering …","CHALK TEST"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",8],["Colum Leggett",1],["Examiner not named",1],["Robert Jackson",1],["Kaiser Ali",1]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q365","sec":12,"q":"How do you prepare the holds and plan for loading (general cargo or bulk)?","tier":"red","rep":101,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Hold preparation<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Cleaning<\/strong> to the standard the cargo needs, for example &quot;<strong>grain clean<\/strong>&quot; or &quot;hospital clean&quot; for some cargoes: <strong>residues removed<\/strong>, a <strong>fresh-water rinse<\/strong> to remove <strong>salt<\/strong>, and holds <strong>dry<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Bilges<\/strong> clean, <strong>bilge suctions<\/strong> and <strong>pumps<\/strong> tested, <strong>non-return valves<\/strong> working, and <strong>wells covered<\/strong> with <strong>burlap<\/strong> where the cargo needs it.<\/li><li><strong>Sounding and air pipes<\/strong>, <strong>ventilators<\/strong>, and <strong>hatch-cover tightness<\/strong> (see Q353): an ultrasonic or hose test.<\/li><li><strong>Fire detection<\/strong> and the <strong>fixed CO₂<\/strong> for the holds tested, and the CO₂ lines clear.<\/li><li><strong>Lighting<\/strong>, <strong>ladders<\/strong> and <strong>structure<\/strong> checked: <strong>frames<\/strong>, <strong>tank top<\/strong>, <strong>damage<\/strong> from the last cargo or the stevedores, loose scale that could contaminate cargo.<\/li><li><strong>Dunnage<\/strong> and <strong>separation material<\/strong> ready.<\/li><li>For some cargoes, a <strong>hold inspection<\/strong> by a <strong>surveyor<\/strong> before loading, for example <strong>grain<\/strong>, or the charterer&#x27;s surveyor.<\/li><li>Any coatings or lime wash if the cargo needs it (for example after sulphur, or before certain bulk cargoes).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The loading plan<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>cargo list<\/strong> with <strong>weights<\/strong>, <strong>ports<\/strong> of discharge, and any <strong>DG<\/strong> or <strong>segregation<\/strong> requirements.<\/li><li><strong>Stability<\/strong>, <strong>shear forces<\/strong> and <strong>bending moments<\/strong> for <strong>every stage<\/strong>, not just the final condition.<\/li><li>The <strong>loading and ballast sequence<\/strong>, agreed with the terminal. For <strong>bulk<\/strong>, this is the <strong>BLU Code<\/strong> ship/shore plan and checklist.<\/li><li>The <strong>draught and trim<\/strong> limits at each port, the <strong>air draught<\/strong>, and the <strong>load line zones<\/strong> on the route.<\/li><li><strong>Securing<\/strong> to the <strong>Cargo Securing Manual<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Port rotation<\/strong>: cargo for the first port accessible, avoiding restows and overstowage.<\/li><li>Cargo compatibility: wet and dry cargo separated, taint, ventilation needs.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Approval and monitoring<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>Master approves<\/strong> the plan.<\/li><li>I <strong>brief<\/strong> the <strong>duty officers<\/strong> with written orders, and monitor <strong>draughts<\/strong> and <strong>soundings<\/strong> against the plan during loading.<\/li><li>Any change to the plan is checked by me before it&#x27;s accepted.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Before sailing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Final stability and stress, draughts read and compared with the calculated ones.<\/li><li>Cargo documents: mate&#x27;s receipts, the cargo manifest, the DG manifest, the stowage plan.<\/li><li>Hatch covers closed and secured, holds ventilated or sealed as the cargo requires.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Hold preparation to the cargo&#x27;s standard (cleaning, rinse, dry, bilges and suctions, pipes and vents, hatch cover tightness, fire detection and CO₂, structure and damage, dunnage, a surveyor&#x27;s inspection where needed); and the loading plan with stability and stress at every stage, the sequence with the BLU Code for bulk, draught and zone limits, securing, approved by the Master and monitored.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The terminal is loading faster than you can deballast. What do you do? (See Q275.)","pa":"<p><strong>Slow or stop loading<\/strong> and tell the terminal. Keep shear forces, bending moments and draught within limits, and agree a revised sequence. Never exceed the limits to keep to the schedule (BLU Code).<\/p>","asked":["Preparation for loading Grain?","Prepare your general cargo vessel for loading?","What is in a Cargo Loading Plan?","How would you prepare for loading?","Preparation for loading coal?"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",11],["Examiner not named",10],["David Carlisle",9],["Shahid Akhter",7],["Muhammad Iqbal",7]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q368","sec":12,"q":"Which certificates and documents does a chemical tanker need?","tier":"orange","rep":38,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The basis<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Besides the usual <strong>SOLAS<\/strong>, <strong>MARPOL<\/strong> and <strong>ISM<\/strong> certificates, a <strong>chemical tanker<\/strong> is built and certified under the <strong>IBC Code<\/strong> (International Code for the Construction and Equipment of Ships Carrying Dangerous Chemicals in Bulk).<\/li><li>It&#x27;s made mandatory by <strong>SOLAS VII<\/strong> (Part B) and <strong>MARPOL Annex II<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Older ships built before 1986 may be under the BCH Code.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Certificates and documents<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>International Certificate of Fitness for the Carriage of Dangerous Chemicals in Bulk<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>It <strong>lists<\/strong> the <strong>products<\/strong> she may carry and in <strong>which tanks<\/strong>, with any conditions.<\/li><li>For an IBC chemical tanker it <strong>also serves<\/strong> for <strong>MARPOL Annex II<\/strong>, in place of a separate <strong>NLS certificate<\/strong> (International Pollution Prevention Certificate for the Carriage of Noxious Liquid Substances in Bulk).<\/li><li>5 years, with annual and intermediate surveys.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Procedures and Arrangements (P&amp;A) Manual<\/strong> (Annex II): approved <strong>discharge<\/strong>, <strong>stripping<\/strong>, <strong>prewash<\/strong> and <strong>tank-cleaning<\/strong> procedures for each product, and the residues allowed.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo Record Book<\/strong> for noxious liquid substances, with <strong>prewash<\/strong> entries <strong>endorsed<\/strong> by the <strong>unloading surveyor<\/strong> where a prewash is mandatory.<\/li><li><strong>SMPEP<\/strong> (Shipboard Marine Pollution Emergency Plan) for noxious liquids, usually <strong>combined<\/strong> with the <strong>SOPEP<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo information<\/strong> (<strong>SDS<\/strong>) for <strong>every product<\/strong>, from the shipper: properties, hazards, emergency procedures.<\/li><li>The <strong>IOPP<\/strong> if she has oil cargo or oil fuel requirements (all ships with oil fuel need one at 400 GT and over).<\/li><li>The <strong>stability booklet<\/strong> with <strong>damage stability<\/strong> for her <strong>ship type<\/strong> (<strong>1, 2 or 3<\/strong>, depending on the hazard of the products).<\/li><li>The <strong>stability instrument<\/strong> approval.<\/li><li>The <strong>cargo tank<\/strong> and <strong>cargo system<\/strong> plans, and the inert gas or nitrogen system documents.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Crew<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>STCW V/1-1<\/strong>: basic and advanced training for oil and chemical tanker cargo operations, endorsed on the certificates, for the officers and crew with cargo duties.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>In port, add<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>ship/shore safety checklist<\/strong> (ISGOTT).<\/li><li>The <strong>tank cleaning<\/strong> and <strong>gas-freeing<\/strong> records.<\/li><li>The <strong>cargo compatibility<\/strong> check (USCG compatibility chart or similar), for adjacent tanks.<\/li><li>Cargo heating records where needed.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The IBC Code under SOLAS VII and MARPOL II, the Certificate of Fitness listing products and tanks and serving as the NLS certificate, the P&amp;A Manual, the Cargo Record Book with surveyor endorsements, the SMPEP, SDS for each product, the IOPP, the damage stability for ship type 1, 2 or 3, STCW V/1-1 training, and the port documents.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"What does a 'Ship Type 2' mean on the Certificate of Fitness?","pa":"<p>An <strong>IBC Code<\/strong> ship type for cargoes with <strong>appreciably severe<\/strong> environmental and safety hazards, needing significant preventive measures. The cargo tanks must be set in from the shell (about <strong>760 mm<\/strong>), and there are specific damage-survival standards. Type 1 is the most severe, type 3 the least.<\/p>","asked":["Document & certificate require for Chemical Tanker?","Type of chemical tanker, Type of chemical cargo?","Content of P & A Manual ?","What are the contents of Certificate of fitness","Content of COF & IBC CODE?"],"ex":[["Zia Ul Haq",19],["Ahsan Khan",7],["Shahid Akhter",6],["Khademul Insan",4],["Colum Leggett",4]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q354","sec":13,"q":"How do you inspect a ballast tank or the forepeak, at sea or in dry dock?","tier":"orange","rep":23,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>It&#x27;s an enclosed space<\/strong>, so the whole procedure applies: the <strong>Enclosed Spaces Regs 2022<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The plan<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong>, approved by the Master.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilate<\/strong>: in dock with <strong>both manholes open<\/strong>, plus <strong>forced ventilation<\/strong>; at sea, ventilate the tank for long enough before entry, and keep it running.<\/li><li><strong>Gas test<\/strong> at <strong>several levels<\/strong>, from outside first:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>O₂<\/strong> about <strong>20.8%<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>flammables<\/strong> under <strong>1% LFL<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>toxic gases<\/strong> (<strong>H₂S<\/strong>, <strong>CO<\/strong>) under their <strong>limits<\/strong>. Ballast tanks can produce H₂S from sediment, and rust uses up O₂.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>A <strong>standby person<\/strong> at the <strong>entrance<\/strong> with communications.<\/li><li>A <strong>rescue team<\/strong> and <strong>equipment<\/strong> ready: BA, tripod, stretcher, resuscitator.<\/li><li><strong>Communications<\/strong> with the bridge or duty officer.<\/li><li><strong>Lighting<\/strong>: intrinsically safe torches and lamps, and a <strong>tank plan<\/strong> for the route, with the manholes marked.<\/li><li>At sea: the tank empty, the ship&#x27;s motion moderate, and the pumps and valves isolated so the tank can&#x27;t be filled.<\/li><li>Personal gas monitors for everyone inside.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What I look at<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Coating<\/strong> condition, rated <strong>good<\/strong>, <strong>fair<\/strong> or <strong>poor<\/strong>, especially <strong>edges<\/strong> and <strong>welds<\/strong>, and on the deckhead and upper parts.<\/li><li><strong>Corrosion<\/strong> and <strong>pitting<\/strong>, and <strong>cracks<\/strong> at <strong>brackets<\/strong>, <strong>stiffener ends<\/strong>, <strong>toe<\/strong> of the brackets, and the <strong>collision bulkhead<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Deformation<\/strong>: buckled frames or plating, from contact or heavy weather.<\/li><li><strong>Anodes<\/strong>: wastage, and whether they need renewal.<\/li><li><strong>Sounding<\/strong> and <strong>air pipes<\/strong>: wastage and the striker plates.<\/li><li>The <strong>bellmouth<\/strong> and <strong>suction<\/strong>: clear, not wasted.<\/li><li><strong>Sediment<\/strong>: depth, and the need to remove it under the BWM plan.<\/li><li>In the <strong>forepeak<\/strong>: the <strong>chain locker drain<\/strong> and the <strong>collision bulkhead&#x27;s integrity<\/strong>, with no openings or leaks.<\/li><li>Ladders and platforms: safe to use.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Photos<\/strong>, the findings in the <strong>PMS<\/strong> and the <strong>tank inspection record<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the <strong>company<\/strong> and <strong>class<\/strong> if there&#x27;s <strong>structural damage<\/strong>.<\/li><li>On <strong>bulk carriers<\/strong> and <strong>tankers<\/strong>, this feeds the <strong>ESP close-up survey<\/strong>, and the coating condition affects the survey intervals.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Finishing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Close the permit<\/strong>, <strong>account for everyone<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Refit<\/strong> and <strong>record<\/strong> the <strong>manhole covers<\/strong>, with new gaskets, checked tight.<\/li><li>Record any sediment removed in the Ballast Water Record Book.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>An enclosed space with the 2022 Regs and MGN 659 Amd 3, RA and permit, ventilation, gas testing at levels with the limits, standby, rescue team, communications and lighting; the inspection items (coating, corrosion, cracks at brackets, deformation, anodes, pipes, bellmouth, sediment, collision bulkhead); records and reporting to class; and closing up properly.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"The gas meter reads O₂ 22% in the tank. Is it safe to enter?","pa":"<p><strong>No, not until it&#x27;s explained.<\/strong> Over 21% is <strong>oxygen enrichment<\/strong>, which raises the fire risk, or it&#x27;s a meter fault. Check the <strong>calibration<\/strong> and find the cause, such as a leaking oxygen cylinder or line. Test for <strong>flammable and toxic gases<\/strong> too. Enter only when every reading is acceptable (O₂ about 21%).<\/p>","asked":["How would you carry out a Ballast tank Inspection?","You are required to inspect the forepeak tank in dry dock, how will you do this?","How will you enter a ballast tank?","What are the Checks you do inside the ballast tank as a chief officer?","After discharge Master tells you to inspect the cargo tanks and ballast tank. Procedure to be followed?"],"ex":[["Ahsan Khan",6],["Shahid Akhter",5],["James Gilbert",3],["Examiner not named",2],["Steve Meaking",2]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q361","sec":13,"q":"What is free surface effect, and how do you reduce it?","tier":"yellow","rep":11,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What free surface effect is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>When a tank is <strong>slack<\/strong> (partly full), the liquid <strong>moves to the low side<\/strong> as the ship <strong>heels<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The tank&#x27;s <strong>centre of gravity<\/strong> moves with it, which reduces the righting lever.<\/li><li>The effect is the <strong>same as G rising<\/strong>: a <strong>virtual loss of GM<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Loss of GM = FSM ÷ Δ<\/strong> (free surface moment in tonne-metres divided by displacement).<\/li><li>For a <strong>rectangular tank<\/strong>: <strong>FSM = ρ × l × b³ ÷ 12<\/strong>, where ρ is the density of the liquid in the tank, l its length and b its breadth.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it depends on<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>breadth cubed<\/strong>: doubling the breadth makes it eight times worse.<\/li><li>The <strong>liquid&#x27;s density<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s displacement: the same tank matters more on a small ship.<\/li><li>It does <strong>not<\/strong> depend on <strong>how much liquid<\/strong> is in the tank, <strong>except<\/strong> when it&#x27;s <strong>nearly empty<\/strong> or <strong>nearly full<\/strong>, when the free surface width reduces.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s not the depth of the tank, and not where the tank is vertically.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How to reduce it<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Keep tanks pressed full or empty<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>One slack tank at a time<\/strong> when ballasting, including during <strong>ballast exchange<\/strong> at sea and bunkering.<\/li><li><strong>Longitudinal subdivision<\/strong>: one centreline division cuts the FSE to a <strong>quarter<\/strong>; two divisions (three equal tanks) to a ninth.<\/li><li>Wash bulkheads reduce the surging but not the static effect.<\/li><li><strong>Always include<\/strong> the <strong>FSM<\/strong> in the <strong>stability calculation<\/strong>. The <strong>loadicator<\/strong> uses the <strong>actual tank levels<\/strong>, and the stability booklet gives the maximum FSM for each tank.<\/li><li>Remember free surface from other sources: water on deck, in holds, fire-fighting water, liquefied cargo, swimming pools.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>To correct a list or loll caused by it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Press up<\/strong> the slack tanks.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t add<\/strong> more slack tanks.<\/li><li><strong>Fill<\/strong> the <strong>low-side double bottoms<\/strong> first, starting with the smallest (see Q285).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Many capsizes involved free surface: water on the car deck of a ro-ro, fire-fighting water on deck, liquefied cargo, or several slack tanks during ballasting.<\/li><li>On a container ship, ballast exchange or heeling tank operations can leave large slack tanks.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Example<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A double bottom tank 20 m long and 15 m wide, half full of sea water: FSM = 1.025 × 20 × 15³ ÷ 12 ≈ 5,766 t·m. On a 40,000 t ship, loss of GM ≈ 0.14 m.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The mechanism and the virtual loss of GM = FSM ÷ Δ, FSM = ρ l b³ ÷ 12 for a rectangular tank, that it depends on breadth cubed and density not the quantity, how to reduce it (full or empty, one slack tank at a time, subdivision to a quarter), always in the calculation, and correcting a list or loll by pressing up and filling low-side tanks first.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why does dividing a tank into two along the centreline reduce the FSE to a quarter, not a half?","pa":"<p>FSE depends on the <strong>cube of the tank&#x27;s breadth<\/strong> (i = lb³/12). Two half-width tanks each have (b/2)³ = <strong>b³/8<\/strong>, and two of them give <strong>2 × 1/8 = 1/4<\/strong>.<\/p>","asked":["How would you correct FSE?","With reference to stability, what are the dangers associated with free surface effect?","Possible free surface movement of water in the water-weight bags.","When ballasting at sea to counteract removal of cargo, due account should be taken of the adverse initial effect of free surface on the vessel's stability.","When stabilizer tanks are in use, the free surface effect should be taken into account in stability calculations. The means of dumping the contents of such tanks in an emergency should be tested."],"ex":[["Shahid Akhter",4],["Examiner not named",2],["Chris Bates",1],["Trevor Hughes",1],["Tim Andrews",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q356","sec":3,"q":"As Master of a ship under 3000 GT, how do you sign on a new 2nd Officer?","tier":"yellow","rep":11,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Check his documents<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>CoC<\/strong> with the right <strong>capacity<\/strong> and <strong>limits<\/strong>: for a ship under 3000 GT, an OOW CoC is enough for the 2/O post, but check any tonnage or area limitations.<\/li><li>For a <strong>foreign CoC<\/strong>, a <strong>UK CEC<\/strong>, or documentary proof he has applied for one, which allows him to serve for up to <strong>3 months<\/strong> (STCW I/10).<\/li><li><strong>STCW certificates<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>Basic Safety Training and its refreshers;<\/li><li><strong>Advanced Fire Fighting<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>PSC&amp;RB<\/strong> (Proficiency in Survival Craft and Rescue Boats);<\/li><li><strong>Medical First Aid<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>GMDSS<\/strong> operator certificate (GOC);<\/li><li><strong>ECDIS<\/strong> generic training, plus type-specific familiarisation on board;<\/li><li><strong>security<\/strong> training for designated duties.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>ENG1<\/strong>, or an accepted foreign medical (MSN 1886 Amd 2 and MSN 1815), <strong>in date<\/strong> for the contract.<\/li><li><strong>Passport, visas<\/strong> and <strong>discharge book<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Sign on<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>His <strong>SEA<\/strong>, signed by <strong>both parties<\/strong>, with a copy for him. He&#x27;s had a chance to read it and take advice.<\/li><li>The <strong>crew list<\/strong> updated.<\/li><li>The <strong>Safe Manning Document<\/strong> still complied with: the right number and grade of officers.<\/li><li><strong>Hours of rest<\/strong> records started.<\/li><li>The <strong>company and agent<\/strong> informed, the <strong>ISPS crew list<\/strong> updated, and immigration formalities through the agent.<\/li><li>The <strong>OLB<\/strong> and the crew agreement updated where required.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Before he takes a watch<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Familiarisation<\/strong> under <strong>STCW I/14<\/strong> and <strong>ISM 6.3<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>safety familiarisation: muster station, emergency duties, alarms, LSA and FFE, escape routes;<\/li><li>bridge familiarisation: radar and ARPA, ECDIS type-specific, GMDSS, steering, BNWAS, the passage plan;<\/li><li>the SMS bridge procedures.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>He <strong>reads and signs<\/strong> my <strong>standing orders<\/strong> and the night order book procedure.<\/li><li>A <strong>first watch with another officer<\/strong>, or with me, so I can see he&#x27;s competent before he&#x27;s alone.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Other points<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If more than <strong>25% of the crew<\/strong> have changed, fire and abandon ship <strong>drills within 24 hours<\/strong> of sailing (SOLAS III/19.3.2), and a security drill within a week (Q307).<\/li><li>Update the <strong>muster list<\/strong> and cabin card.<\/li><li>Check his <strong>rest<\/strong>: has he travelled for 30 hours to join? He shouldn&#x27;t take a watch until he&#x27;s rested.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it matters<\/strong><\/p><p>A new officer who isn&#x27;t familiarised is a classic cause in MAIB reports, for example not knowing how to use the steering modes or the ECDIS.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>CoC and CEC or proof of application, the STCW certificates, ENG1, SEA signed by both, crew list and SMD, familiarisation before duties, standing orders signed, and drills if 25% of the crew changed.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"He produces a foreign medical certificate instead of an ENG1. Acceptable?","pa":"<p><strong>Only if the MCA recognises it.<\/strong> UK ships need an <strong>ENG1<\/strong> or a medical certificate from a country the MCA accepts. Otherwise he needs an ENG1 before he can serve.<\/p>","asked":["Q – master on 3000 gt vsl how do u sign on 2nd officer?","You will be signing on a new 2/O how will you do it ?","2 nd officer subsequently gets sent home, a new 2nd officer joins how do you sign them onto the vessel?","Signing any crew agreement articles","How will you sign on a 2nd mate?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",10],["Ahsan Khan",3],["Muhammad Iqbal",1],["Roddy MacKay",1],["Shahid Akhter",1]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q366","sec":3,"q":"How do you motivate your crew?","tier":"red","rep":55,"src":"Most-asked (482 reports)","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>It&#x27;s part of the <strong>STCW A-II/2<\/strong> competence &quot;use of leadership and managerial skill&quot;, and of <strong>MLC<\/strong> welfare requirements. A motivated crew is a safer crew.<\/p><p><strong>What I do<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>1. Lead by example<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Wear the PPE, follow the permits, respect the rest hours.<\/li><li>Be on deck, not just in the office. Help when it&#x27;s hard work.<\/li><li>Admit my own mistakes.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Communicate<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Daily work plans<\/strong> with the bosun, so everyone knows what&#x27;s happening and why.<\/li><li><strong>Explain why<\/strong>, not just what. People work better when they understand the purpose.<\/li><li><strong>Toolbox talks<\/strong> where the crew&#x27;s ideas are heard.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Involve them<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>safety committee<\/strong>, with reps who are listened to.<\/li><li><strong>Near-miss reporting<\/strong> without blame, with feedback on what changed.<\/li><li>Ask for suggestions to improve the SMS and the way jobs are done.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Recognise and correct fairly<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Recognise good work in public, correct in private.<\/strong><\/li><li><strong>Fair and consistent<\/strong> treatment for everyone, whatever their nationality or rank.<\/li><li>Don&#x27;t have favourites.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Develop people<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Training and <strong>cadet supervision<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Give <strong>responsibility and trust<\/strong>: let a good AB lead a job.<\/li><li>Help ratings work towards their certificates.<\/li><li>Write <strong>honest appraisals<\/strong>, so good people get promoted.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Welfare (MLC)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Plan the work so <strong>rest hours<\/strong> are kept.<\/li><li><strong>Shore leave<\/strong> where possible, even short.<\/li><li><strong>Internet<\/strong>, recreation and good <strong>food<\/strong>. A good cook is worth a lot.<\/li><li>Mark <strong>birthdays<\/strong>, holidays and port calls with a barbecue or a small event.<\/li><li>Watch for <strong>fatigue, stress and bullying<\/strong>, and know how to get help through the company welfare contacts or SeafarerHelp.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Deal with problems early<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Conflict and poor performance: informally first, then the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong> if needed.<\/li><li>A problem left alone affects everyone.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What spoils motivation<\/strong><\/p><p>Unfair work allocation, being shouted at, reports ignored, unpaid overtime, no shore leave, and officers who don&#x27;t do what they demand of others.<\/p><p><strong>The signs it&#x27;s working<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Low incidents and injuries.<\/li><li><strong>Near misses being reported<\/strong>.<\/li><li>People <strong>raising problems openly<\/strong> and challenging unsafe acts.<\/li><li>Good drill performance without being forced.<\/li><li><strong>Crew wanting to come back<\/strong> to the ship.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>STCW leadership competence, lead by example, communicate and involve, fairness, development, welfare under MLC, deal with problems early, and how I&#x27;d know it&#x27;s working. Give a real example from my own ship.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"A cadet tells you the Bosun is bullying him. What do you do? (See Q234.)","pa":"<p><strong>Take it seriously.<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Speak to the cadet in private and record what he says.<\/li><li>Establish the facts, then speak to the Bosun.<\/li><li>Apply the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong> and the company&#x27;s anti-harassment policy.<\/li><li>Protect the cadet: welfare, and change his supervision if needed.<\/li><li>Inform the Master and record it. He&#x27;s probably a <strong>young person<\/strong>, so his welfare matters even more.<\/li><\/ul>","asked":["How would you motivate your crew ?","How would you motivate the crew on board to follow the SMS?","What is your role in implementation of ISM and how you will motivate the crew?","One of the requirements of the ISM Code is for Masters to motivate their crew. How would you go about doing this?","You mentioned Life Saving Equipment, what Life Saving Equipment did you carry?"],"ex":[["Michele Moro",11],["David Carlisle",7],["Zia Ul Haq",4],["Ahsan Khan",4],["Examiner not named",4]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q369","sec":10,"q":"What has changed for enclosed spaces recently?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted 2026","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"MGN 659 Amd 3 / MSC.581(110)","a":"<p><strong>In the UK<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>MS&amp;FV (Entry into Enclosed Spaces) Regulations 2022<\/strong> apply, replacing the 1988 Dangerous Spaces Regs.<\/li><li>The guidance is <strong>MGN 659 (M+F)<\/strong>, now at <strong>Amendment 3<\/strong> (January 2026).<\/li><li>They apply to <strong>all ships<\/strong>, including <strong>fishing vessels<\/strong>, whatever their size.<\/li><li>They require:<\/li><li>a <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> of enclosed spaces;<\/li><li><strong>entry procedures<\/strong> and a <strong>permit<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>atmosphere testing<\/strong> with <strong>calibrated<\/strong> equipment;<\/li><li><strong>enclosed-space drills<\/strong> every <strong>2 months<\/strong>;<\/li><li>a responsible person and an attendant;<\/li><li>rescue and resuscitation equipment ready.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>At IMO<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>revised Recommendations for entering enclosed spaces<\/strong>, <strong>MSC.581(110)<\/strong> (June 2025), replace A.1050(27).<\/li><li>They add that <strong>each ship<\/strong> should keep a <strong>register of its enclosed spaces<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The register <strong>identifies every space<\/strong>, how it&#x27;s <strong>accessed<\/strong>, and its <strong>hazards<\/strong>, and the space should be <strong>marked<\/strong> at the entrance.<\/li><li>They also stress <strong>testing before and during<\/strong> entry, and <strong>rescue planning before entry<\/strong>.<\/li><li>SOLAS XI-1/7 already requires a portable atmosphere testing instrument on board, and SOLAS III/19 the 2-monthly drills.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it means on board<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>register<\/strong> drawn up, and <strong>spaces marked<\/strong> with warning signs.<\/li><li>The <strong>SMS procedure<\/strong> and the <strong>permit<\/strong> form updated to match MGN 659 Amd 3 and MSC.581(110).<\/li><li><strong>Crew trained<\/strong> against the new guidance, including how to read the gas meter and its limits.<\/li><li><strong>Drills<\/strong> run in <strong>different spaces<\/strong>, with <strong>realistic rescues<\/strong>: a dummy, the tripod, the stretcher, BA.<\/li><li><strong>Gas meters<\/strong> calibrated and bump-tested before use, with records.<\/li><li>Make sure <strong>everyone<\/strong>, not only officers, knows which spaces are enclosed: deck stores, chain lockers, void spaces, cargo holds after certain cargoes, and the CO₂ room.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it keeps changing<\/strong><\/p><p>Deaths in enclosed spaces are still among the most common causes of fatalities at sea, often including would-be rescuers.<\/p><p><strong>How to answer<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The UK has the 2022 Enclosed Spaces Regs with MGN 659 now at Amendment 3, and IMO has the revised recommendations MSC.581(110) with a register of enclosed spaces. On board that means a register, marked spaces, an updated procedure and permit, training, and realistic drills every two months.&quot;<\/p><p>(Verify the Amendment 3 date and the register details in MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3 before quoting.)<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The 2022 Regs, MGN 659 Amd 3, all ships, the requirements including 2-monthly drills; MSC.581(110) with the register of enclosed spaces and marking; and what I&#x27;d do on board.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Why should drills be held in different spaces, not always the same one?","pa":"<p>Each space has <strong>different hazards and access<\/strong>: vertical ladders, small manholes, different rescue routes. Varying the space <strong>tests the rescue equipment and the plan<\/strong> properly and avoids complacency (MGN 659 Amd 3; SOLAS III/19 drills every 2 months).<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q370","sec":4,"q":"How is ISM implemented in UK law now, and what changed in 2026?","tier":"new","rep":0,"src":"Predicted 2026","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"ISM Regs 2026","a":"<p><strong>International<\/strong><\/p><p>ISM is mandatory through <strong>SOLAS Chapter IX<\/strong>, applying to passenger ships, and to cargo ships and MODUs of 500 GT and over on international voyages.<\/p><p><strong>UK law now<\/strong><\/p><p>The <strong>Merchant Shipping (International Safety Management (ISM) Code) Regulations 2026<\/strong>, in force <strong>31 March 2026<\/strong>, with guidance in <strong>MGN 708 (M)<\/strong>. They <strong>replace the 2014 Regs<\/strong> and the retained <strong>EC Regulation 336/2006<\/strong>, which had applied ISM to some domestic ships.<\/p><p><strong>Points to know<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>The company DOC<\/strong>: the <strong>MCA<\/strong> audits UK companies itself for the DOC. This <strong>isn&#x27;t delegated<\/strong> to class.<\/li><li><strong>The ship SMC<\/strong>: audits are done by the <strong>MCA<\/strong>, except that under <strong>EASS<\/strong> (MGN 561 (M) Amd 1) an authorised <strong>RO<\/strong> may also do the <strong>SMC<\/strong>, <strong>ISSC<\/strong> and <strong>MLC<\/strong> work for eligible ships. Under <strong>ACSS<\/strong> (MGN 568 Amd 1) the MCA keeps the SMC.<\/li><li><strong>What an SMC audit includes<\/strong>: interviews, records, objective evidence, and usually a general look at the ship and a drill (verify what MGN 708 specifies).<\/li><li><strong>Certificate periods are unchanged<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>DOC 5 years<\/strong>, verified <strong>annually<\/strong> within 3 months of the anniversary;<\/li><li><strong>SMC 5 years<\/strong>, with an <strong>intermediate audit<\/strong> between the <strong>2nd and 3rd anniversary<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>interim DOC 12 months<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>interim SMC 6 months<\/strong>, extendable by <strong>6 months<\/strong> by the flag;<\/li><li>short-term certificates up to <strong>5 months<\/strong> after a successful audit.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>ISM 5.2<\/strong>: the Master&#x27;s <strong>overriding authority<\/strong> is unchanged.<\/li><li><strong>Offences<\/strong>: operating without a valid DOC and SMC, or not complying with the SMS, remain offences for the company and in some cases the Master.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why it matters for me<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I should <strong>quote the 2026 Regs and MGN 708<\/strong>, not the 2014 Regs. Examiners mark down superseded references.<\/li><li>On an EASS ship, my SMC audit may be done by the class auditor, but the standards are the same.<\/li><li>The 2026 Regs are one of three new UK regulations this year: <strong>ISM<\/strong>, <strong>Accident Reporting<\/strong> (SI 2026/430, MGN 564 Amd 2), and <strong>Port State Control<\/strong> (SI 2026/563, MGN 711).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Further detail<\/strong><\/p><p>Check MGN 708 for any detail the examiner may ask, for example on laid-up ships, ships changing flag, or which domestic ships are covered.<\/p><p><strong>History in one line<\/strong><\/p><p>Herald of Free Enterprise and Scandinavian Star, A.741(18) in 1993, SOLAS IX from 1998 and 2002, and now the UK 2026 Regs.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS IX internationally, the 2026 UK Regs in force 31 March 2026 with MGN 708 replacing the 2014 Regs and EC 336/2006, the MCA keeps the DOC, the RO can do the SMC under EASS, the unchanged certificate periods, and ISM 5.2.<\/p><\/div>","pq":"Your ship is in the EASS. Who audits her SMC, and who audits the company's DOC?","pa":"<p>Under <strong>EASS<\/strong> (MGN 561 as amended), an eligible ship&#x27;s <strong>SMC<\/strong> audits can be delegated to the <strong>RO<\/strong>. The company&#x27;s <strong>DOC<\/strong> audit stays with the <strong>MCA<\/strong>. Under the ISM Regs 2026, the MCA keeps company audits in-house.<\/p>","asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q306","sec":0,"q":"Nobody checked your ID at the gangway. What are your concerns?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>Access control is a core measure at every security level under the ISPS Code, Part A section 7 and section 9 (the Ship Security Plan), with guidance in Part B 9.9–9.17. For a UK ship it&#x27;s applied by EC Regulation 725/2004 as retained in UK law, and the Ship and Port Facility (Security) Regulations 2004.<\/p><p><strong>Why it&#x27;s a concern<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It&#x27;s a breach of the Ship Security Plan. At Security Level 1 the SSP requires the ship to check the identity of everyone boarding and confirm their reason.<\/li><li>Anyone could be on board: a stowaway, a thief, someone with a weapon or explosives, a drug smuggler using the ship, or someone planning to cause damage.<\/li><li>If it&#x27;s happening to me, it&#x27;s probably happening to everyone.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s also a likely PSC finding and an ISPS audit non-conformity.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I do at once<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Report it to the Master and the SSO. If I&#x27;m the SSO, I take charge.<\/li><li>Get the gangway watch briefed, or replaced if they&#x27;re not fit for the job, and check they have the visitors&#x27; log, the expected visitors&#x27; list and a radio.<\/li><li>Check the visitors&#x27; log: who&#x27;s on board who hasn&#x27;t left?<\/li><li>Consider how long the gangway has been unmanned. If it&#x27;s been a while, or we&#x27;re in a high-risk port, do a search of the ship to the SSP, especially restricted areas, stores and the engine room, and before sailing a full stowaway search.<\/li><li>Check restricted areas are locked: bridge, engine room, steering gear, CO₂ room.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Recording and follow-up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Record it as a security incident in the security log.<\/li><li>Raise a non-conformity through the SMS and the SSP.<\/li><li>Inform the CSO. If anything suspicious has happened, the PFSO through the agent.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Root cause<\/strong><\/p><p>Look at why it happened: fatigue, not enough people, a watchman given other jobs at the same time, poor training or poor briefing. Fix the cause: watch rotas, a security drill, retraining. Don&#x27;t just shout at the AB.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SSP breach, the risks, tell the Master and SSO, fix the watch, check who&#x27;s on board, search if needed, record it and find the root cause.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q307","sec":0,"q":"More than 25% of the crew changed in this port. Which drills, and when?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. Fire and abandon ship drills: SOLAS III/19.3.2<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every crew member takes part in at least one abandon ship drill and one fire drill every month.<\/li><li>If more than 25% of the crew haven&#x27;t taken part in abandon ship and fire drills on board that particular ship in the previous month, the drills must be held within 24 hours of leaving port.<\/li><li>In the UK, the requirement is in the Merchant Shipping (Musters, Training and Decision Support Systems) Regs 1999, with guidance in MGN 71 (M+F) Amd 1.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Security drill: ISPS Code Part B 13.6<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Security drills are held at least every 3 months.<\/li><li>If more than 25% of the ship&#x27;s personnel have been changed at one time with people who haven&#x27;t taken part in a drill on that ship in the last 3 months, a security drill should be held within one week of the change.<\/li><li>For UK ships Part B is treated as mandatory under EC 725/2004.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Familiarisation before duties: ISM 6.3 and STCW I/14, A-VI/1<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every new joiner gets safety familiarisation before being given any duties: their muster station, alarm signals, how to put on a lifejacket, escape routes, where the LSA and FFE are.<\/li><li>Then ship-specific familiarisation for their job.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Other training<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Enclosed space entry and rescue drill every 2 months under SOLAS III/19.3.6. New joiners should be in the next one.<\/li><li>Onboard training in the use of LSA within 2 weeks of joining, under SOLAS III/19.4.<\/li><li>Steering gear drill every 3 months under SOLAS V/26.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Paperwork<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Update the muster list and the cabin cards with the new names.<\/li><li>Brief the officers on who&#x27;s new and who needs extra attention.<\/li><li>Record the drills in the OLB and the training records.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>I&#x27;d plan it before we sail, so the fire and abandon ship drills happen within 24 hours, the security drill within the week, and every new person is familiarised before they stand any duty. The examiner wants &quot;24 hours&quot;, &quot;SOLAS III/19.3.2&quot;, &quot;one week&quot;, &quot;ISPS B/13.6&quot; and &quot;familiarisation before duties&quot;.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q308","sec":1,"q":"Safety Equipment survey at the next port. How do you prepare, and what is on Form E?","tier":"red","rep":58,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What Form E is<\/strong><\/p><p>Form E is the <strong>Record of Equipment<\/strong> attached to the <strong>Cargo Ship Safety Equipment Certificate<\/strong>, under SOLAS I and the Survey and Certification Regs 2015. It lists the safety equipment the certificate covers, with numbers and types. The surveyor checks the ship against it.<\/p><p><strong>What&#x27;s on Form E<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Ship particulars and the number of persons the LSA is for.<\/li><li><strong>Survival craft:<\/strong> lifeboats, their number, type and capacity, including free-fall; liferafts, their number and capacity, davit-launched or throw-over.<\/li><li><strong>Rescue boats.<\/strong><\/li><li><strong>Lifebuoys<\/strong>, with lights, smoke signals and lines.<\/li><li><strong>Lifejackets<\/strong> and <strong>immersion suits<\/strong>, and thermal protective aids.<\/li><li><strong>Radio LSA:<\/strong> EPIRB, SARTs or AIS-SARTs, portable VHF sets.<\/li><li><strong>Pyrotechnics:<\/strong> rocket parachute flares, line-throwing appliance.<\/li><li><strong>Fire safety systems:<\/strong> fixed fire-fighting systems, fire detection, fire pumps, fireman&#x27;s outfits, EEBDs.<\/li><li><strong>Navigation equipment:<\/strong> radar, ECDIS, gyro, AIS, VDR, BNWAS, echo sounder, speed log and so on.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Preparing for the survey<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Documents<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The last survey report and Form E.<\/li><li>PMS records for LSA and FFE.<\/li><li>Service certificates: liferafts and HRUs, EPIRB shore-based maintenance, fixed fire systems, extinguishers, BA sets and compressor air quality, lifeboat annual and 5-yearly reports (MGN 560 Amd 2).<\/li><li>Drill and training records, the <strong>training manual<\/strong> (SOLAS III/35) and the <strong>fire safety operational booklet<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Fire control plan, muster list and the maintenance instructions for LSA.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On deck<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Walk every item against Form E: correct number, position and condition.<\/li><li>Check every expiry date: pyrotechnics, liferafts, HRUs, EPIRB battery and HRU, SART batteries, medical stores in the lifeboats.<\/li><li>Lifebuoys: lights, self-activating smoke signals, lines, markings.<\/li><li>Lifeboats: engines run, equipment complete, release gear and falls in order, ready for a launch.<\/li><li>Fire: fire pumps and the emergency fire pump tested, hoses and nozzles, fire detection tested, fixed system alarms and pressures, fireman&#x27;s outfits, EEBDs.<\/li><li>Emergency lighting, the emergency generator and the general alarm.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>People<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brief the crew; the surveyor may ask them to demonstrate.<\/li><li>Plan the rest hours so the crew aren&#x27;t exhausted during the survey.<\/li><li>Assign officers to go with the surveyor.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Form E is the Record of Equipment for the SEC; list what&#x27;s on it, including navigation equipment, which people forget; and preparation by documents, a walk-round against Form E, testing, and the crew.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Safety Equipment Survey?","How is the vessel preparation for SEQ survey?","Prepare for an SEC survey?","How would you carry out a Safety Equipment Certificate Renewal Survey?","Cargo Ship Safety Construction (SAFCON)"],"ex":[["Nayyer Naqvi",10],["Zia Ul Haq",10],["Inamul Bhuiyan",6],["Kaiser Ali",6],["Chris Roe",4]],"yrs":"2022–2026"},{"id":"Q309","sec":4,"q":"Define an observation, a non-conformity and a major non-conformity — ISM wording.","tier":"red","rep":19,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Missed the ISM wording — fail, May 2022","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>From ISM Code Part A, section 1.1<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Observation<\/strong> (1.1.8)<\/p><p>&quot;A statement of fact made during a safety management audit and <strong>substantiated by objective evidence<\/strong>.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>It isn&#x27;t a failure. It&#x27;s something the auditor wants recorded, often an area that could become a problem.<\/li><li>Example: the near-miss reports are complete but very few, which may indicate under-reporting.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Non-conformity<\/strong> (1.1.9)<\/p><p>&quot;An observed situation where <strong>objective evidence<\/strong> indicates the <strong>non-fulfilment of a specified requirement<\/strong>.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>The requirement can come from the Code, the SMS, or the rules and regulations.<\/li><li>Example: a permit to work for hot work not signed off, or a drill not held as the SMS requires.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Major non-conformity<\/strong> (1.1.10)<\/p><p>&quot;An <strong>identifiable deviation<\/strong> that poses a <strong>serious threat to the safety of personnel or the ship<\/strong> or a <strong>serious risk to the environment<\/strong> that requires <strong>immediate corrective action<\/strong> or the <strong>lack of effective and systematic implementation<\/strong> of a requirement of this Code.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>Two kinds: a serious threat needing immediate action, or a requirement of the Code not implemented systematically.<\/li><li>Examples: the emergency fire pump unserviceable and not reported; the PMS not implemented at all; the lifeboat release gear known defective with no action; drills not held for months.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Objective evidence<\/strong> (1.1.7)<\/p><p>&quot;Quantitative or qualitative information, records or statements of fact pertaining to safety or to the existence and implementation of a safety management system element, which is based on observation, measurement or test and which can be verified.&quot;<\/p><p><strong>What happens next<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An <strong>observation<\/strong>: no formal action required, but a good company acts on it.<\/li><li>A <strong>non-conformity<\/strong>: root cause, corrective and preventive action, a target date (often within 3 months), verified by the company and closed.<\/li><li>A <strong>major non-conformity<\/strong>: must be <strong>downgraded<\/strong> before the ship sails, by immediate action to remove the serious threat and an agreed plan. Usually followed by an <strong>additional audit<\/strong>. If it isn&#x27;t downgraded, the <strong>SMC isn&#x27;t issued<\/strong> or is <strong>withdrawn<\/strong>, and the ship may be <strong>detained<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>On board<\/strong><\/p><p>Under ISM 9, anyone can raise a non-conformity; the auditor isn&#x27;t the only source.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>This is a word-for-word question. The examiner wants the three definitions exactly, the meaning of objective evidence, and that a major must be downgraded before the ship sails, usually with an additional audit.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What are observations, non-conformities & major non-conformities?","What celestial observation you will get","What are the different types of observation ?","You are chief officer 4-8 watch, on ECDIS, GPS FAIL what will happen, how do you check the vessel position (replied to celestial observation)","Report any defects or non-conformities (NCs)."],"ex":[["Examiner not named",5],["Chris Roe",2],["Tarik Yassin",2],["Muhammad Iqbal",2],["Joe Mattock",1]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q310","sec":10,"q":"When does a hydrostatic release unit expire, and what do you do with the old one?","tier":"yellow","rep":11,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Two types of HRU<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Disposable HRU<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Has a <strong>fixed life<\/strong>, usually <strong>two years<\/strong> from <strong>installation<\/strong>, shown on the unit (the expiry date marked).<\/li><li>It&#x27;s <strong>simply replaced<\/strong>, not serviced.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Serviceable HRU<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Serviced<\/strong> at intervals not exceeding <strong>12 months<\/strong> by an <strong>approved service station<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS III/20.9<\/strong>), with an extension of up to 5 months possible in special cases (verify).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How an HRU works<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It&#x27;s fitted in the liferaft lashing (the gripe).<\/li><li>If the ship sinks, at about <strong>2–4 m<\/strong> depth the <strong>water pressure<\/strong> operates a <strong>membrane<\/strong> and a <strong>knife<\/strong>, which <strong>cuts<\/strong> the lashing.<\/li><li>The liferaft <strong>floats free<\/strong>; the <strong>painter<\/strong>, attached to the ship through a <strong>weak link<\/strong>, pulls tight and <strong>fires the inflation<\/strong> cylinder.<\/li><li>When the raft is inflated, the <strong>weak link breaks<\/strong> and the raft floats clear of the sinking ship.<\/li><li>So the painter must be attached to the <strong>weak link<\/strong> on the HRU, not to the ship&#x27;s structure directly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What to do with the old one<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The examiner liked candidates who said they&#x27;d use the <strong>expired unit<\/strong> to <strong>train the crew<\/strong>: show how the <strong>weak link<\/strong> and the <strong>cutting knife<\/strong> work, and how the painter is connected. Many crew have never seen inside one.<\/li><li>Then mark it clearly as <strong>expired<\/strong> and <strong>dispose<\/strong> of it ashore, so it isn&#x27;t fitted again by mistake.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fitting the new one<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Check the <strong>expiry date<\/strong> on the new unit and the <strong>type<\/strong> matches the raft and the maker&#x27;s instructions.<\/li><li>Fit it so the <strong>painter<\/strong> goes to the <strong>weak link<\/strong>, the <strong>lashing<\/strong> through the release, and the raft can float free.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>manual release<\/strong> (the slip hook) still works for launching by hand.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Record the <strong>replacement<\/strong> in the <strong>PMS<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Update <strong>Form E<\/strong> if needed, and the LSA inventory.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Checks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Monthly: the HRU&#x27;s expiry date, the painter attached correctly, nothing obstructing the raft from floating free.<\/li><li>EPIRBs have their own HRU, with the same principle and expiry.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>A disposable HRU has a fixed life, usually two years, and is replaced; a serviceable one is serviced every 12 months under SOLAS III/20.9; how it works with the knife, weak link and painter; use the old one to train the crew before disposal; and record it in the PMS.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Showed a picture of HRU, how to assemble it in a life raft?","Stevenson screen, EPIRB SART, Hydrostatic release.","Showed picture of hydrostatic release where would I connect painter?","What is HRU and why it is not fitted on Frwd Liferaft ?","Picture of HRU; what is it, how long are they valid for?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",3],["Piers Chubb",3],["Danny Hawkins",2],["Chris Bates",2],["Nozrati",1]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q311","sec":10,"q":"Tell me about the emergency generator — and how is it started by hand?","tier":"red","rep":7,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Emergency generator starting — fail, May 2022","law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>SOLAS II-1/43<\/strong> (emergency source of power on cargo ships) and <strong>II-1/44<\/strong> (starting arrangements for emergency generating sets).<\/p><p><strong>What it does<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On a cargo ship it <strong>starts automatically<\/strong> on <strong>blackout<\/strong> and takes the load within <strong>45 seconds<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It supplies for <strong>18 hours<\/strong> (<strong>36 hours<\/strong> on passenger ships).<\/li><li>It feeds:<\/li><li><strong>emergency lighting<\/strong>, including the muster and embarkation stations;<\/li><li><strong>navigation lights<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>GMDSS<\/strong> radio installations;<\/li><li><strong>internal communications<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>fire detection<\/strong> and the <strong>general alarm<\/strong>;<\/li><li><strong>one steering gear<\/strong> motor, where required;<\/li><li>the <strong>emergency fire pump<\/strong>, if it&#x27;s electric;<\/li><li>watertight doors where fitted.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Where it is<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In its <strong>own space<\/strong>, <strong>above<\/strong> the <strong>uppermost continuous deck<\/strong>, <strong>outside<\/strong> the <strong>machinery casing<\/strong>, accessible from the open deck.<\/li><li>With its <strong>own fuel<\/strong> supply, enough for 18 hours, and its own <strong>emergency switchboard<\/strong> in the same space.<\/li><li>With its own ventilation and cooling that doesn&#x27;t depend on the main machinery.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Starting: SOLAS II-1/44<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Stored energy<\/strong> for at least <strong>three consecutive starts<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>second source<\/strong> of energy for <strong>three more starts<\/strong> within <strong>30 minutes<\/strong>, <strong>unless hand starting<\/strong> is shown to be effective.<\/li><li>The starting systems can be:<\/li><li><strong>electric<\/strong>, with batteries charged from the emergency switchboard;<\/li><li><strong>hydraulic<\/strong>, with an accumulator;<\/li><li><strong>compressed air<\/strong>;<\/li><li>or <strong>hand<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How it&#x27;s started by hand<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>With a <strong>hand crank<\/strong>, a <strong>spring starter<\/strong> (wound by hand, then released to turn the engine), or a <strong>hydraulic accumulator<\/strong> that can be pumped up by hand.<\/li><li>The engine must be able to start in cold conditions (0°C) as required.<\/li><li>The crew must know how: it&#x27;s part of the familiarisation and the blackout drill.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Testing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Tested weekly<\/strong> on the <strong>PMS<\/strong>: started and run.<\/li><li><strong>On load<\/strong> periodically, with a <strong>blackout test<\/strong> of the automatic start and changeover.<\/li><li>Check fuel, coolant, batteries, the starting arrangements and the room&#x27;s ventilation.<\/li><li>Recorded in the engine log.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><p>In a blackout or a fire, this is what keeps the ship&#x27;s safety systems alive. PSC often asks for a demonstration.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>SOLAS II-1/43, automatic start within 45 seconds, 18 hours, what it feeds, where it is; II-1/44, three starts from stored energy and a second source for three more within 30 minutes unless hand starting works; the hand-starting methods; and weekly and on-load tests.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["How do you start the Emergency Generator?","Which all equipments take power from emergency generator?","What causes the emergency generator to activate?","What certificates cover emergency generator?"],"ex":[["Chris Bates",3],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",2],["Examiner not named",1],["Siddhartha Paul",1],["Roddy MacKay",1]],"yrs":"2022–2025"},{"id":"Q312","sec":10,"q":"How does the windlass work, and how is power distributed to it?","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Windlass & power distribution — fail, May 2022","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>How the windlass works<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A <strong>gypsy<\/strong> (cable lifter) with pockets that fit the chain links, driven through a <strong>clutch<\/strong> and <strong>gearing<\/strong> by an <strong>electric<\/strong> or <strong>hydraulic motor<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>band brake<\/strong> on the gypsy, to hold the cable and to control it when letting go.<\/li><li><strong>Warping ends<\/strong> (drums) for handling mooring lines, which can be used with the clutch out.<\/li><li>The <strong>cable<\/strong> runs over the <strong>gypsy<\/strong>, through the <strong>chain stopper<\/strong> (the bow stopper or guillotine, or a devil&#x27;s claw), and down the <strong>spurling pipe<\/strong> into the <strong>chain locker<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>bitter end<\/strong> is secured in the chain locker with a <strong>quick-release<\/strong> arrangement that can be operated from <strong>outside<\/strong> the locker, so the cable can be slipped in an emergency.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How power is distributed to it<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>From the <strong>main switchboard<\/strong>, usually through a <strong>deck machinery distribution board<\/strong>, or to a <strong>hydraulic power pack<\/strong> started from the switchboard or locally.<\/li><li>It <strong>isn&#x27;t on the emergency switchboard<\/strong>. In a blackout, you can let go the anchor with the brake, but you can&#x27;t heave it up.<\/li><li>Some ships have a combined windlass and mooring winch on the same hydraulic system.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Operating<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Deep water<\/strong>: the cable is <strong>walked back under power<\/strong>, with the <strong>clutch in<\/strong>, all the way to near the bottom, so it doesn&#x27;t run away.<\/li><li><strong>Shallow water<\/strong>: the anchor is <strong>walked out<\/strong> clear of the hawse pipe, then the <strong>clutch is taken out<\/strong> and the <strong>brake<\/strong> controls the cable as it runs.<\/li><li>At anchor, the cable is held on the <strong>brake<\/strong> and the <strong>stopper<\/strong>, not on the windlass gears.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Class requirements<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>brake<\/strong> must hold the cable at a specified proportion of its breaking load (around 45% of the chain&#x27;s breaking strength, verify).<\/li><li>The <strong>stopper<\/strong> must hold at a higher proportion (around 80%, verify).<\/li><li>The windlass must <strong>lift<\/strong> the anchor and about <strong>three shackles<\/strong> of cable from about <strong>82.5 m<\/strong> depth at a minimum speed (verify the class figures before quoting).<\/li><li>The windlass isn&#x27;t designed to hold the ship against storm loads; that&#x27;s why the brake and stopper carry the load.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Maintenance<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brake linings, the gypsy wear, the clutch, lubrication, hydraulic oil, and the motor, under the PMS.<\/li><li>Test before arrival.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safety<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Nobody stands in line with the cable, PPE and goggles, communications with the bridge, and the anchor party briefed.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The gypsy, clutch, gearing, motor, band brake, warping ends, stopper, spurling pipe, chain locker and bitter end release; power from the main switchboard via the deck distribution board or a hydraulic pack, not the emergency board; walking back in deep water vs letting go on the brake in shallow water; and the class requirements stated with care.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["You're going into dry dock what will you specifically prepare for an overhaul of the windlass","Machinery knowledge (windlass overhaul and Power distribution requirements)","What maintenance would you do on winches and windlasses?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",2],["Katy McDonald",1]],"yrs":"2020–2022"},{"id":"Q313","sec":5,"q":"How do the steering gear and gyro compass work?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Steering gear & gyro — fail, Bates Nov 2024","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Steering gear: electro-hydraulic<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>bridge<\/strong> sends an <strong>order<\/strong> through the <strong>control system<\/strong>, in <strong>follow-up<\/strong> or <strong>non-follow-up<\/strong> mode.<\/li><li>The order goes to a <strong>variable-delivery pump<\/strong> (such as a Hele-Shaw or swash-plate pump), or to a fixed pump with <strong>solenoid valves<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Hydraulic oil<\/strong> goes to the <strong>actuator<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>a <strong>ram<\/strong> type: two or four rams acting on a tiller on the rudder stock; or<\/li><li>a <strong>rotary vane<\/strong> actuator: vanes on the rudder stock inside a housing.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>The actuator <strong>turns the rudder stock<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>feedback unit<\/strong> (hunting gear or electrical transmitter) <strong>compares the rudder angle with the order<\/strong>, and <strong>stops the pump<\/strong> when they match.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Safety features<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Two pumps<\/strong> and <strong>two control systems<\/strong>, independent, from both sides of the switchboard.<\/li><li><strong>Relief valves<\/strong> to protect against shock loads from the sea.<\/li><li>A <strong>storage tank<\/strong> for refilling, and low-level alarms.<\/li><li><strong>Rudder stops<\/strong> at about 35°.<\/li><li><strong>Alarms<\/strong> on the bridge for power failure, phase failure, overload and low oil level.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>SOLAS II-1/29<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>main<\/strong> steering gear can put the rudder from <strong>35° on one side to 30° on the other in 28 seconds<\/strong>, at the deepest draught and full ahead speed.<\/li><li>The <strong>auxiliary<\/strong> gear from <strong>15° to 15° in 60 seconds<\/strong>, at half speed or 7 knots.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The gyro compass<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>fast-spinning rotor<\/strong> keeps its axis fixed in space: <strong>gyroscopic inertia<\/strong> (rigidity).<\/li><li>It <strong>precesses<\/strong> when a torque is applied: it moves at right angles to the applied force.<\/li><li>Because the earth rotates, a free gyro&#x27;s axis appears to <strong>drift<\/strong> and <strong>tilt<\/strong> relative to the meridian.<\/li><li><strong>Gravity control<\/strong>, by a <strong>pendulous weight<\/strong> or a <strong>liquid ballistic<\/strong> (mercury or oil in pots), makes the earth&#x27;s rotation <strong>tilt<\/strong> the gyro, which then <strong>precesses<\/strong> towards the meridian. That makes it <strong>north-seeking<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Damping<\/strong>, by offset weights or controlled oil flow, stops it <strong>oscillating<\/strong> across the meridian so it <strong>settles<\/strong> on it.<\/li><li>A <strong>follow-up system<\/strong> keeps the sensitive element free of friction, and transmits the heading to the repeaters.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Errors<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Latitude (damping) error<\/strong>: corrected by a latitude setting.<\/li><li><strong>Course, speed and latitude (steaming) error<\/strong>: the ship&#x27;s own north-south motion makes the gyro settle slightly off the meridian. That&#x27;s why it needs <strong>speed and latitude inputs<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Ballistic error<\/strong>: on rapid changes of speed or course, the gyro is temporarily deflected.<\/li><li><strong>Rolling error<\/strong>: reduced by design.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The steering gear chain from order to pump, actuator and feedback, two pumps and systems, the SOLAS 28-second and 60-second tests; and the gyro&#x27;s rigidity and precession, gravity control and damping making it north-seeking and settling, and the errors with the speed and latitude inputs.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["No response from steering gear. Actions?"],"ex":[["Robert Jackson",1],["Zia Ul Haq",1],["Chris Roe",1]],"yrs":"2023–2024"},{"id":"Q314","sec":10,"q":"Pilot ladder photo — what's wrong?","tier":"yellow","rep":14,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"Pilot ladders SOLAS V/23 from 2028","a":"<p><strong>I check the photo against SOLAS V/23, IMO Res A.1045(27) and the IMPA poster.<\/strong> I&#x27;d go through it from the bottom up.<\/p><p><strong>1. Steps<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Equally spaced<\/strong>, <strong>31–35 cm<\/strong> apart.<\/li><li><strong>Horizontal<\/strong>, and resting <strong>firmly against the ship&#x27;s side<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Not painted<\/strong> or slippery, not broken or split, with non-slip surfaces.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Spreaders<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At intervals of <strong>not more than 9 steps<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>5th step from the bottom<\/strong> must be a <strong>spreader<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Spreaders not <strong>lashed<\/strong> between steps.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Side ropes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At least <strong>18 mm<\/strong> diameter, <strong>equally spaced<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>No shackles, knots or splices<\/strong> in them.<\/li><li><strong>Secured to strong points<\/strong>, not to the <strong>rails<\/strong> or bulwark top.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Height<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The pilot should climb <strong>not less than 1.5 m<\/strong> and <strong>not more than 9 m<\/strong>.<\/li><li>If the freeboard is <strong>over 9 m<\/strong>, a <strong>combination arrangement<\/strong> with the <strong>accommodation ladder<\/strong> is used:<\/li><li>the accommodation ladder at <strong>no more than 45°<\/strong>, leading <strong>aft<\/strong>;<\/li><li>its <strong>lower platform horizontal<\/strong> and at least <strong>5 m above the sea<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the pilot ladder extending at least <strong>2 m above<\/strong> the lower platform, secured to the ship&#x27;s side.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. Access to deck<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Handholds<\/strong> <strong>70–80 cm<\/strong> apart at the point of access.<\/li><li>A <strong>gateway<\/strong> in the bulwark, or a bulwark ladder with stanchions.<\/li><li><strong>Man-ropes<\/strong> only if the <strong>pilot asks<\/strong> for them.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Equipment and supervision<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Lit<\/strong> at night, without dazzling the pilot boat.<\/li><li>A <strong>lifebuoy<\/strong> with a <strong>self-igniting light<\/strong>, and a <strong>heaving line<\/strong>, ready.<\/li><li>A <strong>responsible officer<\/strong> in <strong>contact with the bridge<\/strong>, supervising and escorting the pilot.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. Position<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>On the <strong>lee side<\/strong>, within the parallel body, <strong>clear of discharges<\/strong>, away from the propeller and any flare.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Common faults in exam photos<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ladder tied to the rail.<\/li><li>Shackles or knots in the side ropes.<\/li><li>Too few spreaders, or a spreader in the wrong place.<\/li><li>Steps broken or missing, or the ladder hanging away from the side.<\/li><li>Ladder too long, lying in the water, or too short.<\/li><li>No lifebuoy, no officer, no lighting.<\/li><li>Combination arrangement with the accommodation ladder too steep or the pilot ladder not secured.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>New rules<\/strong><\/p><p>New requirements under amended <strong>SOLAS V/23<\/strong> and the new performance standards apply from <strong>1 January 2028<\/strong> (verify detail).<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Me to spot the faults quickly and name the rule: steps 31–35 cm, spreaders every 9 steps with the 5th from the bottom, side ropes 18 mm with no shackles or knots secured to strong points, 1.5 to 9 m, combination over 9 m with 45°, 5 m and 2 m, handholds, lifebuoy, heaving line, lighting and an officer.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["How do you teach your Cadet about Bridge equipment's?","Pilot ladders, Manholes, Sounding pipes, general","Is there any inspection requirements for Pilot ladders?","What is the procedure to Rig Pilot ladder in detail?","Pilot ladder not properly secured on deck."],"ex":[["Chris Bates",2],["Kaiser Ali",2],["Chris Roe",2],["Doug Smith",1],["Rahman (Mamun/Mizan)",1]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q315","sec":10,"q":"A heavy lift with the ship's crane — and there's no test certificate.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Then I don&#x27;t lift.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Why<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Under <strong>LOLER 2006<\/strong> (<strong>MGN 332<\/strong>), lifting equipment must be <strong>thoroughly examined<\/strong>, and <strong>tested<\/strong> where required, with the <strong>certificate<\/strong> entered in the <strong>register of lifting appliances<\/strong>, <strong>before use<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Without a certificate I have no evidence the crane can take the load, and if anything goes wrong, the ship, the Master and I are liable.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Tell the Master<\/strong> and the <strong>company<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Get a <strong>competent person<\/strong>, <strong>class<\/strong> or the <strong>manufacturer<\/strong>, to <strong>examine<\/strong> and <strong>test<\/strong> it and issue the certificate.<\/li><li>Or use a <strong>shore crane<\/strong> or a floating crane.<\/li><li>Record the reason for the delay, and inform the charterer.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If it is certified: planning the heavy lift<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>SWL<\/strong> covers the <strong>load plus the rigging<\/strong>: slings, shackles, spreader beam, and the hook block.<\/li><li>A <strong>lift plan<\/strong>, <strong>risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong>, with a toolbox talk.<\/li><li>The <strong>rigging<\/strong> certified and inspected, with the right slinging method and the centre of gravity known.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Stability during the lift<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>When the load is lifted, its <strong>weight acts at the crane head<\/strong>. <strong>G rises<\/strong> by <strong>w × (height of crane head − original Kg of the load) ÷ Δ<\/strong>.<\/li><li>As the load is swung out, the <strong>heeling moment<\/strong> = <strong>weight × outreach<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So <strong>check<\/strong> the <strong>maximum list<\/strong> with the load at full outreach, against the limits for the crane and the hatch cover or quay clearance.<\/li><li><strong>Remove free surface<\/strong>: tanks pressed up or empty.<\/li><li>Consider <strong>ballasting<\/strong> to counter the list beforehand, or using a heeling tank, but never chase the list during the lift.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>GM<\/strong> is adequate in the worst condition, with the load at the crane head.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Moorings and area<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Slack the moorings<\/strong> a little so the ship can list without breaking lines or overloading bollards, and tend them.<\/li><li><strong>Clear the area<\/strong> under and around the load.<\/li><li>A <strong>banksman<\/strong> with clear signals and radio.<\/li><li>A <strong>slow lift<\/strong>: take the weight, stop, check the slings and the crane, then continue.<\/li><li><strong>Tag lines<\/strong> to control the load.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Securing<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Land the load on the planned position, with dunnage to spread the weight.<\/li><li><strong>Secure<\/strong> it per the <strong>CSM<\/strong>, with lashings calculated for the expected accelerations.<\/li><li>Record the lift and the securing.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No certificate, no lift, because LOLER and MGN 332 require examination and testing before use; get class or the maker to test it, or use a shore crane; then if certified, SWL covering load and rigging, a lift plan, G rising to the crane head and the heeling moment with the list checked, free surface removed, moorings slacked, area cleared, banksman, slow lift, and securing to the CSM.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q316","sec":13,"q":"Read the summer draught from this timber load line photo. Why do timber ships get extra marks and a lower GM?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Reading the timber marks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>timber load line marks<\/strong> are prefixed <strong>L<\/strong> (for lumber): <strong>LS<\/strong> (timber summer), <strong>LW<\/strong> (timber winter), <strong>LT<\/strong> (timber tropical), <strong>LTF<\/strong> (timber tropical fresh), <strong>LF<\/strong> (timber fresh), and <strong>LWNA<\/strong> (timber winter North Atlantic).<\/li><li>They&#x27;re placed <strong>aft of<\/strong> the load line <strong>disc<\/strong>, while the ordinary marks are forward of it.<\/li><li>The <strong>upper edge<\/strong> of each line is the mark.<\/li><li>I&#x27;d read the draught at <strong>LS<\/strong> against the <strong>draught marks<\/strong> on the stem or stern, or work it out from the <strong>freeboard<\/strong>: summer timber freeboard from the deck line, and the moulded depth.<\/li><li>The difference between LS and S is the timber freeboard reduction.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why timber ships get extra marks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>They get a <strong>reduced freeboard<\/strong> because a <strong>properly stowed and secured<\/strong> deck cargo of <strong>timber<\/strong>, of at least <strong>standard superstructure height<\/strong>, covering the <strong>well<\/strong> between superstructures, <strong>adds buoyancy<\/strong> and <strong>protection<\/strong> from the sea, like a superstructure.<\/li><li>The ship needs a <strong>timber load line assignment<\/strong> on her Load Line Certificate, and must load to the <strong>Timber Deck Cargoes Code<\/strong> (TDC Code 2011).<\/li><li>The timber marks only apply when carrying a timber deck cargo stowed to the rules.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why a lower GM is accepted<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>IS Code<\/strong> timber criteria allow <strong>GM ≥ 0.10 m<\/strong> (instead of 0.15 m), with <strong>maximum GZ ≥ 0.25 m<\/strong> and <strong>area<\/strong> to 40° (or flooding) <strong>≥ 0.08 m·rad<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The timber deck cargo can be counted for buoyancy in the GZ curve, which improves the range.<\/li><li>The <strong>allowance<\/strong> for <strong>water absorption<\/strong> must be included in the calculation (the IS Code allowance, about 10–15% depending on the timber; verify), plus icing and consumption.<\/li><li>The <strong>GM is kept lower<\/strong> so she <strong>isn&#x27;t too stiff<\/strong> and <strong>doesn&#x27;t strain<\/strong> the <strong>lashings<\/strong> by <strong>violent rolling<\/strong>. A stiff timber ship can break her lashings and lose the cargo.<\/li><li>But the GM must stay above 0.10 m throughout, and the ship mustn&#x27;t develop a list from water absorption.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Practical points<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Timber ships often sail with a slight list as the cargo absorbs water unevenly, which must be watched.<\/li><li>Lashings retightened regularly.<\/li><li>Jettisoning only to save the ship.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The L-prefixed marks aft of the disc, reading at the upper edge, LS against the draught marks; the reduced freeboard because the timber deck cargo adds buoyancy and protection, with the TDC Code and a timber assignment; and the IS Code timber criteria with the GM of 0.10 m, the water absorption allowance, and the reason the GM is lower: to avoid violent rolling straining the lashings.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Read the Summer Draught for this vessel?"],"ex":[["Alec Keep",2],["Examiner not named",1]],"yrs":"2021–2023"},{"id":"Q317","sec":13,"q":"You've finished loading. How can you check in seconds that the ship is stable?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"No quick max-KG check — fail, Bates Nov 2024","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The quick check<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Look up the <strong>maximum KG<\/strong> (or <strong>minimum GM<\/strong>) curve or table in the <strong>stability booklet<\/strong>, at the current <strong>displacement<\/strong> (and trim, if the table depends on it).<\/li><li>If the <strong>fluid KG<\/strong>, corrected for <strong>free surface<\/strong>, is <strong>below<\/strong> the <strong>maximum allowed<\/strong>, all the <strong>intact criteria<\/strong>, and the <strong>damage criteria<\/strong> where they&#x27;re built in, are <strong>met<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That&#x27;s the one-line check required by <strong>SOLAS II-1/5-1<\/strong>, which requires the stability information to include the limiting KG or GM curves so the Master can check compliance quickly.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Then confirm<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>With the <strong>loadicator<\/strong>: the full GZ curve and all the criteria, and the shear forces and bending moments.<\/li><li><strong>Compare<\/strong> its <strong>draughts<\/strong> with the <strong>observed draughts<\/strong>, fore, aft and midships. If they agree, the inputs are right.<\/li><li>For a container ship, check the lashing software result and the visibility.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why the max KG curve works<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The criteria were all checked at the design stage across the range of displacements; the curve shows the worst KG that still passes all of them.<\/li><li>So one number tells me if the condition is acceptable.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it doesn&#x27;t tell me<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Strength<\/strong>: shear forces and bending moments need the loadicator or the loading manual.<\/li><li>Special criteria: grain heeling moments (the grain loading information), or timber deck cargo criteria if not built in.<\/li><li>Whether the inputs are right, for example misdeclared container weights.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The roll period check at sea<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Time the roll and use T = 2CB ÷ √GM to check the GM is roughly what the loadicator says.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The max KG or min GM curve at the current displacement, fluid KG below it means all intact criteria (and damage where built in) are met, SOLAS II-1/5-1, then confirm with the loadicator and compare its draughts with the actual draughts, and know its limits (strength and special cargoes).<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q318","sec":13,"q":"Ice accretion: what does it do, and may you submerge your load line by ballasting for stability?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>What ice accretion does<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>It <strong>raises G<\/strong>, because the ice builds up <strong>high<\/strong> on the ship: masts, rails, superstructure, deck cargo, container stacks.<\/li><li>It adds <strong>weight<\/strong>, often <strong>unevenly<\/strong>, so <strong>GM falls<\/strong> and the ship may <strong>list<\/strong> to the side facing the wind and spray.<\/li><li>It reduces <strong>freeboard<\/strong> and increases <strong>windage<\/strong>, and blocks <strong>freeing ports<\/strong> and <strong>vents<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It freezes <strong>LSA<\/strong>, <strong>fire-fighting equipment<\/strong>, <strong>aerials<\/strong> and <strong>deck machinery<\/strong>.<\/li><li>In severe cases small ships have <strong>capsized<\/strong> within hours.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Head for <strong>warmer<\/strong> or <strong>sheltered<\/strong> water.<\/li><li><strong>Reduce speed<\/strong>, and alter course to reduce spray: run with the sea if safe.<\/li><li><strong>Remove ice<\/strong> from the <strong>highest structures first<\/strong>, with mallets, scrapers and steam, with the crew safe (lifelines, cold-weather clothing, in pairs).<\/li><li><strong>Recalculate stability<\/strong> with the ice allowance.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> severe icing under <strong>SOLAS V/31<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>May I submerge my load line by ballasting for stability?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Lowering G<\/strong> by <strong>ballasting double bottoms<\/strong> may take her <strong>below her marks<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>Load Line rules don&#x27;t stop<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong> taking action needed for the <strong>safety of the ship<\/strong> in an emergency. The load line is a loading limit for normal conditions, not a reason to capsize.<\/li><li>He uses his <strong>overriding authority<\/strong> (<strong>ISM 5.2<\/strong>, <strong>SOLAS V/34-1<\/strong>).<\/li><li>He <strong>logs the reasons<\/strong> clearly, and <strong>informs<\/strong> the <strong>company<\/strong> and the <strong>flag<\/strong>.<\/li><li>When the danger has passed and the ice is removed, the ballast is discharged (subject to the BWM rules) to bring her back to her marks.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Safety of the ship comes first<\/strong>; the <strong>marks<\/strong> are a <strong>loading limit<\/strong>, not a reason to capsize.<\/p><p><strong>Other considerations<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Ballasting the double bottom is better than other measures because it lowers G without adding free surface if the tanks are pressed full.<\/li><li>Fill tanks one at a time, pressed up, to avoid adding free surface while filling.<\/li><li>In freezing conditions, check the ballast won&#x27;t freeze in the tanks or pipes, and that air pipes aren&#x27;t blocked by ice (which could over-pressurise the tank).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Polar Code<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In polar waters, ships under the Polar Code have ice accretion allowances in their stability calculations and procedures in the PWOM.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Ice raises G, adds weight unevenly, reduces GM and freeboard, may list; head for warmer water, reduce speed, remove ice from the top first; and yes, in an emergency the Master may ballast below the marks to keep the ship safe, under his overriding authority, logging the reasons and informing the company and flag.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q319","sec":16,"q":"You're being overtaken, and a second vessel crosses from your starboard bow. What do you do?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The situation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I&#x27;m <strong>being overtaken<\/strong>: <strong>Rule 13<\/strong> puts the <strong>overtaker<\/strong> under a duty to <strong>keep clear<\/strong>, and <strong>Rule 17<\/strong> says I keep my <strong>course and speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li>But that <strong>doesn&#x27;t make me unable to act<\/strong>, or mean I can ignore the other vessel.<\/li><li>A <strong>second vessel<\/strong> is crossing from my <strong>starboard bow<\/strong>: under <strong>Rule 15<\/strong>, that makes me the <strong>give-way<\/strong> vessel for her. I <strong>must act<\/strong> for her, under <strong>Rule 16<\/strong>: early and substantial action to keep well clear.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The conflict<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>My stand-on duty to the overtaker doesn&#x27;t override my give-way duty to the crossing vessel.<\/li><li>My action for the crossing vessel must also not create a new danger with the overtaker.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Call the Master<\/strong>, put a <strong>man on the wheel<\/strong>, engine ready.<\/li><li><strong>Assess<\/strong> both vessels: radar plots, bearings, where the overtaker is (which side, how close, her speed).<\/li><li><strong>Usually reduce speed<\/strong> to let the crossing vessel <strong>pass ahead<\/strong>. Slowing down:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>keeps clear of the crossing vessel;<\/li><li>and the overtaker should see me slow down (my aspect doesn&#x27;t change), and she still has to keep clear. But she&#x27;ll overtake faster, so I watch her.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Alter to starboard<\/strong> only if the <strong>overtaker<\/strong> is <strong>well clear<\/strong> on that side, because a starboard alteration could turn across her path if she&#x27;s overtaking on my starboard side.<\/li><li><strong>Sound the right signal<\/strong>: <strong>one short<\/strong> blast for an alteration to starboard, <strong>three short<\/strong> for astern propulsion (Rule 34(a)).<\/li><li><strong>Keep watching<\/strong> the <strong>overtaker<\/strong> and the crossing vessel until both are past and clear.<\/li><li>Consider a VHF call to the overtaker to make sure she knows what I&#x27;m doing, without agreeing a non-rule manoeuvre.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What I wouldn&#x27;t do<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Say &quot;I&#x27;m the stand-on vessel&quot; and do nothing.<\/li><li>Alter to port for the crossing vessel, which would take me across her bow and possibly into the overtaker.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Multi-vessel situations test whether I understand that the rules apply to each vessel separately, and that &quot;stand-on&quot; for one doesn&#x27;t remove the duty to give way to another.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The overtaker must keep clear under Rule 13 and I stand on for her under Rule 17, but I&#x27;m the give-way vessel for the crossing vessel on my starboard bow under Rule 15 and must act under Rule 16; usually reduce speed to let her pass ahead, starboard only if the overtaker is clear on that side, the correct signals, call the Master and hand steering, and watch both.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q320","sec":16,"q":"Two set radar plots — work them.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>(a) Leggett, March 2023.<\/strong> Own ship <strong>035° × 10 kn<\/strong>. Target <strong>340° at 8, 7 and 6 nm<\/strong>.\n| | If 6-min intervals (00/06/12) — the likely reading | If 12-min intervals (00/12/24) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Relative speed | 10 kn | 5 kn |\n| CPA | <strong>0<\/strong> (steady bearing) | <strong>0<\/strong> |\n| TCPA from last plot | <strong>36 min<\/strong> | <strong>72 min<\/strong> |\n| Target course / speed | <strong>≈097° / 9.2 kn<\/strong> | <strong>≈065° / 8.2 kn<\/strong> |\n| Aspect | <strong>≈G62<\/strong> | <strong>≈G95<\/strong> |\n| Relative position | 55° on own port bow, crossing port → stbd | same bearing |<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Ask first: \"12 minutes between each observation or in total?\"<\/em> In fog (Rule 19): forward of the beam, <strong>don't alter to port<\/strong>; bold alteration to starboard and/or reduce speed. For the 6-min case the trial manoeuvre gives <strong>CPA ≈2 nm for 030° to starboard (to 065°)<\/strong> and <strong>≈4 nm for 050° (to 085°)<\/strong>; slowing to 5 kn gives ≈3 nm. If own ship stopped, CPA ≈5.3 nm. (All checked by calculation.) In clear weather she'd be crossing from your port side — you'd be stand-on (Rule 17).<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>(b) Roe, April 2023.<\/strong> Heading <strong>000°<\/strong>. Target <strong>040° at 4, 3, 2 nm at 3-minute intervals<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Steady bearing, range closing 1 nm every 3 minutes: relative speed <strong>20 kn<\/strong>, <strong>CPA 0<\/strong>. <strong>TCPA 6 minutes from the last plot<\/strong> — not 12 (the failed candidate counted from the first plot). At 2 nm with 6 minutes left, I <strong>can't avoid close quarters<\/strong>, so <strong>Rule 19(e)<\/strong>: reduce to minimum steerage way, take all way off if necessary, navigate with extreme caution; fog signal, call the Master. Any alteration to <strong>starboard<\/strong>, never port — she's forward of the beam. In sight of each other she'd be crossing from starboard and I'd be give-way under Rules 15 and 16.\"<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q321","sec":16,"q":"Rule 19 situation table — what to avoid and what to do.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span><strong>Rule 19(d)<\/strong> for a target detected by radar alone, with a <strong>close-quarters<\/strong> situation developing or risk of collision. If the action is an alteration of course, avoid as far as possible: <strong>(i)<\/strong> an alteration to <strong>port<\/strong> for a vessel <strong>forward of the beam<\/strong>, other than for a vessel being overtaken; <strong>(ii)<\/strong> an alteration <strong>towards<\/strong> a vessel <strong>abeam or abaft the beam<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>The situation table: target, what to avoid, action<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Fine on the starboard bow or dead ahead, steady bearing<\/strong>: avoid <strong>port<\/strong>. Action: a <strong>bold alteration to starboard<\/strong> and/or <strong>reduce speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Port bow, small CPA<\/strong>: avoid <strong>port<\/strong>. Action: <strong>starboard<\/strong> to open the CPA (making the target pass down my port side), or <strong>reduce speed<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Abeam to starboard, closing<\/strong>: avoid <strong>starboard<\/strong> (towards it). Action: <strong>alter to port<\/strong>, away from it.<\/li><li><strong>Starboard quarter, closing<\/strong>: avoid <strong>starboard<\/strong> (towards it). Action: <strong>alter to port<\/strong>, away.<\/li><li><strong>Port quarter, overtaking me<\/strong>: avoid <strong>port<\/strong> (towards it). Action: <strong>alter to starboard<\/strong>, away.<\/li><li><strong>Starboard bow and starboard quarter together<\/strong>: avoid port for the bow target and starboard for the quarter target. Action: <strong>reduce speed<\/strong> or <strong>stop<\/strong>, since neither alteration is allowed.<\/li><li><strong>Port bow with risk + a vessel on the starboard beam on the same course and speed<\/strong> (examiner Bhuiyan, 2024): avoid <strong>port<\/strong> for the bow target, and a <strong>big starboard<\/strong> turn towards the beam vessel. Action: <strong>reduce speed<\/strong>, because the beam vessel has no relative motion and a speed reduction opens the CPA with the bow target.<\/li><li><strong>The target&#x27;s plot shows O and A on the same spot<\/strong> (no relative motion): the target is on the <strong>same course and speed<\/strong>, so <strong>no risk now<\/strong>; keep watching for any change.<\/li><li><strong>A slower vessel ahead I&#x27;m overtaking<\/strong>: an alteration to <strong>port is allowed<\/strong> (the exception for a vessel being overtaken). Action: pass <strong>wide<\/strong> on the side with <strong>sea room<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Also<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Reducing speed<\/strong> is often the safest action when alterations conflict, and Rule 19(e) requires it for a fog signal heard forward of the beam.<\/li><li>Any alteration must be <strong>large enough<\/strong> to be <strong>readily apparent<\/strong> on the other vessel&#x27;s radar (<strong>Rule 8(b)<\/strong>), typically 30° or more, and checked by continued plotting (<strong>8(d)<\/strong>).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The rules that apply in restricted visibility<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Rules 4–10<\/strong> (Section I, any condition of visibility), with <strong>19(c)<\/strong>: have due regard to the prevailing circumstances and conditions of restricted visibility when complying with Section I. Examiner <strong>Roe failed a candidate<\/strong> for leaving out 19(c).<\/li><li><strong>Rule 19<\/strong> itself.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 35<\/strong> (sound signals).<\/li><li>Plus <strong>Rule 2<\/strong> (responsibility and departure), <strong>Rule 3(l)<\/strong> (definition of restricted visibility), and <strong>Rule 20(c)<\/strong> (lights shown in restricted visibility).<\/li><li><strong>No stand-on vessel in fog<\/strong>: Rules 11 to 18 apply only to vessels in sight of one another.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The Rule 19(d) avoidances, then the right action for each position (starboard for targets forward, away from targets abeam and abaft, reduce speed when they conflict), the special cases (same course and speed, overtaking), the size of alteration under Rule 8, and the rules that apply in restricted visibility including 19(c), with no stand-on vessel.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q322","sec":16,"q":"TCPA is 60 minutes — is there a risk of collision?","tier":"red","rep":35,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"\"No risk because TCPA 60 min\" — fail, Pateman 2022","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Yes, if the CPA is small or the bearing is steady.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Why<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Risk of collision<\/strong> is decided by <strong>Rule 7<\/strong>: proper use of radar, including <strong>long-range scanning<\/strong> to obtain early warning, <strong>radar plotting<\/strong> or equivalent <strong>systematic observation<\/strong>, and <strong>compass bearings<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 7(d)(i)<\/strong>: such risk shall be deemed to exist if the <strong>compass bearing<\/strong> of an approaching vessel doesn&#x27;t <strong>appreciably change<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 7(d)(ii)<\/strong>: risk may sometimes exist even when an appreciable bearing change is evident, particularly when approaching a <strong>very large vessel<\/strong> or a <strong>tow<\/strong>, or at <strong>close range<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Rule 7(a)<\/strong>: every vessel shall use all available means to determine whether risk exists; <strong>if there&#x27;s any doubt<\/strong>, risk shall be deemed to exist.<\/li><li>It&#x27;s decided by the <strong>CPA<\/strong> and the bearing trend, <strong>not<\/strong> by how much <strong>time<\/strong> is left.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What TCPA tells me<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>How <strong>urgent<\/strong> it is, and <strong>how much time<\/strong> I have to take <strong>early, substantial<\/strong> action under <strong>Rules 8 and 16<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Sixty minutes is a gift<\/strong>: at typical speeds the target may be 20–30 nm away. I can act <strong>now<\/strong>, while a <strong>small, clear<\/strong> alteration (still large enough to be readily apparent) still works, and before the situation becomes a close-quarters one.<\/li><li>It also gives me time to plot properly and avoid scanty information.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Plot<\/strong> the target properly: CPA, TCPA, her course and speed, her aspect.<\/li><li><strong>Decide<\/strong> the situation: in sight of one another (Rules 11–18) or restricted visibility (Rule 19).<\/li><li>If I&#x27;m the <strong>give-way<\/strong> vessel, act <strong>early<\/strong>: an alteration of course or speed that results in passing at a safe distance, readily apparent.<\/li><li>If I&#x27;m the <strong>stand-on<\/strong> vessel, keep course and speed, but monitor, and be ready to act under Rule 17.<\/li><li>In restricted visibility, act in ample time under Rule 19(d).<\/li><li><strong>Check<\/strong> the result by continued plotting (Rule 8(d)).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>What&#x27;s a safe CPA?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Set in the Master&#x27;s standing orders: often 1–2 nm in open sea, depending on the traffic and conditions.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Never say there&#x27;s no risk because the TCPA is large.<\/strong><\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Yes, if the CPA is small or the bearing is steady, because Rule 7 decides risk by bearings, plotting and CPA, not by time; 7(d)(i) and (ii) and &quot;if in doubt, risk exists&quot;; TCPA shows urgency and gives time for early action under Rules 8 and 16; and act early while a clear alteration still works.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["One target on stbd bow relative vector find CPA and TCPA?","Radar plot .- find CPA , TCPA, Course and speed?","CPA-0, TCPA- 30 minutes for both vessels in Restricted visibility. Action?","What's the TCPA of target 1?","TCPA, = 48 min"],"ex":[["Colum Leggett",7],["Chris Roe",6],["Kris Askey",4],["Muhammad Iqbal",3],["Nayyer Naqvi",3]],"yrs":"2019–2026"},{"id":"Q323","sec":16,"q":"Give the Rule 3 definitions: NUC, RAM, CBD, underway, restricted visibility.","tier":"red","rep":21,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"NUC definition — fail, Raza 2022","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Rule 3: general definitions<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Not under command (Rule 3(f))<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The term &#x27;vessel not under command&#x27; means a vessel which through some <strong>exceptional circumstance<\/strong> is <strong>unable to manoeuvre<\/strong> as required by these Rules and is therefore <strong>unable to keep out of the way<\/strong> of another vessel.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>Examples: main engine failure, steering failure, a fouled propeller.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Restricted in her ability to manoeuvre (Rule 3(g))<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The term &#x27;vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre&#x27; means a vessel which from the <strong>nature of her work<\/strong> is <strong>restricted<\/strong> in her ability to manoeuvre as required by these Rules and is therefore <strong>unable to keep out of the way<\/strong> of another vessel.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>The rule lists examples, including but not limited to:<\/li><li>laying, servicing or picking up a navigation mark, submarine cable or pipeline;<\/li><li>dredging, surveying or underwater operations;<\/li><li>replenishment or transferring persons, provisions or cargo while under way;<\/li><li>launching or recovering aircraft;<\/li><li>mine clearance operations;<\/li><li>a towing operation which severely restricts the towing vessel and her tow in their ability to deviate from their course.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Constrained by her draught (Rule 3(h))<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The term &#x27;vessel constrained by her draught&#x27; means a <strong>power-driven vessel<\/strong> which, because of her <strong>draught<\/strong> in relation to the <strong>available depth and width<\/strong> of navigable water, is <strong>severely restricted<\/strong> in her ability to <strong>deviate<\/strong> from the course she is following.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>Only a power-driven vessel. Shows the Rule 28 signals: three all-round red lights, or a black cylinder by day.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Underway (Rule 3(i))<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The word &#x27;underway&#x27; means that a vessel is <strong>not at anchor<\/strong>, or <strong>made fast to the shore<\/strong>, or <strong>aground<\/strong>.&quot;<\/p><ul><li>So a ship drifting with engines stopped is under way, but not making way.<\/li><li>&quot;Making way&quot; means moving through the water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Restricted visibility (Rule 3(l))<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The term &#x27;restricted visibility&#x27; means any condition in which visibility is restricted by <strong>fog, mist, falling snow, heavy rainstorms, sandstorms<\/strong> or any other <strong>similar causes<\/strong>.&quot;<\/p><p><strong>Other definitions worth knowing<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Vessel<\/strong> (3(a)): every description of water craft, including non-displacement craft, WIG craft and seaplanes, used or capable of being used as a means of transportation on water.<\/li><li><strong>Power-driven vessel<\/strong> (3(b)): any vessel propelled by machinery.<\/li><li><strong>Sailing vessel<\/strong> (3(c)): any vessel under sail provided that propelling machinery, if fitted, is not being used.<\/li><li><strong>Vessel engaged in fishing<\/strong> (3(d)): fishing with nets, lines, trawls or other apparatus which restrict manoeuvrability, but not trolling lines or other apparatus which don&#x27;t restrict manoeuvrability.<\/li><li><strong>In sight of one another<\/strong> (3(k)): only when one can be observed visually from the other.<\/li><li><strong>Length<\/strong> and <strong>breadth<\/strong> (3(j)): length overall and greatest breadth.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why word for word<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Examiners ask for definitions verbatim because the exact words decide which rule applies. For example, &quot;exceptional circumstance&quot; (NUC) versus &quot;nature of her work&quot; (RAM), and that CBD is only a power-driven vessel.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The five definitions as close to word for word as possible: NUC (exceptional circumstance, unable to manoeuvre, unable to keep out of the way), RAM (nature of her work), CBD (power-driven, draught in relation to depth and width, severely restricted from deviating), underway (not at anchor, made fast to the shore, or aground), and restricted visibility (fog, mist, falling snow, heavy rainstorms, sandstorms or similar).<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What's the definition of a vessel CBD?","What vessels can be ram / what is ram","Standard definition under Rule 3.","You mentioned SEA (seafarer employment agreement). What are the contents, what is CBD?","Discuss lights signalling a vessel aground (>50m), towing RAM, and situations where own vessel is following TSS with a large vessel crossing on STBD bow. Define RAM and clarify if Fishing Vessel (FV) is considered a RAM."],"ex":[["Alec Keep",4],["Inamul Bhuiyan",3],["Examiner not named",3],["Kaiser Ali",2],["Chris Bates",1]],"yrs":"2020–2026"},{"id":"Q324","sec":16,"q":"Day shapes that fail people: aground vs CBD; a black cylinder.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Aground read as CBD / missed cylinder — fails 2023","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Day shapes<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Three balls in a vertical line<\/strong> = <strong>aground<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 30(d)<\/strong>), with the anchor ball if at anchor shapes are also shown. <strong>Not CBD<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>A black cylinder<\/strong> = <strong>CBD<\/strong> (<strong>Rule 28<\/strong>).<\/li><li><strong>One ball<\/strong> = <strong>at anchor<\/strong> (Rule 30(a)).<\/li><li><strong>Two balls<\/strong> in a vertical line = <strong>NUC<\/strong> (Rule 27(a)).<\/li><li><strong>Ball-diamond-ball<\/strong> = <strong>RAM<\/strong> (Rule 27(b)).<\/li><li><strong>A diamond<\/strong> = <strong>towing<\/strong> with the tow <strong>over 200 m<\/strong> (on both the towing vessel and the tow, Rule 24).<\/li><li><strong>Two cones, points together<\/strong> = <strong>fishing<\/strong> (Rule 26). A cone <strong>point up<\/strong> in the direction of gear extending more than 150 m.<\/li><li><strong>A cone point down<\/strong> = <strong>sailing vessel under power<\/strong> (motor-sailing, Rule 25(e)).<\/li><li><strong>Ball-diamond-ball plus two balls and two diamonds<\/strong> = a <strong>dredger<\/strong> or underwater operations with an obstruction (two balls on the obstruction side, two diamonds on the passing side, Rule 27(d)).<\/li><li><strong>Three balls<\/strong> in a triangle (one at the foremast head and one at each yardarm) = <strong>mine clearance<\/strong> (Rule 27(f)).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Read every shape before answering<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>In a photo or card, look at <strong>every<\/strong> shape and its position. The examiner Roe&#x27;s candidate missed the cylinder and treated a <strong>CBD<\/strong> as an <strong>ordinary overtaken ship<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Check whether there are also anchor shapes, which change the meaning.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why aground and CBD are confused<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Both relate to depth: CBD shows <strong>three all-round red lights<\/strong> at night, and aground shows <strong>two all-round red lights<\/strong> plus anchor lights. By day, CBD is a <strong>cylinder<\/strong>, aground is <strong>three balls<\/strong>. Candidates mix up &quot;three reds&quot; with &quot;three balls&quot;.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Aground fog signal (Rule 35(h))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>anchor bell<\/strong> signal (rapid ringing for about <strong>5 seconds<\/strong>) at intervals of not more than one minute, with <strong>three separate and distinct strokes<\/strong> on the bell <strong>immediately before and after<\/strong> the rapid ringing.<\/li><li>If <strong>100 m or more<\/strong>: also the <strong>gong<\/strong> rapidly sounded aft for about 5 seconds, after the bell.<\/li><li>She <strong>may<\/strong> also sound an appropriate whistle signal, such as U (&quot;you are running into danger&quot;).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Action for a vessel aground ahead<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Keep well clear: she&#x27;s on a shoal, so the danger is the shoal, not just the ship.<\/li><li>Check the chart, fix the position, and give a wide berth.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Three balls is aground, a black cylinder is CBD, the other common shapes (one ball anchor, two balls NUC, ball-diamond-ball RAM, diamond tow over 200 m, cones fishing, cone point down motor-sailing), read every shape on the picture, why aground and CBD are confused, and the aground fog signal with the three strokes before and after, plus the gong if 100 m or more.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q325","sec":16,"q":"A dredger with an obstruction on one side; a fishing vessel with gear over 150 m — could she be at anchor?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>A dredger with an obstruction on one side (Rule 27(d))<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Lights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>RAM<\/strong> lights: <strong>red-white-red<\/strong> all-round in a vertical line.<\/li><li>Plus <strong>two all-round red<\/strong> lights in a vertical line on the <strong>side<\/strong> where the <strong>obstruction<\/strong> exists.<\/li><li>Plus <strong>two all-round green<\/strong> lights in a vertical line on the side on which <strong>another vessel may pass<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Masthead lights, sidelights and sternlight when making way through the water; anchor lights are not shown (the RAM and side lights replace them) when at anchor.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Day shapes<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ball-diamond-ball<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Two balls<\/strong> on the <strong>obstruction<\/strong> side.<\/li><li><strong>Two diamonds<\/strong> on the <strong>passing<\/strong> side.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fog signal<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>One prolonged followed by two short blasts at intervals of not more than 2 minutes (Rule 35(c)), the same as other RAM, NUC, fishing and towing vessels.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My action<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Pass on the green/diamond side<\/strong>, at <strong>slow speed<\/strong> to reduce wash, and <strong>keep well clear<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Call her on VHF to confirm the passing side if in doubt.<\/li><li>She&#x27;s RAM, so under Rule 18 I keep out of her way.<\/li><li>Watch for pipelines, anchors and wires from the dredger.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>A fishing vessel with gear over 150 m (Rule 26(c)(ii))<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Lights<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The fishing lights: <strong>red over white<\/strong> all-round for a vessel engaged in fishing other than trawling, or <strong>green over white<\/strong> for a trawler.<\/li><li>When there&#x27;s <strong>outlying gear<\/strong> extending more than <strong>150 m horizontally<\/strong>, an <strong>all-round white light<\/strong> in the <strong>direction of the gear<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>By day<\/strong>: two cones points together, plus a <strong>cone point up<\/strong> in the direction of the gear.<\/li><li>Sidelights and a sternlight <strong>when making way<\/strong> through the water.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Could she be at anchor?<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Yes<\/strong>. Fishing vessels show the <strong>fishing lights<\/strong> whether <strong>making way or not<\/strong>: she may be <strong>at anchor<\/strong>, stopped, or drifting while fishing. A vessel fishing at anchor shows the fishing signals, not anchor lights (Rule 26(b) and (c)).<\/li><li>If <strong>not making way<\/strong>, she shows <strong>no sidelights or sternlight<\/strong>.<\/li><li>So the absence of sidelights tells me she&#x27;s not making way, but she&#x27;s still a vessel engaged in fishing with gear out.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>My action<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Keep clear<\/strong> of her <strong>and her gear<\/strong>: Rule 18(a)(iii) for a power-driven vessel.<\/li><li>Pass on the side <strong>away<\/strong> from the white light (the gear side).<\/li><li>Reduce speed, give her a wide berth, and watch for other boats and floats.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The dredger&#x27;s RAM lights plus two reds on the obstruction side and two greens on the passing side, by day two balls and two diamonds, pass on the green/diamond side slowly and well clear; the fishing vessel&#x27;s extra all-round white light (or cone point up) in the direction of gear over 150 m, that she shows fishing lights whether making way or not so she could be at anchor, no sidelights when not making way, and keep clear of her and her gear away from the white light.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q326","sec":9,"q":"Fire: do you wait for the Master's permission to send the fire team in?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Waited for Master before sending fire team — fail, May 2022","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>No.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>My role<\/strong><\/p><p>As <strong>on-scene commander<\/strong>, I&#x27;m responsible for the <strong>fire-fighting operation at the scene<\/strong>. The <strong>Master<\/strong> has <strong>overall command<\/strong> on the <strong>bridge<\/strong>: the ship, navigation, communications and the big decisions such as <strong>CO₂ release<\/strong> and <strong>abandoning ship<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>What I do before sending the team in<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Assess<\/strong> the fire: location, size, smoke, what&#x27;s burning, any casualties or missing people.<\/li><li><strong>Brief the team<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>the <strong>route<\/strong> in and the <strong>escape route<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>hazards<\/strong>: electrics, fuel, DG, heat, collapse;<\/li><li>the <strong>extinguishing medium<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>exits<\/strong> and the <strong>signals<\/strong> to withdraw.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Check<\/strong> their <strong>BA pressures<\/strong>, <strong>communications<\/strong> (radios working), <strong>lifelines<\/strong>, and that their PPE is correctly donned, with a <strong>buddy check<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Set up the <strong>entry control board<\/strong>: names, pressures, time in, expected time out.<\/li><li>Set up <strong>boundary cooling<\/strong> and a <strong>back-up team<\/strong> in BA ready to go in.<\/li><li>Make sure the space is <strong>isolated<\/strong>: ventilation stopped, fuel shut off, power isolated where possible.<\/li><li>When I&#x27;m <strong>satisfied it&#x27;s safe<\/strong>, I <strong>send them in<\/strong> and <strong>report to the Master<\/strong> that I&#x27;ve done so.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Why not wait for permission<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Delaying a safe attack<\/strong> to wait for permission <strong>lets the fire grow<\/strong>. The first few minutes are critical.<\/li><li>The Master <strong>delegates<\/strong> the on-scene decisions to me through the <strong>muster list<\/strong> and the SMS. He trusts me to manage the scene.<\/li><li>I keep him <strong>informed continuously<\/strong>: what we&#x27;re doing, what we see, what we need.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When I would refer to the Master first<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If the decision affects the <strong>whole ship<\/strong>: CO₂ release, flooding a space, abandoning ship.<\/li><li>If the team would be at <strong>unacceptable risk<\/strong>, and the alternative is to withdraw and seal.<\/li><li>If the fire involves DG and the EmS gives specific warnings.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>When I&#x27;d withdraw the team<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Conditions worsen: heat, a flashover risk, explosions.<\/li><li>BA pressure reaches the return limit.<\/li><li>A team member is injured or communications fail.<\/li><li>Then report to the Master and recommend the next step.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The principle<\/strong><\/p><p>That&#x27;s <strong>management<\/strong>: <strong>decide, act, report<\/strong>. The STCW A-II/2 competence includes organising fire-fighting and leadership. A C/O who waits for permission for every step isn&#x27;t managing.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>No, as on-scene commander I brief, check BA, comms and lifelines, set up entry control, boundary cooling and a back-up team, send them in when safe and report to the Master; the Master keeps overall command and decides CO₂ and abandonment; and decide, act, report.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q327","sec":5,"q":"You're preparing the voyage plan as C/O / Master. What do examiners say candidates miss?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Voyage plan missed watches & drills — fail, Bates Nov 2024","law":null,"a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The passage plan is <strong>APEM<\/strong> under <strong>SOLAS V/34<\/strong> and <strong>A.893(21)<\/strong>. Examiners say candidates describe the 2/O&#x27;s chart work and stop there. At C/O and Master level, the <strong>voyage plan<\/strong> includes the <strong>people and the ship<\/strong>, not just the track.<\/p><p><strong>What examiners say candidates miss<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Watch schedules<\/strong> and <strong>bridge manning levels<\/strong> for each phase of the voyage: open sea, coastal, pilotage, TSS, restricted visibility, HRA.<\/li><li>Their effect on <strong>hours of rest<\/strong> under <strong>MSN 1877 (M) Amd 2<\/strong>, especially around port calls and if extra watches are needed.<\/li><li><strong>Drills and training<\/strong> on passage: when and where, so they&#x27;re realistic and don&#x27;t break rest.<\/li><li><strong>Maintenance<\/strong> and work needing <strong>good weather<\/strong>: painting, working aloft, hatch cover work, planned around the weather and routeing.<\/li><li><strong>Cargo care<\/strong>: reefers and their monitoring, DG checks, lashing inspections after heavy weather, ventilation.<\/li><li><strong>Ballast water exchange<\/strong> areas, or the BWMS operation and record keeping.<\/li><li><strong>ECA fuel changeover<\/strong> points, times and records, agreed with the Chief Engineer.<\/li><li><strong>Load line zones<\/strong> and seasonal dates, and the effect of consumption on the draught.<\/li><li><strong>Security<\/strong> and <strong>HRA<\/strong> measures: security levels, UKMTO reports, ship protection measures, citadel drills.<\/li><li><strong>Stores, fresh water and bunkers<\/strong>: enough for the voyage with a margin, and where to take them.<\/li><li><strong>Garbage<\/strong> and <strong>sewage<\/strong> discharge restrictions, and port reception facilities.<\/li><li><strong>Contingency plans<\/strong>: diversions, ports of refuge, medical evacuation, engine failure, weather routeing options.<\/li><li><strong>Reporting<\/strong>: ship reporting systems, VTS, pre-arrival notifications through the agent, CERS for UK ports.<\/li><li><strong>Communications<\/strong>: the GMDSS sea areas, MSI services, and company reporting.<\/li><li><strong>Air draught and UKC<\/strong> at the critical points for the expected draughts.<\/li><li><strong>Crew changes<\/strong> and certificate expiry dates during the voyage.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The Master approves it<\/strong><\/p><p>The Master approves the whole voyage plan, not just the chart track, and briefs the officers.<\/p><p><strong>How I&#x27;d present it in the exam<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;The 2/O prepares the passage plan to SOLAS V/34 and A.893(21). As C/O, I add the ship&#x27;s condition and cargo requirements, and the Master approves it. But the voyage plan is wider: it covers the people (watch schedules and rest hours), the ship (maintenance, drills, cargo care, ballast, fuel changeover), compliance (load lines, MARPOL, security) and contingencies.&quot;<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>To hear the human and operational parts: manning levels and rest hours, drills, maintenance, cargo care, ballast, ECA changeover, load line zones, security, stores and bunkers, and contingencies, with the Master&#x27;s approval.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q328","sec":13,"q":"Whose safety system applies in dry dock — the ship's or the yard's? An unplanned job comes up.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Agree it before arrival<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>At the <strong>pre-docking meeting<\/strong>, the ship, the yard and the company agree whose safety system applies to what, in writing.<\/li><li><strong>Usually<\/strong>:<\/li><li>the <strong>yard&#x27;s permit system<\/strong> applies to <strong>yard work<\/strong>;<\/li><li>the <strong>ship&#x27;s<\/strong> system applies to <strong>ship&#x27;s work<\/strong> by the crew;<\/li><li>where they <strong>differ<\/strong>, we <strong>adopt the stricter<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The yard&#x27;s rules apply to everyone on the yard&#x27;s premises (the dock floor, the quay, cranes).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How it works day to day<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Daily coordination meetings<\/strong> with the yard safety officer and the yard&#x27;s project manager: the day&#x27;s jobs, hot work, tank openings, isolations, overside work, heavy lifts, and any changes.<\/li><li>A <strong>single hot-work<\/strong> and <strong>enclosed-space permit controller<\/strong>, so two permits don&#x27;t conflict (hot work above an open tank, or electrical work on a system someone is testing).<\/li><li><strong>Fire watches<\/strong> for hot work, and night fire patrols.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s <strong>fire main pressurised<\/strong> from shore.<\/li><li><strong>Isolations<\/strong> with lock-out and tag-out, recorded on a board both sides can see.<\/li><li><strong>Gas-free certificates<\/strong> from the yard&#x27;s chemist for their work, and our own tests for ship&#x27;s crew entering.<\/li><li>A <strong>head count<\/strong> system for everyone on board.<\/li><li>The <strong>emergency procedure<\/strong>: who calls the yard&#x27;s fire brigade, where everyone musters.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>An unplanned job comes up<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It&#x27;s <strong>stopped<\/strong> until it has its <strong>own risk assessment<\/strong> and <strong>permit<\/strong>, and <strong>both sides<\/strong> have <strong>agreed<\/strong> it.<\/li><li><strong>No &quot;just quickly do it&quot;<\/strong>: many dry dock accidents come from unplanned jobs, done in a hurry, without the right isolation or gas test.<\/li><li>Check whether it affects other work, the ship&#x27;s stability, or the docking plan (for example removing weights or opening tanks).<\/li><li>Record it and add it to the next coordination meeting.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Responsibility<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The Master remains responsible for the ship and her crew. The C/O usually coordinates safety with the yard.<\/li><li>The yard is responsible for its own workers and subcontractors, under the local law and the contract.<\/li><li>The ship&#x27;s crew still follow the SMS, and the UK Health and Safety at Work Regs 1997 continue to apply to a UK ship&#x27;s crew.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Agree it at the pre-docking meeting, yard system for yard work and ship&#x27;s for ship&#x27;s work with the stricter where they differ, daily coordination, a single permit controller, fire watches and the fire main, isolations and gas-free certificates, and for an unplanned job, stop it until it has its own RA and permit agreed by both sides.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q329","sec":13,"q":"As she refloats, the ship takes a list. Why, and what now?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>First: stop flooding the dock.<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Likely causes<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>bottom plug<\/strong> not refitted, or <strong>leaking<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A <strong>sea valve<\/strong> or <strong>overboard valve<\/strong> open, or a pipe opened for repair and not closed.<\/li><li>A <strong>tank<\/strong> whose <strong>contents changed<\/strong> during the dock period: drained, filled, or transferred, without being recorded.<\/li><li><strong>Unrecorded weight shifts<\/strong>: equipment moved, removed or added on one side, scrap, stores, the anchor cable ranged on the dock floor and brought back aboard.<\/li><li><strong>Negative GM<\/strong> before she&#x27;s fully afloat, from weights added high up or tanks emptied low down, so she lolls as the blocks take less of her weight.<\/li><li>A <strong>new opening<\/strong> in the hull from the repair work, not closed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Sound every tank<\/strong> and <strong>compare<\/strong> with the <strong>entry condition<\/strong> recorded at docking.<\/li><li><strong>Check for ingress<\/strong>: in the <strong>engine room<\/strong>, at the <strong>plugs<\/strong>, the sea chests, the stern tube, and any repaired areas.<\/li><li><strong>Recalculate stability<\/strong>: the actual weights and their positions, and the GM.<\/li><li><strong>Correct<\/strong> it:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li>if it&#x27;s a <strong>leak<\/strong>, the dock may need to be <strong>lowered<\/strong> so she sits back on the blocks, and the plug or valve fixed;<\/li><li>if it&#x27;s <strong>weight<\/strong>, correct it by ballast or moving the weight;<\/li><li>if it&#x27;s <strong>loll<\/strong>, lower G by filling double bottoms, low side first, never transferring to the high side.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li><strong>Only then<\/strong> continue flooding, slowly, with people watching for leaks at each stage.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Prevention<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The <strong>plug list<\/strong> checked and signed before flooding, with each plug refitted in an officer&#x27;s presence.<\/li><li>A <strong>weight record<\/strong> during the dock period: everything added, removed or moved.<\/li><li>Tank soundings at docking and before undocking.<\/li><li>Stability calculated for the undocking condition.<\/li><li>Valves and openings checked against a list before flooding.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why the examiner asks<\/strong><\/p><p>A list on refloating is a classic exam scenario because it tests understanding of the critical period in reverse, the bottom plug procedure, and the difference between list and loll.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stop flooding; the causes (plugs, valves, tank changes, weight shifts, negative GM, new openings); sound every tank against the entry condition, check for ingress, recalculate, correct it, and only then continue; and the prevention through the plug list, weight record and soundings.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q330","sec":3,"q":"You find white powder in a cabin / the OOW on his phone / a crew member smoking in his cabin.","tier":"green","rep":2,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>1. White powder in a cabin<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Don&#x27;t touch it, smell it or taste it.<\/strong> It could be drugs, but also something toxic.<\/li><li><strong>Secure the cabin<\/strong> and, if appropriate, the <strong>person<\/strong>, without using unnecessary force. Don&#x27;t let anyone remove anything.<\/li><li><strong>Inform the Master<\/strong> at once.<\/li><li><strong>Photograph<\/strong> it where it is, with a witness, and record the time and who was present.<\/li><li>Keep a <strong>chain of custody<\/strong>: if it has to be moved, bag and seal it with gloves, label it, sign it and lock it in the ship&#x27;s safe, with a witness.<\/li><li>Treat it as a possible <strong>drugs offence<\/strong>: inform the <strong>company<\/strong>, the <strong>flag<\/strong>, and the <strong>next port&#x27;s police or customs<\/strong> through the agent. Carrying drugs into a port can lead to the ship being detained and the Master arrested, so it&#x27;s urgent.<\/li><li>Check whether it&#x27;s a wider problem: <strong>search<\/strong> under the SSP if the company procedure requires.<\/li><li>Drug-related misconduct is <strong>gross misconduct<\/strong> under the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong>. <strong>D&amp;A test<\/strong> per company policy.<\/li><li>Record it in the <strong>OLB<\/strong> and the security log.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. The OOW on his phone<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Take the con<\/strong> if needed, check the ship is safe: lookout, traffic, position.<\/li><li>Then deal with it: the <strong>company policy on personal devices<\/strong> on the bridge, and the guidance in COSWP and the MCA on distraction from mobile phones (check the current MGN).<\/li><li>Distraction on watch is a breach of STCW A-VIII/2 and COLREG Rule 5.<\/li><li>Use the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong> ladder: informal or formal warning first, more if repeated or if it caused danger.<\/li><li>Look at the cause: is there a family problem? Is there a fair internet policy so people can contact home off watch?<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. A crew member smoking in his cabin<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Fire risk<\/strong>: stop it, and check the cabin for any smouldering materials.<\/li><li>Warnings under the <strong>Code of Conduct<\/strong> and the company&#x27;s <strong>smoking policy<\/strong>.<\/li><li>A repeat, or a fire caused by it, is <strong>gross misconduct<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Remind everyone of the designated smoking areas, and check the cabin smoke detectors haven&#x27;t been covered or disabled, which is very common and very dangerous.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>For all three<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Record<\/strong> in the <strong>OLB<\/strong> when a formal warning is given, with the seafarer&#x27;s reply and a copy to him.<\/li><li><strong>Report<\/strong> to the company.<\/li><li>Be <strong>fair and consistent<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>For drugs, don&#x27;t touch, secure, photograph, chain of custody, inform company, flag and port authorities; for the phone, take the con then the Code of Conduct; for smoking, the fire risk and the Code of Conduct; and OLB records for formal warnings.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["You find out one of the AB's is smoking in his cabin, what are your actions going to be?"],"ex":[["David Carlisle",2]],"yrs":"2019–2025"},{"id":"Q331","sec":3,"q":"Is a seafarer's SEA still valid if he's held by pirates?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"MLC piracy amendment","a":"<p><strong>Yes.<\/strong> The SEA continues.<\/p><p><strong>Reference:<\/strong> the <strong>2018 amendments to the MLC<\/strong>, in force <strong>26 December 2020<\/strong>, added <strong>Standard A2.1.7<\/strong> and changed Standard A2.2.7.<\/p><p><strong>What they say<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Where a seafarer is <strong>held captive on or off the ship<\/strong> as a result of <strong>acts of piracy or armed robbery against ships<\/strong>, the <strong>SEA continues to have effect<\/strong>.<\/li><li>That applies <strong>even if<\/strong> the agreed contract period has <strong>expired<\/strong>, or either party has given <strong>notice<\/strong> to suspend or terminate it.<\/li><li><strong>Wages and other entitlements<\/strong> under the SEA, the CBA and national law <strong>continue to be paid<\/strong> during the whole period of captivity, and until the seafarer is released and duly repatriated, or the date of death if he dies in captivity.<\/li><li>That includes the <strong>allotments<\/strong> to his family.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Definitions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Piracy<\/strong> has the meaning in <strong>UNCLOS 1982<\/strong>, Article 101: illegal acts of violence or detention committed for private ends on the high seas.<\/li><li><strong>Armed robbery against ships<\/strong> is the same kind of act in a State&#x27;s internal waters, archipelagic waters or territorial sea, as defined in the IMO <strong>Code of Practice<\/strong> for investigating these crimes (A.1025(26)).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it was introduced<\/strong><\/p><p>Before 2018, some seafarers held for months or years by Somali pirates had their contracts expire and their wages stop. Their families lost their income while they were held hostage.<\/p><p><strong>Related protections<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Repatriation<\/strong> at the owner&#x27;s cost after release (A2.5.1).<\/li><li><strong>Medical care<\/strong> and <strong>compensation<\/strong> under MLC Regulation 4.2 and the financial security for shipowners&#x27; liability.<\/li><li><strong>Abandonment<\/strong> financial security if the owner abandons the crew.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>What it means for the company and Master<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The company keeps paying wages and allotments, and keeps in contact with the families.<\/li><li>The company&#x27;s crisis plan for piracy should cover this, along with family support.<\/li><li>The <strong>ship security plan<\/strong> and <strong>BMP Maritime Security<\/strong> guidance reduce the risk in the first place.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Yes, the SEA continues; the 2018 MLC amendments, Standard A2.1.7; wages and entitlements continue until release and repatriation, even if the contract expires. Mentioning the Somali piracy background and UNCLOS Article 101 shows real understanding.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q332","sec":12,"q":"Ship–shore cargo figures don't agree (a shortfall). What do you do?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Recheck my side first<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Repeat<\/strong> the <strong>draught survey<\/strong> with the <strong>surveyor<\/strong>:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li><strong>draughts<\/strong> on <strong>both sides<\/strong>, fore, aft and midships, in calm conditions;<\/li><li>the <strong>dock water density<\/strong> at several depths;<\/li><li><strong>tank soundings<\/strong>: ballast, fresh water and fuel, with the trim and list corrections;<\/li><li>the <strong>constant<\/strong>: compared with previous surveys.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li>Check the calculations: hydrostatics, trim correction, hog and sag correction (the &quot;mean of means&quot;), the density correction.<\/li><li>On a tanker, the <strong>ship&#x27;s figures<\/strong> from ullages, temperatures, and the <strong>VEF<\/strong> (vessel experience factor) compared with the shore tank figures.<\/li><li>Check whether the shore figure includes anything not on board, such as cargo in the lines.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>If the difference remains<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Issue a <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> stating <strong>my figures<\/strong> and that the ship doesn&#x27;t accept the shore figure.<\/li><li><strong>Sign<\/strong> the <strong>mate&#x27;s receipt<\/strong> or the <strong>B/L<\/strong> only with the <strong>correct ship&#x27;s figure<\/strong>, or with a <strong>clause<\/strong>: &quot;weight as declared by shipper, ship&#x27;s figure X tonnes&quot;, or &quot;said to contain&quot;.<\/li><li><strong>Inform<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong>, the <strong>owners<\/strong>, the <strong>charterers<\/strong> and the <strong>P&amp;I club<\/strong>. The club may appoint an independent <strong>surveyor<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Record everything<\/strong>: the survey sheets, the times, the conditions, the people present.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Never sign for a figure I can&#x27;t support.<\/strong> If I sign a mate&#x27;s receipt or B/L for more cargo than the ship has on board, the ship will face a <strong>shortage claim<\/strong> at discharge that it can&#x27;t defend.<\/p><p><strong>At discharge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Do the same: a draught survey before and after, with the receivers&#x27; surveyor present.<\/li><li>Compare with the loading figures.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Common causes of differences<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Wrong dock density, wrong constant, unread tanks, hog and sag not corrected, the shore scale error, or cargo still in the shore lines.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Recheck the ship&#x27;s figures with a careful draught survey (draughts, density, soundings, constant), then a letter of protest, sign only for the ship&#x27;s figure or with a clause, inform the Master, owners, charterers and P&amp;I, record everything, and never sign for a figure I can&#x27;t support.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q333","sec":6,"q":"Moro's casting-off question — single RH fixed-pitch screw, no thruster or tug, port side alongside heading north; leave heading south.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The situation<\/strong><\/p><p>Single <strong>right-handed fixed-pitch<\/strong> screw, <strong>no thruster or tug<\/strong>, <strong>port side alongside<\/strong>, heading <strong>north<\/strong>. I need to leave heading <strong>south<\/strong>. I&#x27;m assuming <strong>no wind or tide<\/strong>; if there is any, I&#x27;d use it or take a tug.<\/p><p><strong>Preparation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Brief the <strong>mooring parties<\/strong>: the order of letting go, communications, snap-back zones.<\/li><li><strong>Fenders<\/strong> ready forward, because the bow will bear on the quay.<\/li><li>Check the <strong>wind and tide<\/strong> first, and the traffic in the channel.<\/li><li>Engines and steering tested.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The manoeuvre: springing the stern off<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Single up<\/strong> to the <strong>springs<\/strong>. Keep the <strong>forward back spring<\/strong> (from the bow leading aft to the quay).<\/li><li><strong>Let go aft<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Hold on the forward back spring<\/strong>, put the <strong>rudder hard to port<\/strong> (towards the quay), and give <strong>dead slow ahead<\/strong>.<\/li><li>The <strong>spring stops headway<\/strong>, and the <strong>rudder<\/strong> and propeller wash throw the <strong>stern out to starboard<\/strong>, away from the quay. The bow is held against the quay on the fenders.<\/li><li>With the <strong>stern 20–30° off<\/strong>, <strong>stop<\/strong> the engine, <strong>rudder amidships<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Let go forward<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Go <strong>slow astern<\/strong> to <strong>back out<\/strong> into the channel. The <strong>RH screw kicks the stern to port<\/strong> when going astern, towards the quay, so I need <strong>enough angle first<\/strong> before going astern, or the stern will come back in.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Once clear in the channel<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Turn short round to starboard<\/strong>, the way a <strong>RH screw turns best<\/strong>:<\/li><li>full rudder to starboard with a short burst ahead to kick the stern to port;<\/li><li>then astern, with transverse thrust pulling the stern to port and the bow swinging to starboard;<\/li><li>repeat until heading south.<\/li><li>Then proceed <strong>south<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Alternatives<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>If there&#x27;s an <strong>offshore wind<\/strong>, single up and let her blow off, then back out.<\/li><li>If there&#x27;s a <strong>current from ahead<\/strong> (from the north), I can use it to lift the stern off with the spring.<\/li><li>If the channel is too narrow to turn, back out further or take a <strong>tug<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Why it works<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The spring and the rudder give a turning moment about the bow, using the engine ahead without gaining headway.<\/li><li>Understanding transverse thrust astern tells me why the stern must be well off before backing.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Spring off the stern with the forward back spring, rudder towards the quay and dead slow ahead; enough angle before going astern because the RH screw kicks the stern to port; then turn short round to starboard; and the honest statement that with any wind or tide I&#x27;d use it or take a tug.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q334","sec":12,"q":"Rusty steel coils, and the shipper wants a clean bill of lading.","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Immediate actions<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Stop loading<\/strong> those coils.<\/li><li><strong>Tell<\/strong> the <strong>Master<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Photograph<\/strong> them and <strong>describe<\/strong> the condition: rust type (light surface rust, heavy rust, pitting), wet, damaged edges, broken bands, the coil numbers.<\/li><li>Call the <strong>P&amp;I surveyor<\/strong> to inspect and record the condition. Many clubs have a pre-loading survey arrangement for steel cargoes.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Clause the mate&#x27;s receipt accurately<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>For example: &quot;<strong>coils rusty<\/strong>&quot;, &quot;<strong>wet before shipment<\/strong>&quot;, &quot;<strong>edges damaged<\/strong>&quot;, &quot;<strong>packing torn<\/strong>&quot;, with the coil numbers.<\/li><li>The clause must be accurate and specific, not vague.<\/li><li>The B/L must carry the same clause.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The clean bill of lading<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>I won&#x27;t sign<\/strong>, or let the <strong>Master sign<\/strong>, a <strong>clean B/L<\/strong> for <strong>damaged cargo<\/strong>, even against a <strong>letter of indemnity (LOI)<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It <strong>misrepresents<\/strong> the cargo to the <strong>buyer<\/strong> and the <strong>bank<\/strong> (under a letter of credit, which often requires a clean B/L).<\/li><li>The <strong>P&amp;I cover<\/strong> may <strong>not respond<\/strong>: clubs exclude claims arising from the knowing issue of a clean B/L for damaged cargo.<\/li><li>The LOI may be <strong>unenforceable<\/strong> as it&#x27;s based on a fraud on third parties.<\/li><li>The ship then faces a <strong>cargo claim<\/strong> at discharge for damage it didn&#x27;t cause.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The shipper&#x27;s pressure<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>The shipper wants a clean B/L because the bank won&#x27;t pay against a claused one.<\/li><li>The solution is for the shipper to replace the coils, or agree with the buyer, not for the ship to sign a false document.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Then<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>A <strong>letter of protest<\/strong> to the shipper and the terminal.<\/li><li><strong>Refer<\/strong> to the <strong>owners<\/strong> and <strong>P&amp;I<\/strong>, who will advise and deal with the shipper and charterer.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> everything in the log, with the photos and the surveyor&#x27;s report.<\/li><li>Under the Hague-Visby Rules, the carrier must issue a B/L showing the apparent order and condition; that&#x27;s the ship&#x27;s legal duty, not an option.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Stop loading, tell the Master, photograph and describe, P&amp;I surveyor; clause the mate&#x27;s receipt accurately; never a clean B/L for damaged cargo even against an LOI, because it misrepresents to the buyer and bank and the P&amp;I cover may not respond; letter of protest; and refer to owners and P&amp;I.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q335","sec":12,"q":"Ship's sweat vs cargo sweat — when do you ventilate?","tier":"green","rep":3,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Ship&#x27;s sweat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Warm, moist hold air<\/strong> condenses on <strong>cold steel<\/strong>: the ship&#x27;s sides, deckhead and hatch covers.<\/li><li>Usually when going from a <strong>warm<\/strong> to a <strong>cold<\/strong> climate: the sea and outside air cool the steel, while the hold air is still warm and moist from loading.<\/li><li>It drips onto the cargo and wets it.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilate<\/strong> if the <strong>outside air&#x27;s dew point<\/strong> is <strong>below<\/strong> the <strong>hold air&#x27;s dew point<\/strong>: the drier outside air replaces the moist hold air and lowers its dew point.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Cargo sweat<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Warm, moist outside air<\/strong> condenses on <strong>cold cargo<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Usually when going from a <strong>cold<\/strong> to a <strong>warm<\/strong> climate: the cargo is still cold, and warm moist air brought in by ventilation condenses on it.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t ventilate<\/strong> (with outside air whose dew point is above the cargo temperature), because that&#x27;s exactly what causes it.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The rule of thumb<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;<strong>Hot to cold, ventilate bold; cold to hot, ventilate not.<\/strong>&quot;<\/p><p><strong>The real test: comparing dew points<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>Take <strong>wet and dry bulb<\/strong> readings of the <strong>outside air<\/strong> and the <strong>hold air<\/strong> (from the ventilator outlets or hold sensors).<\/li><li>Work out the <strong>dew points<\/strong> from the tables.<\/li><li><strong>Ventilate<\/strong> if the <strong>outside dew point<\/strong> is <strong>lower<\/strong> than the <strong>hold dew point<\/strong>, so you&#x27;re bringing in drier air.<\/li><li><strong>Don&#x27;t ventilate<\/strong> if the outside dew point is <strong>higher<\/strong> than the <strong>cargo temperature<\/strong>, because the air will condense on the cargo.<\/li><li>Also consider the cargo&#x27;s own moisture: hygroscopic cargoes like grain, rice, cocoa, coffee and timber give off and absorb moisture.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Record the readings and decisions in the <strong>ventilation log<\/strong>, at least every 4 hours (or as the cargo or charterer requires).<\/li><li>This log is the ship&#x27;s main defence against a sweat damage claim: it shows the ship ventilated correctly.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Other conditions<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Don&#x27;t ventilate in heavy rain or spray, which brings water into the hold.<\/li><li>Some cargoes need no ventilation (for example coal after the first period if self-heating) or specific ventilation.<\/li><li>Container ships: the cargo is in boxes, so the shipper&#x27;s packing (desiccants, ventilated containers) matters most, but the hold ventilation still affects reefers and the hold environment.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Ship&#x27;s sweat (warm moist hold air on cold steel, warm to cold, ventilate) and cargo sweat (warm moist outside air on cold cargo, cold to warm, don&#x27;t ventilate), the rule of thumb, but the real test of comparing dew points from wet and dry bulb readings, and the ventilation log as evidence.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What is Cargo Sweat?"],"ex":[["Chris Roe",2],["Khademul Insan",1]],"yrs":"2022–2023"},{"id":"Q336","sec":12,"q":"On a tanker: how does the inert gas system protect the ship? Draw the flammability diagram.","tier":"red","rep":46,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>How the inert gas system protects the ship<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>It supplies gas with <strong>O₂ ≤5%<\/strong>, so the <strong>cargo tanks<\/strong> stay at <strong>O₂ ≤8%<\/strong> and <strong>positive pressure<\/strong> (<strong>SOLAS II-2/4.5.5<\/strong>, <strong>FSS Code Ch 15<\/strong>).<\/li><li>With so little oxygen, the tank atmosphere <strong>can&#x27;t burn<\/strong>, whatever the hydrocarbon content.<\/li><li>Positive pressure keeps <strong>air out<\/strong> during discharge, ballasting and at sea.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The safety devices<\/strong><\/p><ol><li>The <strong>deck water seal<\/strong> and the <strong>non-return valve<\/strong> stop <strong>hydrocarbon gas<\/strong> going <strong>back<\/strong> to the <strong>machinery spaces<\/strong> through the IG line.<\/li><li>The <strong>P/V breaker<\/strong> protects the tanks from <strong>over- and under-pressure<\/strong>, as a back-up to the P/V valves.<\/li><li><strong>Alarms<\/strong> for <strong>high O₂<\/strong>, <strong>low pressure<\/strong>, <strong>high temperature<\/strong>, <strong>scrubber water<\/strong> supply, and <strong>deck seal<\/strong> water level.<\/li><li>The <strong>scrubber<\/strong> cools the gas and removes soot and sulphur.<\/li><li>Isolating valves and the O₂ analyser with continuous recording.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>The flammability diagram<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Axes: <strong>hydrocarbon %<\/strong> by volume (vertical) against <strong>O₂ %<\/strong> by volume (horizontal), from 0 to 21% O₂.<\/li><li>The <strong>flammable zone<\/strong> is a triangle running roughly between <strong>1%<\/strong> (the <strong>LFL<\/strong>) and <strong>10%<\/strong> (the <strong>UFL<\/strong>) hydrocarbon at 21% O₂, <strong>shrinking<\/strong> as the O₂ falls, and disappearing at about <strong>11%<\/strong> O₂.<\/li><li>Below about 11% O₂, <strong>no mixture<\/strong> of hydrocarbon and inert gas is flammable.<\/li><li>The <strong>air dilution line<\/strong> runs from a point on the hydrocarbon axis to 21% O₂ with no hydrocarbon: it shows what happens when you add air.<\/li><li>The <strong>critical dilution line<\/strong> is the line from 21% O₂ that just touches the flammable zone: a mixture below it can be diluted with air without becoming flammable.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Gas-freeing<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Purge<\/strong> with IG first, reducing the <strong>hydrocarbon<\/strong> content until the tank atmosphere is <strong>below the critical dilution line<\/strong>, typically hydrocarbon below <strong>2%<\/strong> by volume.<\/li><li><strong>Then ventilate with air<\/strong>, so the atmosphere moves along a line that <strong>never passes through the flammable zone<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Continue until O₂ is 21% and hydrocarbons are below 1% LFL, and toxic gases are below their limits, before entry.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Loading and discharge<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Before loading: tanks inerted (O₂ below 8%).<\/li><li>During discharge: IG replaces the cargo.<\/li><li>Crude oil washing only with the tank inerted.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The IG supply ≤5% and tanks ≤8% at positive pressure, SOLAS II-2/4.5.5 and FSS Ch 15, the deck water seal, non-return valve and P/V breaker, the alarms; and the diagram with hydrocarbon against O₂, the flammable zone between about 1% and 10% hydrocarbon shrinking to about 11% O₂, the critical dilution line, and purging below 2% before ventilating with air so the tank never becomes flammable.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["Draw Flammability diagram and explain?","What are the alarms in IG system","What is the Inert Gas System for?","Preparation for tank cleaning procedure on oil tanker / what is flammability diagram?","IG system, Deck water seal. Purpose & what all to check on it?"],"ex":[["Muhammad Iqbal",5],["Khademul Insan",5],["Chris Bates",4],["Siddhartha Paul",4],["Colum Leggett",4]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q337","sec":12,"q":"Ballast water: what does the convention cover — and what if you can't exchange?","tier":"green","rep":1,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"BWM D-2 for all ships","a":"<p class=\"ref\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reference<\/span>The <strong>BWM Convention 2004<\/strong>, in force <strong>8 September 2017<\/strong>. In the UK, the <strong>MS (Control and Management of Ships&#x27; Ballast Water and Sediments) Regs 2022<\/strong> (SI 2022/737), with <strong>MSN 1908 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong> and <strong>MGN 675 (M+F) Amd 2<\/strong>.<\/p><p><strong>What the convention covers<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Ballast water<\/strong> and <strong>sediments<\/strong>: both.<\/li><li><strong>Sediments<\/strong> must be <strong>removed<\/strong> and <strong>disposed of<\/strong> <strong>ashore<\/strong> at a reception facility, or as the <strong>BWM plan<\/strong> says (at sea only in permitted areas), and <strong>recorded<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Ships need a <strong>BWM plan<\/strong>, a <strong>Ballast Water Record Book<\/strong>, and an <strong>International BWM Certificate<\/strong> (400 GT and over).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>D-1: ballast water exchange standard<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>95% volumetric exchange<\/strong>.<\/li><li>At least <strong>200 nm<\/strong> from the nearest land in at least <strong>200 m<\/strong> depth; if that&#x27;s not possible, at least <strong>50 nm<\/strong> from land in at least 200 m depth.<\/li><li>Methods:<\/li><li><strong>sequential<\/strong>: empty and refill each tank;<\/li><li><strong>flow-through<\/strong>: pump in and overflow <strong>three times<\/strong> the tank volume;<\/li><li><strong>dilution<\/strong>: fill from the top while discharging from the bottom.<\/li><li>Each has <strong>stability<\/strong> and <strong>stress<\/strong> risks: sequential exchange can produce large bending moments and free surface, and reduce the draught and bridge visibility. The BWM plan has the safe sequence.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>D-2: ballast water performance standard<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>A limit on viable organisms in the discharged water, met by an <strong>approved treatment system<\/strong> (BWMS): filtration and UV, or electro-chlorination, for example.<\/li><li><strong>All ships<\/strong> by <strong>8 September 2024<\/strong>.<\/li><li>Exchange is now only used as a contingency or with treatment, where permitted.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>If I can&#x27;t exchange safely, or the system fails<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Don&#x27;t put the ship at risk<\/strong> or <strong>deviate unreasonably<\/strong>. The convention says exchange isn&#x27;t required if the Master decides it would threaten the ship&#x27;s safety, for example in heavy weather.<\/li><li>Follow the <strong>BWMP contingency measures<\/strong>: for example, retain the ballast, discharge to a reception facility, or exchange in an area the port State agrees.<\/li><li><strong>Record<\/strong> it in the <strong>BWRB<\/strong>, with the reasons.<\/li><li><strong>Tell the next port State<\/strong> before arrival, and ask what they&#x27;ll accept.<\/li><li>Report the BWMS failure to the company and the flag, and get it repaired.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Records<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Every ballast operation in the BWRB: uptake, treatment, exchange, discharge, with positions, volumes and times.<\/li><li>The BWMS operating records.<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>Ballast water and sediments, the UK 2022 Regs with MSN 1908 and MGN 675, D-1 exchange (95%, 200 nm and 200 m or 50 nm, sequential, flow-through three times, dilution, with the stability risks), D-2 treatment for all ships by 8 September 2024, and if exchange is unsafe or the system fails, don&#x27;t risk the ship, use the BWMP contingency, record it and tell the next port State.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":[],"ex":[],"yrs":""},{"id":"Q338","sec":16,"q":"Can you go to port?","tier":"red","rep":34,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":"Kept an alter-to-port answer — fail, Iqbal 2026","law":null,"a":"<p><strong>Have the answer ready for each situation.<\/strong> The examiner often asks this as a follow-up, and he wants the right rule for the situation he described.<\/p><p><strong>1. Give-way vessel, with a vessel on my starboard side (Rule 15)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An alteration to port is <strong>not forbidden<\/strong>, but Rule 15 says the give-way vessel shall, if the circumstances admit, <strong>avoid crossing ahead<\/strong> of the other vessel.<\/li><li>An alteration to port <strong>usually ends<\/strong> with <strong>crossing ahead<\/strong>, and it puts me on a collision course if she alters to starboard.<\/li><li>So <strong>only if<\/strong> it <strong>clearly avoids<\/strong> that, for example a vessel fine on my starboard bow that I can pass port to port with a large CPA, and even then a starboard alteration or speed reduction is normally better.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>2. Stand-on vessel, acting under Rule 17(a)(ii)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>I <strong>shall not<\/strong>, if the <strong>circumstances admit<\/strong>, alter course to <strong>port<\/strong> for a vessel on my <strong>own port<\/strong> side (<strong>Rule 17(c)<\/strong>).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>3. Head-on (Rule 14)<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>No<\/strong>: each vessel alters to <strong>starboard<\/strong> so that each passes on the port side of the other.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>4. Restricted visibility, target forward of the beam (Rule 19(d)(i))<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Avoid<\/strong> an alteration to port for a vessel forward of the beam, <strong>except<\/strong> for a vessel being <strong>overtaken<\/strong>.<\/li><li>For a vessel abeam or abaft the beam on my starboard side, an alteration to port is actually the right move (away from it, under 19(d)(ii)).<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>5. TSS or narrow channel<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Yes<\/strong>, if there&#x27;s <strong>sea room<\/strong> and the rules allow. Rule 9 says keep to the starboard side of a channel, but altering to port within the fairway to avoid danger or to overtake is allowed where safe. In a TSS, I can alter within the lane. Examiner <strong>Bates<\/strong> checks you know you <strong>may<\/strong>.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>6. Overtaking<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Yes<\/strong>: the overtaking vessel can pass on either side, as long as she keeps clear (Rule 13). In a narrow channel, with the Rule 9(e) signals.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>7. For a vessel I&#x27;m required not to impede, or a vessel I must keep clear of under Rule 18<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Depends on the geometry: the best action to keep clear, which may include port if it opens the CPA and doesn&#x27;t cross ahead.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How to answer<\/strong><\/p><ul><li><strong>Answer exactly what was asked<\/strong>: &quot;Can you go to port?&quot; not &quot;Can you pass ahead?&quot; or &quot;What would you do?&quot;.<\/li><li>Say yes or no, then the rule, then the condition. For example: &quot;Not as the stand-on vessel acting under 17(a)(ii), for a vessel on my port side: Rule 17(c), if the circumstances admit.&quot;<\/li><\/ul><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>The right answer for the situation: as give-way under Rule 15 not forbidden but usually wrong because it crosses ahead; as stand-on acting under 17(a)(ii) not for a vessel on my port side under 17(c); head-on never; in restricted visibility avoid for a target forward of the beam except one being overtaken; in a TSS, channel or overtaking, yes if safe and the rules allow.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["So, can you go to port?","You have ruined your oral examination. Why not alter course to port?","Can the vessel on your port quarter alter to port ?","What does rule 18 say","Lights and shapes for towing vessel fishing vessel, CBD crossing port to stbd action (you PDV, can you go to port?"],"ex":[["Examiner not named",5],["Timothy Cummins",4],["Chris Bates",3],["Ahsan Khan",3],["Khairul Anam",3]],"yrs":"2021–2026"},{"id":"Q339","sec":2,"q":"What new regulations have you read?","tier":"yellow","rep":6,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":"2025–26 changes","a":"<p>Pick <strong>two you can explain in detail<\/strong>, because he&#x27;ll ask about whatever you mention. Here&#x27;s my list, newest first.<\/p><p><strong>UK regulations, 2026<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>The MS (Accident Reporting and Investigation) Regs 2026<\/strong> (SI 2026/430), in force 15 May 2026, with <strong>MGN 564 (M+F) Amd 2<\/strong>. Serious injury now means unable to do usual duties for more than 72 hours or in hospital more than 24 hours. Deliberate acts and detentions alone aren&#x27;t reportable. The MAIB has more discretion on what it investigates.<\/li><li><strong>The MS (ISM Code) Regs 2026<\/strong>, in force 31 March 2026, with <strong>MGN 708<\/strong>. They replaced the 2014 Regs and EC 336/2006.<\/li><li><strong>The MS (Port State Control) Regs 2026<\/strong> (SI 2026/563), with <strong>MGN 711<\/strong>, replacing MSN 1832.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>UK guidance<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>MGN 659 (M+F) Amd 3<\/strong> on entry into enclosed spaces (January 2026), under the Enclosed Spaces Regs 2022. With it, the IMO <strong>revised recommendations MSC.581(110)<\/strong>, which bring in a <strong>register of enclosed spaces<\/strong> on board.<\/li><li><strong>COSWP 2026 edition<\/strong> (MIN 688 Amd 5).<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>IMO, in force recently<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>SOLAS V/31 and V/32<\/strong>, lost-container reporting, from <strong>1 January 2026<\/strong> (MSC.550(108)). The Master reports lost or drifting containers to nearby ships, the coastal State and the flag, with the number, position and DG content.<\/li><li><strong>IMDG Code Amendment 42-24<\/strong>, mandatory from 1 January 2026.<\/li><li><strong>MARPOL Annex VI<\/strong>: the <strong>Mediterranean SOx ECA<\/strong> from 1 May 2025; the <strong>Canadian Arctic and Norwegian Sea ECAs<\/strong>, in force 1 March 2026 with 0.10% sulphur from 1 March 2027; and the enhanced data collection from 1 January 2026.<\/li><li><strong>The Hong Kong Convention<\/strong> on ship recycling, in force <strong>26 June 2025<\/strong>, so an Inventory of Hazardous Materials certificate is needed.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>Coming soon<\/strong><\/p><ol><li><strong>Pilot transfer arrangements<\/strong>: new SOLAS V/23 and the performance standards, MSC.572(110) and MSC.576(110), from 2028.<\/li><li>The <strong>MLC 2025 amendments<\/strong> (verify the in-force date).<\/li><li>The IMO <strong>Net-Zero Framework<\/strong>, with MEPC due to reconvene in November 2026.<\/li><\/ol><p><strong>How to answer<\/strong><\/p><p>&quot;Recently I&#x27;ve read the 2026 Accident Reporting Regulations and MGN 564 Amendment 2, and the SOLAS lost-container reporting rules.&quot; Then give three facts about each. If he asks about one you don&#x27;t know well, say so honestly and offer the other.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>This question tests whether you keep up to date. Examiners mark down candidates who quote superseded regulations, so having the 2026 UK Regs ready is a real advantage.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What new regulations have been brought in recently?","Are you aware of the latest amendments for mooring arrangement/operation?","Under what regulations?","SEEMP new regulation?","What do you know about the latest MGN related to Ballast Water Management?"],"ex":[["Chris Bates",2],["Karen McCartney-Clarke",1],["Examiner not named",1],["Ranjit Joseph",1],["Khademul Insan",1]],"yrs":"2024–2025"},{"id":"Q340","sec":2,"q":"What's the difference between the MAIB and the MCA?","tier":"yellow","rep":5,"src":"Real orals 2022–24","tags":[],"fail":null,"law":null,"a":"<p><strong>The MAIB: Marine Accident Investigation Branch<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Part of the <strong>Department for Transport<\/strong>, independent of the MCA.<\/li><li>Powers under <strong>MSA 1995 s.267<\/strong> and the <strong>Accident Reporting and Investigation Regs 2026<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It investigates accidents <strong>only to find the causes and prevent recurrence<\/strong>.<\/li><li>It <strong>does not apportion blame or liability<\/strong>, and it doesn&#x27;t prosecute.<\/li><li>Its inspectors can board, take evidence, require people to answer questions and seize records, including the VDR. But answers given under compulsion <strong>can&#x27;t be used against that person<\/strong> in criminal proceedings (MSA s.259(12)).<\/li><li>It publishes <strong>investigation reports<\/strong>, <strong>safety bulletins<\/strong> and annual reports, and makes <strong>safety recommendations<\/strong> to the MCA, owners, industry and IMO.<\/li><li>Its reports can&#x27;t be used in court to decide liability, so people talk to it freely.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>The MCA: Maritime and Coastguard Agency<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>An executive agency of the DfT, the UK&#x27;s <strong>regulator and enforcer<\/strong>.<\/li><li><strong>Surveys and certification<\/strong>, directly or through ROs.<\/li><li><strong>Flag State inspections<\/strong> of UK ships, and <strong>Port State Control<\/strong> of foreign ships in UK ports.<\/li><li><strong>Enforcement<\/strong>: improvement notices (s.261), prohibition notices (s.262), <strong>detention<\/strong> (s.95), and <strong>prosecution<\/strong> of owners, Masters and crew.<\/li><li>Seafarer <strong>certification<\/strong> and examinations, like this oral.<\/li><li><strong>HM Coastguard<\/strong>: search and rescue coordination, and the counter-pollution response.<\/li><li>Writing the regulations and M Notices for the DfT.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>How they work after the same accident<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Both can investigate separately.<\/li><li>The MAIB investigates for <strong>safety<\/strong> lessons.<\/li><li>The MCA&#x27;s <strong>Enforcement Unit<\/strong> may investigate for <strong>offences<\/strong>, and can prosecute, but it can&#x27;t use the MAIB report or the compelled evidence given to the MAIB.<\/li><li>The police may also investigate if there&#x27;s a death or a crime.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Example<\/strong><\/p><p>After a fatal enclosed space accident on a UK ship: the MAIB investigates why it happened and recommends changes. The MCA checks the ship and company, may detain the ship or issue notices, and may prosecute the company under the Enclosed Spaces Regs 2022.<\/p><p><strong>What I&#x27;d do as C/O<\/strong><\/p><p>Co-operate fully with both, preserve the evidence, give honest statements, and tell the company. I can have a lawyer or a friend with me when interviewed.<\/p><div class=\"close\"><p class=\"lbl\">What the examiner wants<\/p><p>MAIB is safety only, no blame, protected evidence, reports and recommendations; MCA is regulator and enforcer: surveys, inspections, notices, detention and prosecution. Both can investigate the same accident.<\/p><\/div>","pq":null,"pa":null,"asked":["What's difference between MAIB and MCA?","Difference between MAIB inspection and MCA inspection after collision?"],"ex":[["Bill Bennett",3],["Siddhartha Paul",1],["Adam Stratford",1]],"yrs":"2021–2025"}]};